window.HANDBOOK_DATA = {"generatedAt": "2026-10-26", "cpp": {"title": "C++, FAANG DSA & System Design Study Plan — 21-Day Knowledgebase", "version": "1.0", "total_days": 21, "total_estimated_hours": 114, "description": "A comprehensive day-wise curriculum covering core C++ fundamentals, modern C++23, high-frequency FAANG Data Structures & Algorithms patterns, and distributed System Design architectures.", "days": [{"day": 1, "topic": "Core Language Fundamentals", "estimated_hours": 6, "subtopics": [{"title": "Compilation Model — Translation Units, Preprocessing, ODR", "explanation": "A translation unit is the basic unit of compilation in C++. It consists of a source file (.cpp) along with all the headers it includes (directly or transitively), after preprocessing. The preprocessor handles `#include directives` (textual inclusion), `#define macros`, conditional compilation (`#ifdef`/`#ifndef`/`#if`), and other directives before the compiler sees the code.\n\nThe **One Definition Rule** (**ODR**) states that every entity (variable, function, class, `template`) must have exactly one definition in the entire program, though it may have multiple declarations. Inline functions and templates are exceptions — they may be defined in multiple translation units, but all definitions must be identical (token-for-token). **ODR** violations are undefined behavior and usually not diagnosed by the compiler.\n\nThe compilation model means each .cpp file is compiled independently into an object file (.o/.obj). The linker then combines these object files, resolving symbol references across translation units. This is why you declare functions in headers but define them in source files — to avoid multiple definitions.\n\n### Unforgettable Analogy / Deep Dive\nThink of building a C++ program like baking a layered cake from a recipe book:\n- **`Preprocessing**` is like preparing the ingredients: reading the recipe, getting the tools ready, and copying text from your shopping list (replacing `#include` with actual header text).\n- **`Compilation**` is like baking each layer of the cake (translation units) independently into object files (`.o`/`.obj`). The compiler doesn't know about other layers yet; it just compiles one source file at a time.\n- **`Linking**` is assembling the layers together. The linker checks if any layers are missing and ensures that there is only exactly one recipe definition (**`ODR**`) for each layer.\n- ****One Definition Rule** (**ODR**)** prevents multiple co-authors from defining different recipes for the same layer. If one author defines `LayerA` with chocolate and another with vanilla, the editor gets confused and you get Undefined Behavior.", "examples": [{"title": "Translation Unit Example", "code": "// math.h\n#pragma once\nint add(int a, int b);  // declaration\n\n// math.cpp\n#include \"math.h\"\nint add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }  // definition\n\n// main.cpp\n#include \"math.h\"\nint main() { return add(1, 2); }", "explanation": "math.cpp and main.cpp are separate translation units. The linker resolves the call to add().", "formatted_code": "// math.h\n#pragma once\nint add(int a, int b);  // declaration\n\n// math.cpp\n#include \"math.h\"\nint add(int a, int b)\n{\n    return a + b;\n}  // definition\n\n// main.cpp\n#include \"math.h\"\nint main()\n{\n    return add(1, 2);\n}"}, {"title": "ODR Violation Example", "code": "// a.cpp\nstruct Point { int x; int y; };\n// b.cpp\nstruct Point { double x; double y; };  // ODR violation! Different definition", "explanation": "Both files define Point differently. This is UB — the linker may silently pick one, causing subtle bugs.", "formatted_code": "// a.cpp\nstruct Point\n{\n    int x;\n    int y;\n};\n// b.cpp\nstruct Point\n{\n    double x;\n    double y;\n};  // ODR violation! Different definition"}], "pitfalls": ["Forgetting include guards leads to redefinition errors within a single TU", "**ODR** violations across TUs are usually silent UB — no linker error", "Putting non-`inline` function definitions in headers causes linker 'multiple definition' errors", "Circular `#include dependencies` can cause incomplete type errors"], "tips_to_remember": ["One .cpp file = one translation unit (after preprocessing)", "Headers are textually pasted by `#include` — they're not separate TUs", "**ODR** allows multiple identical definitions for `inline`/`template`, but one definition per program for regular functions", "Use `#pragma once` or include guards in every header"], "questions": [{"question": "What is a translation unit?", "answer": "A translation unit is a source file after preprocessing — the .cpp file with all #includes expanded, macros substituted, and conditional compilation resolved. It is the unit that the compiler processes independently."}, {"question": "What is the One Definition Rule?", "answer": "ODR says every used entity must have exactly one definition in the program. Inline functions and templates may have multiple definitions across TUs if they're all identical. Violating ODR is undefined behavior."}, {"question": "Why can't you put function definitions in headers?", "answer": "If a non-inline function is defined in a header and included by multiple .cpp files, each TU gets a definition, causing a linker 'multiple definition' error. Use inline keyword or define in a single .cpp file."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the exact outcome of violating the One Definition Rule (ODR) for an entity across different translation units in C++ when studying `Compilation Model — Translation Units, Preprocessing, ODR`?", "options": ["The compiler will fail to compile with a duplicate definition error.", "The linker will always fail to link with a multiple definition error.", "The program compiles and links successfully, but triggers Undefined Behavior at runtime.", "The program is guaranteed to run correctly if all optimizations are disabled."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "ODR violations across different translation units are undefined behavior (UB). Linkers are not required to diagnose them, and they often silently select one definition, leading to random runtime crashes or wrong results.", "id": "day_1_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT a translation unit in C++?", "options": ["A single `.cpp` file after preprocessing (with headers expanded).", "An individual `.h` header file compiled by itself as a source.", "A source file with all its included headers, after macros have been replaced.", "A temporary preprocessed file (`.i`) generated by the compiler's preprocessor."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A header file by itself is not a translation unit. A translation unit is a source file (.cpp, .cc) with all its included headers expanded by the preprocessor.", "id": "day_1_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What happens if a header with `#pragma once` is copied under a different name to another directory, both are included in a `.cpp` file, and we build `Compilation Model — Translation Units, Preprocessing, ODR`?", "options": ["The compiler ignores the duplicate header because the contents are identical.", "Both headers are included and parsed, potentially causing redefinition errors.", "The linker throws a duplicate symbol error at link time.", "The preprocessor automatically merges the duplicate files."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`#pragma once` is file-path or file-inode identity based. If the file is copied to a different path with a different name, `#pragma once` cannot detect it as the same file, so it is included twice, potentially causing redefinition errors.", "id": "day_1_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "If you define a non-inline global variable `int x = 42;` in a header file, and that header is included by two `.cpp` files, what error occurs?", "options": ["A compilation error in both translation units.", "A linker error for multiple definitions of symbol `x`.", "No error, the variables are merged into a single static storage.", "A runtime access violation when `x` is modified."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Each `.cpp` file including the header defines `x` as a global variable with external linkage. When the linker combines the object files, it finds duplicate definitions for symbol `x` and fails to link.", "id": "day_1_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "In C++, what linkage type does a namespace-scope `static` variable or function have?", "options": ["External linkage — visible across all translation units.", "Internal linkage — local to the translation unit it is defined in.", "No linkage — only visible within the block scope.", "Dynamic linkage — resolved at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The `static` keyword at namespace scope gives the entity internal linkage, meaning its name is only visible and usable within the translation unit where it is defined.", "id": "day_1_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Why are anonymous namespaces preferred over the `static` keyword for internal linkage in modern C++?", "options": ["Anonymous namespaces are faster at compile time.", "`static` cannot be applied to user-defined types (like classes or structs), but anonymous namespaces can.", "Anonymous namespaces give external linkage but keep the names hidden.", "`static` is deprecated in C++20."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "You cannot declare a `static class` or `static struct` at namespace scope to give it internal linkage in C++. Anonymous namespaces solve this by giving internal linkage to all enclosed names, including types, variables, and functions.", "id": "day_1_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the expansion result of the macro `#define MULTIPLY(x, y) x * y` when invoked as `MULTIPLY(1 + 2, 3 + 4)`?", "options": ["21 (7 * 3)", "11 (1 + 2 * 3 + 4)", "9 (1 + 2 * 4)", "Compilation error due to invalid operator combination."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Macros perform raw textual substitution. `MULTIPLY(1 + 2, 3 + 4)` expands literally to `1 + 2 * 3 + 4`. Due to operator precedence, multiplication runs first (`2 * 3 = 6`), yielding `1 + 6 + 4 = 11`. Always parenthesize macro parameters: `((x) * (y))`.", "id": "day_1_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is the static initialization order fiasco in C++?", "options": ["Static local variables are initialized in random order across threads.", "The order of initialization of static variables within the same translation unit is undefined.", "The order of initialization of non-local static variables in different translation units is undefined.", "Compiler optimization silently reorders global constructor calls."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Within a single translation unit, static variables are initialized in order of definition. However, between different translation units, the order of initialization of non-local static variables is undefined, which can cause one static variable to use another uninitialized one.", "id": "day_1_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "Which C++17 feature resolves the header redefinition problem for global constants without violating ODR?", "options": ["`inline` variables.", "`constexpr` functions.", "Anonymous namespaces.", "External template instantiation."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "C++17 introduced `inline` variables. If a variable is declared `inline` (e.g. `inline constexpr int MaxValue = 100;`), it can be defined in multiple translation units (such as via header inclusion) and the linker will merge all definitions into a single object.", "id": "day_1_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "How do C++20 modules affect macro visibility compared to `#include` headers?", "options": ["Modules export all macros defined inside them by default.", "Macros defined in modules are only visible if the module is imported using `import`.", "Modules do not export macros unless explicitly specified, preventing macro pollution.", "Modules completely disable macro substitution."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "One of the major benefits of C++20 modules is that they do not leak macros. A module file can define macros for internal use, but they will not be exported to the importing file, eliminating name pollution.", "id": "day_1_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_1_sub_0", "day": 1, "index": 0}, {"title": "Storage Duration & Linkage", "explanation": "C++ has four storage durations: automatic (local variables on the stack, destroyed at end of scope), `static` (exist for the entire program lifetime — global/namespace-scope variables, `static` locals, `static` class members), dynamic (allocated via new/malloc, programmer-controlled lifetime), and thread-local (`thread_local` keyword, one instance per thread, C++11).\n\nLinkage determines whether a name can be referred to from other translation units. External linkage means the symbol is visible to the linker across TUs (default for non-const namespace-scope variables and functions). Internal linkage means the symbol is local to its TU — achieved via `static` keyword at namespace scope or anonymous namespaces. No linkage means local variables and function parameters.\n\nStatic local variables are initialized the first time control passes through their declaration and persist until program exit. In C++11+, this initialization is guaranteed to be thread-safe (the 'magic statics' guarantee). The `static` keyword on a namespace-scope entity gives it internal linkage, while on a local variable or class member it affects storage duration, not linkage.\n\n### Unforgettable Analogy / Deep Dive\nThink of variables in your code like items in a city:\n- **Automatic (Stack)** is like a temporary rented scooter. You use it within your local neighborhood (scope) and it is reclaimed immediately when you're done.\n- **`Static**` is a historical monument in the town square. It is erected once (initialized) and stays there forever until the city is closed (program exit).\n- **Dynamic (Heap)** is a private plot of land that you allocate (`new`) and must explicitly sell (`delete`). If you forget, it stays owned by nobody, causing a land leak (memory leak).\n- **Thread-`local**` is like a private passport in each citizen's pocket; each thread gets its own private instance.\n- **Internal `Linkage**` is like a local dialect only spoken inside your house (only visible in this translation unit). **External `Linkage**` is like a global language visible across neighborhoods.", "examples": [{"title": "Storage Duration Examples", "code": "#include <iostream>\nint global = 42;              // static duration, external linkage\nstatic int internal = 10;     // static duration, internal linkage\nthread_local int tls = 0;     // thread-local duration\n\nvoid foo()\n{\n    int local = 5;            // automatic duration\n    static int counter = 0;   // static duration, no linkage\n    counter++;\n    std::cout << counter << '\\n';\n}\n\nint main()\n{\n    int* p = new int(7);      // dynamic duration\n    foo(); foo();  // prints 1, then 2\n    delete p;\n}", "explanation": "Shows all four storage durations and both linkage types.", "formatted_code": "#include <iostream>\nint global = 42;  // static duration, external linkage\nstatic int internal = 10;  // static duration, internal linkage\nthread_local int tls = 0;  // thread-local duration\n\nvoid foo()\n{\n    int local = 5;  // automatic duration\n    static int counter = 0;  // static duration, no linkage\n    counter++;\n    std::cout << counter << '\\n';\n}\n\nint main()\n{\n    int* p = new int(7);  // dynamic duration\n    foo();\n    foo();  // prints 1, then 2\n    delete p;\n}"}, {"title": "Anonymous Namespace for Internal Linkage", "code": "namespace {\n    int helper() { return 42; }  // internal linkage (preferred over static)\n}\n// equivalent to: static int helper() { return 42; }", "explanation": "Anonymous namespaces give internal linkage to all enclosed names. Preferred over 'static' for this purpose in modern C++.", "formatted_code": "namespace\n{\n    int helper()\n    {\n        return 42;\n    }  // internal linkage (preferred over static)\n}\n// equivalent to: static int helper() { return 42; }"}], "pitfalls": ["Confusing '`static`' keyword meanings: namespace-scope (internal linkage) vs local (persistent lifetime) vs class member (shared across instances)", "Forgetting that const at namespace scope implies internal linkage in C++ (unlike C)", "Thread-local variables have their constructors/destructors run per-thread, which can be expensive", "Static local initialization is thread-safe in C++11+, but the function body after initialization is not automatically thread-safe"], "tips_to_remember": ["'`static`' at namespace scope = internal linkage; at block scope = persistent lifetime; in class = shared member", "Anonymous namespace is the modern C++ way to get internal linkage (replaces '`static`' at file scope)", "const namespace-scope variables have internal linkage by default (use extern const to force external)", "`thread_local` can be combined with `static` or extern"], "questions": [{"question": "What are the four storage durations in C++?", "answer": "Automatic (local stack variables, destroyed at scope exit), static (global/static variables, exist for program lifetime), dynamic (new/delete, programmer-controlled), and thread-local (thread_local, one per thread, C++11+)."}, {"question": "What is the difference between internal and external linkage?", "answer": "External linkage means the name is visible across translation units — the linker can resolve references to it from other TUs. Internal linkage means the name is only visible within its TU. Use static or anonymous namespaces for internal linkage."}, {"question": "Is static local variable initialization thread-safe?", "answer": "Yes, since C++11. The standard guarantees that if multiple threads attempt to initialize the same static local concurrently, exactly one will perform the initialization and others will block. This is called 'magic statics'."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the exact outcome of violating the One Definition Rule (ODR) for an entity across different translation units in C++ when studying `Storage Duration & Linkage`?", "options": ["The compiler will fail to compile with a duplicate definition error.", "The linker will always fail to link with a multiple definition error.", "The program compiles and links successfully, but triggers Undefined Behavior at runtime.", "The program is guaranteed to run correctly if all optimizations are disabled."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "ODR violations across different translation units are undefined behavior (UB). Linkers are not required to diagnose them, and they often silently select one definition, leading to random runtime crashes or wrong results.", "id": "day_1_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT a translation unit in C++?", "options": ["A single `.cpp` file after preprocessing (with headers expanded).", "An individual `.h` header file compiled by itself as a source.", "A source file with all its included headers, after macros have been replaced.", "A temporary preprocessed file (`.i`) generated by the compiler's preprocessor."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A header file by itself is not a translation unit. A translation unit is a source file (.cpp, .cc) with all its included headers expanded by the preprocessor.", "id": "day_1_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What happens if a header with `#pragma once` is copied under a different name to another directory, both are included in a `.cpp` file, and we build `Storage Duration & Linkage`?", "options": ["The compiler ignores the duplicate header because the contents are identical.", "Both headers are included and parsed, potentially causing redefinition errors.", "The linker throws a duplicate symbol error at link time.", "The preprocessor automatically merges the duplicate files."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`#pragma once` is file-path or file-inode identity based. If the file is copied to a different path with a different name, `#pragma once` cannot detect it as the same file, so it is included twice, potentially causing redefinition errors.", "id": "day_1_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "If you define a non-inline global variable `int x = 42;` in a header file, and that header is included by two `.cpp` files, what error occurs?", "options": ["A compilation error in both translation units.", "A linker error for multiple definitions of symbol `x`.", "No error, the variables are merged into a single static storage.", "A runtime access violation when `x` is modified."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Each `.cpp` file including the header defines `x` as a global variable with external linkage. When the linker combines the object files, it finds duplicate definitions for symbol `x` and fails to link.", "id": "day_1_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "In C++, what linkage type does a namespace-scope `static` variable or function have?", "options": ["External linkage — visible across all translation units.", "Internal linkage — local to the translation unit it is defined in.", "No linkage — only visible within the block scope.", "Dynamic linkage — resolved at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The `static` keyword at namespace scope gives the entity internal linkage, meaning its name is only visible and usable within the translation unit where it is defined.", "id": "day_1_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Why are anonymous namespaces preferred over the `static` keyword for internal linkage in modern C++?", "options": ["Anonymous namespaces are faster at compile time.", "`static` cannot be applied to user-defined types (like classes or structs), but anonymous namespaces can.", "Anonymous namespaces give external linkage but keep the names hidden.", "`static` is deprecated in C++20."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "You cannot declare a `static class` or `static struct` at namespace scope to give it internal linkage in C++. Anonymous namespaces solve this by giving internal linkage to all enclosed names, including types, variables, and functions.", "id": "day_1_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the expansion result of the macro `#define MULTIPLY(x, y) x * y` when invoked as `MULTIPLY(1 + 2, 3 + 4)`?", "options": ["21 (7 * 3)", "11 (1 + 2 * 3 + 4)", "9 (1 + 2 * 4)", "Compilation error due to invalid operator combination."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Macros perform raw textual substitution. `MULTIPLY(1 + 2, 3 + 4)` expands literally to `1 + 2 * 3 + 4`. Due to operator precedence, multiplication runs first (`2 * 3 = 6`), yielding `1 + 6 + 4 = 11`. Always parenthesize macro parameters: `((x) * (y))`.", "id": "day_1_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is the static initialization order fiasco in C++?", "options": ["Static local variables are initialized in random order across threads.", "The order of initialization of static variables within the same translation unit is undefined.", "The order of initialization of non-local static variables in different translation units is undefined.", "Compiler optimization silently reorders global constructor calls."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Within a single translation unit, static variables are initialized in order of definition. However, between different translation units, the order of initialization of non-local static variables is undefined, which can cause one static variable to use another uninitialized one.", "id": "day_1_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "Which C++17 feature resolves the header redefinition problem for global constants without violating ODR?", "options": ["`inline` variables.", "`constexpr` functions.", "Anonymous namespaces.", "External template instantiation."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "C++17 introduced `inline` variables. If a variable is declared `inline` (e.g. `inline constexpr int MaxValue = 100;`), it can be defined in multiple translation units (such as via header inclusion) and the linker will merge all definitions into a single object.", "id": "day_1_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "How do C++20 modules affect macro visibility compared to `#include` headers?", "options": ["Modules export all macros defined inside them by default.", "Macros defined in modules are only visible if the module is imported using `import`.", "Modules do not export macros unless explicitly specified, preventing macro pollution.", "Modules completely disable macro substitution."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "One of the major benefits of C++20 modules is that they do not leak macros. A module file can define macros for internal use, but they will not be exported to the importing file, eliminating name pollution.", "id": "day_1_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_1_sub_1", "day": 1, "index": 1}, {"title": "Value Categories — lvalue, prvalue, xvalue, glvalue", "explanation": "C++11 introduced a refined value category taxonomy to support move semantics. Every expression has two independent properties: does it have identity (can you take its address)? and can it be moved from? This gives the taxonomy: lvalue (has identity, cannot be moved), xvalue (has identity, can be moved — 'eXpiring'), prvalue (no identity, can be moved — 'pure rvalue'). glvalue = lvalue | xvalue (has identity). rvalue = xvalue | prvalue (can be moved).\n\nAn lvalue designates a persistent object — a named variable, a dereferenced pointer, a string literal. You can take its address `with &`. A prvalue is a temporary that doesn't have identity — the result of 42, x+y, or a function returning by value. An xvalue is the result of ``std::move`(x)` or a function returning `T&&` — it has identity (refers to an existing object) but is marked as safe to move from.\n\nUnderstanding value categories is essential for move semantics, perfect forwarding, and understanding when copies vs moves occur. The key insight: `std::move` doesn't move anything — it's just a cast to xvalue (`T&&`), signaling 'you may steal my resources'. The actual move happens when a move constructor/assignment receives this xvalue.\n\n### Unforgettable Analogy / Deep Dive\nThink of value categories like items in a shipping warehouse:\n- **lvalue (Location Value)** is a labeled storage bin. It has a physical, permanent address in memory (identity) that you can inspect with `&`. You cannot easily move the whole bin, but you can copy items out of it.\n- **prvalue (Pure Result Value)** is a physical object flying on a conveyor belt without a label. It has no permanent address (identity), but it is ready to be caught and put into a bin.\n- **xvalue (eXpiring Value)** is a labeled storage bin that has a flag saying: 'This bin is about to be demolished, so feel free to steal its contents.' It has an identity, but is marked as safe to move from.\nMove semantics simply casts an lvalue (labeled bin) into an xvalue (demolition bin) using `std::move`, allowing the compiler to perform a 'theft by agreement' (move constructor) instead of a slow copy.", "examples": [{"title": "Value Category Identification", "code": "#include <string>\nstd::string getName() { return \"hello\"; }\nstd::string&& getTemp() { static std::string s = \"hi\"; return std::move(s); }\n\nint main()\n{\n    std::string s = \"world\";  // s is lvalue\n    // &s — OK, lvalues have identity\n\n    // getName() is a prvalue (temporary, no identity)\n    // &getName() — ERROR: can't take address of prvalue\n\n    // std::move(s) is an xvalue (has identity, movable)\n    // getTemp() is an xvalue (returns T&&)\n\n    std::string a = getName();      // prvalue -> move/copy elision\n    std::string b = std::move(s);   // xvalue -> move constructor\n}", "explanation": "Demonstrates lvalue (s), prvalue (getName()), and xvalue (std::move(s)).", "formatted_code": "#include <string>\nstd::string getName()\n{\n    return \"hello\";\n}\nstd::string&& getTemp()\n{\n    static std::string s = \"hi\";\n    return std::move(s);\n}\n\nint main()\n{\n    std::string s = \"world\";  // s is lvalue\n    // &s — OK, lvalues have identity\n\n    // getName() is a prvalue (temporary, no identity)\n    // &getName() — ERROR: can't take address of prvalue\n\n    // std::move(s) is an xvalue (has identity, movable)\n    // getTemp() is an xvalue (returns T&&)\n\n    std::string a = getName();  // prvalue -> move/copy elision\n    std::string b = std::move(s);  // xvalue -> move constructor\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["Thinking `std::move` actually moves — it just casts to xvalue", "Confusing rvalue references (`T&&`) with rvalues — a named rvalue reference is an lvalue!", "Returning ``std::move`(localVar)` from a function defeats copy elision (**NRVO**)", "Using a moved-from object without resetting it first"], "tips_to_remember": ["lvalue = has identity + can't be implicitly moved. prvalue = no identity + movable. xvalue = has identity + movable", "glvalue = lvalue ∪ xvalue (has identity). rvalue = prvalue ∪ xvalue (movable)", "A named rvalue reference variable is itself an lvalue — you need `std::move` to cast it back", "`std::move` is just `static_cast<T&&>` — the actual moving is done by the move constructor/assignment"], "questions": [{"question": "What are the five value categories in C++11?", "answer": "lvalue (identity, not movable), xvalue (identity, movable — 'eXpiring'), prvalue (no identity, movable — 'pure rvalue'), glvalue (lvalue or xvalue — has identity), rvalue (xvalue or prvalue — movable)."}, {"question": "Is a named rvalue reference an lvalue or rvalue?", "answer": "A named rvalue reference is an lvalue. If you have void foo(std::string&& s), inside foo, 's' is an lvalue because it has a name and identity. You must use std::move(s) to treat it as an rvalue again."}, {"question": "What does std::move actually do?", "answer": "std::move performs an unconditional cast to an xvalue (T&&). It doesn't move anything. It signals to overload resolution that the object can be moved from, so a move constructor or move assignment operator is selected instead of the copy version."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens if you bind a temporary object (prvalue) to a `const T&` reference?", "options": ["The temporary is destroyed at the end of the expression, leaving a dangling reference.", "The lifetime of the temporary is extended to match the lifetime of the reference.", "The compiler makes a permanent copy of the temporary on the heap.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ guarantees that binding a temporary object to a const lvalue reference extends the lifetime of that temporary to match the lifetime of the reference itself, preventing dangling reference bugs.", "id": "day_1_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you try to modify a member variable in a `const` member function in `Value Categories — lvalue, prvalue, xvalue, glvalue`?", "options": ["It compiles fine, but triggers a segmentation fault at runtime.", "Compilation fails, unless the member variable is marked `mutable`.", "It compiles fine, but the changes are discarded when the function exits.", "It compiles fine only if the class is not allocated on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A `const` member function promises not to modify any observable state of the object. Modifying any member variable inside it is a compilation error, unless the member is declared with the `mutable` keyword.", "id": "day_1_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Can you initialize a `constexpr` variable using the return value of a non-constexpr runtime function?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler will defer evaluation to runtime.", "Yes, if the function has no side effects.", "No, compilation will fail because a `constexpr` value must be known at compile time.", "Yes, but only if compiler optimization is turned on."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "`constexpr` variables must be initialized with constant expressions that can be evaluated at compile time. Initializing one with a runtime value results in a compilation error.", "id": "day_1_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the consequence of calling a `consteval` function with non-constant (runtime) arguments?", "options": ["The function is executed at runtime instead.", "The compiler fails to compile the call.", "The compiler generates a runtime check that throws an exception if arguments are invalid.", "The return value is undefined."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`consteval` (introduced in C++20) specifies an immediate function. Immediate functions MUST be evaluated at compile time. Calling them with runtime values is a compilation error.", "id": "day_1_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Can a `constinit` variable be modified after initialization during runtime?", "options": ["No, `constinit` variables are implicitly `const`.", "Yes, `constinit` only requires compile-time initialization, not runtime immutability.", "Yes, but only inside `consteval` functions.", "No, modifying it triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constinit` (C++20) ensures that a variable is initialized at compile time (preventing static initialization order issues), but it does not make the variable constant. To make it constant, you must explicitly combine it with `const`: `constinit const int x = 42;`.", "id": "day_1_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Is it safe to use `static_cast` to cast a `Base*` to a `Derived*`?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler checks the actual runtime type and returns `nullptr` on failure.", "Yes, but it is compile-time checked only. If the pointer is not actually of the Derived type, dereferencing it is UB.", "No, it is a compilation error. You must use `dynamic_cast`.", "Yes, `static_cast` automatically handles polymorphic conversions safely."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`static_cast` performs compile-time conversions. It does not perform any runtime checks. Casting a base pointer to a derived pointer with `static_cast` is unsafe: if the object is not of the derived type, dereferencing the casted pointer is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_1_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast` do if it fails to cast a reference type `Base&` to `Derived&`?", "options": ["It returns a null reference.", "It throws a `std::bad_cast` exception.", "It aborts the program immediately.", "It sets the reference to an empty object."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since there is no such thing as a null reference in C++, `dynamic_cast` cannot return null when casting reference types. Instead, it throws a `std::bad_cast` exception on failure.", "id": "day_1_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "If you cast an `int*` to a `float*` using `reinterpret_cast` and dereference it to read the value, what is the result?", "options": ["It returns the floating-point representation of the integer bits.", "It is a compiler error.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior due to the strict aliasing rule.", "It triggers a hardware trap."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "The strict aliasing rule states that you cannot access an object of one type through a pointer of a different type (with exceptions like `char*` or signed/unsigned variants). Violating this using `reinterpret_cast` triggers undefined behavior.", "id": "day_1_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "If both `void print(int)` and `template<typename T> void print(T)` match a call `print(42)` equally well, which one is selected by overload resolution?", "options": ["The template function, because it is more generic.", "The non-template function.", "The compiler reports an ambiguity error.", "The compiler selects one randomly."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Overload resolution rules state that if a non-template function and a template specialization match a call equally well, the non-template function is always preferred.", "id": "day_1_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) occur if the function call is fully qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)`)?", "options": ["Yes, ADL searches namespaces associated with the type of `x`.", "No, ADL is only performed for unqualified function calls.", "Yes, but only if `x` is a user-defined type.", "No, qualified calls are handled entirely at link time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) only applies to unqualified names (e.g. `foo(x)`). If the call is qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)` or `MyLib::foo(x)`), the compiler uses normal lookup rules and bypasses ADL completely.", "id": "day_1_sub_2_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_1_sub_2", "day": 1, "index": 2}, {"title": "const-correctness — const, constexpr, consteval, constinit", "explanation": "const means 'this value won't change after initialization' (runtime). `constexpr` (C++11) means 'this can be evaluated at compile time' — a `constexpr` function may run at compile time if given constant arguments, or at runtime otherwise. `consteval` (C++20) means 'this function MUST be evaluated at compile time' — calling it at runtime is an error. `constinit` (C++20) means 'this variable must be initialized at compile time' but can be modified later (prevents the `static` initialization order fiasco).\n\nconst-correctness is a design discipline: mark everything const that shouldn't change. const member functions promise not to modify the object's observable state (the this pointer becomes `const T*`). Const references extend the lifetime of temporaries. The mutable keyword allows a member to be modified even in a const context (useful for caches, mutexes).\n\n`constexpr` has been gradually relaxed: C++14 allowed loops and local variables in `constexpr` functions, C++17 added if `constexpr` for compile-time branching, C++20 added `constexpr` `virtual` functions, `constexpr` `std::string`/vector (with transient allocation), and `consteval`/`constinit`.\n\n### Unforgettable Analogy / Deep Dive\nThink of constness as levels of lock-down for a treasure chest:\n- **`const**` is a read-only padlock. You initialize the variable (at runtime) and lock it. You cannot change it, but it still `requires` checking the lock at runtime.\n- **`constexpr**` is pre-calculating the value. Instead of checking the lock, you write the `final` answer directly on the paper before compile-time is over. A `constexpr` function can run at compile time or runtime.\n- **`consteval**` is an immediate function. `It **``must**` be pre-calculated before compilation finishes. Calling it with runtime values is a compile error.\n- **`constinit**` is setting the starting value at compile time to prevent random initializations (`static` order fiasco), but allowing you to change it later during runtime.", "examples": [{"title": "const vs constexpr vs consteval", "code": "const int a = 42;         // runtime constant\nconstexpr int b = 42;     // compile-time constant\n\nconstexpr int square(int n) { return n * n; }\n// Can run at compile time or runtime:\nstatic_assert(square(5) == 25);  // compile time\nint x; std::cin >> x;\nint y = square(x);               // runtime\n\n// C++20:\nconsteval int compileOnly(int n) { return n * n; }\nauto z = compileOnly(5);  // OK: compile time\n// compileOnly(x);        // ERROR: x not a constant expression\n\nconstinit int global = square(10);  // must init at compile time\n// global = 200;  // OK: can modify later at runtime", "explanation": "Shows the spectrum from runtime const to fully compile-time consteval.", "formatted_code": "const int a = 42;  // runtime constant\nconstexpr int b = 42;  // compile-time constant\n\nconstexpr int square(int n)\n{\n    return n * n;\n}\n// Can run at compile time or runtime:\nstatic_assert(square(5) == 25);  // compile time\nint x;\nstd::cin >> x;\nint y = square(x);  // runtime\n\n// C++20:\nconsteval int compileOnly(int n)\n{\n    return n * n;\n}\nauto z = compileOnly(5);  // OK: compile time\n// compileOnly(x);        // ERROR: x not a constant expression\n\nconstinit int global = square(10);  // must init at compile time\n// global = 200;  // OK: can modify later at runtime"}, {"title": "const Member Functions and mutable", "code": "class Cache\n{\n    mutable std::unordered_map<int, int> cache_;\npublic:\n    int compute(int x) const // const method\n    {\n        auto it = cache_.find(x);\n        if (it != cache_.end()) return it->second;\n        int result = x * x;  // expensive computation\n        cache_[x] = result;  // OK: cache_ is mutable\n        return result;\n    }\n};", "explanation": "mutable allows modifying cache_ even in a const method — the observable behavior is still 'const'.", "formatted_code": "class Cache\n{\n    mutable std::unordered_map<int, int> cache_;\npublic:\n    int compute(int x) const  // const method\n    {\n        auto it = cache_.find(x);\n        if (it != cache_.end()) return it->second;\n        int result = x * x;  // expensive computation\n        cache_[x] = result;  // OK: cache_ is mutable\n        return result;\n    }\n};"}], "pitfalls": ["Using `const_cast` to remove const and then modifying a truly const object is UB", "`constexpr` doesn't guarantee compile-time evaluation — only when used in a `constexpr` context", "Forgetting to make member functions const when they don't modify state — prevents calling on const objects", "`constinit` only ensures compile-time initialization, not immutability"], "tips_to_remember": ["const = runtime immutability. `constexpr` = compile-time capable. `consteval` = compile-time required. `constinit` = compile-time init only", "Always pass large objects by const reference (`const T&`) unless you need a copy", "Mark all non-mutating member functions as const", "mutable is for implementation details (caches, mutexes) that don't affect observable state"], "questions": [{"question": "What is the difference between const and constexpr?", "answer": "const means the value won't change after initialization (enforced at runtime). constexpr means the value or function CAN be evaluated at compile time. A constexpr variable is always a compile-time constant; a constexpr function may run at compile or runtime depending on its arguments."}, {"question": "What is consteval and when would you use it?", "answer": "consteval (C++20) marks a function as an 'immediate function' that MUST be evaluated at compile time. If you call it with runtime values, it's a compiler error. Use it for computations that only make sense at compile time, like generating lookup tables."}, {"question": "What problem does constinit solve?", "answer": "constinit (C++20) ensures a variable with static storage duration is initialized at compile time, preventing the 'static initialization order fiasco' where static variables in different TUs may be initialized in unpredictable order. Unlike constexpr, the variable can be modified after initialization."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens if you bind a temporary object (prvalue) to a `const T&` reference?", "options": ["The temporary is destroyed at the end of the expression, leaving a dangling reference.", "The lifetime of the temporary is extended to match the lifetime of the reference.", "The compiler makes a permanent copy of the temporary on the heap.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ guarantees that binding a temporary object to a const lvalue reference extends the lifetime of that temporary to match the lifetime of the reference itself, preventing dangling reference bugs.", "id": "day_1_sub_3_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you try to modify a member variable in a `const` member function in `const-correctness — const, constexpr, consteval, constinit`?", "options": ["It compiles fine, but triggers a segmentation fault at runtime.", "Compilation fails, unless the member variable is marked `mutable`.", "It compiles fine, but the changes are discarded when the function exits.", "It compiles fine only if the class is not allocated on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A `const` member function promises not to modify any observable state of the object. Modifying any member variable inside it is a compilation error, unless the member is declared with the `mutable` keyword.", "id": "day_1_sub_3_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Can you initialize a `constexpr` variable using the return value of a non-constexpr runtime function?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler will defer evaluation to runtime.", "Yes, if the function has no side effects.", "No, compilation will fail because a `constexpr` value must be known at compile time.", "Yes, but only if compiler optimization is turned on."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "`constexpr` variables must be initialized with constant expressions that can be evaluated at compile time. Initializing one with a runtime value results in a compilation error.", "id": "day_1_sub_3_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the consequence of calling a `consteval` function with non-constant (runtime) arguments?", "options": ["The function is executed at runtime instead.", "The compiler fails to compile the call.", "The compiler generates a runtime check that throws an exception if arguments are invalid.", "The return value is undefined."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`consteval` (introduced in C++20) specifies an immediate function. Immediate functions MUST be evaluated at compile time. Calling them with runtime values is a compilation error.", "id": "day_1_sub_3_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Can a `constinit` variable be modified after initialization during runtime?", "options": ["No, `constinit` variables are implicitly `const`.", "Yes, `constinit` only requires compile-time initialization, not runtime immutability.", "Yes, but only inside `consteval` functions.", "No, modifying it triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constinit` (C++20) ensures that a variable is initialized at compile time (preventing static initialization order issues), but it does not make the variable constant. To make it constant, you must explicitly combine it with `const`: `constinit const int x = 42;`.", "id": "day_1_sub_3_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Is it safe to use `static_cast` to cast a `Base*` to a `Derived*`?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler checks the actual runtime type and returns `nullptr` on failure.", "Yes, but it is compile-time checked only. If the pointer is not actually of the Derived type, dereferencing it is UB.", "No, it is a compilation error. You must use `dynamic_cast`.", "Yes, `static_cast` automatically handles polymorphic conversions safely."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`static_cast` performs compile-time conversions. It does not perform any runtime checks. Casting a base pointer to a derived pointer with `static_cast` is unsafe: if the object is not of the derived type, dereferencing the casted pointer is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_1_sub_3_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast` do if it fails to cast a reference type `Base&` to `Derived&`?", "options": ["It returns a null reference.", "It throws a `std::bad_cast` exception.", "It aborts the program immediately.", "It sets the reference to an empty object."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since there is no such thing as a null reference in C++, `dynamic_cast` cannot return null when casting reference types. Instead, it throws a `std::bad_cast` exception on failure.", "id": "day_1_sub_3_mcq_6"}, {"question": "If you cast an `int*` to a `float*` using `reinterpret_cast` and dereference it to read the value, what is the result?", "options": ["It returns the floating-point representation of the integer bits.", "It is a compiler error.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior due to the strict aliasing rule.", "It triggers a hardware trap."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "The strict aliasing rule states that you cannot access an object of one type through a pointer of a different type (with exceptions like `char*` or signed/unsigned variants). Violating this using `reinterpret_cast` triggers undefined behavior.", "id": "day_1_sub_3_mcq_7"}, {"question": "If both `void print(int)` and `template<typename T> void print(T)` match a call `print(42)` equally well, which one is selected by overload resolution?", "options": ["The template function, because it is more generic.", "The non-template function.", "The compiler reports an ambiguity error.", "The compiler selects one randomly."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Overload resolution rules state that if a non-template function and a template specialization match a call equally well, the non-template function is always preferred.", "id": "day_1_sub_3_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) occur if the function call is fully qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)`)?", "options": ["Yes, ADL searches namespaces associated with the type of `x`.", "No, ADL is only performed for unqualified function calls.", "Yes, but only if `x` is a user-defined type.", "No, qualified calls are handled entirely at link time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) only applies to unqualified names (e.g. `foo(x)`). If the call is qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)` or `MyLib::foo(x)`), the compiler uses normal lookup rules and bypasses ADL completely.", "id": "day_1_sub_3_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_1_sub_3", "day": 1, "index": 3}, {"title": "Casts — static_cast, dynamic_cast, const_cast, reinterpret_cast", "explanation": "C++ provides four named cast operators, each with a specific purpose and safety level. `static_cast` performs conversions that are checked at compile time — numeric conversions, upcasts, explicit downcasts (unchecked), enum to int, `void*` to typed pointer. `dynamic_cast` performs runtime-checked downcasts using RTTI (`requires` polymorphic base class with at least one `virtual` function) — returns `nullptr` for pointers or throws `std::bad_cast` for references on failure.\n\n`const_cast` adds or removes const/`volatile` qualifiers. It's the only cast that can do this. Modifying a truly const object through `const_cast` is UB. `reinterpret_cast` performs low-level reinterpretation of bit patterns — pointer to int, one pointer type to another. Almost never portable, and most uses violate strict aliasing.\n\nC-style casts ((int)x) try each C++ cast in order: `const_cast`, `static_cast`, `static_cast`+`const_cast`, `reinterpret_cast`, `reinterpret_cast`+`const_cast`. They're dangerous because they silently fall through to `reinterpret_cast` without warning. Always prefer named casts — they make intent explicit and are grep-able.\n\n### Unforgettable Analogy / Deep Dive\nThink of C++ casts as different tools in a workshop:\n- **`static_cast**` is a standard adapter. You convert integers to floats or base classes to derived classes when you are sure they fit. Checked at compile time.\n- **`dynamic_cast**` is a security guard check. It checks the ID (`RTTI`) of a polymorphic pointer at runtime. If the pointer isn't actually a Derived class, it returns `nullptr` or throws. Safest but has runtime cost.\n- **`const_cast**` is a key to a display cabinet. It lets you reach inside a const variable to change it (safe only if the original variable wasn't declared const, otherwise it is UB!).\n- **`reinterpret_cast**` is looking at a wooden block and claiming it is a block of metal. It forces the compiler to treat the raw bits as another type. Highly dangerous, violates strict aliasing, and is rarely portable.\n- **C-style `casts**` are like sledgehammers that force shapes through holes. They try `const_cast`, `static_cast`, and then silently fall back to `reinterpret_cast` without warning.", "examples": [{"title": "Named Casts", "code": "class Base { public: virtual ~Base() {} };\nclass Derived : public Base { public: int data = 42; };\n\nBase* b = new Derived();\n\n// static_cast: compile-time checked, no runtime check\nDerived* d1 = static_cast<Derived*>(b);  // OK but unchecked\n\n// dynamic_cast: runtime RTTI check\nDerived* d2 = dynamic_cast<Derived*>(b);  // safe, returns nullptr on failure\nif (d2) { /* use d2 */ }\n\n// const_cast: remove const\nconst int x = 42;\nint& ref = const_cast<int&>(x);  // OK to cast, but modifying is UB!\n\n// reinterpret_cast: bit-level reinterpretation\nlong addr = reinterpret_cast<long>(b);  // pointer to integer\n\ndelete b;", "explanation": "Each cast has a specific, limited purpose — making intent clear and searchable.", "formatted_code": "class Base\n{\n    public: virtual ~Base()\n    {\n    }\n};\nclass Derived : public Base\n{\n    public: int data = 42;\n};\n\nBase* b = new Derived();\n\n// static_cast: compile-time checked, no runtime check\nDerived* d1 = static_cast<Derived*>(b);  // OK but unchecked\n\n// dynamic_cast: runtime RTTI check\nDerived* d2 = dynamic_cast<Derived*>(b);  // safe, returns nullptr on failure\nif (d2)\n{\n    /* use d2 */\n}\n\n// const_cast: remove const\nconst int x = 42;\nint& ref = const_cast<int&>(x);  // OK to cast, but modifying is UB!\n\n// reinterpret_cast: bit-level reinterpretation\nlong addr = reinterpret_cast<long>(b);  // pointer to integer\n\ndelete b;"}], "pitfalls": ["Using `static_cast` for downcasts without being sure of the actual type — UB if wrong", "Using C-style casts that silently become `reinterpret_cast`", "Modifying a truly const object after `const_cast` — undefined behavior", "`dynamic_cast` `requires` a polymorphic type (at least one `virtual` function)"], "tips_to_remember": ["Use `static_cast` for safe, well-defined conversions (numeric, upcast, explicit conversions)", "Use `dynamic_cast` only for runtime-checked polymorphic downcasts", "`const_cast` should be rare — typically for interfacing with legacy C APIs", "Never use C-style casts in C++ code — they hide dangerous reinterpret_casts"], "questions": [{"question": "Why are C-style casts discouraged in C++?", "answer": "C-style casts try multiple cast types silently and may fall through to reinterpret_cast without warning. They're not grep-able, hide intent, and can silently perform dangerous operations. Named casts make intent explicit and limit what conversions are possible."}, {"question": "When does dynamic_cast return nullptr vs throw?", "answer": "dynamic_cast<Derived*>(basePtr) returns nullptr on failure (pointer form). dynamic_cast<Derived&>(baseRef) throws std::bad_cast on failure (reference form). It requires the base class to have at least one virtual function (be polymorphic)."}, {"question": "Is const_cast ever safe?", "answer": "const_cast is safe to use to add const. Removing const is safe ONLY if the original object was not declared const. Modifying a truly const object through const_cast is undefined behavior. Common legitimate use: calling a non-const overload from a const overload (Scott Meyers' pattern)."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens if you bind a temporary object (prvalue) to a `const T&` reference?", "options": ["The temporary is destroyed at the end of the expression, leaving a dangling reference.", "The lifetime of the temporary is extended to match the lifetime of the reference.", "The compiler makes a permanent copy of the temporary on the heap.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ guarantees that binding a temporary object to a const lvalue reference extends the lifetime of that temporary to match the lifetime of the reference itself, preventing dangling reference bugs.", "id": "day_1_sub_4_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you try to modify a member variable in a `const` member function in `Casts — static_cast, dynamic_cast, const_cast, reinterpret_cast`?", "options": ["It compiles fine, but triggers a segmentation fault at runtime.", "Compilation fails, unless the member variable is marked `mutable`.", "It compiles fine, but the changes are discarded when the function exits.", "It compiles fine only if the class is not allocated on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A `const` member function promises not to modify any observable state of the object. Modifying any member variable inside it is a compilation error, unless the member is declared with the `mutable` keyword.", "id": "day_1_sub_4_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Can you initialize a `constexpr` variable using the return value of a non-constexpr runtime function?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler will defer evaluation to runtime.", "Yes, if the function has no side effects.", "No, compilation will fail because a `constexpr` value must be known at compile time.", "Yes, but only if compiler optimization is turned on."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "`constexpr` variables must be initialized with constant expressions that can be evaluated at compile time. Initializing one with a runtime value results in a compilation error.", "id": "day_1_sub_4_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the consequence of calling a `consteval` function with non-constant (runtime) arguments?", "options": ["The function is executed at runtime instead.", "The compiler fails to compile the call.", "The compiler generates a runtime check that throws an exception if arguments are invalid.", "The return value is undefined."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`consteval` (introduced in C++20) specifies an immediate function. Immediate functions MUST be evaluated at compile time. Calling them with runtime values is a compilation error.", "id": "day_1_sub_4_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Can a `constinit` variable be modified after initialization during runtime?", "options": ["No, `constinit` variables are implicitly `const`.", "Yes, `constinit` only requires compile-time initialization, not runtime immutability.", "Yes, but only inside `consteval` functions.", "No, modifying it triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constinit` (C++20) ensures that a variable is initialized at compile time (preventing static initialization order issues), but it does not make the variable constant. To make it constant, you must explicitly combine it with `const`: `constinit const int x = 42;`.", "id": "day_1_sub_4_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Is it safe to use `static_cast` to cast a `Base*` to a `Derived*`?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler checks the actual runtime type and returns `nullptr` on failure.", "Yes, but it is compile-time checked only. If the pointer is not actually of the Derived type, dereferencing it is UB.", "No, it is a compilation error. You must use `dynamic_cast`.", "Yes, `static_cast` automatically handles polymorphic conversions safely."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`static_cast` performs compile-time conversions. It does not perform any runtime checks. Casting a base pointer to a derived pointer with `static_cast` is unsafe: if the object is not of the derived type, dereferencing the casted pointer is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_1_sub_4_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast` do if it fails to cast a reference type `Base&` to `Derived&`?", "options": ["It returns a null reference.", "It throws a `std::bad_cast` exception.", "It aborts the program immediately.", "It sets the reference to an empty object."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since there is no such thing as a null reference in C++, `dynamic_cast` cannot return null when casting reference types. Instead, it throws a `std::bad_cast` exception on failure.", "id": "day_1_sub_4_mcq_6"}, {"question": "If you cast an `int*` to a `float*` using `reinterpret_cast` and dereference it to read the value, what is the result?", "options": ["It returns the floating-point representation of the integer bits.", "It is a compiler error.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior due to the strict aliasing rule.", "It triggers a hardware trap."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "The strict aliasing rule states that you cannot access an object of one type through a pointer of a different type (with exceptions like `char*` or signed/unsigned variants). Violating this using `reinterpret_cast` triggers undefined behavior.", "id": "day_1_sub_4_mcq_7"}, {"question": "If both `void print(int)` and `template<typename T> void print(T)` match a call `print(42)` equally well, which one is selected by overload resolution?", "options": ["The template function, because it is more generic.", "The non-template function.", "The compiler reports an ambiguity error.", "The compiler selects one randomly."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Overload resolution rules state that if a non-template function and a template specialization match a call equally well, the non-template function is always preferred.", "id": "day_1_sub_4_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) occur if the function call is fully qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)`)?", "options": ["Yes, ADL searches namespaces associated with the type of `x`.", "No, ADL is only performed for unqualified function calls.", "Yes, but only if `x` is a user-defined type.", "No, qualified calls are handled entirely at link time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) only applies to unqualified names (e.g. `foo(x)`). If the call is qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)` or `MyLib::foo(x)`), the compiler uses normal lookup rules and bypasses ADL completely.", "id": "day_1_sub_4_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_1_sub_4", "day": 1, "index": 4}, {"title": "Overload Resolution Rules and Ambiguity", "explanation": "When a function call matches multiple overloaded functions, the compiler uses overload resolution to pick the best match. The process: (1) build a candidate set of functions with matching names, (2) filter to viable candidates whose parameters can accept the arguments (with conversions), (3) rank each viable candidate by the conversions required, (4) select the best match or report ambiguity.\n\nConversion ranking from best to worst: exact match (no conversion) > promotion (char→int, float→double) > standard conversion (int→double, `derived*`→`base*`) > user-defined conversion (constructor or conversion operator) > ellipsis match (...). If two candidates require the same rank of conversion, it's ambiguous.\n\nKey subtleties: const overloads (`f(int&)` vs `f(const int&)`), `template` vs non-`template` (non-`template` preferred if equally good match), and narrowing conversions. Adding overloads can break existing code — an overload that's a better match may be selected instead of a previously used one.", "examples": [{"title": "Overload Resolution Example", "code": "void print(int x)    { std::cout << \"int: \" << x << '\\n'; }\nvoid print(double x) { std::cout << \"double: \" << x << '\\n'; }\nvoid print(const std::string& s) { std::cout << \"string: \" << s << '\\n'; }\n\nprint(42);       // exact match: print(int)\nprint(3.14);     // exact match: print(double)\nprint(3.14f);    // promotion: float->double, calls print(double)\n// print(42L);   // ambiguous: long->int and long->double are both standard conversions", "explanation": "Shows exact match, promotion, and ambiguity in overload resolution.", "formatted_code": "void print(int x)\n{\n    std::cout << \"int: \" << x << '\\n';\n}\nvoid print(double x)\n{\n    std::cout << \"double: \" << x << '\\n';\n}\nvoid print(const std::string& s)\n{\n    std::cout << \"string: \" << s << '\\n';\n}\n\nprint(42);  // exact match: print(int)\nprint(3.14);  // exact match: print(double)\nprint(3.14f);  // promotion: float->double, calls print(double)\n// print(42L);   // ambiguous: long->int and long->double are both standard conversions"}], "pitfalls": ["Adding a new overload can silently change which function existing code calls", "Implicit conversions through constructors can create unexpected overload candidates", "bool overloads match almost everything due to implicit conversion", "Template overloads participate in resolution and can win unexpectedly"], "tips_to_remember": ["Exact match > promotion > standard conversion > user-defined conversion", "Non-`template` functions are preferred over templates when both are equally good matches", "Use explicit keyword on constructors to prevent implicit conversions from entering overload resolution", "If confused, use `static_cast` at the call site to force a specific overload"], "questions": [{"question": "What is the order of preference in overload resolution?", "answer": "Exact match (no conversion needed), then promotion (char/short→int, float→double), then standard conversions (int→double, derived*→base*), then user-defined conversions, then ellipsis (...). Within each rank, more specific matches win."}, {"question": "What causes overload ambiguity?", "answer": "Ambiguity occurs when two or more viable overloads require conversions of equal rank and neither is strictly better. For example, calling f(long) when both f(int) and f(double) exist — long→int and long→double are both standard conversions with no preference."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens if you bind a temporary object (prvalue) to a `const T&` reference?", "options": ["The temporary is destroyed at the end of the expression, leaving a dangling reference.", "The lifetime of the temporary is extended to match the lifetime of the reference.", "The compiler makes a permanent copy of the temporary on the heap.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ guarantees that binding a temporary object to a const lvalue reference extends the lifetime of that temporary to match the lifetime of the reference itself, preventing dangling reference bugs.", "id": "day_1_sub_5_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you try to modify a member variable in a `const` member function in `Overload Resolution Rules and Ambiguity`?", "options": ["It compiles fine, but triggers a segmentation fault at runtime.", "Compilation fails, unless the member variable is marked `mutable`.", "It compiles fine, but the changes are discarded when the function exits.", "It compiles fine only if the class is not allocated on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A `const` member function promises not to modify any observable state of the object. Modifying any member variable inside it is a compilation error, unless the member is declared with the `mutable` keyword.", "id": "day_1_sub_5_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Can you initialize a `constexpr` variable using the return value of a non-constexpr runtime function?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler will defer evaluation to runtime.", "Yes, if the function has no side effects.", "No, compilation will fail because a `constexpr` value must be known at compile time.", "Yes, but only if compiler optimization is turned on."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "`constexpr` variables must be initialized with constant expressions that can be evaluated at compile time. Initializing one with a runtime value results in a compilation error.", "id": "day_1_sub_5_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the consequence of calling a `consteval` function with non-constant (runtime) arguments?", "options": ["The function is executed at runtime instead.", "The compiler fails to compile the call.", "The compiler generates a runtime check that throws an exception if arguments are invalid.", "The return value is undefined."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`consteval` (introduced in C++20) specifies an immediate function. Immediate functions MUST be evaluated at compile time. Calling them with runtime values is a compilation error.", "id": "day_1_sub_5_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Can a `constinit` variable be modified after initialization during runtime?", "options": ["No, `constinit` variables are implicitly `const`.", "Yes, `constinit` only requires compile-time initialization, not runtime immutability.", "Yes, but only inside `consteval` functions.", "No, modifying it triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constinit` (C++20) ensures that a variable is initialized at compile time (preventing static initialization order issues), but it does not make the variable constant. To make it constant, you must explicitly combine it with `const`: `constinit const int x = 42;`.", "id": "day_1_sub_5_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Is it safe to use `static_cast` to cast a `Base*` to a `Derived*`?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler checks the actual runtime type and returns `nullptr` on failure.", "Yes, but it is compile-time checked only. If the pointer is not actually of the Derived type, dereferencing it is UB.", "No, it is a compilation error. You must use `dynamic_cast`.", "Yes, `static_cast` automatically handles polymorphic conversions safely."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`static_cast` performs compile-time conversions. It does not perform any runtime checks. Casting a base pointer to a derived pointer with `static_cast` is unsafe: if the object is not of the derived type, dereferencing the casted pointer is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_1_sub_5_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast` do if it fails to cast a reference type `Base&` to `Derived&`?", "options": ["It returns a null reference.", "It throws a `std::bad_cast` exception.", "It aborts the program immediately.", "It sets the reference to an empty object."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since there is no such thing as a null reference in C++, `dynamic_cast` cannot return null when casting reference types. Instead, it throws a `std::bad_cast` exception on failure.", "id": "day_1_sub_5_mcq_6"}, {"question": "If you cast an `int*` to a `float*` using `reinterpret_cast` and dereference it to read the value, what is the result?", "options": ["It returns the floating-point representation of the integer bits.", "It is a compiler error.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior due to the strict aliasing rule.", "It triggers a hardware trap."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "The strict aliasing rule states that you cannot access an object of one type through a pointer of a different type (with exceptions like `char*` or signed/unsigned variants). Violating this using `reinterpret_cast` triggers undefined behavior.", "id": "day_1_sub_5_mcq_7"}, {"question": "If both `void print(int)` and `template<typename T> void print(T)` match a call `print(42)` equally well, which one is selected by overload resolution?", "options": ["The template function, because it is more generic.", "The non-template function.", "The compiler reports an ambiguity error.", "The compiler selects one randomly."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Overload resolution rules state that if a non-template function and a template specialization match a call equally well, the non-template function is always preferred.", "id": "day_1_sub_5_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) occur if the function call is fully qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)`)?", "options": ["Yes, ADL searches namespaces associated with the type of `x`.", "No, ADL is only performed for unqualified function calls.", "Yes, but only if `x` is a user-defined type.", "No, qualified calls are handled entirely at link time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) only applies to unqualified names (e.g. `foo(x)`). If the call is qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)` or `MyLib::foo(x)`), the compiler uses normal lookup rules and bypasses ADL completely.", "id": "day_1_sub_5_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_1_sub_5", "day": 1, "index": 5}, {"title": "Namespaces & ADL (Argument-Dependent Lookup)", "explanation": "Namespaces prevent name collisions by providing scoped naming. **ADL** (also called Koenig Lookup) is a rule that, for unqualified function calls, the compiler also searches the namespaces of the argument types. This is why `std::cout` << x works without writing `std::`operator<<`` — the compiler finds `operator<<` in namespace std because cout is in std.\n\n**ADL** is essential for operator overloading and generic code. Without it, you'd need to qualify every operator call. However, **ADL** can also cause surprises — a function in an associated namespace might be found when you didn't expect it. The using directive ('using namespace std') imports all names and can cause ambiguity.\n\nBest practices: use using declarations (using `std::vector`) rather than using directives (using namespace std). Never use 'using namespace' in header files. Use `inline` namespaces for API versioning.", "examples": [{"title": "ADL in Action", "code": "namespace MyLib\n{\n    struct Widget {};\n    void process(Widget w) { std::cout << \"MyLib::process\\n\"; }\n}\n\nint main()\n{\n    MyLib::Widget w;\n    process(w);  // Found via ADL! No need for MyLib::process(w)\n}", "explanation": "process() is found because w's type is in MyLib, so the compiler searches MyLib.", "formatted_code": "namespace MyLib\n{\n    struct Widget\n    {\n    };\n    void process(Widget w)\n    {\n        std::cout << \"MyLib::process\\n\";\n    }\n}\n\nint main()\n{\n    MyLib::Widget w;\n    process(w);  // Found via ADL! No need for MyLib::process(w)\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["'using namespace std' in headers pollutes every includer's namespace", "**ADL** can find functions you didn't intend — especially problematic with common names like `swap()`", "Forgetting that **ADL** doesn't apply to qualified calls (``MyLib::f`()` won't trigger further **ADL**)"], "tips_to_remember": ["**ADL** searches the namespaces associated with a function's argument types", "Never put 'using namespace' in a header file", "Prefer using declarations (using `std::string`) over using directives (using namespace std)", "Define `swap()` and operator overloads in the same namespace as your type for **ADL** to find them"], "questions": [{"question": "What is ADL and why does it exist?", "answer": "Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) automatically searches the namespaces of a function's argument types during unqualified lookup. It exists primarily to make operator overloading work naturally — without it, std::cout << x would require writing std::operator<<(std::cout, x)."}, {"question": "Why should you never use 'using namespace std' in a header?", "answer": "Because every file that includes your header would have all of std's names dumped into its scope, causing potential name collisions and ambiguity. It defeats the purpose of namespaces and can silently change the meaning of existing code."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the exact outcome of violating the One Definition Rule (ODR) for an entity across different translation units in C++ when studying `Namespaces & ADL (Argument-Dependent Lookup)`?", "options": ["The compiler will fail to compile with a duplicate definition error.", "The linker will always fail to link with a multiple definition error.", "The program compiles and links successfully, but triggers Undefined Behavior at runtime.", "The program is guaranteed to run correctly if all optimizations are disabled."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "ODR violations across different translation units are undefined behavior (UB). Linkers are not required to diagnose them, and they often silently select one definition, leading to random runtime crashes or wrong results.", "id": "day_1_sub_6_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT a translation unit in C++?", "options": ["A single `.cpp` file after preprocessing (with headers expanded).", "An individual `.h` header file compiled by itself as a source.", "A source file with all its included headers, after macros have been replaced.", "A temporary preprocessed file (`.i`) generated by the compiler's preprocessor."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A header file by itself is not a translation unit. A translation unit is a source file (.cpp, .cc) with all its included headers expanded by the preprocessor.", "id": "day_1_sub_6_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What happens if a header with `#pragma once` is copied under a different name to another directory, both are included in a `.cpp` file, and we build `Namespaces & ADL (Argument-Dependent Lookup)`?", "options": ["The compiler ignores the duplicate header because the contents are identical.", "Both headers are included and parsed, potentially causing redefinition errors.", "The linker throws a duplicate symbol error at link time.", "The preprocessor automatically merges the duplicate files."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`#pragma once` is file-path or file-inode identity based. If the file is copied to a different path with a different name, `#pragma once` cannot detect it as the same file, so it is included twice, potentially causing redefinition errors.", "id": "day_1_sub_6_mcq_2"}, {"question": "If you define a non-inline global variable `int x = 42;` in a header file, and that header is included by two `.cpp` files, what error occurs?", "options": ["A compilation error in both translation units.", "A linker error for multiple definitions of symbol `x`.", "No error, the variables are merged into a single static storage.", "A runtime access violation when `x` is modified."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Each `.cpp` file including the header defines `x` as a global variable with external linkage. When the linker combines the object files, it finds duplicate definitions for symbol `x` and fails to link.", "id": "day_1_sub_6_mcq_3"}, {"question": "In C++, what linkage type does a namespace-scope `static` variable or function have?", "options": ["External linkage — visible across all translation units.", "Internal linkage — local to the translation unit it is defined in.", "No linkage — only visible within the block scope.", "Dynamic linkage — resolved at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The `static` keyword at namespace scope gives the entity internal linkage, meaning its name is only visible and usable within the translation unit where it is defined.", "id": "day_1_sub_6_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Why are anonymous namespaces preferred over the `static` keyword for internal linkage in modern C++?", "options": ["Anonymous namespaces are faster at compile time.", "`static` cannot be applied to user-defined types (like classes or structs), but anonymous namespaces can.", "Anonymous namespaces give external linkage but keep the names hidden.", "`static` is deprecated in C++20."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "You cannot declare a `static class` or `static struct` at namespace scope to give it internal linkage in C++. Anonymous namespaces solve this by giving internal linkage to all enclosed names, including types, variables, and functions.", "id": "day_1_sub_6_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the expansion result of the macro `#define MULTIPLY(x, y) x * y` when invoked as `MULTIPLY(1 + 2, 3 + 4)`?", "options": ["21 (7 * 3)", "11 (1 + 2 * 3 + 4)", "9 (1 + 2 * 4)", "Compilation error due to invalid operator combination."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Macros perform raw textual substitution. `MULTIPLY(1 + 2, 3 + 4)` expands literally to `1 + 2 * 3 + 4`. Due to operator precedence, multiplication runs first (`2 * 3 = 6`), yielding `1 + 6 + 4 = 11`. Always parenthesize macro parameters: `((x) * (y))`.", "id": "day_1_sub_6_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is the static initialization order fiasco in C++?", "options": ["Static local variables are initialized in random order across threads.", "The order of initialization of static variables within the same translation unit is undefined.", "The order of initialization of non-local static variables in different translation units is undefined.", "Compiler optimization silently reorders global constructor calls."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Within a single translation unit, static variables are initialized in order of definition. However, between different translation units, the order of initialization of non-local static variables is undefined, which can cause one static variable to use another uninitialized one.", "id": "day_1_sub_6_mcq_7"}, {"question": "Which C++17 feature resolves the header redefinition problem for global constants without violating ODR?", "options": ["`inline` variables.", "`constexpr` functions.", "Anonymous namespaces.", "External template instantiation."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "C++17 introduced `inline` variables. If a variable is declared `inline` (e.g. `inline constexpr int MaxValue = 100;`), it can be defined in multiple translation units (such as via header inclusion) and the linker will merge all definitions into a single object.", "id": "day_1_sub_6_mcq_8"}, {"question": "How do C++20 modules affect macro visibility compared to `#include` headers?", "options": ["Modules export all macros defined inside them by default.", "Macros defined in modules are only visible if the module is imported using `import`.", "Modules do not export macros unless explicitly specified, preventing macro pollution.", "Modules completely disable macro substitution."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "One of the major benefits of C++20 modules is that they do not leak macros. A module file can define macros for internal use, but they will not be exported to the importing file, eliminating name pollution.", "id": "day_1_sub_6_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_1_sub_6", "day": 1, "index": 6}, {"title": "enum vs enum class", "explanation": "C-style enums (enum Color { Red, Green, Blue }) have several problems: their enumerators leak into the enclosing scope, they implicitly convert to int, and different enums can be compared without warning. enum class (scoped enumerations, C++11) fixes all of these: enumerators are scoped (`Color::Red`), no implicit conversion to int, and you can specify the underlying type.\n\nAlways prefer enum class in modern C++. Use `static_cast` when you need integer conversion. You can specify the underlying type for both enum and enum class (enum class Flags : uint8_t).", "examples": [{"title": "enum vs enum class", "code": "// Old-style enum\nenum Color { Red, Green, Blue };  // Red, Green, Blue leak into enclosing scope\nint x = Red;  // implicit conversion to int — no warning\n\n// Modern enum class\nenum class Direction : uint8_t { North, South, East, West };\n// int y = Direction::North;  // ERROR: no implicit conversion\nint y = static_cast<int>(Direction::North);  // explicit conversion required\nauto d = Direction::East;  // scoped access", "explanation": "enum class provides type safety and scoping.", "formatted_code": "// Old-style enum\nenum Color\n{\n    Red, Green, Blue\n};  // Red, Green, Blue leak into enclosing scope\nint x = Red;  // implicit conversion to int — no warning\n\n// Modern enum class\nenum class Direction : uint8_t\n{\n    North, South, East, West\n};\n// int y = Direction::North;  // ERROR: no implicit conversion\nint y = static_cast<int>(Direction::North);  // explicit conversion required\nauto d = Direction::East;  // scoped access"}], "pitfalls": ["Unscoped enums pollute the enclosing namespace with their enumerators", "Implicit int conversion of old enums allows comparing unrelated enum types", "Forgetting `static_cast` when you need int from enum class"], "tips_to_remember": ["Always use enum class unless you need implicit int conversion for legacy interop", "Specify underlying type explicitly when you need a specific size (enum class Flags : uint32_t)", "Use `to_underlying()` (C++23) or `static_cast` to convert enum class to int"], "questions": [{"question": "What's the difference between enum and enum class?", "answer": "enum class (C++11) provides scoped enumerators (accessed as Type::Value), no implicit int conversion, and specifiable underlying type. Plain enum leaks names into enclosing scope and implicitly converts to int. Always prefer enum class."}, {"question": "How do you convert an enum class to its underlying integer type?", "answer": "Use static_cast<int>(myEnum) or static_cast<std::underlying_type_t<MyEnum>>(myEnum). C++23 adds std::to_underlying(myEnum) for convenience."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens if you bind a temporary object (prvalue) to a `const T&` reference?", "options": ["The temporary is destroyed at the end of the expression, leaving a dangling reference.", "The lifetime of the temporary is extended to match the lifetime of the reference.", "The compiler makes a permanent copy of the temporary on the heap.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ guarantees that binding a temporary object to a const lvalue reference extends the lifetime of that temporary to match the lifetime of the reference itself, preventing dangling reference bugs.", "id": "day_1_sub_7_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you try to modify a member variable in a `const` member function in `enum vs enum class`?", "options": ["It compiles fine, but triggers a segmentation fault at runtime.", "Compilation fails, unless the member variable is marked `mutable`.", "It compiles fine, but the changes are discarded when the function exits.", "It compiles fine only if the class is not allocated on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A `const` member function promises not to modify any observable state of the object. Modifying any member variable inside it is a compilation error, unless the member is declared with the `mutable` keyword.", "id": "day_1_sub_7_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Can you initialize a `constexpr` variable using the return value of a non-constexpr runtime function?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler will defer evaluation to runtime.", "Yes, if the function has no side effects.", "No, compilation will fail because a `constexpr` value must be known at compile time.", "Yes, but only if compiler optimization is turned on."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "`constexpr` variables must be initialized with constant expressions that can be evaluated at compile time. Initializing one with a runtime value results in a compilation error.", "id": "day_1_sub_7_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the consequence of calling a `consteval` function with non-constant (runtime) arguments?", "options": ["The function is executed at runtime instead.", "The compiler fails to compile the call.", "The compiler generates a runtime check that throws an exception if arguments are invalid.", "The return value is undefined."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`consteval` (introduced in C++20) specifies an immediate function. Immediate functions MUST be evaluated at compile time. Calling them with runtime values is a compilation error.", "id": "day_1_sub_7_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Can a `constinit` variable be modified after initialization during runtime?", "options": ["No, `constinit` variables are implicitly `const`.", "Yes, `constinit` only requires compile-time initialization, not runtime immutability.", "Yes, but only inside `consteval` functions.", "No, modifying it triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constinit` (C++20) ensures that a variable is initialized at compile time (preventing static initialization order issues), but it does not make the variable constant. To make it constant, you must explicitly combine it with `const`: `constinit const int x = 42;`.", "id": "day_1_sub_7_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Is it safe to use `static_cast` to cast a `Base*` to a `Derived*`?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler checks the actual runtime type and returns `nullptr` on failure.", "Yes, but it is compile-time checked only. If the pointer is not actually of the Derived type, dereferencing it is UB.", "No, it is a compilation error. You must use `dynamic_cast`.", "Yes, `static_cast` automatically handles polymorphic conversions safely."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`static_cast` performs compile-time conversions. It does not perform any runtime checks. Casting a base pointer to a derived pointer with `static_cast` is unsafe: if the object is not of the derived type, dereferencing the casted pointer is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_1_sub_7_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast` do if it fails to cast a reference type `Base&` to `Derived&`?", "options": ["It returns a null reference.", "It throws a `std::bad_cast` exception.", "It aborts the program immediately.", "It sets the reference to an empty object."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since there is no such thing as a null reference in C++, `dynamic_cast` cannot return null when casting reference types. Instead, it throws a `std::bad_cast` exception on failure.", "id": "day_1_sub_7_mcq_6"}, {"question": "If you cast an `int*` to a `float*` using `reinterpret_cast` and dereference it to read the value, what is the result?", "options": ["It returns the floating-point representation of the integer bits.", "It is a compiler error.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior due to the strict aliasing rule.", "It triggers a hardware trap."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "The strict aliasing rule states that you cannot access an object of one type through a pointer of a different type (with exceptions like `char*` or signed/unsigned variants). Violating this using `reinterpret_cast` triggers undefined behavior.", "id": "day_1_sub_7_mcq_7"}, {"question": "If both `void print(int)` and `template<typename T> void print(T)` match a call `print(42)` equally well, which one is selected by overload resolution?", "options": ["The template function, because it is more generic.", "The non-template function.", "The compiler reports an ambiguity error.", "The compiler selects one randomly."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Overload resolution rules state that if a non-template function and a template specialization match a call equally well, the non-template function is always preferred.", "id": "day_1_sub_7_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) occur if the function call is fully qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)`)?", "options": ["Yes, ADL searches namespaces associated with the type of `x`.", "No, ADL is only performed for unqualified function calls.", "Yes, but only if `x` is a user-defined type.", "No, qualified calls are handled entirely at link time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) only applies to unqualified names (e.g. `foo(x)`). If the call is qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)` or `MyLib::foo(x)`), the compiler uses normal lookup rules and bypasses ADL completely.", "id": "day_1_sub_7_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_1_sub_7", "day": 1, "index": 7}, {"title": "UB vs Unspecified vs Implementation-Defined Behavior", "explanation": "Undefined Behavior (UB) means the standard imposes NO requirements — anything can happen (crash, wrong result, nasal demons, time travel). Examples: signed integer overflow, null pointer dereference, use-after-free, data races, out-of-bounds access. Compilers assume UB never happens and optimize accordingly.\n\nImplementation-defined behavior has a specific result that the compiler must document. Examples: `sizeof(int)`, right-shift of negative integers, char being signed or unsigned. Your code may depend on it, but it's portable only if you check.\n\nUnspecified behavior means the standard provides a set of valid options, and the compiler may choose any without documenting which. Examples: order of evaluation of function arguments, order of evaluation of operands (mostly fixed in C++17).", "examples": [{"title": "The Three Kinds", "code": "// Undefined Behavior — anything can happen\nint x = INT_MAX;\nx++;  // signed overflow is UB\n\nint* p = nullptr;\n// *p = 42;  // null dereference is UB\n\n// Implementation-Defined — compiler documents its choice\nstd::cout << sizeof(long);  // 4 on Windows, 8 on Linux x86_64\n\n// Unspecified — valid options, compiler doesn't have to say which\nint a = 1;\n// In C++14: f(a++, a++)  — order of argument evaluation is unspecified\n// In C++17: still unspecified for function arguments (but operands of operators are sequenced)", "explanation": "Understanding these distinctions is critical for writing portable, correct code.", "formatted_code": "// Undefined Behavior — anything can happen\nint x = INT_MAX;\nx++;  // signed overflow is UB\n\nint* p = nullptr;\n// *p = 42;  // null dereference is UB\n\n// Implementation-Defined — compiler documents its choice\nstd::cout << sizeof(long);  // 4 on Windows, 8 on Linux x86_64\n\n// Unspecified — valid options, compiler doesn't have to say which\nint a = 1;\n// In C++14: f(a++, a++)  — order of argument evaluation is unspecified\n// In C++17: still unspecified for function arguments (but operands of operators are sequenced)"}], "pitfalls": ["Assuming signed overflow wraps around (it's UB, not unspecified)", "Relying on evaluation order of function arguments", "Assuming int is always 32 bits (it's implementation-defined)", "Compilers optimize assuming UB never happens — this can remove 'safety' checks"], "tips_to_remember": ["If the standard says UB, your program has no meaning — any result is conforming", "Compilers exploit UB for optimization: 'this path has UB → this path is unreachable → optimize it away'", "Use sanitizers (UBSan, ASan) to catch UB at runtime", "When in doubt, check the standard or cppreference.com"], "questions": [{"question": "What is undefined behavior and why is it dangerous?", "answer": "UB means the C++ standard imposes zero requirements on program behavior. Compilers assume UB never occurs and may optimize code in unexpected ways based on that assumption. This means UB can cause bugs far from the actual problematic code."}, {"question": "Give 5 examples of undefined behavior.", "answer": "1) Signed integer overflow, 2) Dereferencing null/dangling pointers, 3) Accessing array out of bounds, 4) Data races (unsynchronized concurrent access), 5) Using an object after its lifetime ends (use-after-free). All have no defined behavior — any outcome is valid."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens if you bind a temporary object (prvalue) to a `const T&` reference?", "options": ["The temporary is destroyed at the end of the expression, leaving a dangling reference.", "The lifetime of the temporary is extended to match the lifetime of the reference.", "The compiler makes a permanent copy of the temporary on the heap.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ guarantees that binding a temporary object to a const lvalue reference extends the lifetime of that temporary to match the lifetime of the reference itself, preventing dangling reference bugs.", "id": "day_1_sub_8_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you try to modify a member variable in a `const` member function in `UB vs Unspecified vs Implementation-Defined Behavior`?", "options": ["It compiles fine, but triggers a segmentation fault at runtime.", "Compilation fails, unless the member variable is marked `mutable`.", "It compiles fine, but the changes are discarded when the function exits.", "It compiles fine only if the class is not allocated on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A `const` member function promises not to modify any observable state of the object. Modifying any member variable inside it is a compilation error, unless the member is declared with the `mutable` keyword.", "id": "day_1_sub_8_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Can you initialize a `constexpr` variable using the return value of a non-constexpr runtime function?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler will defer evaluation to runtime.", "Yes, if the function has no side effects.", "No, compilation will fail because a `constexpr` value must be known at compile time.", "Yes, but only if compiler optimization is turned on."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "`constexpr` variables must be initialized with constant expressions that can be evaluated at compile time. Initializing one with a runtime value results in a compilation error.", "id": "day_1_sub_8_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the consequence of calling a `consteval` function with non-constant (runtime) arguments?", "options": ["The function is executed at runtime instead.", "The compiler fails to compile the call.", "The compiler generates a runtime check that throws an exception if arguments are invalid.", "The return value is undefined."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`consteval` (introduced in C++20) specifies an immediate function. Immediate functions MUST be evaluated at compile time. Calling them with runtime values is a compilation error.", "id": "day_1_sub_8_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Can a `constinit` variable be modified after initialization during runtime?", "options": ["No, `constinit` variables are implicitly `const`.", "Yes, `constinit` only requires compile-time initialization, not runtime immutability.", "Yes, but only inside `consteval` functions.", "No, modifying it triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constinit` (C++20) ensures that a variable is initialized at compile time (preventing static initialization order issues), but it does not make the variable constant. To make it constant, you must explicitly combine it with `const`: `constinit const int x = 42;`.", "id": "day_1_sub_8_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Is it safe to use `static_cast` to cast a `Base*` to a `Derived*`?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler checks the actual runtime type and returns `nullptr` on failure.", "Yes, but it is compile-time checked only. If the pointer is not actually of the Derived type, dereferencing it is UB.", "No, it is a compilation error. You must use `dynamic_cast`.", "Yes, `static_cast` automatically handles polymorphic conversions safely."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`static_cast` performs compile-time conversions. It does not perform any runtime checks. Casting a base pointer to a derived pointer with `static_cast` is unsafe: if the object is not of the derived type, dereferencing the casted pointer is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_1_sub_8_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast` do if it fails to cast a reference type `Base&` to `Derived&`?", "options": ["It returns a null reference.", "It throws a `std::bad_cast` exception.", "It aborts the program immediately.", "It sets the reference to an empty object."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since there is no such thing as a null reference in C++, `dynamic_cast` cannot return null when casting reference types. Instead, it throws a `std::bad_cast` exception on failure.", "id": "day_1_sub_8_mcq_6"}, {"question": "If you cast an `int*` to a `float*` using `reinterpret_cast` and dereference it to read the value, what is the result?", "options": ["It returns the floating-point representation of the integer bits.", "It is a compiler error.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior due to the strict aliasing rule.", "It triggers a hardware trap."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "The strict aliasing rule states that you cannot access an object of one type through a pointer of a different type (with exceptions like `char*` or signed/unsigned variants). Violating this using `reinterpret_cast` triggers undefined behavior.", "id": "day_1_sub_8_mcq_7"}, {"question": "If both `void print(int)` and `template<typename T> void print(T)` match a call `print(42)` equally well, which one is selected by overload resolution?", "options": ["The template function, because it is more generic.", "The non-template function.", "The compiler reports an ambiguity error.", "The compiler selects one randomly."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Overload resolution rules state that if a non-template function and a template specialization match a call equally well, the non-template function is always preferred.", "id": "day_1_sub_8_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) occur if the function call is fully qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)`)?", "options": ["Yes, ADL searches namespaces associated with the type of `x`.", "No, ADL is only performed for unqualified function calls.", "Yes, but only if `x` is a user-defined type.", "No, qualified calls are handled entirely at link time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) only applies to unqualified names (e.g. `foo(x)`). If the call is qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)` or `MyLib::foo(x)`), the compiler uses normal lookup rules and bypasses ADL completely.", "id": "day_1_sub_8_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_1_sub_8", "day": 1, "index": 8}, {"title": "Preprocessor Macros vs Modern Replacements", "explanation": "The C preprocessor is a text substitution engine that runs before the compiler. Macros (`#define`) have many problems: no type safety, no scope, hard to debug, can have surprising expansions due to operator precedence. Modern C++ provides superior alternatives: templates (generic code), `constexpr` (compile-time constants and computation), `inline` functions (avoid function call overhead), and enum class (named constants).\n\nLegitimate remaining uses of macros: include guards, conditional compilation (`#ifdef`), platform-specific code, and a few corner cases like __LINE__/__FILE__ for logging. Avoid function-like macros entirely — use templates or `inline` functions instead.", "examples": [{"title": "Macro vs Modern Alternatives", "code": "// BAD: macro\n#define MAX(a, b) ((a) > (b) ? (a) : (b))  // double evaluation!\nint x = MAX(i++, j++);  // i or j incremented twice!\n\n// GOOD: constexpr function\nconstexpr auto max_val(auto a, auto b) { return a > b ? a : b; }\n\n// BAD: macro constant\n#define PI 3.14159\n\n// GOOD: constexpr variable\nconstexpr double Pi = 3.14159265358979;", "explanation": "Macros have no type safety and can double-evaluate arguments. constexpr/templates are always better.", "formatted_code": "// BAD: macro\n#define MAX(a, b) ((a) > (b) ? (a) : (b))  // double evaluation!\nint x = MAX(i++, j++);  // i or j incremented twice!\n\n// GOOD: constexpr function\nconstexpr auto max_val(auto a, auto b)\n{\n    return a > b ? a : b;\n}\n\n// BAD: macro constant\n#define PI 3.14159\n\n// GOOD: constexpr variable\nconstexpr double Pi = 3.14159265358979;"}], "pitfalls": ["Macro arguments evaluated multiple times (double evaluation bug)", "Macros don't respect scope — they pollute the global namespace", "Macros can't be debugged — the debugger sees expanded code", "Macro names can conflict with other identifiers (e.g., `#define max` clashes with `std::max`)"], "tips_to_remember": ["Use `constexpr` variables instead of `#define constants`", "Use `inline` functions or templates instead of function-like macros", "Use enum class for groups of related constants", "Only use macros for include guards, conditional compilation, and __LINE__/__FILE__"], "questions": [{"question": "Why are macros discouraged in modern C++?", "answer": "Macros are text substitutions with no type safety, no scope, no debugging support, and can cause bugs through double evaluation and name collisions. Every macro use case has a safer modern alternative: constexpr for constants, templates/inline for generic code, enum class for enumerations."}, {"question": "What macro uses are still legitimate in modern C++?", "answer": "Include guards (#ifndef/#pragma once), conditional compilation (#ifdef for platform-specific code), stringification (#) and token pasting (##) for code generation, and __LINE__/__FILE__/__func__ for logging/diagnostics. Everything else should use constexpr, templates, or inline."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the exact outcome of violating the One Definition Rule (ODR) for an entity across different translation units in C++ when studying `Preprocessor Macros vs Modern Replacements`?", "options": ["The compiler will fail to compile with a duplicate definition error.", "The linker will always fail to link with a multiple definition error.", "The program compiles and links successfully, but triggers Undefined Behavior at runtime.", "The program is guaranteed to run correctly if all optimizations are disabled."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "ODR violations across different translation units are undefined behavior (UB). Linkers are not required to diagnose them, and they often silently select one definition, leading to random runtime crashes or wrong results.", "id": "day_1_sub_9_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT a translation unit in C++?", "options": ["A single `.cpp` file after preprocessing (with headers expanded).", "An individual `.h` header file compiled by itself as a source.", "A source file with all its included headers, after macros have been replaced.", "A temporary preprocessed file (`.i`) generated by the compiler's preprocessor."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A header file by itself is not a translation unit. A translation unit is a source file (.cpp, .cc) with all its included headers expanded by the preprocessor.", "id": "day_1_sub_9_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What happens if a header with `#pragma once` is copied under a different name to another directory, both are included in a `.cpp` file, and we build `Preprocessor Macros vs Modern Replacements`?", "options": ["The compiler ignores the duplicate header because the contents are identical.", "Both headers are included and parsed, potentially causing redefinition errors.", "The linker throws a duplicate symbol error at link time.", "The preprocessor automatically merges the duplicate files."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`#pragma once` is file-path or file-inode identity based. If the file is copied to a different path with a different name, `#pragma once` cannot detect it as the same file, so it is included twice, potentially causing redefinition errors.", "id": "day_1_sub_9_mcq_2"}, {"question": "If you define a non-inline global variable `int x = 42;` in a header file, and that header is included by two `.cpp` files, what error occurs?", "options": ["A compilation error in both translation units.", "A linker error for multiple definitions of symbol `x`.", "No error, the variables are merged into a single static storage.", "A runtime access violation when `x` is modified."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Each `.cpp` file including the header defines `x` as a global variable with external linkage. When the linker combines the object files, it finds duplicate definitions for symbol `x` and fails to link.", "id": "day_1_sub_9_mcq_3"}, {"question": "In C++, what linkage type does a namespace-scope `static` variable or function have?", "options": ["External linkage — visible across all translation units.", "Internal linkage — local to the translation unit it is defined in.", "No linkage — only visible within the block scope.", "Dynamic linkage — resolved at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The `static` keyword at namespace scope gives the entity internal linkage, meaning its name is only visible and usable within the translation unit where it is defined.", "id": "day_1_sub_9_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Why are anonymous namespaces preferred over the `static` keyword for internal linkage in modern C++?", "options": ["Anonymous namespaces are faster at compile time.", "`static` cannot be applied to user-defined types (like classes or structs), but anonymous namespaces can.", "Anonymous namespaces give external linkage but keep the names hidden.", "`static` is deprecated in C++20."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "You cannot declare a `static class` or `static struct` at namespace scope to give it internal linkage in C++. Anonymous namespaces solve this by giving internal linkage to all enclosed names, including types, variables, and functions.", "id": "day_1_sub_9_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the expansion result of the macro `#define MULTIPLY(x, y) x * y` when invoked as `MULTIPLY(1 + 2, 3 + 4)`?", "options": ["21 (7 * 3)", "11 (1 + 2 * 3 + 4)", "9 (1 + 2 * 4)", "Compilation error due to invalid operator combination."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Macros perform raw textual substitution. `MULTIPLY(1 + 2, 3 + 4)` expands literally to `1 + 2 * 3 + 4`. Due to operator precedence, multiplication runs first (`2 * 3 = 6`), yielding `1 + 6 + 4 = 11`. Always parenthesize macro parameters: `((x) * (y))`.", "id": "day_1_sub_9_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is the static initialization order fiasco in C++?", "options": ["Static local variables are initialized in random order across threads.", "The order of initialization of static variables within the same translation unit is undefined.", "The order of initialization of non-local static variables in different translation units is undefined.", "Compiler optimization silently reorders global constructor calls."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Within a single translation unit, static variables are initialized in order of definition. However, between different translation units, the order of initialization of non-local static variables is undefined, which can cause one static variable to use another uninitialized one.", "id": "day_1_sub_9_mcq_7"}, {"question": "Which C++17 feature resolves the header redefinition problem for global constants without violating ODR?", "options": ["`inline` variables.", "`constexpr` functions.", "Anonymous namespaces.", "External template instantiation."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "C++17 introduced `inline` variables. If a variable is declared `inline` (e.g. `inline constexpr int MaxValue = 100;`), it can be defined in multiple translation units (such as via header inclusion) and the linker will merge all definitions into a single object.", "id": "day_1_sub_9_mcq_8"}, {"question": "How do C++20 modules affect macro visibility compared to `#include` headers?", "options": ["Modules export all macros defined inside them by default.", "Macros defined in modules are only visible if the module is imported using `import`.", "Modules do not export macros unless explicitly specified, preventing macro pollution.", "Modules completely disable macro substitution."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "One of the major benefits of C++20 modules is that they do not leak macros. A module file can define macros for internal use, but they will not be exported to the importing file, eliminating name pollution.", "id": "day_1_sub_9_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_1_sub_9", "day": 1, "index": 9}]}, {"day": 2, "topic": "Pointers & References", "estimated_hours": 5, "subtopics": [{"title": "Raw Pointers & Pointer Arithmetic", "explanation": "A pointer holds the memory address of an object. Pointer arithmetic moves in units of the pointed-to type's size: p+1 advances by `sizeof(*p)` bytes. This is fundamental to array traversal and low-level memory manipulation.\n\nPointer arithmetic is only defined within arrays (including one-past-the-end). Arithmetic on unrelated pointers is UB. Subtraction of two pointers to elements of the same array yields ptrdiff_t (the number of elements between them).\n\nModern C++ rarely needs raw pointer arithmetic — prefer iterators, spans, or algorithms. Raw pointers are still used for non-owning observation and interfacing with C APIs.", "examples": [{"title": "Pointer Arithmetic", "code": "int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};\nint* p = arr;        // points to arr[0]\nstd::cout << *p;     // 10\np += 2;              // now points to arr[2]\nstd::cout << *p;     // 30\nstd::cout << (p - arr);  // 2 (ptrdiff_t)\n\n// Iterating with pointers\nfor (int* it = std::begin(arr); it != std::end(arr); ++it)\n    std::cout << *it << ' ';", "explanation": "Pointer arithmetic works in units of the element type's size.", "formatted_code": "int arr[] =\n{\n    10, 20, 30, 40, 50\n};\nint* p = arr;  // points to arr[0]\nstd::cout << *p;  // 10\np += 2;  // now points to arr[2]\nstd::cout << *p;  // 30\nstd::cout << (p - arr);  // 2 (ptrdiff_t)\n\n// Iterating with pointers\nfor (int* it = std::begin(arr); it != std::end(arr); ++it)\nstd::cout << *it << ' ';"}, {"title": "Array Access is Pointer Arithmetic", "code": "int arr[] = {1, 2, 3};\n// arr[i] is equivalent to *(arr + i)\n// which is equivalent to *(i + arr)\n// which means i[arr] is valid C++ (but don't do this!)\nstd::cout << 1[arr];  // prints 2 — please never write this", "explanation": "arr[i] is syntactic sugar for *(arr+i), which is why i[arr] works (but is terrible style).", "formatted_code": "int arr[] =\n{\n    1, 2, 3\n};\n// arr[i] is equivalent to *(arr + i)\n// which is equivalent to *(i + arr)\n// which means i[arr] is valid C++ (but don't do this!)\nstd::cout << 1[arr];  // prints 2 — please never write this"}], "pitfalls": ["Pointer arithmetic past array bounds (other than one-past-end) is UB", "Dereferencing one-past-the-end pointer is UB (you can form it, not dereference it)", "Subtracting pointers to different arrays is UB", "Forgetting that p++ advances by `sizeof(*p)` bytes, not 1 byte"], "tips_to_remember": ["Pointer arithmetic is only valid within the same array", "arr[i] is `exactly *`(arr + i) — understand this equivalence", "Use `std::span` or iterators instead of raw pointer arithmetic in modern code", "`nullptr` dereference is UB — always check before dereferencing"], "questions": [{"question": "What is pointer arithmetic and how does it work?", "answer": "Pointer arithmetic moves a pointer by multiples of the pointed-to type's size. p+n advances p by n*sizeof(*p) bytes. It's only defined for pointers within the same array (or one past the end). Subtraction of two pointers in the same array gives the element count between them."}, {"question": "Is pointer arithmetic on unrelated pointers defined?", "answer": "No. Comparing or subtracting pointers that don't point into the same array (or one past its end) is undefined behavior. The result is meaningless."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you pass an array `int arr[10]` to a function declared as `void process(int arr[])`?", "options": ["The array is passed by value, making a copy of all 10 integers.", "It decays to a raw pointer `int*`, and its size information is lost.", "The compiler verifies the size is exactly 10 and throws a warning if not.", "It is passed as a safe reference to the array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Arrays passed to functions decay to pointers. Inside `process`, `arr` is a raw pointer (`int*`), and `sizeof(arr)` returns the pointer size (e.g. 8 bytes), not the array size.", "id": "day_2_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is a compiler error for `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["Moving it using `std::move`.", "Attempting to copy it via copy assignment or copy constructor.", "Resetting it using `.reset()`.", "Releasing ownership with `.release()`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unique_ptr` represents exclusive ownership. To prevent duplicate owners, copying is disabled (deleted copy constructor/assignment), causing a compilation error.", "id": "day_2_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the outcome of a circular dependency where two objects contain `std::shared_ptr` pointing to each other?", "options": ["The compiler reports a cyclic template dependency error.", "The reference counts will never drop to zero, leaking both objects.", "The destructors are called normally due to automatic cycle detection.", "The program crashes with a stack overflow on deletion."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because each object holds a `std::shared_ptr` to the other, their reference counts remain at least 1, even after all external pointers are destroyed, leaking the memory.", "id": "day_2_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How do you access the managed object of a `std::weak_ptr`?", "options": ["Dereference it directly using `*` or `->`.", "You must call `.lock()` to obtain a `std::shared_ptr` first.", "Call `.get()` to retrieve the raw pointer.", "It is not possible to access the object once it is in a weak pointer."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::weak_ptr` does not provide direct access. You must call `.lock()` which returns a `std::shared_ptr`. If the managed object has already been destroyed, the returned shared pointer will be empty.", "id": "day_2_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of using `std::make_shared` instead of `std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)`?", "options": ["It makes the shared pointer thread-safe.", "It performs a single memory allocation for both the control block and the object, improving performance and cache locality.", "It allows using custom delete functions.", "It prevents compile-time template instantiation overhead."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)` allocates the object first, then allocates the control block. `std::make_shared` allocates a single block of memory big enough to hold both, reducing heap allocation overhead.", "id": "day_2_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "How does a custom deleter function pointer affect the size of a `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; it remains 8 bytes.", "It increases the size of the pointer (typically to 16 bytes) because it must store the function pointer.", "It causes the unique_ptr to allocate memory on the heap.", "It is a compile-time construct and does not exist at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a custom deleter is a function pointer, the unique_ptr must store it, increasing its size. Using a stateless functor (like a lambda with no captures) avoids this size overhead.", "id": "day_2_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of placement `new` in C++?", "options": ["It automatically finds the best place in the heap to allocate memory.", "It constructs an object on a pre-allocated raw memory buffer without allocating new heap memory.", "It moves an object to another thread's stack space.", "It optimizes memory alignment dynamically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Placement `new` (e.g. `new (buffer) MyClass()`) constructs an object at a specific, pre-allocated memory address, avoiding heap allocation costs.", "id": "day_2_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How must you destroy an object constructed via placement `new`?", "options": ["Call `delete ptr;`.", "Explicitly call the object's destructor (`ptr->~T()`) and then manually manage the buffer's memory.", "Let it go out of scope; the stack automatically deletes it.", "Call `free(ptr);`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because placement `new` did not allocate memory from the global heap, calling `delete` is undefined behavior. You must explicitly invoke the destructor and then clean up the raw buffer.", "id": "day_2_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the undefined behavior hazard in the code `int* p = new int[10]; delete p;`?", "options": ["Using the wrong delete operator (`delete` instead of `delete[]`) is Undefined Behavior.", "There is no hazard; `delete` automatically knows it is an array.", "It is a compiler error and won't compile.", "It causes a stack overflow."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Memory allocated with `new[]` must be freed with `delete[]`. Using plain `delete` on an array pointer is undefined behavior, often causing heap corruption or failing to call destructors for elements 1 to 9.", "id": "day_2_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the risk of returning a local variable by reference `const int&` from a function?", "options": ["It copies the variable unnecessarily.", "The local variable is destroyed at function exit, leaving a dangling reference, and accessing it is UB.", "It compiles only if the variable is marked `static`.", "It causes a memory leak on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Local variables have automatic storage duration (stack-allocated) and are destroyed when the function returns. Accessing a reference to a destroyed local variable is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_2_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_2_sub_0", "day": 2, "index": 0}, {"title": "References vs Pointers — Binding Rules", "explanation": "A reference is an alias for an existing object. Unlike pointers, references must be initialized at declaration, cannot be rebound to a different object, and cannot be null. Syntactically, you use a reference like the original object (`no *` dereference needed).\n\nThere are three kinds of references: lvalue references (`T&`), const lvalue references (`const T&`), and rvalue references (`T&&`, C++11). A const lvalue reference can bind to temporaries and extends their lifetime (until the reference goes out of scope). An rvalue reference can also bind to temporaries.\n\nPrefer references over pointers when: the target is guaranteed to exist (non-null), you don't need to reseat, and you want cleaner syntax. Use pointers when: nullability is needed, reseating is needed, or you need pointer arithmetic.", "examples": [{"title": "References vs Pointers", "code": "int x = 42;\nint& ref = x;       // lvalue reference — must init, can't rebind\nref = 100;          // modifies x\n\nint* ptr = &x;      // pointer — can be null, can rebind\nptr = nullptr;      // OK\n// int& bad;        // ERROR: reference must be initialized\n\nconst int& cref = 42;  // const ref extends temporary lifetime\n// The temporary '42' lives as long as cref\n\nvoid foo(const std::string& s);  // non-owning, no copy\nvoid bar(std::string* s);        // might be null", "explanation": "References are safer aliases — no null, no rebinding, no dereferencing syntax.", "formatted_code": "int x = 42;\nint& ref = x;  // lvalue reference — must init, can't rebind\nref = 100;  // modifies x\n\nint* ptr = &x;  // pointer — can be null, can rebind\nptr = nullptr;  // OK\n// int& bad;        // ERROR: reference must be initialized\n\nconst int& cref = 42;  // const ref extends temporary lifetime\n// The temporary '42' lives as long as cref\n\nvoid foo(const std::string& s);  // non-owning, no copy\nvoid bar(std::string* s);  // might be null"}], "pitfalls": ["Returning a reference to a local variable — creates a dangling reference (UB)", "const reference lifetime extension doesn't work through function returns", "References can't be stored in containers (use `std::reference_wrapper`)", "A reference to a temporary only extends lifetime for const lvalue and rvalue references"], "tips_to_remember": ["References can't be null — use when the target must exist", "Prefer `const T&` for function parameters to avoid copies", "Use `T&&` for move semantics and perfect forwarding", "A reference is NOT a pointer under the hood (conceptually) — though compilers may implement it as one"], "questions": [{"question": "What are the key differences between references and pointers?", "answer": "References: must be initialized, cannot be null, cannot be rebound, no dereference syntax needed. Pointers: can be null, can be reseated, require * to dereference, support arithmetic. References are generally safer and preferred when possible."}, {"question": "Can a reference be null?", "answer": "No, by specification. A reference must always refer to a valid object. Creating a reference to null (e.g., int& r = *static_cast<int*>(nullptr)) is undefined behavior at the point of creation, even before you use it."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you pass an array `int arr[10]` to a function declared as `void process(int arr[])`?", "options": ["The array is passed by value, making a copy of all 10 integers.", "It decays to a raw pointer `int*`, and its size information is lost.", "The compiler verifies the size is exactly 10 and throws a warning if not.", "It is passed as a safe reference to the array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Arrays passed to functions decay to pointers. Inside `process`, `arr` is a raw pointer (`int*`), and `sizeof(arr)` returns the pointer size (e.g. 8 bytes), not the array size.", "id": "day_2_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is a compiler error for `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["Moving it using `std::move`.", "Attempting to copy it via copy assignment or copy constructor.", "Resetting it using `.reset()`.", "Releasing ownership with `.release()`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unique_ptr` represents exclusive ownership. To prevent duplicate owners, copying is disabled (deleted copy constructor/assignment), causing a compilation error.", "id": "day_2_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the outcome of a circular dependency where two objects contain `std::shared_ptr` pointing to each other?", "options": ["The compiler reports a cyclic template dependency error.", "The reference counts will never drop to zero, leaking both objects.", "The destructors are called normally due to automatic cycle detection.", "The program crashes with a stack overflow on deletion."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because each object holds a `std::shared_ptr` to the other, their reference counts remain at least 1, even after all external pointers are destroyed, leaking the memory.", "id": "day_2_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How do you access the managed object of a `std::weak_ptr`?", "options": ["Dereference it directly using `*` or `->`.", "You must call `.lock()` to obtain a `std::shared_ptr` first.", "Call `.get()` to retrieve the raw pointer.", "It is not possible to access the object once it is in a weak pointer."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::weak_ptr` does not provide direct access. You must call `.lock()` which returns a `std::shared_ptr`. If the managed object has already been destroyed, the returned shared pointer will be empty.", "id": "day_2_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of using `std::make_shared` instead of `std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)`?", "options": ["It makes the shared pointer thread-safe.", "It performs a single memory allocation for both the control block and the object, improving performance and cache locality.", "It allows using custom delete functions.", "It prevents compile-time template instantiation overhead."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)` allocates the object first, then allocates the control block. `std::make_shared` allocates a single block of memory big enough to hold both, reducing heap allocation overhead.", "id": "day_2_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "How does a custom deleter function pointer affect the size of a `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; it remains 8 bytes.", "It increases the size of the pointer (typically to 16 bytes) because it must store the function pointer.", "It causes the unique_ptr to allocate memory on the heap.", "It is a compile-time construct and does not exist at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a custom deleter is a function pointer, the unique_ptr must store it, increasing its size. Using a stateless functor (like a lambda with no captures) avoids this size overhead.", "id": "day_2_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of placement `new` in C++?", "options": ["It automatically finds the best place in the heap to allocate memory.", "It constructs an object on a pre-allocated raw memory buffer without allocating new heap memory.", "It moves an object to another thread's stack space.", "It optimizes memory alignment dynamically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Placement `new` (e.g. `new (buffer) MyClass()`) constructs an object at a specific, pre-allocated memory address, avoiding heap allocation costs.", "id": "day_2_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How must you destroy an object constructed via placement `new`?", "options": ["Call `delete ptr;`.", "Explicitly call the object's destructor (`ptr->~T()`) and then manually manage the buffer's memory.", "Let it go out of scope; the stack automatically deletes it.", "Call `free(ptr);`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because placement `new` did not allocate memory from the global heap, calling `delete` is undefined behavior. You must explicitly invoke the destructor and then clean up the raw buffer.", "id": "day_2_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the undefined behavior hazard in the code `int* p = new int[10]; delete p;`?", "options": ["Using the wrong delete operator (`delete` instead of `delete[]`) is Undefined Behavior.", "There is no hazard; `delete` automatically knows it is an array.", "It is a compiler error and won't compile.", "It causes a stack overflow."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Memory allocated with `new[]` must be freed with `delete[]`. Using plain `delete` on an array pointer is undefined behavior, often causing heap corruption or failing to call destructors for elements 1 to 9.", "id": "day_2_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the risk of returning a local variable by reference `const int&` from a function?", "options": ["It copies the variable unnecessarily.", "The local variable is destroyed at function exit, leaving a dangling reference, and accessing it is UB.", "It compiles only if the variable is marked `static`.", "It causes a memory leak on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Local variables have automatic storage duration (stack-allocated) and are destroyed when the function returns. Accessing a reference to a destroyed local variable is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_2_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_2_sub_1", "day": 2, "index": 1}, {"title": "Function Pointers & Pointer-to-Member", "explanation": "A function pointer stores the address of a function. The syntax is: ReturnType (*name)(ParamTypes). You can call through it like a regular function. C++ also has pointer-to-member-function and pointer-to-data-member, which are fundamentally different — they require an object to invoke (using .* or ->*).\n\nPointer-to-member-function has type: ReturnType (ClassName::*)(ParamTypes). It cannot be implicitly converted to a regular function pointer because it needs a 'this' pointer. Member function pointers may be larger than regular pointers (to handle `virtual` functions and multiple inheritance).\n\nIn modern C++, prefer lambdas, `std::function`, or templates over raw function pointers. They're more flexible and can capture state.", "examples": [{"title": "Function Pointers", "code": "int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nint sub(int a, int b) { return a - b; }\n\nint (*op)(int, int) = add;  // function pointer\nstd::cout << op(3, 2);      // 5\nop = sub;\nstd::cout << op(3, 2);      // 1\n\n// Using typedef or using for readability\nusing BinaryOp = int(*)(int, int);\nBinaryOp operation = add;", "explanation": "Function pointers let you select functions at runtime — the basis for callbacks.", "formatted_code": "int add(int a, int b)\n{\n    return a + b;\n}\nint sub(int a, int b)\n{\n    return a - b;\n}\n\nint (*op)(int, int) = add;  // function pointer\nstd::cout << op(3, 2);  // 5\nop = sub;\nstd::cout << op(3, 2);  // 1\n\n// Using typedef or using for readability\nusing BinaryOp = int(*)(int, int);\nBinaryOp operation = add;"}, {"title": "Pointer-to-Member", "code": "struct Widget\n{\n    int value = 42;\n    void print() { std::cout << value << '\\n'; }\n};\n\n// Pointer to data member\nint Widget::*pm = &Widget::value;\nWidget w;\nstd::cout << w.*pm;  // 42\n\n// Pointer to member function\nvoid (Widget::*pmf)() = &Widget::print;\n(w.*pmf)();  // calls w.print()\n\nWidget* pw = &w;\n(pw->*pmf)();  // calls through pointer", "explanation": "Member pointers need an object instance — accessed with .* or ->*.", "formatted_code": "struct Widget\n{\n    int value = 42;\n    void print()\n    {\n        std::cout << value << '\\n';\n    }\n};\n\n// Pointer to data member\nint Widget::*pm = &Widget::value;\nWidget w;\nstd::cout << w.*pm;  // 42\n\n// Pointer to member function\nvoid (Widget::*pmf)() = &Widget::print;\n(w.*pmf)();  // calls w.print()\n\nWidget* pw = &w;\n(pw->*pmf)();  // calls through pointer"}], "pitfalls": ["Member function pointers are NOT regular function pointers — can't interchange them", "Member function pointers may be larger than `sizeof(void*)` due to `virtual` dispatch info", "Calling a `virtual` function through a pointer-to-member correctly dispatches virtually", "C-style function pointers can't capture state — use lambdas or `std::function` instead"], "tips_to_remember": ["Use using or typedef to make function pointer types readable", "Prefer lambdas and `std::function` over raw function pointers in modern C++", "Pointer-to-member `requires` .* or ->* syntax — can't call without an object", "`std::invoke` (C++17) uniformly calls functions, member functions, and member pointers"], "questions": [{"question": "How do member function pointers differ from regular function pointers?", "answer": "Member function pointers need an object to call through (using .* or ->*), have a different type signature (RetType (Class::*)(Params)), may be larger in size, and cannot be converted to regular function pointers."}, {"question": "What is std::invoke and why is it useful?", "answer": "std::invoke (C++17) provides a uniform way to call any callable: regular functions, function pointers, member function pointers, member data pointers, lambdas, and functors — all with the same syntax. It simplifies generic code."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you pass an array `int arr[10]` to a function declared as `void process(int arr[])`?", "options": ["The array is passed by value, making a copy of all 10 integers.", "It decays to a raw pointer `int*`, and its size information is lost.", "The compiler verifies the size is exactly 10 and throws a warning if not.", "It is passed as a safe reference to the array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Arrays passed to functions decay to pointers. Inside `process`, `arr` is a raw pointer (`int*`), and `sizeof(arr)` returns the pointer size (e.g. 8 bytes), not the array size.", "id": "day_2_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is a compiler error for `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["Moving it using `std::move`.", "Attempting to copy it via copy assignment or copy constructor.", "Resetting it using `.reset()`.", "Releasing ownership with `.release()`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unique_ptr` represents exclusive ownership. To prevent duplicate owners, copying is disabled (deleted copy constructor/assignment), causing a compilation error.", "id": "day_2_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the outcome of a circular dependency where two objects contain `std::shared_ptr` pointing to each other?", "options": ["The compiler reports a cyclic template dependency error.", "The reference counts will never drop to zero, leaking both objects.", "The destructors are called normally due to automatic cycle detection.", "The program crashes with a stack overflow on deletion."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because each object holds a `std::shared_ptr` to the other, their reference counts remain at least 1, even after all external pointers are destroyed, leaking the memory.", "id": "day_2_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How do you access the managed object of a `std::weak_ptr`?", "options": ["Dereference it directly using `*` or `->`.", "You must call `.lock()` to obtain a `std::shared_ptr` first.", "Call `.get()` to retrieve the raw pointer.", "It is not possible to access the object once it is in a weak pointer."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::weak_ptr` does not provide direct access. You must call `.lock()` which returns a `std::shared_ptr`. If the managed object has already been destroyed, the returned shared pointer will be empty.", "id": "day_2_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of using `std::make_shared` instead of `std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)`?", "options": ["It makes the shared pointer thread-safe.", "It performs a single memory allocation for both the control block and the object, improving performance and cache locality.", "It allows using custom delete functions.", "It prevents compile-time template instantiation overhead."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)` allocates the object first, then allocates the control block. `std::make_shared` allocates a single block of memory big enough to hold both, reducing heap allocation overhead.", "id": "day_2_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "How does a custom deleter function pointer affect the size of a `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; it remains 8 bytes.", "It increases the size of the pointer (typically to 16 bytes) because it must store the function pointer.", "It causes the unique_ptr to allocate memory on the heap.", "It is a compile-time construct and does not exist at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a custom deleter is a function pointer, the unique_ptr must store it, increasing its size. Using a stateless functor (like a lambda with no captures) avoids this size overhead.", "id": "day_2_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of placement `new` in C++?", "options": ["It automatically finds the best place in the heap to allocate memory.", "It constructs an object on a pre-allocated raw memory buffer without allocating new heap memory.", "It moves an object to another thread's stack space.", "It optimizes memory alignment dynamically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Placement `new` (e.g. `new (buffer) MyClass()`) constructs an object at a specific, pre-allocated memory address, avoiding heap allocation costs.", "id": "day_2_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How must you destroy an object constructed via placement `new`?", "options": ["Call `delete ptr;`.", "Explicitly call the object's destructor (`ptr->~T()`) and then manually manage the buffer's memory.", "Let it go out of scope; the stack automatically deletes it.", "Call `free(ptr);`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because placement `new` did not allocate memory from the global heap, calling `delete` is undefined behavior. You must explicitly invoke the destructor and then clean up the raw buffer.", "id": "day_2_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the undefined behavior hazard in the code `int* p = new int[10]; delete p;`?", "options": ["Using the wrong delete operator (`delete` instead of `delete[]`) is Undefined Behavior.", "There is no hazard; `delete` automatically knows it is an array.", "It is a compiler error and won't compile.", "It causes a stack overflow."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Memory allocated with `new[]` must be freed with `delete[]`. Using plain `delete` on an array pointer is undefined behavior, often causing heap corruption or failing to call destructors for elements 1 to 9.", "id": "day_2_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the risk of returning a local variable by reference `const int&` from a function?", "options": ["It copies the variable unnecessarily.", "The local variable is destroyed at function exit, leaving a dangling reference, and accessing it is UB.", "It compiles only if the variable is marked `static`.", "It causes a memory leak on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Local variables have automatic storage duration (stack-allocated) and are destroyed when the function returns. Accessing a reference to a destroyed local variable is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_2_sub_2_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_2_sub_2", "day": 2, "index": 2}, {"title": "void* and Type Punning, Strict Aliasing Rule", "explanation": "`void*` is a generic pointer that can hold any pointer type but cannot be dereferenced directly — you must cast it to a specific type first. It's common in C APIs (malloc returns `void*`) but rare in type-safe C++ code.\n\nType punning is reinterpreting the bytes of one type as another. The strict aliasing rule says you can only access an object through a pointer/reference of its actual type (or char/unsigned char/`std::byte`). Violating this is UB. This means casting a `float*` to `int*` and dereferencing is UB. Use memcpy or `std::bit_cast` (C++20) for safe type punning.\n\nThe strict aliasing rule enables compiler optimizations — if the compiler knows `int*` and `float*` can't alias the same memory, it can keep values in registers longer. Breaking this rule causes real bugs, especially with optimization.", "examples": [{"title": "Safe Type Punning", "code": "// WRONG: strict aliasing violation\nfloat f = 3.14f;\nint i = *reinterpret_cast<int*>(&f);  // UB!\n\n// CORRECT: use memcpy\nfloat f2 = 3.14f;\nint i2;\nstd::memcpy(&i2, &f2, sizeof(i2));  // well-defined\n\n// CORRECT (C++20): use bit_cast\n// auto i3 = std::bit_cast<int>(f2);", "explanation": "memcpy and bit_cast are the only safe ways to reinterpret bytes.", "formatted_code": "// WRONG: strict aliasing violation\nfloat f = 3.14f;\nint i = *reinterpret_cast<int*>(&f);  // UB!\n\n// CORRECT: use memcpy\nfloat f2 = 3.14f;\nint i2;\nstd::memcpy(&i2, &f2, sizeof(i2));  // well-defined\n\n// CORRECT (C++20): use bit_cast\n// auto i3 = std::bit_cast<int>(f2);"}], "pitfalls": ["Casting between unrelated pointer types and dereferencing violates strict aliasing", "Compilers assume strict aliasing for optimization — violations cause real bugs at -O2", "`char*` is the exception — you can always access any object through char/unsigned char", "`void*` from malloc must be cast before use in C++, and the cast doesn't create an object (use placement new)"], "tips_to_remember": ["Use memcpy or `std::bit_cast` (C++20) for type punning — never `reinterpret_cast` on the value", "char/unsigned char/`std::byte` can alias anything — they're the 'universal accessor'", "The compiler uses strict aliasing to optimize — breaking it causes bugs that appear at -O2 but not -O0", "Prefer `std::variant` or unions (with active member tracking) over type punning"], "questions": [{"question": "What is the strict aliasing rule?", "answer": "The strict aliasing rule says accessing an object through a pointer of a different type is UB (with exceptions for char/unsigned char/std::byte). It allows the compiler to assume that pointers of different types don't alias the same memory, enabling powerful optimizations."}, {"question": "How do you safely reinterpret the bytes of one type as another?", "answer": "Use std::memcpy to copy bytes between objects, or std::bit_cast<TargetType>(source) in C++20. Never cast pointers between unrelated types and dereference — that violates strict aliasing and is UB."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_2_sub_3_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_2_sub_3_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_2_sub_3_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_2_sub_3_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `void* and Type Punning, Strict Aliasing Rule`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_2_sub_3_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_2_sub_3_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_2_sub_3_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_2_sub_3_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_2_sub_3_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_2_sub_3_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_2_sub_3", "day": 2, "index": 3}, {"title": "nullptr vs NULL vs 0", "explanation": "`nullptr` (C++11) is a keyword of type `std::nullptr_t` that represents a null pointer. NULL is a macro typically defined as 0 or (`void*`)0 in C. The literal 0 can be implicitly converted to a null pointer of any type.\n\nThe problem with NULL/0: they're integers, which causes overload resolution issues. If you have `f(int)` and `f(int*)`, calling `f(NULL)` may call `f(int)` because NULL is 0. `nullptr` unambiguously means 'null pointer' and always selects the pointer overload.\n\nAlways use `nullptr` in modern C++ code.", "examples": [{"title": "nullptr vs NULL Overload Problem", "code": "void f(int x)    { std::cout << \"int\\n\"; }\nvoid f(int* p)   { std::cout << \"pointer\\n\"; }\n\nf(0);        // calls f(int)\nf(NULL);     // may call f(int) — NULL is typically 0!\nf(nullptr);  // always calls f(int*) — unambiguous", "explanation": "nullptr has type std::nullptr_t, which converts only to pointer types, not to int.", "formatted_code": "void f(int x)\n{\n    std::cout << \"int\\n\";\n}\nvoid f(int* p)\n{\n    std::cout << \"pointer\\n\";\n}\n\nf(0);  // calls f(int)\nf(NULL);  // may call f(int) — NULL is typically 0!\nf(nullptr);  // always calls f(int*) — unambiguous"}], "pitfalls": ["NULL may be 0 (integer), causing wrong overload selection", "Comparing `nullptr` with 0 using == works, but using NULL in `template` code can deduce int instead of pointer type", "Some old code uses (`void*`)0 for NULL in C, which doesn't implicitly convert in C++"], "tips_to_remember": ["Always use `nullptr`, never NULL or 0 for null pointers", "`nullptr` has its own type (`std::nullptr_t`) that converts to any pointer type but not to integral types", "`nullptr` makes intent clear and avoids overload ambiguity"], "questions": [{"question": "Why should you use nullptr instead of NULL?", "answer": "nullptr is type-safe (std::nullptr_t), converts only to pointer types, resolves overload ambiguity correctly, and works properly in template argument deduction. NULL is just 0 or (void*)0, which can be confused with an integer."}, {"question": "What type does nullptr have?", "answer": "nullptr has type std::nullptr_t, which is implicitly convertible to any pointer type or bool, but not to integral types. This prevents the integer/pointer ambiguity that NULL (which is typically 0) causes."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_2_sub_4_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_2_sub_4_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_2_sub_4_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_2_sub_4_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `nullptr vs NULL vs 0`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_2_sub_4_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_2_sub_4_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_2_sub_4_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_2_sub_4_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_2_sub_4_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_2_sub_4_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_2_sub_4", "day": 2, "index": 4}, {"title": "Dangling / Wild Pointers", "explanation": "A dangling pointer points to memory that has been freed or to a local variable that has gone out of scope. Dereferencing it is UB. A wild pointer is an uninitialized pointer — it contains a garbage address. Both are common sources of bugs.\n\nCommon causes of dangling pointers: returning a pointer/reference to a local variable, deleting an object and continuing to use the pointer, iterator invalidation (the underlying memory was reallocated). Modern C++ mitigates this with smart pointers, **RAII**, and value semantics.\n\nBest practice: set pointers to `nullptr` after delete (though smart pointers make this unnecessary). Use AddressSanitizer (ASan) to detect use-after-free at runtime.", "examples": [{"title": "Dangling Pointer Examples", "code": "// 1. Returning address of local variable\nint* bad()\n{\n    int local = 42;\n    return &local;  // DANGLING! local destroyed at scope exit\n}\n\n// 2. Use after delete\nint* p = new int(42);\ndelete p;\n// *p = 10;  // UB: use-after-free\n\n// 3. Iterator invalidation\nstd::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3};\nauto it = v.begin();\nv.push_back(4);  // may reallocate — it is now dangling!\n// *it = 10;  // UB if reallocation occurred", "explanation": "Three common ways to create dangling pointers — all are UB when dereferenced.", "formatted_code": "// 1. Returning address of local variable\nint* bad()\n{\n    int local = 42;\n    return &local;  // DANGLING! local destroyed at scope exit\n}\n\n// 2. Use after delete\nint* p = new int(42);\ndelete p;\n// *p = 10;  // UB: use-after-free\n\n// 3. Iterator invalidation\nstd::vector<int> v =\n{\n    1, 2, 3\n};\nauto it = v.begin();\nv.push_back(4);  // may reallocate — it is now dangling!\n// *it = 10;  // UB if reallocation occurred"}], "pitfalls": ["Returning references/pointers to local variables — the most common dangling bug", "Forgetting that `vector::push_back` can invalidate all iterators/pointers", "Deleting through one pointer while another still refers to the same memory", "Lambda capturing a local by reference outliving the local's scope"], "tips_to_remember": ["Use smart pointers (unique_ptr, shared_ptr) to prevent most dangling pointer issues", "Set deleted pointers to `nullptr`: delete p; p = `nullptr`;", "Know your container's iterator invalidation rules (cppreference has tables)", "Use ASan to detect use-after-free at runtime"], "questions": [{"question": "What is a dangling pointer?", "answer": "A dangling pointer is a pointer that refers to memory that has been freed or to an object that no longer exists (e.g., a local variable after its function returned). Dereferencing it is undefined behavior."}, {"question": "How do smart pointers prevent dangling pointers?", "answer": "unique_ptr ensures exactly one owner — when the owner is destroyed, the memory is freed and no other pointer can dangle. shared_ptr uses reference counting — the memory is only freed when the last shared_ptr to it is destroyed. weak_ptr detects when the object is gone (expired())."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you pass an array `int arr[10]` to a function declared as `void process(int arr[])`?", "options": ["The array is passed by value, making a copy of all 10 integers.", "It decays to a raw pointer `int*`, and its size information is lost.", "The compiler verifies the size is exactly 10 and throws a warning if not.", "It is passed as a safe reference to the array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Arrays passed to functions decay to pointers. Inside `process`, `arr` is a raw pointer (`int*`), and `sizeof(arr)` returns the pointer size (e.g. 8 bytes), not the array size.", "id": "day_2_sub_5_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is a compiler error for `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["Moving it using `std::move`.", "Attempting to copy it via copy assignment or copy constructor.", "Resetting it using `.reset()`.", "Releasing ownership with `.release()`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unique_ptr` represents exclusive ownership. To prevent duplicate owners, copying is disabled (deleted copy constructor/assignment), causing a compilation error.", "id": "day_2_sub_5_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the outcome of a circular dependency where two objects contain `std::shared_ptr` pointing to each other?", "options": ["The compiler reports a cyclic template dependency error.", "The reference counts will never drop to zero, leaking both objects.", "The destructors are called normally due to automatic cycle detection.", "The program crashes with a stack overflow on deletion."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because each object holds a `std::shared_ptr` to the other, their reference counts remain at least 1, even after all external pointers are destroyed, leaking the memory.", "id": "day_2_sub_5_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How do you access the managed object of a `std::weak_ptr`?", "options": ["Dereference it directly using `*` or `->`.", "You must call `.lock()` to obtain a `std::shared_ptr` first.", "Call `.get()` to retrieve the raw pointer.", "It is not possible to access the object once it is in a weak pointer."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::weak_ptr` does not provide direct access. You must call `.lock()` which returns a `std::shared_ptr`. If the managed object has already been destroyed, the returned shared pointer will be empty.", "id": "day_2_sub_5_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of using `std::make_shared` instead of `std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)`?", "options": ["It makes the shared pointer thread-safe.", "It performs a single memory allocation for both the control block and the object, improving performance and cache locality.", "It allows using custom delete functions.", "It prevents compile-time template instantiation overhead."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)` allocates the object first, then allocates the control block. `std::make_shared` allocates a single block of memory big enough to hold both, reducing heap allocation overhead.", "id": "day_2_sub_5_mcq_4"}, {"question": "How does a custom deleter function pointer affect the size of a `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; it remains 8 bytes.", "It increases the size of the pointer (typically to 16 bytes) because it must store the function pointer.", "It causes the unique_ptr to allocate memory on the heap.", "It is a compile-time construct and does not exist at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a custom deleter is a function pointer, the unique_ptr must store it, increasing its size. Using a stateless functor (like a lambda with no captures) avoids this size overhead.", "id": "day_2_sub_5_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of placement `new` in C++?", "options": ["It automatically finds the best place in the heap to allocate memory.", "It constructs an object on a pre-allocated raw memory buffer without allocating new heap memory.", "It moves an object to another thread's stack space.", "It optimizes memory alignment dynamically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Placement `new` (e.g. `new (buffer) MyClass()`) constructs an object at a specific, pre-allocated memory address, avoiding heap allocation costs.", "id": "day_2_sub_5_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How must you destroy an object constructed via placement `new`?", "options": ["Call `delete ptr;`.", "Explicitly call the object's destructor (`ptr->~T()`) and then manually manage the buffer's memory.", "Let it go out of scope; the stack automatically deletes it.", "Call `free(ptr);`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because placement `new` did not allocate memory from the global heap, calling `delete` is undefined behavior. You must explicitly invoke the destructor and then clean up the raw buffer.", "id": "day_2_sub_5_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the undefined behavior hazard in the code `int* p = new int[10]; delete p;`?", "options": ["Using the wrong delete operator (`delete` instead of `delete[]`) is Undefined Behavior.", "There is no hazard; `delete` automatically knows it is an array.", "It is a compiler error and won't compile.", "It causes a stack overflow."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Memory allocated with `new[]` must be freed with `delete[]`. Using plain `delete` on an array pointer is undefined behavior, often causing heap corruption or failing to call destructors for elements 1 to 9.", "id": "day_2_sub_5_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the risk of returning a local variable by reference `const int&` from a function?", "options": ["It copies the variable unnecessarily.", "The local variable is destroyed at function exit, leaving a dangling reference, and accessing it is UB.", "It compiles only if the variable is marked `static`.", "It causes a memory leak on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Local variables have automatic storage duration (stack-allocated) and are destroyed when the function returns. Accessing a reference to a destroyed local variable is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_2_sub_5_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_2_sub_5", "day": 2, "index": 5}, {"title": "Array-to-Pointer Decay", "explanation": "In most contexts, an array name 'decays' to a pointer to its first element. This is an implicit conversion inherited from C. It means you lose the array's size information when passing it to a function — the function just sees a pointer.\n\nExceptions where arrays DON'T decay: `sizeof(arr)` gives the total array size, `decltype(arr)` preserves the array type, binding to a reference to array (`T(&)`[N]), and addressof (&arr gives `T(*)`[N]).\n\nModern C++ solutions: use `std::array` (no decay, knows its size), `std::span` (C++20, non-owning view with size), or templates that deduce the size: `template<size_t N>` void f(int (&arr)[N]).", "examples": [{"title": "Decay in Action", "code": "void printSize(int arr[]) // arr is actually int* — the [] is misleading!\n{\n    std::cout << sizeof(arr);  // prints sizeof(int*), NOT array size!\n}\n\nint arr[10];\nstd::cout << sizeof(arr);  // 40 (10 * sizeof(int)) — no decay here\nprintSize(arr);             // decays to int*, prints 4 or 8\n\n// Prevent decay with reference to array:\ntemplate<size_t N>\nvoid safeSize(int (&arr)[N])\n{\n    std::cout << N;  // correctly deduces size\n}", "explanation": "Array parameters in functions are pointers in disguise — size info is lost.", "formatted_code": "void printSize(int arr[])  // arr is actually int* — the [] is misleading!\n{\n    std::cout << sizeof(arr);  // prints sizeof(int*), NOT array size!\n}\n\nint arr[10];\nstd::cout << sizeof(arr);  // 40 (10 * sizeof(int)) — no decay here\nprintSize(arr);  // decays to int*, prints 4 or 8\n\n// Prevent decay with reference to array:\ntemplate<size_t N>\nvoid safeSize(int (&arr)[N])\n{\n    std::cout << N;  // correctly deduces size\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["Assuming `sizeof(arr)` works inside a function that received the array by pointer", "Function parameter int arr[10] is actually `int*` — the 10 is ignored by the compiler", "Decay loses type information: int[5] and int[10] both decay to `int*`", "Can't return arrays from functions — use `std::array` instead"], "tips_to_remember": ["Array-to-pointer decay is implicit and loses size information", "Use `std::array<T,N> or std::span<T>` instead of C arrays to preserve size", "`template<size_t N>` void f(T (&arr)[N]) deduces array size without decay", "`sizeof` applied directly to an array name gives total bytes — one of the few non-decay contexts"], "questions": [{"question": "What is array-to-pointer decay?", "answer": "When an array is used in most expressions (including passing to functions), it implicitly converts to a pointer to its first element, losing its size information. This inherited C behavior is why functions receiving 'arrays' actually receive pointers."}, {"question": "How can you prevent array decay?", "answer": "Pass by reference to array: void f(int (&arr)[N]). Use template argument deduction: template<size_t N> void f(int (&arr)[N]). Or use std::array/std::span which are objects that don't decay."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you pass an array `int arr[10]` to a function declared as `void process(int arr[])`?", "options": ["The array is passed by value, making a copy of all 10 integers.", "It decays to a raw pointer `int*`, and its size information is lost.", "The compiler verifies the size is exactly 10 and throws a warning if not.", "It is passed as a safe reference to the array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Arrays passed to functions decay to pointers. Inside `process`, `arr` is a raw pointer (`int*`), and `sizeof(arr)` returns the pointer size (e.g. 8 bytes), not the array size.", "id": "day_2_sub_6_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is a compiler error for `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["Moving it using `std::move`.", "Attempting to copy it via copy assignment or copy constructor.", "Resetting it using `.reset()`.", "Releasing ownership with `.release()`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unique_ptr` represents exclusive ownership. To prevent duplicate owners, copying is disabled (deleted copy constructor/assignment), causing a compilation error.", "id": "day_2_sub_6_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the outcome of a circular dependency where two objects contain `std::shared_ptr` pointing to each other?", "options": ["The compiler reports a cyclic template dependency error.", "The reference counts will never drop to zero, leaking both objects.", "The destructors are called normally due to automatic cycle detection.", "The program crashes with a stack overflow on deletion."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because each object holds a `std::shared_ptr` to the other, their reference counts remain at least 1, even after all external pointers are destroyed, leaking the memory.", "id": "day_2_sub_6_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How do you access the managed object of a `std::weak_ptr`?", "options": ["Dereference it directly using `*` or `->`.", "You must call `.lock()` to obtain a `std::shared_ptr` first.", "Call `.get()` to retrieve the raw pointer.", "It is not possible to access the object once it is in a weak pointer."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::weak_ptr` does not provide direct access. You must call `.lock()` which returns a `std::shared_ptr`. If the managed object has already been destroyed, the returned shared pointer will be empty.", "id": "day_2_sub_6_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of using `std::make_shared` instead of `std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)`?", "options": ["It makes the shared pointer thread-safe.", "It performs a single memory allocation for both the control block and the object, improving performance and cache locality.", "It allows using custom delete functions.", "It prevents compile-time template instantiation overhead."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)` allocates the object first, then allocates the control block. `std::make_shared` allocates a single block of memory big enough to hold both, reducing heap allocation overhead.", "id": "day_2_sub_6_mcq_4"}, {"question": "How does a custom deleter function pointer affect the size of a `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; it remains 8 bytes.", "It increases the size of the pointer (typically to 16 bytes) because it must store the function pointer.", "It causes the unique_ptr to allocate memory on the heap.", "It is a compile-time construct and does not exist at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a custom deleter is a function pointer, the unique_ptr must store it, increasing its size. Using a stateless functor (like a lambda with no captures) avoids this size overhead.", "id": "day_2_sub_6_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of placement `new` in C++?", "options": ["It automatically finds the best place in the heap to allocate memory.", "It constructs an object on a pre-allocated raw memory buffer without allocating new heap memory.", "It moves an object to another thread's stack space.", "It optimizes memory alignment dynamically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Placement `new` (e.g. `new (buffer) MyClass()`) constructs an object at a specific, pre-allocated memory address, avoiding heap allocation costs.", "id": "day_2_sub_6_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How must you destroy an object constructed via placement `new`?", "options": ["Call `delete ptr;`.", "Explicitly call the object's destructor (`ptr->~T()`) and then manually manage the buffer's memory.", "Let it go out of scope; the stack automatically deletes it.", "Call `free(ptr);`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because placement `new` did not allocate memory from the global heap, calling `delete` is undefined behavior. You must explicitly invoke the destructor and then clean up the raw buffer.", "id": "day_2_sub_6_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the undefined behavior hazard in the code `int* p = new int[10]; delete p;`?", "options": ["Using the wrong delete operator (`delete` instead of `delete[]`) is Undefined Behavior.", "There is no hazard; `delete` automatically knows it is an array.", "It is a compiler error and won't compile.", "It causes a stack overflow."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Memory allocated with `new[]` must be freed with `delete[]`. Using plain `delete` on an array pointer is undefined behavior, often causing heap corruption or failing to call destructors for elements 1 to 9.", "id": "day_2_sub_6_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the risk of returning a local variable by reference `const int&` from a function?", "options": ["It copies the variable unnecessarily.", "The local variable is destroyed at function exit, leaving a dangling reference, and accessing it is UB.", "It compiles only if the variable is marked `static`.", "It causes a memory leak on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Local variables have automatic storage duration (stack-allocated) and are destroyed when the function returns. Accessing a reference to a destroyed local variable is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_2_sub_6_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_2_sub_6", "day": 2, "index": 6}]}, {"day": 3, "topic": "Memory Management", "estimated_hours": 6, "subtopics": [{"title": "Stack vs Heap — Allocation Cost, Lifetime, Size Limits", "explanation": "Stack allocation is essentially free — the compiler adjusts the stack pointer (a single instruction). Deallocation is automatic when the scope exits. Stack memory is limited (typically 1-8 MB on most systems) and is contiguous per thread.\n\nHeap allocation (new/malloc) is expensive — it involves the allocator searching for a free block, maintaining metadata, and potentially requesting memory from the OS. Heap is virtually unlimited (bounded by available `virtual` memory). Objects on the heap persist until explicitly freed.\n\nPrefer stack allocation for small, short-lived objects. Use heap for large objects, objects with dynamic size, or objects that must outlive their scope. Modern C++ uses **RAII** (smart pointers, containers) to automate heap management.", "examples": [{"title": "Stack vs Heap", "code": "#include <array>\n#include <vector>\n\nvoid demo()\n{\n    int local = 42;                    // stack: instant allocation\n    std::array<int, 100> arr;          // stack: 400 bytes, instant\n    // std::array<int, 10000000> huge; // stack overflow! Too large\n    \n    auto* p = new int(42);             // heap: allocator overhead\n    std::vector<int> v(10000000);      // heap (internally): safe for large sizes\n    delete p;\n}  // local, arr destroyed automatically. v's destructor frees heap.", "explanation": "Stack is fast but small. Heap is slower but large and flexible.", "formatted_code": "#include <array>\n#include <vector>\n\nvoid demo()\n{\n    int local = 42;  // stack: instant allocation\n    std::array<int, 100> arr;  // stack: 400 bytes, instant\n    // std::array<int, 10000000> huge; // stack overflow! Too large\n\n    auto* p = new int(42);  // heap: allocator overhead\n    std::vector<int> v(10000000);  // heap (internally): safe for large sizes\n    delete p;\n}  // local, arr destroyed automatically. v's destructor frees heap."}], "pitfalls": ["Stack overflow from large local arrays — use vector for large dynamic data", "Heap fragmentation over time can degrade allocation performance", "Forgetting to free heap memory → memory leak", "Assuming stack size is the same across platforms (it varies)"], "tips_to_remember": ["Stack: fast, automatic lifetime, small. Heap: slower, manual/**RAII** lifetime, large", "`std::vector`, `std::string` etc. use heap internally but manage it automatically via **RAII**", "For performance-critical paths, consider arena/pool allocators to reduce heap overhead", "Thread stacks are separate — each thread gets its own stack"], "questions": [{"question": "What is the difference between stack and heap allocation?", "answer": "Stack: O(1) allocation (stack pointer adjustment), automatic deallocation at scope exit, limited size (1-8MB typical), contiguous. Heap: more expensive allocation (allocator overhead), manual or RAII-managed lifetime, virtually unlimited, subject to fragmentation."}, {"question": "When should you allocate on the heap?", "answer": "When the object is too large for the stack, when its size is only known at runtime, when it must outlive the current scope, or when you need polymorphic behavior through base class pointers."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you pass an array `int arr[10]` to a function declared as `void process(int arr[])`?", "options": ["The array is passed by value, making a copy of all 10 integers.", "It decays to a raw pointer `int*`, and its size information is lost.", "The compiler verifies the size is exactly 10 and throws a warning if not.", "It is passed as a safe reference to the array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Arrays passed to functions decay to pointers. Inside `process`, `arr` is a raw pointer (`int*`), and `sizeof(arr)` returns the pointer size (e.g. 8 bytes), not the array size.", "id": "day_3_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is a compiler error for `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["Moving it using `std::move`.", "Attempting to copy it via copy assignment or copy constructor.", "Resetting it using `.reset()`.", "Releasing ownership with `.release()`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unique_ptr` represents exclusive ownership. To prevent duplicate owners, copying is disabled (deleted copy constructor/assignment), causing a compilation error.", "id": "day_3_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the outcome of a circular dependency where two objects contain `std::shared_ptr` pointing to each other?", "options": ["The compiler reports a cyclic template dependency error.", "The reference counts will never drop to zero, leaking both objects.", "The destructors are called normally due to automatic cycle detection.", "The program crashes with a stack overflow on deletion."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because each object holds a `std::shared_ptr` to the other, their reference counts remain at least 1, even after all external pointers are destroyed, leaking the memory.", "id": "day_3_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How do you access the managed object of a `std::weak_ptr`?", "options": ["Dereference it directly using `*` or `->`.", "You must call `.lock()` to obtain a `std::shared_ptr` first.", "Call `.get()` to retrieve the raw pointer.", "It is not possible to access the object once it is in a weak pointer."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::weak_ptr` does not provide direct access. You must call `.lock()` which returns a `std::shared_ptr`. If the managed object has already been destroyed, the returned shared pointer will be empty.", "id": "day_3_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of using `std::make_shared` instead of `std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)`?", "options": ["It makes the shared pointer thread-safe.", "It performs a single memory allocation for both the control block and the object, improving performance and cache locality.", "It allows using custom delete functions.", "It prevents compile-time template instantiation overhead."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)` allocates the object first, then allocates the control block. `std::make_shared` allocates a single block of memory big enough to hold both, reducing heap allocation overhead.", "id": "day_3_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "How does a custom deleter function pointer affect the size of a `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; it remains 8 bytes.", "It increases the size of the pointer (typically to 16 bytes) because it must store the function pointer.", "It causes the unique_ptr to allocate memory on the heap.", "It is a compile-time construct and does not exist at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a custom deleter is a function pointer, the unique_ptr must store it, increasing its size. Using a stateless functor (like a lambda with no captures) avoids this size overhead.", "id": "day_3_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of placement `new` in C++?", "options": ["It automatically finds the best place in the heap to allocate memory.", "It constructs an object on a pre-allocated raw memory buffer without allocating new heap memory.", "It moves an object to another thread's stack space.", "It optimizes memory alignment dynamically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Placement `new` (e.g. `new (buffer) MyClass()`) constructs an object at a specific, pre-allocated memory address, avoiding heap allocation costs.", "id": "day_3_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How must you destroy an object constructed via placement `new`?", "options": ["Call `delete ptr;`.", "Explicitly call the object's destructor (`ptr->~T()`) and then manually manage the buffer's memory.", "Let it go out of scope; the stack automatically deletes it.", "Call `free(ptr);`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because placement `new` did not allocate memory from the global heap, calling `delete` is undefined behavior. You must explicitly invoke the destructor and then clean up the raw buffer.", "id": "day_3_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the undefined behavior hazard in the code `int* p = new int[10]; delete p;`?", "options": ["Using the wrong delete operator (`delete` instead of `delete[]`) is Undefined Behavior.", "There is no hazard; `delete` automatically knows it is an array.", "It is a compiler error and won't compile.", "It causes a stack overflow."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Memory allocated with `new[]` must be freed with `delete[]`. Using plain `delete` on an array pointer is undefined behavior, often causing heap corruption or failing to call destructors for elements 1 to 9.", "id": "day_3_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the risk of returning a local variable by reference `const int&` from a function?", "options": ["It copies the variable unnecessarily.", "The local variable is destroyed at function exit, leaving a dangling reference, and accessing it is UB.", "It compiles only if the variable is marked `static`.", "It causes a memory leak on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Local variables have automatic storage duration (stack-allocated) and are destroyed when the function returns. Accessing a reference to a destroyed local variable is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_3_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_3_sub_0", "day": 3, "index": 0}, {"title": "malloc/free vs new/delete — Why Mixing is UB", "explanation": "malloc/calloc/realloc/free are C-style memory functions. They allocate raw bytes without calling constructors or destructors. new/delete are C++ operators that allocate memory AND call constructors/destructors. new[] and delete[] handle arrays.\n\nMixing the two families (e.g., malloc + delete, or new + free) is undefined behavior. They may use different internal heaps, different metadata structures, and delete calls the destructor while free doesn't.\n\nnew can be overloaded per-class. It throws `std::bad_alloc` on failure (not null). Use `new(`std::nothrow`)` to get `nullptr` on failure instead. In modern C++, avoid raw new/delete — use smart pointers and containers.", "examples": [{"title": "new vs malloc", "code": "// C-style\nint* p1 = (int*)malloc(sizeof(int));\n*p1 = 42;\nfree(p1);\n\n// C++ style\nint* p2 = new int(42);  // allocates + initializes\ndelete p2;               // destroys + deallocates\n\n// NEVER MIX:\n// int* bad = new int(42);\n// free(bad);  // UB! delete calls destructor, free doesn't\n\n// For classes, the difference matters even more:\nstruct Widget { Widget() { std::cout << \"ctor\\n\"; } ~Widget() { std::cout << \"dtor\\n\"; } };\nWidget* w = new Widget();  // calls constructor\ndelete w;                   // calls destructor then frees\n// malloc + free would skip both!", "explanation": "new/delete handle object lifetime (ctor/dtor). malloc/free handle raw memory only.", "formatted_code": "// C-style\nint* p1 = (int*)malloc(sizeof(int));\n*p1 = 42;\nfree(p1);\n\n// C++ style\nint* p2 = new int(42);  // allocates + initializes\ndelete p2;  // destroys + deallocates\n\n// NEVER MIX:\n// int* bad = new int(42);\n// free(bad);  // UB! delete calls destructor, free doesn't\n\n// For classes, the difference matters even more:\nstruct Widget\n{\n    Widget()\n    {\n        std::cout << \"ctor\\n\";\n    }\n    ~Widget()\n    {\n        std::cout << \"dtor\\n\";\n    }\n};\nWidget* w = new Widget();  // calls constructor\ndelete w;  // calls destructor then frees\n// malloc + free would skip both!"}], "pitfalls": ["Mixing new with free or malloc with delete — undefined behavior", "Using delete instead of delete[] for arrays — UB (mismatched deallocation)", "malloc doesn't call constructors — using the memory as a C++ object may be UB", "Forgetting that new throws on failure, while malloc returns NULL"], "tips_to_remember": ["new = allocate + construct. delete = destruct + deallocate. malloc/free only handle raw memory", "Never mix new/delete with malloc/free", "Always match new[] with delete[] and new with delete", "In modern C++, use make_unique/make_shared instead of raw new"], "questions": [{"question": "Why is mixing new and free undefined behavior?", "answer": "new and malloc may use different internal allocators with different metadata. delete calls the destructor, free doesn't. new[] records the array size differently than malloc. Mixing them corrupts allocator metadata, causing crashes, leaks, or memory corruption."}, {"question": "What's the difference between new and malloc?", "answer": "new: type-safe (returns typed pointer), calls constructor, throws std::bad_alloc on failure, can be overloaded. malloc: returns void* (needs cast), doesn't call constructors, returns NULL on failure, can't be overloaded."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens if you bind a temporary object (prvalue) to a `const T&` reference?", "options": ["The temporary is destroyed at the end of the expression, leaving a dangling reference.", "The lifetime of the temporary is extended to match the lifetime of the reference.", "The compiler makes a permanent copy of the temporary on the heap.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ guarantees that binding a temporary object to a const lvalue reference extends the lifetime of that temporary to match the lifetime of the reference itself, preventing dangling reference bugs.", "id": "day_3_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you try to modify a member variable in a `const` member function in `malloc/free vs new/delete — Why Mixing is UB`?", "options": ["It compiles fine, but triggers a segmentation fault at runtime.", "Compilation fails, unless the member variable is marked `mutable`.", "It compiles fine, but the changes are discarded when the function exits.", "It compiles fine only if the class is not allocated on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A `const` member function promises not to modify any observable state of the object. Modifying any member variable inside it is a compilation error, unless the member is declared with the `mutable` keyword.", "id": "day_3_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Can you initialize a `constexpr` variable using the return value of a non-constexpr runtime function?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler will defer evaluation to runtime.", "Yes, if the function has no side effects.", "No, compilation will fail because a `constexpr` value must be known at compile time.", "Yes, but only if compiler optimization is turned on."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "`constexpr` variables must be initialized with constant expressions that can be evaluated at compile time. Initializing one with a runtime value results in a compilation error.", "id": "day_3_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the consequence of calling a `consteval` function with non-constant (runtime) arguments?", "options": ["The function is executed at runtime instead.", "The compiler fails to compile the call.", "The compiler generates a runtime check that throws an exception if arguments are invalid.", "The return value is undefined."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`consteval` (introduced in C++20) specifies an immediate function. Immediate functions MUST be evaluated at compile time. Calling them with runtime values is a compilation error.", "id": "day_3_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Can a `constinit` variable be modified after initialization during runtime?", "options": ["No, `constinit` variables are implicitly `const`.", "Yes, `constinit` only requires compile-time initialization, not runtime immutability.", "Yes, but only inside `consteval` functions.", "No, modifying it triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constinit` (C++20) ensures that a variable is initialized at compile time (preventing static initialization order issues), but it does not make the variable constant. To make it constant, you must explicitly combine it with `const`: `constinit const int x = 42;`.", "id": "day_3_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Is it safe to use `static_cast` to cast a `Base*` to a `Derived*`?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler checks the actual runtime type and returns `nullptr` on failure.", "Yes, but it is compile-time checked only. If the pointer is not actually of the Derived type, dereferencing it is UB.", "No, it is a compilation error. You must use `dynamic_cast`.", "Yes, `static_cast` automatically handles polymorphic conversions safely."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`static_cast` performs compile-time conversions. It does not perform any runtime checks. Casting a base pointer to a derived pointer with `static_cast` is unsafe: if the object is not of the derived type, dereferencing the casted pointer is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_3_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast` do if it fails to cast a reference type `Base&` to `Derived&`?", "options": ["It returns a null reference.", "It throws a `std::bad_cast` exception.", "It aborts the program immediately.", "It sets the reference to an empty object."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since there is no such thing as a null reference in C++, `dynamic_cast` cannot return null when casting reference types. Instead, it throws a `std::bad_cast` exception on failure.", "id": "day_3_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "If you cast an `int*` to a `float*` using `reinterpret_cast` and dereference it to read the value, what is the result?", "options": ["It returns the floating-point representation of the integer bits.", "It is a compiler error.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior due to the strict aliasing rule.", "It triggers a hardware trap."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "The strict aliasing rule states that you cannot access an object of one type through a pointer of a different type (with exceptions like `char*` or signed/unsigned variants). Violating this using `reinterpret_cast` triggers undefined behavior.", "id": "day_3_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "If both `void print(int)` and `template<typename T> void print(T)` match a call `print(42)` equally well, which one is selected by overload resolution?", "options": ["The template function, because it is more generic.", "The non-template function.", "The compiler reports an ambiguity error.", "The compiler selects one randomly."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Overload resolution rules state that if a non-template function and a template specialization match a call equally well, the non-template function is always preferred.", "id": "day_3_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) occur if the function call is fully qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)`)?", "options": ["Yes, ADL searches namespaces associated with the type of `x`.", "No, ADL is only performed for unqualified function calls.", "Yes, but only if `x` is a user-defined type.", "No, qualified calls are handled entirely at link time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) only applies to unqualified names (e.g. `foo(x)`). If the call is qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)` or `MyLib::foo(x)`), the compiler uses normal lookup rules and bypasses ADL completely.", "id": "day_3_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_3_sub_1", "day": 3, "index": 1}, {"title": "Placement new and Manual Destructor Invocation", "explanation": "Placement new constructs an object at a specific memory address without allocating memory. Syntax: new (address) `Type(args)`. You must ensure the memory is properly aligned and large enough. This is used for custom allocators, memory pools, and containers like `std::vector`.\n\nWhen using placement new, you must manually call the destructor (~`Type()`) before reusing or freeing the memory. You must NOT use delete — delete would try to free the memory, which wasn't allocated by new. The pattern is: allocate memory → placement new → use → explicit destructor call → deallocate memory.\n\n`std::vector` uses this internally: it allocates raw memory, constructs elements via placement new, and calls destructors explicitly when erasing or shrinking.", "examples": [{"title": "Placement New", "code": "#include <new>  // required for placement new\n\nalignas(int) unsigned char buffer[sizeof(int)];\nint* p = new (buffer) int(42);  // construct int at buffer's address\nstd::cout << *p;  // 42\np->~int();  // not needed for int, but required for non-trivial types\n// Do NOT delete p — we didn't allocate with new!\n\n// For classes:\nstruct Widget\n{\n    std::string name;\n    Widget(std::string n) : name(std::move(n)) { std::cout << \"ctor\\n\"; }\n    ~Widget() { std::cout << \"dtor\\n\"; }\n};\n\nalignas(Widget) unsigned char wbuf[sizeof(Widget)];\nWidget* w = new (wbuf) Widget(\"test\");  // placement new\nstd::cout << w->name;\nw->~Widget();  // MUST call destructor manually", "explanation": "Placement new separates allocation from construction — essential for custom allocators.", "formatted_code": "#include <new>  // required for placement new\n\nalignas(int) unsigned char buffer[sizeof(int)];\nint* p = new (buffer) int(42);  // construct int at buffer's address\nstd::cout << *p;  // 42\np->~int();  // not needed for int, but required for non-trivial types\n// Do NOT delete p — we didn't allocate with new!\n\n// For classes:\nstruct Widget\n{\n    std::string name;\n    Widget(std::string n) : name(std::move(n))\n    {\n        std::cout << \"ctor\\n\";\n    }\n    ~Widget()\n    {\n        std::cout << \"dtor\\n\";\n    }\n};\n\nalignas(Widget) unsigned char wbuf[sizeof(Widget)];\nWidget* w = new (wbuf) Widget(\"test\");  // placement new\nstd::cout << w->name;\nw->~Widget();  // MUST call destructor manually"}], "pitfalls": ["Forgetting to call the destructor before reusing memory", "Using delete on a placement-new'd object (double-frees or frees stack memory!)", "Not ensuring proper alignment for the target type", "Constructing over an already-alive object without destroying it first"], "tips_to_remember": ["Placement new: construct at known address. Manual dtor: destroy without freeing memory", "Always call destructor explicitly before reusing placement-new'd memory", "Never delete a placement-new'd object — it wasn't allocated by new", "`std::vector` uses placement new internally for all element construction"], "questions": [{"question": "What is placement new?", "answer": "Placement new constructs an object at a specific memory address without allocating memory. Syntax: new (addr) T(args). Used for custom allocators, memory pools, and containers. You must manually call the destructor and NOT use delete."}, {"question": "When must you manually call a destructor?", "answer": "When you used placement new to construct an object. You must call obj->~Type() before deallocating or reusing the memory. For trivially destructible types it's technically optional, but it's good practice to always call it."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_3_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_3_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_3_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_3_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Placement new and Manual Destructor Invocation`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_3_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_3_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_3_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_3_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_3_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_3_sub_2_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_3_sub_2", "day": 3, "index": 2}, {"title": "Alignment — alignas, alignof, over-aligned allocation", "explanation": "Every type has an alignment requirement — the memory address must be a multiple of the alignment value. int typically `requires` 4-byte alignment, double `requires` 8-byte. Misaligned access can cause crashes (on some architectures) or performance penalties.\n\n`alignas(N)` specifies minimum alignment for a variable or type. `alignof(T)` returns the alignment requirement of type T. Over-aligned types (alignment > max_align_t, typically 16 bytes) need special allocation support — C++17's operator new supports over-aligned allocation.\n\nAlignment is critical for SIMD operations (SSE `requires` 16-byte, AVX `requires` 32-byte alignment) and for cache-line padding (to prevent false sharing).", "examples": [{"title": "alignas and alignof", "code": "#include <cstddef>\nstruct alignas(64) CacheAligned {  // 64-byte alignment (cache line)\n    int data[16];\n};\nstatic_assert(alignof(CacheAligned) == 64);\nstatic_assert(sizeof(CacheAligned) % 64 == 0);  // size is padded to multiple of alignment\n\n// Default alignment\nstd::cout << alignof(int) << '\\n';     // typically 4\nstd::cout << alignof(double) << '\\n';  // typically 8\nstd::cout << alignof(std::max_align_t) << '\\n';  // typically 16", "explanation": "alignas sets alignment, alignof queries it. Padding ensures sizeof is a multiple of alignment.", "formatted_code": "#include <cstddef>\nstruct alignas(64) CacheAligned\n{  // 64-byte alignment (cache line)\nint data[16];\n};\nstatic_assert(alignof(CacheAligned) == 64);\nstatic_assert(sizeof(CacheAligned) % 64 == 0);  // size is padded to multiple of alignment\n\n// Default alignment\nstd::cout << alignof(int) << '\\n';  // typically 4\nstd::cout << alignof(double) << '\\n';  // typically 8\nstd::cout << alignof(std::max_align_t) << '\\n';  // typically 16"}], "pitfalls": ["Casting to a less-aligned pointer type and dereferencing — UB on strict-alignment architectures", "Allocating over-aligned types with pre-C++17 new — alignment not guaranteed", "Forgetting that `alignas` also affects `sizeof` (padding is added)", "SIMD code crashing due to misaligned loads/stores"], "tips_to_remember": ["`alignof` returns natural alignment. `alignas` sets minimum alignment", "Over-aligned types (> `alignof(max_align_t)`) need C++17 or custom allocators", "Cache line padding (`alignas(64)`) prevents false sharing in multithreaded code", "SIMD: SSE needs 16-byte, AVX needs 32-byte alignment"], "questions": [{"question": "What is alignment and why does it matter?", "answer": "Alignment is the requirement that an object's memory address be a multiple of a specific value. Misaligned access can crash on strict architectures (ARM) or cause performance penalties (x86). Alignment matters for SIMD, cache line optimization, and hardware DMA."}, {"question": "What is an over-aligned type?", "answer": "A type whose alignment requirement exceeds alignof(std::max_align_t) (typically 16 bytes). Before C++17, the default operator new didn't guarantee alignment beyond max_align_t. C++17 added aligned new/delete to handle this."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you pass an array `int arr[10]` to a function declared as `void process(int arr[])`?", "options": ["The array is passed by value, making a copy of all 10 integers.", "It decays to a raw pointer `int*`, and its size information is lost.", "The compiler verifies the size is exactly 10 and throws a warning if not.", "It is passed as a safe reference to the array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Arrays passed to functions decay to pointers. Inside `process`, `arr` is a raw pointer (`int*`), and `sizeof(arr)` returns the pointer size (e.g. 8 bytes), not the array size.", "id": "day_3_sub_3_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is a compiler error for `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["Moving it using `std::move`.", "Attempting to copy it via copy assignment or copy constructor.", "Resetting it using `.reset()`.", "Releasing ownership with `.release()`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unique_ptr` represents exclusive ownership. To prevent duplicate owners, copying is disabled (deleted copy constructor/assignment), causing a compilation error.", "id": "day_3_sub_3_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the outcome of a circular dependency where two objects contain `std::shared_ptr` pointing to each other?", "options": ["The compiler reports a cyclic template dependency error.", "The reference counts will never drop to zero, leaking both objects.", "The destructors are called normally due to automatic cycle detection.", "The program crashes with a stack overflow on deletion."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because each object holds a `std::shared_ptr` to the other, their reference counts remain at least 1, even after all external pointers are destroyed, leaking the memory.", "id": "day_3_sub_3_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How do you access the managed object of a `std::weak_ptr`?", "options": ["Dereference it directly using `*` or `->`.", "You must call `.lock()` to obtain a `std::shared_ptr` first.", "Call `.get()` to retrieve the raw pointer.", "It is not possible to access the object once it is in a weak pointer."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::weak_ptr` does not provide direct access. You must call `.lock()` which returns a `std::shared_ptr`. If the managed object has already been destroyed, the returned shared pointer will be empty.", "id": "day_3_sub_3_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of using `std::make_shared` instead of `std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)`?", "options": ["It makes the shared pointer thread-safe.", "It performs a single memory allocation for both the control block and the object, improving performance and cache locality.", "It allows using custom delete functions.", "It prevents compile-time template instantiation overhead."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)` allocates the object first, then allocates the control block. `std::make_shared` allocates a single block of memory big enough to hold both, reducing heap allocation overhead.", "id": "day_3_sub_3_mcq_4"}, {"question": "How does a custom deleter function pointer affect the size of a `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; it remains 8 bytes.", "It increases the size of the pointer (typically to 16 bytes) because it must store the function pointer.", "It causes the unique_ptr to allocate memory on the heap.", "It is a compile-time construct and does not exist at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a custom deleter is a function pointer, the unique_ptr must store it, increasing its size. Using a stateless functor (like a lambda with no captures) avoids this size overhead.", "id": "day_3_sub_3_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of placement `new` in C++?", "options": ["It automatically finds the best place in the heap to allocate memory.", "It constructs an object on a pre-allocated raw memory buffer without allocating new heap memory.", "It moves an object to another thread's stack space.", "It optimizes memory alignment dynamically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Placement `new` (e.g. `new (buffer) MyClass()`) constructs an object at a specific, pre-allocated memory address, avoiding heap allocation costs.", "id": "day_3_sub_3_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How must you destroy an object constructed via placement `new`?", "options": ["Call `delete ptr;`.", "Explicitly call the object's destructor (`ptr->~T()`) and then manually manage the buffer's memory.", "Let it go out of scope; the stack automatically deletes it.", "Call `free(ptr);`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because placement `new` did not allocate memory from the global heap, calling `delete` is undefined behavior. You must explicitly invoke the destructor and then clean up the raw buffer.", "id": "day_3_sub_3_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the undefined behavior hazard in the code `int* p = new int[10]; delete p;`?", "options": ["Using the wrong delete operator (`delete` instead of `delete[]`) is Undefined Behavior.", "There is no hazard; `delete` automatically knows it is an array.", "It is a compiler error and won't compile.", "It causes a stack overflow."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Memory allocated with `new[]` must be freed with `delete[]`. Using plain `delete` on an array pointer is undefined behavior, often causing heap corruption or failing to call destructors for elements 1 to 9.", "id": "day_3_sub_3_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the risk of returning a local variable by reference `const int&` from a function?", "options": ["It copies the variable unnecessarily.", "The local variable is destroyed at function exit, leaving a dangling reference, and accessing it is UB.", "It compiles only if the variable is marked `static`.", "It causes a memory leak on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Local variables have automatic storage duration (stack-allocated) and are destroyed when the function returns. Accessing a reference to a destroyed local variable is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_3_sub_3_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_3_sub_3", "day": 3, "index": 3}, {"title": "Leaks, Double Free, Use-After-Free, Buffer Overflow", "explanation": "Memory leaks occur when heap-allocated memory is never freed. Double free is freeing the same memory twice — UB that corrupts the allocator's metadata. Use-after-free is accessing memory after it's been freed — UB. Buffer overflow is reading/writing past the bounds of an array — UB that's a major security vulnerability (stack smashing, code injection).\n\nThese are the most common and dangerous memory bugs in C++. Modern C++ defenses: use **RAII** (smart pointers, containers), use ASan/valgrind to detect issues, use `std::span` and range-for to avoid out-of-bounds access.", "examples": [{"title": "Common Memory Bugs", "code": "// Memory leak\nvoid leak()\n{\n    int* p = new int(42);\n    if (someCondition()) return;  // LEAK! p never deleted\n    delete p;\n}\n\n// Double free\nint* p = new int(42);\ndelete p;\n// delete p;  // DOUBLE FREE — UB, allocator corruption\n\n// Use-after-free\nint* q = new int(42);\ndelete q;\n// std::cout << *q;  // UB: use-after-free\n\n// Buffer overflow\nint arr[5];\n// arr[10] = 42;  // UB: out-of-bounds write\n\n// RAII prevents all of these:\nauto safe = std::make_unique<int>(42);\n// No leak, no double-free, no manual delete needed", "explanation": "RAII (smart pointers, containers) prevents these bugs by design.", "formatted_code": "// Memory leak\nvoid leak()\n{\n    int* p = new int(42);\n    if (someCondition()) return;  // LEAK! p never deleted\n    delete p;\n}\n\n// Double free\nint* p = new int(42);\ndelete p;\n// delete p;  // DOUBLE FREE — UB, allocator corruption\n\n// Use-after-free\nint* q = new int(42);\ndelete q;\n// std::cout << *q;  // UB: use-after-free\n\n// Buffer overflow\nint arr[5];\n// arr[10] = 42;  // UB: out-of-bounds write\n\n// RAII prevents all of these:\nauto safe = std::make_unique<int>(42);\n// No leak, no double-free, no manual delete needed"}], "pitfalls": ["Early returns or exceptions bypassing delete — use **RAII**", "Copying raw pointers creates shared ownership without reference counting — double-free risk", "Off-by-one errors in loop bounds causing buffer overflow", "String functions (strcpy, sprintf) not checking buffer sizes"], "tips_to_remember": ["**RAII** eliminates leaks and double-free: unique_ptr for exclusive ownership, shared_ptr for shared", "Use ASan (-fsanitize=address) to detect leaks, use-after-free, and buffer overflows", "Use valgrind (Linux) for memory leak detection", "Prefer `std::vector`/`std::string` over raw arrays — they manage memory and check bounds (with .`at()`)"], "questions": [{"question": "How does RAII prevent memory leaks?", "answer": "RAII ties resource lifetime to object scope. Smart pointers (unique_ptr, shared_ptr) automatically call delete in their destructors, which are guaranteed to run at scope exit — even when exceptions are thrown. You can't forget to free because it's automatic."}, {"question": "What is a double free and why is it dangerous?", "answer": "Double free means calling delete/free on the same memory address twice. It corrupts the allocator's internal data structures, which can cause crashes, silent memory corruption, or security vulnerabilities (exploitation of heap metadata corruption)."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens if you bind a temporary object (prvalue) to a `const T&` reference?", "options": ["The temporary is destroyed at the end of the expression, leaving a dangling reference.", "The lifetime of the temporary is extended to match the lifetime of the reference.", "The compiler makes a permanent copy of the temporary on the heap.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ guarantees that binding a temporary object to a const lvalue reference extends the lifetime of that temporary to match the lifetime of the reference itself, preventing dangling reference bugs.", "id": "day_3_sub_4_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you try to modify a member variable in a `const` member function in `Leaks, Double Free, Use-After-Free, Buffer Overflow`?", "options": ["It compiles fine, but triggers a segmentation fault at runtime.", "Compilation fails, unless the member variable is marked `mutable`.", "It compiles fine, but the changes are discarded when the function exits.", "It compiles fine only if the class is not allocated on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A `const` member function promises not to modify any observable state of the object. Modifying any member variable inside it is a compilation error, unless the member is declared with the `mutable` keyword.", "id": "day_3_sub_4_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Can you initialize a `constexpr` variable using the return value of a non-constexpr runtime function?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler will defer evaluation to runtime.", "Yes, if the function has no side effects.", "No, compilation will fail because a `constexpr` value must be known at compile time.", "Yes, but only if compiler optimization is turned on."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "`constexpr` variables must be initialized with constant expressions that can be evaluated at compile time. Initializing one with a runtime value results in a compilation error.", "id": "day_3_sub_4_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the consequence of calling a `consteval` function with non-constant (runtime) arguments?", "options": ["The function is executed at runtime instead.", "The compiler fails to compile the call.", "The compiler generates a runtime check that throws an exception if arguments are invalid.", "The return value is undefined."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`consteval` (introduced in C++20) specifies an immediate function. Immediate functions MUST be evaluated at compile time. Calling them with runtime values is a compilation error.", "id": "day_3_sub_4_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Can a `constinit` variable be modified after initialization during runtime?", "options": ["No, `constinit` variables are implicitly `const`.", "Yes, `constinit` only requires compile-time initialization, not runtime immutability.", "Yes, but only inside `consteval` functions.", "No, modifying it triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constinit` (C++20) ensures that a variable is initialized at compile time (preventing static initialization order issues), but it does not make the variable constant. To make it constant, you must explicitly combine it with `const`: `constinit const int x = 42;`.", "id": "day_3_sub_4_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Is it safe to use `static_cast` to cast a `Base*` to a `Derived*`?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler checks the actual runtime type and returns `nullptr` on failure.", "Yes, but it is compile-time checked only. If the pointer is not actually of the Derived type, dereferencing it is UB.", "No, it is a compilation error. You must use `dynamic_cast`.", "Yes, `static_cast` automatically handles polymorphic conversions safely."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`static_cast` performs compile-time conversions. It does not perform any runtime checks. Casting a base pointer to a derived pointer with `static_cast` is unsafe: if the object is not of the derived type, dereferencing the casted pointer is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_3_sub_4_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast` do if it fails to cast a reference type `Base&` to `Derived&`?", "options": ["It returns a null reference.", "It throws a `std::bad_cast` exception.", "It aborts the program immediately.", "It sets the reference to an empty object."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since there is no such thing as a null reference in C++, `dynamic_cast` cannot return null when casting reference types. Instead, it throws a `std::bad_cast` exception on failure.", "id": "day_3_sub_4_mcq_6"}, {"question": "If you cast an `int*` to a `float*` using `reinterpret_cast` and dereference it to read the value, what is the result?", "options": ["It returns the floating-point representation of the integer bits.", "It is a compiler error.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior due to the strict aliasing rule.", "It triggers a hardware trap."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "The strict aliasing rule states that you cannot access an object of one type through a pointer of a different type (with exceptions like `char*` or signed/unsigned variants). Violating this using `reinterpret_cast` triggers undefined behavior.", "id": "day_3_sub_4_mcq_7"}, {"question": "If both `void print(int)` and `template<typename T> void print(T)` match a call `print(42)` equally well, which one is selected by overload resolution?", "options": ["The template function, because it is more generic.", "The non-template function.", "The compiler reports an ambiguity error.", "The compiler selects one randomly."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Overload resolution rules state that if a non-template function and a template specialization match a call equally well, the non-template function is always preferred.", "id": "day_3_sub_4_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) occur if the function call is fully qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)`)?", "options": ["Yes, ADL searches namespaces associated with the type of `x`.", "No, ADL is only performed for unqualified function calls.", "Yes, but only if `x` is a user-defined type.", "No, qualified calls are handled entirely at link time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) only applies to unqualified names (e.g. `foo(x)`). If the call is qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)` or `MyLib::foo(x)`), the compiler uses normal lookup rules and bypasses ADL completely.", "id": "day_3_sub_4_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_3_sub_4", "day": 3, "index": 4}, {"title": "RAII — The Core C++ Resource-Management Idiom", "explanation": "**Resource Acquisition Is Initialization** (**RAII**) is the fundamental C++ idiom: acquire a resource in a constructor, release it in the destructor. Since destructors run automatically at scope exit (including exceptions), resources are always properly cleaned up.\n\n**RAII** applies to all resources: memory (smart pointers), files (`std::fstream`), mutexes (lock_guard), network connections, database handles. The key insight: resource lifetime is bound to object lifetime, and C++ guarantees destructors run in reverse order of construction.\n\n**RAII** makes code exception-safe by default and eliminates the need for cleanup sections, try/finally blocks, or manual resource tracking.\n\n### Unforgettable Analogy / Deep Dive\n**RAII** is like a rental car agreement: the moment you sign the keys (constructor), you own the car, and the moment you return the keys (destructor), you release it. The resource's lifetime is bound to the lifespan of the stack object. If the stack unwinds due to an exception or scope exit, C++ guarantees that the destructor is called automatically, preventing resource leaks.", "examples": [{"title": "RAII Pattern", "code": "// RAII for a mutex\n{\n    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);  // acquire\n    // ... critical section ...\n}  // lock released automatically, even if exception thrown\n\n// RAII for a file\n{\n    std::ofstream file(\"data.txt\");  // acquire (open)\n    file << \"hello\";\n}  // file closed automatically\n\n// RAII for heap memory\n{\n    auto ptr = std::make_unique<Widget>();  // acquire (allocate)\n    ptr->doWork();\n}  // memory freed automatically", "explanation": "RAII binds resource lifetime to scope — cleanup is automatic and exception-safe.", "formatted_code": "// RAII for a mutex\n{\n    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);  // acquire\n    // ... critical section ...\n}  // lock released automatically, even if exception thrown\n\n// RAII for a file\n{\n    std::ofstream file(\"data.txt\");  // acquire (open)\n    file << \"hello\";\n}  // file closed automatically\n\n// RAII for heap memory\n{\n    auto ptr = std::make_unique<Widget>();  // acquire (allocate)\n    ptr->doWork();\n}  // memory freed automatically"}], "pitfalls": ["Not using **RAII** for custom resources (manual acquire/release patterns)", "Holding a raw pointer when **RAII** wrapper exists (owning raw pointer to heap memory)", "Storing **RAII** objects in containers that may copy instead of move", "Relying on destructors for time-critical cleanup (dtor timing depends on scope exit)"], "tips_to_remember": ["**RAII** = acquire in ctor, release in dtor. Scope = lifetime.", "Every resource should be owned by an **RAII** object: smart pointers, lock_guard, fstream, etc.", "**RAII** makes code exception-safe for free", "If you write delete, free, close, or unlock manually — you're not using **RAII**"], "questions": [{"question": "What is RAII and why is it important?", "answer": "RAII binds resource lifetime to object lifetime. Resources are acquired in constructors and released in destructors. Since C++ guarantees destructors run at scope exit (even during stack unwinding from exceptions), resources are always properly cleaned up. It's the foundation of resource management in C++."}, {"question": "Name three standard library types that use RAII.", "answer": "1) std::unique_ptr/std::shared_ptr (memory), 2) std::lock_guard/std::unique_lock (mutexes), 3) std::fstream (files). All acquire in constructor and release in destructor."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you pass an array `int arr[10]` to a function declared as `void process(int arr[])`?", "options": ["The array is passed by value, making a copy of all 10 integers.", "It decays to a raw pointer `int*`, and its size information is lost.", "The compiler verifies the size is exactly 10 and throws a warning if not.", "It is passed as a safe reference to the array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Arrays passed to functions decay to pointers. Inside `process`, `arr` is a raw pointer (`int*`), and `sizeof(arr)` returns the pointer size (e.g. 8 bytes), not the array size.", "id": "day_3_sub_5_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is a compiler error for `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["Moving it using `std::move`.", "Attempting to copy it via copy assignment or copy constructor.", "Resetting it using `.reset()`.", "Releasing ownership with `.release()`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unique_ptr` represents exclusive ownership. To prevent duplicate owners, copying is disabled (deleted copy constructor/assignment), causing a compilation error.", "id": "day_3_sub_5_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the outcome of a circular dependency where two objects contain `std::shared_ptr` pointing to each other?", "options": ["The compiler reports a cyclic template dependency error.", "The reference counts will never drop to zero, leaking both objects.", "The destructors are called normally due to automatic cycle detection.", "The program crashes with a stack overflow on deletion."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because each object holds a `std::shared_ptr` to the other, their reference counts remain at least 1, even after all external pointers are destroyed, leaking the memory.", "id": "day_3_sub_5_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How do you access the managed object of a `std::weak_ptr`?", "options": ["Dereference it directly using `*` or `->`.", "You must call `.lock()` to obtain a `std::shared_ptr` first.", "Call `.get()` to retrieve the raw pointer.", "It is not possible to access the object once it is in a weak pointer."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::weak_ptr` does not provide direct access. You must call `.lock()` which returns a `std::shared_ptr`. If the managed object has already been destroyed, the returned shared pointer will be empty.", "id": "day_3_sub_5_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of using `std::make_shared` instead of `std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)`?", "options": ["It makes the shared pointer thread-safe.", "It performs a single memory allocation for both the control block and the object, improving performance and cache locality.", "It allows using custom delete functions.", "It prevents compile-time template instantiation overhead."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)` allocates the object first, then allocates the control block. `std::make_shared` allocates a single block of memory big enough to hold both, reducing heap allocation overhead.", "id": "day_3_sub_5_mcq_4"}, {"question": "How does a custom deleter function pointer affect the size of a `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; it remains 8 bytes.", "It increases the size of the pointer (typically to 16 bytes) because it must store the function pointer.", "It causes the unique_ptr to allocate memory on the heap.", "It is a compile-time construct and does not exist at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a custom deleter is a function pointer, the unique_ptr must store it, increasing its size. Using a stateless functor (like a lambda with no captures) avoids this size overhead.", "id": "day_3_sub_5_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of placement `new` in C++?", "options": ["It automatically finds the best place in the heap to allocate memory.", "It constructs an object on a pre-allocated raw memory buffer without allocating new heap memory.", "It moves an object to another thread's stack space.", "It optimizes memory alignment dynamically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Placement `new` (e.g. `new (buffer) MyClass()`) constructs an object at a specific, pre-allocated memory address, avoiding heap allocation costs.", "id": "day_3_sub_5_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How must you destroy an object constructed via placement `new`?", "options": ["Call `delete ptr;`.", "Explicitly call the object's destructor (`ptr->~T()`) and then manually manage the buffer's memory.", "Let it go out of scope; the stack automatically deletes it.", "Call `free(ptr);`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because placement `new` did not allocate memory from the global heap, calling `delete` is undefined behavior. You must explicitly invoke the destructor and then clean up the raw buffer.", "id": "day_3_sub_5_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the undefined behavior hazard in the code `int* p = new int[10]; delete p;`?", "options": ["Using the wrong delete operator (`delete` instead of `delete[]`) is Undefined Behavior.", "There is no hazard; `delete` automatically knows it is an array.", "It is a compiler error and won't compile.", "It causes a stack overflow."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Memory allocated with `new[]` must be freed with `delete[]`. Using plain `delete` on an array pointer is undefined behavior, often causing heap corruption or failing to call destructors for elements 1 to 9.", "id": "day_3_sub_5_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the risk of returning a local variable by reference `const int&` from a function?", "options": ["It copies the variable unnecessarily.", "The local variable is destroyed at function exit, leaving a dangling reference, and accessing it is UB.", "It compiles only if the variable is marked `static`.", "It causes a memory leak on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Local variables have automatic storage duration (stack-allocated) and are destroyed when the function returns. Accessing a reference to a destroyed local variable is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_3_sub_5_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_3_sub_5", "day": 3, "index": 5}, {"title": "Object Layout — Padding, Struct Packing, #pragma pack", "explanation": "The compiler inserts padding bytes between struct members to satisfy alignment requirements. A member of type T is placed at an offset that is a multiple of `alignof(T)`. The struct's total size is padded to be a multiple of its largest member's alignment.\n\nMember ordering matters: reordering members from largest to smallest alignment can reduce padding and shrink the struct. ``#pragma pack`(N)` forces maximum alignment to N bytes, reducing padding but potentially causing performance penalties or crashes on strict-alignment architectures.\n\nUse `sizeof` and offsetof to inspect layout. Standard layout types have predictable layout compatible with C. Virtual functions add a vptr (pointer to vtable), typically at the beginning of the object.", "examples": [{"title": "Padding Example", "code": "struct Bad\n{\n    char a;     // 1 byte + 3 padding\n    int b;      // 4 bytes\n    char c;     // 1 byte + 3 padding\n};  // sizeof(Bad) == 12\n\nstruct Good\n{\n    int b;      // 4 bytes\n    char a;     // 1 byte\n    char c;     // 1 byte + 2 padding\n};  // sizeof(Good) == 8  — saved 4 bytes!\n\n// Force tight packing (may hurt performance)\n#pragma pack(push, 1)\nstruct Packed\n{\n    char a;\n    int b;\n    char c;\n};  // sizeof(Packed) == 6 — no padding\n#pragma pack(pop)", "explanation": "Reordering members by decreasing alignment minimizes padding.", "formatted_code": "struct Bad\n{\n    char a;  // 1 byte + 3 padding\n    int b;  // 4 bytes\n    char c;  // 1 byte + 3 padding\n};  // sizeof(Bad) == 12\n\nstruct Good\n{\n    int b;  // 4 bytes\n    char a;  // 1 byte\n    char c;  // 1 byte + 2 padding\n};  // sizeof(Good) == 8  — saved 4 bytes!\n\n// Force tight packing (may hurt performance)\n#pragma pack(push, 1)\nstruct Packed\n{\n    char a;\n    int b;\n    char c;\n};  // sizeof(Packed) == 6 — no padding\n#pragma pack(pop)"}], "pitfalls": ["`#pragma pack` can cause misaligned access — undefined behavior on some architectures", "Assuming struct layout matches declaration order across compilers (padding varies)", "Forgetting that `virtual` functions add a hidden vptr pointer to the object", "Network/file I/O structs without explicit packing — different padding on different platforms"], "tips_to_remember": ["Order members from largest to smallest alignment to minimize padding", "`sizeof` includes padding. Use offsetof to check individual member offsets", "Only use `#pragma pack` for wire protocols — it can hurt performance and cause UB", "Virtual functions add `sizeof(void*)` for the vptr"], "questions": [{"question": "How does the compiler add padding to structs?", "answer": "The compiler inserts padding bytes so each member's offset is a multiple of its alignment requirement. The struct's total size is padded to be a multiple of its strictest member alignment. This ensures all members are properly aligned for efficient access."}, {"question": "How can you minimize struct padding?", "answer": "Order members from largest to smallest alignment (e.g., double before int before char). This clusters padding at the end instead of between members. Use sizeof and offsetof to verify. Don't use #pragma pack unless necessary for serialization."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_3_sub_6_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_3_sub_6_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_3_sub_6_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_3_sub_6_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Object Layout — Padding, Struct Packing, #pragma pack`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_3_sub_6_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_3_sub_6_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_3_sub_6_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_3_sub_6_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_3_sub_6_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_3_sub_6_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_3_sub_6", "day": 3, "index": 6}, {"title": "Custom Allocators — std::allocator, Arena/Pool Allocators", "explanation": "The default allocator (`std::allocator`) calls operator new/delete. Custom allocators can improve performance by reducing allocation overhead, improving cache locality, and enabling memory pooling.\n\nArena/linear allocators: allocate from a pre-allocated block, 'free' does nothing, reset the entire arena at once. Excellent for per-frame game allocations. Pool allocators: maintain a free list of fixed-size blocks, `O(1)` allocation and deallocation. Good for same-sized objects (like nodes in a tree).\n\nC++17 introduced pmr (polymorphic memory resources) in <memory_resource>, making custom allocators much easier to use with standard containers via `std::pmr::vector`, `std::pmr::string`, etc.", "examples": [{"title": "PMR Pool Allocator", "code": "#include <memory_resource>\n#include <vector>\n\nchar buffer[1024];  // stack-allocated buffer\nstd::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource pool{buffer, sizeof(buffer)};\n\nstd::pmr::vector<int> vec{&pool};  // allocates from our buffer\nfor (int i = 0; i < 100; ++i)\n    vec.push_back(i);  // no heap allocation until buffer is full", "explanation": "PMR (C++17) makes custom allocators easy to use with standard containers.", "formatted_code": "#include <memory_resource>\n#include <vector>\n\nchar buffer[1024];  // stack-allocated buffer\nstd::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource pool\n{\n    buffer, sizeof(buffer)\n};\n\nstd::pmr::vector<int> vec\n{\n    &pool\n};  // allocates from our buffer\nfor (int i = 0; i < 100; ++i)\nvec.push_back(i);  // no heap allocation until buffer is full"}], "pitfalls": ["Custom allocator must handle alignment correctly", "Arena allocators can't free individual objects — only bulk reset", "Pre-C++17 allocators are stateless by convention — limits what you can do", "Forgetting to ensure the arena outlives all objects allocated from it"], "tips_to_remember": ["Use pmr (C++17) for ergonomic custom allocators with standard containers", "Arena allocators: `O(1)` alloc, `O(1)` bulk free. Perfect for temporary per-frame data", "Pool allocators: `O(1)` alloc/free for fixed-size objects (nodes, particles)", "Profile first — custom allocators are an optimization, not a default choice"], "questions": [{"question": "When should you use a custom allocator?", "answer": "When profiling shows allocation overhead is a bottleneck. Common scenarios: game engines (per-frame arena allocators), high-frequency trading (pool allocators for order objects), embedded systems (no heap), and reducing memory fragmentation in long-running servers."}, {"question": "What is pmr and how does it simplify custom allocators?", "answer": "std::pmr (C++17) provides polymorphic memory resources — allocators that use runtime polymorphism (virtual functions) instead of template parameters. This means you can change allocators at runtime, pass different allocators to the same container type, and use standard containers (std::pmr::vector) without template noise."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you pass an array `int arr[10]` to a function declared as `void process(int arr[])`?", "options": ["The array is passed by value, making a copy of all 10 integers.", "It decays to a raw pointer `int*`, and its size information is lost.", "The compiler verifies the size is exactly 10 and throws a warning if not.", "It is passed as a safe reference to the array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Arrays passed to functions decay to pointers. Inside `process`, `arr` is a raw pointer (`int*`), and `sizeof(arr)` returns the pointer size (e.g. 8 bytes), not the array size.", "id": "day_3_sub_7_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is a compiler error for `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["Moving it using `std::move`.", "Attempting to copy it via copy assignment or copy constructor.", "Resetting it using `.reset()`.", "Releasing ownership with `.release()`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unique_ptr` represents exclusive ownership. To prevent duplicate owners, copying is disabled (deleted copy constructor/assignment), causing a compilation error.", "id": "day_3_sub_7_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the outcome of a circular dependency where two objects contain `std::shared_ptr` pointing to each other?", "options": ["The compiler reports a cyclic template dependency error.", "The reference counts will never drop to zero, leaking both objects.", "The destructors are called normally due to automatic cycle detection.", "The program crashes with a stack overflow on deletion."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because each object holds a `std::shared_ptr` to the other, their reference counts remain at least 1, even after all external pointers are destroyed, leaking the memory.", "id": "day_3_sub_7_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How do you access the managed object of a `std::weak_ptr`?", "options": ["Dereference it directly using `*` or `->`.", "You must call `.lock()` to obtain a `std::shared_ptr` first.", "Call `.get()` to retrieve the raw pointer.", "It is not possible to access the object once it is in a weak pointer."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::weak_ptr` does not provide direct access. You must call `.lock()` which returns a `std::shared_ptr`. If the managed object has already been destroyed, the returned shared pointer will be empty.", "id": "day_3_sub_7_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of using `std::make_shared` instead of `std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)`?", "options": ["It makes the shared pointer thread-safe.", "It performs a single memory allocation for both the control block and the object, improving performance and cache locality.", "It allows using custom delete functions.", "It prevents compile-time template instantiation overhead."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)` allocates the object first, then allocates the control block. `std::make_shared` allocates a single block of memory big enough to hold both, reducing heap allocation overhead.", "id": "day_3_sub_7_mcq_4"}, {"question": "How does a custom deleter function pointer affect the size of a `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; it remains 8 bytes.", "It increases the size of the pointer (typically to 16 bytes) because it must store the function pointer.", "It causes the unique_ptr to allocate memory on the heap.", "It is a compile-time construct and does not exist at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a custom deleter is a function pointer, the unique_ptr must store it, increasing its size. Using a stateless functor (like a lambda with no captures) avoids this size overhead.", "id": "day_3_sub_7_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of placement `new` in C++?", "options": ["It automatically finds the best place in the heap to allocate memory.", "It constructs an object on a pre-allocated raw memory buffer without allocating new heap memory.", "It moves an object to another thread's stack space.", "It optimizes memory alignment dynamically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Placement `new` (e.g. `new (buffer) MyClass()`) constructs an object at a specific, pre-allocated memory address, avoiding heap allocation costs.", "id": "day_3_sub_7_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How must you destroy an object constructed via placement `new`?", "options": ["Call `delete ptr;`.", "Explicitly call the object's destructor (`ptr->~T()`) and then manually manage the buffer's memory.", "Let it go out of scope; the stack automatically deletes it.", "Call `free(ptr);`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because placement `new` did not allocate memory from the global heap, calling `delete` is undefined behavior. You must explicitly invoke the destructor and then clean up the raw buffer.", "id": "day_3_sub_7_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the undefined behavior hazard in the code `int* p = new int[10]; delete p;`?", "options": ["Using the wrong delete operator (`delete` instead of `delete[]`) is Undefined Behavior.", "There is no hazard; `delete` automatically knows it is an array.", "It is a compiler error and won't compile.", "It causes a stack overflow."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Memory allocated with `new[]` must be freed with `delete[]`. Using plain `delete` on an array pointer is undefined behavior, often causing heap corruption or failing to call destructors for elements 1 to 9.", "id": "day_3_sub_7_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the risk of returning a local variable by reference `const int&` from a function?", "options": ["It copies the variable unnecessarily.", "The local variable is destroyed at function exit, leaving a dangling reference, and accessing it is UB.", "It compiles only if the variable is marked `static`.", "It causes a memory leak on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Local variables have automatic storage duration (stack-allocated) and are destroyed when the function returns. Accessing a reference to a destroyed local variable is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_3_sub_7_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_3_sub_7", "day": 3, "index": 7}]}, {"day": 4, "topic": "Smart Pointers", "estimated_hours": 4, "subtopics": [{"title": "unique_ptr — Exclusive Ownership, Move-Only, Custom Deleters", "explanation": "`std::unique_ptr<T>` represents exclusive ownership of a dynamically allocated object. It cannot be copied — only moved. When a unique_ptr is destroyed (goes out of scope), it automatically deletes the owned object. This enforces the single-owner **RAII** pattern.\n\nCustom deleters allow unique_ptr to manage non-memory resources (file handles, C API resources). The deleter is part of the type: unique_ptr<FILE, `decltype(&fclose)`>. With stateless lambdas or function pointers, unique_ptr has zero overhead over a raw pointer.\n\nunique_ptr is the default smart pointer — use it unless you need shared ownership. It's zero-overhead (same size as raw pointer for default deleter) and makes ownership explicit.", "examples": [{"title": "unique_ptr Basics and Custom Deleter", "code": "auto p = std::make_unique<int>(42);  // preferred creation\n// auto p2 = p;  // ERROR: can't copy\nauto p2 = std::move(p);  // OK: transfer ownership\n// p is now nullptr\n\n// Custom deleter for C API\nauto fileDeleter = [](FILE* f) { if (f) fclose(f); };\nstd::unique_ptr<FILE, decltype(fileDeleter)> file(fopen(\"test.txt\", \"r\"), fileDeleter);\n// file automatically closed at scope exit\n\n// Array form\nauto arr = std::make_unique<int[]>(10);  // unique_ptr<int[]>\narr[0] = 42;", "explanation": "unique_ptr: move-only, zero overhead, supports custom deleters.", "formatted_code": "auto p = std::make_unique<int>(42);  // preferred creation\n// auto p2 = p;  // ERROR: can't copy\nauto p2 = std::move(p);  // OK: transfer ownership\n// p is now nullptr\n\n// Custom deleter for C API\nauto fileDeleter = [](FILE* f)\n{\n    if (f) fclose(f);\n};\nstd::unique_ptr<FILE, decltype(fileDeleter)> file(fopen(\"test.txt\", \"r\"), fileDeleter);\n// file automatically closed at scope exit\n\n// Array form\nauto arr = std::make_unique<int[]>(10);  // unique_ptr<int[]>\narr[0] = 42;"}], "pitfalls": ["Using unique_ptr where shared ownership is needed", "Calling .`get()` and storing the raw pointer long-term (it can dangle when unique_ptr dies)", "Forgetting the array form: unique_ptr<T[]> for arrays, not `unique_ptr<T>`", "Custom deleter changes the type — `unique_ptr<T,D1>` ≠ `unique_ptr<T,D2>`"], "tips_to_remember": ["unique_ptr is the default choice — use it first, shared_ptr only when needed", "make_unique is exception-safe and avoids writing new", "Pass unique_ptr by value to transfer ownership, by const ref to observe", "`unique_ptr<T>` with default deleter is the same size as `T*`"], "questions": [{"question": "When should you use unique_ptr vs shared_ptr?", "answer": "Use unique_ptr when there's a single, clear owner. It has zero overhead and makes ownership explicit. Use shared_ptr only when multiple owners genuinely need to share the lifetime of an object (which is rarer than people think)."}, {"question": "What is a custom deleter and when would you use one?", "answer": "A custom deleter tells unique_ptr how to release the resource. Default is delete/delete[]. Use custom deleters for C APIs (fclose, SDL_DestroyWindow), specialized allocators, or any resource that isn't freed with delete."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_4_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_4_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_4_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_4_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `unique_ptr — Exclusive Ownership, Move-Only, Custom Deleters`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_4_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_4_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_4_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_4_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_4_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_4_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_4_sub_0", "day": 4, "index": 0}, {"title": "shared_ptr — Control Block, Atomic Refcount, Overhead", "explanation": "`std::shared_ptr<T>` enables shared ownership through reference counting. Multiple shared_ptrs can point to the same object, and the object is deleted when the last shared_ptr is destroyed. The reference count is stored in a 'control block' allocated alongside the object.\n\nControl block contains: strong reference count (atomic), weak reference count (atomic), deleter, and allocator. Atomic operations make shared_ptr thread-safe for the control block (but not the pointed-to object!). make_shared combines object and control block into a single allocation.\n\nOverhead: shared_ptr is 2 pointers in size (pointer to object + pointer to control block). Copying increments an atomic counter. This is measurably slower than unique_ptr in tight loops.", "examples": [{"title": "shared_ptr Internals", "code": "auto sp1 = std::make_shared<int>(42);  // single allocation for object + control block\nstd::cout << sp1.use_count();  // 1\n\nauto sp2 = sp1;  // copy: refcount becomes 2\nstd::cout << sp1.use_count();  // 2\n\nsp1.reset();  // refcount becomes 1\nstd::cout << sp2.use_count();  // 1\n// Object deleted when sp2 is destroyed (refcount → 0)", "explanation": "shared_ptr uses atomic reference counting with a control block.", "formatted_code": "auto sp1 = std::make_shared<int>(42);  // single allocation for object + control block\nstd::cout << sp1.use_count();  // 1\n\nauto sp2 = sp1;  // copy: refcount becomes 2\nstd::cout << sp1.use_count();  // 2\n\nsp1.reset();  // refcount becomes 1\nstd::cout << sp2.use_count();  // 1\n// Object deleted when sp2 is destroyed (refcount → 0)"}], "pitfalls": ["Creating shared_ptrs from the same raw pointer twice (two control blocks, double delete!)", "Circular references with shared_ptr → memory leak (use weak_ptr to break cycles)", "Assuming shared_ptr makes the pointed-to object thread-safe (it doesn't)", "Overhead of atomic refcount in single-threaded code"], "tips_to_remember": ["NEVER create two shared_ptrs from the same raw pointer — use make_shared or aliasing constructor", "shared_ptr is 2x the size of a raw pointer", "make_shared is preferred: single allocation, exception-safe, cache-friendly", "Thread-safety: control block ops are atomic, but accessing the object needs your own synchronization"], "questions": [{"question": "What is the control block in shared_ptr?", "answer": "The control block is a heap-allocated structure containing the strong reference count, weak reference count, the deleter, and the allocator. make_shared merges the control block and object into a single allocation for efficiency."}, {"question": "Is shared_ptr thread-safe?", "answer": "The reference count operations are atomic and thread-safe. However, accessing the pointed-to object from multiple threads without synchronization is still a data race. Concurrent writes to the same shared_ptr variable also require synchronization (or use std::atomic<std::shared_ptr> in C++20)."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the key difference between `std::thread` and `std::jthread` (C++20) on destruction?", "options": ["`std::jthread` allocates thread resources on the stack.", "`std::jthread` automatically joins and requests cooperative interruption on destruction, while `std::thread` calls `std::terminate` if it is still joinable.", "`std::thread` is faster to construct.", "`std::jthread` does not support lambda execution."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a `std::thread` is destroyed while it is still joinable (running and not joined/detached), it calls `std::terminate`, crashing the program. `std::jthread` automatically joins and cooperatively signals stop on destruction.", "id": "day_4_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What constitutes a data race in C++?", "options": ["Two threads executing different functions simultaneously.", "Two or more threads accessing the same memory location concurrently, where at least one access is a write, and without synchronization.", "Using global variables in a multithreaded application.", "When a thread fails to acquire a lock in time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A data race is defined as concurrent unsynchronized access to the same memory location where at least one thread is writing. Data races are undefined behavior in C++.", "id": "day_4_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why would you prefer `std::unique_lock` over `std::lock_guard`?", "options": ["`std::unique_lock` is faster.", "`std::unique_lock` provides advanced features like deferring lock, explicit unlocking, timeout locking, and move semantics, whereas `std::lock_guard` is strictly scoped.", "`std::lock_guard` causes deadlocks easily.", "`std::unique_lock` does not require a mutex reference."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::lock_guard` is a simple, zero-overhead wrapper for basic scoped locking. `std::unique_lock` has a small overhead but supports complex use cases like deferred locking, manual unlocking, and transferring locks.", "id": "day_4_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How does `std::scoped_lock` (C++17) prevent deadlocks when locking multiple mutexes?", "options": ["It compiles the mutexes into a single lock.", "It uses a deadlock-avoidance algorithm to lock all passed mutexes simultaneously.", "It runs a background thread to monitor locks.", "It converts mutexes to atomic variables."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::scoped_lock` can lock multiple mutexes. It uses the same deadlock avoidance algorithm as `std::lock` under the hood, ensuring they are acquired in a safe, sorted sequence.", "id": "day_4_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the key benefit of `std::atomic` variables?", "options": ["They make entire classes thread-safe.", "Operations on them are guaranteed to be atomic, preventing data races without needing a heavy mutex lock.", "They run code on separate CPU cores.", "They prevent memory allocations."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Operations on `std::atomic` variables are executed as single, indivisible CPU instructions, ensuring thread safety and preventing data races without the system-call overhead of mutexes.", "id": "day_4_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What is the default memory order for atomic operations in C++?", "options": ["`std::memory_order_relaxed`", "`std::memory_order_seq_cst` (Sequential Consistency)", "`std::memory_order_acquire`", "`std::memory_order_release`"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "By default, all atomic operations use `std::memory_order_seq_cst`. This provides sequential consistency, establishing a global, total ordering of all operations visible to all threads.", "id": "day_4_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What guarantees does `std::memory_order_relaxed` provide?", "options": ["It guarantees that operations are atomic, but provides no synchronization or ordering guarantees for surrounding memory accesses.", "It behaves exactly like sequentially consistent order but runs faster.", "It locks the CPU caches during execution.", "It is a runtime check that throws an exception on race."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "`memory_order_relaxed` only guarantees atomicity of the variable itself. The compiler and CPU are free to reorder surrounding read/write instructions around it, providing no synchronization between threads.", "id": "day_4_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "Why must a condition variable wait loop check the predicate condition (e.g. `cv.wait(lock, []{ return ready; })`)?", "options": ["To yield the CPU back to the OS.", "To prevent spurious wakeups, where the thread wakes up without receiving a signal.", "Because condition variables can only wake up once.", "To register the thread with the scheduler."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Condition variables are subject to 'spurious wakeups' due to OS implementation details. A thread can wake up even if no signal was sent. Thus, the thread must always re-verify the predicate condition.", "id": "day_4_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the relationship between `std::promise` and `std::future`?", "options": ["They are synonyms for the same class.", "`std::promise` sets a value or exception in a shared state, which `std::future` retrieves asynchronously.", "`std::future` is used to send values, `std::promise` is used to read them.", "They are used to create recursive mutex locks."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A promise is the writer-end and a future is the reader-end of an asynchronous channel. `std::promise` writes a value or exception to the shared channel state, which is retrieved via `std::future::get()`.", "id": "day_4_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does the `volatile` keyword provide thread synchronization or atomicity in C++?", "options": ["Yes, it makes variables thread-safe.", "No, `volatile` only prevents compiler register caching optimizations for hardware registers; it does NOT prevent data races or guarantee thread memory order.", "Yes, it acts as a lightweight atomic variable.", "No, it is a deprecated keyword in modern C++."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Unlike Java/C#, `volatile` in C++ has nothing to do with multithreading. It only instructs the compiler not to optimize away reads/writes to memory (useful for memory-mapped I/O). It does not generate atomic CPU operations or memory barriers.", "id": "day_4_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_4_sub_1", "day": 4, "index": 1}, {"title": "weak_ptr — Breaking Reference Cycles", "explanation": "`std::weak_ptr<T>` is a non-owning smart pointer that observes a shared_ptr without affecting the reference count. It can be used to check if the object still exists (`expired()`) and to obtain a temporary shared_ptr (`lock()`). Primary use: breaking circular references between shared_ptrs.\n\nCommon pattern: parent owns children with shared_ptr, children reference parent with weak_ptr. Without weak_ptr, circular shared_ptr references would prevent either object from being destroyed (memory leak).\n\nweak_ptr is also useful for caches: cache holds weak_ptr, creates shared_ptr when the object is still alive, or creates a new one when it has expired.", "examples": [{"title": "Breaking Circular References", "code": "struct Node\n{\n    std::shared_ptr<Node> next;\n    std::weak_ptr<Node> prev;  // weak to break cycle!\n    ~Node() { std::cout << \"Node destroyed\\n\"; }\n};\n\nauto a = std::make_shared<Node>();\nauto b = std::make_shared<Node>();\na->next = b;\nb->prev = a;  // weak_ptr: doesn't prevent a from being destroyed\n// Both nodes properly destroyed at scope exit\n\n// Using weak_ptr\nif (auto locked = b->prev.lock())\n{\n    // locked is a valid shared_ptr — object still alive\n}", "explanation": "weak_ptr breaks cycles and allows safe observation of shared objects.", "formatted_code": "struct Node\n{\n    std::shared_ptr<Node> next;\n    std::weak_ptr<Node> prev;  // weak to break cycle!\n    ~Node()\n    {\n        std::cout << \"Node destroyed\\n\";\n    }\n};\n\nauto a = std::make_shared<Node>();\nauto b = std::make_shared<Node>();\na->next = b;\nb->prev = a;  // weak_ptr: doesn't prevent a from being destroyed\n// Both nodes properly destroyed at scope exit\n\n// Using weak_ptr\nif (auto locked = b->prev.lock())\n{\n    // locked is a valid shared_ptr — object still alive\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["Calling `lock()` without checking the result (it can be `nullptr`)", "Using weak_ptr when unique_ptr + raw pointer would suffice", "Excessive weak_ptr usage adding complexity without benefit"], "tips_to_remember": ["Use weak_ptr to break circular shared_ptr references", "Always check the result of `lock()` — it returns `nullptr` if the object is gone", "``weak_ptr::expired`()` can race with deletion — prefer `lock()` + null check", "Good for caches: observe if alive, recreate if expired"], "questions": [{"question": "How does weak_ptr break reference cycles?", "answer": "In a cycle of shared_ptrs (A→B→A), neither object's refcount reaches zero. Replace one link with weak_ptr (A→B, B⇢A weakly). When A's last external shared_ptr dies, A is destroyed, breaking the cycle and allowing B to be destroyed too."}, {"question": "What does weak_ptr::lock() do?", "answer": "lock() atomically checks if the object still exists and returns a shared_ptr to it if so (incrementing the refcount). If the object has been destroyed, it returns an empty shared_ptr. This is the safe way to access a weakly-observed object."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you pass an array `int arr[10]` to a function declared as `void process(int arr[])`?", "options": ["The array is passed by value, making a copy of all 10 integers.", "It decays to a raw pointer `int*`, and its size information is lost.", "The compiler verifies the size is exactly 10 and throws a warning if not.", "It is passed as a safe reference to the array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Arrays passed to functions decay to pointers. Inside `process`, `arr` is a raw pointer (`int*`), and `sizeof(arr)` returns the pointer size (e.g. 8 bytes), not the array size.", "id": "day_4_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is a compiler error for `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["Moving it using `std::move`.", "Attempting to copy it via copy assignment or copy constructor.", "Resetting it using `.reset()`.", "Releasing ownership with `.release()`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unique_ptr` represents exclusive ownership. To prevent duplicate owners, copying is disabled (deleted copy constructor/assignment), causing a compilation error.", "id": "day_4_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the outcome of a circular dependency where two objects contain `std::shared_ptr` pointing to each other?", "options": ["The compiler reports a cyclic template dependency error.", "The reference counts will never drop to zero, leaking both objects.", "The destructors are called normally due to automatic cycle detection.", "The program crashes with a stack overflow on deletion."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because each object holds a `std::shared_ptr` to the other, their reference counts remain at least 1, even after all external pointers are destroyed, leaking the memory.", "id": "day_4_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How do you access the managed object of a `std::weak_ptr`?", "options": ["Dereference it directly using `*` or `->`.", "You must call `.lock()` to obtain a `std::shared_ptr` first.", "Call `.get()` to retrieve the raw pointer.", "It is not possible to access the object once it is in a weak pointer."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::weak_ptr` does not provide direct access. You must call `.lock()` which returns a `std::shared_ptr`. If the managed object has already been destroyed, the returned shared pointer will be empty.", "id": "day_4_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of using `std::make_shared` instead of `std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)`?", "options": ["It makes the shared pointer thread-safe.", "It performs a single memory allocation for both the control block and the object, improving performance and cache locality.", "It allows using custom delete functions.", "It prevents compile-time template instantiation overhead."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)` allocates the object first, then allocates the control block. `std::make_shared` allocates a single block of memory big enough to hold both, reducing heap allocation overhead.", "id": "day_4_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "How does a custom deleter function pointer affect the size of a `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; it remains 8 bytes.", "It increases the size of the pointer (typically to 16 bytes) because it must store the function pointer.", "It causes the unique_ptr to allocate memory on the heap.", "It is a compile-time construct and does not exist at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a custom deleter is a function pointer, the unique_ptr must store it, increasing its size. Using a stateless functor (like a lambda with no captures) avoids this size overhead.", "id": "day_4_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of placement `new` in C++?", "options": ["It automatically finds the best place in the heap to allocate memory.", "It constructs an object on a pre-allocated raw memory buffer without allocating new heap memory.", "It moves an object to another thread's stack space.", "It optimizes memory alignment dynamically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Placement `new` (e.g. `new (buffer) MyClass()`) constructs an object at a specific, pre-allocated memory address, avoiding heap allocation costs.", "id": "day_4_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How must you destroy an object constructed via placement `new`?", "options": ["Call `delete ptr;`.", "Explicitly call the object's destructor (`ptr->~T()`) and then manually manage the buffer's memory.", "Let it go out of scope; the stack automatically deletes it.", "Call `free(ptr);`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because placement `new` did not allocate memory from the global heap, calling `delete` is undefined behavior. You must explicitly invoke the destructor and then clean up the raw buffer.", "id": "day_4_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the undefined behavior hazard in the code `int* p = new int[10]; delete p;`?", "options": ["Using the wrong delete operator (`delete` instead of `delete[]`) is Undefined Behavior.", "There is no hazard; `delete` automatically knows it is an array.", "It is a compiler error and won't compile.", "It causes a stack overflow."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Memory allocated with `new[]` must be freed with `delete[]`. Using plain `delete` on an array pointer is undefined behavior, often causing heap corruption or failing to call destructors for elements 1 to 9.", "id": "day_4_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the risk of returning a local variable by reference `const int&` from a function?", "options": ["It copies the variable unnecessarily.", "The local variable is destroyed at function exit, leaving a dangling reference, and accessing it is UB.", "It compiles only if the variable is marked `static`.", "It causes a memory leak on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Local variables have automatic storage duration (stack-allocated) and are destroyed when the function returns. Accessing a reference to a destroyed local variable is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_4_sub_2_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_4_sub_2", "day": 4, "index": 2}, {"title": "enable_shared_from_this", "explanation": "`std::enable_shared_from_this<T>` is a **CRTP** base class that allows an object to safely create a shared_ptr to itself (`shared_from_this()`). Without it, creating a new shared_ptr from 'this' would create a second control block, leading to double deletion.\n\nUse case: when a member function needs to pass a shared_ptr to itself to an asynchronous callback or register itself with an observer.", "examples": [{"title": "enable_shared_from_this Usage", "code": "class Widget : public std::enable_shared_from_this<Widget> {\npublic:\n    void registerCallback()\n    {\n        auto self = shared_from_this();  // safe!\n        // auto bad = std::shared_ptr<Widget>(this);  // DISASTER: second control block!\n        asyncOp([self]() { self->process(); });\n    }\n    void process() { std::cout << \"processing\\n\"; }\n};\n\nauto w = std::make_shared<Widget>();\nw->registerCallback();  // OK: w was created with make_shared", "explanation": "shared_from_this() safely creates a shared_ptr from within a method.", "formatted_code": "class Widget : public std::enable_shared_from_this<Widget>\n{\npublic:\n    void registerCallback()\n    {\n        auto self = shared_from_this();  // safe!\n        // auto bad = std::shared_ptr<Widget>(this);  // DISASTER: second control block!\n        asyncOp([self]()\n        {\n            self->process();\n        }\n        );\n    }\n    void process()\n    {\n        std::cout << \"processing\\n\";\n    }\n};\n\nauto w = std::make_shared<Widget>();\nw->registerCallback();  // OK: w was created with make_shared"}], "pitfalls": ["Calling `shared_from_this()` on an object not owned by a shared_ptr — UB/throws", "Creating the object on the stack and calling `shared_from_this()` — UB", "Calling `shared_from_this()` in the constructor — UB (shared_ptr not fully set up yet)"], "tips_to_remember": ["The object MUST be owned by a shared_ptr before calling `shared_from_this()`", "Cannot use in constructors — the shared_ptr control block isn't attached yet", "Only use when an object genuinely needs to hand out `shared_ptr<this>` (async callbacks, observers)"], "questions": [{"question": "What problem does enable_shared_from_this solve?", "answer": "It allows an object managed by shared_ptr to safely create additional shared_ptrs to itself without creating a second control block. Without it, shared_ptr<T>(this) would create a new control block, leading to double deletion when either shared_ptr is destroyed."}, {"question": "When is it unsafe to call shared_from_this()?", "answer": "1) When the object isn't owned by any shared_ptr, 2) Inside the constructor (shared_ptr isn't set up yet), 3) When the object is stack-allocated. In all these cases, the behavior is undefined or throws bad_weak_ptr."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_4_sub_3_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_4_sub_3_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_4_sub_3_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_4_sub_3_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `enable_shared_from_this`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_4_sub_3_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_4_sub_3_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_4_sub_3_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_4_sub_3_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_4_sub_3_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_4_sub_3_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_4_sub_3", "day": 4, "index": 3}, {"title": "make_unique / make_shared — Exception Safety, Single Allocation", "explanation": "``std::make_unique<T>`(args)` (C++14) and ``std::make_shared<T>`(args)` (C++11) are factory functions that create smart pointers. They're preferred over new for two reasons: exception safety and (for make_shared) performance.\n\nException safety: in f(`shared_ptr<A>`(new `A()`), `shared_ptr<B>`(new `B()`)), the evaluation order is unspecified. If new `A()` succeeds, then new `B()` throws before the shared_ptr is constructed, A leaks. make_shared prevents this.\n\nmake_shared performs a single allocation for both the object and the control block (instead of two separate allocations), improving cache locality and reducing allocator overhead.\n\n### Unforgettable Analogy / Deep Dive\nException safety guarantees are like database transaction logs:\n- **Basic `Guarantee**`: If an exception occurs, no memory is leaked and objects are in a valid state, but data might be partially changed.\n- **Strong Guarantee (Commit/Rollback)**: The operation either succeeds completely or rolls back to the original state with zero side effects. Usually achieved via 'copy-and-swap' (do work on a copy, then swap).\n- **Nothrow `Guarantee**`: The operation is guaranteed to never fail (`noexcept`), which is critical for destructors, moves, and swaps.", "examples": [{"title": "Why make_shared", "code": "// PREFER:\nauto p = std::make_shared<Widget>(arg1, arg2);  // 1 allocation, exception-safe\n\n// AVOID:\nauto q = std::shared_ptr<Widget>(new Widget(arg1, arg2));  // 2 allocations\n\n// make_shared limitation: can't use custom deleter\n// make_shared limitation: memory for object not freed until last weak_ptr dies\n\n// make_unique (C++14)\nauto u = std::make_unique<Widget>(arg1, arg2);\n// No exception safety issue for unique_ptr, but make_unique is still cleaner", "explanation": "make_shared: single allocation + exception safe. make_unique: clean syntax + no new.", "formatted_code": "// PREFER:\nauto p = std::make_shared<Widget>(arg1, arg2);  // 1 allocation, exception-safe\n\n// AVOID:\nauto q = std::shared_ptr<Widget>(new Widget(arg1, arg2));  // 2 allocations\n\n// make_shared limitation: can't use custom deleter\n// make_shared limitation: memory for object not freed until last weak_ptr dies\n\n// make_unique (C++14)\nauto u = std::make_unique<Widget>(arg1, arg2);\n// No exception safety issue for unique_ptr, but make_unique is still cleaner"}], "pitfalls": ["make_shared delays memory deallocation if weak_ptrs outlive shared_ptrs (control block keeps the memory alive)", "Can't use make_shared with custom deleters — use shared_ptr constructor instead", "Can't use make_shared with {} initializer lists (e.g., `make_shared<vector<int>>`({1,2,3}) doesn't work)"], "tips_to_remember": ["Always prefer make_unique/make_shared over raw new + smart pointer constructor", "make_shared = 1 allocation (object + control block together). Separate construction = 2 allocations", "The only time to use new with shared_ptr: custom deleter or when weak_ptr keeping memory alive is unacceptable"], "questions": [{"question": "Why is make_shared preferred over shared_ptr(new T)?", "answer": "1) Exception safety: prevents memory leaks in expressions with multiple allocations. 2) Performance: single allocation for object + control block (better cache locality, less allocator overhead). 3) Cleaner code: no visible new."}, {"question": "What is the downside of make_shared?", "answer": "The object and control block share the same allocation, so the memory can't be freed until both the last shared_ptr AND the last weak_ptr are gone. With separate allocations, the object memory is freed when the last shared_ptr dies, regardless of weak_ptrs."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you pass an array `int arr[10]` to a function declared as `void process(int arr[])`?", "options": ["The array is passed by value, making a copy of all 10 integers.", "It decays to a raw pointer `int*`, and its size information is lost.", "The compiler verifies the size is exactly 10 and throws a warning if not.", "It is passed as a safe reference to the array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Arrays passed to functions decay to pointers. Inside `process`, `arr` is a raw pointer (`int*`), and `sizeof(arr)` returns the pointer size (e.g. 8 bytes), not the array size.", "id": "day_4_sub_4_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is a compiler error for `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["Moving it using `std::move`.", "Attempting to copy it via copy assignment or copy constructor.", "Resetting it using `.reset()`.", "Releasing ownership with `.release()`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unique_ptr` represents exclusive ownership. To prevent duplicate owners, copying is disabled (deleted copy constructor/assignment), causing a compilation error.", "id": "day_4_sub_4_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the outcome of a circular dependency where two objects contain `std::shared_ptr` pointing to each other?", "options": ["The compiler reports a cyclic template dependency error.", "The reference counts will never drop to zero, leaking both objects.", "The destructors are called normally due to automatic cycle detection.", "The program crashes with a stack overflow on deletion."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because each object holds a `std::shared_ptr` to the other, their reference counts remain at least 1, even after all external pointers are destroyed, leaking the memory.", "id": "day_4_sub_4_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How do you access the managed object of a `std::weak_ptr`?", "options": ["Dereference it directly using `*` or `->`.", "You must call `.lock()` to obtain a `std::shared_ptr` first.", "Call `.get()` to retrieve the raw pointer.", "It is not possible to access the object once it is in a weak pointer."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::weak_ptr` does not provide direct access. You must call `.lock()` which returns a `std::shared_ptr`. If the managed object has already been destroyed, the returned shared pointer will be empty.", "id": "day_4_sub_4_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of using `std::make_shared` instead of `std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)`?", "options": ["It makes the shared pointer thread-safe.", "It performs a single memory allocation for both the control block and the object, improving performance and cache locality.", "It allows using custom delete functions.", "It prevents compile-time template instantiation overhead."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)` allocates the object first, then allocates the control block. `std::make_shared` allocates a single block of memory big enough to hold both, reducing heap allocation overhead.", "id": "day_4_sub_4_mcq_4"}, {"question": "How does a custom deleter function pointer affect the size of a `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; it remains 8 bytes.", "It increases the size of the pointer (typically to 16 bytes) because it must store the function pointer.", "It causes the unique_ptr to allocate memory on the heap.", "It is a compile-time construct and does not exist at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a custom deleter is a function pointer, the unique_ptr must store it, increasing its size. Using a stateless functor (like a lambda with no captures) avoids this size overhead.", "id": "day_4_sub_4_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of placement `new` in C++?", "options": ["It automatically finds the best place in the heap to allocate memory.", "It constructs an object on a pre-allocated raw memory buffer without allocating new heap memory.", "It moves an object to another thread's stack space.", "It optimizes memory alignment dynamically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Placement `new` (e.g. `new (buffer) MyClass()`) constructs an object at a specific, pre-allocated memory address, avoiding heap allocation costs.", "id": "day_4_sub_4_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How must you destroy an object constructed via placement `new`?", "options": ["Call `delete ptr;`.", "Explicitly call the object's destructor (`ptr->~T()`) and then manually manage the buffer's memory.", "Let it go out of scope; the stack automatically deletes it.", "Call `free(ptr);`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because placement `new` did not allocate memory from the global heap, calling `delete` is undefined behavior. You must explicitly invoke the destructor and then clean up the raw buffer.", "id": "day_4_sub_4_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the undefined behavior hazard in the code `int* p = new int[10]; delete p;`?", "options": ["Using the wrong delete operator (`delete` instead of `delete[]`) is Undefined Behavior.", "There is no hazard; `delete` automatically knows it is an array.", "It is a compiler error and won't compile.", "It causes a stack overflow."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Memory allocated with `new[]` must be freed with `delete[]`. Using plain `delete` on an array pointer is undefined behavior, often causing heap corruption or failing to call destructors for elements 1 to 9.", "id": "day_4_sub_4_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the risk of returning a local variable by reference `const int&` from a function?", "options": ["It copies the variable unnecessarily.", "The local variable is destroyed at function exit, leaving a dangling reference, and accessing it is UB.", "It compiles only if the variable is marked `static`.", "It causes a memory leak on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Local variables have automatic storage duration (stack-allocated) and are destroyed when the function returns. Accessing a reference to a destroyed local variable is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_4_sub_4_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_4_sub_4", "day": 4, "index": 4}, {"title": "auto_ptr — Deprecated/Removed", "explanation": "`std::auto_ptr` was C++98's attempt at a smart pointer with unique ownership. It was deprecated in C++11 and removed in C++17. The problem: its copy constructor actually moves (transfers ownership), violating the expected semantics of copy. This made it dangerous in containers and generic code.\n\nLesson: unique_ptr succeeded because C++11 introduced move semantics — a proper language mechanism for transfer of ownership distinct from copying.", "examples": [{"title": "auto_ptr's Broken Copy", "code": "// DON'T USE — removed in C++17\n// std::auto_ptr<int> p1(new int(42));\n// std::auto_ptr<int> p2 = p1;  // 'copy' actually transfers ownership!\n// p1 is now null — surprise!\n// This breaks containers: vector<auto_ptr<T>> is UB\n\n// unique_ptr is the correct replacement:\nstd::unique_ptr<int> u1 = std::make_unique<int>(42);\n// std::unique_ptr<int> u2 = u1;  // ERROR: can't copy — explicit!\nstd::unique_ptr<int> u2 = std::move(u1);  // OK: explicit move", "explanation": "auto_ptr's 'copy' was actually a move — unique_ptr makes this explicit.", "formatted_code": "// DON'T USE — removed in C++17\n// std::auto_ptr<int> p1(new int(42));\n// std::auto_ptr<int> p2 = p1;  // 'copy' actually transfers ownership!\n// p1 is now null — surprise!\n// This breaks containers: vector<auto_ptr<T>> is UB\n\n// unique_ptr is the correct replacement:\nstd::unique_ptr<int> u1 = std::make_unique<int>(42);\n// std::unique_ptr<int> u2 = u1;  // ERROR: can't copy — explicit!\nstd::unique_ptr<int> u2 = std::move(u1);  // OK: explicit move"}], "pitfalls": ["Using auto_ptr in legacy code — it's removed in C++17", "auto_ptr couldn't handle arrays (no delete[] support)", "auto_ptr in containers caused UB due to copy-that-moves semantics"], "tips_to_remember": ["auto_ptr is removed. Always use unique_ptr.", "The lesson: copy should copy, move should move. auto_ptr violated this.", "If you see auto_ptr in legacy code, replace it with unique_ptr"], "questions": [{"question": "Why was auto_ptr removed?", "answer": "Its copy constructor transferred ownership (moved), breaking the contract that copying should create an independent copy. This made it unsafe in containers (sorting copies elements) and generic code. unique_ptr, using C++11 move semantics, explicitly requires std::move for ownership transfer."}, {"question": "How do you migrate auto_ptr to unique_ptr?", "answer": "Replace std::auto_ptr with std::unique_ptr. Replace copies with std::move. The code will then explicitly show ownership transfers, and accidental copies will be compile-time errors instead of silent bugs."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_4_sub_5_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_4_sub_5_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_4_sub_5_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_4_sub_5_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `auto_ptr — Deprecated/Removed`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_4_sub_5_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_4_sub_5_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_4_sub_5_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_4_sub_5_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_4_sub_5_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_4_sub_5_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_4_sub_5", "day": 4, "index": 5}, {"title": "When Raw Pointer/Reference Is the Correct Non-Owning Choice", "explanation": "Not every pointer needs to be smart. Raw pointers and references are the correct choice for non-owning, observing access — when the pointer/reference doesn't control the lifetime of the object.\n\nUse raw pointer when: the target might be null, you need reseating, or you're interfacing with C APIs. Use reference when: the target is guaranteed to exist and you don't need null. The key rule: raw pointers/references should NEVER own (control lifetime of) resources.\n\nSmart pointers express ownership. Raw pointers/references express observation. This distinction is core to modern C++ design.", "examples": [{"title": "Ownership vs Observation", "code": "class Renderer\n{\n    // Renderer doesn't own the window — it just uses it\n    Window* window_;  // raw pointer: non-owning observer\npublic:\n    Renderer(Window* w) : window_(w) {}\n    void draw() { window_->clear(); /* ... */ }\n};\n\nclass Application\n{\n    std::unique_ptr<Window> window_;  // Application owns the window\n    std::unique_ptr<Renderer> renderer_;\npublic:\n    Application()\n    {\n        window_ = std::make_unique<Window>();\n        renderer_ = std::make_unique<Renderer>(window_.get());  // pass raw ptr\n    }\n};", "explanation": "unique_ptr for ownership, raw pointer for observation. Clear lifetime semantics.", "formatted_code": "class Renderer\n{\n    // Renderer doesn't own the window — it just uses it\n    Window* window_;  // raw pointer: non-owning observer\npublic:\n    Renderer(Window* w) : window_(w)\n    {\n    }\n    void draw()\n    {\n        window_->clear();\n        /* ... */\n    }\n};\n\nclass Application\n{\n    std::unique_ptr<Window> window_;  // Application owns the window\n    std::unique_ptr<Renderer> renderer_;\npublic:\n    Application()\n    {\n        window_ = std::make_unique<Window>();\n        renderer_ = std::make_unique<Renderer>(window_.get());  // pass raw ptr\n    }\n};"}], "pitfalls": ["Using raw pointer for ownership (calling delete on it)", "Passing unique_ptr when the callee doesn't need ownership", "Using shared_ptr everywhere 'just to be safe' — hides ownership semantics and adds overhead"], "tips_to_remember": ["Raw pointer = 'I don't own this'. Smart pointer = 'I own this'.", "Function parameters: `T*` or `T&` for non-owning. `unique_ptr<T> to transfer ownership. shared_ptr<T>` to share ownership.", "Returning unique_ptr signals 'caller now owns this'", "If the lifetime is guaranteed (stack object passed to function), use `const T&` or `T&`"], "questions": [{"question": "When should you use a raw pointer instead of a smart pointer?", "answer": "When the pointer is non-owning — it observes an object whose lifetime is managed elsewhere. Examples: function parameters that don't take ownership, back-pointers to a parent, pointers to objects with known-longer lifetime. The key: raw pointers never call delete."}, {"question": "What is the difference between T*, T&, unique_ptr<T>, and shared_ptr<T> as function parameters?", "answer": "T& or const T&: non-owning, target must exist. T*: non-owning, target may be null. unique_ptr<T>: transfers ownership to callee. const unique_ptr<T>&: observes but doesn't take ownership. shared_ptr<T>: shares ownership."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you pass an array `int arr[10]` to a function declared as `void process(int arr[])`?", "options": ["The array is passed by value, making a copy of all 10 integers.", "It decays to a raw pointer `int*`, and its size information is lost.", "The compiler verifies the size is exactly 10 and throws a warning if not.", "It is passed as a safe reference to the array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Arrays passed to functions decay to pointers. Inside `process`, `arr` is a raw pointer (`int*`), and `sizeof(arr)` returns the pointer size (e.g. 8 bytes), not the array size.", "id": "day_4_sub_6_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is a compiler error for `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["Moving it using `std::move`.", "Attempting to copy it via copy assignment or copy constructor.", "Resetting it using `.reset()`.", "Releasing ownership with `.release()`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unique_ptr` represents exclusive ownership. To prevent duplicate owners, copying is disabled (deleted copy constructor/assignment), causing a compilation error.", "id": "day_4_sub_6_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the outcome of a circular dependency where two objects contain `std::shared_ptr` pointing to each other?", "options": ["The compiler reports a cyclic template dependency error.", "The reference counts will never drop to zero, leaking both objects.", "The destructors are called normally due to automatic cycle detection.", "The program crashes with a stack overflow on deletion."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because each object holds a `std::shared_ptr` to the other, their reference counts remain at least 1, even after all external pointers are destroyed, leaking the memory.", "id": "day_4_sub_6_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How do you access the managed object of a `std::weak_ptr`?", "options": ["Dereference it directly using `*` or `->`.", "You must call `.lock()` to obtain a `std::shared_ptr` first.", "Call `.get()` to retrieve the raw pointer.", "It is not possible to access the object once it is in a weak pointer."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::weak_ptr` does not provide direct access. You must call `.lock()` which returns a `std::shared_ptr`. If the managed object has already been destroyed, the returned shared pointer will be empty.", "id": "day_4_sub_6_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of using `std::make_shared` instead of `std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)`?", "options": ["It makes the shared pointer thread-safe.", "It performs a single memory allocation for both the control block and the object, improving performance and cache locality.", "It allows using custom delete functions.", "It prevents compile-time template instantiation overhead."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)` allocates the object first, then allocates the control block. `std::make_shared` allocates a single block of memory big enough to hold both, reducing heap allocation overhead.", "id": "day_4_sub_6_mcq_4"}, {"question": "How does a custom deleter function pointer affect the size of a `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; it remains 8 bytes.", "It increases the size of the pointer (typically to 16 bytes) because it must store the function pointer.", "It causes the unique_ptr to allocate memory on the heap.", "It is a compile-time construct and does not exist at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a custom deleter is a function pointer, the unique_ptr must store it, increasing its size. Using a stateless functor (like a lambda with no captures) avoids this size overhead.", "id": "day_4_sub_6_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of placement `new` in C++?", "options": ["It automatically finds the best place in the heap to allocate memory.", "It constructs an object on a pre-allocated raw memory buffer without allocating new heap memory.", "It moves an object to another thread's stack space.", "It optimizes memory alignment dynamically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Placement `new` (e.g. `new (buffer) MyClass()`) constructs an object at a specific, pre-allocated memory address, avoiding heap allocation costs.", "id": "day_4_sub_6_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How must you destroy an object constructed via placement `new`?", "options": ["Call `delete ptr;`.", "Explicitly call the object's destructor (`ptr->~T()`) and then manually manage the buffer's memory.", "Let it go out of scope; the stack automatically deletes it.", "Call `free(ptr);`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because placement `new` did not allocate memory from the global heap, calling `delete` is undefined behavior. You must explicitly invoke the destructor and then clean up the raw buffer.", "id": "day_4_sub_6_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the undefined behavior hazard in the code `int* p = new int[10]; delete p;`?", "options": ["Using the wrong delete operator (`delete` instead of `delete[]`) is Undefined Behavior.", "There is no hazard; `delete` automatically knows it is an array.", "It is a compiler error and won't compile.", "It causes a stack overflow."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Memory allocated with `new[]` must be freed with `delete[]`. Using plain `delete` on an array pointer is undefined behavior, often causing heap corruption or failing to call destructors for elements 1 to 9.", "id": "day_4_sub_6_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the risk of returning a local variable by reference `const int&` from a function?", "options": ["It copies the variable unnecessarily.", "The local variable is destroyed at function exit, leaving a dangling reference, and accessing it is UB.", "It compiles only if the variable is marked `static`.", "It causes a memory leak on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Local variables have automatic storage duration (stack-allocated) and are destroyed when the function returns. Accessing a reference to a destroyed local variable is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_4_sub_6_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_4_sub_6", "day": 4, "index": 6}]}, {"day": 5, "topic": "Object-Oriented Programming", "estimated_hours": 7, "subtopics": [{"title": "Encapsulation, Abstraction, Inheritance, Polymorphism", "explanation": "The four pillars of OOP. Encapsulation: bundling data and methods, controlling access via public/protected/private. Abstraction: exposing a simplified interface, hiding implementation details. Inheritance: deriving new classes from existing ones, creating is-a relationships. Polymorphism: treating derived class objects through base class interfaces, with `virtual` dispatch selecting the correct implementation at runtime.\n\nC++ supports all four but also offers alternatives. Prefer composition over inheritance when you need a has-a relationship. Use templates for compile-time polymorphism (no `virtual` dispatch overhead).", "examples": [{"title": "Four Pillars", "code": "class Shape\n{\npublic:\n    virtual double area() const = 0;      // abstraction (pure interface)\n    virtual ~Shape() = default;\nprotected:\n    std::string color_;                   // encapsulation (protected data)\n};\n\nclass Circle : public Shape {            // inheritance\n    double radius_;\npublic:\n    Circle(double r) : radius_(r) {}\n    double area() const override { return 3.14159 * radius_ * radius_; }  // polymorphism\n};\n\nvoid printArea(const Shape& s) { std::cout << s.area(); }  // works with any Shape", "explanation": "Pure virtual functions define an interface. override ensures correct polymorphism.", "formatted_code": "class Shape\n{\npublic:\n    virtual double area() const = 0;  // abstraction (pure interface)\n    virtual ~Shape() = default;\nprotected:\n    std::string color_;  // encapsulation (protected data)\n};\n\nclass Circle : public Shape\n{  // inheritance\ndouble radius_;\npublic:\nCircle(double r) : radius_(r)\n{\n}\ndouble area() const override\n{\n    return 3.14159 * radius_ * radius_;\n}  // polymorphism\n};\n\nvoid printArea(const Shape& s)\n{\n    std::cout << s.area();\n}  // works with any Shape"}], "pitfalls": ["Making data members public — breaks encapsulation", "Deep inheritance hierarchies (more than 2-3 levels) — fragile base class problem", "Forgetting `override` keyword — may accidentally create a new function instead of overriding", "Slicing when passing derived by value to a function expecting base"], "tips_to_remember": ["Keep inheritance hierarchies shallow. Prefer composition.", "Always use `override` keyword when overriding `virtual` functions", "Make base class destructors `virtual`", "struct defaults to public access, class defaults to private"], "questions": [{"question": "What are the four pillars of OOP?", "answer": "Encapsulation (data hiding + access control), Abstraction (simplified interface hiding complexity), Inheritance (is-a relationships, code reuse), Polymorphism (treating derived objects through base interfaces, runtime dispatch)."}, {"question": "When should you use inheritance vs composition?", "answer": "Use inheritance for genuine is-a relationships (Circle is-a Shape). Use composition for has-a relationships (Car has-a Engine). Composition is more flexible, easier to test, and avoids the fragile base class problem."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you assign a `Derived` class object to a `Base` class object by value (`Base b = derivedObj;`)?", "options": ["A runtime error occurs because the sizes do not match.", "Object slicing occurs: the derived-specific data and vtable are stripped away, leaving a plain Base object.", "The Base object inherits the virtual table of the Derived object dynamically.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Assigning by value copies only the Base portion of the Derived object. This is called 'object slicing'. All derived members and polymorphic behavior are lost.", "id": "day_5_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you delete a `Derived` object via a `Base*` pointer when the Base class destructor is NOT marked `virtual`?", "options": ["The compiler generates a warning and inserts the virtual call automatically.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior; typically the Derived destructor is not called, causing resource leaks.", "The program compiles but throws a `std::bad_cast` at runtime.", "The memory is freed, and the destructors are called in reverse order anyway."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Deleting a derived object through a base pointer without a virtual destructor is undefined behavior. The derived destructor will not execute, leading to leaks of resources owned by the derived class.", "id": "day_5_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What happens if you call a pure virtual function inside the constructor of a Base class?", "options": ["It compiles and calls the Derived class implementation dynamically.", "It compiles but triggers Undefined Behavior (typically a crash) because the Derived part is not yet constructed and its vtable is not bound.", "The compiler forces the virtual function to be inline.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "During base class construction, the object is of the base type, not the derived type. Calling a virtual function calls the base implementation. If it is pure virtual, it has no implementation, leading to a 'pure virtual method call' crash (undefined behavior).", "id": "day_5_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the size of an empty class `class Empty {};` in C++?", "options": ["0 bytes, to save memory.", "At least 1 byte, to ensure different objects of this class have unique memory addresses.", "4 bytes, which is the default word size.", "8 bytes, because of vptr alignment."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ requires all objects to have unique memory addresses. An empty class is given a size of at least 1 byte so that `Empty a, b;` have distinct pointers `&a != &b`.", "id": "day_5_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What problem does virtual inheritance (`class Derived : virtual public Base`) solve?", "options": ["It makes all member functions virtual automatically.", "It resolves the diamond problem in multiple inheritance, ensuring only one instance of the Base class is shared.", "It speeds up dynamic cast lookup times.", "It prevents memory leaks in polymorphic interfaces."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Virtual inheritance ensures that only one shared instance of a common base class is constructed, resolving ambiguity and duplication in diamond inheritance topologies.", "id": "day_5_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What happens if you mark a member function as `override` but its signature does not match any virtual function in the Base class?", "options": ["The compiler compiles it as a new virtual function.", "It is a compilation error.", "The compiler ignores it and warns the developer.", "It runs slower at runtime due to search latency."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The `override` keyword forces the compiler to verify that the function matches an inherited virtual function. If it doesn't, it is a compilation error, preventing silent signature mismatch bugs.", "id": "day_5_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is a covariant return type in C++ virtual functions?", "options": ["A virtual function in Derived that returns a pointer/reference to Derived, while the Base virtual function returns a pointer/reference to Base.", "A return type that changes from `int` to `double` depending on runtime arguments.", "A template return type that adapts to compile-time parameters.", "An invalid return type that triggers a compilation error."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "C++ allows virtual functions to override base implementations with covariant return types. If the base returns a pointer/reference to `Base`, the override can return a pointer/reference to `Derived`.", "id": "day_5_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How does adding virtual functions affect the size of a class object?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; virtual functions are stored in the code segment.", "It adds the size of a virtual table pointer (`vptr`), typically 8 bytes on 64-bit systems.", "It multiplies the size of the class by the number of virtual functions.", "It adds 1 byte for each virtual function."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Adding virtual functions forces the compiler to insert a virtual table pointer (`vptr`) into each object of the class to resolve calls at runtime, adding 8 bytes on a 64-bit architecture.", "id": "day_5_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "Why is `dynamic_cast` significantly slower than `static_cast`?", "options": ["It performs file operations under the hood.", "It queries Run-Time Type Information (RTTI) and traverses the inheritance hierarchy at runtime to check safety.", "It copies the entire object in memory during casting.", "It disables compiler optimizations for the enclosing function."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`dynamic_cast` must check the validity of the cast at runtime by checking the class hierarchy (using RTTI metadata), which requires table lookups and pointer traversals, making it slower.", "id": "day_5_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of the Type Erasure design pattern (like `std::function`)?", "options": ["It deletes objects automatically when they are no longer used.", "It provides polymorphism for unrelated types without requiring a shared base class or virtual hierarchy.", "It compresses the size of types in memory.", "It converts types to string representations automatically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Type erasure hides the concrete type of an object behind a uniform interface (e.g. `std::function` hiding lambdas, function pointers, and functors) without requiring common base class inheritance.", "id": "day_5_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_5_sub_0", "day": 5, "index": 0}, {"title": "Virtual Functions — vtable/vptr Mechanics, Cost", "explanation": "When a class has `virtual` functions, the compiler creates a vtable (`virtual` function table) — an array of function pointers, one per `virtual` function. Each object of that class contains a hidden vptr (`virtual` pointer) pointing to the class's vtable. Virtual dispatch: call goes through vptr → vtable → actual function.\n\nCost: one pointer per object (vptr), one indirection per `virtual` call, potential cache miss when jumping to the function. For most code, this cost is negligible. For performance-critical hot loops, consider **CRTP** (`static` polymorphism) or templates.\n\n### Unforgettable Analogy / Deep Dive\nVtable is like a hotel reception desk directory. When a guest calls a polymorphic method (e.g. `draw()`), the compiler doesn't jump directly to a function. Instead, it looks up the directory index (`vptr` -> `vtable`) to see which function pointer is currently registered for that specific sub-class.", "examples": [{"title": "vtable Illustration", "code": "class Base\n{\npublic:\n    virtual void foo() { std::cout << \"Base::foo\\n\"; }\n    virtual void bar() { std::cout << \"Base::bar\\n\"; }\n    virtual ~Base() = default;\n    // Object layout: [vptr | data...]\n    // vtable: [&Base::foo, &Base::bar, &Base::~Base]\n};\n\nclass Derived : public Base {\npublic:\n    void foo() override { std::cout << \"Derived::foo\\n\"; }\n    // vtable: [&Derived::foo, &Base::bar, &Derived::~Derived]\n};\n\nBase* p = new Derived();\np->foo();  // vptr → Derived's vtable → Derived::foo\np->bar();  // vptr → Derived's vtable → Base::bar (not overridden)\ndelete p;", "explanation": "Each class gets its own vtable. The vptr in the object points to its class's vtable.", "formatted_code": "class Base\n{\npublic:\n    virtual void foo()\n    {\n        std::cout << \"Base::foo\\n\";\n    }\n    virtual void bar()\n    {\n        std::cout << \"Base::bar\\n\";\n    }\n    virtual ~Base() = default;\n    // Object layout: [vptr | data...]\n    // vtable: [&Base::foo, &Base::bar, &Base::~Base]\n};\n\nclass Derived : public Base\n{\npublic:\n    void foo() override\n    {\n        std::cout << \"Derived::foo\\n\";\n    }\n    // vtable: [&Derived::foo, &Base::bar, &Derived::~Derived]\n};\n\nBase* p = new Derived();\np->foo();  // vptr → Derived's vtable → Derived::foo\np->bar();  // vptr → Derived's vtable → Base::bar (not overridden)\ndelete p;"}], "pitfalls": ["Calling `virtual` functions in constructors/destructors — dispatches to the current class, not the `final` derived class", "Performance: `virtual` calls prevent inlining and cause indirect branch mispredictions", "Adding first `virtual` function to a class increases object size by `sizeof(void*)`"], "tips_to_remember": ["Virtual dispatch goes: object.vptr → class vtable → function pointer → call", "Virtual functions in constructors/destructors dispatch to the constructing/destructing class", "Cost: ~5ns per `virtual` call on modern CPUs. Usually negligible. Profile before optimizing.", "`final` keyword can help the compiler devirtualize"], "questions": [{"question": "How does virtual dispatch work?", "answer": "Each polymorphic object has a hidden vptr pointing to its class's vtable (array of function pointers). A virtual call: 1) load vptr from object, 2) index into vtable, 3) call through function pointer. This allows runtime polymorphism."}, {"question": "What is the overhead of virtual functions?", "answer": "One vptr per object (typically 8 bytes on 64-bit). One indirection per call (2 memory loads vs 0 for non-virtual). Can prevent inlining. Can cause cache misses. Usually negligible but can matter in tight loops."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you assign a `Derived` class object to a `Base` class object by value (`Base b = derivedObj;`)?", "options": ["A runtime error occurs because the sizes do not match.", "Object slicing occurs: the derived-specific data and vtable are stripped away, leaving a plain Base object.", "The Base object inherits the virtual table of the Derived object dynamically.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Assigning by value copies only the Base portion of the Derived object. This is called 'object slicing'. All derived members and polymorphic behavior are lost.", "id": "day_5_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you delete a `Derived` object via a `Base*` pointer when the Base class destructor is NOT marked `virtual`?", "options": ["The compiler generates a warning and inserts the virtual call automatically.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior; typically the Derived destructor is not called, causing resource leaks.", "The program compiles but throws a `std::bad_cast` at runtime.", "The memory is freed, and the destructors are called in reverse order anyway."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Deleting a derived object through a base pointer without a virtual destructor is undefined behavior. The derived destructor will not execute, leading to leaks of resources owned by the derived class.", "id": "day_5_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What happens if you call a pure virtual function inside the constructor of a Base class?", "options": ["It compiles and calls the Derived class implementation dynamically.", "It compiles but triggers Undefined Behavior (typically a crash) because the Derived part is not yet constructed and its vtable is not bound.", "The compiler forces the virtual function to be inline.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "During base class construction, the object is of the base type, not the derived type. Calling a virtual function calls the base implementation. If it is pure virtual, it has no implementation, leading to a 'pure virtual method call' crash (undefined behavior).", "id": "day_5_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the size of an empty class `class Empty {};` in C++?", "options": ["0 bytes, to save memory.", "At least 1 byte, to ensure different objects of this class have unique memory addresses.", "4 bytes, which is the default word size.", "8 bytes, because of vptr alignment."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ requires all objects to have unique memory addresses. An empty class is given a size of at least 1 byte so that `Empty a, b;` have distinct pointers `&a != &b`.", "id": "day_5_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What problem does virtual inheritance (`class Derived : virtual public Base`) solve?", "options": ["It makes all member functions virtual automatically.", "It resolves the diamond problem in multiple inheritance, ensuring only one instance of the Base class is shared.", "It speeds up dynamic cast lookup times.", "It prevents memory leaks in polymorphic interfaces."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Virtual inheritance ensures that only one shared instance of a common base class is constructed, resolving ambiguity and duplication in diamond inheritance topologies.", "id": "day_5_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What happens if you mark a member function as `override` but its signature does not match any virtual function in the Base class?", "options": ["The compiler compiles it as a new virtual function.", "It is a compilation error.", "The compiler ignores it and warns the developer.", "It runs slower at runtime due to search latency."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The `override` keyword forces the compiler to verify that the function matches an inherited virtual function. If it doesn't, it is a compilation error, preventing silent signature mismatch bugs.", "id": "day_5_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is a covariant return type in C++ virtual functions?", "options": ["A virtual function in Derived that returns a pointer/reference to Derived, while the Base virtual function returns a pointer/reference to Base.", "A return type that changes from `int` to `double` depending on runtime arguments.", "A template return type that adapts to compile-time parameters.", "An invalid return type that triggers a compilation error."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "C++ allows virtual functions to override base implementations with covariant return types. If the base returns a pointer/reference to `Base`, the override can return a pointer/reference to `Derived`.", "id": "day_5_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How does adding virtual functions affect the size of a class object?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; virtual functions are stored in the code segment.", "It adds the size of a virtual table pointer (`vptr`), typically 8 bytes on 64-bit systems.", "It multiplies the size of the class by the number of virtual functions.", "It adds 1 byte for each virtual function."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Adding virtual functions forces the compiler to insert a virtual table pointer (`vptr`) into each object of the class to resolve calls at runtime, adding 8 bytes on a 64-bit architecture.", "id": "day_5_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "Why is `dynamic_cast` significantly slower than `static_cast`?", "options": ["It performs file operations under the hood.", "It queries Run-Time Type Information (RTTI) and traverses the inheritance hierarchy at runtime to check safety.", "It copies the entire object in memory during casting.", "It disables compiler optimizations for the enclosing function."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`dynamic_cast` must check the validity of the cast at runtime by checking the class hierarchy (using RTTI metadata), which requires table lookups and pointer traversals, making it slower.", "id": "day_5_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of the Type Erasure design pattern (like `std::function`)?", "options": ["It deletes objects automatically when they are no longer used.", "It provides polymorphism for unrelated types without requiring a shared base class or virtual hierarchy.", "It compresses the size of types in memory.", "It converts types to string representations automatically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Type erasure hides the concrete type of an object behind a uniform interface (e.g. `std::function` hiding lambdas, function pointers, and functors) without requiring common base class inheritance.", "id": "day_5_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_5_sub_1", "day": 5, "index": 1}, {"title": "Multiple Inheritance — Diamond Problem, Virtual Inheritance", "explanation": "C++ allows a class to inherit from multiple base classes. The diamond problem occurs when a class inherits from two classes that share a common base — the derived class gets two copies of the common base's members. Virtual inheritance solves this by ensuring only one copy of the common base exists.\n\nVirtual inheritance adds overhead (extra vptr for `virtual` base) and complexity. It's used in the standard library (`std::iostream` inherits from both istream and ostream, which both virtually inherit from ios_base).", "examples": [{"title": "Diamond Problem and Virtual Inheritance", "code": "struct Animal { int weight; };\nstruct Dog : virtual Animal {};    // virtual inheritance\nstruct Cat : virtual Animal {};    // virtual inheritance\nstruct DogCat : Dog, Cat {};       // only ONE Animal subobject\n\nDogCat dc;\ndc.weight = 10;  // unambiguous — only one Animal::weight\n\n// Without virtual:\nstruct Dog2 : Animal {};\nstruct Cat2 : Animal {};\nstruct DogCat2 : Dog2, Cat2 {};\n// dc2.weight is ambiguous — two Animal subobjects!", "explanation": "Virtual inheritance ensures only one copy of the shared base class.", "formatted_code": "struct Animal\n{\n    int weight;\n};\nstruct Dog : virtual Animal\n{\n};  // virtual inheritance\nstruct Cat : virtual Animal\n{\n};  // virtual inheritance\nstruct DogCat : Dog, Cat\n{\n};  // only ONE Animal subobject\n\nDogCat dc;\ndc.weight = 10;  // unambiguous — only one Animal::weight\n\n// Without virtual:\nstruct Dog2 : Animal\n{\n};\nstruct Cat2 : Animal\n{\n};\nstruct DogCat2 : Dog2, Cat2\n{\n};\n// dc2.weight is ambiguous — two Animal subobjects!"}], "pitfalls": ["Forgetting `virtual` keyword on intermediate classes — both bases get separate copies", "Virtual inheritance adds vtable overhead and slows down casting", "Constructor initialization: most derived class must initialize `virtual` bases directly"], "tips_to_remember": ["Use `virtual` inheritance ONLY when diamond problem exists", "Most derived class is responsible for calling `virtual` base constructors", "Prefer composition over multiple inheritance when possible", "`dynamic_cast` is needed for cross-casts with `virtual` inheritance"], "questions": [{"question": "What is the diamond problem?", "answer": "When class D inherits from B and C, which both inherit from A, D gets two copies of A's members, causing ambiguity. Virtual inheritance makes B and C virtually inherit from A, ensuring D has only one copy of A."}, {"question": "What are the costs of virtual inheritance?", "answer": "Extra vptr/offset for accessing the virtual base subobject. More complex constructor ordering (most derived class must initialize virtual bases). Slower dynamic_cast and upcasting. Generally avoid unless the diamond problem requires it."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you assign a `Derived` class object to a `Base` class object by value (`Base b = derivedObj;`)?", "options": ["A runtime error occurs because the sizes do not match.", "Object slicing occurs: the derived-specific data and vtable are stripped away, leaving a plain Base object.", "The Base object inherits the virtual table of the Derived object dynamically.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Assigning by value copies only the Base portion of the Derived object. This is called 'object slicing'. All derived members and polymorphic behavior are lost.", "id": "day_5_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you delete a `Derived` object via a `Base*` pointer when the Base class destructor is NOT marked `virtual`?", "options": ["The compiler generates a warning and inserts the virtual call automatically.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior; typically the Derived destructor is not called, causing resource leaks.", "The program compiles but throws a `std::bad_cast` at runtime.", "The memory is freed, and the destructors are called in reverse order anyway."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Deleting a derived object through a base pointer without a virtual destructor is undefined behavior. The derived destructor will not execute, leading to leaks of resources owned by the derived class.", "id": "day_5_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What happens if you call a pure virtual function inside the constructor of a Base class?", "options": ["It compiles and calls the Derived class implementation dynamically.", "It compiles but triggers Undefined Behavior (typically a crash) because the Derived part is not yet constructed and its vtable is not bound.", "The compiler forces the virtual function to be inline.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "During base class construction, the object is of the base type, not the derived type. Calling a virtual function calls the base implementation. If it is pure virtual, it has no implementation, leading to a 'pure virtual method call' crash (undefined behavior).", "id": "day_5_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the size of an empty class `class Empty {};` in C++?", "options": ["0 bytes, to save memory.", "At least 1 byte, to ensure different objects of this class have unique memory addresses.", "4 bytes, which is the default word size.", "8 bytes, because of vptr alignment."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ requires all objects to have unique memory addresses. An empty class is given a size of at least 1 byte so that `Empty a, b;` have distinct pointers `&a != &b`.", "id": "day_5_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What problem does virtual inheritance (`class Derived : virtual public Base`) solve?", "options": ["It makes all member functions virtual automatically.", "It resolves the diamond problem in multiple inheritance, ensuring only one instance of the Base class is shared.", "It speeds up dynamic cast lookup times.", "It prevents memory leaks in polymorphic interfaces."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Virtual inheritance ensures that only one shared instance of a common base class is constructed, resolving ambiguity and duplication in diamond inheritance topologies.", "id": "day_5_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What happens if you mark a member function as `override` but its signature does not match any virtual function in the Base class?", "options": ["The compiler compiles it as a new virtual function.", "It is a compilation error.", "The compiler ignores it and warns the developer.", "It runs slower at runtime due to search latency."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The `override` keyword forces the compiler to verify that the function matches an inherited virtual function. If it doesn't, it is a compilation error, preventing silent signature mismatch bugs.", "id": "day_5_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is a covariant return type in C++ virtual functions?", "options": ["A virtual function in Derived that returns a pointer/reference to Derived, while the Base virtual function returns a pointer/reference to Base.", "A return type that changes from `int` to `double` depending on runtime arguments.", "A template return type that adapts to compile-time parameters.", "An invalid return type that triggers a compilation error."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "C++ allows virtual functions to override base implementations with covariant return types. If the base returns a pointer/reference to `Base`, the override can return a pointer/reference to `Derived`.", "id": "day_5_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How does adding virtual functions affect the size of a class object?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; virtual functions are stored in the code segment.", "It adds the size of a virtual table pointer (`vptr`), typically 8 bytes on 64-bit systems.", "It multiplies the size of the class by the number of virtual functions.", "It adds 1 byte for each virtual function."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Adding virtual functions forces the compiler to insert a virtual table pointer (`vptr`) into each object of the class to resolve calls at runtime, adding 8 bytes on a 64-bit architecture.", "id": "day_5_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "Why is `dynamic_cast` significantly slower than `static_cast`?", "options": ["It performs file operations under the hood.", "It queries Run-Time Type Information (RTTI) and traverses the inheritance hierarchy at runtime to check safety.", "It copies the entire object in memory during casting.", "It disables compiler optimizations for the enclosing function."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`dynamic_cast` must check the validity of the cast at runtime by checking the class hierarchy (using RTTI metadata), which requires table lookups and pointer traversals, making it slower.", "id": "day_5_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of the Type Erasure design pattern (like `std::function`)?", "options": ["It deletes objects automatically when they are no longer used.", "It provides polymorphism for unrelated types without requiring a shared base class or virtual hierarchy.", "It compresses the size of types in memory.", "It converts types to string representations automatically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Type erasure hides the concrete type of an object behind a uniform interface (e.g. `std::function` hiding lambdas, function pointers, and functors) without requiring common base class inheritance.", "id": "day_5_sub_2_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_5_sub_2", "day": 5, "index": 2}, {"title": "Rule of Three / Five / Zero", "explanation": "Rule of Three (C++98): If you define any of destructor, copy constructor, or copy assignment, you probably need all three. Rule of Five (C++11): add move constructor and move assignment. Rule of Zero: prefer to use **RAII** members (smart pointers, containers) so you don't need any custom special members.\n\nThe Rule of Zero is the modern ideal: your class has data members that manage themselves, so compiler-generated defaults do the right thing.", "examples": [{"title": "Rule of Five", "code": "class ResourceOwner\n{\n    int* data_;\n    size_t size_;\npublic:\n    ResourceOwner(size_t n) : data_(new int[n]), size_(n) {}\n    ~ResourceOwner() { delete[] data_; }\n    ResourceOwner(const ResourceOwner& other)  // copy ctor\n        : data_(new int[other.size_]), size_(other.size_)\n        {\n        std::copy(other.data_, other.data_ + size_, data_);\n    }\n    ResourceOwner& operator=(const ResourceOwner& other) // copy assign\n    {\n        if (this != &other)\n        {\n            delete[] data_;\n            data_ = new int[other.size_];\n            size_ = other.size_;\n            std::copy(other.data_, other.data_ + size_, data_);\n        }\n        return *this;\n    }\n    ResourceOwner(ResourceOwner&& other) noexcept  // move ctor\n        : data_(other.data_), size_(other.size_)\n        {\n        other.data_ = nullptr; other.size_ = 0;\n    }\n    ResourceOwner& operator=(ResourceOwner&& other) noexcept // move assign\n    {\n        if (this != &other) { delete[] data_; data_ = other.data_; size_ = other.size_;\n            other.data_ = nullptr; other.size_ = 0; }\n        return *this;\n    }\n};\n\n// Rule of Zero alternative:\nclass BetterOwner\n{\n    std::vector<int> data_;  // vector handles everything!\n};", "explanation": "Rule of Five requires all 5 special members. Rule of Zero uses RAII members instead.", "formatted_code": "class ResourceOwner\n{\n    int* data_;\n    size_t size_;\npublic:\n    ResourceOwner(size_t n) : data_(new int[n]), size_(n)\n    {\n    }\n    ~ResourceOwner()\n    {\n        delete[] data_;\n    }\n    ResourceOwner(const ResourceOwner& other)  // copy ctor\n    : data_(new int[other.size_]), size_(other.size_)\n    {\n        std::copy(other.data_, other.data_ + size_, data_);\n    }\n    ResourceOwner& operator=(const ResourceOwner& other)  // copy assign\n    {\n        if (this != &other)\n        {\n            delete[] data_;\n            data_ = new int[other.size_];\n            size_ = other.size_;\n            std::copy(other.data_, other.data_ + size_, data_);\n        }\n        return *this;\n    }\n    ResourceOwner(ResourceOwner&& other) noexcept  // move ctor\n    : data_(other.data_), size_(other.size_)\n    {\n        other.data_ = nullptr;\n        other.size_ = 0;\n    }\n    ResourceOwner& operator=(ResourceOwner&& other) noexcept  // move assign\n    {\n        if (this != &other)\n        {\n            delete[] data_;\n            data_ = other.data_;\n            size_ = other.size_;\n            other.data_ = nullptr;\n            other.size_ = 0;\n        }\n        return *this;\n    }\n};\n\n// Rule of Zero alternative:\nclass BetterOwner\n{\n    std::vector<int> data_;  // vector handles everything!\n};"}], "pitfalls": ["Defining destructor without copy/move — compiler may generate shallow copies that double-free", "Missing `noexcept` on move operations — containers won't use move", "Rule of Five but forgetting self-assignment check in copy assignment"], "tips_to_remember": ["Rule of Zero is ideal: use smart pointers and containers as members", "If you must define one of the 5, define all 5 (or = delete the ones you don't want)", "Move operations should be `noexcept`", "= default and = delete to be explicit about special member functions"], "questions": [{"question": "What is the Rule of Zero?", "answer": "Design classes so they don't need custom destructors, copy/move constructors, or copy/move assignment. Use RAII members (unique_ptr, vector, string) that handle their own resources. The compiler-generated defaults do the right thing."}, {"question": "Why must move operations be noexcept?", "answer": "std::vector and other containers only use move operations during reallocation if they're noexcept. If move might throw, the container falls back to copying to maintain the strong exception safety guarantee."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_5_sub_3_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_5_sub_3_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_5_sub_3_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_5_sub_3_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Rule of Three / Five / Zero`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_5_sub_3_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_5_sub_3_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_5_sub_3_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_5_sub_3_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_5_sub_3_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_5_sub_3_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_5_sub_3", "day": 5, "index": 3}, {"title": "CRTP — Static Polymorphism Without Virtual Dispatch", "explanation": "The **Curiously Recurring Template Pattern**: a class derives from a `template` base instantiated with itself: class Derived : `Base<Derived>`. The base class can call derived class methods at compile time (via ``static_cast<Derived*>`(this)`), avoiding `virtual` dispatch overhead.\n\nUsed for: `static` polymorphism, mixin functionality, enabling the 'this' type to be known in the base class. `std::enable_shared_from_this` uses **CRTP**.", "examples": [{"title": "CRTP Example", "code": "template<typename Derived>\nclass Shape\n{\npublic:\n    double area() const\n    {\n        return static_cast<const Derived*>(this)->area_impl();\n    }\n};\n\nclass Circle : public Shape<Circle> {\n    double r_;\npublic:\n    Circle(double r) : r_(r) {}\n    double area_impl() const { return 3.14159 * r_ * r_; }\n};\n\nclass Square : public Shape<Square> {\n    double s_;\npublic:\n    Square(double s) : s_(s) {}\n    double area_impl() const { return s_ * s_; }\n};\n\n// No virtual dispatch — resolved at compile time\ntemplate<typename S>\nvoid print(const Shape<S>& s) { std::cout << s.area(); }", "explanation": "CRTP enables compile-time polymorphism — no vtable, no virtual dispatch overhead.", "formatted_code": "template<typename Derived>\nclass Shape\n{\npublic:\n    double area() const\n    {\n        return static_cast<const Derived*>(this)->area_impl();\n    }\n};\n\nclass Circle : public Shape<Circle>\n{\n    double r_;\npublic:\n    Circle(double r) : r_(r)\n    {\n    }\n    double area_impl() const\n    {\n        return 3.14159 * r_ * r_;\n    }\n};\n\nclass Square : public Shape<Square>\n{\n    double s_;\npublic:\n    Square(double s) : s_(s)\n    {\n    }\n    double area_impl() const\n    {\n        return s_ * s_;\n    }\n};\n\n// No virtual dispatch — resolved at compile time\ntemplate<typename S>\nvoid print(const Shape<S>& s)\n{\n    std::cout << s.area();\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["**CRTP** doesn't provide runtime polymorphism — can't store different types in a container", "Accidentally passing wrong derived type to `template`: class Foo : `Base<Bar>` — hard to catch", "More complex code than `virtual` functions for similar functionality"], "tips_to_remember": ["Use **CRTP** when performance is critical and you don't need runtime polymorphism", "**CRTP** is compile-time polymorphism — each `Shape<Circle>, Shape<Square>` is a different type", "Consider C++20 concepts as a simpler alternative for compile-time polymorphism"], "questions": [{"question": "What is CRTP?", "answer": "Curiously Recurring Template Pattern: class Derived : Base<Derived>. The base class knows the derived type at compile time and can call its methods without virtual dispatch. Used for static polymorphism, mixins, and type-aware base classes."}, {"question": "When to use CRTP vs virtual functions?", "answer": "CRTP: when performance matters, when you don't need to store heterogeneous types in a container, when all types are known at compile time. Virtual functions: when you need runtime polymorphism, when types are only known at runtime, when code simplicity matters more than performance."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you assign a `Derived` class object to a `Base` class object by value (`Base b = derivedObj;`)?", "options": ["A runtime error occurs because the sizes do not match.", "Object slicing occurs: the derived-specific data and vtable are stripped away, leaving a plain Base object.", "The Base object inherits the virtual table of the Derived object dynamically.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Assigning by value copies only the Base portion of the Derived object. This is called 'object slicing'. All derived members and polymorphic behavior are lost.", "id": "day_5_sub_4_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you delete a `Derived` object via a `Base*` pointer when the Base class destructor is NOT marked `virtual`?", "options": ["The compiler generates a warning and inserts the virtual call automatically.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior; typically the Derived destructor is not called, causing resource leaks.", "The program compiles but throws a `std::bad_cast` at runtime.", "The memory is freed, and the destructors are called in reverse order anyway."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Deleting a derived object through a base pointer without a virtual destructor is undefined behavior. The derived destructor will not execute, leading to leaks of resources owned by the derived class.", "id": "day_5_sub_4_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What happens if you call a pure virtual function inside the constructor of a Base class?", "options": ["It compiles and calls the Derived class implementation dynamically.", "It compiles but triggers Undefined Behavior (typically a crash) because the Derived part is not yet constructed and its vtable is not bound.", "The compiler forces the virtual function to be inline.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "During base class construction, the object is of the base type, not the derived type. Calling a virtual function calls the base implementation. If it is pure virtual, it has no implementation, leading to a 'pure virtual method call' crash (undefined behavior).", "id": "day_5_sub_4_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the size of an empty class `class Empty {};` in C++?", "options": ["0 bytes, to save memory.", "At least 1 byte, to ensure different objects of this class have unique memory addresses.", "4 bytes, which is the default word size.", "8 bytes, because of vptr alignment."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ requires all objects to have unique memory addresses. An empty class is given a size of at least 1 byte so that `Empty a, b;` have distinct pointers `&a != &b`.", "id": "day_5_sub_4_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What problem does virtual inheritance (`class Derived : virtual public Base`) solve?", "options": ["It makes all member functions virtual automatically.", "It resolves the diamond problem in multiple inheritance, ensuring only one instance of the Base class is shared.", "It speeds up dynamic cast lookup times.", "It prevents memory leaks in polymorphic interfaces."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Virtual inheritance ensures that only one shared instance of a common base class is constructed, resolving ambiguity and duplication in diamond inheritance topologies.", "id": "day_5_sub_4_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What happens if you mark a member function as `override` but its signature does not match any virtual function in the Base class?", "options": ["The compiler compiles it as a new virtual function.", "It is a compilation error.", "The compiler ignores it and warns the developer.", "It runs slower at runtime due to search latency."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The `override` keyword forces the compiler to verify that the function matches an inherited virtual function. If it doesn't, it is a compilation error, preventing silent signature mismatch bugs.", "id": "day_5_sub_4_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is a covariant return type in C++ virtual functions?", "options": ["A virtual function in Derived that returns a pointer/reference to Derived, while the Base virtual function returns a pointer/reference to Base.", "A return type that changes from `int` to `double` depending on runtime arguments.", "A template return type that adapts to compile-time parameters.", "An invalid return type that triggers a compilation error."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "C++ allows virtual functions to override base implementations with covariant return types. If the base returns a pointer/reference to `Base`, the override can return a pointer/reference to `Derived`.", "id": "day_5_sub_4_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How does adding virtual functions affect the size of a class object?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; virtual functions are stored in the code segment.", "It adds the size of a virtual table pointer (`vptr`), typically 8 bytes on 64-bit systems.", "It multiplies the size of the class by the number of virtual functions.", "It adds 1 byte for each virtual function."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Adding virtual functions forces the compiler to insert a virtual table pointer (`vptr`) into each object of the class to resolve calls at runtime, adding 8 bytes on a 64-bit architecture.", "id": "day_5_sub_4_mcq_7"}, {"question": "Why is `dynamic_cast` significantly slower than `static_cast`?", "options": ["It performs file operations under the hood.", "It queries Run-Time Type Information (RTTI) and traverses the inheritance hierarchy at runtime to check safety.", "It copies the entire object in memory during casting.", "It disables compiler optimizations for the enclosing function."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`dynamic_cast` must check the validity of the cast at runtime by checking the class hierarchy (using RTTI metadata), which requires table lookups and pointer traversals, making it slower.", "id": "day_5_sub_4_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of the Type Erasure design pattern (like `std::function`)?", "options": ["It deletes objects automatically when they are no longer used.", "It provides polymorphism for unrelated types without requiring a shared base class or virtual hierarchy.", "It compresses the size of types in memory.", "It converts types to string representations automatically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Type erasure hides the concrete type of an object behind a uniform interface (e.g. `std::function` hiding lambdas, function pointers, and functors) without requiring common base class inheritance.", "id": "day_5_sub_4_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_5_sub_4", "day": 5, "index": 4}]}, {"day": 6, "topic": "Move Semantics", "estimated_hours": 5, "subtopics": [{"title": "Rvalue References, std::move, std::forward, Perfect Forwarding", "explanation": "An rvalue reference (`T&&`) can bind to rvalues (temporaries, `std::move`'d objects). `std::move` is just `static_cast<T&&>` — it casts an lvalue to an xvalue, signaling it's safe to move from. It doesn't actually move anything.\n\nA 'forwarding reference' (also called 'universal reference') is `T&&` where T is a deduced `template` parameter. It can bind to both lvalues and rvalues due to reference collapsing rules. `std::forward<T>` preserves the original value category — forwards lvalues as lvalues and rvalues as rvalues. This enables 'perfect forwarding'.\n\nReference collapsing rules: `T&` & → `T&`, `T&` && → `T&`, `T&&` & → `T&`, `T&&` && → `T&&`. When an lvalue of type X is passed to `template<typename T>` void `f(T&&)`, T deduces as `X&` and `T&&` collapses to `X&`.", "examples": [{"title": "std::move and std::forward", "code": "// std::move: unconditional cast to rvalue\nstd::string s = \"hello\";\nstd::string s2 = std::move(s);  // s is now in valid but unspecified state\n\n// Perfect forwarding:\ntemplate<typename T>\nvoid wrapper(T&& arg)\n{\n    target(std::forward<T>(arg));  // preserves lvalue/rvalue-ness\n}\n\nstd::string x = \"test\";\nwrapper(x);             // T=string&, forwards as lvalue\nwrapper(std::move(x));  // T=string, forwards as rvalue\nwrapper(\"literal\");     // T=const char(&)[8], forwards as lvalue", "explanation": "std::move casts to rvalue. std::forward preserves the original value category.", "formatted_code": "// std::move: unconditional cast to rvalue\nstd::string s = \"hello\";\nstd::string s2 = std::move(s);  // s is now in valid but unspecified state\n\n// Perfect forwarding:\ntemplate<typename T>\nvoid wrapper(T&& arg)\n{\n    target(std::forward<T>(arg));  // preserves lvalue/rvalue-ness\n}\n\nstd::string x = \"test\";\nwrapper(x);  // T=string&, forwards as lvalue\nwrapper(std::move(x));  // T=string, forwards as rvalue\nwrapper(\"literal\");  // T=const char(&)[8], forwards as lvalue"}, {"title": "Reference Collapsing", "code": "// T&&  where T is deduced:\n// If you pass lvalue (X&):  T = X&,   T&& = X& && = X&   (lvalue ref)\n// If you pass rvalue (X&&): T = X,    T&& = X&&          (rvalue ref)\n// This is why T&& in templates is called a 'forwarding reference'", "explanation": "Reference collapsing is the mechanism behind forwarding references.", "formatted_code": "// T&&  where T is deduced:\n// If you pass lvalue (X&):  T = X&,   T&& = X& && = X&   (lvalue ref)\n// If you pass rvalue (X&&): T = X,    T&& = X&&          (rvalue ref)\n// This is why T&& in templates is called a 'forwarding reference'"}], "pitfalls": ["Using `std::move` on const objects — returns `const T&&`, which matches copy constructor, not move", "Forgetting that a named rvalue reference is an lvalue", "Using `std::move` when `std::forward` is needed (in forwarding contexts)", "Moving from an object and then using it (valid but unspecified state)", "`std::move` doesn't move — it's just a cast"], "tips_to_remember": ["`std::move` = unconditional cast to `T&&`. `std::forward` = conditional cast based on T", "After `std::move`, the object is in a 'valid but unspecified state' — only safe to assign to or destroy", "`T&&` is an rvalue reference when T is a specific type, but a forwarding reference when T is deduced", "Never `std::move` a return value — it prevents copy elision"], "questions": [{"question": "What does std::move actually do?", "answer": "std::move is static_cast<std::remove_reference_t<T>&&>(arg) — it casts its argument to an xvalue (rvalue reference). It doesn't move anything. The actual move happens when a move constructor or move assignment receives this xvalue."}, {"question": "What is perfect forwarding?", "answer": "Forwarding an argument to another function while preserving its original value category (lvalue or rvalue). Achieved with forwarding references (T&&) and std::forward<T>. Without it, wrapping functions would always pass arguments as lvalues."}, {"question": "What is a forwarding reference?", "answer": "T&& where T is a deduced template parameter. Due to reference collapsing, it can bind to both lvalues (deducing T as X&) and rvalues (deducing T as X). Also called 'universal reference'. Not the same as a plain rvalue reference like string&&."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens if you bind a temporary object (prvalue) to a `const T&` reference?", "options": ["The temporary is destroyed at the end of the expression, leaving a dangling reference.", "The lifetime of the temporary is extended to match the lifetime of the reference.", "The compiler makes a permanent copy of the temporary on the heap.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ guarantees that binding a temporary object to a const lvalue reference extends the lifetime of that temporary to match the lifetime of the reference itself, preventing dangling reference bugs.", "id": "day_6_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you try to modify a member variable in a `const` member function in `Rvalue References, std::move, std::forward, Perfect Forwarding`?", "options": ["It compiles fine, but triggers a segmentation fault at runtime.", "Compilation fails, unless the member variable is marked `mutable`.", "It compiles fine, but the changes are discarded when the function exits.", "It compiles fine only if the class is not allocated on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A `const` member function promises not to modify any observable state of the object. Modifying any member variable inside it is a compilation error, unless the member is declared with the `mutable` keyword.", "id": "day_6_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Can you initialize a `constexpr` variable using the return value of a non-constexpr runtime function?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler will defer evaluation to runtime.", "Yes, if the function has no side effects.", "No, compilation will fail because a `constexpr` value must be known at compile time.", "Yes, but only if compiler optimization is turned on."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "`constexpr` variables must be initialized with constant expressions that can be evaluated at compile time. Initializing one with a runtime value results in a compilation error.", "id": "day_6_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the consequence of calling a `consteval` function with non-constant (runtime) arguments?", "options": ["The function is executed at runtime instead.", "The compiler fails to compile the call.", "The compiler generates a runtime check that throws an exception if arguments are invalid.", "The return value is undefined."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`consteval` (introduced in C++20) specifies an immediate function. Immediate functions MUST be evaluated at compile time. Calling them with runtime values is a compilation error.", "id": "day_6_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Can a `constinit` variable be modified after initialization during runtime?", "options": ["No, `constinit` variables are implicitly `const`.", "Yes, `constinit` only requires compile-time initialization, not runtime immutability.", "Yes, but only inside `consteval` functions.", "No, modifying it triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constinit` (C++20) ensures that a variable is initialized at compile time (preventing static initialization order issues), but it does not make the variable constant. To make it constant, you must explicitly combine it with `const`: `constinit const int x = 42;`.", "id": "day_6_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Is it safe to use `static_cast` to cast a `Base*` to a `Derived*`?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler checks the actual runtime type and returns `nullptr` on failure.", "Yes, but it is compile-time checked only. If the pointer is not actually of the Derived type, dereferencing it is UB.", "No, it is a compilation error. You must use `dynamic_cast`.", "Yes, `static_cast` automatically handles polymorphic conversions safely."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`static_cast` performs compile-time conversions. It does not perform any runtime checks. Casting a base pointer to a derived pointer with `static_cast` is unsafe: if the object is not of the derived type, dereferencing the casted pointer is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_6_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast` do if it fails to cast a reference type `Base&` to `Derived&`?", "options": ["It returns a null reference.", "It throws a `std::bad_cast` exception.", "It aborts the program immediately.", "It sets the reference to an empty object."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since there is no such thing as a null reference in C++, `dynamic_cast` cannot return null when casting reference types. Instead, it throws a `std::bad_cast` exception on failure.", "id": "day_6_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "If you cast an `int*` to a `float*` using `reinterpret_cast` and dereference it to read the value, what is the result?", "options": ["It returns the floating-point representation of the integer bits.", "It is a compiler error.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior due to the strict aliasing rule.", "It triggers a hardware trap."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "The strict aliasing rule states that you cannot access an object of one type through a pointer of a different type (with exceptions like `char*` or signed/unsigned variants). Violating this using `reinterpret_cast` triggers undefined behavior.", "id": "day_6_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "If both `void print(int)` and `template<typename T> void print(T)` match a call `print(42)` equally well, which one is selected by overload resolution?", "options": ["The template function, because it is more generic.", "The non-template function.", "The compiler reports an ambiguity error.", "The compiler selects one randomly."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Overload resolution rules state that if a non-template function and a template specialization match a call equally well, the non-template function is always preferred.", "id": "day_6_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) occur if the function call is fully qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)`)?", "options": ["Yes, ADL searches namespaces associated with the type of `x`.", "No, ADL is only performed for unqualified function calls.", "Yes, but only if `x` is a user-defined type.", "No, qualified calls are handled entirely at link time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) only applies to unqualified names (e.g. `foo(x)`). If the call is qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)` or `MyLib::foo(x)`), the compiler uses normal lookup rules and bypasses ADL completely.", "id": "day_6_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_6_sub_0", "day": 6, "index": 0}, {"title": "Move Constructor / Move Assignment", "explanation": "A move constructor (`T(T&& other)`) transfers resources from other to the new object, leaving other in a valid but unspecified state (typically empty/null). A move assignment (`T&` ``operator=`(T&& other)`) does the same for existing objects, releasing current resources first.\n\nMove operations should be `noexcept` — `std::vector` won't use move during reallocation unless it's `noexcept` (to preserve strong exception safety). The compiler generates move operations if you don't declare any copy operations, move operations, or destructor.\n\nThe moved-from state must be valid: you must be able to destroy it and potentially assign to it. Common pattern: swap all members with other, or set other's members to null/zero.", "examples": [{"title": "Move Constructor and Assignment", "code": "class Buffer\n{\n    int* data_;\n    size_t size_;\npublic:\n    Buffer(size_t n) : data_(new int[n]), size_(n) {}\n    ~Buffer() { delete[] data_; }\n    \n    // Move constructor\n    Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept\n        : data_(other.data_), size_(other.size_)\n        {\n        other.data_ = nullptr;  // leave other in valid state\n        other.size_ = 0;\n    }\n    \n    // Move assignment\n    Buffer& operator=(Buffer&& other) noexcept\n    {\n        if (this != &other)\n        {\n            delete[] data_;  // release current\n            data_ = other.data_;  size_ = other.size_;\n            other.data_ = nullptr;  other.size_ = 0;\n        }\n        return *this;\n    }\n};", "explanation": "Move operations steal resources and null out the source. Always mark noexcept.", "formatted_code": "class Buffer\n{\n    int* data_;\n    size_t size_;\npublic:\n    Buffer(size_t n) : data_(new int[n]), size_(n)\n    {\n    }\n    ~Buffer()\n    {\n        delete[] data_;\n    }\n\n    // Move constructor\n    Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept\n    : data_(other.data_), size_(other.size_)\n    {\n        other.data_ = nullptr;  // leave other in valid state\n        other.size_ = 0;\n    }\n\n    // Move assignment\n    Buffer& operator=(Buffer&& other) noexcept\n    {\n        if (this != &other)\n        {\n            delete[] data_;  // release current\n            data_ = other.data_;\n            size_ = other.size_;\n            other.data_ = nullptr;\n            other.size_ = 0;\n        }\n        return *this;\n    }\n};"}], "pitfalls": ["Forgetting `noexcept` on move operations — containers fall back to copying", "Not leaving the moved-from object in a valid state (destructor must still work)", "Self-move-assignment without checking this != &other", "Declaring a destructor prevents compiler from generating move operations"], "tips_to_remember": ["Always mark move operations `noexcept`", "Moved-from state: valid for destruction and assignment, nothing else guaranteed", "Declaring any of: destructor, copy ctor, copy assign — suppresses implicit move operations", "For types with only movable members (like unique_ptr), the compiler generates correct move operations automatically"], "questions": [{"question": "Why must move operations be noexcept?", "answer": "std::vector uses move during reallocation only if move is noexcept. If move might throw, the vector copies instead (slower but safe). If you're halfway through moving elements and one throws, you can't undo the moves already done — the strong exception safety guarantee would be violated."}, {"question": "What state must a moved-from object be in?", "answer": "Valid but unspecified. The destructor must work correctly. Assignment should work. You shouldn't rely on any particular value. Common implementation: set pointers to null, sizes to 0."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens if you bind a temporary object (prvalue) to a `const T&` reference?", "options": ["The temporary is destroyed at the end of the expression, leaving a dangling reference.", "The lifetime of the temporary is extended to match the lifetime of the reference.", "The compiler makes a permanent copy of the temporary on the heap.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ guarantees that binding a temporary object to a const lvalue reference extends the lifetime of that temporary to match the lifetime of the reference itself, preventing dangling reference bugs.", "id": "day_6_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you try to modify a member variable in a `const` member function in `Move Constructor / Move Assignment`?", "options": ["It compiles fine, but triggers a segmentation fault at runtime.", "Compilation fails, unless the member variable is marked `mutable`.", "It compiles fine, but the changes are discarded when the function exits.", "It compiles fine only if the class is not allocated on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A `const` member function promises not to modify any observable state of the object. Modifying any member variable inside it is a compilation error, unless the member is declared with the `mutable` keyword.", "id": "day_6_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Can you initialize a `constexpr` variable using the return value of a non-constexpr runtime function?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler will defer evaluation to runtime.", "Yes, if the function has no side effects.", "No, compilation will fail because a `constexpr` value must be known at compile time.", "Yes, but only if compiler optimization is turned on."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "`constexpr` variables must be initialized with constant expressions that can be evaluated at compile time. Initializing one with a runtime value results in a compilation error.", "id": "day_6_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the consequence of calling a `consteval` function with non-constant (runtime) arguments?", "options": ["The function is executed at runtime instead.", "The compiler fails to compile the call.", "The compiler generates a runtime check that throws an exception if arguments are invalid.", "The return value is undefined."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`consteval` (introduced in C++20) specifies an immediate function. Immediate functions MUST be evaluated at compile time. Calling them with runtime values is a compilation error.", "id": "day_6_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Can a `constinit` variable be modified after initialization during runtime?", "options": ["No, `constinit` variables are implicitly `const`.", "Yes, `constinit` only requires compile-time initialization, not runtime immutability.", "Yes, but only inside `consteval` functions.", "No, modifying it triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constinit` (C++20) ensures that a variable is initialized at compile time (preventing static initialization order issues), but it does not make the variable constant. To make it constant, you must explicitly combine it with `const`: `constinit const int x = 42;`.", "id": "day_6_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Is it safe to use `static_cast` to cast a `Base*` to a `Derived*`?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler checks the actual runtime type and returns `nullptr` on failure.", "Yes, but it is compile-time checked only. If the pointer is not actually of the Derived type, dereferencing it is UB.", "No, it is a compilation error. You must use `dynamic_cast`.", "Yes, `static_cast` automatically handles polymorphic conversions safely."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`static_cast` performs compile-time conversions. It does not perform any runtime checks. Casting a base pointer to a derived pointer with `static_cast` is unsafe: if the object is not of the derived type, dereferencing the casted pointer is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_6_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast` do if it fails to cast a reference type `Base&` to `Derived&`?", "options": ["It returns a null reference.", "It throws a `std::bad_cast` exception.", "It aborts the program immediately.", "It sets the reference to an empty object."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since there is no such thing as a null reference in C++, `dynamic_cast` cannot return null when casting reference types. Instead, it throws a `std::bad_cast` exception on failure.", "id": "day_6_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "If you cast an `int*` to a `float*` using `reinterpret_cast` and dereference it to read the value, what is the result?", "options": ["It returns the floating-point representation of the integer bits.", "It is a compiler error.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior due to the strict aliasing rule.", "It triggers a hardware trap."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "The strict aliasing rule states that you cannot access an object of one type through a pointer of a different type (with exceptions like `char*` or signed/unsigned variants). Violating this using `reinterpret_cast` triggers undefined behavior.", "id": "day_6_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "If both `void print(int)` and `template<typename T> void print(T)` match a call `print(42)` equally well, which one is selected by overload resolution?", "options": ["The template function, because it is more generic.", "The non-template function.", "The compiler reports an ambiguity error.", "The compiler selects one randomly."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Overload resolution rules state that if a non-template function and a template specialization match a call equally well, the non-template function is always preferred.", "id": "day_6_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) occur if the function call is fully qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)`)?", "options": ["Yes, ADL searches namespaces associated with the type of `x`.", "No, ADL is only performed for unqualified function calls.", "Yes, but only if `x` is a user-defined type.", "No, qualified calls are handled entirely at link time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) only applies to unqualified names (e.g. `foo(x)`). If the call is qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)` or `MyLib::foo(x)`), the compiler uses normal lookup rules and bypasses ADL completely.", "id": "day_6_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_6_sub_1", "day": 6, "index": 1}, {"title": "Copy Elision & RVO/NRVO", "explanation": "Copy elision is an optimization where the compiler constructs a return value directly in the caller's memory, skipping copy/move constructors entirely. **RVO** (**Return Value Optimization**) applies to unnamed temporaries: return `T(args)`. **NRVO** (Named **RVO**) applies to named local variables: return localVar.\n\nC++17 mandates copy elision for prvalue returns (**RVO**). **NRVO** is still optional but performed by all major compilers. Copy elision happens even if the copy/move constructor has side effects — it's one of the few places the compiler can change observable behavior.\n\nDon't return ``std::move`(localVar)` — it defeats **NRVO**! The compiler can elide the copy/move if you return by name. `std::move` forces a move instead of elision.", "examples": [{"title": "RVO and NRVO", "code": "struct Widget\n{\n    Widget() { std::cout << \"ctor\\n\"; }\n    Widget(const Widget&) { std::cout << \"copy\\n\"; }\n    Widget(Widget&&) { std::cout << \"move\\n\"; }\n};\n\nWidget makeWidget()\n{\n    Widget w;       // named local\n    return w;       // NRVO: may elide copy/move\n}\n\nWidget makeWidget2()\n{\n    return Widget{};  // RVO: guaranteed elision since C++17\n}\n\n// DON'T DO THIS:\nWidget bad()\n{\n    Widget w;\n    return std::move(w);  // DEFEATS NRVO! Forces a move.\n}", "explanation": "Return by name for NRVO. Never std::move a return value.", "formatted_code": "struct Widget\n{\n    Widget()\n    {\n        std::cout << \"ctor\\n\";\n    }\n    Widget(const Widget&)\n    {\n        std::cout << \"copy\\n\";\n    }\n    Widget(Widget&&)\n    {\n        std::cout << \"move\\n\";\n    }\n};\n\nWidget makeWidget()\n{\n    Widget w;  // named local\n    return w;  // NRVO: may elide copy/move\n}\n\nWidget makeWidget2()\n{\n    return Widget\n    {\n    };  // RVO: guaranteed elision since C++17\n}\n\n// DON'T DO THIS:\nWidget bad()\n{\n    Widget w;\n    return std::move(w);  // DEFEATS NRVO! Forces a move.\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["Returning ``std::move`(localVar)` — prevents **NRVO**, forces a move", "Assuming **NRVO** is guaranteed — it's optional (but practically universal)", "Multiple return paths returning different local variables — may prevent **NRVO**"], "tips_to_remember": ["C++17 guarantees copy elision for prvalue returns (**RVO**). **NRVO** is practically guaranteed", "Never return ``std::move`(x)` — just return x and let **NRVO** work", "Return by value in modern C++ is cheap — elision makes it zero-cost", "If a function has multiple return statements with different local variables, **NRVO** may not apply"], "questions": [{"question": "What is copy elision?", "answer": "An optimization where the compiler constructs an object directly in the destination, skipping copy/move constructors. RVO (unnamed temporaries) is mandatory since C++17. NRVO (named locals) is optional but universally performed."}, {"question": "Why should you never return std::move(localVar)?", "answer": "Because NRVO constructs the local variable directly in the caller's space — zero copies, zero moves. std::move forces the compiler to use the move constructor instead, which is strictly worse than elision."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What causes false sharing (cache line bouncing) in multithreaded systems?", "options": ["Multiple threads trying to modify the same global atomic variable.", "Two or more threads on different cores modifying independent variables that happen to reside on the same CPU cache line.", "When threads are run on separate physical CPUs.", "Lock contention on a shared mutex."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "CPU caches track memory in blocks called cache lines (typically 64 bytes). If two threads modify separate variables on the same cache line, the cores constantly invalidate each other's cache line copy, causing severe cache line bouncing.", "id": "day_6_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "How can you prevent false sharing in C++17?", "options": ["Wrap variables inside a `std::mutex`.", "Align variables using `alignas(std::hardware_destructive_interference_size)`.", "Use `std::atomic` variables.", "Compile with `-O3` optimization level."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++17 introduced `std::hardware_destructive_interference_size` which specifies the minimum byte alignment needed to prevent variables from sharing the same cache line (usually 64 bytes).", "id": "day_6_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the memory size of `struct S { char c; int i; };` on a standard 32/64-bit system?", "options": ["5 bytes (1 byte for char, 4 bytes for int).", "8 bytes, because the compiler adds 3 bytes of padding to align the 4-byte `int` on a 4-byte address boundary.", "12 bytes, due to vptr alignment.", "16 bytes."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To optimize CPU access, the compiler aligns data types to their natural sizes. A 4-byte integer must start on an address multiple of 4, forcing the insertion of 3 padding bytes after the 1-byte character.", "id": "day_6_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does returning `std::move(local)` from a function prevent NRVO (Named Return Value Optimization)?", "options": ["It copies the object instead of moving it.", "It casts the local variable to an rvalue reference (xvalue), which matches a move constructor call instead of triggering direct copy elision.", "The compiler does not optimize move-only types.", "It causes a stack overflow."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Returning a local by value allows the compiler to construct the return value directly in the caller's stack frame (NRVO). Using `std::move(local)` forces the function to return a reference (`T&&`), forcing a move constructor call and preventing NRVO.", "id": "day_6_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Does marking a function `inline` guarantee it will be inlined by the compiler?", "options": ["Yes, it is a mandatory instruction to the compiler.", "No, it is only a optimization hint; the compiler makes the final decision based on code size and heuristics.", "No, `inline` is only used to allow defining functions in header files without violating ODR.", "Yes, but only in debug builds."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`inline` has two meanings: it hints to the compiler to inline code (optional), and it allows the function to be defined in multiple translation units (such as in header files) without violating the One Definition Rule (ODR).", "id": "day_6_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What is the strict aliasing rule in C++?", "options": ["Pointers can only point to objects of the exact same type (ignoring const and signedness). Dereferencing pointers to incompatible types is UB.", "Variables must be named strictly according to their type.", "You cannot point two reference variables to the same object.", "It requires all pointers to be initialized to `nullptr`."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "The strict aliasing rule states that two pointers of incompatible types cannot point to the same memory location, allowing the compiler to assume that writing to one does not modify the other, enabling optimization.", "id": "day_6_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of alignment in C++ memory management?", "options": ["To sort pointers in alphabetical order.", "To ensure objects are allocated at memory addresses that are multiples of their alignment size, enabling fast CPU fetches.", "To match the type boundaries of template classes.", "To prevent memory leaks."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "CPUs fetch memory in aligned blocks. If data is unaligned, a single read might span two cache lines, requiring two memory fetches instead of one. Alignment guarantees optimal fetch speeds.", "id": "day_6_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "When is Return Value Optimization (RVO) guaranteed by the standard (C++17 onwards)?", "options": ["When a function returns a named local variable.", "When a function returns a temporary prvalue of the same type as the return type.", "Only when compiler optimization is set to `-O2` or higher.", "When returning pointers to heap objects."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since C++17, RVO is guaranteed (not just an optimization pass) when returning a prvalue (temporary object). The compiler is required to construct the object directly in the target storage, bypassing copies/moves.", "id": "day_6_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the penalty of unaligned memory access on modern CPU architectures?", "options": ["It causes a compilation warning.", "It can cause slower execution speed due to double cache fetches, or trigger a hardware alignment fault crash on strict architectures.", "It increases the binary file size.", "It disables multi-core execution."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Some architectures (like x86) handle unaligned reads with a performance penalty by doing two fetches. Others (like ARM) might trigger a hardware misaligned-memory-access exception, crashing the program.", "id": "day_6_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Why is the loop condition `for (int i = 0; i < vec.size(); ++i)` sometimes inefficient compared to caching the size?", "options": ["Because `size()` has O(n) complexity.", "If `size()` is not inline, or the compiler cannot verify that the vector size is constant (e.g. if loop body writes to pointers), it must call `size()` every iteration.", "Because `size()` allocates memory on the stack.", "There is no difference; compilers always optimize this."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If the compiler cannot prove that the vector size doesn't change during the loop (for example, if you write to raw pointers in the loop), it is forced to re-call `size()` on every iteration, wasting CPU cycles.", "id": "day_6_sub_2_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_6_sub_2", "day": 6, "index": 2}, {"title": "Copy-and-Swap Idiom", "explanation": "The copy-and-swap idiom implements copy assignment using the copy constructor and a non-throwing swap. Pattern: take the parameter by value (which copies), swap with current state, old state is destroyed in the parameter's destructor.\n\nBenefits: exception-safe (strong guarantee), self-assignment safe, and reuses the copy constructor. In C++11+, it also handles move assignment automatically (pass-by-value uses move constructor when given an rvalue).\n\nDownside: always makes a copy even if the current object has enough capacity (e.g., vector assignment could reuse existing buffer). For containers, a separate copy assignment may be more efficient.", "examples": [{"title": "Copy-and-Swap", "code": "class Buffer\n{\n    int* data_;\n    size_t size_;\npublic:\n    Buffer(size_t n) : data_(new int[n]{}), size_(n) {}\n    ~Buffer() { delete[] data_; }\n    Buffer(const Buffer& other) : data_(new int[other.size_]), size_(other.size_)\n    {\n        std::copy(other.data_, other.data_ + size_, data_);\n    }\n    Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept : data_(other.data_), size_(other.size_)\n    {\n        other.data_ = nullptr; other.size_ = 0;\n    }\n    \n    friend void swap(Buffer& a, Buffer& b) noexcept\n    {\n        using std::swap;\n        swap(a.data_, b.data_);\n        swap(a.size_, b.size_);\n    }\n    \n    Buffer& operator=(Buffer other) noexcept // pass by value!\n    {\n        swap(*this, other);\n        return *this;\n    }\n};", "explanation": "Pass by value copies/moves. Swap exchanges. Destructor cleans up old. Exception-safe by construction.", "formatted_code": "class Buffer\n{\n    int* data_;\n    size_t size_;\npublic:\n    Buffer(size_t n) : data_(new int[n]\n    {\n    }\n    ), size_(n)\n    {\n    }\n    ~Buffer()\n    {\n        delete[] data_;\n    }\n    Buffer(const Buffer& other) : data_(new int[other.size_]), size_(other.size_)\n    {\n        std::copy(other.data_, other.data_ + size_, data_);\n    }\n    Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept : data_(other.data_), size_(other.size_)\n    {\n        other.data_ = nullptr;\n        other.size_ = 0;\n    }\n\n    friend void swap(Buffer& a, Buffer& b) noexcept\n    {\n        using std::swap;\n        swap(a.data_, b.data_);\n        swap(a.size_, b.size_);\n    }\n\n    Buffer& operator=(Buffer other) noexcept  // pass by value!\n    {\n        swap(*this, other);\n        return *this;\n    }\n};"}], "pitfalls": ["Not making swap `noexcept` — defeats the strong exception safety purpose", "Using copy-and-swap for containers loses the optimization of reusing existing capacity", "Forgetting the friend swap — **ADL** won't find the custom swap"], "tips_to_remember": ["Copy-and-swap: strong exception safety, self-assignment safe, DRY (reuses copy ctor)", "Parameter by value: copies for lvalues, moves for rvalues — handles both copy and move assignment", "The swap must be `noexcept` for the strong guarantee", "For high-performance containers, consider separate copy/move assignments that reuse capacity"], "questions": [{"question": "How does copy-and-swap work?", "answer": "Take the assignment parameter by value (this performs the copy/move). Then swap the copy with *this (noexcept). The old state is now in the parameter, which is destroyed at scope exit. This provides strong exception safety and self-assignment safety."}, {"question": "Why is copy-and-swap exception-safe?", "answer": "The copy (which might throw) happens in the parameter construction, before any modification to *this. The swap is noexcept. If the copy throws, *this is untouched. If it succeeds, the noexcept swap commits the change atomically."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_6_sub_3_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_6_sub_3_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_6_sub_3_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_6_sub_3_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Copy-and-Swap Idiom`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_6_sub_3_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_6_sub_3_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_6_sub_3_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_6_sub_3_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_6_sub_3_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_6_sub_3_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_6_sub_3", "day": 6, "index": 3}]}, {"day": 7, "topic": "Templates & Generic Programming", "estimated_hours": 6, "subtopics": [{"title": "Function & Class Templates, Argument Deduction", "explanation": "Function templates let you write type-independent code. The compiler generates a specific instantiation for each set of `template` arguments used. Class templates (e.g., `std::vector<T>`) parameterize entire classes. Template argument deduction (TAD) lets the compiler deduce T from the function arguments, so you rarely need to write ``f<int>`(42)` — just `f(42)`.\n\n**Class Template Argument Deduction** (**CTAD**, C++17) extends this to class templates: `std::vector` v = {1, 2, 3} deduces `vector<int>`. Deduction guides can customize this behavior.", "examples": [{"title": "Template Basics", "code": "template<typename T>\nT maximum(T a, T b) { return a > b ? a : b; }\n\nauto x = maximum(3, 5);       // T deduced as int\nauto y = maximum(3.0, 5.0);   // T deduced as double\n// maximum(3, 5.0);           // ERROR: ambiguous (int vs double)\nmaximum<double>(3, 5.0);      // OK: explicit template argument\n\n// CTAD (C++17)\nstd::pair p(1, 2.0);          // deduces pair<int, double>\nstd::vector v = {1, 2, 3};    // deduces vector<int>", "explanation": "Template argument deduction infers types from arguments.", "formatted_code": "template<typename T>\nT maximum(T a, T b)\n{\n    return a > b ? a : b;\n}\n\nauto x = maximum(3, 5);  // T deduced as int\nauto y = maximum(3.0, 5.0);  // T deduced as double\n// maximum(3, 5.0);           // ERROR: ambiguous (int vs double)\nmaximum<double>(3, 5.0);  // OK: explicit template argument\n\n// CTAD (C++17)\nstd::pair p(1, 2.0);  // deduces pair<int, double>\nstd::vector v =\n{\n    1, 2, 3\n};  // deduces vector<int>"}], "pitfalls": ["Forgetting that each `template` instantiation is a separate function — code bloat", "Mixing deduced and non-deduced contexts causing deduction failure", "**CTAD** may deduce unexpected types with initializer lists"], "tips_to_remember": ["Templates are compiled per-instantiation — define them in headers", "**CTAD** (C++17) works for most standard containers", "Use auto for return types in `template` functions when appropriate"], "questions": [{"question": "What is template argument deduction?", "answer": "The compiler infers template type parameters from function arguments. For f(T), calling f(42) deduces T=int. Deduction follows specific rules and fails when arguments are ambiguous."}, {"question": "What is CTAD?", "answer": "Class Template Argument Deduction (C++17) allows the compiler to deduce class template arguments from constructor arguments. std::pair p(1, 2.0) deduces pair<int,double>"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "When does template instantiation occur in C++?", "options": ["At runtime, when the function is called.", "At compile time, when the template is instantiated with concrete types.", "At link time, when symbols are resolved.", "During preprocessing."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Templates are compile-time constructs. The compiler generates concrete class or function code (instantiation) during compilation when it sees the template used with specific type arguments.", "id": "day_7_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is SFINAE (Substitution Failure Is Not An Error) in C++ templates?", "options": ["A compiler flag that ignores all syntax errors inside templates.", "A rule stating that if a substitution failure occurs during overload candidate resolution, the candidate is discarded without causing a compilation error.", "A template debugging tool.", "A C++20 feature that replaces concepts."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "SFINAE states that if a type substitution fails while inspecting overloaded templates, it does not stop compilation. The compiler simply removes that candidate from the overload set and keeps searching.", "id": "day_7_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "How do you define an explicit template specialization for class `Box` with type `int`?", "options": ["`template<int> class Box { ... };`", "`template<> class Box<int> { ... };`", "`template class Box<int>;`", "`specialized class Box<int> { ... };`"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Explicit (or full) template specialization is declared with `template<>` followed by the class definition specifying the type in brackets: `class Box<int> { ... };`.", "id": "day_7_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why is the `typename` keyword required before a nested dependent name (e.g. `typename T::iterator`) in templates?", "options": ["It is a hint to the compiler to inline the type.", "To resolve ambiguity, informing the compiler that the name refers to a nested type rather than a static member variable.", "To import the type into the namespace.", "It is only required in C++98, not modern C++."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because `T` is a template parameter, the compiler cannot inspect it during the first phase of template parsing. It assumes nested names are static members unless prefixed with `typename` to signify a type.", "id": "day_7_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "How does `std::enable_if` conditionally enable templates?", "options": ["It throws a compilation warning if the condition is false.", "It uses SFINAE: if the condition is false, the nested type does not exist, causing substitution to fail and candidate discard.", "It is a runtime check that throws an exception.", "It disables optimizer passes for the template."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::enable_if_t<Cond, T>` defines a nested `type` equal to `T` only if `Cond` is true. If `Cond` is false, it has no nested type, triggering a substitution failure (SFINAE) and excluding the template from overload resolution.", "id": "day_7_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What is the expanded result of the C++17 fold expression `(... + args)` where `args` is `1, 2, 3`?", "options": ["`(1 + (2 + 3))`", "`((1 + 2) + 3)`", "`1 + 2 + 3` (flat summation)", "It is invalid syntax."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`(... + args)` is a unary left fold. It expands starting from the left: `((1 + 2) + 3)`. A right fold is `(args + ...)` which expands to `(1 + (2 + 3))`.", "id": "day_7_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What happens if a template argument fails to satisfy a C++20 `concept` constraint (e.g. `requires std::integral<T>`)?", "options": ["The compiler falls back to runtime type checks.", "It produces a clean compilation error at the call site, listing which constraint failed.", "The compiler ignores the template and uses default arguments.", "It triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++20 concepts replace complex template SFINAE hacks with clear, readable constraint requirements. If constraint checks fail, the compiler outputs a clean, readable error explaining exactly which constraint was violated.", "id": "day_7_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "Can you partially specialize a function template in C++?", "options": ["Yes, using the standard `template<typename T> void f<T*>()` syntax.", "No, function templates can only be fully specialized; you must use function overloading instead.", "Yes, but only inside namespaces.", "Yes, if the return type is `auto`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ does not allow partial specialization of function templates. If you need to partially specialize a function template, you must use function overloading or delegate to a helper class template.", "id": "day_7_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is two-phase lookup in template compilation?", "options": ["The compiler parses the template once for syntax, and once for linkage.", "Non-dependent names are checked in phase 1 (definition); dependent names are checked in phase 2 (instantiation).", "It is a runtime optimization phase.", "The compiler compiles the header first, then the source."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "During phase 1, the compiler parses the template and checks all names that do not depend on template parameters. During phase 2 (when the template is instantiated with actual types), the compiler resolves all dependent names.", "id": "day_7_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is Class Template Argument Deduction (CTAD) introduced in C++17?", "options": ["It allows class templates to inherit template arguments from base classes.", "It allows the compiler to deduce class template parameters from constructor arguments, avoiding explicit template parameters.", "It compiles templates faster by caching template types.", "It is a dynamic cast for templates."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "CTAD allows writing `std::pair p(1, 2.5)` instead of `std::pair<int, double> p(1, 2.5)`. The compiler automatically infers the template types by inspecting constructor parameters.", "id": "day_7_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_7_sub_0", "day": 7, "index": 0}, {"title": "Specialization — Full and Partial", "explanation": "Full specialization provides a complete implementation for specific type arguments: `template<> class Foo<int>` {...}. Partial specialization provides implementations for a subset of types (only for class templates): `template<typename T> class Foo<T*>` {...}. Function templates cannot be partially specialized — use overloading instead.\n\nSpecialization is checked after the primary `template`. The compiler selects the most specific match.", "examples": [{"title": "Specialization", "code": "// Primary\ntemplate<typename T> struct TypeName { static const char* name() { return \"unknown\"; } };\n// Full specialization\ntemplate<> struct TypeName<int> { static const char* name() { return \"int\"; } };\n// Partial specialization (pointer types)\ntemplate<typename T> struct TypeName<T*> { static const char* name() { return \"pointer\"; } };\n\nstd::cout << TypeName<double>::name();  // \"unknown\"\nstd::cout << TypeName<int>::name();     // \"int\"\nstd::cout << TypeName<int*>::name();    // \"pointer\"", "explanation": "Full specialization for int, partial for all pointer types.", "formatted_code": "// Primary\ntemplate<typename T> struct TypeName\n{\n    static const char* name()\n    {\n        return \"unknown\";\n    }\n};\n// Full specialization\ntemplate<> struct TypeName<int>\n{\n    static const char* name()\n    {\n        return \"int\";\n    }\n};\n// Partial specialization (pointer types)\ntemplate<typename T> struct TypeName<T*>\n{\n    static const char* name()\n    {\n        return \"pointer\";\n    }\n};\n\nstd::cout << TypeName<double>::name();  // \"unknown\"\nstd::cout << TypeName<int>::name();  // \"int\"\nstd::cout << TypeName<int*>::name();  // \"pointer\""}], "pitfalls": ["Function templates can't be partially specialized — use overloading", "Specialization must be declared after the primary `template`", "**ODR** violations from specializations in different TUs"], "tips_to_remember": ["Partial specialization: class templates only. For functions, use overloads.", "Specializations must appear after the primary `template` declaration", "Prefer concepts (C++20) over **SFINAE**/specialization for constraining templates"], "questions": [{"question": "Can you partially specialize function templates?", "answer": "No. Function templates cannot be partially specialized in C++. Use function overloading instead, which achieves the same effect and is more idiomatic."}, {"question": "What's the difference between full and partial specialization?", "answer": "Full specialization provides an implementation for specific, concrete types (template<> class C<int>). Partial specialization provides an implementation for a family of types (template<typename T> class C<T*>). Partial is only for class templates."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_7_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_7_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_7_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_7_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Specialization — Full and Partial`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_7_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_7_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_7_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_7_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_7_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_7_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_7_sub_1", "day": 7, "index": 1}, {"title": "SFINAE", "explanation": "**Substitution Failure Is Not An Error**: when substituting `template` arguments causes an invalid type/expression in the function declaration, that overload is silently removed from the candidate set instead of causing a compile error.\n\n**SFINAE** enables conditional compilation: enable a function only when a type has certain properties. Pre-C++20, this was done with `std::enable_if`, `decltype`, and void_t. C++20 concepts are the modern replacement.\n\n### Unforgettable Analogy / Deep Dive\n**SFINAE** (**Substitution Failure Is Not An Error**) is like a casting call for a movie. If an actor cannot speak a specific line (`template` substitution fails), the director doesn't cancel the movie (compiler error). They simply skip that actor and try the next one in the candidate list.", "examples": [{"title": "SFINAE with enable_if", "code": "// Only enable for integral types\ntemplate<typename T>\ntypename std::enable_if<std::is_integral_v<T>, T>::type\ndouble_it(T x) { return x * 2; }\n\n// C++20 equivalent with concepts:\ntemplate<std::integral T>\nT double_it_v2(T x) { return x * 2; }\n\nauto a = double_it(42);     // OK\n// auto b = double_it(3.14); // SFINAE: removed from overload set", "explanation": "SFINAE silently removes invalid overloads. Concepts are the modern alternative.", "formatted_code": "// Only enable for integral types\ntemplate<typename T>\ntypename std::enable_if<std::is_integral_v<T>, T>::type\ndouble_it(T x)\n{\n    return x * 2;\n}\n\n// C++20 equivalent with concepts:\ntemplate<std::integral T>\nT double_it_v2(T x)\n{\n    return x * 2;\n}\n\nauto a = double_it(42);  // OK\n// auto b = double_it(3.14); // SFINAE: removed from overload set"}], "pitfalls": ["**SFINAE** error messages are notoriously unreadable", "Applying **SFINAE** incorrectly — the failure must be in the 'immediate context'", "Using **SFINAE** when a simple overload or if `constexpr` would suffice"], "tips_to_remember": ["**SFINAE** = substitution failure removes a candidate, not an error", "Prefer C++20 concepts over **SFINAE** — much cleaner and better error messages", "enable_if goes in return type, default `template` argument, or extra `template` parameter"], "questions": [{"question": "What is SFINAE?", "answer": "When template argument substitution creates an invalid type or expression in a function's declaration, that function is silently excluded from overload resolution instead of causing a compile error. This allows 'compile-time if' — enabling functions only for types with certain properties."}, {"question": "Why are concepts preferred over SFINAE?", "answer": "Concepts provide the same functionality with much clearer syntax, better error messages, and composability. template<Integral T> is clearer than enable_if<is_integral_v<T>>."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "When does template instantiation occur in C++?", "options": ["At runtime, when the function is called.", "At compile time, when the template is instantiated with concrete types.", "At link time, when symbols are resolved.", "During preprocessing."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Templates are compile-time constructs. The compiler generates concrete class or function code (instantiation) during compilation when it sees the template used with specific type arguments.", "id": "day_7_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is SFINAE (Substitution Failure Is Not An Error) in C++ templates?", "options": ["A compiler flag that ignores all syntax errors inside templates.", "A rule stating that if a substitution failure occurs during overload candidate resolution, the candidate is discarded without causing a compilation error.", "A template debugging tool.", "A C++20 feature that replaces concepts."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "SFINAE states that if a type substitution fails while inspecting overloaded templates, it does not stop compilation. The compiler simply removes that candidate from the overload set and keeps searching.", "id": "day_7_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "How do you define an explicit template specialization for class `Box` with type `int`?", "options": ["`template<int> class Box { ... };`", "`template<> class Box<int> { ... };`", "`template class Box<int>;`", "`specialized class Box<int> { ... };`"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Explicit (or full) template specialization is declared with `template<>` followed by the class definition specifying the type in brackets: `class Box<int> { ... };`.", "id": "day_7_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why is the `typename` keyword required before a nested dependent name (e.g. `typename T::iterator`) in templates?", "options": ["It is a hint to the compiler to inline the type.", "To resolve ambiguity, informing the compiler that the name refers to a nested type rather than a static member variable.", "To import the type into the namespace.", "It is only required in C++98, not modern C++."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because `T` is a template parameter, the compiler cannot inspect it during the first phase of template parsing. It assumes nested names are static members unless prefixed with `typename` to signify a type.", "id": "day_7_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "How does `std::enable_if` conditionally enable templates?", "options": ["It throws a compilation warning if the condition is false.", "It uses SFINAE: if the condition is false, the nested type does not exist, causing substitution to fail and candidate discard.", "It is a runtime check that throws an exception.", "It disables optimizer passes for the template."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::enable_if_t<Cond, T>` defines a nested `type` equal to `T` only if `Cond` is true. If `Cond` is false, it has no nested type, triggering a substitution failure (SFINAE) and excluding the template from overload resolution.", "id": "day_7_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What is the expanded result of the C++17 fold expression `(... + args)` where `args` is `1, 2, 3`?", "options": ["`(1 + (2 + 3))`", "`((1 + 2) + 3)`", "`1 + 2 + 3` (flat summation)", "It is invalid syntax."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`(... + args)` is a unary left fold. It expands starting from the left: `((1 + 2) + 3)`. A right fold is `(args + ...)` which expands to `(1 + (2 + 3))`.", "id": "day_7_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What happens if a template argument fails to satisfy a C++20 `concept` constraint (e.g. `requires std::integral<T>`)?", "options": ["The compiler falls back to runtime type checks.", "It produces a clean compilation error at the call site, listing which constraint failed.", "The compiler ignores the template and uses default arguments.", "It triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++20 concepts replace complex template SFINAE hacks with clear, readable constraint requirements. If constraint checks fail, the compiler outputs a clean, readable error explaining exactly which constraint was violated.", "id": "day_7_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "Can you partially specialize a function template in C++?", "options": ["Yes, using the standard `template<typename T> void f<T*>()` syntax.", "No, function templates can only be fully specialized; you must use function overloading instead.", "Yes, but only inside namespaces.", "Yes, if the return type is `auto`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ does not allow partial specialization of function templates. If you need to partially specialize a function template, you must use function overloading or delegate to a helper class template.", "id": "day_7_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is two-phase lookup in template compilation?", "options": ["The compiler parses the template once for syntax, and once for linkage.", "Non-dependent names are checked in phase 1 (definition); dependent names are checked in phase 2 (instantiation).", "It is a runtime optimization phase.", "The compiler compiles the header first, then the source."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "During phase 1, the compiler parses the template and checks all names that do not depend on template parameters. During phase 2 (when the template is instantiated with actual types), the compiler resolves all dependent names.", "id": "day_7_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is Class Template Argument Deduction (CTAD) introduced in C++17?", "options": ["It allows class templates to inherit template arguments from base classes.", "It allows the compiler to deduce class template parameters from constructor arguments, avoiding explicit template parameters.", "It compiles templates faster by caching template types.", "It is a dynamic cast for templates."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "CTAD allows writing `std::pair p(1, 2.5)` instead of `std::pair<int, double> p(1, 2.5)`. The compiler automatically infers the template types by inspecting constructor parameters.", "id": "day_7_sub_2_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_7_sub_2", "day": 7, "index": 2}, {"title": "Concepts & Constraints (C++20)", "explanation": "Concepts are named Boolean predicates on `template` parameters. They replace **SFINAE** with readable, composable constraints. A `concept` is defined with the `requires` clause and tested with `requires` expressions.\n\nConcepts provide clear error messages when constraints aren't met, and they participate in overload resolution (more constrained overload wins).", "examples": [{"title": "Concepts", "code": "// Define a concept\ntemplate<typename T>\nconcept Printable = requires(T t)\n{\n    { std::cout << t } -> std::same_as<std::ostream&>;\n};\n\n// Use in template\ntemplate<Printable T>\nvoid print(T val) { std::cout << val << '\\n'; }\n\n// Or with requires clause:\ntemplate<typename T> requires std::integral<T>\nT square(T x) { return x * x; }\n\n// Or abbreviated function template:\nvoid process(std::integral auto x) { /* ... */ }", "explanation": "Concepts constrain templates with readable, meaningful names.", "formatted_code": "// Define a concept\ntemplate<typename T>\nconcept Printable = requires(T t)\n{\n    {\n        std::cout << t\n    }\n    -> std::same_as<std::ostream&>;\n};\n\n// Use in template\ntemplate<Printable T>\nvoid print(T val)\n{\n    std::cout << val << '\\n';\n}\n\n// Or with requires clause:\ntemplate<typename T> requires std::integral<T>\nT square(T x)\n{\n    return x * x;\n}\n\n// Or abbreviated function template:\nvoid process(std::integral auto x)\n{\n    /* ... */\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["Overcomplicating concepts — keep them simple and composable", "Forgetting that concepts affect overload resolution (more constrained wins)", "Not using standard concepts from <concepts> header"], "tips_to_remember": ["Concepts replace **SFINAE** with readable, named constraints", "More constrained overload wins in overload resolution", "Use standard concepts (`std::integral`, `std::floating_point`, `std::copyable`, etc.) before writing custom ones"], "questions": [{"question": "What is a concept?", "answer": "A concept is a named compile-time predicate that constrains template parameters. It specifies requirements on types (operations they must support, properties they must have). Defined with requires expressions and used to constrain templates."}, {"question": "How do concepts improve error messages?", "answer": "Without concepts, template errors show deep substitution failures. With concepts, the compiler says 'type X does not satisfy concept Y' — directly pointing to which requirement failed."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens if you bind a temporary object (prvalue) to a `const T&` reference?", "options": ["The temporary is destroyed at the end of the expression, leaving a dangling reference.", "The lifetime of the temporary is extended to match the lifetime of the reference.", "The compiler makes a permanent copy of the temporary on the heap.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ guarantees that binding a temporary object to a const lvalue reference extends the lifetime of that temporary to match the lifetime of the reference itself, preventing dangling reference bugs.", "id": "day_7_sub_3_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you try to modify a member variable in a `const` member function in `Concepts & Constraints (C++20)`?", "options": ["It compiles fine, but triggers a segmentation fault at runtime.", "Compilation fails, unless the member variable is marked `mutable`.", "It compiles fine, but the changes are discarded when the function exits.", "It compiles fine only if the class is not allocated on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A `const` member function promises not to modify any observable state of the object. Modifying any member variable inside it is a compilation error, unless the member is declared with the `mutable` keyword.", "id": "day_7_sub_3_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Can you initialize a `constexpr` variable using the return value of a non-constexpr runtime function?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler will defer evaluation to runtime.", "Yes, if the function has no side effects.", "No, compilation will fail because a `constexpr` value must be known at compile time.", "Yes, but only if compiler optimization is turned on."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "`constexpr` variables must be initialized with constant expressions that can be evaluated at compile time. Initializing one with a runtime value results in a compilation error.", "id": "day_7_sub_3_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the consequence of calling a `consteval` function with non-constant (runtime) arguments?", "options": ["The function is executed at runtime instead.", "The compiler fails to compile the call.", "The compiler generates a runtime check that throws an exception if arguments are invalid.", "The return value is undefined."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`consteval` (introduced in C++20) specifies an immediate function. Immediate functions MUST be evaluated at compile time. Calling them with runtime values is a compilation error.", "id": "day_7_sub_3_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Can a `constinit` variable be modified after initialization during runtime?", "options": ["No, `constinit` variables are implicitly `const`.", "Yes, `constinit` only requires compile-time initialization, not runtime immutability.", "Yes, but only inside `consteval` functions.", "No, modifying it triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constinit` (C++20) ensures that a variable is initialized at compile time (preventing static initialization order issues), but it does not make the variable constant. To make it constant, you must explicitly combine it with `const`: `constinit const int x = 42;`.", "id": "day_7_sub_3_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Is it safe to use `static_cast` to cast a `Base*` to a `Derived*`?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler checks the actual runtime type and returns `nullptr` on failure.", "Yes, but it is compile-time checked only. If the pointer is not actually of the Derived type, dereferencing it is UB.", "No, it is a compilation error. You must use `dynamic_cast`.", "Yes, `static_cast` automatically handles polymorphic conversions safely."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`static_cast` performs compile-time conversions. It does not perform any runtime checks. Casting a base pointer to a derived pointer with `static_cast` is unsafe: if the object is not of the derived type, dereferencing the casted pointer is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_7_sub_3_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast` do if it fails to cast a reference type `Base&` to `Derived&`?", "options": ["It returns a null reference.", "It throws a `std::bad_cast` exception.", "It aborts the program immediately.", "It sets the reference to an empty object."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since there is no such thing as a null reference in C++, `dynamic_cast` cannot return null when casting reference types. Instead, it throws a `std::bad_cast` exception on failure.", "id": "day_7_sub_3_mcq_6"}, {"question": "If you cast an `int*` to a `float*` using `reinterpret_cast` and dereference it to read the value, what is the result?", "options": ["It returns the floating-point representation of the integer bits.", "It is a compiler error.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior due to the strict aliasing rule.", "It triggers a hardware trap."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "The strict aliasing rule states that you cannot access an object of one type through a pointer of a different type (with exceptions like `char*` or signed/unsigned variants). Violating this using `reinterpret_cast` triggers undefined behavior.", "id": "day_7_sub_3_mcq_7"}, {"question": "If both `void print(int)` and `template<typename T> void print(T)` match a call `print(42)` equally well, which one is selected by overload resolution?", "options": ["The template function, because it is more generic.", "The non-template function.", "The compiler reports an ambiguity error.", "The compiler selects one randomly."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Overload resolution rules state that if a non-template function and a template specialization match a call equally well, the non-template function is always preferred.", "id": "day_7_sub_3_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) occur if the function call is fully qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)`)?", "options": ["Yes, ADL searches namespaces associated with the type of `x`.", "No, ADL is only performed for unqualified function calls.", "Yes, but only if `x` is a user-defined type.", "No, qualified calls are handled entirely at link time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) only applies to unqualified names (e.g. `foo(x)`). If the call is qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)` or `MyLib::foo(x)`), the compiler uses normal lookup rules and bypasses ADL completely.", "id": "day_7_sub_3_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_7_sub_3", "day": 7, "index": 3}, {"title": "Fold Expressions (C++17)", "explanation": "Fold expressions simplify variadic `template` operations. They apply a binary operator to a parameter pack: (args + ...) computes the sum. Four forms: unary left fold, unary right fold, binary left fold, binary right fold.\n\nBefore C++17, you needed recursive `template` instantiation. Fold expressions do it in one line.", "examples": [{"title": "Fold Expressions", "code": "template<typename... Args>\nauto sum(Args... args) { return (args + ...); }  // unary right fold\n\ntemplate<typename... Args>\nvoid print_all(Args... args)\n{\n    (std::cout << ... << args) << '\\n';  // binary left fold\n}\n\nauto s = sum(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);  // 15\nprint_all(\"hello\", \" \", \"world\");  // hello world", "explanation": "Fold expressions collapse parameter packs with a binary operator.", "formatted_code": "template<typename... Args>\nauto sum(Args... args)\n{\n    return (args + ...);\n}  // unary right fold\n\ntemplate<typename... Args>\nvoid print_all(Args... args)\n{\n    (std::cout << ... << args) << '\\n';  // binary left fold\n}\n\nauto s = sum(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);  // 15\nprint_all(\"hello\", \" \", \"world\");  // hello world"}], "pitfalls": ["Forgetting that unary fold over empty pack is only defined `for &&`, ||, and ,", "Operator precedence surprises in complex fold expressions"], "tips_to_remember": ["(args op ...) = unary right fold. (... op args) = unary left fold", "Binary fold: (init op ... op args) provides an initial value", "Fold over comma: (`f(args)`, ...) calls f for each argument"], "questions": [{"question": "What are fold expressions?", "answer": "C++17 feature that applies a binary operator to all elements of a parameter pack. (args + ...) computes the sum. Replaces recursive template instantiation for variadic operations."}, {"question": "What are the four forms of fold expressions?", "answer": "Unary right fold: (args op ...). Unary left fold: (... op args). Binary right fold: (args op ... op init). Binary left fold: (init op ... op args)."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_7_sub_4_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_7_sub_4_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_7_sub_4_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_7_sub_4_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Fold Expressions (C++17)`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_7_sub_4_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_7_sub_4_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_7_sub_4_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_7_sub_4_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_7_sub_4_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_7_sub_4_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_7_sub_4", "day": 7, "index": 4}]}, {"day": 8, "topic": "Exception Handling", "estimated_hours": 5, "subtopics": [{"title": "try/catch/throw, Stack Unwinding", "explanation": "When an exception is thrown, the runtime performs stack unwinding: it destroys all local objects in reverse construction order as it unwinds stack frames looking for a matching catch handler. This is why **RAII** is critical — destructors run during unwinding, ensuring resources are cleaned up.\n\nCatch by const reference (catch (`const `std::exception`&` e)) to avoid slicing and unnecessary copies. Rethrow with throw; (not throw e;) to preserve the original exception type.", "examples": [{"title": "Exception Basics", "code": "void risky()\n{\n    std::string local = \"will be destroyed during unwinding\";\n    throw std::runtime_error(\"something went wrong\");\n}\n\ntry\n{\n    risky();\n} catch (const std::runtime_error& e)\n{\n    std::cerr << e.what() << '\\n';\n} catch (const std::exception& e)\n{\n    std::cerr << \"Generic: \" << e.what() << '\\n';\n} catch (...)\n{\n    std::cerr << \"Unknown exception\\n\";\n    throw;  // rethrow — use throw; not throw e;\n}", "explanation": "Catch by const reference. Most specific catch first. Rethrow with throw;.", "formatted_code": "void risky()\n{\n    std::string local = \"will be destroyed during unwinding\";\n    throw std::runtime_error(\"something went wrong\");\n}\n\ntry\n{\n    risky();\n}\ncatch (const std::runtime_error& e)\n{\n    std::cerr << e.what() << '\\n';\n}\ncatch (const std::exception& e)\n{\n    std::cerr << \"Generic: \" << e.what() << '\\n';\n}\ncatch (...)\n{\n    std::cerr << \"Unknown exception\\n\";\n    throw;  // rethrow — use throw; not throw e;\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["Catching by value causes slicing — always catch by const reference", "throw e; instead of throw; rethrows a copy, losing derived type info", "Throwing in a destructor during stack unwinding calls `std::terminate`"], "tips_to_remember": ["Catch by const reference to avoid slicing", "Order catch handlers from most specific to most general", "throw; rethrows the current exception. throw e; throws a copy.", "Never throw from destructors (can cause `std::terminate` during unwinding)"], "questions": [{"question": "What is stack unwinding?", "answer": "When an exception is thrown, the runtime destroys local objects in reverse construction order as it unwinds the call stack looking for a matching catch handler. RAII objects (smart pointers, lock_guard) are properly cleaned up during this process."}, {"question": "Why catch by const reference?", "answer": "Catching by value slices the exception object (loses derived class info). Catching by non-const reference is fine but const is preferred since you rarely need to modify the exception."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_8_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_8_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_8_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_8_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `try/catch/throw, Stack Unwinding`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_8_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_8_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_8_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_8_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_8_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_8_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_8_sub_0", "day": 8, "index": 0}, {"title": "noexcept — Effect on Move Semantics and Containers", "explanation": "`noexcept` declares that a function won't throw exceptions. If it does throw, `std::terminate` is called. This is critical for move operations: `std::vector` only uses move during reallocation if the move constructor is `noexcept`.\n\n`noexcept` is a compile-time contract — the compiler can optimize knowing no exceptions will be thrown (no need to generate stack unwinding code). `noexcept(expr)` is conditional: `noexcept` only if expr evaluates to true.\n\n### Unforgettable Analogy / Deep Dive\nThink of moving vs copying like transferring files between hard drives:\n- **`Copying**` is copying a 10 GB file byte-by-byte from Folder A to Folder B. It takes time, allocates new memory, and copies all data.\n- **`Moving**` is simply renaming the directory pointer (cut and paste). Instead of copying the 10 GB of data, you copy the small 8-byte pointer address pointing to the data, and clear the source directory pointer to `nullptr`. Zero allocation, near-zero cost.\n- **Rule of `5**`: If you manage resource lifespans manually, you must implement all five pillars to prevent resource leaks: Destructor, Copy Constructor, Copy Assignment, Move Constructor, and Move Assignment.", "examples": [{"title": "noexcept and vector", "code": "struct Widget\n{\n    Widget(Widget&&) noexcept { /* ... */ }  // vector will use this for reallocation\n};\n\nstruct BadWidget\n{\n    BadWidget(BadWidget&&) { /* might throw */ }  // vector will COPY instead!\n};\n\n// Conditional noexcept\ntemplate<typename T>\nvoid swap(T& a, T& b) noexcept(std::is_nothrow_move_constructible_v<T>)\n{\n    T tmp = std::move(a);\n    a = std::move(b);\n    b = std::move(tmp);\n}", "explanation": "noexcept on move ctor is critical for container performance.", "formatted_code": "struct Widget\n{\n    Widget(Widget&&) noexcept\n    {\n        /* ... */\n    }  // vector will use this for reallocation\n};\n\nstruct BadWidget\n{\n    BadWidget(BadWidget&&)\n    {\n        /* might throw */\n    }  // vector will COPY instead!\n};\n\n// Conditional noexcept\ntemplate<typename T>\nvoid swap(T& a, T& b) noexcept(std::is_nothrow_move_constructible_v<T>)\n{\n    T tmp = std::move(a);\n    a = std::move(b);\n    b = std::move(tmp);\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["Marking a function `noexcept` when it can actually throw — `std::terminate`!", "Forgetting `noexcept` on move operations", "`noexcept` prevents the compiler from generating unwind tables — removing `noexcept` later is an ABI break"], "tips_to_remember": ["Always mark move constructors and move assignment `noexcept`", "`noexcept(false)` is the default — explicit `noexcept` opts in to the no-throw contract", "Destructors are implicitly `noexcept`", "`noexcept` is part of the function type since C++17"], "questions": [{"question": "Why is noexcept important for move operations?", "answer": "std::vector uses move during reallocation only if move is noexcept. Without noexcept, it falls back to copying for strong exception safety. This can be a massive performance difference for types like std::string."}, {"question": "What happens if a noexcept function throws?", "answer": "std::terminate is called. The stack may or may not be unwound (implementation-defined). noexcept is a hard contract."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_8_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_8_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_8_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_8_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `noexcept — Effect on Move Semantics and Containers`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_8_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_8_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_8_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_8_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_8_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_8_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_8_sub_1", "day": 8, "index": 1}, {"title": "Exception Safety Guarantees", "explanation": "Three levels: No-throw guarantee (operation never throws — destructors, swap, move). Strong guarantee (if it throws, state is unchanged — copy-and-swap achieves this). Basic guarantee (if it throws, invariants are preserved but state may change — no resource leaks, objects in valid state).\n\nAll code should provide at least the basic guarantee. Performance-critical code often targets the strong guarantee using copy-and-swap or transactional patterns.\n\n### Unforgettable Analogy / Deep Dive\nException safety guarantees are like database transaction logs:\n- **Basic `Guarantee**`: If an exception occurs, no memory is leaked and objects are in a valid state, but data might be partially changed.\n- **Strong Guarantee (Commit/Rollback)**: The operation either succeeds completely or rolls back to the original state with zero side effects. Usually achieved via 'copy-and-swap' (do work on a copy, then swap).\n- **Nothrow `Guarantee**`: The operation is guaranteed to never fail (`noexcept`), which is critical for destructors, moves, and swaps.", "examples": [{"title": "Exception Safety Levels", "code": "// No-throw: swap, destructors, move operations\nvoid swap(Widget& a, Widget& b) noexcept { /* ... */ }\n\n// Strong: copy-and-swap\nWidget& Widget::operator=(Widget other) noexcept\n{\n    swap(*this, other);\n    return *this;\n}\n\n// Basic: valid state but may differ from original\nvoid Widget::addItem(const Item& item)\n{\n    items_.push_back(item);  // might throw, but vector maintains invariants\n    ++count_;                // only executed if push_back succeeds\n}", "explanation": "Three tiers: no-throw (noexcept), strong (all-or-nothing), basic (no leaks, valid state).", "formatted_code": "// No-throw: swap, destructors, move operations\nvoid swap(Widget& a, Widget& b) noexcept\n{\n    /* ... */\n}\n\n// Strong: copy-and-swap\nWidget& Widget::operator=(Widget other) noexcept\n{\n    swap(*this, other);\n    return *this;\n}\n\n// Basic: valid state but may differ from original\nvoid Widget::addItem(const Item& item)\n{\n    items_.push_back(item);  // might throw, but vector maintains invariants\n    ++count_;  // only executed if push_back succeeds\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["Providing no exception safety — leaking resources on throw", "Assuming strong guarantee when only basic is provided", "Not making move/swap/destructors no-throw"], "tips_to_remember": ["Destructors, move operations, and swap should be no-throw", "Use **RAII** for basic guarantee 'for free'", "Copy-and-swap provides strong guarantee", "Document the exception safety level of your public APIs"], "questions": [{"question": "What are the three exception safety guarantees?", "answer": "No-throw: never throws. Strong: if it throws, state is unchanged (atomic). Basic: if it throws, no leaks and invariants maintained (but state may differ). All code should provide at least basic."}, {"question": "How does RAII provide basic exception safety?", "answer": "RAII objects release resources in their destructors, which run during stack unwinding. Even if an exception is thrown, resources are properly cleaned up — no leaks."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What are the three exception safety guarantees in C++?", "options": ["Compile-time, link-time, and runtime safety.", "Basic guarantee (no leaks, valid state), Strong guarantee (commit or rollback), and Nothrow guarantee (cannot fail).", "Try, catch, and throw guarantees.", "Checked exceptions, unchecked exceptions, and system panics."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The three standard guarantees are: Basic (objects are in a valid state, no leaks), Strong (operation commits or rolls back completely), and Nothrow (`noexcept` functions that cannot fail).", "id": "day_8_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if an exception is thrown out of a destructor during stack unwinding?", "options": ["The exception is caught by the compiler runtime and ignored.", "The program aborts immediately via `std::terminate`.", "The destructor resumes from the next line.", "The program switches to a backup call stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a destructor throws an exception while another exception is active (during stack unwinding), C++ cannot handle two active exceptions at once and immediately calls `std::terminate`.", "id": "day_8_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `static_assert` preferred over runtime assertions (`assert`) for checking invariants?", "options": ["`static_assert` runs faster at runtime.", "`static_assert` is evaluated at compile time, catching errors before the program even runs, with zero runtime cost.", "`static_assert` can check heap memory allocations.", "`assert` is deprecated in C++."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`static_assert` evaluates constant expressions at compile time. If the assertion fails, compilation fails, catching bugs early without shipping checks to runtime.", "id": "day_8_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the primary bug type caught by AddressSanitizer (ASan)?", "options": ["Deadlocks in lock acquisition.", "Out-of-bounds memory accesses (buffer overflows) and use-after-free bugs.", "Thread synchronization races.", "Integer divisions by zero."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "AddressSanitizer (ASan) instrument compiles code to detect out-of-bounds array access, use-after-free (dangling pointers), and stack/global overflows at runtime.", "id": "day_8_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What does ThreadSanitizer (TSan) primarily detect?", "options": ["Threads that are sleeping too long.", "Data races (concurrent unsynchronized memory accesses) at runtime.", "Deadlocks and thread leaks.", "Stack overflows in thread functions."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "ThreadSanitizer (TSan) monitors memory access patterns of all threads at runtime to identify when two threads access the same memory location concurrently without proper synchronization.", "id": "day_8_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What happens if a function marked `noexcept` throws an exception at runtime?", "options": ["The exception is caught by the caller's catch block.", "The program immediately terminates via `std::terminate` without complete stack unwinding.", "The compiler replaces the return value with a default object.", "The exception is silently ignored."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Marking a function `noexcept` promises that it won't leak exceptions. If it does, C++ calls `std::terminate` immediately, bypassing normal exception catch blocks.", "id": "day_8_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "Which compiler flag promotes all compiler warnings to compilation errors?", "options": ["`-Wall`", "`-Werror` (or `/WX` on MSVC)", "`-Wextra`", "`-O3`"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "` -Werror` (GCC/Clang) and `/WX` (MSVC) treat all warnings as compilation errors, forcing developers to resolve warnings before compilation succeeds.", "id": "day_8_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is the risk of using runtime assertions (`assert`) for critical validation check logic in production builds?", "options": ["Assertions are slow to parse at startup.", "Assertions are completely compiled out (ignored) in release builds (`-DNDEBUG` flag), disabling the checks.", "Assertions cause heap fragmentation.", "Assertions cannot be compiled on Linux."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Standard assertions (`assert`) are active only in debug builds. When building for production/release, the `NDEBUG` macro is defined, disabling all `assert` statements, which can disable critical validations if used incorrectly.", "id": "day_8_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "How does Undefined Behavior (UB) differ from Implementation-Defined Behavior?", "options": ["UB must trigger a compilation error, whereas implementation-defined behavior does not.", "Implementation-defined behavior must be consistent and documented by the compiler vendor; UB has no rules, and the compiler can generate any arbitrary instruction.", "There is no difference; they are synonyms.", "UB only occurs on older compilers."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Implementation-defined behavior (like the size of `int`) must be documented and consistent for a compiler. Undefined behavior has no rules: the program might crash, print wrong results, or run fine temporarily.", "id": "day_8_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What does a compiler warning about a 'dangling reference' usually indicate?", "options": ["The compiler is cleaning up heap memory.", "A reference points to a variable whose lifetime has ended (like a local variable returned from a function).", "A pointer has been cast to a float.", "A template class has not been instantiated."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A dangling reference points to destroyed memory. Accessing it is undefined behavior, and compilers warn you when a reference's target object goes out of scope.", "id": "day_8_sub_2_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_8_sub_2", "day": 8, "index": 2}, {"title": "Exceptions vs Error Codes vs std::expected (C++23) / std::optional", "explanation": "Exceptions: good for truly exceptional/unexpected errors. Overhead only on the throw path (zero-cost happy path on most implementations). Error codes: good for expected, frequent errors (e.g., file not found). No overhead but easy to ignore, verbose error propagation.\n\n`std::expected<T,E>` (C++23) is a value-or-error type — similar to Rust's `Result<T,E>`. It forces the caller to handle the error (can't ignore it like error codes) without the overhead of exceptions. `std::optional<T>` is simpler: value-or-nothing (no error information).", "examples": [{"title": "std::expected", "code": "// C++23 std::expected\nstd::expected<int, std::string> divide(int a, int b)\n{\n    if (b == 0) return std::unexpected(\"division by zero\");\n    return a / b;\n}\n\nauto result = divide(10, 0);\nif (result)\n{\n    std::cout << *result;\n} else\n{\n    std::cerr << result.error();\n}\n\n// Monadic operations:\nauto final = divide(10, 2)\n    .transform([](int v) { return v * 2; })\n    .transform_error([](auto e) { return \"Error: \" + e; });", "explanation": "std::expected is a modern error-handling type that can't be silently ignored.", "formatted_code": "// C++23 std::expected\nstd::expected<int, std::string> divide(int a, int b)\n{\n    if (b == 0) return std::unexpected(\"division by zero\");\n    return a / b;\n}\n\nauto result = divide(10, 0);\nif (result)\n{\n    std::cout << *result;\n}\nelse\n{\n    std::cerr << result.error();\n}\n\n// Monadic operations:\nauto final = divide(10, 2)\n.transform([](int v)\n{\n    return v * 2;\n}\n)\n.transform_error([](auto e)\n{\n    return \"Error: \" + e;\n}\n);"}], "pitfalls": ["Ignoring error return codes (the main problem they have)", "Using exceptions for expected control flow (e.g., end of file)", "Mixing exception and error code styles inconsistently in an API"], "tips_to_remember": ["Use exceptions for unexpected errors, expected/error codes for expected failures", "`std::expected` (C++23) combines the best of both: no overhead + can't be ignored", "`std::optional` for 'might not have a value' (no error info needed)", "Be consistent within a codebase — don't mix styles randomly"], "questions": [{"question": "When should you use exceptions vs error codes?", "answer": "Exceptions: for truly unexpected errors, when error handling is far from the error site, when you want automatic propagation. Error codes/expected: for expected failures (file not found, parse error), in performance-critical paths, in APIs that must never throw (embedded, kernel)."}, {"question": "What is std::expected and how does it differ from std::optional?", "answer": "std::expected<T,E> holds either a T value or an E error — like Rust's Result. std::optional<T> holds either a T or nothing. expected carries error information, optional doesn't."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What are the three exception safety guarantees in C++?", "options": ["Compile-time, link-time, and runtime safety.", "Basic guarantee (no leaks, valid state), Strong guarantee (commit or rollback), and Nothrow guarantee (cannot fail).", "Try, catch, and throw guarantees.", "Checked exceptions, unchecked exceptions, and system panics."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The three standard guarantees are: Basic (objects are in a valid state, no leaks), Strong (operation commits or rolls back completely), and Nothrow (`noexcept` functions that cannot fail).", "id": "day_8_sub_3_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if an exception is thrown out of a destructor during stack unwinding?", "options": ["The exception is caught by the compiler runtime and ignored.", "The program aborts immediately via `std::terminate`.", "The destructor resumes from the next line.", "The program switches to a backup call stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a destructor throws an exception while another exception is active (during stack unwinding), C++ cannot handle two active exceptions at once and immediately calls `std::terminate`.", "id": "day_8_sub_3_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `static_assert` preferred over runtime assertions (`assert`) for checking invariants?", "options": ["`static_assert` runs faster at runtime.", "`static_assert` is evaluated at compile time, catching errors before the program even runs, with zero runtime cost.", "`static_assert` can check heap memory allocations.", "`assert` is deprecated in C++."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`static_assert` evaluates constant expressions at compile time. If the assertion fails, compilation fails, catching bugs early without shipping checks to runtime.", "id": "day_8_sub_3_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the primary bug type caught by AddressSanitizer (ASan)?", "options": ["Deadlocks in lock acquisition.", "Out-of-bounds memory accesses (buffer overflows) and use-after-free bugs.", "Thread synchronization races.", "Integer divisions by zero."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "AddressSanitizer (ASan) instrument compiles code to detect out-of-bounds array access, use-after-free (dangling pointers), and stack/global overflows at runtime.", "id": "day_8_sub_3_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What does ThreadSanitizer (TSan) primarily detect?", "options": ["Threads that are sleeping too long.", "Data races (concurrent unsynchronized memory accesses) at runtime.", "Deadlocks and thread leaks.", "Stack overflows in thread functions."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "ThreadSanitizer (TSan) monitors memory access patterns of all threads at runtime to identify when two threads access the same memory location concurrently without proper synchronization.", "id": "day_8_sub_3_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What happens if a function marked `noexcept` throws an exception at runtime?", "options": ["The exception is caught by the caller's catch block.", "The program immediately terminates via `std::terminate` without complete stack unwinding.", "The compiler replaces the return value with a default object.", "The exception is silently ignored."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Marking a function `noexcept` promises that it won't leak exceptions. If it does, C++ calls `std::terminate` immediately, bypassing normal exception catch blocks.", "id": "day_8_sub_3_mcq_5"}, {"question": "Which compiler flag promotes all compiler warnings to compilation errors?", "options": ["`-Wall`", "`-Werror` (or `/WX` on MSVC)", "`-Wextra`", "`-O3`"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "` -Werror` (GCC/Clang) and `/WX` (MSVC) treat all warnings as compilation errors, forcing developers to resolve warnings before compilation succeeds.", "id": "day_8_sub_3_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is the risk of using runtime assertions (`assert`) for critical validation check logic in production builds?", "options": ["Assertions are slow to parse at startup.", "Assertions are completely compiled out (ignored) in release builds (`-DNDEBUG` flag), disabling the checks.", "Assertions cause heap fragmentation.", "Assertions cannot be compiled on Linux."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Standard assertions (`assert`) are active only in debug builds. When building for production/release, the `NDEBUG` macro is defined, disabling all `assert` statements, which can disable critical validations if used incorrectly.", "id": "day_8_sub_3_mcq_7"}, {"question": "How does Undefined Behavior (UB) differ from Implementation-Defined Behavior?", "options": ["UB must trigger a compilation error, whereas implementation-defined behavior does not.", "Implementation-defined behavior must be consistent and documented by the compiler vendor; UB has no rules, and the compiler can generate any arbitrary instruction.", "There is no difference; they are synonyms.", "UB only occurs on older compilers."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Implementation-defined behavior (like the size of `int`) must be documented and consistent for a compiler. Undefined behavior has no rules: the program might crash, print wrong results, or run fine temporarily.", "id": "day_8_sub_3_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What does a compiler warning about a 'dangling reference' usually indicate?", "options": ["The compiler is cleaning up heap memory.", "A reference points to a variable whose lifetime has ended (like a local variable returned from a function).", "A pointer has been cast to a float.", "A template class has not been instantiated."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A dangling reference points to destroyed memory. Accessing it is undefined behavior, and compilers warn you when a reference's target object goes out of scope.", "id": "day_8_sub_3_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_8_sub_3", "day": 8, "index": 3}]}, {"day": 9, "topic": "STL & Standard Library", "estimated_hours": 6, "subtopics": [{"title": "Containers & Underlying Structures", "explanation": "vector: contiguous dynamic array, `O(1)` amortized `push_back`, `O(1)` random access. deque: double-ended queue, `O(1)` push front/back, random access. list: doubly-linked list, `O(1)` insert/delete at known position, no random access. map/set: red-black tree, `O(log n)` operations, sorted. unordered_map/set: hash table, `O(1)` average operations, unsorted.\n\nChoosing the right container: vector is the default (cache-friendly). Use map/set when you need sorted order. Use unordered_map/set for fastest lookups. list is rarely the best choice (poor cache locality).", "examples": [{"title": "Container Comparison", "code": "std::vector<int> v = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5};  // contiguous, cache-friendly\nstd::deque<int> d = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5};   // fast front/back insertion\nstd::list<int> l = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5};    // O(1) splice, poor cache\n\nstd::map<std::string, int> m;           // sorted by key, O(log n)\nm[\"alpha\"] = 1;\n\nstd::unordered_map<std::string, int> um; // hash table, O(1) average\num[\"alpha\"] = 1;\n\n// vector is almost always the right choice:\n// Even for 'insert in middle', vector is often faster than list\n// due to cache effects — profile before choosing list!", "explanation": "vector is the default. Profile before choosing a 'theoretically faster' container.", "formatted_code": "std::vector<int> v =\n{\n    3, 1, 4, 1, 5\n};  // contiguous, cache-friendly\nstd::deque<int> d =\n{\n    3, 1, 4, 1, 5\n};  // fast front/back insertion\nstd::list<int> l =\n{\n    3, 1, 4, 1, 5\n};  // O(1) splice, poor cache\n\nstd::map<std::string, int> m;  // sorted by key, O(log n)\nm[\"alpha\"] = 1;\n\nstd::unordered_map<std::string, int> um;  // hash table, O(1) average\num[\"alpha\"] = 1;\n\n// vector is almost always the right choice:\n// Even for 'insert in middle', vector is often faster than list\n// due to cache effects — profile before choosing list!"}], "pitfalls": ["Choosing list for '`O(1)` insert' without considering cache effects", "unordered_map with a bad hash function degrades to `O(n)`", "Not reserving vector capacity when size is known — causes reallocations"], "tips_to_remember": ["vector is the default container — contiguous memory = cache-friendly", "map for sorted data, unordered_map for fastest lookup", "Reserve vector capacity when you know the size: v.`reserve(n)`", "`emplace_back` is slightly more efficient than `push_back` for constructed objects"], "questions": [{"question": "When should you use vector vs list?", "answer": "Almost always vector. Even insertions in the middle are often faster with vector due to cache locality. List is better only when you need guaranteed O(1) splice, stable iterators after insertion, or very large elements that are expensive to move."}, {"question": "What is the underlying data structure of std::map?", "answer": "A self-balancing binary search tree, typically a red-black tree. All operations (insert, find, erase) are O(log n). Elements are stored in sorted key order."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_9_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_9_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_9_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_9_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Containers & Underlying Structures`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_9_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_9_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_9_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_9_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_9_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_9_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_9_sub_0", "day": 9, "index": 0}, {"title": "Iterator Categories & Invalidation Rules", "explanation": "Five categories (weakest to strongest): input, output, forward, bidirectional, random access. C++20 adds contiguous. Each category supports a superset of operations of weaker categories.\n\nIterator invalidation: operations on containers can invalidate iterators. `vector::push_back` invalidates all if reallocation occurs. `vector::erase` invalidates at and after the erased position. map/`set::erase` only invalidates the erased element. Understanding invalidation is critical to avoid dangling iterator bugs.", "examples": [{"title": "Invalidation", "code": "std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\nauto it = v.begin() + 2;  // points to 3\nv.push_back(6);  // MAY invalidate it (if reallocation)!\n// *it is now UNDEFINED if reallocation occurred\n\n// Safe pattern: erase in loop\nfor (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); )\n{\n    if (*it % 2 == 0)\n        it = v.erase(it);  // erase returns next valid iterator\n    else\n        ++it;\n}\n\n// std::erase_if (C++20) — simpler:\nstd::erase_if(v, [](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });", "explanation": "Know which operations invalidate iterators. Use erase-remove idiom or C++20 erase_if.", "formatted_code": "std::vector<int> v =\n{\n    1, 2, 3, 4, 5\n};\nauto it = v.begin() + 2;  // points to 3\nv.push_back(6);  // MAY invalidate it (if reallocation)!\n// *it is now UNDEFINED if reallocation occurred\n\n// Safe pattern: erase in loop\nfor (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); )\n{\n    if (*it % 2 == 0)\n    it = v.erase(it);  // erase returns next valid iterator\n    else\n    ++it;\n}\n\n// std::erase_if (C++20) — simpler:\nstd::erase_if(v, [](int x)\n{\n    return x % 2 == 0;\n}\n);"}], "pitfalls": ["Using an iterator after the container was modified (invalidation)", "Forgetting that vector reallocation invalidates ALL iterators/pointers", "Erasing in a loop without updating the iterator"], "tips_to_remember": ["vector: any reallocation invalidates everything. erase invalidates at/after position", "map/set: erase only invalidates the erased element", "`unordered_*`: rehash invalidates everything", "Use C++20 `std::erase_if` for safe container erasure"], "questions": [{"question": "Which vector operations invalidate iterators?", "answer": "push_back: all iterators if reallocation, none otherwise. insert: all if realloc, at/after insertion point otherwise. erase: at and after erased position. clear: all. reserve: all (may reallocate). resize: potentially all."}, {"question": "How do you safely erase elements from a vector in a loop?", "answer": "Use the erase-remove idiom: v.erase(std::remove_if(v.begin(), v.end(), pred), v.end()). Or in C++20: std::erase_if(v, pred). Or manually: it = v.erase(it) returns the next valid iterator."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_9_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_9_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_9_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_9_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Iterator Categories & Invalidation Rules`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_9_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_9_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_9_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_9_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_9_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_9_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_9_sub_1", "day": 9, "index": 1}, {"title": "Lambdas, std::function, Functors", "explanation": "Lambdas (C++11) are anonymous function objects. They can capture local variables by value [=] or reference [&]. They're more concise than functors and more flexible than function pointers.\n\n`std::function`<`R(Args...)`> is a type-erased callable wrapper — it can hold lambdas, function pointers, functors, or member function pointers. Overhead: heap allocation (for large closures), `virtual` dispatch. Prefer auto or templates when the concrete type is known.\n\n`std::bind` is mostly obsoleted by lambdas. Lambdas are clearer, faster (no type erasure), and more flexible.", "examples": [{"title": "Lambdas", "code": "// Lambda with capture\nint multiplier = 3;\nauto triple = [multiplier](int x) { return x * multiplier; };\nstd::cout << triple(10);  // 30\n\n// Generic lambda (C++14)\nauto add = [](auto a, auto b) { return a + b; };\n\n// Lambda with mutable capture\nint count = 0;\nauto counter = [count]() mutable { return ++count; };\n\n// std::function for type-erased storage\nstd::function<int(int)> fn = triple;  // type-erased, has overhead\n\n// Prefer auto or templates when possible:\nauto fn2 = triple;  // no type erasure overhead", "explanation": "Lambdas replace functors and std::bind. Prefer auto over std::function for local use.", "formatted_code": "// Lambda with capture\nint multiplier = 3;\nauto triple = [multiplier](int x)\n{\n    return x * multiplier;\n};\nstd::cout << triple(10);  // 30\n\n// Generic lambda (C++14)\nauto add = [](auto a, auto b)\n{\n    return a + b;\n};\n\n// Lambda with mutable capture\nint count = 0;\nauto counter = [count]() mutable\n{\n    return ++count;\n};\n\n// std::function for type-erased storage\nstd::function<int(int)> fn = triple;  // type-erased, has overhead\n\n// Prefer auto or templates when possible:\nauto fn2 = triple;  // no type erasure overhead"}], "pitfalls": ["Capturing by reference when the lambda outlives the referenced variable", "Using `std::function` when auto would work (unnecessary heap allocation)", "Forgetting mutable for lambdas that modify captured values"], "tips_to_remember": ["Capture by value [=] for safety, by reference [&] when you need to modify", "Use auto for lambda types — `std::function` has overhead", "Lambda captures are const by default — use mutable to modify", "Prefer lambdas over `std::bind` — cleaner, faster, more readable"], "questions": [{"question": "Why are lambdas preferred over std::bind?", "answer": "Lambdas are more readable, support captures naturally, are inline-friendly (no type erasure), handle overloaded functions easily, and don't have the dangling reference pitfalls of std::bind's argument copies."}, {"question": "When should you use std::function?", "answer": "When you need to store a callable in a data structure, pass it across API boundaries, or when the exact callable type isn't known at compile time. For local use, prefer auto or templates to avoid type erasure overhead."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_9_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_9_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_9_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_9_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Lambdas, std::function, Functors`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_9_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_9_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_9_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_9_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_9_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_9_sub_2_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_9_sub_2", "day": 9, "index": 2}, {"title": "optional, variant, any (C++17)", "explanation": "`std::optional<T>` holds either a T or nothing. Good for 'might not have a value' without using pointers or sentinel values. `std::variant<Ts...>` is a type-safe union — holds exactly one of its alternative types. Use `std::visit` for pattern matching. `std::any` holds any copyable type — like variant but unbounded.\n\nPrefer optional for nullable values, variant for a known set of types, and any only when the type set is truly unknown (rare).", "examples": [{"title": "C++17 Vocabulary Types", "code": "// optional\nstd::optional<int> findIndex(const std::vector<int>& v, int target)\n{\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i)\n        if (v[i] == target) return i;\n    return std::nullopt;\n}\nauto idx = findIndex(v, 42);\nif (idx) std::cout << *idx;\n\n// variant\nstd::variant<int, double, std::string> value = \"hello\";\nstd::visit([](auto&& v) { std::cout << v; }, value);\n\n// any\nstd::any a = 42;\na = std::string(\"hello\");\nstd::cout << std::any_cast<std::string>(a);  // throws bad_any_cast if wrong", "explanation": "optional for nullable, variant for type-safe union, any for truly dynamic types.", "formatted_code": "// optional\nstd::optional<int> findIndex(const std::vector<int>& v, int target)\n{\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i)\n    if (v[i] == target) return i;\n    return std::nullopt;\n}\nauto idx = findIndex(v, 42);\nif (idx) std::cout << *idx;\n\n// variant\nstd::variant<int, double, std::string> value = \"hello\";\nstd::visit([](auto&& v)\n{\n    std::cout << v;\n}\n, value);\n\n// any\nstd::any a = 42;\na = std::string(\"hello\");\nstd::cout << std::any_cast<std::string>(a);  // throws bad_any_cast if wrong"}], "pitfalls": ["Accessing optional without checking — UB (dereferencing nullopt)", "variant can't hold references or void", "any_cast throws on type mismatch — always handle bad_any_cast"], "tips_to_remember": ["Use optional for 'might not have a value'. Never use -1 or `nullptr` as sentinel.", "variant replaces union with type safety and visitor pattern", "any is rarely needed — prefer variant when you know the possible types", "optional has monadic operations in C++23: transform, and_then, or_else"], "questions": [{"question": "When should you use optional vs a pointer?", "answer": "Use optional when the absence of a value is a normal part of the interface (not an error). optional is value-semantic (no heap allocation, no null pointer issues). Use pointers only for non-owning observation of existing objects."}, {"question": "How does std::variant differ from a C union?", "answer": "variant is type-safe: it tracks which type is currently stored, prevents accessing the wrong type (throws bad_variant_access), calls constructors/destructors properly, and supports visitor pattern with std::visit."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_9_sub_3_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_9_sub_3_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_9_sub_3_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_9_sub_3_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `optional, variant, any (C++17)`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_9_sub_3_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_9_sub_3_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_9_sub_3_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_9_sub_3_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_9_sub_3_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_9_sub_3_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_9_sub_3", "day": 9, "index": 3}, {"title": "std::string_view & std::span — Non-Owning Views", "explanation": "`std::string_view` (C++17) is a non-owning view of a character sequence — it stores a pointer and a length, never copies or owns the data. It's the ideal parameter type for read-only string functions: zero-copy, no allocation, and it accepts `std::string`, `const char*`, and string literals. `std::span` (C++20) is the same idea for contiguous sequences of any type — a non-owning view over a contiguous range (arrays, `std::vector`, `std::array`).\n\nThe critical caveat: views do NOT own their data. If the underlying buffer is destroyed or reallocated, the view dangles. string_view is not guaranteed to be null-terminated — passing .`data()` to C functions expecting a null terminator is a bug. Always ensure the referenced data outlives the view.", "examples": [{"title": "Views in Action", "code": "#include <string_view>\n#include <span>\n\n// string_view: zero-copy read-only string parameter\nvoid log(std::string_view msg) // accepts std::string, const char*, literals\n{\n    std::cout << msg << '\\n';\n}\nlog(\"hello\");          // const char* -> string_view\nstd::string s = \"world\";\nlog(s);                 // std::string -> string_view (no copy!)\n\n// span: non-owning view over contiguous data\nvoid sum(std::span<const int> data)\n{\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int x : data) total += x;\n}\nint arr[] = {1, 2, 3};\nstd::vector<int> v = {4, 5, 6};\nsum(arr);  // array -> span\nsum(v);    // vector -> span (no copy)", "explanation": "Views are zero-copy and accept multiple container types.", "formatted_code": "#include <string_view>\n#include <span>\n\n// string_view: zero-copy read-only string parameter\nvoid log(std::string_view msg)  // accepts std::string, const char*, literals\n{\n    std::cout << msg << '\\n';\n}\nlog(\"hello\");  // const char* -> string_view\nstd::string s = \"world\";\nlog(s);  // std::string -> string_view (no copy!)\n\n// span: non-owning view over contiguous data\nvoid sum(std::span<const int> data)\n{\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int x : data) total += x;\n}\nint arr[] =\n{\n    1, 2, 3\n};\nstd::vector<int> v =\n{\n    4, 5, 6\n};\nsum(arr);  // array -> span\nsum(v);  // vector -> span (no copy)"}], "pitfalls": ["Passing string_view.`data()` to a C function expecting null-termination — string_view is NOT null-terminated", "Storing a string_view/span beyond the lifetime of the referenced data (dangling view)", "Returning a string_view to a local `std::string` — dangling", "Using string_view as a key in a map that outlives the referenced string"], "tips_to_remember": ["string_view/span are non-owning — the referenced data must outlive the view", "string_view is not null-terminated — don't pass .`data()` to C string functions", "Prefer string_view for read-only string parameters (zero-copy)", "span is the modern replacement for (`T*` ptr, size_t n) function parameters"], "questions": [{"question": "Why is passing str_view.data() to strlen dangerous?", "answer": "std::string_view is not guaranteed to be null-terminated. It can refer to a substring of a larger buffer (e.g. a slice of a string). Passing its .data() pointer to strlen or other C functions that scan for a null terminator reads past the view's bounds — undefined behavior."}, {"question": "What is the difference between std::string_view and std::span?", "answer": "std::string_view is specifically for character sequences (strings) and provides string-like operations (find, substr, comparison). std::span is a general-purpose non-owning view over any contiguous sequence of objects (arrays, vectors). Both are non-owning and zero-copy."}, {"question": "When should you use std::span instead of (T* ptr, size_t n)?", "answer": "std::span bundles the pointer and size into a single type-safe parameter, preventing the classic 'forgot to pass the size' bug. It also supports range-based for loops, STL algorithms, and bounds-aware subviews. Use std::span for contiguous data."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_9_sub_4_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_9_sub_4_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_9_sub_4_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_9_sub_4_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `std::string_view & std::span — Non-Owning Views`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_9_sub_4_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_9_sub_4_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_9_sub_4_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_9_sub_4_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_9_sub_4_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_9_sub_4_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_9_sub_4", "day": 9, "index": 4}]}, {"day": 10, "topic": "Concurrency & Multithreading", "estimated_hours": 7, "subtopics": [{"title": "std::thread — join vs detach", "explanation": "`std::thread` represents a thread of execution. After construction, you must either `join()` (wait for completion) or `detach()` (let it run independently). If a joinable thread is destroyed without either, `std::terminate` is called.\n\njthread (C++20) is an improved thread that automatically joins in its destructor and supports cooperative cancellation via stop_token.\n\n### Unforgettable Analogy / Deep Dive\nThreads are like multiple chefs in a single kitchen sharing the same ingredients (heap/memory). A data race is when two chefs try to slice the same carrot at the same time. A Mutex is a lock on the kitchen counter — only one chef who holds the key can work on that counter.", "examples": [{"title": "Thread Basics", "code": "std::thread t([]{ std::cout << \"Hello from thread\\n\"; });\nt.join();  // wait for completion\n\n// C++20 jthread: auto-joins\nstd::jthread jt([]{ std::cout << \"Auto-joining\\n\"; });\n// No need to call join — jt's destructor does it", "explanation": "Always join or detach. Prefer jthread (C++20) for automatic join.", "formatted_code": "std::thread t([]\n{\n    std::cout << \"Hello from thread\\n\";\n}\n);\nt.join();  // wait for completion\n\n// C++20 jthread: auto-joins\nstd::jthread jt([]\n{\n    std::cout << \"Auto-joining\\n\";\n}\n);\n// No need to call join — jt's destructor does it"}], "pitfalls": ["Forgetting to join or detach — `std::terminate` on destruction", "Detaching a thread that accesses local variables — dangling references", "Not handling exceptions in threads — unhandled exception calls `std::terminate`"], "tips_to_remember": ["jthread (C++20) is the default — auto-joins and supports cancellation", "Never detach threads that reference local variables", "Use `join()` to wait for results, `detach()` for fire-and-forget"], "questions": [{"question": "What happens if you destroy a joinable thread without joining?", "answer": "std::terminate is called. This is by design — forgetting to join is a bug. C++20's jthread fixes this by joining in its destructor."}, {"question": "What is the difference between join and detach?", "answer": "join() blocks the calling thread until the target thread finishes. detach() lets the thread run independently (daemon thread) — but you lose the ability to wait for it or check its status."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the key difference between `std::thread` and `std::jthread` (C++20) on destruction?", "options": ["`std::jthread` allocates thread resources on the stack.", "`std::jthread` automatically joins and requests cooperative interruption on destruction, while `std::thread` calls `std::terminate` if it is still joinable.", "`std::thread` is faster to construct.", "`std::jthread` does not support lambda execution."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a `std::thread` is destroyed while it is still joinable (running and not joined/detached), it calls `std::terminate`, crashing the program. `std::jthread` automatically joins and cooperatively signals stop on destruction.", "id": "day_10_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What constitutes a data race in C++?", "options": ["Two threads executing different functions simultaneously.", "Two or more threads accessing the same memory location concurrently, where at least one access is a write, and without synchronization.", "Using global variables in a multithreaded application.", "When a thread fails to acquire a lock in time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A data race is defined as concurrent unsynchronized access to the same memory location where at least one thread is writing. Data races are undefined behavior in C++.", "id": "day_10_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why would you prefer `std::unique_lock` over `std::lock_guard`?", "options": ["`std::unique_lock` is faster.", "`std::unique_lock` provides advanced features like deferring lock, explicit unlocking, timeout locking, and move semantics, whereas `std::lock_guard` is strictly scoped.", "`std::lock_guard` causes deadlocks easily.", "`std::unique_lock` does not require a mutex reference."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::lock_guard` is a simple, zero-overhead wrapper for basic scoped locking. `std::unique_lock` has a small overhead but supports complex use cases like deferred locking, manual unlocking, and transferring locks.", "id": "day_10_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How does `std::scoped_lock` (C++17) prevent deadlocks when locking multiple mutexes?", "options": ["It compiles the mutexes into a single lock.", "It uses a deadlock-avoidance algorithm to lock all passed mutexes simultaneously.", "It runs a background thread to monitor locks.", "It converts mutexes to atomic variables."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::scoped_lock` can lock multiple mutexes. It uses the same deadlock avoidance algorithm as `std::lock` under the hood, ensuring they are acquired in a safe, sorted sequence.", "id": "day_10_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the key benefit of `std::atomic` variables?", "options": ["They make entire classes thread-safe.", "Operations on them are guaranteed to be atomic, preventing data races without needing a heavy mutex lock.", "They run code on separate CPU cores.", "They prevent memory allocations."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Operations on `std::atomic` variables are executed as single, indivisible CPU instructions, ensuring thread safety and preventing data races without the system-call overhead of mutexes.", "id": "day_10_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What is the default memory order for atomic operations in C++?", "options": ["`std::memory_order_relaxed`", "`std::memory_order_seq_cst` (Sequential Consistency)", "`std::memory_order_acquire`", "`std::memory_order_release`"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "By default, all atomic operations use `std::memory_order_seq_cst`. This provides sequential consistency, establishing a global, total ordering of all operations visible to all threads.", "id": "day_10_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What guarantees does `std::memory_order_relaxed` provide?", "options": ["It guarantees that operations are atomic, but provides no synchronization or ordering guarantees for surrounding memory accesses.", "It behaves exactly like sequentially consistent order but runs faster.", "It locks the CPU caches during execution.", "It is a runtime check that throws an exception on race."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "`memory_order_relaxed` only guarantees atomicity of the variable itself. The compiler and CPU are free to reorder surrounding read/write instructions around it, providing no synchronization between threads.", "id": "day_10_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "Why must a condition variable wait loop check the predicate condition (e.g. `cv.wait(lock, []{ return ready; })`)?", "options": ["To yield the CPU back to the OS.", "To prevent spurious wakeups, where the thread wakes up without receiving a signal.", "Because condition variables can only wake up once.", "To register the thread with the scheduler."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Condition variables are subject to 'spurious wakeups' due to OS implementation details. A thread can wake up even if no signal was sent. Thus, the thread must always re-verify the predicate condition.", "id": "day_10_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the relationship between `std::promise` and `std::future`?", "options": ["They are synonyms for the same class.", "`std::promise` sets a value or exception in a shared state, which `std::future` retrieves asynchronously.", "`std::future` is used to send values, `std::promise` is used to read them.", "They are used to create recursive mutex locks."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A promise is the writer-end and a future is the reader-end of an asynchronous channel. `std::promise` writes a value or exception to the shared channel state, which is retrieved via `std::future::get()`.", "id": "day_10_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does the `volatile` keyword provide thread synchronization or atomicity in C++?", "options": ["Yes, it makes variables thread-safe.", "No, `volatile` only prevents compiler register caching optimizations for hardware registers; it does NOT prevent data races or guarantee thread memory order.", "Yes, it acts as a lightweight atomic variable.", "No, it is a deprecated keyword in modern C++."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Unlike Java/C#, `volatile` in C++ has nothing to do with multithreading. It only instructs the compiler not to optimize away reads/writes to memory (useful for memory-mapped I/O). It does not generate atomic CPU operations or memory barriers.", "id": "day_10_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_10_sub_0", "day": 10, "index": 0}, {"title": "Mutexes and Locks", "explanation": "`std::mutex` protects shared data from data races. lock_guard is an **RAII** wrapper that locks in ctor and unlocks in dtor. unique_lock is more flexible (deferred locking, try-lock, timed lock). scoped_lock (C++17) locks multiple mutexes without deadlock.\n\nrecursive_mutex allows the same thread to lock multiple times (but is usually a code smell). timed_mutex supports try_lock_for/try_lock_until.\n\n### Unforgettable Analogy / Deep Dive\nThreads are like multiple chefs in a single kitchen sharing the same ingredients (heap/memory). A data race is when two chefs try to slice the same carrot at the same time. A Mutex is a lock on the kitchen counter — only one chef who holds the key can work on that counter.", "examples": [{"title": "RAII Locks", "code": "std::mutex mtx;\nint shared_data = 0;\n\nvoid increment()\n{\n    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);  // RAII lock\n    ++shared_data;\n}  // automatically unlocked\n\n// C++17 scoped_lock for multiple mutexes (deadlock-free)\nstd::mutex m1, m2;\nstd::scoped_lock lock(m1, m2);  // locks both, CTAD deduces types", "explanation": "Always use RAII locks. scoped_lock for multiple mutexes.", "formatted_code": "std::mutex mtx;\nint shared_data = 0;\n\nvoid increment()\n{\n    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);  // RAII lock\n    ++shared_data;\n}  // automatically unlocked\n\n// C++17 scoped_lock for multiple mutexes (deadlock-free)\nstd::mutex m1, m2;\nstd::scoped_lock lock(m1, m2);  // locks both, CTAD deduces types"}], "pitfalls": ["Locking mutexes in different orders in different threads — deadlock", "Holding a lock while calling unknown code (callbacks) — potential deadlock", "Using recursive_mutex as a band-aid for poor design"], "tips_to_remember": ["Always use **RAII** locks (lock_guard/scoped_lock) — never raw `lock()`/`unlock()`", "Lock multiple mutexes with scoped_lock to avoid deadlock", "Keep critical sections as short as possible", "recursive_mutex is usually a design smell — redesign instead"], "questions": [{"question": "How does scoped_lock prevent deadlock?", "answer": "scoped_lock uses a deadlock avoidance algorithm (like std::lock) to lock multiple mutexes simultaneously. It doesn't matter what order you specify them — it avoids the circular-wait deadlock condition."}, {"question": "When should you use unique_lock vs lock_guard?", "answer": "lock_guard for simple scope-based locking. unique_lock when you need deferred locking, try_lock, timed lock, or passing the lock to a condition_variable."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the key difference between `std::thread` and `std::jthread` (C++20) on destruction?", "options": ["`std::jthread` allocates thread resources on the stack.", "`std::jthread` automatically joins and requests cooperative interruption on destruction, while `std::thread` calls `std::terminate` if it is still joinable.", "`std::thread` is faster to construct.", "`std::jthread` does not support lambda execution."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a `std::thread` is destroyed while it is still joinable (running and not joined/detached), it calls `std::terminate`, crashing the program. `std::jthread` automatically joins and cooperatively signals stop on destruction.", "id": "day_10_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What constitutes a data race in C++?", "options": ["Two threads executing different functions simultaneously.", "Two or more threads accessing the same memory location concurrently, where at least one access is a write, and without synchronization.", "Using global variables in a multithreaded application.", "When a thread fails to acquire a lock in time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A data race is defined as concurrent unsynchronized access to the same memory location where at least one thread is writing. Data races are undefined behavior in C++.", "id": "day_10_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why would you prefer `std::unique_lock` over `std::lock_guard`?", "options": ["`std::unique_lock` is faster.", "`std::unique_lock` provides advanced features like deferring lock, explicit unlocking, timeout locking, and move semantics, whereas `std::lock_guard` is strictly scoped.", "`std::lock_guard` causes deadlocks easily.", "`std::unique_lock` does not require a mutex reference."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::lock_guard` is a simple, zero-overhead wrapper for basic scoped locking. `std::unique_lock` has a small overhead but supports complex use cases like deferred locking, manual unlocking, and transferring locks.", "id": "day_10_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How does `std::scoped_lock` (C++17) prevent deadlocks when locking multiple mutexes?", "options": ["It compiles the mutexes into a single lock.", "It uses a deadlock-avoidance algorithm to lock all passed mutexes simultaneously.", "It runs a background thread to monitor locks.", "It converts mutexes to atomic variables."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::scoped_lock` can lock multiple mutexes. It uses the same deadlock avoidance algorithm as `std::lock` under the hood, ensuring they are acquired in a safe, sorted sequence.", "id": "day_10_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the key benefit of `std::atomic` variables?", "options": ["They make entire classes thread-safe.", "Operations on them are guaranteed to be atomic, preventing data races without needing a heavy mutex lock.", "They run code on separate CPU cores.", "They prevent memory allocations."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Operations on `std::atomic` variables are executed as single, indivisible CPU instructions, ensuring thread safety and preventing data races without the system-call overhead of mutexes.", "id": "day_10_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What is the default memory order for atomic operations in C++?", "options": ["`std::memory_order_relaxed`", "`std::memory_order_seq_cst` (Sequential Consistency)", "`std::memory_order_acquire`", "`std::memory_order_release`"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "By default, all atomic operations use `std::memory_order_seq_cst`. This provides sequential consistency, establishing a global, total ordering of all operations visible to all threads.", "id": "day_10_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What guarantees does `std::memory_order_relaxed` provide?", "options": ["It guarantees that operations are atomic, but provides no synchronization or ordering guarantees for surrounding memory accesses.", "It behaves exactly like sequentially consistent order but runs faster.", "It locks the CPU caches during execution.", "It is a runtime check that throws an exception on race."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "`memory_order_relaxed` only guarantees atomicity of the variable itself. The compiler and CPU are free to reorder surrounding read/write instructions around it, providing no synchronization between threads.", "id": "day_10_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "Why must a condition variable wait loop check the predicate condition (e.g. `cv.wait(lock, []{ return ready; })`)?", "options": ["To yield the CPU back to the OS.", "To prevent spurious wakeups, where the thread wakes up without receiving a signal.", "Because condition variables can only wake up once.", "To register the thread with the scheduler."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Condition variables are subject to 'spurious wakeups' due to OS implementation details. A thread can wake up even if no signal was sent. Thus, the thread must always re-verify the predicate condition.", "id": "day_10_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the relationship between `std::promise` and `std::future`?", "options": ["They are synonyms for the same class.", "`std::promise` sets a value or exception in a shared state, which `std::future` retrieves asynchronously.", "`std::future` is used to send values, `std::promise` is used to read them.", "They are used to create recursive mutex locks."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A promise is the writer-end and a future is the reader-end of an asynchronous channel. `std::promise` writes a value or exception to the shared channel state, which is retrieved via `std::future::get()`.", "id": "day_10_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does the `volatile` keyword provide thread synchronization or atomicity in C++?", "options": ["Yes, it makes variables thread-safe.", "No, `volatile` only prevents compiler register caching optimizations for hardware registers; it does NOT prevent data races or guarantee thread memory order.", "Yes, it acts as a lightweight atomic variable.", "No, it is a deprecated keyword in modern C++."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Unlike Java/C#, `volatile` in C++ has nothing to do with multithreading. It only instructs the compiler not to optimize away reads/writes to memory (useful for memory-mapped I/O). It does not generate atomic CPU operations or memory barriers.", "id": "day_10_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_10_sub_1", "day": 10, "index": 1}, {"title": "std::atomic & Memory Ordering", "explanation": "`std::atomic<T>` provides lock-free (or lock-based) atomic operations on a variable. memory_order controls how non-atomic memory operations are ordered around atomic ones.\n\nrelaxed: no ordering constraints. acquire/release: synchronizes producer/consumer. seq_cst (default): total order across all threads. Use the weakest ordering that's correct — stronger ordering is slower on weakly-ordered architectures (ARM).", "examples": [{"title": "Atomic Operations", "code": "std::atomic<int> counter{0};\n\n// Multiple threads can safely increment:\nvoid worker()\n{\n    counter.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);  // atomic increment\n}\n\n// Acquire/Release for producer-consumer:\nstd::atomic<bool> ready{false};\nint data = 0;\n\nvoid producer()\n{\n    data = 42;  // non-atomic write\n    ready.store(true, std::memory_order_release);  // publishes data\n}\n\nvoid consumer()\n{\n    while (!ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {}  // waits for publish\n    assert(data == 42);  // guaranteed to see 42\n}", "explanation": "Acquire/release synchronizes data between threads without full seq_cst overhead.", "formatted_code": "std::atomic<int> counter\n{\n    0\n};\n\n// Multiple threads can safely increment:\nvoid worker()\n{\n    counter.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);  // atomic increment\n}\n\n// Acquire/Release for producer-consumer:\nstd::atomic<bool> ready\n{\n    false\n};\nint data = 0;\n\nvoid producer()\n{\n    data = 42;  // non-atomic write\n    ready.store(true, std::memory_order_release);  // publishes data\n}\n\nvoid consumer()\n{\n    while (!ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire))\n    {\n    }  // waits for publish\n    assert(data == 42);  // guaranteed to see 42\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["Using relaxed ordering when you need synchronization — data races", "Assuming atomic makes surrounding non-atomic accesses safe (only with proper ordering)", "Lock-free != wait-free — lock-free can still starve"], "tips_to_remember": ["seq_cst is the default and safest — use weaker orderings only with expertise", "`atomic<bool>` is a valid alternative to mutex for simple flags", "Atomic operations are not composable — two atomic ops aren't automatically atomic together", "Use atomic_ref (C++20) for atomic access to non-atomic variables"], "questions": [{"question": "What does memory_order_acquire/release mean?", "answer": "Release: all writes before this store are visible to any thread that acquires the same atomic. Acquire: all writes by the releasing thread are visible after this load. Together they form a producer-consumer synchronization pair."}, {"question": "Is std::atomic lock-free?", "answer": "It depends on the type and platform. atomic<int> and atomic<ptr> are typically lock-free. atomic<large_struct> probably isn't. Check with atomic<T>::is_lock_free() or is_always_lock_free."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the key difference between `std::thread` and `std::jthread` (C++20) on destruction?", "options": ["`std::jthread` allocates thread resources on the stack.", "`std::jthread` automatically joins and requests cooperative interruption on destruction, while `std::thread` calls `std::terminate` if it is still joinable.", "`std::thread` is faster to construct.", "`std::jthread` does not support lambda execution."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a `std::thread` is destroyed while it is still joinable (running and not joined/detached), it calls `std::terminate`, crashing the program. `std::jthread` automatically joins and cooperatively signals stop on destruction.", "id": "day_10_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What constitutes a data race in C++?", "options": ["Two threads executing different functions simultaneously.", "Two or more threads accessing the same memory location concurrently, where at least one access is a write, and without synchronization.", "Using global variables in a multithreaded application.", "When a thread fails to acquire a lock in time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A data race is defined as concurrent unsynchronized access to the same memory location where at least one thread is writing. Data races are undefined behavior in C++.", "id": "day_10_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why would you prefer `std::unique_lock` over `std::lock_guard`?", "options": ["`std::unique_lock` is faster.", "`std::unique_lock` provides advanced features like deferring lock, explicit unlocking, timeout locking, and move semantics, whereas `std::lock_guard` is strictly scoped.", "`std::lock_guard` causes deadlocks easily.", "`std::unique_lock` does not require a mutex reference."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::lock_guard` is a simple, zero-overhead wrapper for basic scoped locking. `std::unique_lock` has a small overhead but supports complex use cases like deferred locking, manual unlocking, and transferring locks.", "id": "day_10_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How does `std::scoped_lock` (C++17) prevent deadlocks when locking multiple mutexes?", "options": ["It compiles the mutexes into a single lock.", "It uses a deadlock-avoidance algorithm to lock all passed mutexes simultaneously.", "It runs a background thread to monitor locks.", "It converts mutexes to atomic variables."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::scoped_lock` can lock multiple mutexes. It uses the same deadlock avoidance algorithm as `std::lock` under the hood, ensuring they are acquired in a safe, sorted sequence.", "id": "day_10_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the key benefit of `std::atomic` variables?", "options": ["They make entire classes thread-safe.", "Operations on them are guaranteed to be atomic, preventing data races without needing a heavy mutex lock.", "They run code on separate CPU cores.", "They prevent memory allocations."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Operations on `std::atomic` variables are executed as single, indivisible CPU instructions, ensuring thread safety and preventing data races without the system-call overhead of mutexes.", "id": "day_10_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What is the default memory order for atomic operations in C++?", "options": ["`std::memory_order_relaxed`", "`std::memory_order_seq_cst` (Sequential Consistency)", "`std::memory_order_acquire`", "`std::memory_order_release`"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "By default, all atomic operations use `std::memory_order_seq_cst`. This provides sequential consistency, establishing a global, total ordering of all operations visible to all threads.", "id": "day_10_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What guarantees does `std::memory_order_relaxed` provide?", "options": ["It guarantees that operations are atomic, but provides no synchronization or ordering guarantees for surrounding memory accesses.", "It behaves exactly like sequentially consistent order but runs faster.", "It locks the CPU caches during execution.", "It is a runtime check that throws an exception on race."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "`memory_order_relaxed` only guarantees atomicity of the variable itself. The compiler and CPU are free to reorder surrounding read/write instructions around it, providing no synchronization between threads.", "id": "day_10_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "Why must a condition variable wait loop check the predicate condition (e.g. `cv.wait(lock, []{ return ready; })`)?", "options": ["To yield the CPU back to the OS.", "To prevent spurious wakeups, where the thread wakes up without receiving a signal.", "Because condition variables can only wake up once.", "To register the thread with the scheduler."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Condition variables are subject to 'spurious wakeups' due to OS implementation details. A thread can wake up even if no signal was sent. Thus, the thread must always re-verify the predicate condition.", "id": "day_10_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the relationship between `std::promise` and `std::future`?", "options": ["They are synonyms for the same class.", "`std::promise` sets a value or exception in a shared state, which `std::future` retrieves asynchronously.", "`std::future` is used to send values, `std::promise` is used to read them.", "They are used to create recursive mutex locks."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A promise is the writer-end and a future is the reader-end of an asynchronous channel. `std::promise` writes a value or exception to the shared channel state, which is retrieved via `std::future::get()`.", "id": "day_10_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does the `volatile` keyword provide thread synchronization or atomicity in C++?", "options": ["Yes, it makes variables thread-safe.", "No, `volatile` only prevents compiler register caching optimizations for hardware registers; it does NOT prevent data races or guarantee thread memory order.", "Yes, it acts as a lightweight atomic variable.", "No, it is a deprecated keyword in modern C++."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Unlike Java/C#, `volatile` in C++ has nothing to do with multithreading. It only instructs the compiler not to optimize away reads/writes to memory (useful for memory-mapped I/O). It does not generate atomic CPU operations or memory barriers.", "id": "day_10_sub_2_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_10_sub_2", "day": 10, "index": 2}, {"title": "False Sharing", "explanation": "False sharing occurs when two threads modify different variables that happen to reside on the same cache line (typically 64 bytes). Each modification invalidates the cache line for the other core, causing expensive cache-coherency traffic even though the data is logically independent.\n\nSolution: pad data to cache line boundaries using `alignas(64)` or `std::hardware_destructive_interference_size` (C++17).", "examples": [{"title": "False Sharing Fix", "code": "// BAD: false sharing — counters on same cache line\nstruct Counters\n{\n    std::atomic<int> a;  // likely on same 64-byte cache line\n    std::atomic<int> b;\n};\n\n// GOOD: padded to separate cache lines\nstruct PaddedCounters\n{\n    alignas(64) std::atomic<int> a;\n    alignas(64) std::atomic<int> b;\n};", "explanation": "Pad to cache line boundaries to prevent false sharing.", "formatted_code": "// BAD: false sharing — counters on same cache line\nstruct Counters\n{\n    std::atomic<int> a;  // likely on same 64-byte cache line\n    std::atomic<int> b;\n};\n\n// GOOD: padded to separate cache lines\nstruct PaddedCounters\n{\n    alignas(64) std::atomic<int> a;\n    alignas(64) std::atomic<int> b;\n};"}], "pitfalls": ["Not recognizing false sharing — silent performance degradation", "Over-padding wasting memory", "Assuming `std::hardware_destructive_interference_size` is always available"], "tips_to_remember": ["False sharing = different variables on same cache line modified by different threads", "Fix: `alignas(64)` or `alignas(`std::hardware_destructive_interference_size`)`", "Profile with perf c2c or VTune to detect false sharing"], "questions": [{"question": "What is false sharing?", "answer": "When two threads modify variables on the same cache line. Each write invalidates the line for other cores, causing ping-ponging even though the data is logically independent. Can cause 10-100x slowdown."}, {"question": "How do you detect and fix false sharing?", "answer": "Detect: profiling tools (perf c2c, VTune). Fix: align each thread's data to separate cache lines using alignas(64). C++17 provides std::hardware_destructive_interference_size for portable cache line size."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_10_sub_3_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_10_sub_3_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_10_sub_3_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_10_sub_3_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `False Sharing`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_10_sub_3_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_10_sub_3_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_10_sub_3_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_10_sub_3_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_10_sub_3_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_10_sub_3_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_10_sub_3", "day": 10, "index": 3}]}, {"day": 11, "topic": "Cache & Performance", "estimated_hours": 5, "subtopics": [{"title": "Cache Lines, Spatial/Temporal Locality", "explanation": "CPU caches are organized in cache lines (typically 64 bytes). When you access a byte, the entire 64-byte cache line is loaded. Spatial locality: accessing nearby memory (sequential array traversal) is fast because adjacent data is in the same line. Temporal locality: recently accessed data is likely still in cache.\n\nData structures with good spatial locality (arrays, vectors) are much faster than those with poor locality (linked lists, trees with node pointers) due to cache effects.", "examples": [{"title": "Locality", "code": "// Good spatial locality — sequential access\nstd::vector<int> v(1000000);\nfor (auto& x : v) x *= 2;  // cache-friendly\n\n// Poor spatial locality — random pointer chasing\nstd::list<int> l;\nfor (auto& x : l) x *= 2;  // each node may be in a different cache line\n\n// Benchmark shows vector is often 10-100x faster than list for iteration", "explanation": "Arrays have excellent spatial locality. Linked lists have terrible locality.", "formatted_code": "// Good spatial locality — sequential access\nstd::vector<int> v(1000000);\nfor (auto& x : v) x *= 2;  // cache-friendly\n\n// Poor spatial locality — random pointer chasing\nstd::list<int> l;\nfor (auto& x : l) x *= 2;  // each node may be in a different cache line\n\n// Benchmark shows vector is often 10-100x faster than list for iteration"}], "pitfalls": ["Assuming theoretical `O(1)` operations are always fast — cache misses dominate", "Ignoring memory layout when designing data structures", "Premature optimization — always profile first"], "tips_to_remember": ["Cache line is typically 64 bytes — access patterns that stay within lines are fast", "Sequential access is fast (prefetcher detects patterns). Random access is slow.", "vector > list for almost everything due to cache effects", "Measure, don't guess — use cachegrind or VTune"], "questions": [{"question": "Why is std::vector usually faster than std::list?", "answer": "vector stores elements contiguously — the CPU prefetcher can predict sequential access patterns and preload cache lines. list stores nodes scattered in memory — each next-pointer dereference may be a cache miss."}, {"question": "What is a cache line?", "answer": "The unit of data transfer between cache levels. Typically 64 bytes. When you access one byte, the entire 64-byte line is loaded. Accessing other bytes in the same line is essentially free."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What causes false sharing (cache line bouncing) in multithreaded systems?", "options": ["Multiple threads trying to modify the same global atomic variable.", "Two or more threads on different cores modifying independent variables that happen to reside on the same CPU cache line.", "When threads are run on separate physical CPUs.", "Lock contention on a shared mutex."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "CPU caches track memory in blocks called cache lines (typically 64 bytes). If two threads modify separate variables on the same cache line, the cores constantly invalidate each other's cache line copy, causing severe cache line bouncing.", "id": "day_11_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "How can you prevent false sharing in C++17?", "options": ["Wrap variables inside a `std::mutex`.", "Align variables using `alignas(std::hardware_destructive_interference_size)`.", "Use `std::atomic` variables.", "Compile with `-O3` optimization level."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++17 introduced `std::hardware_destructive_interference_size` which specifies the minimum byte alignment needed to prevent variables from sharing the same cache line (usually 64 bytes).", "id": "day_11_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the memory size of `struct S { char c; int i; };` on a standard 32/64-bit system?", "options": ["5 bytes (1 byte for char, 4 bytes for int).", "8 bytes, because the compiler adds 3 bytes of padding to align the 4-byte `int` on a 4-byte address boundary.", "12 bytes, due to vptr alignment.", "16 bytes."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To optimize CPU access, the compiler aligns data types to their natural sizes. A 4-byte integer must start on an address multiple of 4, forcing the insertion of 3 padding bytes after the 1-byte character.", "id": "day_11_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does returning `std::move(local)` from a function prevent NRVO (Named Return Value Optimization)?", "options": ["It copies the object instead of moving it.", "It casts the local variable to an rvalue reference (xvalue), which matches a move constructor call instead of triggering direct copy elision.", "The compiler does not optimize move-only types.", "It causes a stack overflow."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Returning a local by value allows the compiler to construct the return value directly in the caller's stack frame (NRVO). Using `std::move(local)` forces the function to return a reference (`T&&`), forcing a move constructor call and preventing NRVO.", "id": "day_11_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Does marking a function `inline` guarantee it will be inlined by the compiler?", "options": ["Yes, it is a mandatory instruction to the compiler.", "No, it is only a optimization hint; the compiler makes the final decision based on code size and heuristics.", "No, `inline` is only used to allow defining functions in header files without violating ODR.", "Yes, but only in debug builds."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`inline` has two meanings: it hints to the compiler to inline code (optional), and it allows the function to be defined in multiple translation units (such as in header files) without violating the One Definition Rule (ODR).", "id": "day_11_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What is the strict aliasing rule in C++?", "options": ["Pointers can only point to objects of the exact same type (ignoring const and signedness). Dereferencing pointers to incompatible types is UB.", "Variables must be named strictly according to their type.", "You cannot point two reference variables to the same object.", "It requires all pointers to be initialized to `nullptr`."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "The strict aliasing rule states that two pointers of incompatible types cannot point to the same memory location, allowing the compiler to assume that writing to one does not modify the other, enabling optimization.", "id": "day_11_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of alignment in C++ memory management?", "options": ["To sort pointers in alphabetical order.", "To ensure objects are allocated at memory addresses that are multiples of their alignment size, enabling fast CPU fetches.", "To match the type boundaries of template classes.", "To prevent memory leaks."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "CPUs fetch memory in aligned blocks. If data is unaligned, a single read might span two cache lines, requiring two memory fetches instead of one. Alignment guarantees optimal fetch speeds.", "id": "day_11_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "When is Return Value Optimization (RVO) guaranteed by the standard (C++17 onwards)?", "options": ["When a function returns a named local variable.", "When a function returns a temporary prvalue of the same type as the return type.", "Only when compiler optimization is set to `-O2` or higher.", "When returning pointers to heap objects."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since C++17, RVO is guaranteed (not just an optimization pass) when returning a prvalue (temporary object). The compiler is required to construct the object directly in the target storage, bypassing copies/moves.", "id": "day_11_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the penalty of unaligned memory access on modern CPU architectures?", "options": ["It causes a compilation warning.", "It can cause slower execution speed due to double cache fetches, or trigger a hardware alignment fault crash on strict architectures.", "It increases the binary file size.", "It disables multi-core execution."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Some architectures (like x86) handle unaligned reads with a performance penalty by doing two fetches. Others (like ARM) might trigger a hardware misaligned-memory-access exception, crashing the program.", "id": "day_11_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Why is the loop condition `for (int i = 0; i < vec.size(); ++i)` sometimes inefficient compared to caching the size?", "options": ["Because `size()` has O(n) complexity.", "If `size()` is not inline, or the compiler cannot verify that the vector size is constant (e.g. if loop body writes to pointers), it must call `size()` every iteration.", "Because `size()` allocates memory on the stack.", "There is no difference; compilers always optimize this."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If the compiler cannot prove that the vector size doesn't change during the loop (for example, if you write to raw pointers in the loop), it is forced to re-call `size()` on every iteration, wasting CPU cycles.", "id": "day_11_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_11_sub_0", "day": 11, "index": 0}, {"title": "Data-Oriented Design — AoS vs SoA", "explanation": "**Array of Structs** (**AoS**): struct { float x, y, z; } positions[N]. **Struct of Arrays** (**SoA**): struct { float x[N], y[N], z[N]; }. **SoA** has better cache utilization when you process one field at a time (e.g., update all x coordinates) because the data is contiguous.\n\n**SoA** is common in game engines, physics simulations, and SIMD-heavy code. **AoS** is more intuitive and better when you always access all fields together.", "examples": [{"title": "AoS vs SoA", "code": "// AoS — intuitive but wastes cache when processing single field\nstruct Particle { float x, y, z, mass; };\nstd::vector<Particle> particles(10000);\nfor (auto& p : particles) p.x += 1.0f;  // loads y,z,mass too (wasted)\n\n// SoA — cache-friendly for per-field processing\nstruct Particles\n{\n    std::vector<float> x, y, z, mass;\n};\nParticles ps;\nfor (auto& xi : ps.x) xi += 1.0f;  // only loads x data — cache-optimal", "explanation": "SoA processes one field at a time without loading unrelated data.", "formatted_code": "// AoS — intuitive but wastes cache when processing single field\nstruct Particle\n{\n    float x, y, z, mass;\n};\nstd::vector<Particle> particles(10000);\nfor (auto& p : particles) p.x += 1.0f;  // loads y,z,mass too (wasted)\n\n// SoA — cache-friendly for per-field processing\nstruct Particles\n{\n    std::vector<float> x, y, z, mass;\n};\nParticles ps;\nfor (auto& xi : ps.x) xi += 1.0f;  // only loads x data — cache-optimal"}], "pitfalls": ["**SoA** is harder to maintain — adding a field `requires` updating all arrays", "**AoS** wastes cache bandwidth when only some fields are accessed", "Premature **SoA** — only worth it for hot loops identified by profiling"], "tips_to_remember": ["Profile first. **SoA** is worth it for hot loops processing single fields.", "Game engines and physics engines commonly use **SoA** for particle systems", "Consider hybrid: **AoS** for cold data, **SoA** for hot data"], "questions": [{"question": "When should you use SoA over AoS?", "answer": "When profiling shows that you frequently iterate over large arrays accessing only a subset of fields. SoA avoids loading unused fields into cache. Common in game physics, particle systems, and SIMD-heavy code."}, {"question": "What is data-oriented design?", "answer": "A programming paradigm that organizes data for efficient processing rather than for object-oriented modeling. Focus on data layout, access patterns, and cache utilization. SoA is a key technique."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_11_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_11_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_11_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_11_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Data-Oriented Design — AoS vs SoA`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_11_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_11_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_11_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_11_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_11_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_11_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_11_sub_1", "day": 11, "index": 1}, {"title": "Branch Prediction — [[likely]]/[[unlikely]]", "explanation": "Modern CPUs speculatively execute one branch before the condition is evaluated. Mispredictions flush the pipeline (~15 cycles penalty). [[likely]] and [[unlikely]] (C++20) hint to the compiler which branch is hot.\n\nSorted data is faster to process than unsorted when the processing involves branches — the branch predictor learns the pattern.", "examples": [{"title": "Branch Hints", "code": "int process(int x)\n{\n    if (x > 0) [[likely]] {  // compiler optimizes for this path\n        return x * 2;\n    } else [[unlikely]] {\n        return handleError(x);\n    }\n}\n\n// Classic branch prediction example:\nstd::vector<int> data(100000);\n// ... fill with random values ...\nstd::sort(data.begin(), data.end());  // sorting makes branches predictable!\n\nint sum = 0;\nfor (int x : data)\n    if (x > 50000) sum += x;  // branch predictor learns pattern after sorting", "explanation": "[[likely]]/[[unlikely]] hint the compiler. Sorted data helps the branch predictor.", "formatted_code": "int process(int x)\n{\n    if (x > 0) [[likely]]\n    {  // compiler optimizes for this path\n    return x * 2;\n}\nelse [[unlikely]]\n{\n    return handleError(x);\n}\n}\n\n// Classic branch prediction example:\nstd::vector<int> data(100000);\n// ... fill with random values ...\nstd::sort(data.begin(), data.end());  // sorting makes branches predictable!\n\nint sum = 0;\nfor (int x : data)\nif (x > 50000) sum += x;  // branch predictor learns pattern after sorting"}], "pitfalls": ["Over-using [[likely]]/[[unlikely]] without profiling", "Reorganizing code to avoid branches can be more effective than hints", "Branch prediction is a hardware feature — hints only affect code layout"], "tips_to_remember": ["[[likely]] and [[unlikely]] are hints, not guarantees", "Branchless code (conditional moves, arithmetic) avoids misprediction entirely", "Profile with perf stat to check branch misprediction rates"], "questions": [{"question": "What is branch prediction?", "answer": "CPU hardware that speculates which way a branch will go before the condition is evaluated. Correct prediction: zero overhead. Misprediction: pipeline flush, ~15 cycle penalty."}, {"question": "How do [[likely]]/[[unlikely]] work?", "answer": "They're C++20 attributes that hint to the compiler which branch is more probable. The compiler uses this for code layout optimization (putting the likely path in the fall-through position, improving instruction cache utilization)."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_11_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_11_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_11_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_11_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Branch Prediction — [[likely]]/[[unlikely]]`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_11_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_11_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_11_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_11_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_11_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_11_sub_2_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_11_sub_2", "day": 11, "index": 2}]}, {"day": 12, "topic": "Compilation, Linking & Build", "estimated_hours": 5, "subtopics": [{"title": "Compilation Pipeline", "explanation": "Four stages: (1) Preprocessing: `#include expansion`, macro substitution, conditional compilation. (2) Compilation: C++ source → assembly. (3) Assembly: assembly → object files (.o/.obj). (4) Linking: combines object files, resolves symbols, produces executable/library.\n\nEach .cpp file is compiled independently. The linker is the first tool that sees all TUs together.", "examples": [{"title": "Pipeline", "code": "// Preprocess: g++ -E main.cpp > main.i\n// Compile:    g++ -S main.cpp → main.s (assembly)\n// Assemble:   g++ -c main.cpp → main.o (object file)\n// Link:       g++ main.o utils.o -o app (executable)\n\n// All at once:\n// g++ main.cpp utils.cpp -o app", "explanation": "Understanding the pipeline helps debug compilation and linking errors.", "formatted_code": "// Preprocess: g++ -E main.cpp > main.i\n// Compile:    g++ -S main.cpp → main.s (assembly)\n// Assemble:   g++ -c main.cpp → main.o (object file)\n// Link:       g++ main.o utils.o -o app (executable)\n\n// All at once:\n// g++ main.cpp utils.cpp -o app"}], "pitfalls": ["Confusing compiler errors (wrong syntax) with linker errors (undefined reference)", "Include order dependencies from transitive `#includes`", "Not understanding that each .cpp compiles independently"], "tips_to_remember": ["Compiler error: code issue. Linker error: missing definition or multiple definitions", "Use -E to see preprocessed output, -S for assembly, -c for object files only", "Translation units are independent — symbols connect only at link time"], "questions": [{"question": "What are the four stages of C++ compilation?", "answer": "1) Preprocessing (#include, macros, conditionals), 2) Compilation (C++ to assembly), 3) Assembly (assembly to object code), 4) Linking (combine objects, resolve symbols, produce binary)."}, {"question": "What is the difference between a compiler error and a linker error?", "answer": "Compiler error: syntax/type/semantic issue within one TU. Linker error: symbol not found across TUs (undefined reference) or defined multiple times (multiple definition)."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the exact outcome of violating the One Definition Rule (ODR) for an entity across different translation units in C++ when studying `Compilation Pipeline`?", "options": ["The compiler will fail to compile with a duplicate definition error.", "The linker will always fail to link with a multiple definition error.", "The program compiles and links successfully, but triggers Undefined Behavior at runtime.", "The program is guaranteed to run correctly if all optimizations are disabled."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "ODR violations across different translation units are undefined behavior (UB). Linkers are not required to diagnose them, and they often silently select one definition, leading to random runtime crashes or wrong results.", "id": "day_12_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT a translation unit in C++?", "options": ["A single `.cpp` file after preprocessing (with headers expanded).", "An individual `.h` header file compiled by itself as a source.", "A source file with all its included headers, after macros have been replaced.", "A temporary preprocessed file (`.i`) generated by the compiler's preprocessor."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A header file by itself is not a translation unit. A translation unit is a source file (.cpp, .cc) with all its included headers expanded by the preprocessor.", "id": "day_12_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What happens if a header with `#pragma once` is copied under a different name to another directory, both are included in a `.cpp` file, and we build `Compilation Pipeline`?", "options": ["The compiler ignores the duplicate header because the contents are identical.", "Both headers are included and parsed, potentially causing redefinition errors.", "The linker throws a duplicate symbol error at link time.", "The preprocessor automatically merges the duplicate files."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`#pragma once` is file-path or file-inode identity based. If the file is copied to a different path with a different name, `#pragma once` cannot detect it as the same file, so it is included twice, potentially causing redefinition errors.", "id": "day_12_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "If you define a non-inline global variable `int x = 42;` in a header file, and that header is included by two `.cpp` files, what error occurs?", "options": ["A compilation error in both translation units.", "A linker error for multiple definitions of symbol `x`.", "No error, the variables are merged into a single static storage.", "A runtime access violation when `x` is modified."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Each `.cpp` file including the header defines `x` as a global variable with external linkage. When the linker combines the object files, it finds duplicate definitions for symbol `x` and fails to link.", "id": "day_12_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "In C++, what linkage type does a namespace-scope `static` variable or function have?", "options": ["External linkage — visible across all translation units.", "Internal linkage — local to the translation unit it is defined in.", "No linkage — only visible within the block scope.", "Dynamic linkage — resolved at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The `static` keyword at namespace scope gives the entity internal linkage, meaning its name is only visible and usable within the translation unit where it is defined.", "id": "day_12_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Why are anonymous namespaces preferred over the `static` keyword for internal linkage in modern C++?", "options": ["Anonymous namespaces are faster at compile time.", "`static` cannot be applied to user-defined types (like classes or structs), but anonymous namespaces can.", "Anonymous namespaces give external linkage but keep the names hidden.", "`static` is deprecated in C++20."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "You cannot declare a `static class` or `static struct` at namespace scope to give it internal linkage in C++. Anonymous namespaces solve this by giving internal linkage to all enclosed names, including types, variables, and functions.", "id": "day_12_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the expansion result of the macro `#define MULTIPLY(x, y) x * y` when invoked as `MULTIPLY(1 + 2, 3 + 4)`?", "options": ["21 (7 * 3)", "11 (1 + 2 * 3 + 4)", "9 (1 + 2 * 4)", "Compilation error due to invalid operator combination."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Macros perform raw textual substitution. `MULTIPLY(1 + 2, 3 + 4)` expands literally to `1 + 2 * 3 + 4`. Due to operator precedence, multiplication runs first (`2 * 3 = 6`), yielding `1 + 6 + 4 = 11`. Always parenthesize macro parameters: `((x) * (y))`.", "id": "day_12_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is the static initialization order fiasco in C++?", "options": ["Static local variables are initialized in random order across threads.", "The order of initialization of static variables within the same translation unit is undefined.", "The order of initialization of non-local static variables in different translation units is undefined.", "Compiler optimization silently reorders global constructor calls."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Within a single translation unit, static variables are initialized in order of definition. However, between different translation units, the order of initialization of non-local static variables is undefined, which can cause one static variable to use another uninitialized one.", "id": "day_12_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "Which C++17 feature resolves the header redefinition problem for global constants without violating ODR?", "options": ["`inline` variables.", "`constexpr` functions.", "Anonymous namespaces.", "External template instantiation."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "C++17 introduced `inline` variables. If a variable is declared `inline` (e.g. `inline constexpr int MaxValue = 100;`), it can be defined in multiple translation units (such as via header inclusion) and the linker will merge all definitions into a single object.", "id": "day_12_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "How do C++20 modules affect macro visibility compared to `#include` headers?", "options": ["Modules export all macros defined inside them by default.", "Macros defined in modules are only visible if the module is imported using `import`.", "Modules do not export macros unless explicitly specified, preventing macro pollution.", "Modules completely disable macro substitution."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "One of the major benefits of C++20 modules is that they do not leak macros. A module file can define macros for internal use, but they will not be exported to the importing file, eliminating name pollution.", "id": "day_12_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_12_sub_0", "day": 12, "index": 0}, {"title": "Static vs Dynamic Linking, ABI Compatibility", "explanation": "Static linking: library code is copied into the executable. Larger binary, no runtime dependency, better optimization (LTO). Dynamic linking: library loaded at runtime (.so/.dll). Smaller binary, shared across processes, can update library without recompiling.\n\nABI (Application Binary Interface) compatibility means a new version of a shared library works with existing binaries without recompilation. Breaking ABI: changing class layout, `virtual` function order, function signatures. ABI stability is a major concern for shared libraries.", "examples": [{"title": "Static vs Dynamic", "code": "// Static linking:\n// g++ main.cpp -static -lmylib\n// Binary includes all of mylib — no runtime dependency\n\n// Dynamic linking:\n// g++ main.cpp -lmylib\n// Binary references mylib.so — must be present at runtime\n\n// ABI-breaking changes:\n// - Adding/removing virtual functions\n// - Changing member variable types or order\n// - Changing function signatures\n// PIMPL idiom helps maintain ABI stability", "explanation": "Static = self-contained. Dynamic = smaller, shareable, updateable.", "formatted_code": "// Static linking:\n// g++ main.cpp -static -lmylib\n// Binary includes all of mylib — no runtime dependency\n\n// Dynamic linking:\n// g++ main.cpp -lmylib\n// Binary references mylib.so — must be present at runtime\n\n// ABI-breaking changes:\n// - Adding/removing virtual functions\n// - Changing member variable types or order\n// - Changing function signatures\n// PIMPL idiom helps maintain ABI stability"}], "pitfalls": ["Breaking ABI of a shared library — all users must recompile", "Missing .dll/.so at runtime — program won't start", "Symbol versioning issues with different library versions"], "tips_to_remember": ["Static: no runtime deps, larger binary. Dynamic: shared, smaller, updateable", "ABI stability matters for shared libraries — use **PIMPL** to hide implementation", "Linux: .so (shared object). Windows: .dll (dynamic link library). macOS: .dylib", "Use symbol versioning for backward-compatible library updates"], "questions": [{"question": "What is ABI compatibility?", "answer": "ABI compatibility means a compiled binary works with a new version of a shared library without recompilation. Breaking ABI means changing the binary interface: class layout, vtable order, calling conventions, or name mangling."}, {"question": "What is the advantage of static linking?", "answer": "No runtime dependency on shared libraries (self-contained binary). Better optimization through link-time optimization (LTO). No ABI compatibility concerns. Downside: larger binary, can't update library without recompiling."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_12_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_12_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_12_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_12_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Static vs Dynamic Linking, ABI Compatibility`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_12_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_12_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_12_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_12_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_12_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_12_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_12_sub_1", "day": 12, "index": 1}, {"title": "Build Systems — CMake, Make", "explanation": "CMake is the dominant C++ build system generator. It generates native build files (Makefiles, Visual Studio projects, Ninja files) from a CMakeLists.txt description. Modern CMake (3.x) uses targets and properties rather than directory-based variables.\n\nPackage managers: Conan and vcpkg manage third-party library dependencies. vcpkg integrates with CMake via toolchain files. Conan uses recipes and profiles.", "examples": [{"title": "Modern CMake", "code": "# CMakeLists.txt\ncmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.20)\nproject(MyApp LANGUAGES CXX)\n\nset(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 20)\nset(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON)\n\nadd_executable(myapp main.cpp utils.cpp)\ntarget_include_directories(myapp PRIVATE include/)\ntarget_link_libraries(myapp PRIVATE fmt::fmt)\n\n# Using vcpkg:\n# cmake -B build -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=/path/to/vcpkg/scripts/buildsystems/vcpkg.cmake", "explanation": "Modern CMake uses targets (add_executable, target_link_libraries) not variables.", "formatted_code": "# CMakeLists.txt\ncmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.20)\nproject(MyApp LANGUAGES CXX)\n\nset(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 20)\nset(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON)\n\nadd_executable(myapp main.cpp utils.cpp)\ntarget_include_directories(myapp PRIVATE include/)\ntarget_link_libraries(myapp PRIVATE fmt::fmt)\n\n# Using vcpkg:\n# cmake -B build -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=/path/to/vcpkg/scripts/buildsystems/vcpkg.cmake"}], "pitfalls": ["Using old-style CMake (include_directories, link_libraries) instead of target-based", "Not setting CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED — fallback to older standard silently", "Hardcoding paths instead of using find_package"], "tips_to_remember": ["Use modern CMake: `target_*` commands, not directory-level commands", "vcpkg or Conan for dependency management", "Ninja is faster than Make for large projects", "Always set CXX_STANDARD and CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED"], "questions": [{"question": "What is the difference between CMake and Make?", "answer": "Make is a build tool that executes build rules from a Makefile. CMake is a build system generator that produces Makefiles (or Ninja files, VS projects) from a higher-level CMakeLists.txt. CMake adds portability and modern dependency management."}, {"question": "What are vcpkg and Conan?", "answer": "C++ package managers. vcpkg (Microsoft) integrates with CMake via toolchain files, binary caching. Conan is more flexible with recipes and profiles, supports multiple build systems."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_12_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_12_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_12_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_12_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Build Systems — CMake, Make`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_12_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_12_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_12_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_12_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_12_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_12_sub_2_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_12_sub_2", "day": 12, "index": 2}]}, {"day": 13, "topic": "Debugging & Tooling", "estimated_hours": 4, "subtopics": [{"title": "Sanitizers — ASan, UBSan, TSan, MSan", "explanation": "AddressSanitizer (ASan): detects buffer overflows, use-after-free, double-free, memory leaks. ~2x slowdown. UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer (UBSan): detects signed overflow, null dereference, alignment violations, shift past bit-width. Minimal overhead. ThreadSanitizer (TSan): detects data races. ~5-15x slowdown. MemorySanitizer (MSan): detects use of uninitialized memory. ~3x slowdown.\n\nEnable with compiler flags: -fsanitize=address, -fsanitize=undefined, etc. Run your test suite with sanitizers regularly.", "examples": [{"title": "Using Sanitizers", "code": "// Compile with ASan:\n// g++ -fsanitize=address -g -O1 main.cpp\n\n// Compile with UBSan:\n// g++ -fsanitize=undefined -g main.cpp\n\n// Compile with TSan:\n// g++ -fsanitize=thread -g main.cpp\n\n// Example: ASan catches buffer overflow\nint arr[5];\narr[10] = 42;  // ASan reports: heap-buffer-overflow\n\n// Example: UBSan catches signed overflow\nint x = INT_MAX;\nx++;  // UBSan reports: signed integer overflow", "explanation": "Compile with -fsanitize=X to enable. Run normally — sanitizer reports errors at runtime.", "formatted_code": "// Compile with ASan:\n// g++ -fsanitize=address -g -O1 main.cpp\n\n// Compile with UBSan:\n// g++ -fsanitize=undefined -g main.cpp\n\n// Compile with TSan:\n// g++ -fsanitize=thread -g main.cpp\n\n// Example: ASan catches buffer overflow\nint arr[5];\narr[10] = 42;  // ASan reports: heap-buffer-overflow\n\n// Example: UBSan catches signed overflow\nint x = INT_MAX;\nx++;  // UBSan reports: signed integer overflow"}], "pitfalls": ["Not running sanitizers regularly in CI — bugs slip through", "ASan and TSan can't be used simultaneously", "MSan `requires` the entire program (including libraries) to be instrumented"], "tips_to_remember": ["Run ASan and UBSan in CI on every commit", "TSan finds data races that are otherwise nearly impossible to reproduce", "Use -fno-omit-frame-pointer with sanitizers for better stack traces", "ASan + UBSan can be combined: -fsanitize=address,undefined"], "questions": [{"question": "What are the four main sanitizers?", "answer": "ASan (memory errors: overflow, use-after-free, leaks), UBSan (undefined behavior: overflow, alignment, null deref), TSan (data races), MSan (uninitialized reads). All are compile-time instrumentation."}, {"question": "Can you combine sanitizers?", "answer": "ASan + UBSan: yes. ASan + TSan: no (incompatible). MSan + ASan: no. TSan + UBSan: yes. Typical CI setup: one build with ASan+UBSan, another with TSan."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens if you bind a temporary object (prvalue) to a `const T&` reference?", "options": ["The temporary is destroyed at the end of the expression, leaving a dangling reference.", "The lifetime of the temporary is extended to match the lifetime of the reference.", "The compiler makes a permanent copy of the temporary on the heap.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ guarantees that binding a temporary object to a const lvalue reference extends the lifetime of that temporary to match the lifetime of the reference itself, preventing dangling reference bugs.", "id": "day_13_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you try to modify a member variable in a `const` member function in `Sanitizers — ASan, UBSan, TSan, MSan`?", "options": ["It compiles fine, but triggers a segmentation fault at runtime.", "Compilation fails, unless the member variable is marked `mutable`.", "It compiles fine, but the changes are discarded when the function exits.", "It compiles fine only if the class is not allocated on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A `const` member function promises not to modify any observable state of the object. Modifying any member variable inside it is a compilation error, unless the member is declared with the `mutable` keyword.", "id": "day_13_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Can you initialize a `constexpr` variable using the return value of a non-constexpr runtime function?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler will defer evaluation to runtime.", "Yes, if the function has no side effects.", "No, compilation will fail because a `constexpr` value must be known at compile time.", "Yes, but only if compiler optimization is turned on."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "`constexpr` variables must be initialized with constant expressions that can be evaluated at compile time. Initializing one with a runtime value results in a compilation error.", "id": "day_13_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the consequence of calling a `consteval` function with non-constant (runtime) arguments?", "options": ["The function is executed at runtime instead.", "The compiler fails to compile the call.", "The compiler generates a runtime check that throws an exception if arguments are invalid.", "The return value is undefined."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`consteval` (introduced in C++20) specifies an immediate function. Immediate functions MUST be evaluated at compile time. Calling them with runtime values is a compilation error.", "id": "day_13_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Can a `constinit` variable be modified after initialization during runtime?", "options": ["No, `constinit` variables are implicitly `const`.", "Yes, `constinit` only requires compile-time initialization, not runtime immutability.", "Yes, but only inside `consteval` functions.", "No, modifying it triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constinit` (C++20) ensures that a variable is initialized at compile time (preventing static initialization order issues), but it does not make the variable constant. To make it constant, you must explicitly combine it with `const`: `constinit const int x = 42;`.", "id": "day_13_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Is it safe to use `static_cast` to cast a `Base*` to a `Derived*`?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler checks the actual runtime type and returns `nullptr` on failure.", "Yes, but it is compile-time checked only. If the pointer is not actually of the Derived type, dereferencing it is UB.", "No, it is a compilation error. You must use `dynamic_cast`.", "Yes, `static_cast` automatically handles polymorphic conversions safely."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`static_cast` performs compile-time conversions. It does not perform any runtime checks. Casting a base pointer to a derived pointer with `static_cast` is unsafe: if the object is not of the derived type, dereferencing the casted pointer is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_13_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast` do if it fails to cast a reference type `Base&` to `Derived&`?", "options": ["It returns a null reference.", "It throws a `std::bad_cast` exception.", "It aborts the program immediately.", "It sets the reference to an empty object."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since there is no such thing as a null reference in C++, `dynamic_cast` cannot return null when casting reference types. Instead, it throws a `std::bad_cast` exception on failure.", "id": "day_13_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "If you cast an `int*` to a `float*` using `reinterpret_cast` and dereference it to read the value, what is the result?", "options": ["It returns the floating-point representation of the integer bits.", "It is a compiler error.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior due to the strict aliasing rule.", "It triggers a hardware trap."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "The strict aliasing rule states that you cannot access an object of one type through a pointer of a different type (with exceptions like `char*` or signed/unsigned variants). Violating this using `reinterpret_cast` triggers undefined behavior.", "id": "day_13_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "If both `void print(int)` and `template<typename T> void print(T)` match a call `print(42)` equally well, which one is selected by overload resolution?", "options": ["The template function, because it is more generic.", "The non-template function.", "The compiler reports an ambiguity error.", "The compiler selects one randomly."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Overload resolution rules state that if a non-template function and a template specialization match a call equally well, the non-template function is always preferred.", "id": "day_13_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) occur if the function call is fully qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)`)?", "options": ["Yes, ADL searches namespaces associated with the type of `x`.", "No, ADL is only performed for unqualified function calls.", "Yes, but only if `x` is a user-defined type.", "No, qualified calls are handled entirely at link time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) only applies to unqualified names (e.g. `foo(x)`). If the call is qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)` or `MyLib::foo(x)`), the compiler uses normal lookup rules and bypasses ADL completely.", "id": "day_13_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_13_sub_0", "day": 13, "index": 0}, {"title": "Common UB Traps", "explanation": "Signed integer overflow (INT_MAX + 1), strict aliasing violations (casting `float*` to `int*` and dereferencing), uninitialized reads, null pointer dereference, shift by negative or >= bit-width, infinite loops without side effects (optimizer may remove them), modifying a const object through `const_cast`.\n\nCompilers assume UB never happens and optimize accordingly. This means UB can cause code to be removed, reordered, or produce impossible results.", "examples": [{"title": "UB Examples", "code": "// Signed overflow (UB)\nint x = INT_MAX;\nx = x + 1;  // UB! Compiler may assume this never happens\n\n// Strict aliasing (UB)\nfloat f = 1.0f;\nint i = *(int*)&f;  // UB! Use memcpy or bit_cast instead\n\n// Uninitialized read (UB)\nint y;  // uninitialized\nif (y > 0) { /* ... */ }  // UB: reading uninitialized variable\n\n// Shift past bit-width (UB)\nint z = 1 << 32;  // UB if int is 32 bits\n\n// Infinite loop without side effects (UB since C++11)\n// while (true) {}  // May be optimized away!", "explanation": "These are the most common UB traps. Use UBSan to detect them at runtime.", "formatted_code": "// Signed overflow (UB)\nint x = INT_MAX;\nx = x + 1;  // UB! Compiler may assume this never happens\n\n// Strict aliasing (UB)\nfloat f = 1.0f;\nint i = *(int*)&f;  // UB! Use memcpy or bit_cast instead\n\n// Uninitialized read (UB)\nint y;  // uninitialized\nif (y > 0)\n{\n    /* ... */\n}  // UB: reading uninitialized variable\n\n// Shift past bit-width (UB)\nint z = 1 << 32;  // UB if int is 32 bits\n\n// Infinite loop without side effects (UB since C++11)\n// while (true) {}  // May be optimized away!"}], "pitfalls": ["Assuming signed overflow wraps (it doesn't in C++ — use unsigned for wrap-around)", "Type punning through pointer casts — use memcpy", "Uninitialized local variables — always initialize", "Shift operators with negative or too-large shift amounts"], "tips_to_remember": ["Use -Werror -Wall -Wextra and sanitizers to catch UB", "unsigned arithmetic wraps (defined). signed arithmetic overflows (UB).", "Always initialize variables. Use {} for zero-initialization.", "memcpy is the safe way to type-pun. `reinterpret_cast` on values is UB."], "questions": [{"question": "Why is signed integer overflow UB?", "answer": "The standard doesn't define wrap-around behavior for signed integers (unlike unsigned). Compilers exploit this: if x > 0, the optimizer assumes x+1 > 0 too (since overflow 'can't happen'). This enables optimizations like loop bound simplification."}, {"question": "What is strict aliasing and why does it matter?", "answer": "The rule that an object can only be accessed through a pointer of its actual type (or char/byte). Violating it is UB. The compiler assumes different-typed pointers don't alias, enabling register optimization. Breaking it causes bugs at -O2 that don't appear at -O0."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens if you bind a temporary object (prvalue) to a `const T&` reference?", "options": ["The temporary is destroyed at the end of the expression, leaving a dangling reference.", "The lifetime of the temporary is extended to match the lifetime of the reference.", "The compiler makes a permanent copy of the temporary on the heap.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ guarantees that binding a temporary object to a const lvalue reference extends the lifetime of that temporary to match the lifetime of the reference itself, preventing dangling reference bugs.", "id": "day_13_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you try to modify a member variable in a `const` member function in `Common UB Traps`?", "options": ["It compiles fine, but triggers a segmentation fault at runtime.", "Compilation fails, unless the member variable is marked `mutable`.", "It compiles fine, but the changes are discarded when the function exits.", "It compiles fine only if the class is not allocated on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A `const` member function promises not to modify any observable state of the object. Modifying any member variable inside it is a compilation error, unless the member is declared with the `mutable` keyword.", "id": "day_13_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Can you initialize a `constexpr` variable using the return value of a non-constexpr runtime function?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler will defer evaluation to runtime.", "Yes, if the function has no side effects.", "No, compilation will fail because a `constexpr` value must be known at compile time.", "Yes, but only if compiler optimization is turned on."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "`constexpr` variables must be initialized with constant expressions that can be evaluated at compile time. Initializing one with a runtime value results in a compilation error.", "id": "day_13_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the consequence of calling a `consteval` function with non-constant (runtime) arguments?", "options": ["The function is executed at runtime instead.", "The compiler fails to compile the call.", "The compiler generates a runtime check that throws an exception if arguments are invalid.", "The return value is undefined."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`consteval` (introduced in C++20) specifies an immediate function. Immediate functions MUST be evaluated at compile time. Calling them with runtime values is a compilation error.", "id": "day_13_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Can a `constinit` variable be modified after initialization during runtime?", "options": ["No, `constinit` variables are implicitly `const`.", "Yes, `constinit` only requires compile-time initialization, not runtime immutability.", "Yes, but only inside `consteval` functions.", "No, modifying it triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constinit` (C++20) ensures that a variable is initialized at compile time (preventing static initialization order issues), but it does not make the variable constant. To make it constant, you must explicitly combine it with `const`: `constinit const int x = 42;`.", "id": "day_13_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Is it safe to use `static_cast` to cast a `Base*` to a `Derived*`?", "options": ["Yes, the compiler checks the actual runtime type and returns `nullptr` on failure.", "Yes, but it is compile-time checked only. If the pointer is not actually of the Derived type, dereferencing it is UB.", "No, it is a compilation error. You must use `dynamic_cast`.", "Yes, `static_cast` automatically handles polymorphic conversions safely."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`static_cast` performs compile-time conversions. It does not perform any runtime checks. Casting a base pointer to a derived pointer with `static_cast` is unsafe: if the object is not of the derived type, dereferencing the casted pointer is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_13_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast` do if it fails to cast a reference type `Base&` to `Derived&`?", "options": ["It returns a null reference.", "It throws a `std::bad_cast` exception.", "It aborts the program immediately.", "It sets the reference to an empty object."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since there is no such thing as a null reference in C++, `dynamic_cast` cannot return null when casting reference types. Instead, it throws a `std::bad_cast` exception on failure.", "id": "day_13_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "If you cast an `int*` to a `float*` using `reinterpret_cast` and dereference it to read the value, what is the result?", "options": ["It returns the floating-point representation of the integer bits.", "It is a compiler error.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior due to the strict aliasing rule.", "It triggers a hardware trap."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "The strict aliasing rule states that you cannot access an object of one type through a pointer of a different type (with exceptions like `char*` or signed/unsigned variants). Violating this using `reinterpret_cast` triggers undefined behavior.", "id": "day_13_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "If both `void print(int)` and `template<typename T> void print(T)` match a call `print(42)` equally well, which one is selected by overload resolution?", "options": ["The template function, because it is more generic.", "The non-template function.", "The compiler reports an ambiguity error.", "The compiler selects one randomly."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Overload resolution rules state that if a non-template function and a template specialization match a call equally well, the non-template function is always preferred.", "id": "day_13_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) occur if the function call is fully qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)`)?", "options": ["Yes, ADL searches namespaces associated with the type of `x`.", "No, ADL is only performed for unqualified function calls.", "Yes, but only if `x` is a user-defined type.", "No, qualified calls are handled entirely at link time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) only applies to unqualified names (e.g. `foo(x)`). If the call is qualified (e.g. `std::foo(x)` or `MyLib::foo(x)`), the compiler uses normal lookup rules and bypasses ADL completely.", "id": "day_13_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_13_sub_1", "day": 13, "index": 1}]}, {"day": 14, "topic": "Design Patterns & Idioms", "estimated_hours": 5, "subtopics": [{"title": "RAII and PIMPL Idiom", "explanation": "**RAII** (covered in Day 3) is C++'s most important idiom. **PIMPL** (Pointer to IMPLementation) hides implementation details behind a pointer to a forward-declared class. Benefits: reduces compilation dependencies (changes to implementation don't require recompiling users), maintains ABI stability (the pointer size doesn't change when implementation changes).\n\n**PIMPL** pattern: public header declares only a `unique_ptr<Impl>` member. The Impl class is defined only in the .cpp file. Constructor, destructor, copy/move must be in the .cpp (where Impl is complete).\n\n### Unforgettable Analogy / Deep Dive\n**RAII** is like a rental car agreement: the moment you sign the keys (constructor), you own the car, and the moment you return the keys (destructor), you release it. The resource's lifetime is bound to the lifespan of the stack object. If the stack unwinds due to an exception or scope exit, C++ guarantees that the destructor is called automatically, preventing resource leaks.", "examples": [{"title": "PIMPL Pattern", "code": "// widget.h — public header\nclass Widget\n{\npublic:\n    Widget();\n    ~Widget();  // must be in .cpp where Impl is defined\n    Widget(Widget&&) noexcept;\n    Widget& operator=(Widget&&) noexcept;\n    void doWork();\nprivate:\n    struct Impl;  // forward declaration only\n    std::unique_ptr<Impl> pImpl_;\n};\n\n// widget.cpp — implementation\nstruct Widget::Impl {\n    std::string name;\n    std::vector<int> data;\n    void internalWork() { /* ... */ }\n};\nWidget::Widget() : pImpl_(std::make_unique<Impl>()) {}\nWidget::~Widget() = default;  // must be here, where Impl is complete\nWidget::Widget(Widget&&) noexcept = default;\nWidget& Widget::operator=(Widget&&) noexcept = default;\nvoid Widget::doWork() { pImpl_->internalWork(); }", "explanation": "PIMPL hides implementation, reduces compilation deps, maintains ABI.", "formatted_code": "// widget.h — public header\nclass Widget\n{\npublic:\n    Widget();\n    ~Widget();  // must be in .cpp where Impl is defined\n    Widget(Widget&&) noexcept;\n    Widget& operator=(Widget&&) noexcept;\n    void doWork();\nprivate:\n    struct Impl;  // forward declaration only\n    std::unique_ptr<Impl> pImpl_;\n};\n\n// widget.cpp — implementation\nstruct Widget::Impl\n{\n    std::string name;\n    std::vector<int> data;\n    void internalWork()\n    {\n        /* ... */\n    }\n};\nWidget::Widget() : pImpl_(std::make_unique<Impl>())\n{\n}\nWidget::~Widget() = default;  // must be here, where Impl is complete\nWidget::Widget(Widget&&) noexcept = default;\nWidget& Widget::operator=(Widget&&) noexcept = default;\nvoid Widget::doWork()\n{\n    pImpl_->internalWork();\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["Forgetting to define destructor in .cpp — unique_ptr needs complete type for deletion", "**PIMPL** adds heap allocation and indirection overhead", "Not providing move operations — unique_ptr member makes class move-only"], "tips_to_remember": ["**PIMPL** = ABI stability + reduced compile deps + true encapsulation", "Destructor and move operations MUST be in .cpp where Impl is defined", "**PIMPL** adds one indirection per method call — negligible for most uses"], "questions": [{"question": "What is PIMPL and when should you use it?", "answer": "PIMPL hides implementation behind a unique_ptr to a forward-declared struct. Use for: ABI stability (shared libraries), reducing compilation dependencies (heavy headers), hiding platform-specific implementation. Cost: one heap allocation + one indirection per call."}, {"question": "Why must the destructor be in the .cpp file with PIMPL?", "answer": "unique_ptr's destructor needs to see the complete Impl type to call delete. In the header, Impl is only forward-declared (incomplete). The destructor must be defined in the .cpp where struct Impl is fully defined."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What happens when you pass an array `int arr[10]` to a function declared as `void process(int arr[])`?", "options": ["The array is passed by value, making a copy of all 10 integers.", "It decays to a raw pointer `int*`, and its size information is lost.", "The compiler verifies the size is exactly 10 and throws a warning if not.", "It is passed as a safe reference to the array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Arrays passed to functions decay to pointers. Inside `process`, `arr` is a raw pointer (`int*`), and `sizeof(arr)` returns the pointer size (e.g. 8 bytes), not the array size.", "id": "day_14_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Which of the following is a compiler error for `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["Moving it using `std::move`.", "Attempting to copy it via copy assignment or copy constructor.", "Resetting it using `.reset()`.", "Releasing ownership with `.release()`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unique_ptr` represents exclusive ownership. To prevent duplicate owners, copying is disabled (deleted copy constructor/assignment), causing a compilation error.", "id": "day_14_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the outcome of a circular dependency where two objects contain `std::shared_ptr` pointing to each other?", "options": ["The compiler reports a cyclic template dependency error.", "The reference counts will never drop to zero, leaking both objects.", "The destructors are called normally due to automatic cycle detection.", "The program crashes with a stack overflow on deletion."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because each object holds a `std::shared_ptr` to the other, their reference counts remain at least 1, even after all external pointers are destroyed, leaking the memory.", "id": "day_14_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How do you access the managed object of a `std::weak_ptr`?", "options": ["Dereference it directly using `*` or `->`.", "You must call `.lock()` to obtain a `std::shared_ptr` first.", "Call `.get()` to retrieve the raw pointer.", "It is not possible to access the object once it is in a weak pointer."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::weak_ptr` does not provide direct access. You must call `.lock()` which returns a `std::shared_ptr`. If the managed object has already been destroyed, the returned shared pointer will be empty.", "id": "day_14_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary benefit of using `std::make_shared` instead of `std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)`?", "options": ["It makes the shared pointer thread-safe.", "It performs a single memory allocation for both the control block and the object, improving performance and cache locality.", "It allows using custom delete functions.", "It prevents compile-time template instantiation overhead."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)` allocates the object first, then allocates the control block. `std::make_shared` allocates a single block of memory big enough to hold both, reducing heap allocation overhead.", "id": "day_14_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "How does a custom deleter function pointer affect the size of a `std::unique_ptr`?", "options": ["It does not affect the size; it remains 8 bytes.", "It increases the size of the pointer (typically to 16 bytes) because it must store the function pointer.", "It causes the unique_ptr to allocate memory on the heap.", "It is a compile-time construct and does not exist at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a custom deleter is a function pointer, the unique_ptr must store it, increasing its size. Using a stateless functor (like a lambda with no captures) avoids this size overhead.", "id": "day_14_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of placement `new` in C++?", "options": ["It automatically finds the best place in the heap to allocate memory.", "It constructs an object on a pre-allocated raw memory buffer without allocating new heap memory.", "It moves an object to another thread's stack space.", "It optimizes memory alignment dynamically."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Placement `new` (e.g. `new (buffer) MyClass()`) constructs an object at a specific, pre-allocated memory address, avoiding heap allocation costs.", "id": "day_14_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "How must you destroy an object constructed via placement `new`?", "options": ["Call `delete ptr;`.", "Explicitly call the object's destructor (`ptr->~T()`) and then manually manage the buffer's memory.", "Let it go out of scope; the stack automatically deletes it.", "Call `free(ptr);`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because placement `new` did not allocate memory from the global heap, calling `delete` is undefined behavior. You must explicitly invoke the destructor and then clean up the raw buffer.", "id": "day_14_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the undefined behavior hazard in the code `int* p = new int[10]; delete p;`?", "options": ["Using the wrong delete operator (`delete` instead of `delete[]`) is Undefined Behavior.", "There is no hazard; `delete` automatically knows it is an array.", "It is a compiler error and won't compile.", "It causes a stack overflow."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Memory allocated with `new[]` must be freed with `delete[]`. Using plain `delete` on an array pointer is undefined behavior, often causing heap corruption or failing to call destructors for elements 1 to 9.", "id": "day_14_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the risk of returning a local variable by reference `const int&` from a function?", "options": ["It copies the variable unnecessarily.", "The local variable is destroyed at function exit, leaving a dangling reference, and accessing it is UB.", "It compiles only if the variable is marked `static`.", "It causes a memory leak on the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Local variables have automatic storage duration (stack-allocated) and are destroyed when the function returns. Accessing a reference to a destroyed local variable is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_14_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_14_sub_0", "day": 14, "index": 0}, {"title": "Classic GoF Patterns in C++ — Singleton, Factory, Observer, Strategy, Visitor", "explanation": "Singleton: exactly one instance. Meyer's Singleton (function-`static`) is thread-safe since C++11. Factory: creates objects without specifying exact types. Observer: event notification pattern. Strategy: interchangeable algorithms. Visitor: double dispatch for operations on variant types — modernized with `std::visit` + `std::variant` in C++17.", "examples": [{"title": "Modern Patterns", "code": "// Meyer's Singleton (thread-safe since C++11)\nclass Config\n{\npublic:\n    static Config& instance()\n    {\n        static Config cfg;  // magic static: thread-safe init\n        return cfg;\n    }\n    Config(const Config&) = delete;\n    Config& operator=(const Config&) = delete;\nprivate:\n    Config() = default;\n};\n\n// Modern Visitor with std::variant\nusing Shape = std::variant<Circle, Rectangle, Triangle>;\ndouble area(const Shape& s)\n{\n    return std::visit([](const auto& shape) { return shape.area(); }, s);\n}", "explanation": "Meyer's Singleton is thread-safe. std::visit replaces traditional Visitor pattern.", "formatted_code": "// Meyer's Singleton (thread-safe since C++11)\nclass Config\n{\npublic:\n    static Config& instance()\n    {\n        static Config cfg;  // magic static: thread-safe init\n        return cfg;\n    }\n    Config(const Config&) = delete;\n    Config& operator=(const Config&) = delete;\nprivate:\n    Config() = default;\n};\n\n// Modern Visitor with std::variant\nusing Shape = std::variant<Circle, Rectangle, Triangle>;\ndouble area(const Shape& s)\n{\n    return std::visit([](const auto& shape)\n    {\n        return shape.area();\n    }\n    , s);\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["Singleton overuse — it's essentially a global variable with extra steps", "Factory returning raw pointers — return unique_ptr instead", "Observer holding raw pointers to subjects — use weak_ptr for safety"], "tips_to_remember": ["Singletons are often an anti-pattern — consider dependency injection", "Return unique_ptr from factories, not raw pointers", "`std::variant` + `std::visit` is the modern Visitor — no `virtual` dispatch needed", "Strategy pattern = just pass a callable (lambda, `std::function`)"], "questions": [{"question": "What is Meyer's Singleton?", "answer": "A Singleton using a function-static variable: static T& instance() { static T obj; return obj; }. Thread-safe since C++11 (magic statics guarantee). Lazy initialization. Simple and correct."}, {"question": "How does std::variant replace the Visitor pattern?", "answer": "Traditional Visitor requires virtual functions and double dispatch. std::variant<Types...> with std::visit provides the same functionality: pattern matching on a closed set of types, but without inheritance, vtables, or heap allocation."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_14_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_14_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_14_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_14_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Classic GoF Patterns in C++ — Singleton, Factory, Observer, Strategy, Visitor`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_14_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_14_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_14_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_14_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_14_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_14_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_14_sub_1", "day": 14, "index": 1}, {"title": "Type Erasure", "explanation": "Type erasure allows storing objects of different types behind a common interface without inheritance. `std::function` is the canonical example — it wraps any callable (lambda, function pointer, functor) behind a uniform type.\n\nImplementation: a base class with `virtual` methods holds the type-erased interface. A templated derived class wraps the concrete type. The outer class holds a unique_ptr to the base. Small Buffer Optimization (SBO) avoids heap allocation for small types.", "examples": [{"title": "Custom Type Erasure", "code": "// Simplified type-erased Callable (like std::function)\nclass AnyCallable\n{\n    struct Base\n    {\n        virtual ~Base() = default;\n        virtual int call(int) = 0;\n    };\n    template<typename F>\n    struct Model : Base {\n        F func;\n        Model(F f) : func(std::move(f)) {}\n        int call(int x) override { return func(x); }\n    };\n    std::unique_ptr<Base> ptr_;\npublic:\n    template<typename F>\n    AnyCallable(F f) : ptr_(std::make_unique<Model<F>>(std::move(f))) {}\n    int operator()(int x) { return ptr_->call(x); }\n};\n\n// Usage:\nAnyCallable fn = [](int x) { return x * 2; };\nstd::cout << fn(21);  // 42", "explanation": "Type erasure: interface via base class, storage via templated derived class, hidden behind a value type.", "formatted_code": "// Simplified type-erased Callable (like std::function)\nclass AnyCallable\n{\n    struct Base\n    {\n        virtual ~Base() = default;\n        virtual int call(int) = 0;\n    };\n    template<typename F>\n    struct Model : Base\n    {\n        F func;\n        Model(F f) : func(std::move(f))\n        {\n        }\n        int call(int x) override\n        {\n            return func(x);\n        }\n    };\n    std::unique_ptr<Base> ptr_;\npublic:\n    template<typename F>\n    AnyCallable(F f) : ptr_(std::make_unique<Model<F>>(std::move(f)))\n    {\n    }\n    int operator()(int x)\n    {\n        return ptr_->call(x);\n    }\n};\n\n// Usage:\nAnyCallable fn = [](int x)\n{\n    return x * 2;\n};\nstd::cout << fn(21);  // 42"}], "pitfalls": ["Type erasure adds heap allocation and `virtual` dispatch overhead", "Not implementing SBO for small callables (`std::function` does this)", "Losing type information makes debugging harder"], "tips_to_remember": ["Type erasure = value semantics + `virtual` dispatch hidden behind a facade", "`std::function`, `std::any`, and `std::move_only_function` all use type erasure", "SBO (Small Buffer Optimization) avoids heap for small types"], "questions": [{"question": "What is type erasure?", "answer": "A technique to store objects of arbitrary types behind a common, non-template interface. The type information is 'erased' at compile time but the correct behavior is preserved through virtual dispatch internally. std::function is the most common example."}, {"question": "How does type erasure differ from inheritance?", "answer": "With inheritance, the user writes the derived class and passes pointers/references. With type erasure, the wrapping class internally creates the derived class — the user just passes any conforming object by value. The user doesn't need to inherit from anything."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_14_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_14_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_14_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_14_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Type Erasure`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_14_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_14_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_14_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_14_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_14_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_14_sub_2_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_14_sub_2", "day": 14, "index": 2}]}, {"day": 15, "topic": "Low-Level & Systems Programming", "estimated_hours": 6, "subtopics": [{"title": "Bitwise Operations & Bit-Fields", "explanation": "Bitwise operators: & (AND), | (OR), ^ (XOR), ~ (NOT), << (left shift), >> (right shift). Used for flags, masks, hardware registers, and compact data storage. Bit-fields allow specifying the number of bits for struct members: struct { unsigned flag : 1; unsigned value : 4; }.\n\nCommon idiom: flags with enum class and bitwise operators.", "examples": [{"title": "Bitwise Operations", "code": "// Setting, clearing, toggling bits\nunsigned flags = 0;\nflags |= (1 << 3);   // set bit 3\nflags &= ~(1 << 3);  // clear bit 3\nflags ^= (1 << 3);   // toggle bit 3\nbool isSet = flags & (1 << 3);  // test bit 3\n\n// Bit-fields\nstruct Flags\n{\n    unsigned read  : 1;\n    unsigned write : 1;\n    unsigned exec  : 1;\n    unsigned mode  : 4;  // 4 bits (0-15)\n};  // Packs into minimal space", "explanation": "Bitwise ops for flags. Bit-fields for compact storage.", "formatted_code": "// Setting, clearing, toggling bits\nunsigned flags = 0;\nflags |= (1 << 3);  // set bit 3\nflags &= ~(1 << 3);  // clear bit 3\nflags ^= (1 << 3);  // toggle bit 3\nbool isSet = flags & (1 << 3);  // test bit 3\n\n// Bit-fields\nstruct Flags\n{\n    unsigned read  : 1;\n    unsigned write : 1;\n    unsigned exec  : 1;\n    unsigned mode  : 4;  // 4 bits (0-15)\n};  // Packs into minimal space"}], "pitfalls": ["Right-shifting signed integers is implementation-defined (arithmetic vs logical)", "Bit-field layout (order, padding) is implementation-defined — not portable for serialization", "Shifting by >= bit-width is UB"], "tips_to_remember": ["Use unsigned types for bitwise operations", "Bit-field layout is not portable — don't use for network/file formats", "Prefer enum class with operator overloads for type-safe flags"], "questions": [{"question": "Are bit-fields portable?", "answer": "No. The layout of bit-fields (order, padding, signedness) is implementation-defined. Different compilers may arrange them differently. Don't use bit-fields for cross-platform serialization or hardware register mapping — use explicit shifts and masks instead."}, {"question": "What is the difference between arithmetic and logical right shift?", "answer": "Arithmetic shift (signed) preserves the sign bit (fills with 1 for negative). Logical shift (unsigned) fills with 0. C++ right-shift of negative integers is implementation-defined."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_15_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_15_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_15_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_15_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Bitwise Operations & Bit-Fields`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_15_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_15_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_15_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_15_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_15_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_15_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_15_sub_0", "day": 15, "index": 0}, {"title": "Endianness", "explanation": "Byte order: how multi-byte values are stored in memory. Little-endian (x86, ARM default): least significant byte first. Big-endian (network byte order, some ARM modes): most significant byte first.\n\nEndianness matters for: network protocols (always big-endian by convention), file formats, cross-platform serialization. Use `ntohl`/`htonl` for network byte order conversion. C++20 adds `std::endian` for compile-time endianness detection.", "examples": [{"title": "Endianness", "code": "// C++20 endianness detection\n#include <bit>\nif constexpr (std::endian::native == std::endian::little)\n{\n    std::cout << \"Little-endian system\\n\";\n}\n\n// Network byte order conversion\n#include <arpa/inet.h>  // or <winsock2.h> on Windows\nuint32_t hostVal = 0x12345678;\nuint32_t netVal = htonl(hostVal);  // convert to network (big-endian)\nuint32_t backToHost = ntohl(netVal);  // convert back", "explanation": "Use htonl/ntohl for network byte order. std::endian (C++20) for detection.", "formatted_code": "// C++20 endianness detection\n#include <bit>\nif constexpr (std::endian::native == std::endian::little)\n{\n    std::cout << \"Little-endian system\\n\";\n}\n\n// Network byte order conversion\n#include <arpa/inet.h>  // or <winsock2.h> on Windows\nuint32_t hostVal = 0x12345678;\nuint32_t netVal = htonl(hostVal);  // convert to network (big-endian)\nuint32_t backToHost = ntohl(netVal);  // convert back"}], "pitfalls": ["Assuming endianness — code breaks on different architectures", "Not converting byte order in network protocols — garbled data", "Type-punning to check endianness (UB) instead of using `std::endian`"], "tips_to_remember": ["Network byte order is always big-endian — use `htonl`/`ntohl`", "x86/x64 is always little-endian. ARM can be either.", "C++20 `std::endian::native` tells you at compile time"], "questions": [{"question": "When does endianness matter?", "answer": "Network protocols (TCP/IP uses big-endian), file format reading/writing, cross-platform data exchange, hardware register access. For local computation it's invisible."}, {"question": "How do you handle endianness in portable code?", "answer": "Use htonl/ntohl/htons/ntohs for network byte order conversion. Use std::endian (C++20) for compile-time detection. Write serialization code that explicitly specifies byte order."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_15_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_15_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_15_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_15_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Endianness`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_15_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_15_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_15_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_15_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_15_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_15_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_15_sub_1", "day": 15, "index": 1}, {"title": "volatile vs atomic", "explanation": "`volatile` tells the compiler not to optimize away reads/writes to a variable — every access goes to memory. It does NOT provide atomicity or synchronization. It's for memory-mapped I/O, signal handlers, and setjmp/longjmp.\n\n`std::atomic` provides atomicity AND synchronization (with memory ordering). For multithreading, always use atomic, never `volatile`. `volatile` atomic exists but is rarely needed.", "examples": [{"title": "volatile vs atomic", "code": "// volatile: prevents optimization, no synchronization\nvolatile int* hw_reg = (volatile int*)0xFFFF0000;\n*hw_reg = 1;  // compiler won't optimize this write away\nint val = *hw_reg;  // compiler won't cache this read\n\n// atomic: provides atomicity AND synchronization\nstd::atomic<int> counter{0};\ncounter++;  // atomic read-modify-write, with synchronization\n\n// WRONG: using volatile for multithreading\nvolatile int shared = 0;  // NOT thread-safe! No atomicity!\n// Use std::atomic<int> instead", "explanation": "volatile = don't optimize away. atomic = thread-safe. They serve different purposes.", "formatted_code": "// volatile: prevents optimization, no synchronization\nvolatile int* hw_reg = (volatile int*)0xFFFF0000;\n*hw_reg = 1;  // compiler won't optimize this write away\nint val = *hw_reg;  // compiler won't cache this read\n\n// atomic: provides atomicity AND synchronization\nstd::atomic<int> counter\n{\n    0\n};\ncounter++;  // atomic read-modify-write, with synchronization\n\n// WRONG: using volatile for multithreading\nvolatile int shared = 0;  // NOT thread-safe! No atomicity!\n// Use std::atomic<int> instead"}], "pitfalls": ["Using `volatile` for thread synchronization — it provides NO atomicity", "Assuming `volatile` prevents reordering — it doesn't (only compiler, not CPU)", "Java's `volatile` ≠ C++'s `volatile` (Java's `volatile` IS like C++'s atomic)"], "tips_to_remember": ["`volatile` = hardware/signal handler use ONLY. atomic = multithreading.", "`volatile` prevents compiler optimizations. atomic prevents compiler AND CPU reordering.", "In C++, `volatile` has nothing to do with threads"], "questions": [{"question": "What is the difference between volatile and atomic?", "answer": "volatile prevents the compiler from optimizing away reads/writes (for hardware registers, signal handlers). atomic provides atomicity and synchronization for multithreading. volatile does NOT make operations atomic or provide any threading guarantees."}, {"question": "When should you use volatile?", "answer": "Memory-mapped I/O (hardware registers), signal handlers (volatile sig_atomic_t), setjmp/longjmp. Never for multithreading — use std::atomic instead."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the key difference between `std::thread` and `std::jthread` (C++20) on destruction?", "options": ["`std::jthread` allocates thread resources on the stack.", "`std::jthread` automatically joins and requests cooperative interruption on destruction, while `std::thread` calls `std::terminate` if it is still joinable.", "`std::thread` is faster to construct.", "`std::jthread` does not support lambda execution."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a `std::thread` is destroyed while it is still joinable (running and not joined/detached), it calls `std::terminate`, crashing the program. `std::jthread` automatically joins and cooperatively signals stop on destruction.", "id": "day_15_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What constitutes a data race in C++?", "options": ["Two threads executing different functions simultaneously.", "Two or more threads accessing the same memory location concurrently, where at least one access is a write, and without synchronization.", "Using global variables in a multithreaded application.", "When a thread fails to acquire a lock in time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A data race is defined as concurrent unsynchronized access to the same memory location where at least one thread is writing. Data races are undefined behavior in C++.", "id": "day_15_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why would you prefer `std::unique_lock` over `std::lock_guard`?", "options": ["`std::unique_lock` is faster.", "`std::unique_lock` provides advanced features like deferring lock, explicit unlocking, timeout locking, and move semantics, whereas `std::lock_guard` is strictly scoped.", "`std::lock_guard` causes deadlocks easily.", "`std::unique_lock` does not require a mutex reference."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::lock_guard` is a simple, zero-overhead wrapper for basic scoped locking. `std::unique_lock` has a small overhead but supports complex use cases like deferred locking, manual unlocking, and transferring locks.", "id": "day_15_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "How does `std::scoped_lock` (C++17) prevent deadlocks when locking multiple mutexes?", "options": ["It compiles the mutexes into a single lock.", "It uses a deadlock-avoidance algorithm to lock all passed mutexes simultaneously.", "It runs a background thread to monitor locks.", "It converts mutexes to atomic variables."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::scoped_lock` can lock multiple mutexes. It uses the same deadlock avoidance algorithm as `std::lock` under the hood, ensuring they are acquired in a safe, sorted sequence.", "id": "day_15_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the key benefit of `std::atomic` variables?", "options": ["They make entire classes thread-safe.", "Operations on them are guaranteed to be atomic, preventing data races without needing a heavy mutex lock.", "They run code on separate CPU cores.", "They prevent memory allocations."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Operations on `std::atomic` variables are executed as single, indivisible CPU instructions, ensuring thread safety and preventing data races without the system-call overhead of mutexes.", "id": "day_15_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What is the default memory order for atomic operations in C++?", "options": ["`std::memory_order_relaxed`", "`std::memory_order_seq_cst` (Sequential Consistency)", "`std::memory_order_acquire`", "`std::memory_order_release`"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "By default, all atomic operations use `std::memory_order_seq_cst`. This provides sequential consistency, establishing a global, total ordering of all operations visible to all threads.", "id": "day_15_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What guarantees does `std::memory_order_relaxed` provide?", "options": ["It guarantees that operations are atomic, but provides no synchronization or ordering guarantees for surrounding memory accesses.", "It behaves exactly like sequentially consistent order but runs faster.", "It locks the CPU caches during execution.", "It is a runtime check that throws an exception on race."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "`memory_order_relaxed` only guarantees atomicity of the variable itself. The compiler and CPU are free to reorder surrounding read/write instructions around it, providing no synchronization between threads.", "id": "day_15_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "Why must a condition variable wait loop check the predicate condition (e.g. `cv.wait(lock, []{ return ready; })`)?", "options": ["To yield the CPU back to the OS.", "To prevent spurious wakeups, where the thread wakes up without receiving a signal.", "Because condition variables can only wake up once.", "To register the thread with the scheduler."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Condition variables are subject to 'spurious wakeups' due to OS implementation details. A thread can wake up even if no signal was sent. Thus, the thread must always re-verify the predicate condition.", "id": "day_15_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the relationship between `std::promise` and `std::future`?", "options": ["They are synonyms for the same class.", "`std::promise` sets a value or exception in a shared state, which `std::future` retrieves asynchronously.", "`std::future` is used to send values, `std::promise` is used to read them.", "They are used to create recursive mutex locks."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A promise is the writer-end and a future is the reader-end of an asynchronous channel. `std::promise` writes a value or exception to the shared channel state, which is retrieved via `std::future::get()`.", "id": "day_15_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Does the `volatile` keyword provide thread synchronization or atomicity in C++?", "options": ["Yes, it makes variables thread-safe.", "No, `volatile` only prevents compiler register caching optimizations for hardware registers; it does NOT prevent data races or guarantee thread memory order.", "Yes, it acts as a lightweight atomic variable.", "No, it is a deprecated keyword in modern C++."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Unlike Java/C#, `volatile` in C++ has nothing to do with multithreading. It only instructs the compiler not to optimize away reads/writes to memory (useful for memory-mapped I/O). It does not generate atomic CPU operations or memory barriers.", "id": "day_15_sub_2_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_15_sub_2", "day": 15, "index": 2}]}, {"day": 16, "topic": "Standard Evolution", "estimated_hours": 5, "subtopics": [{"title": "C++11 — The Modern C++ Revolution", "explanation": "C++11 was the biggest evolution of C++: auto type deduction, lambdas, move semantics (&&, `std::move`), smart pointers (unique_ptr, shared_ptr), range-for loops, `nullptr`, `constexpr`, variadic templates, `std::thread`, `std::atomic`, enum class, `static_assert`, `decltype`, initializer lists.\n\nThis was when 'Modern C++' began. Almost all new C++ code should use C++11 features as a baseline.", "examples": [{"title": "C++11 Highlights", "code": "auto x = 42;                           // auto deduction\nauto v = std::vector<int>{1, 2, 3};    // initializer list\nfor (auto& item : v) item *= 2;        // range-for\n\nauto ptr = std::make_unique<int>(42);   // smart pointer\nauto fn = [](int x) { return x * 2; }; // lambda\n\nstd::string s = \"hello\";\nauto s2 = std::move(s);                 // move semantics\n\nenum class Color { Red, Green, Blue };  // scoped enum\nconstexpr int sq(int n) { return n*n; };// constexpr", "explanation": "C++11 introduced the foundation of modern C++.", "formatted_code": "auto x = 42;  // auto deduction\nauto v = std::vector<int>\n{\n    1, 2, 3\n};  // initializer list\nfor (auto& item : v) item *= 2;  // range-for\n\nauto ptr = std::make_unique<int>(42);  // smart pointer\nauto fn = [](int x)\n{\n    return x * 2;\n};  // lambda\n\nstd::string s = \"hello\";\nauto s2 = std::move(s);  // move semantics\n\nenum class Color\n{\n    Red, Green, Blue\n};  // scoped enum\nconstexpr int sq(int n)\n{\n    return n*n;\n};  // constexpr"}], "pitfalls": ["Overusing auto when the type isn't obvious — hurts readability", "Forgetting `std::move` returns an xvalue, not a moved object", "Using NULL instead of `nullptr`"], "tips_to_remember": ["C++11 is the baseline for modern C++", "auto, lambdas, move semantics, smart pointers, range-for — use them everywhere", "`nullptr` replaces NULL. enum class replaces enum."], "questions": [{"question": "What are the most important C++11 features?", "answer": "Move semantics (performance), smart pointers (safety), lambdas (expressiveness), auto (convenience), range-for (readability), constexpr (compile-time), nullptr (type safety), variadic templates (generic programming), threads/atomics (standard concurrency)."}, {"question": "Why was C++11 so significant?", "answer": "It modernized C++ from a language stuck in 1998. It added: zero-cost abstractions (move semantics), safety (smart pointers, nullptr), expressiveness (lambdas, auto, range-for), and standardized concurrency (threads, atomics)."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_16_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_16_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_16_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_16_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `C++11 — The Modern C++ Revolution`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_16_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_16_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_16_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_16_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_16_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_16_sub_0_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_16_sub_0", "day": 16, "index": 0}, {"title": "C++14/17 — Refinements and Vocabulary Types", "explanation": "C++14: generic lambdas (auto parameters), relaxed `constexpr` (loops, local vars), variable templates, `std::make_unique`, binary literals (0b1010), digit separators (1'000'000).\n\nC++17: structured bindings (auto [x,y] = pair), if `constexpr`, `std::optional`/variant/any, parallel STL algorithms, `std::filesystem`, fold expressions, `inline` variables, class `template` argument deduction (**CTAD**), `std::string_view`, nested namespaces (`A::B::C`), [[nodiscard]], [[maybe_unused]].", "examples": [{"title": "C++17 Highlights", "code": "// Structured bindings\nauto [key, value] = std::pair(\"hello\", 42);\n\n// if constexpr — compile-time branching\ntemplate<typename T>\nauto stringify(T val)\n{\n    if constexpr (std::is_same_v<T, std::string>) return val;\n    else return std::to_string(val);\n}\n\n// std::optional\nstd::optional<int> result = findValue();\nif (result) use(*result);\n\n// Parallel algorithms\nstd::sort(std::execution::par, v.begin(), v.end());\n\n// Inline variables\ninline constexpr int MaxSize = 100;  // can be in header", "explanation": "C++17 added vocabulary types, compile-time branching, and parallel STL.", "formatted_code": "// Structured bindings\nauto [key, value] = std::pair(\"hello\", 42);\n\n// if constexpr — compile-time branching\ntemplate<typename T>\nauto stringify(T val)\n{\n    if constexpr (std::is_same_v<T, std::string>) return val;\n    else return std::to_string(val);\n}\n\n// std::optional\nstd::optional<int> result = findValue();\nif (result) use(*result);\n\n// Parallel algorithms\nstd::sort(std::execution::par, v.begin(), v.end());\n\n// Inline variables\ninline constexpr int MaxSize = 100;  // can be in header"}], "pitfalls": ["Assuming if `constexpr` removes code — it's still parsed, just not instantiated", "string_view dangling: creating string_view from a temporary string", "Parallel algorithms need TBB or similar backend"], "tips_to_remember": ["C++14: relaxed `constexpr` + generic lambdas = more expressive templates", "C++17: structured bindings + optional/variant + if `constexpr` = huge readability wins", "`inline` variables solve the 'define in header' problem for constants"], "questions": [{"question": "What is if constexpr?", "answer": "Compile-time branching: if constexpr (condition) { ... } else { ... }. The false branch is not instantiated (for templates). This replaces SFINAE and tag dispatch for many use cases."}, {"question": "What are structured bindings?", "answer": "auto [a, b, c] = tuple_or_struct; — unpacks aggregates, tuples, pairs, and arrays into named variables. Much cleaner than std::get<0>(tuple)."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_16_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_16_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_16_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_16_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `C++14/17 — Refinements and Vocabulary Types`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_16_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_16_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_16_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_16_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_16_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_16_sub_1_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_16_sub_1", "day": 16, "index": 1}, {"title": "C++20/23 — Concepts, Ranges, Coroutines, Expected", "explanation": "C++20: concepts (constrained templates), ranges (composable algorithms), coroutines (`co_await`/`co_yield`), modules (replace headers), spaceship operator (<=>), `std::format` (type-safe printf), `consteval`/`constinit`, `std::span`, `std::jthread`, `std::latch`/barrier/semaphore.\n\nC++23: `std::expected<T,E>` (Result type), deducing this (explicit object parameter), more `constexpr` relaxations, `std::print`, multidimensional `operator[]`, `std::generator`, `std::flat_map`.", "examples": [{"title": "C++20/23 Features", "code": "// C++20 Concepts\ntemplate<std::integral T>\nT gcd(T a, T b) { return b == 0 ? a : gcd(b, a % b); }\n\n// C++20 Ranges\nauto evens = v | std::views::filter([](int n){ return n%2==0; })\n               | std::views::transform([](int n){ return n*2; });\n\n// C++20 Spaceship operator\nstruct Point\n{\n    int x, y;\n    auto operator<=>(const Point&) const = default;  // generates all 6 comparisons\n};\n\n// C++23 std::expected\nstd::expected<int, std::string> parse(const std::string& s)\n{\n    try { return std::stoi(s); }\n    catch (...) { return std::unexpected(\"parse error\"); }\n}\n\n// C++23 deducing this\nstruct Widget\n{\n    void foo(this const Widget& self) { /* ... */ }  // explicit this parameter\n};", "explanation": "C++20/23: concepts for safety, ranges for composition, expected for error handling.", "formatted_code": "// C++20 Concepts\ntemplate<std::integral T>\nT gcd(T a, T b)\n{\n    return b == 0 ? a : gcd(b, a % b);\n}\n\n// C++20 Ranges\nauto evens = v | std::views::filter([](int n)\n{\n    return n%2==0;\n}\n)\n| std::views::transform([](int n)\n{\n    return n*2;\n}\n);\n\n// C++20 Spaceship operator\nstruct Point\n{\n    int x, y;\n    auto operator<=>(const Point&) const = default;  // generates all 6 comparisons\n};\n\n// C++23 std::expected\nstd::expected<int, std::string> parse(const std::string& s)\n{\n    try\n    {\n        return std::stoi(s);\n    }\n    catch (...)\n    {\n        return std::unexpected(\"parse error\");\n    }\n}\n\n// C++23 deducing this\nstruct Widget\n{\n    void foo(this const Widget& self)\n    {\n        /* ... */\n    }  // explicit this parameter\n};"}], "pitfalls": ["Overusing concepts — simple functions don't always need constraints", "Coroutines are powerful but complex — debug tooling is still maturing", "Modules adoption is slow — build system support varies"], "tips_to_remember": ["Concepts replace **SFINAE** for readable `template` constraints", "Ranges enable functional-style data pipelines with lazy evaluation", "<=> (spaceship) generates all comparison operators from one declaration", "`std::expected` is C++'s Result type — use for expected errors without exceptions"], "questions": [{"question": "What are C++20 ranges?", "answer": "Composable, lazy algorithms using the pipe operator. Views transform data without copying. v | filter(pred) | transform(fn) creates a lazy pipeline evaluated on demand."}, {"question": "What is the spaceship operator?", "answer": "auto operator<=>(const T&) const = default; generates all six comparison operators (<, <=, ==, !=, >=, >) from one declaration. Returns an ordering category (strong_ordering, weak_ordering, partial_ordering)."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "When does template instantiation occur in C++?", "options": ["At runtime, when the function is called.", "At compile time, when the template is instantiated with concrete types.", "At link time, when symbols are resolved.", "During preprocessing."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Templates are compile-time constructs. The compiler generates concrete class or function code (instantiation) during compilation when it sees the template used with specific type arguments.", "id": "day_16_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is SFINAE (Substitution Failure Is Not An Error) in C++ templates?", "options": ["A compiler flag that ignores all syntax errors inside templates.", "A rule stating that if a substitution failure occurs during overload candidate resolution, the candidate is discarded without causing a compilation error.", "A template debugging tool.", "A C++20 feature that replaces concepts."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "SFINAE states that if a type substitution fails while inspecting overloaded templates, it does not stop compilation. The compiler simply removes that candidate from the overload set and keeps searching.", "id": "day_16_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "How do you define an explicit template specialization for class `Box` with type `int`?", "options": ["`template<int> class Box { ... };`", "`template<> class Box<int> { ... };`", "`template class Box<int>;`", "`specialized class Box<int> { ... };`"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Explicit (or full) template specialization is declared with `template<>` followed by the class definition specifying the type in brackets: `class Box<int> { ... };`.", "id": "day_16_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why is the `typename` keyword required before a nested dependent name (e.g. `typename T::iterator`) in templates?", "options": ["It is a hint to the compiler to inline the type.", "To resolve ambiguity, informing the compiler that the name refers to a nested type rather than a static member variable.", "To import the type into the namespace.", "It is only required in C++98, not modern C++."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because `T` is a template parameter, the compiler cannot inspect it during the first phase of template parsing. It assumes nested names are static members unless prefixed with `typename` to signify a type.", "id": "day_16_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "How does `std::enable_if` conditionally enable templates?", "options": ["It throws a compilation warning if the condition is false.", "It uses SFINAE: if the condition is false, the nested type does not exist, causing substitution to fail and candidate discard.", "It is a runtime check that throws an exception.", "It disables optimizer passes for the template."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::enable_if_t<Cond, T>` defines a nested `type` equal to `T` only if `Cond` is true. If `Cond` is false, it has no nested type, triggering a substitution failure (SFINAE) and excluding the template from overload resolution.", "id": "day_16_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What is the expanded result of the C++17 fold expression `(... + args)` where `args` is `1, 2, 3`?", "options": ["`(1 + (2 + 3))`", "`((1 + 2) + 3)`", "`1 + 2 + 3` (flat summation)", "It is invalid syntax."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`(... + args)` is a unary left fold. It expands starting from the left: `((1 + 2) + 3)`. A right fold is `(args + ...)` which expands to `(1 + (2 + 3))`.", "id": "day_16_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What happens if a template argument fails to satisfy a C++20 `concept` constraint (e.g. `requires std::integral<T>`)?", "options": ["The compiler falls back to runtime type checks.", "It produces a clean compilation error at the call site, listing which constraint failed.", "The compiler ignores the template and uses default arguments.", "It triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++20 concepts replace complex template SFINAE hacks with clear, readable constraint requirements. If constraint checks fail, the compiler outputs a clean, readable error explaining exactly which constraint was violated.", "id": "day_16_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "Can you partially specialize a function template in C++?", "options": ["Yes, using the standard `template<typename T> void f<T*>()` syntax.", "No, function templates can only be fully specialized; you must use function overloading instead.", "Yes, but only inside namespaces.", "Yes, if the return type is `auto`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ does not allow partial specialization of function templates. If you need to partially specialize a function template, you must use function overloading or delegate to a helper class template.", "id": "day_16_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is two-phase lookup in template compilation?", "options": ["The compiler parses the template once for syntax, and once for linkage.", "Non-dependent names are checked in phase 1 (definition); dependent names are checked in phase 2 (instantiation).", "It is a runtime optimization phase.", "The compiler compiles the header first, then the source."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "During phase 1, the compiler parses the template and checks all names that do not depend on template parameters. During phase 2 (when the template is instantiated with actual types), the compiler resolves all dependent names.", "id": "day_16_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is Class Template Argument Deduction (CTAD) introduced in C++17?", "options": ["It allows class templates to inherit template arguments from base classes.", "It allows the compiler to deduce class template parameters from constructor arguments, avoiding explicit template parameters.", "It compiles templates faster by caching template types.", "It is a dynamic cast for templates."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "CTAD allows writing `std::pair p(1, 2.5)` instead of `std::pair<int, double> p(1, 2.5)`. The compiler automatically infers the template types by inspecting constructor parameters.", "id": "day_16_sub_2_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_16_sub_2", "day": 16, "index": 2}, {"title": "Coroutines — co_await, co_yield, co_return", "explanation": "Coroutines (C++20) are functions that can suspend and resume, enabling asynchronous and generator-style code without threads. A coroutine is any function containing `co_await`, `co_yield`, or `co_return`. The compiler transforms it into a state machine, allocating a coroutine frame on the heap to hold suspended state.\n\nKey concepts: `co_await` suspends until an awaiter is ready; `co_yield` produces a value in a generator; `co_return` returns from the coroutine. The return type must be a coroutine type (e.g. `std::generator<T>` in C++23, or a custom task type). Coroutines are the foundation for async I/O, lazy generators, and cooperative multitasking.\n\nC++23 adds `std::generator<T>` which dramatically simplifies writing generators — you just write a function with `co_yield` and it becomes an iterable range.", "examples": [{"title": "Coroutine Generator (C++23)", "code": "#include <generator>\n\n// A generator that yields the Fibonacci sequence\nstd::generator<int> fibonacci()\n{\n    int a = 0, b = 1;\n    while (true)\n    {\n        co_yield a;          // produce a value, suspend\n        int next = a + b;\n        a = b;\n        b = next;\n    }\n}\n\nint main()\n{\n    for (int n : fibonacci()) // iterate lazily\n    {\n        if (n > 100) break;\n        std::cout << n << ' ';\n    }\n}", "explanation": "std::generator (C++23) makes coroutine generators trivial — co_yield produces values lazily.", "formatted_code": "#include <generator>\n\n// A generator that yields the Fibonacci sequence\nstd::generator<int> fibonacci()\n{\n    int a = 0, b = 1;\n    while (true)\n    {\n        co_yield a;  // produce a value, suspend\n        int next = a + b;\n        a = b;\n        b = next;\n    }\n}\n\nint main()\n{\n    for (int n : fibonacci())  // iterate lazily\n    {\n        if (n > 100) break;\n        std::cout << n << ' ';\n    }\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["Forgetting that coroutine frames are heap-allocated — can be a performance concern", "Coroutines can't be used in `constexpr` functions", "A coroutine that never suspends still allocates a frame", "Debugging coroutines is harder — the stack is split across suspension points"], "tips_to_remember": ["`co_await` suspends, `co_yield` produces a value, `co_return` returns", "Coroutines enable async code without threads and lazy generators without manual state machines", "`std::generator<T>` (C++23) is the easy way to write generators", "The coroutine frame holds suspended state — it's heap-allocated"], "questions": [{"question": "What is a coroutine?", "answer": "A function that can suspend and resume. It contains co_await, co_yield, or co_return. The compiler transforms it into a state machine with a heap-allocated frame. Coroutines enable async I/O and lazy generators without threads."}, {"question": "What is the difference between co_await and co_yield?", "answer": "co_await suspends the coroutine until an asynchronous operation completes (used in async/task coroutines). co_yield produces a value and suspends, used in generator coroutines to lazily produce a sequence of values."}, {"question": "What does std::generator do in C++23?", "answer": "std::generator<T> is a standard coroutine type that makes writing generators trivial. A function returning std::generator<T> that uses co_yield becomes a lazily-evaluated, iterable range — no manual coroutine boilerplate needed."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_16_sub_3_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_16_sub_3_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_16_sub_3_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_16_sub_3_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `Coroutines — co_await, co_yield, co_return`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_16_sub_3_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_16_sub_3_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_16_sub_3_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_16_sub_3_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_16_sub_3_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_16_sub_3_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_16_sub_3", "day": 16, "index": 3}, {"title": "std::format & std::print — Modern Text Formatting", "explanation": "`std::format` (C++20) provides type-safe, Python-style string formatting: `std::format`(\"{} + {} = {}\", a, b, a+b). It's faster and safer than printf (no format-string/argument mismatch) and more readable than iostreams. `std::print` (C++23) writes formatted output directly to stdout without stream overhead.\n\nFormat specifiers: {:d} integer, {:f} float, {:x} hex, {:>10} right-align width 10, {:.2f} two decimals. `std::format_to` writes into a buffer. `std::formatter<T>` can be specialized for user-defined types.", "examples": [{"title": "Formatting", "code": "#include <format>\n#include <print>\n\nint x = 42;\ndouble pi = 3.14159;\n\n// Type-safe formatting\nstd::string s = std::format(\"x = {}, pi = {:.2f}\", x, pi);\n// s == \"x = 42, pi = 3.14\"\n\n// Alignment and width\nstd::format(\"{:>10}\", \"right\");   // right-align in 10 chars\nstd::format(\"{:<10}\", \"left\");    // left-align\nstd::format(\"{:^10}\", \"center\");  // center\n\n// C++23 std::print — direct to stdout\nstd::print(\"Hello, {}!\\n\", \"world\");\n\n// Formatting user types via std::formatter specialization\nstruct Point { int x, y; };\ntemplate<> struct std::formatter<Point> {\n    constexpr auto parse(format_parse_context& ctx) { return ctx.begin(); }\n    auto format(const Point& p, format_context& ctx) const\n    {\n        return std::format_to(ctx.out(), \"({}, {})\", p.x, p.y);\n    }\n};", "explanation": "std::format is type-safe and fast. std::print writes directly to stdout.", "formatted_code": "#include <format>\n#include <print>\n\nint x = 42;\ndouble pi = 3.14159;\n\n// Type-safe formatting\nstd::string s = std::format(\"x = {}, pi = {:.2f}\", x, pi);\n// s == \"x = 42, pi = 3.14\"\n\n// Alignment and width\nstd::format(\"{:>10}\", \"right\");  // right-align in 10 chars\nstd::format(\"{:<10}\", \"left\");  // left-align\nstd::format(\"{:^10}\", \"center\");  // center\n\n// C++23 std::print — direct to stdout\nstd::print(\"Hello, {}!\\n\", \"world\");\n\n// Formatting user types via std::formatter specialization\nstruct Point\n{\n    int x, y;\n};\ntemplate<> struct std::formatter<Point>\n{\n    constexpr auto parse(format_parse_context& ctx)\n    {\n        return ctx.begin();\n    }\n    auto format(const Point& p, format_context& ctx) const\n    {\n        return std::format_to(ctx.out(), \"({}, {})\", p.x, p.y);\n    }\n};"}], "pitfalls": ["Using printf with mismatched format specifiers — UB", "Forgetting that `std::format` throws `std::format_error` on invalid format strings", "`std::format` is not a stream — you can't chain with <<"], "tips_to_remember": ["`std::format` is type-safe — the compiler checks format strings against arguments", "Use {:.2f} for precision, {:>10} for alignment", "`std::print` (C++23) is faster than `std::cout` — no stream overhead", "Specialize `std::formatter<T>` to format your own types"], "questions": [{"question": "What is the advantage of std::format over printf?", "answer": "std::format is type-safe: the format string is checked against the argument types at compile time, eliminating printf's format-specifier/argument mismatch bugs. It's also faster than iostreams and more readable."}, {"question": "What is the difference between std::format and std::print?", "answer": "std::format returns a formatted std::string. std::print (C++23) formats and writes directly to stdout, avoiding the intermediate string and stream overhead — it's faster and produces smaller binaries."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What action invalidates all active iterators of a `std::vector`?", "options": ["Modifying an element inside the vector.", "An insertion that causes the vector to exceed its current `capacity()`, triggering memory reallocation.", "Calling `.reserve()` with a value smaller than the current capacity.", "Iterating through the vector in reverse order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If a vector grows beyond its `capacity()`, it allocates a new larger heap block, copies elements over, and frees the old block. All pointers, references, and iterators pointing to the old memory become invalid (dangling).", "id": "day_16_sub_4_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the average search complexity of `std::unordered_map` vs `std::map`?", "options": ["O(n) vs O(1)", "O(1) (hash table lookup) vs O(log n) (balanced binary tree traversal).", "O(log n) vs O(n log n)", "They have the same complexity."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::unordered_map` uses a hash table, providing O(1) average search complexity. `std::map` uses a red-black tree, requiring O(log n) comparisons.", "id": "day_16_sub_4_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::vector<bool>` space-optimized, and what is its major drawback?", "options": ["It uses compression algorithms, making it slow.", "It packs elements as single bits (1 bit per bool), but since bits are not addressable, it cannot return a standard `bool&` reference, returning a proxy instead.", "It cannot be resized after construction.", "It is a compile-time constant array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To save space, `std::vector<bool>` stores 8 booleans per byte. Because C++ cannot reference sub-byte memory bits, `operator[]` returns a proxy object (`std::vector<bool>::reference`) instead of `bool&`, which can break generic code.", "id": "day_16_sub_4_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Why does calling `std::sort` on a `std::list` result in a compilation error?", "options": ["`std::list` cannot be sorted.", "`std::sort` requires random-access iterators; `std::list` only provides bidirectional iterators. You must use `std::list::sort()` instead.", "`std::list` elements are constant.", "You must include `<list_sort>`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::sort` uses algorithms that require random-access iterators (like `std::vector` or arrays). `std::list` is a doubly linked list and only supports bidirectional traversal, so you must use the container-specific member `.sort()`.", "id": "day_16_sub_4_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the primary danger of using `std::string_view` in `std::format & std::print — Modern Text Formatting`?", "options": ["It copies strings, reducing performance.", "It is a non-owning view; if the underlying string object is destroyed (e.g. temporary string), the `string_view` becomes a dangling pointer.", "It cannot be converted to `std::string`.", "It requires compile-time evaluation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` stores a pointer and a length. It does not own the characters. If the owner string is modified, moved, or destroyed, the `string_view` refers to invalid memory.", "id": "day_16_sub_4_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Does calling the `std::remove` algorithm actually erase elements and resize a container?", "options": ["Yes, it removes elements and changes the container size.", "No, it only shifts the elements to the front and returns an iterator to the new logical end; you must call the container's `.erase()` to resize it (Erase-Remove idiom).", "No, it is a compile-time check only.", "Yes, but only for `std::list`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::remove` operates on iterators and cannot access the container's size properties. It moves elements to the front, leaving the container size unchanged. You must call `.erase(new_end, end)` to delete the trailing elements.", "id": "day_16_sub_4_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does C++23 `std::expected<T, E>` represent?", "options": ["A template class that estimates execution time.", "A vocabulary type that holds either a success value of type `T` or an error value of type `E` (unexpected), replacing exceptions for expected errors.", "A replacement for `std::future`.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` provides functional error handling. It returns either the success result or the error payload in a single type-safe object, avoiding exception-handling overhead for predictable failures.", "id": "day_16_sub_4_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is a `std::span` introduced in C++20?", "options": ["A wrapper that runs objects across multiple CPU cores.", "A non-owning view over a contiguous sequence of elements (like an array or vector), storing a pointer and a size.", "A compiler instruction for thread interference size.", "A class that measures time spans."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is a lightweight, non-owning view that provides safe, contiguous access to arrays, vectors, or buffers without copying data or depending on specific container types.", "id": "day_16_sub_4_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What happens if a lambda captures a local variable by reference `[&]`, and is executed after the variable goes out of scope?", "options": ["The lambda copies the variable before destruction.", "Accessing the reference triggers Undefined Behavior because it points to a destroyed stack variable.", "The compiler extends the variable's lifetime automatically.", "It is a compile-time error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Capturing by reference `[&]` does not extend the lifetime of stack variables. If the lambda executes after the variable goes out of scope, it is a dangling reference and accessing it is undefined behavior.", "id": "day_16_sub_4_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What category is returned by the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["`std::strong_ordering`", "`std::weak_ordering`", "`std::partial_ordering` (because `NaN` is unordered)", "`std::float_ordering`"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point numbers can represent `NaN` (Not a Number). Since `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers (`NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false), comparisons are partially ordered, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_16_sub_4_mcq_9"}], "id": "day_16_sub_4", "day": 16, "index": 4}]}, {"day": 17, "topic": "Summary Revision", "estimated_hours": 4, "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the value category of the variable `r` when evaluated in the expression `r` if declared as `int&& r = 42;`?", "options": ["lvalue", "prvalue", "xvalue", "rvalue reference"], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Although `r` is declared as an rvalue reference, when used as an expression, any named variable (including rvalue references) is an lvalue. To treat it as an rvalue, you must cast it, e.g., using `std::move(r)`."}, {"question": "What is the resulting type of the conditional expression `true ? 1 : 2.0` in C++?", "options": ["int", "double", "float", "Compilation error"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The conditional operator (?:) requires a common type between the second and third operands. The compiler performs standard arithmetic promotions, converting `int` (1) to `double` (1.0), so the type is `double`."}, {"question": "In C++11, what is the behavior of the expression `int x = 1; int y = x++ + ++x;`?", "options": ["y is 3", "y is 4", "y is 5", "Undefined Behavior"], "correct_option": 3, "explanation": "In C++11, the order of evaluation of operands in arithmetic operators (like `+`) is unsequenced. Modifying `x` twice without an intervening sequence point triggers Undefined Behavior. In C++17, sequence rules were refined, but double modification in a single expression is still UB if unsequenced."}, {"question": "What is the exact value returned by `sizeof(char)` in C++ on any compliant platform?", "options": ["Always 1", "Implementation-defined (typically 1)", "Depends on the system word size", "Depends on compilation flags"], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "By definition in the C++ standard, `sizeof(char)`, `sizeof(signed char)`, and `sizeof(unsigned char)` are always exactly 1. A byte in C++ is defined as the size of a char, which can contain more than 8 bits on some DSPs, but `sizeof(char)` is still 1."}, {"question": "What happens when you pass a `const std::string` object to `std::move` and use it to initialize another string?", "options": ["It is moved successfully, leaving the source empty.", "It silently falls back to copy construction because the const qualifier prevents resource theft.", "It causes a compilation error due to const mismatch.", "It triggers undefined behavior at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::move` only casts the argument to an rvalue reference: `const std::string&&`. Since the type is `const`, the move constructor of `std::string` (which requires `std::string&&`) cannot be bound. The compiler falls back to the copy constructor (`const std::string&`)."}, {"question": "Suppose we have `auto [x, y] = my_pair;` where `my_pair` is a `std::pair<int, int>`. What are the types of `x` and `y`?", "options": ["References pointing directly to elements of `my_pair`.", "Independent copies of the elements of `my_pair`.", "Names that refer to members of an anonymous copy of `my_pair`.", "Pointers to the elements of `my_pair`."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Structured bindings initialize an anonymous copy of the initializer (e.g., `auto __decomp = my_pair;`) and bind `x` and `y` as aliases to the members of that anonymous copy. To refer to the original, you must use `auto& [x, y] = my_pair;`."}, {"question": "Given `int x = 10;`, what is the deduced type of `y` for `decltype(auto) y = (x);`?", "options": ["int", "int&", "int&&", "const int&"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`decltype(auto)` uses `decltype` rules for deduction. A parenthesized variable expression `(x)` is evaluated as an lvalue expression, so `decltype((x))` yields `int&`. Thus, `y` becomes a reference to `x`."}, {"question": "Why is returning `std::move(local_var)` from a function returning by value considered a bad practice?", "options": ["It causes a memory leak.", "It defeats NRVO (Named Return Value Optimization), forcing a move instead of zero-cost copy elision.", "It triggers compilation warnings on all C++20 compilers.", "It causes local_var to be destroyed before returning."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Returning a local variable by value allows the compiler to construct it directly in the caller's frame (NRVO). Returning `std::move(x)` returns an xvalue (`T&&`), forcing a move constructor call and preventing elision."}, {"question": "Why is `&v[0]` invalid for `std::vector<bool> v;`?", "options": ["vector elements are allocated on the stack.", "std::vector<bool> is space-optimized as a bitmask, so individual elements don't have distinct byte addresses.", "It is a const vector and address-of is disabled.", "Vector elements are only initialized on demand."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::vector<bool>` is a specialized template that packs booleans as single bits. Since the smallest addressable unit of memory is a byte, individual bits cannot be pointed to by raw pointers. Thus, `operator[]` returns a proxy class instead of `bool&`."}, {"question": "What is the primary risk of deleting a Derived object through a Base pointer when the Base class has virtual methods but no virtual destructor?", "options": ["The program will fail to compile.", "Only the Base destructor is called, leaking Derived resources and triggering Undefined Behavior.", "The Derived destructor is called, but Base destructor is skipped.", "The compiler automatically generates a virtual destructor, so there is no risk."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Deleting a derived object through a base class pointer without a virtual destructor is Undefined Behavior. In practice, only the Base destructor executes, leaking Derived members and corrupting heap state."}, {"question": "What happens in the code `Base b = Derived();`?", "options": ["b operates as a polymorphic Derived object.", "Object slicing occurs: the Derived portion is stripped, and only the Base copy constructor is invoked.", "Compilation fails due to size mismatch.", "b holds a reference to the temporary Derived object."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Assigning a derived object to a base object by value causes 'object slicing'. The derived members are sliced off, and only the base subobject is copied into `b`, losing all polymorphic capabilities."}, {"question": "In what order are member variables initialized in a class constructor?", "options": ["In the order they are listed in the constructor's initializer list.", "In the order they are declared in the class definition.", "Alphabetical order.", "Compiler-dependent random order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Member variables are always initialized in the order of their declaration in the class definition, regardless of the order in the constructor's initializer list. Listing them out-of-order can lead to bugs if one member depends on another."}, {"question": "Suppose Base has `virtual void f(int x = 10)` and Derived has `void f(int x = 20) override`. What is printed by `Base* p = new Derived(); p->f();`?", "options": ["Derived implementation is called with x = 20.", "Derived implementation is called with x = 10.", "Base implementation is called with x = 10.", "Compilation error due to default value mismatch."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Virtual function binding happens at runtime, but default parameter resolution happens at compile time based on the static type of the pointer (`Base*`). Thus, Derived's `f()` is executed, but with Base's default value of 10."}, {"question": "What is the size of an empty class `class Empty {};` in C++?", "options": ["0 bytes", "At least 1 byte", "4 bytes", "8 bytes"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ requires all objects to have a unique address. If the size of an empty class were 0, two consecutive objects in an array would share the same address. Thus, empty classes have a size of at least 1 byte."}, {"question": "Under what condition does Empty Base Optimization (EBO) fail?", "options": ["When the base class is virtual.", "When the first member of the derived class has the same type as the empty base class.", "When the derived class has virtual functions.", "When the compiler optimization level is set to -O0."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "EBO allows the empty base to occupy 0 bytes. However, if the first member of the derived class is of the same type as the empty base, C++ address uniqueness rules require them to have different addresses, forcing the empty base to occupy space (usually 1+ bytes)."}, {"question": "Which pointer cast violates the strict aliasing rule?", "options": ["Casting `int*` to `const int*`.", "Casting `int*` to `float*` and dereferencing it.", "Casting `int*` to `char*` and dereferencing it.", "Casting `Derived*` to `Base*`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Strict aliasing states that pointers of different incompatible types cannot point to the same object. Dereferencing a `float*` pointing to `int` memory is UB. Casting to `char*` or `unsigned char*` is an explicit standard exception."}, {"question": "What is the main benefit of the copy-and-swap idiom for assignment operators?", "options": ["It runs faster than simple assignments.", "It guarantees strong exception safety (all-or-nothing) and self-assignment safety.", "It eliminates the need for constructors.", "It does not copy members."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Copy-and-swap creates a copy of the parameter first (handling exceptions before modifying the target). Then, a non-throwing swap is called. If copying fails, the target is unmodified, providing strong exception safety and self-assignment safety."}, {"question": "What happens in: `const int& r = 10;`?", "options": ["r binds to a dangling temporary.", "The lifetime of the temporary integer 10 is extended to match the lifetime of reference r.", "It is a compilation error because 10 is an rvalue.", "r holds a pointer to 10."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Binding a temporary (prvalue) to a const lvalue reference (or an rvalue reference) extends the lifetime of that temporary to match the reference's scope, avoiding dangling references."}, {"question": "What happens if a class's move constructor is not marked `noexcept`?", "options": ["It cannot be moved.", "Standard containers like `std::vector` will fall back to copy operations during reallocation to preserve exception guarantees.", "The program will fail to compile.", "The compiler automatically adds noexcept anyway."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::vector::reserve` or reallocation moves elements only if their move constructor is marked `noexcept`. If it can throw, the vector copies them instead so that if an exception occurs mid-way, it can roll back to the original state."}, {"question": "Which part of `std::shared_ptr` is thread-safe by default?", "options": ["Both the control block and the pointed-to object.", "Only the atomic reference counting inside the control block; modifying the pointed-to object from multiple threads requires external synchronization.", "Only the pointed-to object.", "Neither, shared_ptr is completely thread-unsafe."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The reference count increments/decrements in the control block are atomic and thread-safe. However, concurrent writes to the pointed-to object itself or to the same `shared_ptr` instance are not synchronized and require mutexes."}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast<Derived&>(base_ref)` do if base_ref is not actually a Derived object?", "options": ["Returns a null reference.", "Throws a `std::bad_cast` exception.", "Returns a fallback default object.", "Triggers undefined behavior at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "References cannot be null, so `dynamic_cast` for reference types cannot return null on failure. Instead, it throws a `std::bad_cast` exception to report dynamic type mismatch."}, {"question": "Is it legal for a pure virtual function to have a body in C++?", "options": ["No, pure virtual means `= 0` and cannot have any code.", "Yes, it can have a definition, but it must be defined outside the class declaration. It can be called statically.", "Yes, but only if the class is not abstract.", "Yes, it must be inline inside the class."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A pure virtual function can have a body: `void Base::f() { ... }`. Derived classes must still override it, but they can invoke the base implementation statically using `Base::f()`."}, {"question": "Why is `std::weak_ptr` designed without `operator*` and `operator->`?", "options": ["It is a raw pointer wrapper.", "To prevent accessing an object that may have been deleted concurrently; you must call `.lock()` to get a `shared_ptr` first.", "It is a compile-time optimization.", "It only holds type information."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`weak_ptr` does not own the object. Accessing it directly could race with destruction. Calling `.lock()` atomically checks if the object is alive and increments the reference count, securing a safe `shared_ptr` for access."}, {"question": "What is the key performance difference between `std::any` and `std::variant`?", "options": ["any is faster because it uses type erasure.", "variant stores its alternatives inline (no heap allocation), whereas any dynamically allocates memory if the object size exceeds the small buffer threshold.", "any does not support copy operations.", "variant uses virtual table lookup for all accesses."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::variant` is a template-based type-safe union with inline storage (no heap). `std::any` is an unbounded container that uses type erasure and heap allocation for storing values larger than its internal small buffer."}, {"question": "Where must a substitution failure occur for SFINAE to apply?", "options": ["Anywhere within the function body.", "Only in the immediate context of the function template declaration (e.g., return type, template parameters, function arguments).", "In any of the header files included.", "Only inside class definitions."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "SFINAE only applies to failures in the 'immediate context' of the template declaration (deducing argument types, parsing function signatures). A template that compiles successfully but fails inside its body is a hard compiler error."}, {"question": "What happens to dependent names (names depending on template parameters) during the first phase of template compilation?", "options": ["They are fully compiled and optimized.", "They are only checked for basic syntax; verification is deferred to the second phase (instantiation).", "They are resolved using global namespace symbols.", "They are ignored entirely."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ templates use two-phase lookup. Non-dependent names are resolved in Phase 1 (at template definition). Dependent names (e.g. `T::type`) are only parsed for syntax in Phase 1, and fully resolved in Phase 2 (instantiation)."}, {"question": "Since C++11, how is thread-safe initialization of static local variables ('magic statics') achieved?", "options": ["The developer must wrap the initialization in a mutex.", "The compiler automatically inserts synchronization (locks/fences) to guarantee thread-safe initialization.", "It is not thread-safe; magic statics only work in single-threaded builds.", "Threads must be spawned after static variables are initialized."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++11 mandates that local static variables are initialized in a thread-safe manner. The compiler wraps the initialization block with atomic checks and locks so that multiple threads concurrently passing it will block until initialization is complete."}, {"question": "What happens if a `std::thread` object is destroyed while it is still joinable?", "options": ["The thread is joined automatically.", "The thread is detached automatically.", "The program terminates immediately via `std::terminate`.", "The thread is paused until a new object binds to it."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "A joinable thread has an active thread of execution. If the `std::thread` wrapper goes out of scope and is destroyed without calling `.join()` or `.detach()`, the destructor invokes `std::terminate`, crashing the program."}, {"question": "What does the `volatile` keyword guarantee in C++ multithreaded programs?", "options": ["Atomicity of operations.", "Memory visibility fences to prevent CPU reordering.", "Nothing regarding thread synchronization; it only prevents compiler optimizations.", "Locking of cache lines."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "`volatile` tells the compiler that the value can change outside the program's control (e.g. hardware register). It forces access to memory, but it does NOT generate atomic instructions or CPU memory barriers. Do not use `volatile` for threads."}, {"question": "What is a major risk when using placement `new` with a pre-allocated stack byte array?", "options": ["Stack memory is too slow.", "Misalignment: if the byte array address is not a multiple of the class alignment (`alignof`), it triggers Undefined Behavior.", "Stack arrays cannot hold class objects.", "Placement new automatically frees the stack array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Objects must be placed at memory addresses aligned to their types. A simple `char buffer[N]` has 1-byte alignment. Constructing an `int` or a class on it using placement new without aligning the buffer (e.g., using `alignas`) is UB."}, {"question": "How do you clean up an object constructed using placement `new`?", "options": ["Call `delete ptr;`.", "Manually call its destructor: `ptr->~T();`.", "It is cleaned up automatically when the buffer is destroyed.", "Call `free(ptr);`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since placement `new` did not allocate memory from the global heap, calling `delete` is undefined behavior. You must explicitly invoke the destructor `ptr->~T()` and then release or reuse the memory buffer."}, {"question": "When does a C-style array `int arr[5]` NOT decay to a pointer `int*`?", "options": ["When passed as an argument to a function.", "When used as the operand of `sizeof` or address-of `&` operator.", "When assigned to another array.", "It always decays to a pointer."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "An array decays to a pointer in most contexts. However, when passed to `sizeof(arr)` (returns the total size in bytes of the array) or `&arr` (returns a pointer to the array `int(*)[5]`), decay does not occur."}, {"question": "What is the hazard of `#define SQUARE(x) x * x` when called as `SQUARE(1 + 2)`?", "options": ["It returns 9.", "It expands to `1 + 2 * 1 + 2`, returning 5.", "It triggers a compilation error.", "It causes undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Macros perform literal textual replacement. `SQUARE(1 + 2)` is replaced with `1 + 2 * 1 + 2`. Due to operator precedence, `2 * 1` is evaluated first, resulting in `1 + 2 + 2 = 5`. Macros must be parenthesized: `((x) * (x))`."}, {"question": "Why would a developer write `void foo(double) = delete;` next to `void foo(int);`?", "options": ["To prevent implicit conversion: passing a float/double to `foo` will trigger a compilation error instead of silently converting to `int`.", "To speed up template compilation.", "To declare `foo(double)` as inline.", "It is a syntax error."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Declaring a function overload as `= delete` removes it from compilation. If a user tries to call `foo` with a double, overload resolution matches the deleted `foo(double)` instead of converting it to `foo(int)`, raising a compiler error."}, {"question": "Does Koenig Lookup (ADL) apply to a call `std::swap(a, b)`?", "options": ["Yes, it searches the namespace of a and b.", "No, ADL is only performed for unqualified function calls; qualified calls follow standard lookup rules.", "Yes, but only in template functions.", "No, swap is a compiler built-in."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "ADL only occurs when the function name is unqualified (e.g. `swap(a, b)`). If it is qualified (`std::swap(a, b)`), the compiler only looks inside the `std` namespace and ignores associated namespaces of `a` and `b`."}, {"question": "What is the core difference between `constexpr` and C++20 `consteval`?", "options": ["constexpr is faster.", "constexpr functions can be evaluated at compile time OR runtime; consteval functions (immediate functions) MUST be evaluated at compile time.", "consteval is only for class member functions.", "constexpr is deprecated in favor of consteval."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constexpr` indicates compile-time capability. If called with runtime arguments, a `constexpr` function runs at runtime. `consteval` guarantees compile-time execution; calling it with runtime arguments is a compilation error."}, {"question": "Does `constinit` guarantee that a variable is immutable (read-only) at runtime?", "options": ["Yes, it is equivalent to constexpr.", "No, it only guarantees that the variable is initialized at compile time (preventing static initialization order issues), but it can be mutable at runtime.", "Yes, but only for global variables.", "No, it is a compilation error to modify it unless it is marked const."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constinit` enforces compile-time initialization of variables with static/thread storage. It does not enforce runtime immutability. To make it read-only, you must combine it with const: `constinit const int x = 42;`."}, {"question": "How does CRTP (Curiously Recurring Template Pattern) achieve polymorphism without virtual functions?", "options": ["By using macro expansion.", "The base class templates on the derived class, allowing static casts to call derived member functions at compile time.", "By using run-time RTTI lookup tables.", "CRTP requires virtual tables."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "In CRTP, base inherits from template parameter: `class Base : public Class<Base>`. The base class casts its `this` pointer to the Derived template parameter: `static_cast<Derived*>(this)->foo()`, resolving calls at compile time."}, {"question": "What is a covariant return type in virtual overrides?", "options": ["A return type that changes from float to double.", "An override that returns a pointer/reference to a class derived from the base return type.", "An invalid return type that triggers compiler warnings.", "A templated return type."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "An overriding virtual function can return a pointer/reference to a class derived from the class returned by the base virtual function. This is covariant return types, allowing more specific type returns without casting."}, {"question": "What is the purpose of `std::launder` (C++17)?", "options": ["It cleans up unused heap memory.", "It informs the optimizer that an object has been constructed in a memory location formerly occupied by another object, preventing invalid compiler optimizations based on old object properties.", "It casts pointers safely in templates.", "It validates JSON strings."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If you reuse memory (e.g. via placement new) and construct an object with const/reference members, compiler optimizations might assume the old members are still valid. `std::launder` forces the compiler to re-fetch the object state."}, {"question": "What is the main design difference between `std::byte` (C++17) and `unsigned char`?", "options": ["std::byte is larger.", "std::byte is a distinct enum class type that disables arithmetic operations (preventing treating byte streams as numbers) but permits bitwise operations.", "std::byte is faster to read.", "std::byte is stack-allocated only."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::byte` represents raw storage bits. To prevent treating raw bytes as characters or integers, it does not support arithmetic (`+`, `-`, etc.) or implicit conversions. You must perform bitwise operations (`&`, `|`, `<<`, `>>`) or casts."}, {"question": "Why does the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` return `std::partial_ordering` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["Because floats are not precise.", "Because floating-point comparisons can involve `NaN` (Not a Number), which is unordered relative to all values.", "To save compile time.", "It returns strong_ordering instead."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`NaN` is unordered relative to numbers (e.g., `NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false). Because there are values for which ordering cannot be established, floating-point comparisons return `std::partial_ordering`."}, {"question": "How do you initialize a `std::expected<int, std::string>` representing an error state?", "options": ["return \"error string\";", "return std::unexpected(\"error string\");", "return std::optional<std::string>(\"error string\");", "throw std::runtime_error(\"error string\");"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` implicitly constructs the success value from the initializer. To return the error type instead, you must wrap the error in `std::unexpected(...)`, signaling the expected class to bind the error path."}, {"question": "What is the difference between `v.reserve(100)` and `v.resize(100)` for a vector `v`?", "options": ["reserve changes the size, resize changes capacity.", "reserve allocates memory but does not construct elements (size is unchanged); resize allocates memory and constructs 100 elements (size becomes 100).", "They are identical.", "resize only works for integer vectors."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`reserve()` allocates raw memory for future insertions, leaving `size()` unchanged. `resize()` actually inserts or removes elements, default-constructing new elements if growing, which changes `size()`."}, {"question": "What is a major pitfall of using `operator[]` on a `std::map<int, BigClass>` to check if a key exists?", "options": ["It throws a key-not-found exception.", "It silently inserts a key with a default-constructed `BigClass` object if the key was missing, causing performance overhead and unwanted map entries.", "It runs in O(n) search time.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::map::operator[]` is designed to insert keys if they are not present. Using it to inspect keys default-constructs the value type in the map. Use `.find()` or `.contains()` (C++20) to check for existence safely."}, {"question": "What is the size of `std::vector` after calling `std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), 42)`?", "options": ["Reduced by the number of elements containing 42.", "Unchanged; elements matching 42 are moved to the end, and you must call `v.erase()` to shrink the vector.", "0, the vector is cleared.", "Compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Algorithms operate on iterators, not containers. `std::remove` cannot resize the vector. It shifts non-matching elements forward and returns the new logical end iterator. You must call `v.erase(new_end, v.end())` to resize."}, {"question": "Prior to C++20, what was the restriction of capturing structured bindings in lambdas?", "options": ["They could only be captured by reference.", "They could not be captured in lambdas because they were aliases, not true variables.", "They could only be captured by value.", "There were no restrictions."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Prior to C++20, structured bindings were aliases and could not be captured by lambdas. C++20 resolved this, allowing structured bindings to be captured by value or reference like ordinary variables."}, {"question": "What happens if you use `const_cast` to modify a variable that was originally declared as `const`?", "options": ["The variable is successfully modified.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior.", "It causes a compilation error.", "It behaves as a mutable variable."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`const_cast` can strip `const` from pointers/references. However, if the underlying object was originally declared as a `const` object (e.g. `const int x = 10;`), attempting to write to it via a cast pointer is Undefined Behavior."}, {"question": "Which of the following is Undefined Behavior under the strict aliasing rule?", "options": ["Reading an `int` through a `char*`.", "Reading an `int` through a `float*` using `reinterpret_cast`.", "Writing to an `unsigned char` buffer using a pointer.", "Casting a pointer to `std::uintptr_t` and back."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Casting pointers to incompatible types and dereferencing them violates the strict aliasing rule. `char*` and `unsigned char*` are explicit exceptions in the standard, allowing byte inspection of any object."}, {"question": "Under what condition does throwing an exception from a destructor call `std::terminate`?", "options": ["Destructors can never throw under any condition.", "If an exception is thrown from a destructor during stack unwinding (when another exception is already active).", "If the destructor is virtual.", "Only on Windows platforms."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ cannot process two active exceptions at the same time. If a destructor throws an exception while another exception is active (unwinding the stack), the runtime aborts the program immediately by calling `std::terminate`."}, {"question": "Given `int x = 0;`, what are the types of `decltype(x)` and `decltype((x))`?", "options": ["int, int", "int, int&", "int&, int", "int&, int&"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`decltype(variable)` returns the declared type of the variable (`int`). `decltype(expression)` returns the type of the expression: since `(x)` is an lvalue expression, it yields `int&`."}, {"question": "What is the actual type of parameter `a` inside the function `void foo(int a[10][20])`?", "options": ["int a[10][20]", "int (*a)[20] (pointer to array of 20 ints)", "int** a", "int (*a)[10][20]"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Only the first dimension of an array parameter decays to a pointer. `int a[10][20]` decays to a pointer to the second dimension, which is `int (*a)[20]`."}, {"question": "How do you safely allocate a stack buffer for placement new of type `T`?", "options": ["char buf[sizeof(T)];", "alignas(T) char buf[sizeof(T)];", "static char buf[sizeof(T)];", "char* buf = new char[sizeof(T)];"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To avoid misalignment UB, the buffer must be aligned. `alignas(T)` ensures the stack array starts at a memory address compatible with `T`'s alignment requirements."}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast<Derived*>(base_ptr)` return on failure?", "options": ["A default constructed Derived object", "Throws std::bad_cast", "nullptr", "Undefined Behavior"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Unlike reference dynamic casting which throws an exception, dynamic casting pointers returns `nullptr` on failure, allowing you to check success with a simple if condition."}, {"question": "Which of the following causes a compilation error for a function marked `override`?", "options": ["The base class function is not virtual.", "The base class function has a different const-qualifier or signature.", "There is no matching function in the base class.", "All of the above."], "correct_option": 3, "explanation": "The `override` keyword requires a matching virtual function signature in the base class. If the base function is non-virtual, has different constness, or doesn't exist, compilation fails."}, {"question": "What is the typical size of a class with virtual functions but no data members on a 64-bit compiler?", "options": ["1 byte", "4 bytes", "8 bytes", "16 bytes"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "A class with virtual functions contains a virtual table pointer (`vptr`). On 64-bit systems, pointers are 8 bytes, so the object size is 8 bytes."}, {"question": "What is the danger of returning a `std::initializer_list` from a function?", "options": ["It copies elements too slowly.", "The underlying array of the initializer list is a temporary on the stack; returning it results in a dangling reference.", "It is a compilation error.", "It leaks memory on the heap."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::initializer_list` does not own its elements; it points to a temporary stack array constructed by the compiler. Once the function returns, the temporary array is destroyed, leaving the list dangling."}, {"question": "What is the result of `int* p = new int(10); std::shared_ptr<int> s1(p); std::shared_ptr<int> s2(p);`?", "options": ["s1 and s2 share ownership correctly.", "Double-delete crash at scope exit because s1 and s2 create independent control blocks.", "Compilation error.", "A memory leak."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Initializing two independent smart pointers from the same raw pointer makes them unaware of each other. They will create separate control blocks and both delete `p` at scope exit, causing double-free UB."}, {"question": "What is a potential downside of `std::make_shared` compared to `std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)`?", "options": ["It is slower.", "If `weak_ptr` instances outlive all `shared_ptr` instances, the object memory cannot be freed until all weak pointers are destroyed (since object and control block share one allocation).", "It does not support custom allocators.", "It causes memory fragmentation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because `std::make_shared` allocates the control block and object in one chunk, the memory block stays allocated as long as the control block exists. Even if reference count is 0, the chunk remains until weak count is 0."}, {"question": "Why is virtual inheritance used in diamond inheritance structures?", "options": ["To speed up virtual function calls.", "To ensure only one instance of the common grandparent base class is constructed and shared by the sibling derived classes.", "To prevent the derived classes from being instantiated.", "To compile files faster."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Virtual inheritance resolves the diamond problem: it instructs the compiler to share a single instance of the root base class among all inheriting paths, avoiding duplication and member lookup ambiguity."}, {"question": "If you explicitly declare a destructor for a class, what happens to the implicitly generated move constructor?", "options": ["It is generated normally.", "It is suppressed (not declared), forcing fallback to copying.", "It is declared as deleted.", "It is generated as inline."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Under the Rule of 5 guidelines, declaring a custom destructor, copy constructor, or copy assignment operator tells the compiler that resource management is custom, suppressing implicit move constructor generation."}, {"question": "What happens if you return `std::move(local_const)` where `local_const` is a `const MyClass`?", "options": ["It is moved successfully.", "It falls back to copy construction because const objects cannot be moved.", "It causes a compilation error.", "It triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::move` casts to `const MyClass&&`. Since move constructors require `MyClass&&` (non-const), the compiler cannot bind it and falls back to the copy constructor (`const MyClass&`)."}, {"question": "Why is passing `str_view.data()` to a C-style function (like `strlen`) dangerous?", "options": ["str_view is slower.", "std::string_view is not guaranteed to be null-terminated; it can refer to a substring of a larger buffer.", "It causes a compile error.", "It copies the string view data."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` only contains a pointer and a length. It can represent a slice of a string (e.g. `\"hello\"` without the null byte). Passing its `.data()` pointer to functions that expect a null terminator triggers buffer reads past bounds."}, {"question": "Can a `constexpr` function contain a loop in C++14?", "options": ["No, constexpr functions in C++14 are restricted to a single return statement.", "Yes, C++14 relaxed constexpr constraints to allow loops, local variables, and multiple return statements.", "No, loops are only allowed since C++20.", "Yes, but only if they are recursive."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++11 restricted constexpr functions to a single return statement. C++14 relaxed these rules significantly, permitting local variables, loops (`for`, `while`), and branching logic inside constexpr bodies."}, {"question": "How can you use SFINAE inside template parameters?", "options": ["Using `std::enable_if` in a default template parameter (e.g., `typename = std::enable_if_t<Cond>`).", "Using `#ifdef` inside the template block.", "By declaring the template parameter as static.", "You cannot use SFINAE inside template parameters."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "A common SFINAE technique is using `std::enable_if_t<Cond, int> = 0` as a default template parameter. If `Cond` is false, substitution fails, removing the overload from candidate sets."}, {"question": "What happens if you try to modify a variable captured by value inside a lambda?", "options": ["It compiles and modifies the original variable.", "It is a compilation error because captures are const by default; you must mark the lambda as `mutable`.", "It compiles and modifies a local copy without affecting the original.", "It triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "By default, the call operator of the generated closure class is `const`, so value captures are read-only. To modify them, add the `mutable` keyword to the lambda declaration."}, {"question": "Does `volatile std::atomic<int> x;` prevent thread races?", "options": ["No, volatile makes atomic variables unsafe.", "Yes, it combines hardware access guarantees with atomic operations safely.", "No, volatile is ignored in atomics.", "Yes, but only on ARM architectures."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::atomic` handles multithreading synchronization. `volatile` prevents the compiler from optimizing away reads/writes (useful for memory-mapped hardware). `volatile std::atomic` is valid for threads mapped directly to hardware registers."}, {"question": "What is the behavior of unsigned integer overflow in C++?", "options": ["It is Undefined Behavior.", "It is guaranteed to wrap around modulo $2^n$ (well-defined).", "It throws an arithmetic overflow exception.", "It clamps to the maximum type value."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The C++ standard defines unsigned integer arithmetic to perform modulo arithmetic. If an overflow occurs, the value wraps around modulo $2^N$ where $N$ is the number of bits."}, {"question": "What is the behavior of signed integer overflow in C++?", "options": ["It wraps around using two's complement.", "It is Undefined Behavior.", "It throws a std::overflow_error.", "It clamps to the maximum signed integer value."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Unlike unsigned integers, overflow of signed integers is Undefined Behavior in C++. Compilers assume signed overflow never happens and can optimize away loops or checks based on this assumption."}, {"question": "How does `std::scoped_lock` lock multiple mutexes safely?", "options": ["It locks them one by one in the order passed.", "It uses a deadlock avoidance algorithm to lock all of them without deadlocks.", "It converts them to spinlocks.", "It runs them on separate cores."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::scoped_lock` locks all passed mutexes using a deadlock avoidance algorithm (typically sorting them by address or using a back-off lock), ensuring they are acquired without deadlock cycles."}, {"question": "Does `std::span` perform bounds checking by default on `operator[]`?", "options": ["Yes, it throws `std::out_of_range`.", "No, it does not perform bounds checking; accessing out of bounds is Undefined Behavior.", "Yes, it aborts the program.", "It is compile-time checked."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is designed for high performance and zero overhead. Like `std::vector::operator[]`, it does not perform runtime bounds checks. Accessing elements out of bounds is UB."}, {"question": "How do you request cooperative cancellation for a running `std::jthread`?", "options": ["Call `.kill()` on the thread object.", "Call `.request_stop()` on the thread object and check `stop_token` inside the thread loop.", "Call `.join()`.", "You cannot cancel running threads in C++."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::jthread` supports cooperative stopping. You call `.request_stop()` on the jthread object. The thread function receives a `stop_token` and periodically queries `token.stop_requested()` to exit cleanly."}, {"question": "What does `alignof(T)` return?", "options": ["The memory size of T.", "The alignment requirement of type T (in bytes). The address must be a multiple of this value.", "The number of bits in T.", "The memory address of T."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`alignof(T)` returns the alignment requirement of type `T` in bytes. The compiler guarantees that all instances of `T` are allocated at memory addresses that are multiples of `alignof(T)`."}, {"question": "What is the C++17 constant used to align variables to prevent false sharing?", "options": ["`std::cache_line_size`", "`std::hardware_destructive_interference_size`", "`std::hardware_constructive_interference_size`", "`std::align_size`"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::hardware_destructive_interference_size` (defined in `<new>`) specifies the byte boundary to prevent false sharing (usually 64 bytes on modern CPUs)."}, {"question": "What ordering category is returned by `<=>` for floating-point values?", "options": ["std::strong_ordering", "std::weak_ordering", "std::partial_ordering", "std::float_ordering"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point comparisons are partially ordered because values like `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers, returning `std::partial_ordering`."}, {"question": "What C++23 feature simplifies writing coroutine generators?", "options": ["`std::coroutine_handle`", "`std::generator<T>`", "`std::co_yield`", "`std::async_generator`"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++23 introduced `std::generator<T>` in `<generator>` header. It wraps coroutine boilerplate, allowing you to write coroutines that yield elements using `co_yield` in a clean, iterable wrapper."}, {"question": "What is the effect of marking a single-argument constructor as `explicit`?", "options": ["It makes the class abstract.", "It prevents implicit conversions during assignment or function calls (e.g. `MyClass x = 10;`).", "It makes the constructor faster.", "It exposes the constructor to templates."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`explicit` tells the compiler not to perform implicit type conversions using the constructor. Users must construct the object explicitly: `MyClass x(10);` or `static_cast<MyClass>(10)`."}, {"question": "What is Small String Optimization (SSO) in `std::string`?", "options": ["An algorithm that compresses string text.", "An optimization where small strings are stored inline inside the string object buffer, avoiding heap allocation.", "A compile-time template wrapper.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string` contains a small internal buffer (typically 15-22 bytes). For strings shorter than this, the characters are stored directly inside the stack object, skipping costly heap allocations."}, {"question": "What happens if you catch a derived exception by value: `catch (std::exception e)`?", "options": ["It compiles and preserves polymorphism.", "Object slicing occurs: the derived details are stripped, and you only receive a base `std::exception` object copy.", "It is a compilation error.", "It triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Catching by value copies the base portion of the exception object, slicing off the derived class attributes. Always catch exceptions by const reference: `catch (const std::exception& e)`."}, {"question": "What is the difference between `throw e;` and `throw;` inside a catch block?", "options": ["They are identical.", "`throw e;` rethrows a copy of the exception, slicing it to the catch type; `throw;` rethrows the original exception preserving its dynamic type.", "`throw;` is a compile error.", "`throw e;` clears the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`throw e;` throws a copy of `e`, which can slice polymorphic exception types. `throw;` rethrows the active exception object directly, maintaining the original type and call stack context."}, {"question": "What is the status of dynamic exception specifications (e.g. `void foo() throw(int)`) in modern C++?", "options": ["They are standard practice.", "They are deprecated and completely removed in C++17 (replaced by `noexcept`).", "They are compile-time checked in C++20.", "They speed up compilation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Dynamic exception specifications were deprecated in C++11 and completely removed in C++17. They were inefficient and difficult to verify at compile time. Modern C++ uses `noexcept` for no-throw contracts."}, {"question": "Is type punning using a `union` (writing to member A, reading from member B) legal in C++?", "options": ["Yes, it is standard practice.", "No, reading a union member other than the one last written to is Undefined Behavior in C++ (unlike C). Use `memcpy` or `std::bit_cast` instead.", "Yes, but only for integer types.", "Yes, if the union is marked packed."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "In C, union type punning is permitted. In C++, it is technically Undefined Behavior to read a union member that was not the active initialized member. The standard way is using `std::bit_cast` (C++20) or `std::memcpy`."}, {"question": "What is the outcome of modifying a member variable marked `mutable` inside a const member function?", "options": ["It is Undefined Behavior.", "It is perfectly safe and legal; `mutable` explicitly permits modifications in const contexts.", "It triggers a compilation error.", "It is compiler-dependent."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The `mutable` keyword is designed exactly for this. It tells the compiler that the variable does not affect the logical constness of the object (e.g. cache variables, mutexes), allowing safe modification inside const functions."}, {"question": "Is it safe to cast a function pointer to another function pointer type using `reinterpret_cast`?", "options": ["Yes, and calling the casted pointer is always safe.", "Yes, but calling the casted pointer with mismatched signatures is Undefined Behavior.", "No, it is a compilation error.", "It causes a crash on construction."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "You can cast any function pointer to another function pointer type using `reinterpret_cast`. However, attempting to invoke the function through the mismatched signature triggers Undefined Behavior. You must cast it back first."}, {"question": "What is the size of `std::variant<int, float, double>` on most 64-bit platforms?", "options": ["8 bytes.", "16 bytes (8 bytes for double, plus 8 bytes of padding/discriminator).", "20 bytes.", "24 bytes."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::variant` needs to store the largest active type (double = 8 bytes) plus a discriminator index indicating which type is active (typically 1-8 bytes depending on alignment/padding), resulting in 16 bytes."}, {"question": "Can structured bindings be captured by value in a lambda in C++20?", "options": ["No, lambdas still cannot capture them.", "Yes, C++20 resolved the limitation, allowing them to be captured by value or reference.", "Yes, but only if they are captured by reference.", "Yes, but only if they are converted to pointers first."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++17 introduced structured bindings but omitted lambda capture support for them. C++20 explicitly updated lambda capture rules to allow binding variables to be captured directly."}, {"question": "What does `result.value_or(fallback)` do for a `std::expected<T, E> result`?", "options": ["Throws an exception if result contains an error.", "Returns the stored value if successful; otherwise, returns the fallback value.", "Always returns the fallback value.", "It is invalid syntax."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`value_or()` provides a convenient fallback path. If the expected object contains a success value, it returns it. Otherwise, it returns the provided fallback parameter."}, {"question": "What is static polymorphism?", "options": ["Polymorphism resolved at runtime using virtual tables.", "Polymorphism resolved at compile time (e.g. templates, CRTP, function overloading), eliminating runtime virtual call overhead.", "Polymorphism where classes cannot be inherited.", "A deprecated feature in C++20."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Static polymorphism resolves call paths at compile time (via template instantiations, CRTP, or overload sets), saving runtime vtable lookups and permitting compiler optimizations like inlining."}, {"question": "Where does Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) look for functions?", "options": ["Only in the global namespace.", "In the namespaces of the function's argument types, in addition to the standard scope lookup.", "In the directories where the headers reside.", "Only in the `std` namespace."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Koenig Lookup (ADL) tells the compiler to search the namespaces associated with the types of the arguments passed to the function, enabling operators and helper functions to be found without namespace qualifications."}, {"question": "What is the key difference between `std::latch` and `std::barrier` (C++20)?", "options": ["latch is faster.", "`std::latch` is a single-use down-counter; `std::barrier` is reusable and runs a completion phase callback when the counter reaches zero.", "barrier is only for thread pools.", "They are identical."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::latch` counts down once and cannot be reset. `std::barrier` is a reusable synchronization point: when all threads arrive, the barrier runs a callback and resets the count for the next cycle."}, {"question": "What does C++20 `std::atomic_ref<T>` allow you to do?", "options": ["Perform atomic operations on non-atomic objects for the duration of the reference.", "Make references thread-safe.", "Prevent memory allocation for references.", "Cast atomic objects safely."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "`std::atomic_ref` lets you perform atomic operations on a normal, non-atomic variable. While the reference is active, all accesses must be done through it to prevent data races."}, {"question": "What does `std::memory_order_acquire` guarantee for atomic loads?", "options": ["No memory accesses can be reordered after the load.", "No reads or writes in the current thread can be reordered before this load. It synchronizes with a release write in another thread.", "The load is executed on the stack.", "All variables are synced globally."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`acquire` ordering ensures that subsequent memory accesses (reads/writes) cannot be reordered before the load, guaranteeing visibility of writes published by a corresponding atomic `release` store."}, {"question": "What does `std::memory_order_release` guarantee for atomic stores?", "options": ["No writes can be reordered after this store.", "No reads or writes in the current thread can be reordered after this store, publishing all prior modifications to threads acquiring the same atomic.", "The store is lock-free.", "The store is executed at compile time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`release` ordering ensures that preceding memory operations (reads/writes) cannot be reordered after the store, making those writes visible to other threads that load the atomic with `acquire` or stronger."}, {"question": "Why is it Undefined Behavior to call `delete ptr;` on a placement new pointer?", "options": ["It will try to free a stack or pre-allocated buffer address using the global heap allocator, corrupting the heap.", "It will call the destructor twice.", "It doesn't call the destructor.", "It is compile-time checked."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "`delete` tries to call the destructor and then release the memory back to the heap manager. Since placement new constructs on an arbitrary pre-allocated buffer, the address is not a heap block, causing heap corruption."}, {"question": "What is the advantage of C++23 `std::print` over `std::cout`?", "options": ["It uses type-safe format strings, compiles faster, and outputs directly without stream buffer overhead.", "It is a macro.", "It only prints integers.", "It is slower but safer."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "`std::print` integrates `std::format` formatting with direct console output. It avoids the overhead of C++ stream formatting (`std::ostream`), yields smaller binaries, and executes faster."}, {"question": "What is the benefit of C++23 'deducing this' (explicit object parameter)?", "options": ["It deduces template parameters automatically.", "It allows ref-qualifying member functions without duplicating code for const, non-const, lvalue, and rvalue overloads.", "It makes this pointer optional.", "It is a runtime optimization."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Prior to C++23, to support all value categories, you had to write 4 overloads of a function. With 'deducing this', you write a single templated function: `void foo(this Self&& self) { ... }`, letting the compiler deduce cv/ref qualifiers."}, {"question": "How do you customize a user-defined class to support structured bindings?", "options": ["By declaring members public.", "By implementing `get<I>()` and specializing `std::tuple_size` and `std::tuple_element` for the class.", "By inheriting from `std::tuple`.", "You cannot customize structured bindings."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To support structured bindings for classes with private members, you must opt-in to the tuple-like protocol by specializing `std::tuple_size`, `std::tuple_element`, and providing a `get` function."}, {"question": "Are virtual function calls allowed inside `constexpr` functions in C++20?", "options": ["No, virtual calls are resolved at runtime and can never be constexpr.", "Yes, virtual function calls are allowed in constexpr functions if the dynamic type of the object can be determined at compile time.", "Yes, but only in debug mode.", "No, virtual functions are deprecated in constexpr."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++20 allows virtual function calls in constant expressions, provided the compiler can determine the exact type of the object at compile time to resolve the virtual dispatch statically."}, {"question": "If you return `std::move(x)` from `MyClass foo()` where `x` is a local variable of type `MyClass`, what does the compiler do?", "options": ["Elides the return completely (NRVO).", "Invokes the move constructor of MyClass, preventing copy elision.", "Invokes the copy constructor.", "Triggers a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Returning `std::move(x)` casts `x` to an rvalue reference, changing the return expression type from `MyClass` to `MyClass&&`. This does not match the return type `MyClass` directly, blocking NRVO and forcing a move constructor call."}], "quick_reference": [{"day": 1, "topic": "Core Language Fundamentals", "key_points": ["Translation units compile independently; ODR requires one definition per program", "Value categories: lvalue (identity, not movable), prvalue (no identity, movable), xvalue (identity, movable)", "const = runtime immutable, constexpr = compile-time capable, consteval = compile-time required"]}, {"day": 2, "topic": "Pointers & References", "key_points": ["References can't be null, can't be rebound — prefer over pointers when possible", "Strict aliasing: only access through correct type, char*, or memcpy/bit_cast", "nullptr is type-safe (std::nullptr_t), always prefer over NULL/0"]}, {"day": 3, "topic": "Memory Management", "key_points": ["RAII: acquire in ctor, release in dtor — the foundation of C++ resource management", "Never mix new/delete with malloc/free — different allocators", "Order struct members by decreasing alignment to minimize padding"]}, {"day": 4, "topic": "Smart Pointers", "key_points": ["unique_ptr: default choice, zero overhead, exclusive ownership, move-only", "shared_ptr: reference-counted, control block, atomic ops — use only for shared ownership", "make_unique/make_shared: exception-safe, single allocation (for shared)"]}, {"day": 5, "topic": "Object-Oriented Programming", "key_points": ["Virtual dispatch: object→vptr→vtable→function. One vptr per polymorphic object", "Rule of Zero: use RAII members so you don't need custom special members", "CRTP for static polymorphism when you don't need runtime dispatch"]}, {"day": 6, "topic": "Move Semantics", "key_points": ["std::move is just a cast to T&&. It doesn't move. Move ctor/assign does the actual moving", "Mark move operations noexcept — containers won't use move without it", "Never return std::move(x) — it defeats copy elision (NRVO)"]}, {"day": 7, "topic": "Templates & Generic Programming", "key_points": ["SFINAE: substitution failure removes overload, not an error. C++20 concepts are the replacement", "Partial specialization: class templates only. Use overloading for function templates", "Fold expressions (C++17): (args + ...) collapses parameter packs with operators"]}, {"day": 8, "topic": "Exception Handling", "key_points": ["Catch by const reference. Rethrow with throw; not throw e;", "noexcept on move ops is critical for container performance", "Three guarantees: no-throw (destructors/swap), strong (copy-and-swap), basic (RAII)"]}, {"day": 9, "topic": "STL & Standard Library", "key_points": ["vector is the default container — contiguous memory = cache-friendly", "Know iterator invalidation rules for each container", "Prefer lambdas over std::bind, auto over std::function for local callables"]}, {"day": 10, "topic": "Concurrency & Multithreading", "key_points": ["Always join/detach threads (or use jthread). RAII locks (lock_guard/scoped_lock)", "atomic with memory ordering: relaxed (no sync), acquire/release (producer-consumer), seq_cst (total order)", "False sharing: different variables on same cache line → pad with alignas(64)"]}, {"day": 11, "topic": "Cache & Performance", "key_points": ["Cache line = 64 bytes. Sequential access is fast, random access is slow.", "SoA > AoS when processing single fields across many objects", "Profile first (perf, VTune, cachegrind). Never optimize without data."]}, {"day": 12, "topic": "Compilation, Linking & Build", "key_points": ["Preprocess → compile → assemble → link. Each .cpp is an independent TU.", "Static linking: self-contained. Dynamic linking: smaller, shareable, ABI concerns", "Modern CMake: target-based commands. vcpkg/Conan for dependencies."]}, {"day": 13, "topic": "Debugging & Tooling", "key_points": ["ASan (memory), UBSan (UB), TSan (races), MSan (uninit reads) — run in CI", "Signed overflow is UB. Strict aliasing violations are UB. Use sanitizers to catch them.", "Compilers assume UB never happens and optimize accordingly"]}, {"day": 14, "topic": "Design Patterns & Idioms", "key_points": ["PIMPL: ABI stability + compilation firewall via unique_ptr<Impl>", "Meyer's Singleton (static local) is thread-safe since C++11", "Type erasure: value semantics + virtual dispatch hidden behind a facade (std::function)"]}, {"day": 15, "topic": "Low-Level & Systems", "key_points": ["Bit-field layout is implementation-defined — don't use for serialization", "volatile ≠ atomic. volatile prevents compiler optimization. atomic provides thread safety.", "Network byte order is big-endian. Use htonl/ntohl for conversion."]}, {"day": 16, "topic": "Standard Evolution", "key_points": ["C++11: modern C++ began (move, lambdas, smart ptrs). C++17: vocabulary types (optional, variant)", "C++20: concepts, ranges, coroutines, modules, <=>. C++23: expected, deducing this", "Each standard built on the previous — know what features came when"]}], "cheat_sheet": ["RAII: acquire in ctor, release in dtor. This is THE C++ idiom.", "Rule of Zero > Rule of Five > Rule of Three. Use RAII members.", "unique_ptr is the default smart pointer. shared_ptr only for shared ownership.", "std::move is just static_cast<T&&>. It doesn't move anything.", "Always mark move operations noexcept.", "Never return std::move(x) — it defeats NRVO.", "const& for input, T for sink, T&& for forwarding, T* for optional non-owning.", "Catch exceptions by const reference. Rethrow with throw; not throw e;", "vector is the default container. Profile before choosing alternatives.", "Use RAII locks (lock_guard/scoped_lock), never raw lock()/unlock().", "Signed overflow is UB. Unsigned wraps. Use unsigned for bit manipulation.", "Strict aliasing: use memcpy or bit_cast for type punning, never pointer casts.", "nullptr, not NULL or 0. enum class, not enum.", "volatile is NOT for threads. Use std::atomic.", "Concepts (C++20) replace SFINAE for template constraints.", "Profile before optimizing. Measure, don't guess."], "cross_topic_questions": [{"question": "How does RAII relate to exception safety?", "answer": "RAII objects release resources in their destructors, which run during stack unwinding. This provides at least the basic exception safety guarantee automatically — no resource leaks even when exceptions are thrown.", "topics_covered": ["Memory Management", "Exception Handling"]}, {"question": "Why must move constructors be noexcept for std::vector to use them?", "answer": "vector needs the strong exception guarantee during reallocation. If move might throw, a partially-moved state can't be rolled back. noexcept move lets vector safely transfer elements. Without it, vector copies instead.", "topics_covered": ["Move Semantics", "STL", "Exception Handling"]}, {"question": "How does false sharing relate to cache lines and atomic operations?", "answer": "When two threads modify different atomic variables on the same cache line (~64 bytes), each modification invalidates the other core's cache line. This causes expensive coherency traffic even though the data is logically independent. Fix: alignas(64) padding.", "topics_covered": ["Concurrency", "Cache & Performance"]}, {"question": "How does the vtable mechanism interact with multiple inheritance?", "answer": "With multiple inheritance, an object has multiple vptrs — one for each base class with virtual functions. Each vptr points to the corresponding base's vtable. Casting between bases adjusts the this pointer (thunk). Virtual inheritance adds additional indirection.", "topics_covered": ["OOP", "Memory Management"]}, {"question": "How do smart pointers use RAII and move semantics together?", "answer": "Smart pointers are RAII wrappers for heap memory. unique_ptr uses move semantics for ownership transfer (can't copy, only move). shared_ptr uses atomic reference counting in its control block. Both release memory in their destructors.", "topics_covered": ["Smart Pointers", "Move Semantics", "Memory Management"]}, {"question": "How does copy elision interact with move semantics?", "answer": "Copy elision is better than moving — it constructs directly in the destination (zero copies, zero moves). That's why returning std::move(x) is wrong: it forces a move when NRVO would have given zero-cost construction. C++17 mandates elision for prvalue returns.", "topics_covered": ["Move Semantics", "Templates"]}, {"question": "What is the relationship between constexpr and template metaprogramming?", "answer": "constexpr is the modern replacement for much template metaprogramming. Instead of recursive template instantiation for compile-time computation, constexpr functions use normal loops and variables. Simpler, faster compilation, better error messages.", "topics_covered": ["Core Language", "Templates"]}, {"question": "How does the strict aliasing rule affect compiler optimization?", "answer": "Strict aliasing lets the compiler assume pointers of different types don't alias the same memory. This enables keeping values in registers longer, reordering loads/stores, and eliminating redundant reads. Violating it causes bugs that only appear at -O2.", "topics_covered": ["Pointers", "Cache & Performance", "Debugging"]}, {"question": "How does PIMPL relate to dynamic linking and ABI stability?", "answer": "PIMPL hides implementation behind a pointer. Since the pointer size never changes, adding/removing private members doesn't change the class layout, preserving ABI. This is critical for shared libraries (.so/.dll) where recompilation of users isn't possible.", "topics_covered": ["Design Patterns", "Compilation & Build"]}, {"question": "How do concepts (C++20) improve on SFINAE for template constraints?", "answer": "SFINAE works by making substitution failures silently remove overloads — the mechanism is indirect and error messages are terrible. Concepts directly express requirements with named predicates, give clear error messages, and participate in overload resolution (more constrained wins).", "topics_covered": ["Templates", "Standard Evolution"]}, {"question": "What is the relationship between std::variant and the Visitor pattern?", "answer": "std::variant is a type-safe union. std::visit implements the Visitor pattern without virtual dispatch — it uses a function table indexed by the variant's current type. This replaces the classic GoF Visitor's double dispatch with a cleaner, value-semantic approach.", "topics_covered": ["STL", "Design Patterns"]}, {"question": "How do cache effects influence container choice?", "answer": "vector (contiguous) has excellent spatial locality — prefetcher predicts sequential access. list/map nodes are scattered in memory — each access may be a cache miss. Even for 'O(1) insertion', vector often wins due to cache effects. Always profile.", "topics_covered": ["STL", "Cache & Performance"]}, {"question": "How does memory ordering in atomics relate to hardware architecture?", "answer": "x86 has a strong memory model (most operations are already acquire/release). ARM/POWER have weak models requiring explicit barriers. seq_cst inserts full barriers everywhere (expensive on ARM). Using relaxed/acquire-release reduces barrier overhead on weak architectures.", "topics_covered": ["Concurrency", "Low-Level Systems"]}, {"question": "Why is type erasure related to both OOP and templates?", "answer": "Type erasure uses OOP internally (virtual base class + templated derived class for concrete types) but presents a value-semantic, non-OOP interface externally. Templates provide the 'any type' capability; virtuals provide the runtime dispatch.", "topics_covered": ["Design Patterns", "Templates", "OOP"]}, {"question": "How do sanitizers relate to undefined behavior categories?", "answer": "ASan catches memory UB (overflow, use-after-free). UBSan catches language UB (signed overflow, shifts, alignment). TSan catches concurrency UB (data races). MSan catches uninitialized reads. Together they cover most common UB sources.", "topics_covered": ["Debugging", "Core Language"]}, {"question": "How does const-correctness interact with thread safety?", "answer": "In C++11, const member functions are expected to be thread-safe (callable from multiple threads simultaneously). If a const function modifies a mutable member, that member should be atomic or protected by a mutex. The standard library follows this convention.", "topics_covered": ["Core Language", "Concurrency"]}, {"question": "What is the connection between placement new and custom allocators?", "answer": "Custom allocators (arena, pool) allocate raw memory. Placement new constructs objects at specific addresses within that memory. The allocator handles memory lifecycle; placement new handles object lifecycle. Destructors must be called manually.", "topics_covered": ["Memory Management", "STL"]}, {"question": "How do inline variables (C++17) solve the static initialization order fiasco?", "answer": "Inline variables can be defined in headers without ODR violations. Combined with constinit (C++20), they ensure compile-time initialization, eliminating the fiasco entirely. Before C++17, the Meyers Singleton pattern was the workaround.", "topics_covered": ["Core Language", "Compilation & Build"]}, {"question": "How does object slicing relate to value categories and move semantics?", "answer": "Slicing occurs when a derived object is copied/moved into a base object — the derived part is lost. This happens with pass-by-value. Pass by reference (const Base&) or pointer (Base*) prevents slicing. unique_ptr<Base> preserves polymorphism.", "topics_covered": ["OOP", "Move Semantics", "Smart Pointers"]}, {"question": "How does endianness interact with the compilation and linking process?", "answer": "Endianness is a hardware property, not a compilation artifact. But it matters for cross-compilation: code compiled for big-endian ARM reads multi-byte values differently than little-endian x86. Serialized data must use explicit byte order (usually network byte order = big-endian).", "topics_covered": ["Low-Level Systems", "Compilation & Build"]}], "common_interview_mistakes": [{"topic": "Core Language", "mistakes": ["Not knowing the difference between UB, unspecified, and implementation-defined", "Thinking const and constexpr are the same thing", "Not understanding ADL and its impact on operator overloading"]}, {"topic": "Pointers & References", "mistakes": ["Claiming references are 'just syntactic sugar for pointers'", "Not knowing the strict aliasing rule", "Confusing nullptr, NULL, and 0"]}, {"topic": "Memory Management", "mistakes": ["Not knowing what RAII stands for or why it matters", "Thinking stack and heap are C++ concepts (they're implementation details)", "Not understanding alignment requirements"]}, {"topic": "Smart Pointers", "mistakes": ["Using shared_ptr everywhere 'to be safe'", "Not knowing about the control block in shared_ptr", "Creating shared_ptr from raw pointer twice (double delete)"]}, {"topic": "OOP", "mistakes": ["Not knowing vtable/vptr mechanics", "Forgetting virtual destructors in base classes", "Not knowing Rule of Five/Zero"]}, {"topic": "Move Semantics", "mistakes": ["Thinking std::move actually moves something", "Not marking move operations noexcept", "Returning std::move(localVar) (defeats NRVO)"]}, {"topic": "Templates", "mistakes": ["Not being able to explain SFINAE", "Confusing full and partial specialization", "Not knowing concepts as SFINAE replacement"]}, {"topic": "Exceptions", "mistakes": ["Catching by value instead of const reference", "Not knowing the three exception safety guarantees", "Using throw e; instead of throw;"]}, {"topic": "STL", "mistakes": ["Not knowing container complexity guarantees", "Not knowing iterator invalidation rules", "Using std::function when auto would do"]}, {"topic": "Concurrency", "mistakes": ["Not knowing the difference between join and detach", "Using volatile for thread synchronization", "Not knowing what false sharing is"]}, {"topic": "Cache & Performance", "mistakes": ["Not knowing cache line sizes or their impact", "Not understanding AoS vs SoA tradeoffs", "Optimizing without profiling"]}, {"topic": "Low-Level", "mistakes": ["Confusing volatile with atomic", "Not knowing endianness implications for network protocols", "Assuming bit-field layout is portable"]}], "revision_strategy_tips": ["Review each day's 'tips_to_remember' first — they're the distilled knowledge", "For each topic, explain the key concept out loud in 2-3 sentences (Feynman technique)", "Focus on cross-topic connections (the cross_topic_questions above)", "Write code examples from memory for the trickiest concepts (move semantics, SFINAE, atomics)", "Review the 'pitfalls' lists — interviewers love asking about common mistakes", "Time yourself answering the questions — aim for 2-3 minutes per question", "For each topic, know: what it is, why it exists, how it works internally, and when to use it", "Prioritize topics that appear in multiple cross-topic questions — they're the most interconnected"], "subtopics": [{"id": "day_17_sub_0", "day": 17, "index": 0, "title": "C++ 16-Day Quick Reference Guide", "explanation": "### C++ 16-Day Quick Reference Guide\nA condensed day-by-day summary of the foundational concepts, modern techniques, and architectural paradigms covered throughout the 16-day core C++ curriculum.\n\n#### Day 1: Core Language Fundamentals\n- Translation units compile independently; ODR requires one definition per program\n- Value categories: lvalue (identity, not movable), prvalue (no identity, movable), xvalue (identity, movable)\n- const = runtime immutable, constexpr = compile-time capable, consteval = compile-time required\n\n#### Day 2: Pointers & References\n- References can't be null, can't be rebound — prefer over pointers when possible\n- Strict aliasing: only access through correct type, char*, or memcpy/bit_cast\n- nullptr is type-safe (std::nullptr_t), always prefer over NULL/0\n\n#### Day 3: Memory Management\n- RAII: acquire in ctor, release in dtor — the foundation of C++ resource management\n- Never mix new/delete with malloc/free — different allocators\n- Order struct members by decreasing alignment to minimize padding\n\n#### Day 4: Smart Pointers\n- unique_ptr: default choice, zero overhead, exclusive ownership, move-only\n- shared_ptr: reference-counted, control block, atomic ops — use only for shared ownership\n- make_unique/make_shared: exception-safe, single allocation (for shared)\n\n#### Day 5: Object-Oriented Programming\n- Virtual dispatch: object→vptr→vtable→function. One vptr per polymorphic object\n- Rule of Zero: use RAII members so you don't need custom special members\n- CRTP for static polymorphism when you don't need runtime dispatch\n\n#### Day 6: Move Semantics\n- std::move is just a cast to T&&. It doesn't move. Move ctor/assign does the actual moving\n- Mark move operations noexcept — containers won't use move without it\n- Never return std::move(x) — it defeats copy elision (NRVO)\n\n#### Day 7: Templates & Generic Programming\n- SFINAE: substitution failure removes overload, not an error. C++20 concepts are the replacement\n- Partial specialization: class templates only. Use overloading for function templates\n- Fold expressions (C++17): (args + ...) collapses parameter packs with operators\n\n#### Day 8: Exception Handling\n- Catch by const reference. Rethrow with throw; not throw e;\n- noexcept on move ops is critical for container performance\n- Three guarantees: no-throw (destructors/swap), strong (copy-and-swap), basic (RAII)\n\n#### Day 9: STL & Standard Library\n- vector is the default container — contiguous memory = cache-friendly\n- Know iterator invalidation rules for each container\n- Prefer lambdas over std::bind, auto over std::function for local callables\n\n#### Day 10: Concurrency & Multithreading\n- Always join/detach threads (or use jthread). RAII locks (lock_guard/scoped_lock)\n- atomic with memory ordering: relaxed (no sync), acquire/release (producer-consumer), seq_cst (total order)\n- False sharing: different variables on same cache line → pad with alignas(64)\n\n#### Day 11: Cache & Performance\n- Cache line = 64 bytes. Sequential access is fast, random access is slow.\n- SoA > AoS when processing single fields across many objects\n- Profile first (perf, VTune, cachegrind). Never optimize without data.\n\n#### Day 12: Compilation, Linking & Build\n- Preprocess → compile → assemble → link. Each .cpp is an independent TU.\n- Static linking: self-contained. Dynamic linking: smaller, shareable, ABI concerns\n- Modern CMake: target-based commands. vcpkg/Conan for dependencies.\n\n#### Day 13: Debugging & Tooling\n- ASan (memory), UBSan (UB), TSan (races), MSan (uninit reads) — run in CI\n- Signed overflow is UB. Strict aliasing violations are UB. Use sanitizers to catch them.\n- Compilers assume UB never happens and optimize accordingly\n\n#### Day 14: Design Patterns & Idioms\n- PIMPL: ABI stability + compilation firewall via unique_ptr<Impl>\n- Meyer's Singleton (static local) is thread-safe since C++11\n- Type erasure: value semantics + virtual dispatch hidden behind a facade (std::function)\n\n#### Day 15: Low-Level & Systems\n- Bit-field layout is implementation-defined — don't use for serialization\n- volatile ≠ atomic. volatile prevents compiler optimization. atomic provides thread safety.\n- Network byte order is big-endian. Use htonl/ntohl for conversion.\n\n#### Day 16: Standard Evolution\n- C++11: modern C++ began (move, lambdas, smart ptrs). C++17: vocabulary types (optional, variant)\n- C++20: concepts, ranges, coroutines, modules, <=>. C++23: expected, deducing this\n- Each standard built on the previous — know what features came when", "examples": [], "pitfalls": [], "tips_to_remember": ["Translation units compile independently; ODR requires one definition per program", "Value categories: lvalue (identity, not movable), prvalue (no identity, movable), xvalue (identity, movable)", "const = runtime immutable, constexpr = compile-time capable, consteval = compile-time required", "References can't be null, can't be rebound — prefer over pointers when possible", "Strict aliasing: only access through correct type, char*, or memcpy/bit_cast", "nullptr is type-safe (std::nullptr_t), always prefer over NULL/0", "RAII: acquire in ctor, release in dtor — the foundation of C++ resource management", "Never mix new/delete with malloc/free — different allocators", "Order struct members by decreasing alignment to minimize padding", "unique_ptr: default choice, zero overhead, exclusive ownership, move-only", "shared_ptr: reference-counted, control block, atomic ops — use only for shared ownership", "make_unique/make_shared: exception-safe, single allocation (for shared)"], "questions": [], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the value category of the variable `r` when evaluated in the expression `r` if declared as `int&& r = 42;`?", "options": ["lvalue", "prvalue", "xvalue", "rvalue reference"], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Although `r` is declared as an rvalue reference, when used as an expression, any named variable (including rvalue references) is an lvalue. To treat it as an rvalue, you must cast it, e.g., using `std::move(r)`.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What is the resulting type of the conditional expression `true ? 1 : 2.0` in C++?", "options": ["int", "double", "float", "Compilation error"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The conditional operator (?:) requires a common type between the second and third operands. The compiler performs standard arithmetic promotions, converting `int` (1) to `double` (1.0), so the type is `double`.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_1"}, {"question": "In C++11, what is the behavior of the expression `int x = 1; int y = x++ + ++x;`?", "options": ["y is 3", "y is 4", "y is 5", "Undefined Behavior"], "correct_option": 3, "explanation": "In C++11, the order of evaluation of operands in arithmetic operators (like `+`) is unsequenced. Modifying `x` twice without an intervening sequence point triggers Undefined Behavior. In C++17, sequence rules were refined, but double modification in a single expression is still UB if unsequenced.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the exact value returned by `sizeof(char)` in C++ on any compliant platform?", "options": ["Always 1", "Implementation-defined (typically 1)", "Depends on the system word size", "Depends on compilation flags"], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "By definition in the C++ standard, `sizeof(char)`, `sizeof(signed char)`, and `sizeof(unsigned char)` are always exactly 1. A byte in C++ is defined as the size of a char, which can contain more than 8 bits on some DSPs, but `sizeof(char)` is still 1.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What happens when you pass a `const std::string` object to `std::move` and use it to initialize another string?", "options": ["It is moved successfully, leaving the source empty.", "It silently falls back to copy construction because the const qualifier prevents resource theft.", "It causes a compilation error due to const mismatch.", "It triggers undefined behavior at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::move` only casts the argument to an rvalue reference: `const std::string&&`. Since the type is `const`, the move constructor of `std::string` (which requires `std::string&&`) cannot be bound. The compiler falls back to the copy constructor (`const std::string&`).", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Suppose we have `auto [x, y] = my_pair;` where `my_pair` is a `std::pair<int, int>`. What are the types of `x` and `y`?", "options": ["References pointing directly to elements of `my_pair`.", "Independent copies of the elements of `my_pair`.", "Names that refer to members of an anonymous copy of `my_pair`.", "Pointers to the elements of `my_pair`."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Structured bindings initialize an anonymous copy of the initializer (e.g., `auto __decomp = my_pair;`) and bind `x` and `y` as aliases to the members of that anonymous copy. To refer to the original, you must use `auto& [x, y] = my_pair;`.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_5"}, {"question": "Given `int x = 10;`, what is the deduced type of `y` for `decltype(auto) y = (x);`?", "options": ["int", "int&", "int&&", "const int&"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`decltype(auto)` uses `decltype` rules for deduction. A parenthesized variable expression `(x)` is evaluated as an lvalue expression, so `decltype((x))` yields `int&`. Thus, `y` becomes a reference to `x`.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_6"}, {"question": "Why is returning `std::move(local_var)` from a function returning by value considered a bad practice?", "options": ["It causes a memory leak.", "It defeats NRVO (Named Return Value Optimization), forcing a move instead of zero-cost copy elision.", "It triggers compilation warnings on all C++20 compilers.", "It causes local_var to be destroyed before returning."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Returning a local variable by value allows the compiler to construct it directly in the caller's frame (NRVO). Returning `std::move(x)` returns an xvalue (`T&&`), forcing a move constructor call and preventing elision.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_7"}, {"question": "Why is `&v[0]` invalid for `std::vector<bool> v;`?", "options": ["vector elements are allocated on the stack.", "std::vector<bool> is space-optimized as a bitmask, so individual elements don't have distinct byte addresses.", "It is a const vector and address-of is disabled.", "Vector elements are only initialized on demand."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::vector<bool>` is a specialized template that packs booleans as single bits. Since the smallest addressable unit of memory is a byte, individual bits cannot be pointed to by raw pointers. Thus, `operator[]` returns a proxy class instead of `bool&`.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the primary risk of deleting a Derived object through a Base pointer when the Base class has virtual methods but no virtual destructor?", "options": ["The program will fail to compile.", "Only the Base destructor is called, leaking Derived resources and triggering Undefined Behavior.", "The Derived destructor is called, but Base destructor is skipped.", "The compiler automatically generates a virtual destructor, so there is no risk."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Deleting a derived object through a base class pointer without a virtual destructor is Undefined Behavior. In practice, only the Base destructor executes, leaking Derived members and corrupting heap state.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_9"}, {"question": "What happens in the code `Base b = Derived();`?", "options": ["b operates as a polymorphic Derived object.", "Object slicing occurs: the Derived portion is stripped, and only the Base copy constructor is invoked.", "Compilation fails due to size mismatch.", "b holds a reference to the temporary Derived object."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Assigning a derived object to a base object by value causes 'object slicing'. The derived members are sliced off, and only the base subobject is copied into `b`, losing all polymorphic capabilities.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_10"}, {"question": "In what order are member variables initialized in a class constructor?", "options": ["In the order they are listed in the constructor's initializer list.", "In the order they are declared in the class definition.", "Alphabetical order.", "Compiler-dependent random order."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Member variables are always initialized in the order of their declaration in the class definition, regardless of the order in the constructor's initializer list. Listing them out-of-order can lead to bugs if one member depends on another.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_11"}, {"question": "Suppose Base has `virtual void f(int x = 10)` and Derived has `void f(int x = 20) override`. What is printed by `Base* p = new Derived(); p->f();`?", "options": ["Derived implementation is called with x = 20.", "Derived implementation is called with x = 10.", "Base implementation is called with x = 10.", "Compilation error due to default value mismatch."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Virtual function binding happens at runtime, but default parameter resolution happens at compile time based on the static type of the pointer (`Base*`). Thus, Derived's `f()` is executed, but with Base's default value of 10.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_12"}, {"question": "What is the size of an empty class `class Empty {};` in C++?", "options": ["0 bytes", "At least 1 byte", "4 bytes", "8 bytes"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ requires all objects to have a unique address. If the size of an empty class were 0, two consecutive objects in an array would share the same address. Thus, empty classes have a size of at least 1 byte.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_13"}, {"question": "Under what condition does Empty Base Optimization (EBO) fail?", "options": ["When the base class is virtual.", "When the first member of the derived class has the same type as the empty base class.", "When the derived class has virtual functions.", "When the compiler optimization level is set to -O0."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "EBO allows the empty base to occupy 0 bytes. However, if the first member of the derived class is of the same type as the empty base, C++ address uniqueness rules require them to have different addresses, forcing the empty base to occupy space (usually 1+ bytes).", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_14"}, {"question": "Which pointer cast violates the strict aliasing rule?", "options": ["Casting `int*` to `const int*`.", "Casting `int*` to `float*` and dereferencing it.", "Casting `int*` to `char*` and dereferencing it.", "Casting `Derived*` to `Base*`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Strict aliasing states that pointers of different incompatible types cannot point to the same object. Dereferencing a `float*` pointing to `int` memory is UB. Casting to `char*` or `unsigned char*` is an explicit standard exception.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_15"}, {"question": "What is the main benefit of the copy-and-swap idiom for assignment operators?", "options": ["It runs faster than simple assignments.", "It guarantees strong exception safety (all-or-nothing) and self-assignment safety.", "It eliminates the need for constructors.", "It does not copy members."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Copy-and-swap creates a copy of the parameter first (handling exceptions before modifying the target). Then, a non-throwing swap is called. If copying fails, the target is unmodified, providing strong exception safety and self-assignment safety.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_16"}, {"question": "What happens in: `const int& r = 10;`?", "options": ["r binds to a dangling temporary.", "The lifetime of the temporary integer 10 is extended to match the lifetime of reference r.", "It is a compilation error because 10 is an rvalue.", "r holds a pointer to 10."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Binding a temporary (prvalue) to a const lvalue reference (or an rvalue reference) extends the lifetime of that temporary to match the reference's scope, avoiding dangling references.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_17"}, {"question": "What happens if a class's move constructor is not marked `noexcept`?", "options": ["It cannot be moved.", "Standard containers like `std::vector` will fall back to copy operations during reallocation to preserve exception guarantees.", "The program will fail to compile.", "The compiler automatically adds noexcept anyway."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::vector::reserve` or reallocation moves elements only if their move constructor is marked `noexcept`. If it can throw, the vector copies them instead so that if an exception occurs mid-way, it can roll back to the original state.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_18"}, {"question": "Which part of `std::shared_ptr` is thread-safe by default?", "options": ["Both the control block and the pointed-to object.", "Only the atomic reference counting inside the control block; modifying the pointed-to object from multiple threads requires external synchronization.", "Only the pointed-to object.", "Neither, shared_ptr is completely thread-unsafe."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The reference count increments/decrements in the control block are atomic and thread-safe. However, concurrent writes to the pointed-to object itself or to the same `shared_ptr` instance are not synchronized and require mutexes.", "id": "day_17_sub_0_mcq_19"}]}, {"id": "day_17_sub_1", "day": 17, "index": 1, "title": "C++ Core Cheat Sheet & Golden Rules", "explanation": "### C++ Core Cheat Sheet & Golden Rules\nThe ultimate set of golden rules, best practices, and essential idioms every senior C++ engineer must commit to memory.\n\n- RAII: acquire in ctor, release in dtor. This is THE C++ idiom.\n- Rule of Zero > Rule of Five > Rule of Three. Use RAII members.\n- unique_ptr is the default smart pointer. shared_ptr only for shared ownership.\n- std::move is just static_cast<T&&>. It doesn't move anything.\n- Always mark move operations noexcept.\n- Never return std::move(x) — it defeats NRVO.\n- const& for input, T for sink, T&& for forwarding, T* for optional non-owning.\n- Catch exceptions by const reference. Rethrow with throw; not throw e;\n- vector is the default container. Profile before choosing alternatives.\n- Use RAII locks (lock_guard/scoped_lock), never raw lock()/unlock().\n- Signed overflow is UB. Unsigned wraps. Use unsigned for bit manipulation.\n- Strict aliasing: use memcpy or bit_cast for type punning, never pointer casts.\n- nullptr, not NULL or 0. enum class, not enum.\n- volatile is NOT for threads. Use std::atomic.\n- Concepts (C++20) replace SFINAE for template constraints.\n- Profile before optimizing. Measure, don't guess.", "examples": [], "pitfalls": ["Returning std::move(x) on local variables defeats NRVO copy elision", "Using volatile for multi-threading instead of std::atomic", "Violating strict aliasing through raw reinterpret_cast"], "tips_to_remember": ["RAII: acquire in ctor, release in dtor. This is THE C++ idiom.", "Rule of Zero > Rule of Five > Rule of Three. Use RAII members.", "unique_ptr is the default smart pointer. shared_ptr only for shared ownership.", "std::move is just static_cast<T&&>. It doesn't move anything.", "Always mark move operations noexcept.", "Never return std::move(x) — it defeats NRVO.", "const& for input, T for sink, T&& for forwarding, T* for optional non-owning.", "Catch exceptions by const reference. Rethrow with throw; not throw e;", "vector is the default container. Profile before choosing alternatives.", "Use RAII locks (lock_guard/scoped_lock), never raw lock()/unlock().", "Signed overflow is UB. Unsigned wraps. Use unsigned for bit manipulation.", "Strict aliasing: use memcpy or bit_cast for type punning, never pointer casts.", "nullptr, not NULL or 0. enum class, not enum.", "volatile is NOT for threads. Use std::atomic.", "Concepts (C++20) replace SFINAE for template constraints.", "Profile before optimizing. Measure, don't guess."], "questions": [], "mcqs": [{"question": "What does `dynamic_cast<Derived&>(base_ref)` do if base_ref is not actually a Derived object?", "options": ["Returns a null reference.", "Throws a `std::bad_cast` exception.", "Returns a fallback default object.", "Triggers undefined behavior at runtime."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "References cannot be null, so `dynamic_cast` for reference types cannot return null on failure. Instead, it throws a `std::bad_cast` exception to report dynamic type mismatch.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Is it legal for a pure virtual function to have a body in C++?", "options": ["No, pure virtual means `= 0` and cannot have any code.", "Yes, it can have a definition, but it must be defined outside the class declaration. It can be called statically.", "Yes, but only if the class is not abstract.", "Yes, it must be inline inside the class."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "A pure virtual function can have a body: `void Base::f() { ... }`. Derived classes must still override it, but they can invoke the base implementation statically using `Base::f()`.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is `std::weak_ptr` designed without `operator*` and `operator->`?", "options": ["It is a raw pointer wrapper.", "To prevent accessing an object that may have been deleted concurrently; you must call `.lock()` to get a `shared_ptr` first.", "It is a compile-time optimization.", "It only holds type information."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`weak_ptr` does not own the object. Accessing it directly could race with destruction. Calling `.lock()` atomically checks if the object is alive and increments the reference count, securing a safe `shared_ptr` for access.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the key performance difference between `std::any` and `std::variant`?", "options": ["any is faster because it uses type erasure.", "variant stores its alternatives inline (no heap allocation), whereas any dynamically allocates memory if the object size exceeds the small buffer threshold.", "any does not support copy operations.", "variant uses virtual table lookup for all accesses."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::variant` is a template-based type-safe union with inline storage (no heap). `std::any` is an unbounded container that uses type erasure and heap allocation for storing values larger than its internal small buffer.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_3"}, {"question": "Where must a substitution failure occur for SFINAE to apply?", "options": ["Anywhere within the function body.", "Only in the immediate context of the function template declaration (e.g., return type, template parameters, function arguments).", "In any of the header files included.", "Only inside class definitions."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "SFINAE only applies to failures in the 'immediate context' of the template declaration (deducing argument types, parsing function signatures). A template that compiles successfully but fails inside its body is a hard compiler error.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What happens to dependent names (names depending on template parameters) during the first phase of template compilation?", "options": ["They are fully compiled and optimized.", "They are only checked for basic syntax; verification is deferred to the second phase (instantiation).", "They are resolved using global namespace symbols.", "They are ignored entirely."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ templates use two-phase lookup. Non-dependent names are resolved in Phase 1 (at template definition). Dependent names (e.g. `T::type`) are only parsed for syntax in Phase 1, and fully resolved in Phase 2 (instantiation).", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_5"}, {"question": "Since C++11, how is thread-safe initialization of static local variables ('magic statics') achieved?", "options": ["The developer must wrap the initialization in a mutex.", "The compiler automatically inserts synchronization (locks/fences) to guarantee thread-safe initialization.", "It is not thread-safe; magic statics only work in single-threaded builds.", "Threads must be spawned after static variables are initialized."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++11 mandates that local static variables are initialized in a thread-safe manner. The compiler wraps the initialization block with atomic checks and locks so that multiple threads concurrently passing it will block until initialization is complete.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What happens if a `std::thread` object is destroyed while it is still joinable?", "options": ["The thread is joined automatically.", "The thread is detached automatically.", "The program terminates immediately via `std::terminate`.", "The thread is paused until a new object binds to it."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "A joinable thread has an active thread of execution. If the `std::thread` wrapper goes out of scope and is destroyed without calling `.join()` or `.detach()`, the destructor invokes `std::terminate`, crashing the program.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What does the `volatile` keyword guarantee in C++ multithreaded programs?", "options": ["Atomicity of operations.", "Memory visibility fences to prevent CPU reordering.", "Nothing regarding thread synchronization; it only prevents compiler optimizations.", "Locking of cache lines."], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "`volatile` tells the compiler that the value can change outside the program's control (e.g. hardware register). It forces access to memory, but it does NOT generate atomic instructions or CPU memory barriers. Do not use `volatile` for threads.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is a major risk when using placement `new` with a pre-allocated stack byte array?", "options": ["Stack memory is too slow.", "Misalignment: if the byte array address is not a multiple of the class alignment (`alignof`), it triggers Undefined Behavior.", "Stack arrays cannot hold class objects.", "Placement new automatically frees the stack array."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Objects must be placed at memory addresses aligned to their types. A simple `char buffer[N]` has 1-byte alignment. Constructing an `int` or a class on it using placement new without aligning the buffer (e.g., using `alignas`) is UB.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_9"}, {"question": "How do you clean up an object constructed using placement `new`?", "options": ["Call `delete ptr;`.", "Manually call its destructor: `ptr->~T();`.", "It is cleaned up automatically when the buffer is destroyed.", "Call `free(ptr);`."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Since placement `new` did not allocate memory from the global heap, calling `delete` is undefined behavior. You must explicitly invoke the destructor `ptr->~T()` and then release or reuse the memory buffer.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_10"}, {"question": "When does a C-style array `int arr[5]` NOT decay to a pointer `int*`?", "options": ["When passed as an argument to a function.", "When used as the operand of `sizeof` or address-of `&` operator.", "When assigned to another array.", "It always decays to a pointer."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "An array decays to a pointer in most contexts. However, when passed to `sizeof(arr)` (returns the total size in bytes of the array) or `&arr` (returns a pointer to the array `int(*)[5]`), decay does not occur.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_11"}, {"question": "What is the hazard of `#define SQUARE(x) x * x` when called as `SQUARE(1 + 2)`?", "options": ["It returns 9.", "It expands to `1 + 2 * 1 + 2`, returning 5.", "It triggers a compilation error.", "It causes undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Macros perform literal textual replacement. `SQUARE(1 + 2)` is replaced with `1 + 2 * 1 + 2`. Due to operator precedence, `2 * 1` is evaluated first, resulting in `1 + 2 + 2 = 5`. Macros must be parenthesized: `((x) * (x))`.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_12"}, {"question": "Why would a developer write `void foo(double) = delete;` next to `void foo(int);`?", "options": ["To prevent implicit conversion: passing a float/double to `foo` will trigger a compilation error instead of silently converting to `int`.", "To speed up template compilation.", "To declare `foo(double)` as inline.", "It is a syntax error."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "Declaring a function overload as `= delete` removes it from compilation. If a user tries to call `foo` with a double, overload resolution matches the deleted `foo(double)` instead of converting it to `foo(int)`, raising a compiler error.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_13"}, {"question": "Does Koenig Lookup (ADL) apply to a call `std::swap(a, b)`?", "options": ["Yes, it searches the namespace of a and b.", "No, ADL is only performed for unqualified function calls; qualified calls follow standard lookup rules.", "Yes, but only in template functions.", "No, swap is a compiler built-in."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "ADL only occurs when the function name is unqualified (e.g. `swap(a, b)`). If it is qualified (`std::swap(a, b)`), the compiler only looks inside the `std` namespace and ignores associated namespaces of `a` and `b`.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_14"}, {"question": "What is the core difference between `constexpr` and C++20 `consteval`?", "options": ["constexpr is faster.", "constexpr functions can be evaluated at compile time OR runtime; consteval functions (immediate functions) MUST be evaluated at compile time.", "consteval is only for class member functions.", "constexpr is deprecated in favor of consteval."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constexpr` indicates compile-time capability. If called with runtime arguments, a `constexpr` function runs at runtime. `consteval` guarantees compile-time execution; calling it with runtime arguments is a compilation error.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_15"}, {"question": "Does `constinit` guarantee that a variable is immutable (read-only) at runtime?", "options": ["Yes, it is equivalent to constexpr.", "No, it only guarantees that the variable is initialized at compile time (preventing static initialization order issues), but it can be mutable at runtime.", "Yes, but only for global variables.", "No, it is a compilation error to modify it unless it is marked const."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`constinit` enforces compile-time initialization of variables with static/thread storage. It does not enforce runtime immutability. To make it read-only, you must combine it with const: `constinit const int x = 42;`.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_16"}, {"question": "How does CRTP (Curiously Recurring Template Pattern) achieve polymorphism without virtual functions?", "options": ["By using macro expansion.", "The base class templates on the derived class, allowing static casts to call derived member functions at compile time.", "By using run-time RTTI lookup tables.", "CRTP requires virtual tables."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "In CRTP, base inherits from template parameter: `class Base : public Class<Base>`. The base class casts its `this` pointer to the Derived template parameter: `static_cast<Derived*>(this)->foo()`, resolving calls at compile time.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_17"}, {"question": "What is a covariant return type in virtual overrides?", "options": ["A return type that changes from float to double.", "An override that returns a pointer/reference to a class derived from the base return type.", "An invalid return type that triggers compiler warnings.", "A templated return type."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "An overriding virtual function can return a pointer/reference to a class derived from the class returned by the base virtual function. This is covariant return types, allowing more specific type returns without casting.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_18"}, {"question": "What is the purpose of `std::launder` (C++17)?", "options": ["It cleans up unused heap memory.", "It informs the optimizer that an object has been constructed in a memory location formerly occupied by another object, preventing invalid compiler optimizations based on old object properties.", "It casts pointers safely in templates.", "It validates JSON strings."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "If you reuse memory (e.g. via placement new) and construct an object with const/reference members, compiler optimizations might assume the old members are still valid. `std::launder` forces the compiler to re-fetch the object state.", "id": "day_17_sub_1_mcq_19"}]}, {"id": "day_17_sub_2", "day": 17, "index": 2, "title": "Cross-Topic Architectural Deep Dives (20 Synthesis Q&As)", "explanation": "### Cross-Topic Architectural & Interview Questions\nHigh-level interview questions that bridge multiple C++ subsystems — connecting RAII with exception safety, move semantics with vector reallocation, cache lines with atomics, and vtables with multiple inheritance.\n\n#### Q: How does RAII relate to exception safety?\nRAII objects release resources in their destructors, which run during stack unwinding. This provides at least the basic exception safety guarantee automatically — no resource leaks even when exceptions are thrown.\n*Topics Covered: Memory Management, Exception Handling*\n\n#### Q: Why must move constructors be noexcept for std::vector to use them?\nvector needs the strong exception guarantee during reallocation. If move might throw, a partially-moved state can't be rolled back. noexcept move lets vector safely transfer elements. Without it, vector copies instead.\n*Topics Covered: Move Semantics, STL, Exception Handling*\n\n#### Q: How does false sharing relate to cache lines and atomic operations?\nWhen two threads modify different atomic variables on the same cache line (~64 bytes), each modification invalidates the other core's cache line. This causes expensive coherency traffic even though the data is logically independent. Fix: alignas(64) padding.\n*Topics Covered: Concurrency, Cache & Performance*\n\n#### Q: How does the vtable mechanism interact with multiple inheritance?\nWith multiple inheritance, an object has multiple vptrs — one for each base class with virtual functions. Each vptr points to the corresponding base's vtable. Casting between bases adjusts the this pointer (thunk). Virtual inheritance adds additional indirection.\n*Topics Covered: OOP, Memory Management*\n\n#### Q: How do smart pointers use RAII and move semantics together?\nSmart pointers are RAII wrappers for heap memory. unique_ptr uses move semantics for ownership transfer (can't copy, only move). shared_ptr uses atomic reference counting in its control block. Both release memory in their destructors.\n*Topics Covered: Smart Pointers, Move Semantics, Memory Management*\n\n#### Q: How does copy elision interact with move semantics?\nCopy elision is better than moving — it constructs directly in the destination (zero copies, zero moves). That's why returning std::move(x) is wrong: it forces a move when NRVO would have given zero-cost construction. C++17 mandates elision for prvalue returns.\n*Topics Covered: Move Semantics, Templates*\n\n#### Q: What is the relationship between constexpr and template metaprogramming?\nconstexpr is the modern replacement for much template metaprogramming. Instead of recursive template instantiation for compile-time computation, constexpr functions use normal loops and variables. Simpler, faster compilation, better error messages.\n*Topics Covered: Core Language, Templates*\n\n#### Q: How does the strict aliasing rule affect compiler optimization?\nStrict aliasing lets the compiler assume pointers of different types don't alias the same memory. This enables keeping values in registers longer, reordering loads/stores, and eliminating redundant reads. Violating it causes bugs that only appear at -O2.\n*Topics Covered: Pointers, Cache & Performance, Debugging*\n\n#### Q: How does PIMPL relate to dynamic linking and ABI stability?\nPIMPL hides implementation behind a pointer. Since the pointer size never changes, adding/removing private members doesn't change the class layout, preserving ABI. This is critical for shared libraries (.so/.dll) where recompilation of users isn't possible.\n*Topics Covered: Design Patterns, Compilation & Build*\n\n#### Q: How do concepts (C++20) improve on SFINAE for template constraints?\nSFINAE works by making substitution failures silently remove overloads — the mechanism is indirect and error messages are terrible. Concepts directly express requirements with named predicates, give clear error messages, and participate in overload resolution (more constrained wins).\n*Topics Covered: Templates, Standard Evolution*\n\n#### Q: What is the relationship between std::variant and the Visitor pattern?\nstd::variant is a type-safe union. std::visit implements the Visitor pattern without virtual dispatch — it uses a function table indexed by the variant's current type. This replaces the classic GoF Visitor's double dispatch with a cleaner, value-semantic approach.\n*Topics Covered: STL, Design Patterns*\n\n#### Q: How do cache effects influence container choice?\nvector (contiguous) has excellent spatial locality — prefetcher predicts sequential access. list/map nodes are scattered in memory — each access may be a cache miss. Even for 'O(1) insertion', vector often wins due to cache effects. Always profile.\n*Topics Covered: STL, Cache & Performance*\n\n#### Q: How does memory ordering in atomics relate to hardware architecture?\nx86 has a strong memory model (most operations are already acquire/release). ARM/POWER have weak models requiring explicit barriers. seq_cst inserts full barriers everywhere (expensive on ARM). Using relaxed/acquire-release reduces barrier overhead on weak architectures.\n*Topics Covered: Concurrency, Low-Level Systems*\n\n#### Q: Why is type erasure related to both OOP and templates?\nType erasure uses OOP internally (virtual base class + templated derived class for concrete types) but presents a value-semantic, non-OOP interface externally. Templates provide the 'any type' capability; virtuals provide the runtime dispatch.\n*Topics Covered: Design Patterns, Templates, OOP*\n\n#### Q: How do sanitizers relate to undefined behavior categories?\nASan catches memory UB (overflow, use-after-free). UBSan catches language UB (signed overflow, shifts, alignment). TSan catches concurrency UB (data races). MSan catches uninitialized reads. Together they cover most common UB sources.\n*Topics Covered: Debugging, Core Language*\n\n#### Q: How does const-correctness interact with thread safety?\nIn C++11, const member functions are expected to be thread-safe (callable from multiple threads simultaneously). If a const function modifies a mutable member, that member should be atomic or protected by a mutex. The standard library follows this convention.\n*Topics Covered: Core Language, Concurrency*\n\n#### Q: What is the connection between placement new and custom allocators?\nCustom allocators (arena, pool) allocate raw memory. Placement new constructs objects at specific addresses within that memory. The allocator handles memory lifecycle; placement new handles object lifecycle. Destructors must be called manually.\n*Topics Covered: Memory Management, STL*\n\n#### Q: How do inline variables (C++17) solve the static initialization order fiasco?\nInline variables can be defined in headers without ODR violations. Combined with constinit (C++20), they ensure compile-time initialization, eliminating the fiasco entirely. Before C++17, the Meyers Singleton pattern was the workaround.\n*Topics Covered: Core Language, Compilation & Build*\n\n#### Q: How does object slicing relate to value categories and move semantics?\nSlicing occurs when a derived object is copied/moved into a base object — the derived part is lost. This happens with pass-by-value. Pass by reference (const Base&) or pointer (Base*) prevents slicing. unique_ptr<Base> preserves polymorphism.\n*Topics Covered: OOP, Move Semantics, Smart Pointers*\n\n#### Q: How does endianness interact with the compilation and linking process?\nEndianness is a hardware property, not a compilation artifact. But it matters for cross-compilation: code compiled for big-endian ARM reads multi-byte values differently than little-endian x86. Serialized data must use explicit byte order (usually network byte order = big-endian).\n*Topics Covered: Low-Level Systems, Compilation & Build*", "examples": [], "pitfalls": [], "tips_to_remember": ["Always consider how language features interact (e.g., move semantics with NRVO and noexcept exception safety)", "Hardware realities (64-byte cache lines, false sharing, branch prediction) directly impact software architecture"], "questions": [{"question": "How does RAII relate to exception safety?", "answer": "RAII objects release resources in their destructors, which run during stack unwinding. This provides at least the basic exception safety guarantee automatically — no resource leaks even when exceptions are thrown.", "topics_covered": ["Memory Management", "Exception Handling"]}, {"question": "Why must move constructors be noexcept for std::vector to use them?", "answer": "vector needs the strong exception guarantee during reallocation. If move might throw, a partially-moved state can't be rolled back. noexcept move lets vector safely transfer elements. Without it, vector copies instead.", "topics_covered": ["Move Semantics", "STL", "Exception Handling"]}, {"question": "How does false sharing relate to cache lines and atomic operations?", "answer": "When two threads modify different atomic variables on the same cache line (~64 bytes), each modification invalidates the other core's cache line. This causes expensive coherency traffic even though the data is logically independent. Fix: alignas(64) padding.", "topics_covered": ["Concurrency", "Cache & Performance"]}, {"question": "How does the vtable mechanism interact with multiple inheritance?", "answer": "With multiple inheritance, an object has multiple vptrs — one for each base class with virtual functions. Each vptr points to the corresponding base's vtable. Casting between bases adjusts the this pointer (thunk). Virtual inheritance adds additional indirection.", "topics_covered": ["OOP", "Memory Management"]}, {"question": "How do smart pointers use RAII and move semantics together?", "answer": "Smart pointers are RAII wrappers for heap memory. unique_ptr uses move semantics for ownership transfer (can't copy, only move). shared_ptr uses atomic reference counting in its control block. Both release memory in their destructors.", "topics_covered": ["Smart Pointers", "Move Semantics", "Memory Management"]}, {"question": "How does copy elision interact with move semantics?", "answer": "Copy elision is better than moving — it constructs directly in the destination (zero copies, zero moves). That's why returning std::move(x) is wrong: it forces a move when NRVO would have given zero-cost construction. C++17 mandates elision for prvalue returns.", "topics_covered": ["Move Semantics", "Templates"]}, {"question": "What is the relationship between constexpr and template metaprogramming?", "answer": "constexpr is the modern replacement for much template metaprogramming. Instead of recursive template instantiation for compile-time computation, constexpr functions use normal loops and variables. Simpler, faster compilation, better error messages.", "topics_covered": ["Core Language", "Templates"]}, {"question": "How does the strict aliasing rule affect compiler optimization?", "answer": "Strict aliasing lets the compiler assume pointers of different types don't alias the same memory. This enables keeping values in registers longer, reordering loads/stores, and eliminating redundant reads. Violating it causes bugs that only appear at -O2.", "topics_covered": ["Pointers", "Cache & Performance", "Debugging"]}, {"question": "How does PIMPL relate to dynamic linking and ABI stability?", "answer": "PIMPL hides implementation behind a pointer. Since the pointer size never changes, adding/removing private members doesn't change the class layout, preserving ABI. This is critical for shared libraries (.so/.dll) where recompilation of users isn't possible.", "topics_covered": ["Design Patterns", "Compilation & Build"]}, {"question": "How do concepts (C++20) improve on SFINAE for template constraints?", "answer": "SFINAE works by making substitution failures silently remove overloads — the mechanism is indirect and error messages are terrible. Concepts directly express requirements with named predicates, give clear error messages, and participate in overload resolution (more constrained wins).", "topics_covered": ["Templates", "Standard Evolution"]}, {"question": "What is the relationship between std::variant and the Visitor pattern?", "answer": "std::variant is a type-safe union. std::visit implements the Visitor pattern without virtual dispatch — it uses a function table indexed by the variant's current type. This replaces the classic GoF Visitor's double dispatch with a cleaner, value-semantic approach.", "topics_covered": ["STL", "Design Patterns"]}, {"question": "How do cache effects influence container choice?", "answer": "vector (contiguous) has excellent spatial locality — prefetcher predicts sequential access. list/map nodes are scattered in memory — each access may be a cache miss. Even for 'O(1) insertion', vector often wins due to cache effects. Always profile.", "topics_covered": ["STL", "Cache & Performance"]}, {"question": "How does memory ordering in atomics relate to hardware architecture?", "answer": "x86 has a strong memory model (most operations are already acquire/release). ARM/POWER have weak models requiring explicit barriers. seq_cst inserts full barriers everywhere (expensive on ARM). Using relaxed/acquire-release reduces barrier overhead on weak architectures.", "topics_covered": ["Concurrency", "Low-Level Systems"]}, {"question": "Why is type erasure related to both OOP and templates?", "answer": "Type erasure uses OOP internally (virtual base class + templated derived class for concrete types) but presents a value-semantic, non-OOP interface externally. Templates provide the 'any type' capability; virtuals provide the runtime dispatch.", "topics_covered": ["Design Patterns", "Templates", "OOP"]}, {"question": "How do sanitizers relate to undefined behavior categories?", "answer": "ASan catches memory UB (overflow, use-after-free). UBSan catches language UB (signed overflow, shifts, alignment). TSan catches concurrency UB (data races). MSan catches uninitialized reads. Together they cover most common UB sources.", "topics_covered": ["Debugging", "Core Language"]}, {"question": "How does const-correctness interact with thread safety?", "answer": "In C++11, const member functions are expected to be thread-safe (callable from multiple threads simultaneously). If a const function modifies a mutable member, that member should be atomic or protected by a mutex. The standard library follows this convention.", "topics_covered": ["Core Language", "Concurrency"]}, {"question": "What is the connection between placement new and custom allocators?", "answer": "Custom allocators (arena, pool) allocate raw memory. Placement new constructs objects at specific addresses within that memory. The allocator handles memory lifecycle; placement new handles object lifecycle. Destructors must be called manually.", "topics_covered": ["Memory Management", "STL"]}, {"question": "How do inline variables (C++17) solve the static initialization order fiasco?", "answer": "Inline variables can be defined in headers without ODR violations. Combined with constinit (C++20), they ensure compile-time initialization, eliminating the fiasco entirely. Before C++17, the Meyers Singleton pattern was the workaround.", "topics_covered": ["Core Language", "Compilation & Build"]}, {"question": "How does object slicing relate to value categories and move semantics?", "answer": "Slicing occurs when a derived object is copied/moved into a base object — the derived part is lost. This happens with pass-by-value. Pass by reference (const Base&) or pointer (Base*) prevents slicing. unique_ptr<Base> preserves polymorphism.", "topics_covered": ["OOP", "Move Semantics", "Smart Pointers"]}, {"question": "How does endianness interact with the compilation and linking process?", "answer": "Endianness is a hardware property, not a compilation artifact. But it matters for cross-compilation: code compiled for big-endian ARM reads multi-byte values differently than little-endian x86. Serialized data must use explicit byte order (usually network byte order = big-endian).", "topics_covered": ["Low-Level Systems", "Compilation & Build"]}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the main design difference between `std::byte` (C++17) and `unsigned char`?", "options": ["std::byte is larger.", "std::byte is a distinct enum class type that disables arithmetic operations (preventing treating byte streams as numbers) but permits bitwise operations.", "std::byte is faster to read.", "std::byte is stack-allocated only."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::byte` represents raw storage bits. To prevent treating raw bytes as characters or integers, it does not support arithmetic (`+`, `-`, etc.) or implicit conversions. You must perform bitwise operations (`&`, `|`, `<<`, `>>`) or casts.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Why does the C++20 spaceship operator `<=>` return `std::partial_ordering` for floating-point comparisons?", "options": ["Because floats are not precise.", "Because floating-point comparisons can involve `NaN` (Not a Number), which is unordered relative to all values.", "To save compile time.", "It returns strong_ordering instead."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`NaN` is unordered relative to numbers (e.g., `NaN < 1` is false, and `NaN > 1` is false). Because there are values for which ordering cannot be established, floating-point comparisons return `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_1"}, {"question": "How do you initialize a `std::expected<int, std::string>` representing an error state?", "options": ["return \"error string\";", "return std::unexpected(\"error string\");", "return std::optional<std::string>(\"error string\");", "throw std::runtime_error(\"error string\");"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::expected` implicitly constructs the success value from the initializer. To return the error type instead, you must wrap the error in `std::unexpected(...)`, signaling the expected class to bind the error path.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_2"}, {"question": "What is the difference between `v.reserve(100)` and `v.resize(100)` for a vector `v`?", "options": ["reserve changes the size, resize changes capacity.", "reserve allocates memory but does not construct elements (size is unchanged); resize allocates memory and constructs 100 elements (size becomes 100).", "They are identical.", "resize only works for integer vectors."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`reserve()` allocates raw memory for future insertions, leaving `size()` unchanged. `resize()` actually inserts or removes elements, default-constructing new elements if growing, which changes `size()`.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is a major pitfall of using `operator[]` on a `std::map<int, BigClass>` to check if a key exists?", "options": ["It throws a key-not-found exception.", "It silently inserts a key with a default-constructed `BigClass` object if the key was missing, causing performance overhead and unwanted map entries.", "It runs in O(n) search time.", "It is a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::map::operator[]` is designed to insert keys if they are not present. Using it to inspect keys default-constructs the value type in the map. Use `.find()` or `.contains()` (C++20) to check for existence safely.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What is the size of `std::vector` after calling `std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), 42)`?", "options": ["Reduced by the number of elements containing 42.", "Unchanged; elements matching 42 are moved to the end, and you must call `v.erase()` to shrink the vector.", "0, the vector is cleared.", "Compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Algorithms operate on iterators, not containers. `std::remove` cannot resize the vector. It shifts non-matching elements forward and returns the new logical end iterator. You must call `v.erase(new_end, v.end())` to resize.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_5"}, {"question": "Prior to C++20, what was the restriction of capturing structured bindings in lambdas?", "options": ["They could only be captured by reference.", "They could not be captured in lambdas because they were aliases, not true variables.", "They could only be captured by value.", "There were no restrictions."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Prior to C++20, structured bindings were aliases and could not be captured by lambdas. C++20 resolved this, allowing structured bindings to be captured by value or reference like ordinary variables.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What happens if you use `const_cast` to modify a variable that was originally declared as `const`?", "options": ["The variable is successfully modified.", "It triggers Undefined Behavior.", "It causes a compilation error.", "It behaves as a mutable variable."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`const_cast` can strip `const` from pointers/references. However, if the underlying object was originally declared as a `const` object (e.g. `const int x = 10;`), attempting to write to it via a cast pointer is Undefined Behavior.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_7"}, {"question": "Which of the following is Undefined Behavior under the strict aliasing rule?", "options": ["Reading an `int` through a `char*`.", "Reading an `int` through a `float*` using `reinterpret_cast`.", "Writing to an `unsigned char` buffer using a pointer.", "Casting a pointer to `std::uintptr_t` and back."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Casting pointers to incompatible types and dereferencing them violates the strict aliasing rule. `char*` and `unsigned char*` are explicit exceptions in the standard, allowing byte inspection of any object.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_8"}, {"question": "Under what condition does throwing an exception from a destructor call `std::terminate`?", "options": ["Destructors can never throw under any condition.", "If an exception is thrown from a destructor during stack unwinding (when another exception is already active).", "If the destructor is virtual.", "Only on Windows platforms."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++ cannot process two active exceptions at the same time. If a destructor throws an exception while another exception is active (unwinding the stack), the runtime aborts the program immediately by calling `std::terminate`.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_9"}, {"question": "Given `int x = 0;`, what are the types of `decltype(x)` and `decltype((x))`?", "options": ["int, int", "int, int&", "int&, int", "int&, int&"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`decltype(variable)` returns the declared type of the variable (`int`). `decltype(expression)` returns the type of the expression: since `(x)` is an lvalue expression, it yields `int&`.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_10"}, {"question": "What is the actual type of parameter `a` inside the function `void foo(int a[10][20])`?", "options": ["int a[10][20]", "int (*a)[20] (pointer to array of 20 ints)", "int** a", "int (*a)[10][20]"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Only the first dimension of an array parameter decays to a pointer. `int a[10][20]` decays to a pointer to the second dimension, which is `int (*a)[20]`.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_11"}, {"question": "How do you safely allocate a stack buffer for placement new of type `T`?", "options": ["char buf[sizeof(T)];", "alignas(T) char buf[sizeof(T)];", "static char buf[sizeof(T)];", "char* buf = new char[sizeof(T)];"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To avoid misalignment UB, the buffer must be aligned. `alignas(T)` ensures the stack array starts at a memory address compatible with `T`'s alignment requirements.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_12"}, {"question": "What does `dynamic_cast<Derived*>(base_ptr)` return on failure?", "options": ["A default constructed Derived object", "Throws std::bad_cast", "nullptr", "Undefined Behavior"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Unlike reference dynamic casting which throws an exception, dynamic casting pointers returns `nullptr` on failure, allowing you to check success with a simple if condition.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_13"}, {"question": "Which of the following causes a compilation error for a function marked `override`?", "options": ["The base class function is not virtual.", "The base class function has a different const-qualifier or signature.", "There is no matching function in the base class.", "All of the above."], "correct_option": 3, "explanation": "The `override` keyword requires a matching virtual function signature in the base class. If the base function is non-virtual, has different constness, or doesn't exist, compilation fails.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_14"}, {"question": "What is the typical size of a class with virtual functions but no data members on a 64-bit compiler?", "options": ["1 byte", "4 bytes", "8 bytes", "16 bytes"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "A class with virtual functions contains a virtual table pointer (`vptr`). On 64-bit systems, pointers are 8 bytes, so the object size is 8 bytes.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_15"}, {"question": "What is the danger of returning a `std::initializer_list` from a function?", "options": ["It copies elements too slowly.", "The underlying array of the initializer list is a temporary on the stack; returning it results in a dangling reference.", "It is a compilation error.", "It leaks memory on the heap."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::initializer_list` does not own its elements; it points to a temporary stack array constructed by the compiler. Once the function returns, the temporary array is destroyed, leaving the list dangling.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_16"}, {"question": "What is the result of `int* p = new int(10); std::shared_ptr<int> s1(p); std::shared_ptr<int> s2(p);`?", "options": ["s1 and s2 share ownership correctly.", "Double-delete crash at scope exit because s1 and s2 create independent control blocks.", "Compilation error.", "A memory leak."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Initializing two independent smart pointers from the same raw pointer makes them unaware of each other. They will create separate control blocks and both delete `p` at scope exit, causing double-free UB.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_17"}, {"question": "What is a potential downside of `std::make_shared` compared to `std::shared_ptr<T>(new T)`?", "options": ["It is slower.", "If `weak_ptr` instances outlive all `shared_ptr` instances, the object memory cannot be freed until all weak pointers are destroyed (since object and control block share one allocation).", "It does not support custom allocators.", "It causes memory fragmentation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Because `std::make_shared` allocates the control block and object in one chunk, the memory block stays allocated as long as the control block exists. Even if reference count is 0, the chunk remains until weak count is 0.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_18"}, {"question": "Why is virtual inheritance used in diamond inheritance structures?", "options": ["To speed up virtual function calls.", "To ensure only one instance of the common grandparent base class is constructed and shared by the sibling derived classes.", "To prevent the derived classes from being instantiated.", "To compile files faster."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Virtual inheritance resolves the diamond problem: it instructs the compiler to share a single instance of the root base class among all inheriting paths, avoiding duplication and member lookup ambiguity.", "id": "day_17_sub_2_mcq_19"}]}, {"id": "day_17_sub_3", "day": 17, "index": 3, "title": "Common Interview Pitfalls & Mistakes by Subsystem", "explanation": "### Common C++ Interview Mistakes by Topic\nSenior interviewers frequently test candidates on subtle misconceptions and common blunders across core language, memory management, smart pointers, and concurrency.\n\n#### Core Language\n- Not knowing the difference between UB, unspecified, and implementation-defined\n- Thinking const and constexpr are the same thing\n- Not understanding ADL and its impact on operator overloading\n\n#### Pointers & References\n- Claiming references are 'just syntactic sugar for pointers'\n- Not knowing the strict aliasing rule\n- Confusing nullptr, NULL, and 0\n\n#### Memory Management\n- Not knowing what RAII stands for or why it matters\n- Thinking stack and heap are C++ concepts (they're implementation details)\n- Not understanding alignment requirements\n\n#### Smart Pointers\n- Using shared_ptr everywhere 'to be safe'\n- Not knowing about the control block in shared_ptr\n- Creating shared_ptr from raw pointer twice (double delete)\n\n#### OOP\n- Not knowing vtable/vptr mechanics\n- Forgetting virtual destructors in base classes\n- Not knowing Rule of Five/Zero\n\n#### Move Semantics\n- Thinking std::move actually moves something\n- Not marking move operations noexcept\n- Returning std::move(localVar) (defeats NRVO)\n\n#### Templates\n- Not being able to explain SFINAE\n- Confusing full and partial specialization\n- Not knowing concepts as SFINAE replacement\n\n#### Exceptions\n- Catching by value instead of const reference\n- Not knowing the three exception safety guarantees\n- Using throw e; instead of throw;\n\n#### STL\n- Not knowing container complexity guarantees\n- Not knowing iterator invalidation rules\n- Using std::function when auto would do\n\n#### Concurrency\n- Not knowing the difference between join and detach\n- Using volatile for thread synchronization\n- Not knowing what false sharing is\n\n#### Cache & Performance\n- Not knowing cache line sizes or their impact\n- Not understanding AoS vs SoA tradeoffs\n- Optimizing without profiling\n\n#### Low-Level\n- Confusing volatile with atomic\n- Not knowing endianness implications for network protocols\n- Assuming bit-field layout is portable", "examples": [], "pitfalls": ["Not knowing the difference between UB, unspecified, and implementation-defined", "Thinking const and constexpr are the same thing", "Not understanding ADL and its impact on operator overloading", "Claiming references are 'just syntactic sugar for pointers'", "Not knowing the strict aliasing rule", "Confusing nullptr, NULL, and 0", "Not knowing what RAII stands for or why it matters", "Thinking stack and heap are C++ concepts (they're implementation details)", "Not understanding alignment requirements", "Using shared_ptr everywhere 'to be safe'", "Not knowing about the control block in shared_ptr", "Creating shared_ptr from raw pointer twice (double delete)", "Not knowing vtable/vptr mechanics", "Forgetting virtual destructors in base classes", "Not knowing Rule of Five/Zero", "Thinking std::move actually moves something", "Not marking move operations noexcept", "Returning std::move(localVar) (defeats NRVO)", "Not being able to explain SFINAE", "Confusing full and partial specialization", "Not knowing concepts as SFINAE replacement", "Catching by value instead of const reference", "Not knowing the three exception safety guarantees", "Using throw e; instead of throw;", "Not knowing container complexity guarantees", "Not knowing iterator invalidation rules", "Using std::function when auto would do", "Not knowing the difference between join and detach", "Using volatile for thread synchronization", "Not knowing what false sharing is", "Not knowing cache line sizes or their impact", "Not understanding AoS vs SoA tradeoffs", "Optimizing without profiling", "Confusing volatile with atomic", "Not knowing endianness implications for network protocols", "Assuming bit-field layout is portable"], "tips_to_remember": ["Be precise with C++ terminology: undefined behavior vs unspecified vs implementation-defined", "Know the Rule of Zero/Five and never forget virtual destructors in polymorphic base classes"], "questions": [], "mcqs": [{"question": "If you explicitly declare a destructor for a class, what happens to the implicitly generated move constructor?", "options": ["It is generated normally.", "It is suppressed (not declared), forcing fallback to copying.", "It is declared as deleted.", "It is generated as inline."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Under the Rule of 5 guidelines, declaring a custom destructor, copy constructor, or copy assignment operator tells the compiler that resource management is custom, suppressing implicit move constructor generation.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_0"}, {"question": "What happens if you return `std::move(local_const)` where `local_const` is a `const MyClass`?", "options": ["It is moved successfully.", "It falls back to copy construction because const objects cannot be moved.", "It causes a compilation error.", "It triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::move` casts to `const MyClass&&`. Since move constructors require `MyClass&&` (non-const), the compiler cannot bind it and falls back to the copy constructor (`const MyClass&`).", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_1"}, {"question": "Why is passing `str_view.data()` to a C-style function (like `strlen`) dangerous?", "options": ["str_view is slower.", "std::string_view is not guaranteed to be null-terminated; it can refer to a substring of a larger buffer.", "It causes a compile error.", "It copies the string view data."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string_view` only contains a pointer and a length. It can represent a slice of a string (e.g. `\"hello\"` without the null byte). Passing its `.data()` pointer to functions that expect a null terminator triggers buffer reads past bounds.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Can a `constexpr` function contain a loop in C++14?", "options": ["No, constexpr functions in C++14 are restricted to a single return statement.", "Yes, C++14 relaxed constexpr constraints to allow loops, local variables, and multiple return statements.", "No, loops are only allowed since C++20.", "Yes, but only if they are recursive."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++11 restricted constexpr functions to a single return statement. C++14 relaxed these rules significantly, permitting local variables, loops (`for`, `while`), and branching logic inside constexpr bodies.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_3"}, {"question": "How can you use SFINAE inside template parameters?", "options": ["Using `std::enable_if` in a default template parameter (e.g., `typename = std::enable_if_t<Cond>`).", "Using `#ifdef` inside the template block.", "By declaring the template parameter as static.", "You cannot use SFINAE inside template parameters."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "A common SFINAE technique is using `std::enable_if_t<Cond, int> = 0` as a default template parameter. If `Cond` is false, substitution fails, removing the overload from candidate sets.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_4"}, {"question": "What happens if you try to modify a variable captured by value inside a lambda?", "options": ["It compiles and modifies the original variable.", "It is a compilation error because captures are const by default; you must mark the lambda as `mutable`.", "It compiles and modifies a local copy without affecting the original.", "It triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "By default, the call operator of the generated closure class is `const`, so value captures are read-only. To modify them, add the `mutable` keyword to the lambda declaration.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_5"}, {"question": "Does `volatile std::atomic<int> x;` prevent thread races?", "options": ["No, volatile makes atomic variables unsafe.", "Yes, it combines hardware access guarantees with atomic operations safely.", "No, volatile is ignored in atomics.", "Yes, but only on ARM architectures."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::atomic` handles multithreading synchronization. `volatile` prevents the compiler from optimizing away reads/writes (useful for memory-mapped hardware). `volatile std::atomic` is valid for threads mapped directly to hardware registers.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is the behavior of unsigned integer overflow in C++?", "options": ["It is Undefined Behavior.", "It is guaranteed to wrap around modulo $2^n$ (well-defined).", "It throws an arithmetic overflow exception.", "It clamps to the maximum type value."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The C++ standard defines unsigned integer arithmetic to perform modulo arithmetic. If an overflow occurs, the value wraps around modulo $2^N$ where $N$ is the number of bits.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_7"}, {"question": "What is the behavior of signed integer overflow in C++?", "options": ["It wraps around using two's complement.", "It is Undefined Behavior.", "It throws a std::overflow_error.", "It clamps to the maximum signed integer value."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Unlike unsigned integers, overflow of signed integers is Undefined Behavior in C++. Compilers assume signed overflow never happens and can optimize away loops or checks based on this assumption.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_8"}, {"question": "How does `std::scoped_lock` lock multiple mutexes safely?", "options": ["It locks them one by one in the order passed.", "It uses a deadlock avoidance algorithm to lock all of them without deadlocks.", "It converts them to spinlocks.", "It runs them on separate cores."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::scoped_lock` locks all passed mutexes using a deadlock avoidance algorithm (typically sorting them by address or using a back-off lock), ensuring they are acquired without deadlock cycles.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_9"}, {"question": "Does `std::span` perform bounds checking by default on `operator[]`?", "options": ["Yes, it throws `std::out_of_range`.", "No, it does not perform bounds checking; accessing out of bounds is Undefined Behavior.", "Yes, it aborts the program.", "It is compile-time checked."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::span` is designed for high performance and zero overhead. Like `std::vector::operator[]`, it does not perform runtime bounds checks. Accessing elements out of bounds is UB.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_10"}, {"question": "How do you request cooperative cancellation for a running `std::jthread`?", "options": ["Call `.kill()` on the thread object.", "Call `.request_stop()` on the thread object and check `stop_token` inside the thread loop.", "Call `.join()`.", "You cannot cancel running threads in C++."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::jthread` supports cooperative stopping. You call `.request_stop()` on the jthread object. The thread function receives a `stop_token` and periodically queries `token.stop_requested()` to exit cleanly.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_11"}, {"question": "What does `alignof(T)` return?", "options": ["The memory size of T.", "The alignment requirement of type T (in bytes). The address must be a multiple of this value.", "The number of bits in T.", "The memory address of T."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`alignof(T)` returns the alignment requirement of type `T` in bytes. The compiler guarantees that all instances of `T` are allocated at memory addresses that are multiples of `alignof(T)`.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_12"}, {"question": "What is the C++17 constant used to align variables to prevent false sharing?", "options": ["`std::cache_line_size`", "`std::hardware_destructive_interference_size`", "`std::hardware_constructive_interference_size`", "`std::align_size`"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::hardware_destructive_interference_size` (defined in `<new>`) specifies the byte boundary to prevent false sharing (usually 64 bytes on modern CPUs).", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_13"}, {"question": "What ordering category is returned by `<=>` for floating-point values?", "options": ["std::strong_ordering", "std::weak_ordering", "std::partial_ordering", "std::float_ordering"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Floating-point comparisons are partially ordered because values like `NaN` cannot be compared to other numbers, returning `std::partial_ordering`.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_14"}, {"question": "What C++23 feature simplifies writing coroutine generators?", "options": ["`std::coroutine_handle`", "`std::generator<T>`", "`std::co_yield`", "`std::async_generator`"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++23 introduced `std::generator<T>` in `<generator>` header. It wraps coroutine boilerplate, allowing you to write coroutines that yield elements using `co_yield` in a clean, iterable wrapper.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_15"}, {"question": "What is the effect of marking a single-argument constructor as `explicit`?", "options": ["It makes the class abstract.", "It prevents implicit conversions during assignment or function calls (e.g. `MyClass x = 10;`).", "It makes the constructor faster.", "It exposes the constructor to templates."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`explicit` tells the compiler not to perform implicit type conversions using the constructor. Users must construct the object explicitly: `MyClass x(10);` or `static_cast<MyClass>(10)`.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_16"}, {"question": "What is Small String Optimization (SSO) in `std::string`?", "options": ["An algorithm that compresses string text.", "An optimization where small strings are stored inline inside the string object buffer, avoiding heap allocation.", "A compile-time template wrapper.", "An optimizer flag."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::string` contains a small internal buffer (typically 15-22 bytes). For strings shorter than this, the characters are stored directly inside the stack object, skipping costly heap allocations.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_17"}, {"question": "What happens if you catch a derived exception by value: `catch (std::exception e)`?", "options": ["It compiles and preserves polymorphism.", "Object slicing occurs: the derived details are stripped, and you only receive a base `std::exception` object copy.", "It is a compilation error.", "It triggers undefined behavior."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Catching by value copies the base portion of the exception object, slicing off the derived class attributes. Always catch exceptions by const reference: `catch (const std::exception& e)`.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_18"}, {"question": "What is the difference between `throw e;` and `throw;` inside a catch block?", "options": ["They are identical.", "`throw e;` rethrows a copy of the exception, slicing it to the catch type; `throw;` rethrows the original exception preserving its dynamic type.", "`throw;` is a compile error.", "`throw e;` clears the stack."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`throw e;` throws a copy of `e`, which can slice polymorphic exception types. `throw;` rethrows the active exception object directly, maintaining the original type and call stack context.", "id": "day_17_sub_3_mcq_19"}]}, {"id": "day_17_sub_4", "day": 17, "index": 4, "title": "Mastery Revision Strategy & Final Exam", "explanation": "### Mastery Revision Strategy & Comprehensive Review\nProven preparation strategies and techniques to maximize retention, ace senior technical interviews, and apply modern C++ principles with confidence.\n\n- Review each day's 'tips_to_remember' first — they're the distilled knowledge\n- For each topic, explain the key concept out loud in 2-3 sentences (Feynman technique)\n- Focus on cross-topic connections (the cross_topic_questions above)\n- Write code examples from memory for the trickiest concepts (move semantics, SFINAE, atomics)\n- Review the 'pitfalls' lists — interviewers love asking about common mistakes\n- Time yourself answering the questions — aim for 2-3 minutes per question\n- For each topic, know: what it is, why it exists, how it works internally, and when to use it\n- Prioritize topics that appear in multiple cross-topic questions — they're the most interconnected", "examples": [], "pitfalls": [], "tips_to_remember": ["Review each day's 'tips_to_remember' first — they're the distilled knowledge", "For each topic, explain the key concept out loud in 2-3 sentences (Feynman technique)", "Focus on cross-topic connections (the cross_topic_questions above)", "Write code examples from memory for the trickiest concepts (move semantics, SFINAE, atomics)", "Review the 'pitfalls' lists — interviewers love asking about common mistakes", "Time yourself answering the questions — aim for 2-3 minutes per question", "For each topic, know: what it is, why it exists, how it works internally, and when to use it", "Prioritize topics that appear in multiple cross-topic questions — they're the most interconnected"], "questions": [], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the status of dynamic exception specifications (e.g. `void foo() throw(int)`) in modern C++?", "options": ["They are standard practice.", "They are deprecated and completely removed in C++17 (replaced by `noexcept`).", "They are compile-time checked in C++20.", "They speed up compilation."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Dynamic exception specifications were deprecated in C++11 and completely removed in C++17. They were inefficient and difficult to verify at compile time. Modern C++ uses `noexcept` for no-throw contracts.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_0"}, {"question": "Is type punning using a `union` (writing to member A, reading from member B) legal in C++?", "options": ["Yes, it is standard practice.", "No, reading a union member other than the one last written to is Undefined Behavior in C++ (unlike C). Use `memcpy` or `std::bit_cast` instead.", "Yes, but only for integer types.", "Yes, if the union is marked packed."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "In C, union type punning is permitted. In C++, it is technically Undefined Behavior to read a union member that was not the active initialized member. The standard way is using `std::bit_cast` (C++20) or `std::memcpy`.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_1"}, {"question": "What is the outcome of modifying a member variable marked `mutable` inside a const member function?", "options": ["It is Undefined Behavior.", "It is perfectly safe and legal; `mutable` explicitly permits modifications in const contexts.", "It triggers a compilation error.", "It is compiler-dependent."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "The `mutable` keyword is designed exactly for this. It tells the compiler that the variable does not affect the logical constness of the object (e.g. cache variables, mutexes), allowing safe modification inside const functions.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_2"}, {"question": "Is it safe to cast a function pointer to another function pointer type using `reinterpret_cast`?", "options": ["Yes, and calling the casted pointer is always safe.", "Yes, but calling the casted pointer with mismatched signatures is Undefined Behavior.", "No, it is a compilation error.", "It causes a crash on construction."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "You can cast any function pointer to another function pointer type using `reinterpret_cast`. However, attempting to invoke the function through the mismatched signature triggers Undefined Behavior. You must cast it back first.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_3"}, {"question": "What is the size of `std::variant<int, float, double>` on most 64-bit platforms?", "options": ["8 bytes.", "16 bytes (8 bytes for double, plus 8 bytes of padding/discriminator).", "20 bytes.", "24 bytes."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::variant` needs to store the largest active type (double = 8 bytes) plus a discriminator index indicating which type is active (typically 1-8 bytes depending on alignment/padding), resulting in 16 bytes.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_4"}, {"question": "Can structured bindings be captured by value in a lambda in C++20?", "options": ["No, lambdas still cannot capture them.", "Yes, C++20 resolved the limitation, allowing them to be captured by value or reference.", "Yes, but only if they are captured by reference.", "Yes, but only if they are converted to pointers first."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++17 introduced structured bindings but omitted lambda capture support for them. C++20 explicitly updated lambda capture rules to allow binding variables to be captured directly.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_5"}, {"question": "What does `result.value_or(fallback)` do for a `std::expected<T, E> result`?", "options": ["Throws an exception if result contains an error.", "Returns the stored value if successful; otherwise, returns the fallback value.", "Always returns the fallback value.", "It is invalid syntax."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`value_or()` provides a convenient fallback path. If the expected object contains a success value, it returns it. Otherwise, it returns the provided fallback parameter.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_6"}, {"question": "What is static polymorphism?", "options": ["Polymorphism resolved at runtime using virtual tables.", "Polymorphism resolved at compile time (e.g. templates, CRTP, function overloading), eliminating runtime virtual call overhead.", "Polymorphism where classes cannot be inherited.", "A deprecated feature in C++20."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Static polymorphism resolves call paths at compile time (via template instantiations, CRTP, or overload sets), saving runtime vtable lookups and permitting compiler optimizations like inlining.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_7"}, {"question": "Where does Argument-Dependent Lookup (ADL) look for functions?", "options": ["Only in the global namespace.", "In the namespaces of the function's argument types, in addition to the standard scope lookup.", "In the directories where the headers reside.", "Only in the `std` namespace."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Koenig Lookup (ADL) tells the compiler to search the namespaces associated with the types of the arguments passed to the function, enabling operators and helper functions to be found without namespace qualifications.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_8"}, {"question": "What is the key difference between `std::latch` and `std::barrier` (C++20)?", "options": ["latch is faster.", "`std::latch` is a single-use down-counter; `std::barrier` is reusable and runs a completion phase callback when the counter reaches zero.", "barrier is only for thread pools.", "They are identical."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`std::latch` counts down once and cannot be reset. `std::barrier` is a reusable synchronization point: when all threads arrive, the barrier runs a callback and resets the count for the next cycle.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_9"}, {"question": "What does C++20 `std::atomic_ref<T>` allow you to do?", "options": ["Perform atomic operations on non-atomic objects for the duration of the reference.", "Make references thread-safe.", "Prevent memory allocation for references.", "Cast atomic objects safely."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "`std::atomic_ref` lets you perform atomic operations on a normal, non-atomic variable. While the reference is active, all accesses must be done through it to prevent data races.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_10"}, {"question": "What does `std::memory_order_acquire` guarantee for atomic loads?", "options": ["No memory accesses can be reordered after the load.", "No reads or writes in the current thread can be reordered before this load. It synchronizes with a release write in another thread.", "The load is executed on the stack.", "All variables are synced globally."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`acquire` ordering ensures that subsequent memory accesses (reads/writes) cannot be reordered before the load, guaranteeing visibility of writes published by a corresponding atomic `release` store.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_11"}, {"question": "What does `std::memory_order_release` guarantee for atomic stores?", "options": ["No writes can be reordered after this store.", "No reads or writes in the current thread can be reordered after this store, publishing all prior modifications to threads acquiring the same atomic.", "The store is lock-free.", "The store is executed at compile time."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "`release` ordering ensures that preceding memory operations (reads/writes) cannot be reordered after the store, making those writes visible to other threads that load the atomic with `acquire` or stronger.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_12"}, {"question": "Why is it Undefined Behavior to call `delete ptr;` on a placement new pointer?", "options": ["It will try to free a stack or pre-allocated buffer address using the global heap allocator, corrupting the heap.", "It will call the destructor twice.", "It doesn't call the destructor.", "It is compile-time checked."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "`delete` tries to call the destructor and then release the memory back to the heap manager. Since placement new constructs on an arbitrary pre-allocated buffer, the address is not a heap block, causing heap corruption.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_13"}, {"question": "What is the advantage of C++23 `std::print` over `std::cout`?", "options": ["It uses type-safe format strings, compiles faster, and outputs directly without stream buffer overhead.", "It is a macro.", "It only prints integers.", "It is slower but safer."], "correct_option": 0, "explanation": "`std::print` integrates `std::format` formatting with direct console output. It avoids the overhead of C++ stream formatting (`std::ostream`), yields smaller binaries, and executes faster.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_14"}, {"question": "What is the benefit of C++23 'deducing this' (explicit object parameter)?", "options": ["It deduces template parameters automatically.", "It allows ref-qualifying member functions without duplicating code for const, non-const, lvalue, and rvalue overloads.", "It makes this pointer optional.", "It is a runtime optimization."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Prior to C++23, to support all value categories, you had to write 4 overloads of a function. With 'deducing this', you write a single templated function: `void foo(this Self&& self) { ... }`, letting the compiler deduce cv/ref qualifiers.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_15"}, {"question": "How do you customize a user-defined class to support structured bindings?", "options": ["By declaring members public.", "By implementing `get<I>()` and specializing `std::tuple_size` and `std::tuple_element` for the class.", "By inheriting from `std::tuple`.", "You cannot customize structured bindings."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "To support structured bindings for classes with private members, you must opt-in to the tuple-like protocol by specializing `std::tuple_size`, `std::tuple_element`, and providing a `get` function.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_16"}, {"question": "Are virtual function calls allowed inside `constexpr` functions in C++20?", "options": ["No, virtual calls are resolved at runtime and can never be constexpr.", "Yes, virtual function calls are allowed in constexpr functions if the dynamic type of the object can be determined at compile time.", "Yes, but only in debug mode.", "No, virtual functions are deprecated in constexpr."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "C++20 allows virtual function calls in constant expressions, provided the compiler can determine the exact type of the object at compile time to resolve the virtual dispatch statically.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_17"}, {"question": "If you return `std::move(x)` from `MyClass foo()` where `x` is a local variable of type `MyClass`, what does the compiler do?", "options": ["Elides the return completely (NRVO).", "Invokes the move constructor of MyClass, preventing copy elision.", "Invokes the copy constructor.", "Triggers a compilation error."], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Returning `std::move(x)` casts `x` to an rvalue reference, changing the return expression type from `MyClass` to `MyClass&&`. This does not match the return type `MyClass` directly, blocking NRVO and forcing a move constructor call.", "id": "day_17_sub_4_mcq_18"}]}]}, {"day": 18, "topic": "FAANG DSA — High-Frequency Problem Patterns in C++", "estimated_hours": 6, "subtopics": [{"title": "Sliding Window & Two Pointers (Dynamic Window, Substrings, 3Sum)", "explanation": "The Sliding Window pattern is used for problems on arrays or strings where we seek a subsegment (contiguous) satisfying specific constraints. Instead of an O(N^2) brute force, we maintain two pointers `left` and `right`. The right pointer expands the window to include new elements, while the left pointer shrinks the window when constraints are violated.\n\n### Core Variants:\n1. **Fixed Size Window**: Window length is constant `K`. As `right` advances, `left = right - K + 1` drops the outgoing element.\n2. **Dynamic / Flexible Window**: Expand `right` until valid or invalid, then contract `left` while condition holds (e.g., Minimum Window Substring, Longest Substring with At Most K Distinct Characters).\n3. **Two Pointers (Bidirectional)**: Used for sorted arrays (e.g., 3Sum, Container With Most Water). Pointers move toward each other to eliminate suboptimal search spaces in O(N).\n\n### Complexity:\n- Time: O(N) because both `left` and `right` traverse the array at most once.\n- Space: O(1) or O(K) where K is alphabet size (e.g., `std::unordered_map` or `int freq[128]` for ASCII characters).", "examples": [{"title": "Minimum Window Substring in C++20", "code": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nstd::string minWindow(std::string s, std::string t) {\n    if (s.empty() || t.empty()) return \"\";\n    std::vector<int> target(128, 0);\n    for (char c : t) target[c]++;\n\n    int required = 0;\n    for (int count : target) if (count > 0) required++;\n\n    std::vector<int> window(128, 0);\n    int formed = 0, left = 0, minLen = INT_MAX, startIdx = 0;\n\n    for (int right = 0; right < s.size(); ++right) {\n        char c = s[right];\n        window[c]++;\n        if (target[c] > 0 && window[c] == target[c]) formed++;\n\n        while (left <= right && formed == required) {\n            if (right - left + 1 < minLen) {\n                minLen = right - left + 1;\n                startIdx = left;\n            }\n            char leftChar = s[left++];\n            window[leftChar]--;\n            if (target[leftChar] > 0 && window[leftChar] < target[leftChar]) {\n                formed--;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return minLen == INT_MAX ? \"\" : s.substr(startIdx, minLen);\n}", "explanation": "Uses a fixed 128-element vector for ASCII direct indexing to avoid hash table overhead. Left contracts dynamically once all required characters are formed.", "formatted_code": "#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n\nstd::string minWindow(std::string s, std::string t)\n{\n    if (s.empty() || t.empty()) return \"\";\n    std::vector<int> target(128, 0);\n    for (char c : t) target[c]++;\n\n    int required = 0;\n    for (int count : target) if (count > 0) required++;\n\n    std::vector<int> window(128, 0);\n    int formed = 0, left = 0, minLen = INT_MAX, startIdx = 0;\n\n    for (int right = 0; right < s.size(); ++right)\n    {\n        char c = s[right];\n        window[c]++;\n        if (target[c] > 0 && window[c] == target[c]) formed++;\n\n        while (left <= right && formed == required)\n        {\n            if (right - left + 1 < minLen)\n            {\n                minLen = right - left + 1;\n                startIdx = left;\n            }\n            char leftChar = s[left++];\n            window[leftChar]--;\n            if (target[leftChar] > 0 && window[leftChar] < target[leftChar])\n            {\n                formed--;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return minLen == INT_MAX ? \"\" : s.substr(startIdx, minLen);\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["Using `std::unordered_map` instead of fixed-size array/vector when alphabet is small (adds unnecessary hash table overhead).", "Off-by-one errors when shrinking `left` pointer.", "Not checking if `minLen` remained `INT_MAX` when no valid window is found."], "tips_to_remember": ["Both left and right pointers only move forward — total moves <= 2N, giving O(N) linear time.", "For ASCII string problems, `int count[128] = {0}` is 10x faster in interviews than `unordered_map`.", "In 3Sum, remember to skip duplicates: `while (l < r && nums[l] == nums[l-1]) l++;`"], "questions": [{"question": "Why is sliding window guaranteed O(N) even with a nested while loop?", "answer": "Because the inner while loop only increments the left pointer. Across the entire execution of the algorithm, left is incremented at most N times, and right is incremented at most N times. Therefore, the amortized cost per element is O(1), and total time is O(N)."}, {"question": "How do you avoid duplicates in 3Sum in C++?", "answer": "First sort the array in O(N log N). For the outer loop `i`, if `i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i-1]`, continue. For the two-pointer inner search (left and right), after recording a valid triplet with sum == 0, advance `left` while `nums[left] == nums[left+1]` and decrement `right` while `nums[right] == nums[right-1]`."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the worst-case time complexity of the Sliding Window technique on an array of size N?", "options": ["O(N log N)", "O(N)", "O(N^2)", "O(log N)"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Even though there is a nested while loop for the left pointer, each pointer traverses each index at most once, making total operations <= 2N -> O(N).", "id": "day_18_sub_0_mcq_0"}], "id": "day_18_sub_0", "day": 18, "index": 0}, {"title": "Monotonic Stack & Priority Queue (Next Greater Element, Histogram, Top-K)", "explanation": "A **Monotonic Stack** maintains its elements in strictly increasing or decreasing order. It is the standard optimal technique for problems requiring finding the nearest larger or smaller element for every index in O(N).\n\n### Patterns:\n1. **Next Greater Element**: Monotonic decreasing stack. When a new element `x` is greater than stack top, the top element has found its next greater element (`x`). Pop it and record.\n2. **Largest Rectangle in Histogram**: Monotonic increasing stack storing indices. When a smaller bar appears, pop and calculate the rectangle with the popped bar as the shortest height.\n3. **Top K Frequent Elements**: Min-Heap (`std::priority_queue` with `std::greater`) of size K. Maintains the largest K elements in O(N log K) time and O(K) space.", "examples": [{"title": "Next Greater Element in C++ using std::stack", "code": "#include <vector>\n#include <stack>\n\nstd::vector<int> nextGreaterElements(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n    int n = nums.size();\n    std::vector<int> result(n, -1);\n    std::stack<int> st; // stores indices\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        while (!st.empty() && nums[i] > nums[st.top()]) {\n            result[st.top()] = nums[i];\n            st.pop();\n        }\n        st.push(i);\n    }\n    return result;\n}", "explanation": "Indices are pushed onto the stack. Elements that are smaller than the current element get their answers resolved and popped.", "formatted_code": "#include <vector>\n#include <stack>\n\nstd::vector<int> nextGreaterElements(const std::vector<int>& nums)\n{\n    int n = nums.size();\n    std::vector<int> result(n, -1);\n    std::stack<int> st;  // stores indices\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)\n    {\n        while (!st.empty() && nums[i] > nums[st.top()])\n        {\n            result[st.top()] = nums[i];\n            st.pop();\n        }\n        st.push(i);\n    }\n    return result;\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["Storing values instead of indices in the stack (indices allow computing distances and widths).", "For circular arrays, forgetting to run the loop up to `2 * n - 1` with modulo `i % n`."], "tips_to_remember": ["For 'Next Greater', use a decreasing stack. For 'Next Smaller', use an increasing stack.", "For Top-K largest, use a Min-Heap of size K (not Max-Heap), so that smallest elements are ejected first."], "questions": [{"question": "Why use a Min-Heap instead of a Max-Heap for Top-K Largest elements?", "answer": "With a Min-Heap of capacity K, the minimum of the top-K elements sits at the root. When a new element arrives, if it is larger than the root, we pop the root and push the new element. This keeps space at O(K) and time at O(N log K), compared to O(N) space and O(N log N) time for building a full heap."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the time complexity of finding the Next Greater Element for all items in an array of size N using a Monotonic Stack?", "options": ["O(N^2)", "O(N log N)", "O(N)", "O(1)"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "Every element is pushed onto the stack once and popped at most once, resulting in 2N total operations, which is O(N).", "id": "day_18_sub_1_mcq_0"}], "id": "day_18_sub_1", "day": 18, "index": 1}, {"title": "Graph BFS/DFS, Topological Sort & Dijkstra's Shortest Path", "explanation": "Graph algorithms form a core pillar of FAANG interviews. Mastery of adjacency representations, cycle detection, topological ordering (dependency resolution), and shortest path is essential.\n\n### Essential Algorithms:\n1. **Breadth-First Search (BFS)**: Uses a FIFO `std::queue`. Guarantees shortest path in unweighted graphs. Time: O(V + E).\n2. **Depth-First Search (DFS)**: Uses recursion or explicit stack. Ideal for connected components, flood fill, and cycle detection (3-state coloring: unvisited, visiting, visited).\n3. **Topological Sort (Kahn's Algorithm)**: Computes in-degrees for all vertices. Push 0 in-degree nodes to queue. As nodes are processed, decrement neighbor in-degrees. If processed count < V, a cycle exists (Course Schedule problem).\n4. **Dijkstra's Algorithm**: Single-source shortest path for non-negative weighted graphs using `std::priority_queue<std::pair<int, int>, std::vector<std::pair<int, int>>, std::greater<>>`. Time: O((V + E) log V).", "examples": [{"title": "Kahn's Topological Sort in C++ (Course Schedule)", "code": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nbool canFinish(int numCourses, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& prerequisites) {\n    std::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(numCourses);\n    std::vector<int> inDegree(numCourses, 0);\n\n    for (const auto& pre : prerequisites) {\n        adj[pre[1]].push_back(pre[0]);\n        inDegree[pre[0]]++;\n    }\n\n    std::queue<int> q;\n    for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; ++i) {\n        if (inDegree[i] == 0) q.push(i);\n    }\n\n    int visitedCount = 0;\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int curr = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        visitedCount++;\n\n        for (int neighbor : adj[curr]) {\n            if (--inDegree[neighbor] == 0) {\n                q.push(neighbor);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return visitedCount == numCourses;\n}", "explanation": "If visitedCount equals numCourses, all courses can be finished without circular dependencies.", "formatted_code": "#include <vector>\n#include <queue>\n\nbool canFinish(int numCourses, const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& prerequisites)\n{\n    std::vector<std::vector<int>> adj(numCourses);\n    std::vector<int> inDegree(numCourses, 0);\n\n    for (const auto& pre : prerequisites)\n    {\n        adj[pre[1]].push_back(pre[0]);\n        inDegree[pre[0]]++;\n    }\n\n    std::queue<int> q;\n    for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; ++i)\n    {\n        if (inDegree[i] == 0) q.push(i);\n    }\n\n    int visitedCount = 0;\n    while (!q.empty())\n    {\n        int curr = q.front();\n        q.pop();\n        visitedCount++;\n\n        for (int neighbor : adj[curr])\n        {\n            if (--inDegree[neighbor] == 0)\n            {\n                q.push(neighbor);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return visitedCount == numCourses;\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["Forgetting to check for cycles in directed graphs (causes infinite loops in naive DFS).", "Applying Dijkstra on graphs with negative edge weights (requires Bellman-Ford or SPFA instead)."], "tips_to_remember": ["In C++, `std::priority_queue` is a Max-Heap by default. Use `std::greater<>` to turn it into a Min-Heap for Dijkstra.", "Topological sort only exists for Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs)."], "questions": [{"question": "What is the difference between Kahn's algorithm and DFS-based topological sort?", "answer": "Kahn's algorithm is iterative, uses in-degrees and a queue, and naturally detects cycles when the processed node count does not equal V. DFS-based topological sort uses post-order traversal and prepends nodes to a list, requiring 3-color cycle detection to prevent infinite recursion on cycles."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the time complexity of Dijkstra's algorithm implemented with a binary min-heap?", "options": ["O(V^2)", "O((V + E) log V)", "O(V + E)", "O(E log E)"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "With an adjacency list and binary min-heap, each vertex is extracted once (O(V log V)) and each edge can trigger a decrease-key/insert (O(E log V)), giving O((V + E) log V).", "id": "day_18_sub_2_mcq_0"}], "id": "day_18_sub_2", "day": 18, "index": 2}]}, {"day": 19, "topic": "FAANG DSA — Advanced Structures & Hard Patterns in C++", "estimated_hours": 6, "subtopics": [{"title": "Disjoint Set Union (DSU / Union-Find) with Path Compression & Rank", "explanation": "Disjoint Set Union (DSU) maintains partition of a set into disjoint subsets. It is essential for dynamic connectivity queries, detecting cycles in undirected graphs, and Kruskal's Minimum Spanning Tree algorithm.\n\n### Two Critical Optimizations:\n1. **Path Compression**: During `find(x)`, point every visited node directly to the root: `parent[x] = find(parent[x])`. Flattens the tree.\n2. **Union by Rank / Size**: Attach smaller depth tree under the root of the deeper tree, keeping depth logarithmic.\n\n### Complexity:\nCombining Path Compression and Union by Rank yields nearly O(1) amortized time per operation: O(alpha(N)) where alpha is the inverse Ackermann function (alpha(N) < 5 for all realistic universe values).", "examples": [{"title": "Production-Ready DSU Class in Modern C++", "code": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nclass DisjointSetUnion {\nprivate:\n    std::vector<int> parent;\n    std::vector<int> rank;\n    int numComponents;\n\npublic:\n    explicit DisjointSetUnion(int n) : parent(n), rank(n, 0), numComponents(n) {\n        std::iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);\n    }\n\n    int find(int x) {\n        if (parent[x] != x) {\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x]); // Path compression\n        }\n        return parent[x];\n    }\n\n    bool unite(int x, int y) {\n        int rootX = find(x);\n        int rootY = find(y);\n        if (rootX == rootY) return false; // Already in same set\n\n        if (rank[rootX] < rank[rootY]) {\n            parent[rootX] = rootY;\n        } else if (rank[rootX] > rank[rootY]) {\n            parent[rootY] = rootX;\n        } else {\n            parent[rootY] = rootX;\n            rank[rootX]++;\n        }\n        numComponents--;\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    int getComponentsCount() const { return numComponents; }\n};", "explanation": "Standard FAANG template for DSU with path compression and rank optimization, maintaining component count in O(alpha(N)) time.", "formatted_code": "#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n\nclass DisjointSetUnion\n{\nprivate:\n    std::vector<int> parent;\n    std::vector<int> rank;\n    int numComponents;\n\npublic:\n    explicit DisjointSetUnion(int n) : parent(n), rank(n, 0), numComponents(n)\n    {\n        std::iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);\n    }\n\n    int find(int x)\n    {\n        if (parent[x] != x)\n        {\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x]);  // Path compression\n        }\n        return parent[x];\n    }\n\n    bool unite(int x, int y)\n    {\n        int rootX = find(x);\n        int rootY = find(y);\n        if (rootX == rootY) return false;  // Already in same set\n\n        if (rank[rootX] < rank[rootY])\n        {\n            parent[rootX] = rootY;\n        }\n        else if (rank[rootX] > rank[rootY])\n        {\n            parent[rootY] = rootX;\n        }\n        else\n        {\n            parent[rootY] = rootX;\n            rank[rootX]++;\n        }\n        numComponents--;\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    int getComponentsCount() const\n    {\n        return numComponents;\n    }\n};"}], "pitfalls": ["Forgetting recursive `parent[x] = find(parent[x])` and only doing single-hop `parent[x]` (loses path compression).", "Applying DSU to directed cycle detection (DSU only works for undirected graphs; use Kahn's or 3-color DFS for directed)."], "tips_to_remember": ["DSU amortized time is O(alpha(N)) ≈ O(1) for all practical purposes.", "Use `std::iota` from `<numeric>` to initialize parent array from 0 to N-1."], "questions": [{"question": "Can DSU be used to detect cycles in a directed graph?", "answer": "No. In a directed graph, edge A->B and edge A->C do not create a cycle with edge B->C (it's a diamond, not a cycle). DSU cannot distinguish edge directions. Use DFS with 3-state coloring or Kahn's algorithm for directed graphs."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the amortized time complexity of Union-Find with both path compression and union by rank?", "options": ["O(log N)", "O(N)", "O(alpha(N)) practically O(1)", "O(1) strictly worst case"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "The combination of path compression and union by rank achieves O(alpha(N)) amortized time, where alpha is the inverse Ackermann function.", "id": "day_19_sub_0_mcq_0"}], "id": "day_19_sub_0", "day": 19, "index": 0}, {"title": "Dynamic Programming Patterns (0/1 Knapsack, LCS, LIS in O(N log N))", "explanation": "Dynamic Programming solves complex problems by breaking them down into overlapping subproblems with optimal substructure.\n\n### High-Frequency FAANG DP Patterns:\n1. **0/1 Knapsack Pattern**: Subset sum, partition equal subset sum. Traverse items, iterate weight backwards to reuse 1D state array.\n2. **Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)**: Edit distance, shortest common supersequence. 2D grid `dp[i][j] = (s1[i]==s2[j]) ? 1 + dp[i-1][j-1] : max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])`.\n3. **Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS)**: Standard DP is O(N^2). Optimal FAANG approach is O(N log N) using Patience Sorting with `std::lower_bound` on an auxiliary array `tails`.\n4. **Interval / Matrix Chain Multiplication DP**: `dp[i][j] = min(dp[i][k] + dp[k+1][j] + cost)` for bursting balloons, matrix multiplication.", "examples": [{"title": "LIS in O(N log N) using std::lower_bound in C++", "code": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint lengthOfLIS(const std::vector<int>& nums) {\n    if (nums.empty()) return 0;\n    std::vector<int> tails; // tails[i] stores smallest tail of all increasing subsequences of length i+1\n\n    for (int x : nums) {\n        auto it = std::lower_bound(tails.begin(), tails.end(), x);\n        if (it == tails.end()) {\n            tails.push_back(x);\n        } else {\n            *it = x;\n        }\n    }\n    return tails.size();\n}", "explanation": "If x is greater than all tails, it extends the longest subsequence. Otherwise, it replaces the first tail >= x to create a more greedy baseline for future extensions.", "formatted_code": "#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint lengthOfLIS(const std::vector<int>& nums)\n{\n    if (nums.empty()) return 0;\n    std::vector<int> tails;  // tails[i] stores smallest tail of all increasing subsequences of length i+1\n\n    for (int x : nums)\n    {\n        auto it = std::lower_bound(tails.begin(), tails.end(), x);\n        if (it == tails.end())\n        {\n            tails.push_back(x);\n        }\n        else\n        {\n            *it = x;\n        }\n    }\n    return tails.size();\n}"}], "pitfalls": ["In 1D Knapsack DP, iterating the inner capacity loop forwards instead of backwards (accidentally converts 0/1 Knapsack into Unbounded Knapsack).", "Overcomplicating LIS with O(N^2) state when N = 10^5 requires O(N log N)."], "tips_to_remember": ["For 0/1 Knapsack: loop capacity backwards `for (int w = W; w >= weight[i]; --w)`.", "For Unbounded Knapsack (Coin Change): loop capacity forwards `for (int w = weight[i]; w <= W; ++w)`."], "questions": [{"question": "Why do we iterate capacity backwards in 0/1 Knapsack 1D DP?", "answer": "Iterating backwards ensures that `dp[w - weight[i]]` represents the state from the previous item `i-1`, preventing the current item `i` from being used multiple times in the same knapsack."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the optimal time complexity to find the length of the Longest Increasing Subsequence of an array of N elements?", "options": ["O(N^2)", "O(N log N)", "O(2^N)", "O(N)"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Using binary search (std::lower_bound) on a tails array achieves O(N log N) time and O(N) space.", "id": "day_19_sub_1_mcq_0"}], "id": "day_19_sub_1", "day": 19, "index": 1}]}, {"day": 20, "topic": "FAANG System Design — Distributed Systems Fundamentals", "estimated_hours": 6, "subtopics": [{"title": "Scalability, CAP Theorem & PACELC Tradeoffs", "explanation": "In FAANG system design interviews, the opening 5 minutes require articulating functional requirements, non-functional requirements (Availability, Latency, Consistency), and calculating back-of-the-envelope estimations.\n\n### The CAP Theorem:\nIn any distributed asynchronous network prone to network partitions (P), a system can guarantee either **Consistency (C)** or **Availability (A)**, but never both simultaneously.\n- **CP Systems (e.g., Zookeeper, Spanner, HBase)**: Returns error or blocks when partition occurs to maintain single-source-of-truth state.\n- **AP Systems (e.g., Cassandra, DynamoDB with eventual consistency)**: Always returns responses even if stale, reconciles later via vector clocks or last-write-wins.\n\n### PACELC Extension:\nIf there is a Partition (P), trade off Availability (A) and Consistency (C); Else (E), trade off Latency (L) and Consistency (C). For example, Dynamo is PA/EL: during partition it favors Availability; under normal operation it favors low Latency over strong consistency.", "examples": [{"title": "Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation Formula", "code": "// Quick interview mental math:\n// 1 Million seconds ~= 11.5 days\n// 1 Day = 86,400 seconds ~= 10^5 seconds\n// 100 Million daily active users (DAU)\n// If each user makes 10 requests/day:\n// Total requests/day = 10^8 * 10 = 10^9 requests/day\n// Average QPS = 10^9 / 10^5 = 10,000 QPS\n// Peak QPS = Average QPS * 2 to 3 = 20,000 - 30,000 QPS\n// Storage: 1 KB/request * 10^9 = 1 TB/day => ~365 TB/year", "explanation": "Standard FAANG back-of-the-envelope estimation template for QPS, bandwidth, and storage sizing.", "formatted_code": "// Quick interview mental math:\n// 1 Million seconds ~= 11.5 days\n// 1 Day = 86,400 seconds ~= 10^5 seconds\n// 100 Million daily active users (DAU)\n// If each user makes 10 requests/day:\n// Total requests/day = 10^8 * 10 = 10^9 requests/day\n// Average QPS = 10^9 / 10^5 = 10,000 QPS\n// Peak QPS = Average QPS * 2 to 3 = 20,000 - 30,000 QPS\n// Storage: 1 KB/request * 10^9 = 1 TB/day => ~365 TB/year"}], "pitfalls": ["Claiming a distributed system can achieve CA (Consistency + Availability) across a network (networks always experience latency or packet drops).", "Forgetting to design for peak traffic (typically 2x to 5x average QPS)."], "tips_to_remember": ["Remember 1 day ≈ 100,000 seconds (86,400s) for rapid mental QPS math.", "PACELC: If Partition -> choose A or C; Else -> choose L or C."], "questions": [{"question": "What is the difference between Strong Consistency and Eventual Consistency?", "answer": "Strong consistency guarantees that any read operation immediately following a write returns the newly written value across all nodes (requires synchronous replication and higher latency). Eventual consistency allows temporary staleness: if no new updates are made, all replicas will eventually converge to the same value (enables high write availability and low latency)."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "According to the CAP Theorem, when a network partition occurs, what choice must a distributed system make?", "options": ["Choose between Throughput and Latency", "Choose between Consistency and Availability", "Choose between Reliability and Fault Tolerance", "Provide all three: Consistency, Availability, and Partition Tolerance"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Network partitions are unavoidable in physical networks; therefore, the system must choose between guaranteeing Consistency (rejecting writes that cannot replicate) or Availability (serving stale data).", "id": "day_20_sub_0_mcq_0"}], "id": "day_20_sub_0", "day": 20, "index": 0}, {"title": "Consistent Hashing & Distributed Caching (LRU, Cache-Aside, Write-Through)", "explanation": "When scaling cache or database nodes horizontally, naive hashing `hash(key) % N` fails disastrously: adding or removing a single node invalidates nearly 100% of keys (`(N-1)/N`), triggering a cache stampede on the primary database.\n\n### Consistent Hashing:\n- Both cache servers and keys are hashed onto a conceptual 360-degree circular ring (0 to 2^32 - 1).\n- A key is assigned to the first server encountered moving clockwise on the ring.\n- When a node is added/removed, only `K / N` keys are remapped on average.\n- **Virtual Nodes (vnodes)**: Each physical node is hashed to multiple positions on the ring (e.g., 100-200 vnodes per server) to ensure uniform load distribution and eliminate hotspots.\n\n### Caching Strategies:\n- **Cache-Aside (Lazy Loading)**: App reads from cache; on miss, reads DB, writes to cache. Tolerates cache failures.\n- **Write-Through**: App writes to cache; cache synchronously writes to DB. High consistency, higher write latency.\n- **Write-Behind (Write-Back)**: App writes to cache; cache asynchronously writes to DB in batches. Ultra-low write latency, risk of data loss on cache crash.", "examples": [{"title": "Consistent Hash Ring Implementation in Modern C++", "code": "#include <iostream>\n#include <map>\n#include <string>\n#include <functional>\n\nclass ConsistentHashRing {\nprivate:\n    std::map<uint32_t, std::string> ring; // Sorted hash ring\n    int virtualNodes;\n    std::hash<std::string> hasher;\n\npublic:\n    explicit ConsistentHashRing(int vnodes = 100) : virtualNodes(vnodes) {}\n\n    void addServer(const std::string& server) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < virtualNodes; ++i) {\n            std::string vNodeKey = server + \"#\" + std::to_string(i);\n            uint32_t hashVal = static_cast<uint32_t>(hasher(vNodeKey));\n            ring[hashVal] = server;\n        }\n    }\n\n    std::string getServer(const std::string& key) const {\n        if (ring.empty()) return \"\";\n        uint32_t hashVal = static_cast<uint32_t>(hasher(key));\n        auto it = ring.lower_bound(hashVal);\n        if (it == ring.end()) {\n            it = ring.begin(); // Wrap around ring\n        }\n        return it->second;\n    }\n};", "explanation": "Uses std::map (red-black tree) for O(log(N * V)) binary search lookup with std::map::lower_bound to find the clockwise server on the ring.", "formatted_code": "#include <iostream>\n#include <map>\n#include <string>\n#include <functional>\n\nclass ConsistentHashRing\n{\nprivate:\n    std::map<uint32_t, std::string> ring;  // Sorted hash ring\n    int virtualNodes;\n    std::hash<std::string> hasher;\n\npublic:\n    explicit ConsistentHashRing(int vnodes = 100) : virtualNodes(vnodes)\n    {\n    }\n\n    void addServer(const std::string& server)\n    {\n        for (int i = 0; i < virtualNodes; ++i)\n        {\n            std::string vNodeKey = server + \"#\" + std::to_string(i);\n            uint32_t hashVal = static_cast<uint32_t>(hasher(vNodeKey));\n            ring[hashVal] = server;\n        }\n    }\n\n    std::string getServer(const std::string& key) const\n    {\n        if (ring.empty()) return \"\";\n        uint32_t hashVal = static_cast<uint32_t>(hasher(key));\n        auto it = ring.lower_bound(hashVal);\n        if (it == ring.end())\n        {\n            it = ring.begin();  // Wrap around ring\n        }\n        return it->second;\n    }\n};"}], "pitfalls": ["Not using virtual nodes, resulting in non-uniform data distribution (hotspots).", "Cache stampede (Thundering Herd): multiple concurrent misses for an expired hot key all hit the database simultaneously. Fix with mutex locks or probabilistic early expiration."], "tips_to_remember": ["Virtual nodes solve two problems: non-uniform distribution and cascading failures when a node dies.", "Consistent hashing re-routes only K/N keys when cluster size changes from N to N+1."], "questions": [{"question": "What is the Cache Stampede (Thundering Herd) problem and how do you mitigate it?", "answer": "When a popular cached item expires, hundreds of concurrent read requests encounter a cache miss simultaneously and query the database at the exact same moment, overwhelming the DB. Mitigations include: (1) Mutual exclusion locking (mutex) so only one worker queries DB while others wait; (2) Probabilistic early expiration (XFetch algorithm); (3) Background worker pre-refreshing the cache before TTL expires."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What proportion of keys must be relocated on average when a new node is added to a consistent hash ring with N servers?", "options": ["100%", "50%", "1/N", "(N-1)/N"], "correct_option": 2, "explanation": "In consistent hashing, adding a node only affects the keys that fall between the new node and its predecessor on the ring, which represents 1/N of total keys.", "id": "day_20_sub_1_mcq_0"}], "id": "day_20_sub_1", "day": 20, "index": 1}]}, {"day": 21, "topic": "FAANG System Design — High-Scale Architectures", "estimated_hours": 6, "subtopics": [{"title": "Design a Scalable URL Shortener (TinyURL)", "explanation": "TinyURL is the quintessential FAANG introductory system design question. It tests capacity estimation, ID generation algorithms, database choices (SQL vs NoSQL), caching, and 301 vs 302 HTTP redirection tradeoffs.\n\n### Architecture Blueprint:\n1. **API Endpoints**:\n   - `POST /api/v1/shorten` -> `{ longUrl: string }` returns `{ shortUrl: string }`\n   - `GET /{shortCode}` -> 301 or 302 redirect to original long URL\n2. **301 vs 302 Redirection**:\n   - **301 Moved Permanently**: Browser caches redirect locally. Subsequent clicks bypass your servers, reducing load. But analytics/click tracking cannot be captured.\n   - **302 Found (Temporary)**: Every click hits your server first. Enables real-time click analytics and telemetry at the cost of higher server traffic.\n3. **Short Code Generation Strategy**:\n   - Using Base62 alphabet `[a-zA-Z0-9]` (62 chars).\n   - 7 characters: 62^7 = 3.52 Trillion unique URLs (plenty for 100+ years).\n   - Generate unique 64-bit ID using distributed ID generator (e.g., Twitter Snowflake), then convert integer to Base62.\n4. **Storage Layer**:\n   - Key-Value NoSQL Store (DynamoDB / Cassandra) or Relational DB with unique index on shortCode.\n   - Redis cache cluster with LRU eviction storing top 20% hot URLs (80-20 rule).", "examples": [{"title": "Base62 Encoding & Decoding in Modern C++", "code": "#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nclass Base62Codec {\nprivate:\n    inline static const std::string BASE62_CHARS = \n        \"0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ\";\n\npublic:\n    static std::string encode(uint64_t id) {\n        if (id == 0) return \"0\";\n        std::string shortCode;\n        while (id > 0) {\n            shortCode.push_back(BASE62_CHARS[id % 62]);\n            id /= 62;\n        }\n        std::reverse(shortCode.begin(), shortCode.end());\n        return shortCode;\n    }\n\n    static uint64_t decode(const std::string& code) {\n        uint64_t id = 0;\n        for (char c : code) {\n            size_t pos = BASE62_CHARS.find(c);\n            if (pos == std::string::npos) return 0;\n            id = id * 62 + pos;\n        }\n        return id;\n    }\n};", "explanation": "Converts 64-bit auto-increment or Snowflake IDs into compact 7-character alphanumeric strings.", "formatted_code": "#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nclass Base62Codec\n{\nprivate:\n    inline static const std::string BASE62_CHARS =\n    \"0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ\";\n\npublic:\n    static std::string encode(uint64_t id)\n    {\n        if (id == 0) return \"0\";\n        std::string shortCode;\n        while (id > 0)\n        {\n            shortCode.push_back(BASE62_CHARS[id % 62]);\n            id /= 62;\n        }\n        std::reverse(shortCode.begin(), shortCode.end());\n        return shortCode;\n    }\n\n    static uint64_t decode(const std::string& code)\n    {\n        uint64_t id = 0;\n        for (char c : code)\n        {\n            size_t pos = BASE62_CHARS.find(c);\n            if (pos == std::string::npos) return 0;\n            id = id * 62 + pos;\n        }\n        return id;\n    }\n};"}], "pitfalls": ["Hashing long URL with MD5/SHA256 and taking first 7 chars (causes hash collisions; requires expensive collision resolution loops).", "Single point of failure on the ID generator."], "tips_to_remember": ["62^7 ≈ 3.5 Trillion combinations.", "Use 301 for maximum CDN/browser caching efficiency; use 302 when click analytics are required.", "Cache hot 20% of URLs in Redis to absorb 80% of read traffic."], "questions": [{"question": "Why is Base62 encoding preferred over Base64 for URL shorteners?", "answer": "Base64 includes characters like '+' and '/' (or '=' for padding) which have reserved semantic meanings in URLs and query strings, requiring URL percent-encoding. Base62 only uses [0-9], [a-z], and [A-Z], making it clean and safe across all HTTP clients and browsers without escaping."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the primary trade-off of using HTTP 301 redirect instead of HTTP 302 in a URL shortener?", "options": ["301 is slower than 302 on initial request", "301 prevents capturing click analytics on subsequent visits because browser caches the target URL", "301 cannot redirect to HTTPS domains", "301 consumes double the server bandwidth"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "With HTTP 301 (Moved Permanently), modern browsers cache the destination URL locally and route subsequent requests directly to the target URL without hitting the shortening server, preventing telemetry collection.", "id": "day_21_sub_0_mcq_0"}], "id": "day_21_sub_0", "day": 21, "index": 0}, {"title": "Design a Distributed Message Queue (Kafka / Event Bus)", "explanation": "A distributed message queue decouples producers and consumers, absorbs burst traffic (peak shaving), and enables event-driven asynchronous architectures.\n\n### Architecture Blueprint:\n1. **Topic & Partitioning**:\n   - Topics are split into multiple partitions for horizontal scaling.\n   - Partitions are ordered, immutable append-only logs.\n   - Messages with the same partition key (e.g., `userId`) are guaranteed to arrive in strict chronological order.\n2. **Storage Engine (Log-Structured Append-Only)**:\n   - Disk sequential I/O is comparable to memory random I/O (hundreds of MB/s).\n   - Zero-Copy optimization (`sendfile()` system call) streams bytes directly from OS Page Cache to NIC network buffer without copying into user-space memory.\n3. **Consumer Groups**:\n   - Each partition is consumed by exactly one consumer within a consumer group (enables parallel processing without locks).\n   - Consumers track progress via committed offsets.\n4. **Delivery Semantics**:\n   - **At-most-once**: Consumer commits offset before processing (messages may be lost, never duplicated).\n   - **At-least-once**: Consumer commits offset after processing (no loss, but messages may be duplicated on consumer failure; requires idempotency).\n   - **Exactly-once**: Two-phase commit or transactional outbox with consumer deduplication keys.", "examples": [{"title": "Thread-Safe Ring Buffer / Disruptor Queue in Modern C++", "code": "#include <vector>\n#include <atomic>\n#include <optional>\n\ntemplate <typename T, size_t Capacity>\nclass LockFreeQueue {\n    static_assert((Capacity & (Capacity - 1)) == 0, \"Capacity must be power of 2\");\nprivate:\n    std::vector<T> buffer;\n    alignas(64) std::atomic<size_t> head{0};\n    alignas(64) std::atomic<size_t> tail{0};\n\npublic:\n    LockFreeQueue() : buffer(Capacity) {}\n\n    bool push(const T& item) {\n        size_t currentTail = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);\n        if (currentTail - head.load(std::memory_order_acquire) >= Capacity) {\n            return false; // Queue full\n        }\n        buffer[currentTail & (Capacity - 1)] = item;\n        tail.store(currentTail + 1, std::memory_order_release);\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    std::optional<T> pop() {\n        size_t currentHead = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);\n        if (currentHead == tail.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {\n            return std::nullopt; // Queue empty\n        }\n        T item = buffer[currentHead & (Capacity - 1)];\n        head.store(currentHead + 1, std::memory_order_release);\n        return item;\n    }\n};", "explanation": "Single-Producer Single-Consumer (SPSC) lock-free ring buffer utilizing memory order semantics and cache-line padding (alignas 64) to prevent false sharing.", "formatted_code": "#include <vector>\n#include <atomic>\n#include <optional>\n\ntemplate <typename T, size_t Capacity>\nclass LockFreeQueue\n{\n    static_assert((Capacity & (Capacity - 1)) == 0, \"Capacity must be power of 2\");\nprivate:\n    std::vector<T> buffer;\n    alignas(64) std::atomic<size_t> head\n    {\n        0\n    };\n    alignas(64) std::atomic<size_t> tail\n    {\n        0\n    };\n\npublic:\n    LockFreeQueue() : buffer(Capacity)\n    {\n    }\n\n    bool push(const T& item)\n    {\n        size_t currentTail = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);\n        if (currentTail - head.load(std::memory_order_acquire) >= Capacity)\n        {\n            return false;  // Queue full\n        }\n        buffer[currentTail & (Capacity - 1)] = item;\n        tail.store(currentTail + 1, std::memory_order_release);\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    std::optional<T> pop()\n    {\n        size_t currentHead = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);\n        if (currentHead == tail.load(std::memory_order_acquire))\n        {\n            return std::nullopt;  // Queue empty\n        }\n        T item = buffer[currentHead & (Capacity - 1)];\n        head.store(currentHead + 1, std::memory_order_release);\n        return item;\n    }\n};"}], "pitfalls": ["Assuming message queues provide global ordering (ordering is only guaranteed within a single partition).", "Consumer lag: consumers falling behind can exhaust disk retention or trigger buffer overflows."], "tips_to_remember": ["Messages in Kafka are ordered strictly per-partition, not globally across the topic.", "Zero-copy optimization bypasses user-space buffer copies via Linux `sendfile`.", "Always design consumers to be idempotent (handling duplicate messages safely)."], "questions": [{"question": "How does Kafka achieve high write and read throughput despite persisting all data to disk?", "answer": "Kafka achieves high performance through: (1) Sequential I/O: Append-only disk writes avoid disk head random seeks; (2) OS Page Cache: All reads and writes go through the Linux OS kernel page cache; (3) Zero-Copy optimization: sendfile() transfers bytes directly from OS cache to network socket without user-space buffer context switches; (4) Batching: Producers and consumers batch records together to minimize network syscall overhead."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "In Apache Kafka, what guarantees strict ordering of messages?", "options": ["Messages are ordered across all partitions in a topic", "Messages are strictly ordered only within a single partition", "Messages are ordered by consumer group priority", "Ordering is never guaranteed in distributed queues"], "correct_option": 1, "explanation": "Kafka only guarantees total order within a single partition. If strict ordering is required for an entity, all related events must share the same partition key.", "id": "day_21_sub_1_mcq_0"}], "id": "day_21_sub_1", "day": 21, "index": 1}]}]}, "nr": {"title": "3GPP 5G NR MAC Downlink & Carrier Aggregation Mastery Curriculum", "version": "2.0", "totalWeeks": 10, "totalModules": 40, "description": "Comprehensive, exhaustive transition curriculum and reference blueprint for senior telecom software engineers transitioning from 4G LTE L2 to 5G NR Baseband and Cloud-RAN architecture. Covers 3GPP Rel-15/16/17 specifications, low-level modern C++ systems, real-time RTOS determinism, calculation drills, and field log triage.", "weeks": [{"week": 1, "phase": 1, "phaseTitle": "Spec Architecture & Systems Foundation", "title": "5G Numerologies, Slot Formats & 125 µs Real-Time Constraints", "estimatedHours": 8, "specs": ["TS 38.211 §4.2, §4.3", "TS 38.213 §11.1", "PREEMPT_RT"], "summary": "Master flexible OFDM numerologies (15 to 120 kHz), scalable slot grids, normal vs extended CP, slot format indicator (SFI DCI 2_0), and the 125 µs hard scheduler deadline under real-time Linux.", "deliverable": "C++ numerology & slot lookup table, PREEMPT_RT core-pinning setup, and 125 µs scheduler latency budget model.", "topics": [{"id": "w1_t1", "title": "Flexible Numerologies (SCS) & Scalable Time Grid", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.211 §4.2", "explanation": "Unlike 4G LTE's fixed 15 kHz Subcarrier Spacing (1 ms subframe / 1 ms TTI), 5G NR defines an exponential scalable numerology:\nΔf = 2^μ · 15 kHz, where μ ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}.\n- μ = 0 (15 kHz SCS): 1.0 ms slot duration, 1 slot per 1 ms subframe (FR1 Sub-3 GHz macro coverage).\n- μ = 1 (30 kHz SCS): 0.5 ms slot duration, 2 slots per subframe (FR1 C-band standard deployment, 3.5 GHz).\n- μ = 2 (60 kHz SCS): 0.25 ms slot duration, 4 slots per subframe (FR1/FR2 transition band).\n- μ = 3 (120 kHz SCS): 125 µs slot duration, 8 slots per subframe (FR2 mmWave 28 GHz ultra-high throughput).\n- μ = 4 (240 kHz SCS): 62.5 µs slot duration, 16 slots per subframe (FR2 SSB beam sweeping).\n\nKey Architectural Principle:\nThe radio frame (10 ms) and subframe (1 ms) durations remain constant across all numerologies as the global timing anchors. Only the slot duration shrinks as μ increases, while the number of OFDM symbols per slot remains strictly 14 under normal cyclic prefix.", "keyFormulas": ["Δf = 2^μ · 15 kHz", "Slot Duration T_slot = 1 / 2^μ ms", "Slots per subframe N_slot^subframe,μ = 2^μ", "Slots per radio frame N_slot^frame,μ = 10 · 2^μ", "OFDM Symbol Duration T_symb = T_slot / 14", "PRB Bandwidth BW_PRB = 12 · Δf"], "memoryAids": ["Rule of Powers of 2: Incrementing μ doubles SCS, halves slot duration, and doubles slot count per 1 ms subframe.", "Subframe is the clock (1 ms); Slot is the worker."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing Subframe (always 1 ms) with Slot (varies from 1 ms down to 62.5 µs).", "Assuming symbol count per slot varies with numerology — it is always 14 symbols under normal CP."], "codeSnippet": "// 5G NR Numerology Table Lookups\nstruct NrNumerologyConfig {\n    uint8_t  mu;\n    uint32_t scs_khz;\n    double   slot_duration_ms;\n    uint8_t  slots_per_subframe;\n    uint32_t prb_bandwidth_khz;\n};\n\nconstexpr NrNumerologyConfig NUMEROLOGIES[5] = {\n    {0,  15, 1.0,    1,  180},\n    {1,  30, 0.5,    2,  360},\n    {2,  60, 0.25,   4,  720},\n    {3, 120, 0.125,  8, 1440},\n    {4, 240, 0.0625, 16, 2880}\n};"}, {"id": "w1_t2", "title": "Normal vs Extended CP, Slot Formats & SFI (DCI 2_0)", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.211 §4.3.2, TS 38.213 §11.1", "explanation": "5G NR defines two Cyclic Prefix formats:\n1. **Normal CP**: 14 OFDM symbols per slot. Supported across all numerologies (μ = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4).\n2. **Extended CP**: 12 OFDM symbols per slot. Supported ONLY at μ = 2 (60 kHz SCS). Extended CP provides a 4.16 µs guard interval to absorb extreme multipath delay spread in high-dispersion macro cell mmWave environments.\n\n**Slot Formats & Dynamic TDD**:\nEvery symbol inside a 14-symbol slot is designated as:\n- Downlink (D): Reserved for PDSCH, PDCCH, SSB, CSI-RS.\n- Uplink (U): Reserved for PUSCH, PUCCH, PRACH, SRS.\n- Flexible (F): Dynamic symbol that can be scheduled as either DL or UL via DCI grants, or used as a guard period (GP) for DL-to-UL switching.\n\nSlot formats are signaled semi-statically via RRC `tdd-UL-DL-ConfigurationCommon` or dynamically overridden slot-by-slot using DCI format 2_0 (Slot Format Indicator - SFI).", "keyFormulas": ["Symbols/slot: Normal CP = 14, Extended CP = 12 (at μ = 2 only)", "DCI 2_0 SFI Field: 8-bit index mapping to 256 predefined slot format tables in TS 38.213 Table 11.1.1-1", "Guard Period GP duration: GP ≥ 2 · Propagation Delay + DL-to-UL Rx/Tx switching time"], "memoryAids": ["D = Data Down, U = User Uplink, F = Flexible/Flip.", "Extended CP only lives at 60 kHz (μ = 2) to fight heavy multipath echoes."], "pitfalls": ["Scheduling DL transmission on U symbols or F symbols without explicit DCI grant overrides.", "Assuming Extended CP is supported on standard 30 kHz FR1 (it is strictly prohibited by 38.211)."], "codeSnippet": "// Slot symbol configuration mask\nenum class SymbolType : uint8_t {\n    DOWNLINK = 0,\n    UPLINK   = 1,\n    FLEXIBLE = 2\n};\n\nstruct SlotFormat {\n    uint8_t format_id;\n    SymbolType symbols[14];\n};\n\nbool is_schedulable_dl(SymbolType s) {\n    return (s == SymbolType::DOWNLINK || s == SymbolType::FLEXIBLE);\n}"}, {"id": "w1_t3", "title": "Real-Time Latency Budgets & Scheduler Execution Pipeline", "specClause": "TS 38.321 & O-RAN CUS Latency Constraints", "explanation": "A 5G NR baseband scheduler executing at 30 kHz (500 µs slot) or 120 kHz (125 µs slot) operates under hard real-time execution constraints. Missing the slot tick means the radio transmits zeros or unmodulated carriers, dropping commercial throughput immediately.\n\n**The 5-Stage Scheduler Pipeline**:\n- Stage 1: Feedback Ingestion (15 µs) - Process L1 UCI Indications (HARQ ACK/NACK, CQI/PMI), update OLLA offsets, re-queue NACKed processes.\n- Stage 2: Time-Domain Candidate Filtering (20 µs) - Scan connected UEs, compute Proportional Fair metrics, AVX-512 partial sort top candidates.\n- Stage 3: Frequency-Domain Resource Allocation (45 µs) - Allocate PRBs, look up TBS from TS 38.214 tables, handle retransmissions.\n- Stage 4: PDU Assembly & FAPI Dispatch (30 µs) - Assemble MAC subheaders + MAC CEs, pull RLC SDUs via zero-copy descriptors, emit FAPI DL_TTI.request.\n- Stage 5: DMA Baseband Transfer & Safety Margin (15 µs).\nTotal execution time: ~110 µs, strictly fitting inside the 125 µs deadline.", "keyFormulas": ["Hard Deadline: T_exec ≤ T_slot - T_DMA - T_margin", "At 30 kHz: T_exec ≤ 380 µs; At 120 kHz: T_exec ≤ 110 µs", "Proportional Fair Metric: W_i = (R_i / Avg_R_i) · QoS_weight"], "memoryAids": ["110 µs finish: Ingest → Filter → Allocate → Dispatch. Clock tick waits for no thread."], "pitfalls": ["Calling malloc/new, printf, or blocking system calls inside the real-time scheduling loop.", "Using non-priority-inheritance mutexes that allow priority inversion to stall the scheduler core."], "codeSnippet": "#include <pthread.h>\n#include <sched.h>\n#include <sys/mman.h>\n\nvoid configure_realtime_thread(int core_id, int priority_level) {\n    // 1. Lock all memory into RAM to eliminate page faults\n    mlockall(MCL_CURRENT | MCL_FUTURE);\n\n    // 2. Pin thread to isolated CPU core (isolcpus kernel parameter)\n    cpu_set_t cpuset;\n    CPU_ZERO(&cpuset);\n    CPU_SET(core_id, &cpuset);\n    pthread_setaffinity_np(pthread_self(), sizeof(cpu_set_t), &cpuset);\n\n    // 3. Set POSIX real-time FIFO scheduling policy\n    struct sched_param param;\n    param.sched_priority = priority_level; // e.g., 95\n    pthread_setschedparam(pthread_self(), SCHED_FIFO, &param);\n}"}, {"id": "w1_t4", "title": "Modern C++ Systems: Cache Line Alignment & Memory Pools", "specClause": "L2 High-Throughput Baseband Architecture", "explanation": "In high-throughput 5G NR software, copying packet buffers (`memcpy`) between PHY, MAC, and RLC saturates CPU memory buses. Furthermore, false sharing across CPU cores running parallel threads causes 10x latency spikes.\n\n**False Sharing Prevention**:\nModern CPUs transfer data in 64-byte cache lines. If Core 1 writes to UE A stats and Core 2 writes to UE B stats on the same 64-byte line, cache coherency (MESI protocol) invalidates each core's L1 cache continually. Use `alignas(64)` on all per-core/per-thread data structures.\n\n**Zero-Copy Memory Pools**:\nDynamic memory allocation is strictly prohibited in real-time baseband loops. Packets and descriptors are allocated during boot in fixed-size slab pools backed by 1 GB hugepages. Subheaders are inserted into pre-allocated headroom via pointer adjustment (`data_ptr -= header_len`), eliminating all buffer copies.", "keyFormulas": ["Cache Line Size = 64 bytes", "Memory Overhead = ≤ 63 bytes padding per aligned struct", "Slab Pool Allocation: O(1) pointer adjustment"], "memoryAids": ["Align to 64 to keep cores from war. Pre-allocate in pools so your latency stays cool."], "pitfalls": ["Putting atomic variables updated by different threads into the same struct without 64-byte padding.", "Using standard STL containers like std::vector that dynamically resize and reallocate on the heap."], "codeSnippet": "// Production-Grade Zero-Copy Packet Descriptor\nstruct alignas(64) PacketDescriptor {\n    uint8_t*  head_ptr;      // Start of physical memory buffer\n    uint8_t*  data_ptr;      // Current active protocol payload\n    uint32_t  data_len;      // Payload length in bytes\n    uint16_t  headroom;      // Reserved prefix for MAC/RLC headers\n    uint16_t  tailroom;      // Reserved suffix for CRC/Padding\n    std::atomic<uint32_t> ref_count; // For HARQ retransmissions\n    uint32_t  pool_index;    // Index in fixed-size slab allocator\n};\n\n// Zero-copy subheader prepend:\nvoid prepend_subheader(PacketDescriptor* desc, const uint8_t* hdr, uint16_t len) {\n    desc->data_ptr -= len;\n    desc->data_len += len;\n    desc->headroom -= len;\n    std::memcpy(desc->data_ptr, hdr, len);\n}"}]}, {"week": 2, "phase": 1, "phaseTitle": "Spec Architecture & Systems Foundation", "title": "TS 38.321 NR MAC Protocol, PDU Redesign & LCP", "estimatedHours": 8, "specs": ["TS 38.321 §5.1, §5.2, §5.4, §6.1"], "summary": "Master the redesigned NR MAC PDU layout with subheaders at the payload head, Logical Channel Prioritization restrictions (allowedSCS, maxPUSCH-Duration), 4-step vs Rel-16 2-step RACH, and the Timing Advance control loop.", "deliverable": "C++ zero-copy MAC PDU subheader builder, LCP token-bucket allocator, and RACH state machine.", "topics": [{"id": "w2_t1", "title": "NR MAC PDU Architecture: Subheaders at Payload Head & LCID", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.321 §6.1.2, §6.1.3", "explanation": "In 4G LTE, all MAC subheaders were grouped together at the very beginning of the MAC PDU. This forced the MAC entity to calculate and serialize all subheaders before transmitting payload data.\n\nIn 5G NR, TS 38.321 completely redesigned the MAC PDU:\nEach MAC subheader is placed immediately preceding its corresponding MAC SDU or MAC Control Element (CE).\n- **Subheader Format**:\n  - 1-byte subheader: R (1 bit reserved) + F (1 bit format) + LCID (6 bits) for fixed-length MAC CEs.\n  - 2-byte subheader: R/F/LCID + L (8 bits) when SDU length L < 256 bytes (F = 0).\n  - 3-byte subheader: R/F/LCID + L (16 bits) when SDU length L ≥ 256 bytes (F = 1).\n- **PDU Placement Order**: MAC CEs are placed at the beginning, followed by MAC SDUs, followed by optional padding at the end.\nThis architecture enables hardware DMA streaming: RLC can push SDUs into the radio buffer while MAC writes subheaders just-in-time into the descriptor headroom without re-copying!", "keyFormulas": ["Short subheader (L < 256): 2 bytes (F=0, L=8 bits)", "Long subheader (L ≥ 256): 3 bytes (F=1, L=16 bits)", "LCID: 6 bits (values 0..63 in Rel-15; extended eLCID in Rel-16)"], "memoryAids": ["4G: All headers in the lobby. 5G: Header attached to each room's door! Allows just-in-time streaming."], "pitfalls": ["Placing MAC CEs at the end of the PDU (in 5G NR, MAC CEs are placed at the beginning of the MAC PDU).", "Forgetting that the 1-bit F flag sets whether the L length field is 8 bits (0) or 16 bits (1)."], "codeSnippet": "// 5G NR MAC Subheader Encoder\nuint8_t encode_nr_subhead(uint8_t* buf, uint8_t lcid, uint16_t sdu_len) {\n    if (sdu_len < 256) {\n        buf[0] = (0 << 6) | (lcid & 0x3F); // F = 0\n        buf[1] = static_cast<uint8_t>(sdu_len);\n        return 2;\n    } else {\n        buf[0] = (1 << 6) | (lcid & 0x3F); // F = 1\n        buf[1] = static_cast<uint8_t>((sdu_len >> 8) & 0xFF);\n        buf[2] = static_cast<uint8_t>(sdu_len & 0xFF);\n        return 3;\n    }\n}"}, {"id": "w2_t2", "title": "Logical Channel Prioritization (LCP) Algorithm & Restrictions", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.4.3.1", "explanation": "Logical Channel Prioritization (LCP) multiplexes data from multiple RLC radio bearers into a single transport block grant.\n\n**The 2-Step Allocation**:\n- Step 1: Allocate resources to logical channels with Bj > 0 in decreasing priority order up to their Prioritized Bit Rate (PBR).\n- Step 2: Allocate remaining grant resources in strict priority order until the grant or data is exhausted.\n- **Token Bucket Mechanics**: Bj is incremented each TTI: Bj = min(Bj + PBR · Δt, Bucket Size Duration · PBR).\n\n**5G NR LCP Restrictions (Clause 5.4.3.1)**:\nTo prevent sub-optimal mapping of sensitive services (e.g. scheduling high-SCS URLLC grants for bulk eMBB data), RRC can restrict a logical channel with:\n1. `allowedSCS-List`: Logical channel can only use grants with matching numerology.\n2. `maxPUSCH-Duration`: Prevents URLLC from being mapped onto long slots.\n3. `configuredGrantType1Allowed`: Restricts autonomous grant usage.\n4. `allowedServingCells`: Maps critical traffic only to specific carriers.", "keyFormulas": ["Bj = min(Bj + PBR · Δt, BSD · PBR)", "Grant_remaining = Grant - ∑ allocated_bytes", "Bj can become negative after allocation, deducting from future tokens"], "memoryAids": ["PBR first, Priority second, Restrictions always! Bj is the piggy bank preventing starvation."], "pitfalls": ["Allowing bulk background data to consume an ultra-low latency grant because allowedSCS-List check was missed.", "Failing to deduct bytes from Bj when grant exceeds current bucket, breaking token-bucket rate limits."], "codeSnippet": "struct LogicalChannel {\n    uint8_t  id;\n    uint8_t  priority;\n    uint32_t pbr_bytes_per_sec;\n    int32_t  bj_tokens;\n    uint32_t max_bucket_bytes;\n    uint8_t  allowed_scs_mask;\n    uint8_t  max_pusch_duration_symb;\n};\n\nbool check_lcp_restrictions(const LogicalChannel& lch, uint8_t grant_scs, uint8_t grant_duration) {\n    if ((lch.allowed_scs_mask & (1 << grant_scs)) == 0) return false;\n    if (grant_duration > lch.max_pusch_duration_symb) return false;\n    return true;\n}"}, {"id": "w2_t3", "title": "Random Access (RACH): 4-Step CBRA vs Rel-16 2-Step RACH", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.1", "explanation": "The Random Access Procedure establishes initial uplink synchronization, RRC connection, and beam failure recovery.\n\n**4-Step Contention-Based RACH (CBRA)**:\n1. **Msg1**: UE transmits random access preamble on PRACH. Base station detects preamble and estimates round-trip delay.\n2. **Msg2 (RAR)**: gNodeB sends Random Access Response on PDSCH (addressed by RA-RNTI). Contains Timing Advance (TA), Uplink Grant, and Temporary C-RNTI (TC-RNTI).\n3. **Msg3**: UE transmits scheduled transmission on PUSCH with CCCH SDU or C-RNTI MAC CE.\n4. **Msg4 (Contention Resolution)**: gNodeB echoes UE identity on PDCCH/PDSCH. If identity matches, contention is resolved.\n\n**Rel-16 2-Step RACH (CFRA/CBRA)**:\nMerges the 4 steps into 2 to cut latency by 50%:\n- **MsgA**: Combines Msg1 preamble on PRACH + Msg3 PUSCH payload.\n- **MsgB**: Combines Msg2 RAR + Msg4 Contention Resolution on PDSCH.\nIdeal for Small Data Transmission (SDT), IoT, and fast handovers.", "keyFormulas": ["RA-RNTI = 1 + s_id + 14 · t_id + 14 · 80 · f_id + 14 · 80 · 8 · ul_carrier_id", "RAR Window: ra-ResponseWindow (configured in SIB1/RRC)", "Contention Resolution Timer: ra-ContentionResolutionTimer (8 to 64 slots)"], "memoryAids": ["4-Step: Knock → Open → Introduce → Handshake.", "2-Step: Knock & Introduce → Handshake & Enter!"], "pitfalls": ["Assuming MsgA fallback always retransmits both parts: if preamble is detected but PUSCH fails, UE falls back to Msg3 without re-sending preamble.", "Miscalculating RA-RNTI due to incorrect subframe t_id or symbol s_id coordinates."], "codeSnippet": "uint32_t calculate_ra_rnti(uint8_t s_id, uint8_t t_id, uint8_t f_id, uint8_t ul_carrier_id) {\n    return 1 + s_id + (14 * t_id) + (14 * 80 * f_id) + (14 * 80 * 8 * ul_carrier_id);\n}"}, {"id": "w2_t4", "title": "Timing Advance (TA) Control Loop, Timers & Expiry", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.2", "explanation": "Because UEs reside at varying distances from the gNodeB, uplink transmissions must be advanced in time so they arrive perfectly synchronized at the base station antenna array.\n\n**Initial vs Ongoing TA**:\n1. **Initial TA**: 12-bit value in RAR Msg2 ($N_{TA} \\in [0, 3846]$).\n2. **Ongoing TA**: Sent in Timing Advance Command MAC CE (6-bit index $T_A \\in [0, 63]$).\n   Formula: $N_{TA,new} = N_{TA,old} + (T_A - 31) \\cdot 16 \\cdot 64 / 2^\\mu$.\n   $T_A = 31$ represents NO CHANGE. $T_A < 31$ advances transmission earlier; $T_A > 31$ delays it.\n\n**Timing Alignment Timer (`timeAlignmentTimer`)**:\n- Maintained per Timing Advance Group (TAG: pTAG and sTAG).\n- Reset whenever a TA MAC CE is received.\n- **On TAT Expiry**: UE immediately flushes all HARQ buffers for that TAG, clears configured DL/UL grants, releases PUCCH and SRS resources, and must perform RACH before transmitting uplink data.", "keyFormulas": ["TA adjustment: ΔN_TA = (T_A - 31) · 16 · 64 / 2^μ", "Absolute Time Offset: T_TA = (N_TA + N_TA,offset) · T_c, where T_c ≈ 0.509 ns", "T_A = 31 is zero adjustment"], "memoryAids": ["31 is zero change! Lower pulls earlier, higher pushes later. TAT expiry kills UL grants instantly."], "pitfalls": ["Transmitting PUCCH or PUSCH after TAT expiry (causes immediate cell drop).", "Applying a single TAT across different carriers that belong to separate pTAG and sTAG groups."], "codeSnippet": "int32_t compute_ta_adjustment(uint8_t ta_command, uint8_t mu) {\n    int32_t delta_index = static_cast<int32_t>(ta_command) - 31;\n    return delta_index * 16 * (64 >> mu);\n}"}]}, {"week": 3, "phase": 1, "phaseTitle": "Spec Architecture & Systems Foundation", "title": "Bandwidth Part (BWP) Framework & SCell Power Dynamics", "estimatedHours": 8, "specs": ["TS 38.321 §5.7, §5.13, §5.15", "TS 38.213 §12"], "summary": "Understand BWP operations, dynamic switching via DCI 0_1/1_1, bwp-InactivityTimer, Rel-16 Dormant BWP power saving, and the connected-mode DRX (cDRX) state machine.", "deliverable": "C++ BWP switching state manager, DCI 2_6 dormant BWP activator, and DRX active-time calculator.", "topics": [{"id": "w3_t1", "title": "Bandwidth Part (BWP) Architecture: Initial, Active & Default", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.15, TS 38.213 §12", "explanation": "A Bandwidth Part is a contiguous subset of physical resource blocks (PRBs) on a carrier with a specific numerology (SCS and CP).\n- **Core Principle**: A UE can be configured with up to 4 DL BWPs and 4 UL BWPs per serving cell, but only ONE DL and ONE UL BWP is actively monitored at any given instant.\n- **BWP Types**:\n  1. **Initial BWP**: Configured via SIB1. Used for cell selection, RACH, and initial RRC connection.\n  2. **Active BWP**: Currently active bandwidth where UE monitors PDCCH and receives PDSCH.\n  3. **Default BWP**: Low-power fallback BWP (e.g. 20 MHz). When traffic pauses and `bwp-InactivityTimer` expires, UE autonomously switches back to the default BWP.\nThis eliminates the massive battery drain of keeping a 100 MHz wideband RF front-end active during idle intervals.", "keyFormulas": ["Max configured BWPs = 4 DL + 4 UL per cell", "Active BWPs at any instant = 1 DL + 1 UL (in Rel-15)", "PRB coordinates: N_BWP^start ≤ PRB < N_BWP^start + N_BWP^size"], "memoryAids": ["Initial to attach, Active to transact, Default to relax! UE only pays RF power for what it needs."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming multiple BWPs can be active simultaneously on a single carrier in Rel-15 (only one active BWP).", "Forgetting that numerology can change when switching BWPs, altering slot duration mid-session."], "codeSnippet": "struct BandwidthPart {\n    uint8_t  bwp_id;\n    uint8_t  mu;             // Numerology (SCS)\n    uint16_t start_prb;\n    uint16_t num_prbs;\n    bool     is_default;\n    bool     is_active;\n};"}, {"id": "w3_t2", "title": "Dynamic BWP Switching Mechanics: DCI Formats & Timers", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.15, TS 38.212 §7.3", "explanation": "BWP switching mechanisms:\n1. **DCI-Based Switching**: 1-bit or 2-bit Bandwidth Part Indicator field in DCI format 0_1 (UL) or 1_1 (DL) explicitly switches the active BWP.\n2. **Timer-Based Fallback**: `bwp-InactivityTimer`. When active DL BWP is not the default BWP and no DCI grant is received before the timer expires, UE autonomously reverts to the default BWP.\n3. **RRC-Based Switching**: Reconfigured via `RRCReconfiguration`.\n\n**The BWP Switching Gap**:\nDuring BWP retuning, the UE cannot transmit or receive for a transition gap ($T_{BWP\\_switch}$ up to several slots). The gNodeB MAC scheduler must never allocate grants during this retuning gap.", "keyFormulas": ["BWP Indicator in DCI: 0 bits (if 1 BWP), 1 bit (if 2 BWPs), 2 bits (if 3-4 BWPs)", "Retuning latency: Type 1 (fast baseband) vs Type 2 (slow RF retune) slots", "Inactivity condition: Timer expires && Active BWP ≠ Default BWP ⇒ Switch to Default BWP"], "memoryAids": ["DCI switches UP on demand; Inactivity Timer drops DOWN to save power automatically."], "pitfalls": ["Scheduling PDSCH/PUSCH inside the BWP switching gap causes immediate packet drop.", "DCI format 1_0 cannot trigger BWP switch (only fallback DCI)."], "codeSnippet": "void handle_bwp_inactivity_tick(BandwidthPart& active_bwp, const BandwidthPart& default_bwp, uint32_t& timer) {\n    if (active_bwp.bwp_id != default_bwp.bwp_id) {\n        if (timer > 0) {\n            timer--;\n            if (timer == 0) {\n                // Autonomous switch to default BWP\n                active_bwp = default_bwp;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}"}, {"id": "w3_t3", "title": "Rel-16 Dormant BWP Operation in Carrier Aggregation", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.13, §5.15", "explanation": "In Rel-15 Carrier Aggregation, when an SCell is deactivated to save battery, all channel measurements cease. Re-activating it takes 24-34 ms for RRC signaling, beam alignment, and channel feedback restart.\n\n**Rel-16 Dormant BWP Innovation**:\nThe SCell enters a 'Dormant BWP' state:\n- UE **stops PDCCH blind decoding** (saving 90% of baseband and RF reception energy).\n- UE **continues** periodic CSI measurements, CQI/RI reporting, and beam tracking towards the PCell.\n- Fast Activation: Switching from Dormant BWP back to Active BWP executes in **2 to 4 ms** via a dedicated DCI 1_1 or DCI 2_6 bitmap.\nDelivers near-instantaneous multi-gigabit CA throughput bursts without baseline battery drain.", "keyFormulas": ["Activation latency: Rel-15 SCell activation ≈ 24-34 ms; Rel-16 Dormant BWP switch ≈ 2-4 ms", "Power reduction: ~85-90% PDCCH decoding energy saved", "DCI 2_6: Wake-Up Signal (WUS) bitmap triggering Dormant to Active transition"], "memoryAids": ["Dormant = Eyes closed, Ears open! No PDCCH decoding, but continuous CSI reporting."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing SCell deactivation with Dormant BWP: Deactivated SCell stops all CSI measurements; Dormant BWP continues reporting CSI."], "codeSnippet": "struct SCellState {\n    uint8_t scell_index;\n    bool is_activated;\n    bool is_dormant;\n    \n    void transition_to_active() {\n        is_activated = true;\n        is_dormant = false; // PDCCH monitoring enabled\n    }\n    \n    void transition_to_dormant() {\n        is_activated = true;\n        is_dormant = true;  // Stop PDCCH, continue CSI reporting\n    }\n};"}, {"id": "w3_t4", "title": "Discontinuous Reception (cDRX) State Machine & Timers", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.7", "explanation": "Connected-mode DRX (cDRX) powers down UE radio circuits during inactivity while maintaining active RRC connection.\n\n**Key Timers & Parameters**:\n- `drx-onDurationTimer`: Duration the UE stays awake at the start of each DRX cycle.\n- `drx-InactivityTimer`: Duration the UE stays awake after decoding a PDCCH grant indicating new transmission. Every new grant restarts this timer!\n- `drx-RetransmissionTimerDL/UL`: Duration UE stays awake waiting for a retransmission after HARQ RTT expires.\n- `drx-HARQ-RTT-TimerDL`: Minimum time before a DL retransmission is expected.\n- **Active Time**: UE is in Active Time (awake) if ANY of these timers are running, or a Scheduling Request is pending, or a RAR is awaited.", "keyFormulas": ["Cycle condition: [(SFN · 10) + subframe] mod drx-LongCycle == drx-StartOffset", "Active Time = onDuration || InactivityTimer || RetransmissionTimer || SRPending", "Duty cycle: η = T_active / T_cycle"], "memoryAids": ["On-Duration wakes you up; Inactivity timer keeps the party going if packets keep arriving."], "pitfalls": ["Scheduling new DL data when UE is in DRX sleep (data must wait until next onDuration or trigger paging).", "Confusing HARQ RTT timer with retransmission timer: UE sleeps during RTT timer!"], "codeSnippet": "struct DrxContext {\n    uint32_t on_duration_timer;\n    uint32_t inactivity_timer;\n    uint32_t retrans_timer_dl;\n    bool     sr_pending;\n    \n    bool is_in_active_time() const {\n        return (on_duration_timer > 0 || inactivity_timer > 0 || \n                retrans_timer_dl > 0 || sr_pending);\n    }\n};"}]}, {"week": 4, "phase": 2, "phaseTitle": "DL Physical Procedures & Resource Allocation", "title": "PDSCH TDRA (SLIV), FDRA & Mathematical TBS Determination", "estimatedHours": 9, "specs": ["TS 38.214 §5.1.2, §5.1.3", "TS 38.212 §5.4.2"], "summary": "Deep dive into 3GPP TS 38.214 data procedures: PDSCH Mapping Type A vs Type B, exact SLIV encoding/decoding, FDRA Type 0 bitmap vs Type 1 RIV, the complete 5-step TBS determination algorithm, and LBRM soft buffer sizing.", "deliverable": "C++ SLIV encoder/decoder, Type 1 RIV encoder, and verbatim TS 38.214 §5.1.3.2 TBS calculation engine.", "topics": [{"id": "w4_t1", "title": "PDSCH TDRA: Mapping Type A/B & Exact SLIV Formula", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.1.2.1", "explanation": "Time Domain Resource Allocation (TDRA) indicates slot offset K0, start symbol S, and allocated length L via the 4-bit 'Time domain resource assignment' field in DCI format 1_0/1_1.\n- **Mapping Type A (Slot-Based)**: DM-RS is fixed at symbol 2 or 3 of the slot. S ∈ {2, 3}, L ∈ {3..14}, S + L ≤ 14.\n- **Mapping Type B (Mini-Slot / Non-Slot Based)**: DM-RS is at the FIRST symbol of the PDSCH allocation. S ∈ {0..12}, L ∈ {2, 4, 7}, S + L ≤ 14. Essential for URLLC preemption!\n\n**Start and Length Indicator Value (SLIV)**:\nCompresses S and L into an integer between 0 and 127:\n- If (L - 1) ≤ 7: SLIV = 14 · (L - 1) + S\n- Else: SLIV = 14 · (14 - L + 1) + (14 - 1 - S)", "keyFormulas": ["Condition: 0 < L ≤ 14 - S", "SLIV ≤ 127 (7-bit field)", "Decoding: q = ⌊SLIV/14⌋, r = SLIV mod 14. If q+r ≤ 13 ⇒ L=q+1, S=r; Else ⇒ L=14-q+1, S=14-1-r"], "memoryAids": ["Type A is the full-slot Apartment (DMRS at symbol 2/3); Type B is the nimble Bicycle (DMRS at first symbol, fast preemption!).", "SLIV flips formula when Length > 7."], "pitfalls": ["Attempting S + L > 14 (invalid SLIV that crashes baseband decoders).", "Using Mapping Type A with S ≠ 2, 3."], "codeSnippet": "uint16_t encode_sliv(uint8_t S, uint8_t L) {\n    if (L == 0 || (S + L) > 14) return 0xFFFF; // Invalid\n    if ((L - 1) <= 7) {\n        return 14 * (L - 1) + S;\n    } else {\n        return 14 * (14 - L + 1) + (14 - 1 - S);\n    }\n}\n\nbool decode_sliv(uint16_t sliv, uint8_t& S, uint8_t& L) {\n    if (sliv > 127) return false;\n    uint8_t q = sliv / 14;\n    uint8_t r = sliv % 14;\n    if ((q + r) <= 13) {\n        L = q + 1;\n        S = r;\n    } else {\n        L = 14 - q + 1;\n        S = 14 - 1 - r;\n    }\n    return (S + L <= 14);\n}"}, {"id": "w4_t2", "title": "Frequency Domain Resource Allocation: Type 0 vs Type 1 RIV", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.1.2.2", "explanation": "FDRA determines which Physical Resource Blocks (PRBs) carry PDSCH:\n- **Type 0 (Resource Block Group Bitmap)**: PRBs are clustered into RBGs of size P ∈ {2, 4, 8, 16}. A bitmap in DCI assigns individual RBGs (1 bit per RBG). Allows non-contiguous frequency-selective scheduling to exploit channel peaks at the cost of higher DCI bit overhead.\n- **Type 1 (Contiguous Resource Indication Value - RIV)**: Allocates a contiguous block of PRBs defined by starting PRB RB_start and length L_RBs.\n  - If (L_RBs - 1) ≤ ⌊N_BWP^size / 2⌋: RIV = N_BWP^size · (L_RBs - 1) + RB_start\n  - Else: RIV = N_BWP^size · (N_BWP^size - L_RBs + 1) + (N_BWP^size - 1 - RB_start)\nField size: ⌈log2(N_BWP · (N_BWP + 1) / 2)⌉ bits (e.g. 16 bits for 273 PRBs).", "keyFormulas": ["RIV bit width = ⌈log2(N_BWP(N_BWP+1)/2)⌉", "RBG size P: Table 5.1.2.2.1-1 (Config 1 vs Config 2)", "Contiguous PRB mapping reduces DCI signaling overhead"], "memoryAids": ["Type 0 is the selective cookie cutter (bitmap of groups); Type 1 is the continuous slice (RIV single block)."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming Type 0 RBGs are always uniform (the first and last RBGs in a BWP can be smaller due to carrier boundaries).", "Applying Type 1 RIV formula with N_BWP size instead of active BWP size."], "codeSnippet": "uint32_t encode_riv(uint32_t rb_start, uint32_t num_rbs, uint32_t bwp_size) {\n    if ((num_rbs - 1) <= (bwp_size / 2)) {\n        return bwp_size * (num_rbs - 1) + rb_start;\n    } else {\n        return bwp_size * (bwp_size - num_rbs + 1) + (bwp_size - 1 - rb_start);\n    }\n}"}, {"id": "w4_t3", "title": "Exact Transport Block Size (TBS) Determination: 5-Step Algorithm", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.1.3.2", "explanation": "The 5-step mathematical procedure the MAC scheduler executes every slot to determine transport block size in bits:\n- **Step 1**: Resource Elements per PRB: N'_RE = 12 · N_symb^sh - N_DMRS^PRB - N_oh^PRB. Total REs: N_RE = min(156, N'_RE) · n_PRB.\n- **Step 2**: Unquantized Information Bits: N_info = N_RE · R · Q_m · ν (where R is target code rate, Q_m is modulation order, ν is layers).\n- **Step 3**: Small Transport Blocks (N_info ≤ 3824): Quantize using TS 38.214 Table 5.1.3.2-1.\n- **Step 4**: Large Transport Blocks (N_info > 3824): Compute exponent n = ⌊log2(N_info - 24)⌋ - 5, round N'_info = max(3840, 2^n · round((N_info - 24) / 2^n)).\n- **Step 5**: LDPC Code Block Segmentation & Parity: If R ≤ 1/4 ⇒ Base Graph 2 (K_cb = 3840). If R > 1/4 and N'_info > 8424 ⇒ Base Graph 1 (K_cb = 8448).\n  Code blocks C = ⌈(N'_info + 24) / (K_cb - 24)⌉.\n  Exact TBS = 8 · C · ⌈(N'_info + 24) / (8 · C)⌉ - 24 bits.", "keyFormulas": ["N'_RE = 12 · N_symb - N_DMRS - N_oh", "N_RE = min(156, N'_RE) · n_PRB", "N_info = N_RE · R · Q_m · ν", "n = ⌊log2(N_info - 24)⌋ - 5", "TBS = 8 · C · ⌈(N'_info + 24) / (8 · C)⌉ - 24"], "memoryAids": ["RE Count → Info Bits → Exponent Quantize → LDPC Base Graph Segment → Final Byte-Aligned TBS."], "pitfalls": ["Forgetting to cap N'_RE at 156 (RE limit per PRB).", "Omitting the 24-bit CRC deduction before quantization.", "Choosing Base Graph 1 when code rate R ≤ 1/4."], "codeSnippet": "#include <cmath>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <cstdint>\n\nuint32_t calculate_tbs(uint32_t n_prb, uint8_t n_symb, uint8_t n_dmrs, \n                      uint8_t q_m, double code_rate, uint8_t layers) {\n    uint32_t n_re_prime = 12 * n_symb - n_dmrs;\n    uint32_t n_re = std::min<uint32_t>(156, n_re_prime) * n_prb;\n    double n_info = n_re * code_rate * q_m * layers;\n    \n    if (n_info <= 3824.0) {\n        // Look up in TS 38.214 Table 5.1.3.2-1\n        return static_cast<uint32_t>(n_info); // Simplified lookup\n    }\n    \n    int n = static_cast<int>(std::floor(std::log2(n_info - 24.0))) - 5;\n    double step = std::pow(2.0, n);\n    uint32_t n_info_prime = std::max<uint32_t>(3840, \n        static_cast<uint32_t>(step * std::round((n_info - 24.0) / step)));\n        \n    uint32_t k_cb = (n_info_prime > 8424 && code_rate > 0.25) ? 8448 : 3840;\n    uint32_t c = (n_info_prime + 24 + (k_cb - 25)) / (k_cb - 24);\n    uint32_t tbs = 8 * c * ((n_info_prime + 24 + (8 * c - 1)) / (8 * c)) - 24;\n    return tbs;\n}"}, {"id": "w4_t4", "title": "Limited Buffer Rate Matching (LBRM) & Soft Buffer Sizing", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.212 §5.4.2", "explanation": "In high-throughput 5G NR, storing unpunctured soft LLR bits for up to 16 HARQ processes across multiple carriers would require prohibitive on-chip SRAM in the UE.\nLimited Buffer Rate Matching (LBRM) defines a soft buffer limit:\n- Maximum code block soft buffer size: N_cb = min(N_IR, N_ref), where N_IR is the soft buffer capacity for the process.\n- Circular buffer rate matching punctures systematic and parity bits when the required code rate exceeds channel limits.\n- LDPC Base Graph 1 supports information block size up to K = 8448 bits; Base Graph 2 supports up to K = 3840 bits.\n- Redundancy Versions (RV ∈ {0, 2, 3, 1}): RV0 transmits all systematic bits; RV2, RV3, and RV1 transmit parity bits with incremental redundancy.", "keyFormulas": ["K_cb = 8448 (BG1), K_cb = 3840 (BG2)", "N_cb = min(N_IR, N_ref)", "RV Sequence: 0 → 2 → 3 → 1"], "memoryAids": ["LBRM protects the chip from memory blowout! RV sequence 0-2-3-1: 0 has systematic data, 2/3/1 pump parity."], "pitfalls": ["Inverting the RV sequence (sending RV3 before RV2 severely degrades incremental redundancy combining gains).", "Assuming LBRM is disabled by default (it is enabled for high-bandwidth carrier aggregation)."], "codeSnippet": "// Standard 3GPP Rel-15 RV Cycle\nconstexpr uint8_t RV_CYCLE[4] = {0, 2, 3, 1};\n\nuint8_t get_next_rv(uint8_t transmission_count) {\n    return RV_CYCLE[transmission_count % 4];\n}"}]}, {"week": 5, "phase": 2, "phaseTitle": "DL Physical Procedures & Resource Allocation", "title": "NR DL HARQ Entity, Dynamic Timing & Codebooks", "estimatedHours": 9, "specs": ["TS 38.321 §5.3", "TS 38.213 §9.2.3", "TS 38.214 §5.1.7"], "summary": "Master asynchronous HARQ, dynamic K1 timing, NDI toggle semantics, Code Block Group (CBG) retransmissions, Type-1 Semi-Static HARQ codebook, and Type-2 Dynamic HARQ codebook with cDAI/tDAI.", "deliverable": "C++ 16-process HARQ entity manager with CBG tracking, and Type-2 codebook generator with modulo-4 gap detection.", "topics": [{"id": "w5_t1", "title": "NR DL HARQ Entity: Asynchronous Mechanics & NDI Toggling", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.3, TS 38.214 §5.1", "explanation": "Unlike 4G LTE FDD's rigid 4 ms synchronous HARQ timeline, 5G NR implements fully asynchronous HARQ:\n- Up to 16 parallel HARQ processes per component carrier.\n- **Dynamic K1 Timing**: DCI format 1_0/1_1 explicitly instructs the UE when to report HARQ feedback via the 'PDSCH-to-HARQ_feedback timing indicator' field (K1 slots after PDSCH).\n- **New Data Indicator (NDI)**:\n  - If the NDI bit in DCI has TOGGLED relative to the previous transmission on that HARQ process ID ⇒ NEW DATA transmission. Soft buffer is cleared.\n  - If NDI has NOT toggled ⇒ RETRANSMISSION. UE combines received symbols with soft buffer.", "keyFormulas": ["PUCCH Slot = n_PDSCH + K1", "Max HARQ Processes = 16 per carrier", "NDI Toggled: New transmission; NDI Untoggled: Retransmission"], "memoryAids": ["4G was an express train stopping every 4 ms; 5G is an on-demand taxi where DCI sets the exact drop-off slot K1.", "NDI Toggle = New data, NDI Same = Same data (Retransmission)."], "pitfalls": ["False NDI toggle due to missed DCI: if UE misses a DCI, it misses the grant; if next DCI has toggled NDI, UE discards previous retransmission data without realizing!", "Assuming HARQ process IDs are sequential (asynchronous scheduling allows arbitrary process ordering)."], "codeSnippet": "struct HarqProcess {\n    uint8_t  process_id;\n    uint8_t  last_ndi;\n    uint8_t  round;\n    bool     is_active;\n    \n    bool is_new_data(uint8_t received_ndi) {\n        if (!is_active) return true;\n        return (received_ndi != last_ndi);\n    }\n};"}, {"id": "w5_t2", "title": "Code Block Group (CBG) Based Retransmissions", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.3.2.2, TS 38.214 §5.1.7", "explanation": "In gigabit 5G transmissions, a transport block is segmented into tens of LDPC code blocks (e.g. 24 blocks). In traditional TB-based HARQ, if even a single code block is corrupted, the entire 24-block transport block must be retransmitted over the air.\nWith CBG configuration (`maxCodeBlockGroupsPerTransportBlock` ∈ {2, 4, 6, 8}):\n- Code blocks are clustered into up to 8 CBGs.\n- The UE feeds back a multi-bit HARQ-ACK bitmap (1 bit per CBG).\n- On receiving NACK for CBG 3, gNodeB retransmits ONLY CBG 3 via the CBGTI field in DCI.\nSaves up to 75-80% radio bandwidth under high-BLER channel conditions!", "keyFormulas": ["CBG_count = min(M, C), where M ∈ {2, 4, 6, 8} and C is code block count", "CBGTI (CBG Transmission Info): bitmap indicating retransmitted CBGs", "CBGFI (CBG Flushing Out Info): 1 bit indicating if soft buffer should be flushed"], "memoryAids": ["Why return the entire textbook when only Chapter 3 has a typo? CBG retransmits only the corrupted chapters!"], "pitfalls": ["DCI bit overhead increases by up to 8 bits for CBGTI and 1 bit for CBGFI.", "Ignoring CBGFI causing incorrect soft buffer combining."], "codeSnippet": "struct CbgConfig {\n    uint8_t num_cbgs;\n    uint8_t cbg_ack_mask; // Bitmask of ACKs per CBG\n    \n    bool all_acknowledged() const {\n        uint8_t expected_mask = (1 << num_cbgs) - 1;\n        return (cbg_ack_mask & expected_mask) == expected_mask;\n    }\n};"}, {"id": "w5_t3", "title": "Type-1 Semi-Static HARQ-ACK Codebook Determination", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.213 §9.2.3", "explanation": "How the UE constructs the HARQ-ACK payload to transmit on PUCCH:\n- **Type-1 (Semi-Static) Codebook**:\n  - The UE evaluates all theoretical candidate PDSCH reception occasions that could report HARQ-ACK in the target PUCCH slot, based on RRC set of K1 values and TDRA SLIV table.\n  - If a candidate occasion was NOT scheduled by the gNodeB, the UE inserts a NACK.\n  - **Pro**: Invariant to missed DCIs (both gNodeB and UE agree on exact bit positions regardless of channel drops).\n  - **Con**: Inefficient overhead when traffic is sparse (e.g. sending 16 bits of NACKs when only 1 TB was scheduled).", "keyFormulas": ["Codebook size O_ACK = ∑_CC ∑_K1 M_candidate", "Fixed grid indexing ensures alignment between UE and gNodeB"], "memoryAids": ["Semi-Static is the reserved seating chart: every seat is assigned beforehand. If you don't show up, your seat is marked absent (NACK)."], "pitfalls": ["High uplink PUCCH coding rate and coverage reduction in Carrier Aggregation due to bloated semi-static codebook size."], "codeSnippet": "// Semi-static codebook slot checker\nbool is_candidate_occasion(uint8_t slot, const std::vector<uint8_t>& k1_set, uint8_t pucch_slot) {\n    for (uint8_t k1 : k1_set) {\n        if (slot + k1 == pucch_slot) return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n}"}, {"id": "w5_t4", "title": "Type-2 Dynamic HARQ-ACK Codebook: cDAI & tDAI Mechanics", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.213 §9.2.3", "explanation": "Dynamically sizes the HARQ-ACK payload to report ONLY the transmissions actually scheduled.\nUses two fields in DCI:\n- **Counter DAI (cDAI, 2 bits)**: Accumulates the count of scheduled grants up to the current serving cell.\n- **Total DAI (tDAI, 2 bits)**: Indicates the total count across all carriers up to the current monitoring occasion.\n- **Modulo-4 Arithmetic**: Values map to {1, 2, 3, 4} ≡ {00, 01, 10, 11}_2.\n- **Missed DCI Detection**: If UE receives cDAI = 1 and next cDAI = 3, it detects that DCI with cDAI = 2 was dropped, inserts NACK at position 2, preserving bit alignment!", "keyFormulas": ["V_DAI = (N - 1) mod 4 + 1", "Missed DCI condition: (cDAI_curr - cDAI_prev - 1) mod 4 > 0", "Payload size: strictly matches Total DAI count"], "memoryAids": ["cDAI is your ticket number in line; tDAI is the total number of tickets called today. If ticket 2 is missing between 1 and 3, ticket 2 was dropped!"], "pitfalls": ["In Carrier Aggregation, if the LAST scheduled DCI is lost, UE cannot detect it with cDAI alone — tDAI from other carriers is required to catch trailing losses."], "codeSnippet": "// Type-2 DAI Gap Detector\nuint8_t detect_missed_grants(uint8_t prev_cdai, uint8_t curr_cdai) {\n    // Both 1-based (1, 2, 3, 4)\n    uint8_t diff = (curr_cdai + 4 - prev_cdai) % 4;\n    if (diff == 0) return 0;\n    return diff - 1; // Number of lost grants between them\n}"}]}, {"week": 6, "phase": 2, "phaseTitle": "DL Physical Procedures & Resource Allocation", "title": "Beam Management, TCI States, QCL & Systems", "estimatedHours": 8, "specs": ["TS 38.214 §5.1.5, §5.2.2", "TS 38.321 §5.17", "ISO C++17/20"], "summary": "Master massive MIMO beam management (P-1, P-2, P-3), Beam Failure Recovery (BFR), TCI states, QCL Types A/B/C/D, Outer Loop Link Adaptation (OLLA), and lock-free SPSC ring buffers in modern C++.", "deliverable": "C++ OLLA link adapter, TCI state manager, and lock-free SPSC queue with acquire/release memory semantics.", "topics": [{"id": "w6_t1", "title": "Beam Management: P-1/P-2/P-3 Sweeping & Beam Failure Recovery", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.2, TS 38.321 §5.17", "explanation": "Massive MIMO and mmWave directional transmissions:\n- **P-1 (Beam Selection)**: Simultaneous Tx and Rx sweeping across wide SSB/CSI-RS beams for initial UE pairing.\n- **P-2 (gNodeB Beam Refinement)**: Narrow gNodeB beam sweeping with fixed UE Rx beam.\n- **P-3 (UE Beam Refinement)**: Fixed gNodeB Tx beam with narrow UE Rx beam sweeping.\n\n**Beam Failure Recovery (BFR)**:\nWhen all candidate serving beams fall below threshold Q_out (hypothetical PDCCH BLER ≥ 10%), `beamFailureInstanceMaxCount` triggers BFR. The UE selects a new candidate SSB/CSI-RS with L1-RSRP ≥ Q_in, transmits a dedicated PRACH preamble on the new beam, and monitors a dedicated BFR search space.", "keyFormulas": ["L1-RSRP ≥ rsrp-ThresholdSSB", "Timer: beamFailureRecoveryTimer", "P-1 = Coarse pair, P-2 = gNodeB fine, P-3 = UE fine"], "memoryAids": ["P-1 Pairs you, P-2 Pinpoints base station beam, P-3 Pinpoints UE beam. BFR is your emergency reboot when beams drop."], "pitfalls": ["Slow BFR timer configuration causing prolonged radio link failure (RLF).", "Scheduling PDSCH on a failed beam before BFR completes."], "codeSnippet": "struct BeamContext {\n    uint8_t ssb_index;\n    int16_t l1_rsrp_dbm;\n    bool    is_failed;\n    \n    void update_measurement(int16_t rsrp, int16_t threshold_qout) {\n        l1_rsrp_dbm = rsrp;\n        is_failed = (rsrp < threshold_qout);\n    }\n};"}, {"id": "w6_t2", "title": "TCI States & Quasi-Co-Location (QCL) Types A/B/C/D", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.1.5", "explanation": "QCL defines when channel properties of one reference signal can be used to estimate another:\n- **QCL-TypeA**: Doppler shift, Doppler spread, Average delay, Delay spread (full multipath).\n- **QCL-TypeB**: Doppler shift, Doppler spread.\n- **QCL-TypeC**: Doppler shift, Average delay.\n- **QCL-TypeD**: **Spatial Rx parameter** (Angle of Arrival / same Rx beamforming weights for mmWave!).\n\n**TCI State Signaling**:\n- RRC configures up to 128 TCI states.\n- MAC CE activates a subset of up to 8 active TCI states.\n- DCI 1_1 uses a 3-bit field to dynamically switch between the 8 active beams slot-by-slot.", "keyFormulas": ["TCI field in DCI: 3 bits (≤ 8 active states)", "MAC CE activation bitmap: 8 octets", "QCL-TypeD = Spatial Rx beamforming weights"], "memoryAids": ["Type A: All multi-path & Doppler. Type D: Direction! (Spatial Rx beam).", "RRC configures 128, MAC CE activates 8, DCI picks 1."], "pitfalls": ["Scheduling PDSCH with Type D on FR1 where spatial Rx beamforming is not applicable.", "Scheduling before MAC CE activation completes."], "codeSnippet": "struct TciState {\n    uint8_t tci_id;\n    uint8_t source_rs_type; // 0=SSB, 1=CSI-RS\n    uint8_t source_rs_id;\n    uint8_t qcl_type;       // 0=A, 1=B, 2=C, 3=D\n};"}, {"id": "w6_t3", "title": "Outer Loop Link Adaptation (OLLA) & CQI Processing", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.2.2 & Scheduler Algorithms", "explanation": "Inner Loop Link Adaptation maps periodic UE CQI reports (1..15) to spectral efficiency and MCS (0..28) based on static AWGN lookup tables.\nOuter Loop Link Adaptation (OLLA) dynamically adjusts effective SINR:\nSINR_eff = SINR_CQI - Δ_OLLA\n\n**Closed-Loop Step Dynamics**:\n- On HARQ ACK: Δ_OLLA = Δ_OLLA - Δ_down\n- On HARQ NACK: Δ_OLLA = Δ_OLLA + Δ_up\nTo converge to target 10% BLER:\nΔ_down = Δ_up · (BLER_target / (1 - BLER_target)) = Δ_up · (0.10 / 0.90) = Δ_up / 9.\nExample: Δ_up = 1.0 dB implies Δ_down = 0.111 dB.", "keyFormulas": ["SINR_eff = SINR_CQI - Δ_OLLA", "Step ratio: Δ_down = Δ_up · (BLER_target / (1 - BLER_target))", "Target BLER = 10%"], "memoryAids": ["Step up fast on NACK (+1 dB), step down slowly on ACK (-0.11 dB). Ratio 1 to 9 guarantees 10% target BLER!"], "pitfalls": ["Setting Δ_up too large causes massive MCS oscillations.", "Failing to clamp Δ_OLLA between [-6 dB, +6 dB] leads to divergence."], "codeSnippet": "class OllaController {\n    float delta_olla_db_{0.0f};\n    const float delta_up_db_{1.0f};\n    const float delta_down_db_{1.0f / 9.0f}; // 10% target BLER\n\npublic:\n    void on_harq_feedback(bool is_ack) {\n        if (is_ack) {\n            delta_olla_db_ -= delta_down_db_;\n        } else {\n            delta_olla_db_ += delta_up_db_;\n        }\n        // Clamp to prevent unbounded drift\n        delta_olla_db_ = std::clamp(delta_olla_db_, -6.0f, 6.0f);\n    }\n\n    float get_offset() const { return delta_olla_db_; }\n};"}, {"id": "w6_t4", "title": "Low-Level Systems: Lock-Free SPSC Ring Buffer with Acquire/Release", "specClause": "Modern Systems & Memory Ordering / ISO C++17/20", "explanation": "Multi-threaded baseband communication between PHY receiver threads and MAC scheduler threads cannot use mutexes (`std::mutex`) due to kernel context-switch latency (~2-5 µs) and risk of priority inversion.\nSingle-Producer Single-Consumer (SPSC) ring buffers with `std::atomic` and `memory_order_acquire` / `memory_order_release` allow zero-lock communication.\n\n**Why Acquire/Release instead of `seq_cst`**:\n- `seq_cst` forces expensive CPU bus-lock cycles (e.g. `MFENCE` on x86, `DMB ISH` on ARM).\n- Acquire/release provides pairwise synchronization: memory writes preceding the release store in producer are guaranteed to be visible to the consumer after its acquire load.", "keyFormulas": ["Capacity must be power of two: (Capacity & (Capacity - 1)) == 0", "Index masking: head & (Capacity - 1)", "Acquire/Release creates pairwise synchronization edge"], "memoryAids": ["Release seals the envelope (writes finished). Acquire opens the envelope (reads begin). Zero mutexes, zero context switches!"], "pitfalls": ["Putting head and tail in the same cache line (causes devastating false sharing).", "Using non-power-of-two capacity requiring expensive modulo division."], "codeSnippet": "#include <atomic>\n#include <cstdint>\n#include <optional>\n\ntemplate <typename T, size_t Capacity>\nclass LockFreeSPSCQueue {\n    static_assert((Capacity & (Capacity - 1)) == 0, \"Capacity must be power of two!\");\n    \n    alignas(64) std::atomic<size_t> head_{0}; // Written only by Producer\n    alignas(64) std::atomic<size_t> tail_{0}; // Written only by Consumer\n    alignas(64) T ring_buffer_[Capacity];\n\npublic:\n    bool enqueue(const T& item) {\n        const size_t current_head = head_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);\n        const size_t current_tail = tail_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);\n        \n        if ((current_head - current_tail) >= Capacity) return false; // Full\n        \n        ring_buffer_[current_head & (Capacity - 1)] = item;\n        head_.store(current_head + 1, std::memory_order_release);\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    std::optional<T> dequeue() {\n        const size_t current_tail = tail_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);\n        const size_t current_head = head_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);\n        \n        if (current_head == current_tail) return std::nullopt; // Empty\n        \n        T item = ring_buffer_[current_tail & (Capacity - 1)];\n        tail_.store(current_tail + 1, std::memory_order_release);\n        return item;\n    }\n};"}]}, {"week": 7, "phase": 3, "phaseTitle": "Multi-Carrier & Dual Connectivity Integration", "title": "NR Carrier Aggregation, CIF & Mixed Numerologies", "estimatedHours": 8, "specs": ["TS 38.300 §6.4", "TS 38.321 §5.13", "TS 38.213 §10.1"], "summary": "Master Carrier Aggregation architecture (PCell vs SCell), SCell activation/deactivation MAC CE, cross-carrier scheduling with CIF (TS 38.213 §10.1), and mixed numerology slot alignments.", "deliverable": "C++ SCell state machine, cross-carrier CIF grant mapper, and mixed numerology slot translator.", "topics": [{"id": "w7_t1", "title": "NR Carrier Aggregation Architecture: PCell, PSCell & SCells", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.300 §6.4, TS 38.321 §5.13", "explanation": "Carrier Aggregation aggregates multiple component carriers (up to 16 in NR) to deliver multi-gigabit throughput:\n- **Single MAC Entity**: A single MAC entity at the gNodeB and UE controls scheduling across all aggregated CCs.\n- **Primary Cell (PCell)**: Handles RRC connection, PUCCH transmission (by default), security, and initial access. Never deactivated.\n- **Secondary Cells (SCells)**: Provide supplementary DL and/or UL data capacity.\n- **SCell State Machine**: DEACTIVATED ↔ ACTIVATED ↔ DORMANT (Rel-16).", "keyFormulas": ["Max aggregated carriers = 16 CCs", "Max aggregated bandwidth = 16 · 400 MHz = 6.4 GHz (FR2)", "PCell is the persistent anchor"], "memoryAids": ["PCell is the Anchor (never deactivated); SCells are the Boosters (activated when heavy cargo arrives)."], "pitfalls": ["Attempting to deactivate the PCell (PCell cannot be deactivated).", "Assuming SCell can transmit PUCCH without explicit PUCCH-SCell configuration."], "codeSnippet": "struct CarrierConfig {\n    uint8_t  cell_index;\n    bool     is_pcell;\n    uint32_t dl_bandwidth_mhz;\n    uint8_t  mu;\n};"}, {"id": "w7_t2", "title": "SCell Activation/Deactivation State Machine & MAC CE", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.13", "explanation": "SCell Activation/Deactivation MAC CE contains a 1-octet (for SCell index 1-7) or 4-octet (for SCell index 1-31) bitmap:\n- Bit C_i = 1 activates SCell i; C_i = 0 deactivates it.\n- Timer: `sCellDeactivationTimer` per SCell. Reset upon receiving PDCCH grant on that SCell.\n- **Upon Deactivation**: UE stops PDCCH monitoring on SCell, stops SRS transmissions, stops CSI reporting for SCell, flushes all HARQ buffers for that SCell, and clears configured grants. Power saving is significant, but reactivation latency is 24-34 ms.", "keyFormulas": ["Bitmap: C_i = 1 (Activate), C_i = 0 (Deactivate)", "Reactivation delay: 24 to 34 ms", "Timer: sCellDeactivationTimer (20 ms to 1280 ms)"], "memoryAids": ["Bit 1 turns the SCell ON, Bit 0 turns it OFF. Timer resets on every grant; silence puts it to sleep."], "pitfalls": ["Failing to flush HARQ buffers on SCell deactivation leads to stale soft-combining errors when the cell is reactivated."], "codeSnippet": "void process_scell_act_deact_ce(uint8_t bitmap, SCellState scells[7]) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < 7; i++) {\n        bool activate = (bitmap & (1 << (i + 1))) != 0;\n        if (activate) scells[i].transition_to_active();\n        else scells[i].is_activated = false;\n    }\n}"}, {"id": "w7_t3", "title": "Cross-Carrier Scheduling with CIF (TS 38.213 §10.1)", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.213 §10.1, TS 38.212 §7.3", "explanation": "In Carrier Aggregation, self-scheduling allows each carrier's PDCCH to schedule its own PDSCH. Cross-carrier scheduling allows PDCCH transmitted on one carrier (e.g. PCell at lower frequency with better coverage) to schedule PDSCH on another carrier (e.g. SCell at higher frequency).\n- **Carrier Indicator Field (CIF)**: A 3-bit field prepended to DCI formats 0_1 and 1_1, identifying the target serving cell index (CIF ∈ [0, 7]).\n- **DCI Size Alignment**: DCI sizes across scheduled carriers are aligned with zero-padding to minimize blind decoding attempts (max 4 unique DCI sizes monitored per slot).", "keyFormulas": ["CIF field size = 3 bits (≤ 8 carriers)", "DCI size budget: max 4 unique sizes monitored per slot to protect UE blind decodes"], "memoryAids": ["CIF is the shipping label: PDCCH printed on Carrier A, package delivered on Carrier B."], "pitfalls": ["DCI format 1_0 cannot carry CIF (fallback DCI is strictly self-scheduling).", "Exceeding the 4-DCI-size blind decode limit."], "codeSnippet": "struct DciFormat1_1 {\n    uint8_t  cif; // 3-bit Carrier Indicator Field\n    uint16_t riv;\n    uint8_t  sliv;\n    uint8_t  mcs;\n    uint8_t  harq_pid;\n    uint8_t  ndi;\n    uint8_t  rv;\n};"}, {"id": "w7_t4", "title": "Mixed Numerology Cross-Carrier Scheduling Mechanics", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.1.2.1, TS 38.213 §10.1", "explanation": "When cross-carrier scheduling across different numerologies (e.g. PCell at μ = 0, SCell at μ = 1):\n- **Case 1 (μ_PDCCH < μ_PDSCH)**: 1 PDCCH slot covers 2 PDSCH slots. DCI indicates which sub-slot (2n or 2n+1) is scheduled.\n- **Case 2 (μ_PDCCH > μ_PDSCH)**: Multiple PDCCH slots fall within one PDSCH slot. Scheduler must avoid overlapping allocations.\n- **Processing Time (Np,1)**: Time between PDCCH end and PDSCH start must satisfy UE preparation time Np,1 based on min(μ_PDCCH, μ_PDSCH).", "keyFormulas": ["Slot Ratio = 2^(μ_PDSCH - μ_PDCCH)", "Preparation Timeline: T_prep ≥ N_p,1 · T_slot^min"], "memoryAids": ["Fast schedules slow with ease; slow schedules fast by picking which half-slot to hit!"], "pitfalls": ["Violating Np,1 processing time: scheduling PDSCH too close to PDCCH on higher numerologies causes UE demodulation abort."], "codeSnippet": "uint32_t map_subslot(uint32_t pdcch_slot, uint8_t mu_pdcch, uint8_t mu_pdsch, uint8_t subslot_idx) {\n    uint32_t ratio = 1 << (mu_pdsch - mu_pdcch);\n    return pdcch_slot * ratio + subslot_idx;\n}"}]}, {"week": 8, "phase": 3, "phaseTitle": "Multi-Carrier & Dual Connectivity Integration", "title": "Multi-RAT Dual Connectivity (EN-DC Option 3x & NR-DC)", "estimatedHours": 8, "specs": ["TS 37.340", "TS 38.300 §6.5", "TS 38.425 DDDS"], "summary": "Master EN-DC Option 3x Non-Standalone architecture, split bearer data flow at NR PDCP, TS 38.425 Downlink Data Delivery Status (DDDS) flow control, and inter-site CA coordination using ML.", "deliverable": "C++ TS 38.425 DDDS frame serializer, and real-time PUCCH allocation predictor.", "topics": [{"id": "w8_t1", "title": "EN-DC Option 3x Architecture: MeNB, SgNB & Bearers", "specClause": "3GPP TS 37.340, TS 38.300 §6.5", "explanation": "E-UTRA-NR Dual Connectivity (EN-DC Option 3x) is the dominant Non-Standalone (NSA) 5G deployment model:\n1. **Control Plane**: Master eNodeB (MeNB, 4G LTE) anchors S1-MME to the EPC. SgNB (5G NR) coordinates with MeNB over X2-C.\n2. **User Plane**: S1-U user plane traffic from the Serving Gateway terminates directly at the 5G SgNB.\n3. **SCG Split Bearer Flow**:\n   - Packets enter NR PDCP at the SgNB, where PDCP Sequence Numbers are assigned.\n   - SgNB splits traffic: sending high-throughput chunks directly over 5G RLC/MAC/PHY, and routing secondary chunks over the X2-U interface to the MeNB for 4G LTE transmission.", "keyFormulas": ["Control Anchor = 4G MeNB (S1-C)", "Data Anchor = 5G SgNB (S1-U to NR PDCP)", "Bearer Types: MCG Bearer, SCG Bearer, Split Bearer"], "memoryAids": ["Option 3x: 5G SgNB holds the steering wheel for data (NR PDCP), while 4G MeNB holds the anchor for signaling."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing Option 3 (data terminates at 4G MeNB and saturates 4G backhaul) with Option 3x (data terminates at 5G SgNB)."], "codeSnippet": "enum class BearerType : uint8_t {\n    MCG_BEARER   = 0, // 4G LTE only\n    SCG_BEARER   = 1, // 5G NR only\n    SPLIT_BEARER = 2  // NR PDCP split across 4G and 5G\n};"}, {"id": "w8_t2", "title": "Split Bearer Data Flow at NR PDCP & TS 38.425 DDDS", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.425 (NR User Plane Protocol)", "explanation": "In Option 3x SCG split bearer, NR PDCP at the SgNB receives downlink IP packets, assigns PDCP SNs, and splits traffic between 5G radio and X2-U to the MeNB.\nTo prevent MeNB buffers from overflowing or underflowing, 3GPP TS 38.425 defines the Downlink Data Delivery Status (DDDS) frame format:\n- MeNB sends periodic feedback to SgNB reporting:\n  1. 'Highest Successfully Delivered PDCP SN'\n  2. 'Desired Buffer Size' in bytes\n  3. 'Lost Packet Range'\nSgNB adjusts forwarding rate dynamically using token-bucket flow control.", "keyFormulas": ["DDDS Frame Type = 1 (User Plane Feedback)", "Flow Control Rate: Rate_target = Desired_Buffer_Size / RTT_X2"], "memoryAids": ["DDDS is the flow control valve between 5G and 4G: 'I have 50 KB space left, send me more' or 'Stop, I am choked!'"], "pitfalls": ["Slow X2-U feedback causing packet out-of-order reordering at UE and TCP throughput collapse.", "Assuming LTE PDCP can handle NR split (NR PDCP must be used)."], "codeSnippet": "struct DddsFrame {\n    uint8_t  pdu_type;                // Type 1\n    uint32_t highest_delivered_pdcp_sn;\n    uint32_t desired_buffer_size_bytes;\n    uint16_t num_lost_packets;\n};"}, {"id": "w8_t3", "title": "NR-DC Architecture & Multi-Node Coordination", "specClause": "3GPP TS 37.340 §4.3, TS 38.300 §6.5", "explanation": "NR Dual Connectivity connects a UE simultaneously to two separate 5G gNodeBs: Master Node (MN) running FR1 sub-6 GHz and Secondary Node (SN) running FR2 mmWave, connected to a 5G Core (5GC).\n- **Difference between CA and DC**:\n  - CA: Single MAC entity, tightly coupled baseband, microsecond-level synchronization.\n  - DC: Two independent MAC entities with separate schedulers, operating over non-ideal backhaul with millisecond latency.\nEach cell group maintains its own BWP configurations, HARQ entities, and PRACH resources.", "keyFormulas": ["Throughput_total = Throughput_MN + Throughput_SN", "Backhaul latency: 10-20 ms (Xn non-ideal)"], "memoryAids": ["CA is one brain controlling two hands; DC is two distinct brains coordinating via phone calls."], "pitfalls": ["Attempting tight subframe-by-subframe cross-carrier scheduling across DC cell groups (impossible due to Xn backhaul latency)."], "codeSnippet": "struct DualConnectivityUeContext {\n    uint32_t ue_id;\n    uint8_t  mcg_cell_id;\n    uint8_t  scg_cell_id;\n    bool     is_nr_dc;\n};"}, {"id": "w8_t4", "title": "Inter-Site CA Coordination over Non-Ideal Backhaul & ML", "specClause": "Machine Learning in L2 Architecture / Candidate Research", "explanation": "When Carrier Aggregation spans geographically separated sites (Inter-Site CA) connected over non-ideal IP backhaul (10-20 ms delay), PCell must allocate PUCCH resources for SCell HARQ-ACK without real-time visibility into SCell's per-slot scheduling decisions.\n- Static allocation wastes 58% of uplink PUCCH capacity; reactive allocation starves SCell.\n- **Machine Learning Solution (developed by Akhzar Farhan at IISc/Samsung)**:\n  - Deploying an ultra-compact Treelite compiled decision tree (~280 KB native C binary) on PCell MAC.\n  - Predicts SCell scheduling probability in **0.42 ms** based on historical buffer depth and flow variance.\n  - Raised scheduling hit rate to 91% and cut PUCCH resource waste to 6.4%!", "keyFormulas": ["Inference latency: T_inf = 0.42 ms < 1.0 ms TTI", "Memory footprint: ~280 KB SRAM", "Hit rate boost: 42% → 91%"], "memoryAids": ["Predict before you allocate! Machine Learning eliminates the blind spot caused by 15 ms backhaul lag."], "pitfalls": ["Deploying heavy Python/PyTorch inference engines inside the real-time scheduling loop (violates deterministic sub-millisecond execution)."], "codeSnippet": "// Ultra-fast C predictor compiled via Treelite\nextern \"C\" float predict_scell_schedule_prob(const float* features, int num_features);\n\nbool should_allocate_pucch_format3(const float* buffer_features) {\n    float prob = predict_scell_schedule_prob(buffer_features, 8);\n    return (prob > 0.65f); // 65% confidence threshold\n}"}]}, {"week": 9, "phase": 4, "phaseTitle": "O-RAN, Open Stacks & System Design Mastery", "title": "O-RAN Split 7-2x, SCF FAPI & Open-Source Stacks", "estimatedHours": 8, "specs": ["O-RAN WG4 Fronthaul", "SCF 222 v10.02", "OpenAirInterface", "srsRAN"], "summary": "Master O-RAN Split 7-2x architecture (C/U/S/M-Planes), Small Cell Forum FAPI interface messages, and code walkthroughs of OpenAirInterface (gNB_scheduler_dlsch.c) and srsRAN (mac_dl_processor.cc).", "deliverable": "C++ FAPI message structure serializer, and OAI scheduler code analysis report.", "topics": [{"id": "w9_t1", "title": "O-RAN Functional Split 7-2x Architecture: O-DU vs O-RU", "specClause": "O-RAN Alliance Working Group 4 (Fronthaul Interface)", "explanation": "Disaggregation of the base station into O-CU (RRC/PDCP), O-DU (RLC/MAC/High-PHY), and O-RU (Low-PHY/RF):\n- **Split 7-2x Boundary**: High-PHY (RE mapping, FFT/IFFT, modulation, LDPC coding) resides in the O-DU; Low-PHY (digital beamforming, PRACH filtering, DAC/ADC) resides in the O-RU.\n- **The 4 Planes**:\n  1. **Control Plane (C-Plane)**: Scheduling commands, PRB allocations, beamforming weights.\n  2. **User Plane (U-Plane)**: Frequency-domain IQ data transported over eCPRI.\n  3. **Synchronization Plane (S-Plane)**: IEEE 1588 PTP nanosecond-precision timing.\n  4. **Management Plane (M-Plane)**: NETCONF/YANG configuration of O-RU parameters.", "keyFormulas": ["Fronthaul compression: Transmits frequency-domain IQ samples, reducing bandwidth from ~40 Gbps to ~10 Gbps for 100 MHz 64T64R", "Timing accuracy: S-Plane synchronization within ±130 ns"], "memoryAids": ["Split 7-2x: O-DU thinks (MAC & Coding); O-RU radiates (Antennas & Beams). eCPRI connects them."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming Split 7-2x sends time-domain samples (it sends frequency-domain unmodulated/modulated symbols).", "Jitter on S-Plane violating 130 ns time alignment."], "codeSnippet": "struct EcPriHeader {\n    uint8_t  protocol_rev;\n    uint8_t  message_type; // 0=IQ data, 2=RTC Control\n    uint16_t payload_size;\n    uint16_t pc_id;        // Packet Component ID\n    uint16_t seq_id;       // Sequence ID\n};"}, {"id": "w9_t2", "title": "Small Cell Forum FAPI Interface (SCF 222 v10.02)", "specClause": "Small Cell Forum FAPI Technical Report (SCF 222 v10.02)", "explanation": "Standardized software API between Layer 2 (MAC) and Layer 1 (PHY) baseband firmware.\nKey Message Flows:\n- `DL_TTI.request`: Sent by MAC to PHY every slot. Contains resource assignments for PDCCH, PDSCH, and CSI-RS (SLIV, PRBs, MCS, precoding).\n- `TX_Data.request`: Contains the actual MAC PDU payload bytes to be modulated and transmitted.\n- `UL_TTI.request`: Instructs PHY which PUSCH, PUCCH, and PRACH resources to listen for in the upcoming slot.\n- `UCI.indication`: Delivers decoded Uplink Control Information (HARQ ACK/NACK, CQI, PMI, RI) from PHY to MAC.", "keyFormulas": ["Slot dispatch pipeline: MAC prepares DL_TTI.request + TX_Data.request at slot n - k so PHY can execute DMA before slot n", "SCF 222 defines exact byte offsets for interoperability"], "memoryAids": ["DL_TTI is the blueprint; TX_Data is the brick. UCI is the customer review (ACK/NACK) coming back."], "pitfalls": ["Sending TX_Data.request without matching PDU count in DL_TTI.request (causes baseband crash / buffer desync)."], "codeSnippet": "struct FapiDlTtiRequest {\n    uint16_t sfn;\n    uint16_t slot;\n    uint8_t  num_pdus;\n    // PDU descriptors follow in contiguous memory\n};\n\nstruct FapiTxDataRequest {\n    uint16_t sfn;\n    uint16_t slot;\n    uint8_t  num_pdus;\n    // Payload byte pointers follow\n};"}, {"id": "w9_t3", "title": "OpenAirInterface (OAI) gNodeB Scheduler: `gNB_scheduler_dlsch.c`", "specClause": "OpenAirInterface 5G NR Software Architecture", "explanation": "Deep dive into open-source 5G baseband codebase OpenAirInterface (OAI):\nKey source file: `openair2/LAYER2/NR_MAC_gNB/gNB_scheduler_dlsch.c`.\n- **Execution Flow**:\n  1. Entrypoint `nr_schedule_ue_spec()` triggered on each slot tick by timer or L1 interrupt.\n  2. Calls `nr_fill_DLSCH_dci()`: encodes DCI format 1_1 with RIV, SLIV, MCS, and HARQ process ID.\n  3. Calls `nr_generate_dlsch_pdu()`: pulls data from RLC buffers, encodes MAC subheaders, and places them into the FAPI TX PDU buffer.\n  4. Tracks HARQ state in `NR_UE_harq_t`: updates round count, toggles NDI, and stores redundancy version.", "keyFormulas": ["OAI Function Chain: nr_schedule_response() → nr_schedule_ue_spec() → nr_fill_DLSCH_dci() → nr_generate_dlsch_pdu()"], "memoryAids": ["Inspect OAI to see 3GPP specs in action: RIV, SLIV, and FAPI are real C structs, not abstract concepts."], "pitfalls": ["Race conditions between L1 RX thread and scheduler thread if mutex locking around NR_UE_info_t is modified improperly."], "codeSnippet": "// Excerpt from OAI gNB_scheduler_dlsch.c logic:\nvoid nr_fill_dlsch_dci(dci_pdu_rel15_t *dci_pdu, uint16_t rnti, uint8_t harq_pid, \n                       uint8_t ndi, uint8_t rv, uint16_t riv, uint8_t sliv, uint8_t mcs) {\n    dci_pdu->frequency_domain_assignment.val = riv;\n    dci_pdu->time_domain_assignment.val = sliv;\n    dci_pdu->mcs = mcs;\n    dci_pdu->harq_pid = harq_pid;\n    dci_pdu->ndi = ndi;\n    dci_pdu->rv = rv;\n}"}, {"id": "w9_t4", "title": "srsRAN 5G Architecture: `mac_dl_processor.cc` & Pipelining", "specClause": "srsRAN Project 5G Architecture", "explanation": "srsRAN Project is a modular, high-performance C++ 5G baseband stack.\nKey source file: `lib/src/mac/mac_dl_processor.cc`.\n- **Software Highlights**:\n  - Zero dynamic memory allocation in the fast path: uses pre-allocated object pools and intrusive pointer reference counting.\n  - Multi-threaded slot pipelining: Core 1 processes slot n MAC scheduling, Core 2 executes slot n-1 L1 modulation, Core 3 handles slot n+1 user metric sorting.\n  - Lock-free message passing using bounded ring buffers.\n  - Clean separation between RRC configuration layer and real-time MAC worker loops.", "keyFormulas": ["Pipeline: 3-stage worker pool maintains 100% CPU utilization across multi-core server processors"], "memoryAids": ["srsRAN is modern clean C++: object pools, intrusive pointers, and multi-stage pipelining."], "pitfalls": ["Missing memory barriers when passing task descriptors between worker threads causes stale slot configurations."], "codeSnippet": "// srsRAN slot pipelining pattern\nclass MacDlProcessor {\npublic:\n    void process_slot(uint32_t slot_idx) {\n        collect_uci_feedback(slot_idx);\n        sort_candidate_users(slot_idx);\n        allocate_resources(slot_idx);\n        dispatch_fapi_pdus(slot_idx);\n    }\n};"}]}, {"week": 10, "phase": 4, "phaseTitle": "O-RAN, Open Stacks & System Design Mastery", "title": "Diagnostic Field Log Triage, System Design & Interview Mastery", "estimatedHours": 8, "specs": ["Qualcomm QXDM", "Field Debugging", "German Telecom Market"], "summary": "Master commercial field debugging using Qualcomm QXDM diagnostic packets, execute the 4-step HARQ NACK storm root cause decision tree, review the 125 µs scheduler system design, and master candidate positioning for Ericsson Germany and European employers.", "deliverable": "QXDM packet decoding cheat sheet, HARQ NACK storm diagnostic decision tree, and German telecom CV narrative.", "topics": [{"id": "w10_t1", "title": "Qualcomm QXDM Log Analysis Playbook & Packet Codes", "specClause": "Qualcomm Diagnostic Protocol / 3GPP Field Debugging", "explanation": "How tier-1 modem and network engineers debug commercial issues:\n- `0xB887` (**NR5G MAC PDSCH Status**): Real-time TB size, MIMO layers, modulation order (QPSK to 256QAM), and HARQ outcome (ACK/NACK/DTX).\n- `0xB883` (**NR5G MAC UL Schedule Report**): Real-time UL grants, PUSCH TBS, and BSR reporting.\n- `0xB821` (**NR5G RRC OTA Packet**): Complete ASN.1 decoded control messages (`RRCReconfiguration`, `ServingCellConfig`, `BWP-Downlink`).\n- `0xB825` (**NR5G RRC Configuration Info**): Active cell groups, bearer mappings.\n- `0xB97F` (**NR5G ML1 Searcher Measurement**): Beam tracking, L1-RSRP for all SSB beams, active TCI states.\n- `0xB173` / `0xB14D` / `0xB139`: LTE counterpart packets for cross-RAT EN-DC debugging.", "keyFormulas": ["0xB887 = PDSCH status, 0xB821 = RRC signaling, 0xB97F = Beam measurements", "DTX = DCI missed by UE; NACK = DCI decoded, PDSCH CRC failed"], "memoryAids": ["0xB821 configures the pipe (RRC); 0xB887 monitors the flow (MAC DL); 0xB97F aims the flashlight (Beams)."], "pitfalls": ["Mistaking DTX (UE didn't decode DCI at all) for NACK (UE decoded DCI but failed CRC check). DTX means PDCCH failure; NACK means PDSCH failure!"], "codeSnippet": "struct QxdmPdschStatus_0xB887 {\n    uint32_t sfn_slot;\n    uint8_t  harq_pid;\n    uint8_t  mcs;\n    uint8_t  num_layers;\n    uint32_t tb_size_bytes;\n    uint8_t  crc_result; // 0=Pass, 1=Fail, 2=DTX\n};"}, {"id": "w10_t2", "title": "The HARQ NACK Storm Diagnostic Decision Tree", "specClause": "Field Troubleshooting & RCA Playbook", "explanation": "Systematic 4-step decision tree for resolving sudden DL throughput collapses and continuous NACK storms:\n- **Step 1: Check PDSCH CRC in 0xB887**:\n  - If CRC is FAIL: Real air-interface degradation. Proceed to Step 2.\n  - If CRC is PASS at UE, but gNodeB keeps retransmitting: Uplink feedback problem! UE is sending ACK, but gNodeB fails to decode PUCCH (interpreting DTX as NACK). Root cause: High UL interference or Timing Advance drift.\n- **Step 2: Check SNR & Interference in 0xB97F / 0xB821**:\n  - If SNR dropped sharply: External RF interference or beam misalignment.\n  - If SNR is high (> 20 dB) but BLER is 100%: Link adaptation failure! OLLA offset drifted, forcing MCS 27 on a channel that only supports MCS 16.\n- **Step 3: Check Cross-Carrier & BWP Configuration in 0xB821**:\n  - Verify TDRA SLIV validity and DCI alignment.\n- **Step 4: Check L1/L2 Latency & Scheduler Misses in baseband telemetry**:\n  - Inspect if O-DU missed the slot tick, resulting in empty/zero transmissions.", "keyFormulas": ["BLER = (N_NACK + N_DTX) / N_total · 100%", "Target BLER = 10%", "CRC Pass at UE + Retransmit at gNodeB ⇒ Uplink PUCCH failure"], "memoryAids": ["CRC Pass at UE + Retransmit at gNodeB = Uplink PUCCH dead! CRC Fail at UE + High SNR = OLLA broke the MCS!"], "pitfalls": ["Blaming the radio environment when the real root cause was an eNodeB/gNodeB scheduler bug misinterpreting PUCCH DTX."], "codeSnippet": "enum class RootCause {\n    RADIO_INTERFERENCE,\n    OLLA_MISCONFIGURATION,\n    UPLINK_PUCCH_FAILURE,\n    BEAM_MISALIGNMENT,\n    SCHEDULER_DEADLINE_MISS\n};\n\nRootCause diagnose_nack_storm(bool ue_crc_pass, float snr_db, bool is_gnb_retransmitting) {\n    if (ue_crc_pass && is_gnb_retransmitting) return RootCause::UPLINK_PUCCH_FAILURE;\n    if (!ue_crc_pass && snr_db > 20.0f) return RootCause::OLLA_MISCONFIGURATION;\n    return RootCause::RADIO_INTERFERENCE;\n}"}, {"id": "w10_t3", "title": "Telecom System Design: Scalable 125 µs MAC DL Scheduler", "specClause": "L2 Software Architecture / 3GPP gNodeB System Design", "explanation": "Complete system design for an enterprise-grade 5G NR MAC Downlink Scheduler operating under a 125 µs deadline at 120 kHz SCS (or 500 µs at 30 kHz SCS):\n- **Core Allocation**: Cores 2-7 isolated (`isolcpus`), Core 2 pins Feedback Ingestion, Core 3 pins Candidate Metric Sorting (AVX-512 Proportional Fair), Core 4 pins Resource Allocation, Core 5 pins FAPI Serialization.\n- **Memory Architecture**: Zero heap allocation in steady state. All UE contexts (up to 1,000 UEs) and packet descriptors pre-allocated in 1 GB hugepages.\n- **IPC**: Lock-free SPSC queues with acquire/release semantics between cores.\n- **5-Stage Pipeline**: 1) UCI feedback ingestion (15 µs) → 2) Candidate filtering (20 µs) → 3) Resource allocation (45 µs) → 4) PDU assembly (30 µs) → 5) DMA dispatch (15 µs margin).", "keyFormulas": ["Latency Budget: T_total = 15 + 20 + 45 + 30 = 110 µs < 125 µs", "Throughput: 1,000 UEs supported concurrently"], "memoryAids": ["5 stages, 4 cores, zero mallocs, 110 µs finish. Determinism is king."], "pitfalls": ["Using condition variables or sleeping locks to synchronize pipeline stages.", "Failing to lock memory (mlockall) leading to major page faults."], "codeSnippet": "class MacSchedulerPipeline {\n    LockFreeSPSCQueue<UciFeedback, 1024>   feedback_q_;\n    LockFreeSPSCQueue<CandidateList, 1024> candidate_q_;\n    LockFreeSPSCQueue<GrantList, 1024>     grant_q_;\n\npublic:\n    void execute_pipeline_tick(uint32_t slot) {\n        // Stage 1 -> Stage 2 -> Stage 3 -> Stage 4\n    }\n};"}, {"id": "w10_t4", "title": "Candidate Positioning, IISc ML Thesis Narrative & Germany Strategy", "specClause": "Career Strategy / German Job Market & EU Blue Card", "explanation": "Executive strategy for positioning Samsung SRI-B 4G LTE L2 expertise when targeting senior 5G NR Baseband and Cloud RAN roles in Germany (Ericsson Aachen/Frankfurt, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz Munich, Nokia Munich/Ulm) and Ericsson India.\n- **The Unfair Advantage**: 5+ years of hardened production LTE MAC DL debugging + M.Tech in Data Science from IISc Bangalore.\n- **The Core Bridge**: \"4G and 5G share the identical fundamental physics and scheduler objectives (fairness, throughput, latency), but 5G NR makes everything dynamic, flexible, and asynchronous (flexible numerologies, dynamic K1, BWP, CBG). Having lived through every LTE failure mode, I understand exactly *why* 5G was engineered this way and how to build and debug it.\"\n- **Connecting the IISc ML Thesis**: Explain the Treelite decision-tree deployment for Inter-Site CA PUCCH prediction (0.42 ms latency, 91% hit rate), demonstrating how ML can be applied within strict real-time RTOS constraints.\n- **Germany Relocation**: EU Blue Card fast-track roadmap (Section 81a AufenthG), Anabin database degree recognition, ATS-optimized German tech CV.", "keyFormulas": ["EU Blue Card threshold for IT/Engineering ≈ €45,300 (market offers: €85k - €115k)", "Fast-track permanent residency in 21-27 months with B1 German"], "memoryAids": ["Experience is the anchor; 5G specs are the sails; Machine Learning is the turbocharger."], "pitfalls": ["Sounding like a generic data scientist rather than a hardcore telecom systems engineer.", "Having an unformatted, narrative-heavy CV rather than a concise, ATS-scannable document."], "codeSnippet": "// German Interview Elevator Pitch Framework:\n// 1. \"I am a Lead Systems Engineer with 5+ years specializing in 3GPP MAC Downlink scheduling and Carrier Aggregation at Samsung SRI-B.\"\n// 2. \"I hold an M.Tech in Data Science from IISc Bangalore, where my thesis applied ML within sub-millisecond RTOS scheduling loops.\"\n// 3. \"I bridge low-level C++ systems determinism with deep 3GPP specification mastery across TS 38.321 and TS 38.214.\" "}]}], "evolutionMatrix": [{"dimension": "Subcarrier Spacing & Numerology", "lte": "Fixed 15 kHz Subcarrier Spacing (1 ms subframe, 1 ms TTI).", "nr": "Flexible numerology: Δf = 2^μ · 15 kHz (15, 30, 60, 120, 240 kHz). Slot durations scale from 1.0 ms down to 62.5 µs.", "impact": "Demands sub-millisecond scheduling loops (125 µs at 120 kHz SCS) and mixed-numerology co-existence."}, {"dimension": "Bandwidth & RF Adaptation", "lte": "Fixed channel bandwidth up to 20 MHz per carrier. UE must monitor full bandwidth continuously.", "nr": "Bandwidth Parts (BWP): Up to 4 BWPs per cell, each with independent SCS, bandwidth, and location. Rel-16 Dormant BWP.", "impact": "Slashes UE RF power consumption by 90% during idle or low-data periods; allows fast 2 ms reactivation."}, {"dimension": "Downlink HARQ Timeline", "lte": "Rigid synchronous HARQ: Fixed 4 ms feedback offset and fixed 8 ms retransmission cycle in FDD.", "nr": "Fully asynchronous HARQ: Dynamic K1 slot offset signaled per DCI grant. Up to 16 HARQ processes per carrier.", "impact": "Eliminates latency bottlenecks and enables seamless adaptation to TDD, URLLC, and mixed numerologies."}, {"dimension": "Retransmission Granularity", "lte": "Transport Block (TB) level only. If 1 code block fails CRC, the entire multi-megabit TB is retransmitted.", "nr": "Code Block Group (CBG) level: TB segmented into up to 8 CBGs. Retransmits ONLY failed CBGs via CBGTI bitmap.", "impact": "Reduces radio retransmission overhead by 75-80% under high-BLER radio conditions."}, {"dimension": "MAC PDU Subheader Format", "lte": "All MAC subheaders clustered at the front of the PDU. Requires knowing all SDU sizes before assembly.", "nr": "Subheaders placed immediately in front of each corresponding SDU or CE (R/F/LCID/L). MAC CEs at front.", "impact": "Enables hardware DMA streaming and just-in-time subheader insertion without multi-pass buffer copying."}, {"dimension": "Uplink HARQ-ACK Codebook", "lte": "Fixed subframe bitmaps or basic ACK/NACK bundling based on rigid TDD configurations.", "nr": "Type-1 Semi-Static (candidate occasion grid) and Type-2 Dynamic (Counter & Total DAI modulo-4 counting).", "impact": "Prevents false NACK cascades and minimizes uplink PUCCH payload overhead across wide carrier aggregation."}, {"dimension": "Channel Bandwidth & Aggregation", "lte": "Max 20 MHz per carrier, up to 5 carriers aggregated (100 MHz total bandwidth).", "nr": "Max 100 MHz (FR1) or 400 MHz (FR2) per carrier, up to 16 carriers aggregated (up to 6.4 GHz bandwidth).", "impact": "Requires zero-copy lock-free multi-carrier scheduler pipelines to handle gigabit data throughput."}, {"dimension": "MIMO & Beam Management", "lte": "Cell-specific reference signals (CRS) broadcasted omni-directionally or with static sector beams.", "nr": "Beamformed SSB and CSI-RS with dynamic TCI States and QCL Types (A/B/C/D). Real-time beam tracking and BFR.", "impact": "MAC scheduler must track active beam index and spatial Rx parameters per UE for every PDSCH allocation."}, {"dimension": "Architecture & Disaggregation", "lte": "Monolithic eNodeB (BBU connected to RRH via CPRI). Rigid proprietary hardware.", "nr": "Disaggregated O-RAN: O-CU (PDCP/RRC), O-DU (RLC/MAC/High-PHY), O-RU (Low-PHY/RF) over Split 7-2x and SCF FAPI.", "impact": "Enables open vendor interoperability, cloud baseband virtualization (Cloud-RAN), and standardized FAPI APIs."}], "topInterviewQuestions": [{"id": "TOP_01", "question": "How does Downlink HARQ differ between 4G LTE and 5G NR?", "specs": "TS 38.321 §5.3, TS 38.214 §5.1, TS 38.213 §9.2.3", "detailedAnswer": "In 4G LTE FDD, Downlink HARQ follows a rigid, synchronous timeline: feedback arrives exactly 4 ms after transmission, and retransmissions occur at fixed 8 ms intervals with 8 stop-and-wait processes per carrier.\n\nIn 5G NR, HARQ is completely redesigned for extreme flexibility across varied numerologies (15 kHz to 120 kHz):\n1. **Fully Asynchronous & Dynamic Timing (K1)**: The gNodeB explicitly dictates feedback timing via the 'PDSCH-to-HARQ_feedback timing indicator' field in DCI format 1_0/1_1. The slot offset K1 dynamically instructs the UE when to transmit PUCCH HARQ-ACK.\n2. **Up to 16 Parallel HARQ Processes**: Accommodates high-throughput mmWave and carrier aggregation pipelines, preventing scheduler stall.\n3. **Code Block Group (CBG) Retransmissions (TS 38.321 §5.3.2.2)**: Transport blocks segmented into multiple code blocks can be grouped into up to 8 CBGs. Retransmissions target only failed CBGs via CBGTI bitmasks, cutting radio retransmission overhead by 75-80%.\n4. **Dynamic HARQ-ACK Codebooks (TS 38.213 §9.2.3)**: Uses Counter DAI (cDAI) and Total DAI (tDAI) to size the feedback payload dynamically and catch missed DCIs, replacing LTE's rigid subframe bitmaps.", "modelAnswer": "In 4G LTE FDD, Downlink HARQ follows a rigid, synchronous timeline: feedback arrives exactly 4 ms after transmission, and retransmissions occur at fixed 8 ms intervals with 8 stop-and-wait processes per carrier.\n\nIn 5G NR, HARQ is completely redesigned for extreme flexibility across varied numerologies (15 kHz to 120 kHz):\n1. **Fully Asynchronous & Dynamic Timing (K1)**: The gNodeB explicitly dictates feedback timing via the 'PDSCH-to-HARQ_feedback timing indicator' field in DCI format 1_0/1_1. The slot offset K1 dynamically instructs the UE when to transmit PUCCH HARQ-ACK.\n2. **Up to 16 Parallel HARQ Processes**: Accommodates high-throughput mmWave and carrier aggregation pipelines, preventing scheduler stall.\n3. **Code Block Group (CBG) Retransmissions (TS 38.321 §5.3.2.2)**: Transport blocks segmented into multiple code blocks can be grouped into up to 8 CBGs. Retransmissions target only failed CBGs via CBGTI bitmasks, cutting radio retransmission overhead by 75-80%.\n4. **Dynamic HARQ-ACK Codebooks (TS 38.213 §9.2.3)**: Uses Counter DAI (cDAI) and Total DAI (tDAI) to size the feedback payload dynamically and catch missed DCIs, replacing LTE's rigid subframe bitmaps."}, {"id": "TOP_02", "question": "Explain the Bandwidth Part (BWP) framework and its significance in MAC Downlink.", "specs": "TS 38.321 §5.15, TS 38.213 §12", "detailedAnswer": "A Bandwidth Part (BWP) is a contiguous subset of physical resource blocks (PRBs) on a carrier with a specific numerology (subcarrier spacing and cyclic prefix).\n- **Core Architecture**: A UE can be configured with up to 4 DL BWPs and 4 UL BWPs per serving cell, but only ONE DL and ONE UL BWP is active at any given instant (in Rel-15).\n- **Types**: Initial BWP (for initial access/RACH), Active BWP (for current data transfer), and Default BWP (for battery saving when inactivity timer expires).\n- **Significance in MAC DL**:\n  1. **Energy Efficiency**: Eliminates the requirement for UEs to monitor wideband 100 MHz channels when idle, shrinking RF reception bandwidth to 20 MHz or less.\n  2. **Dynamic Adaptation**: MAC switches BWPs dynamically via 1-2 bits in DCI 0_1/1_1 or via `bwp-InactivityTimer`.\n  3. **Multi-Numerology Operation**: Different BWPs on the same carrier can use different numerologies (e.g. 15 kHz for wide coverage, 30 kHz for high throughput).", "modelAnswer": "A Bandwidth Part (BWP) is a contiguous subset of physical resource blocks (PRBs) on a carrier with a specific numerology (subcarrier spacing and cyclic prefix).\n- **Core Architecture**: A UE can be configured with up to 4 DL BWPs and 4 UL BWPs per serving cell, but only ONE DL and ONE UL BWP is active at any given instant (in Rel-15).\n- **Types**: Initial BWP (for initial access/RACH), Active BWP (for current data transfer), and Default BWP (for battery saving when inactivity timer expires).\n- **Significance in MAC DL**:\n  1. **Energy Efficiency**: Eliminates the requirement for UEs to monitor wideband 100 MHz channels when idle, shrinking RF reception bandwidth to 20 MHz or less.\n  2. **Dynamic Adaptation**: MAC switches BWPs dynamically via 1-2 bits in DCI 0_1/1_1 or via `bwp-InactivityTimer`.\n  3. **Multi-Numerology Operation**: Different BWPs on the same carrier can use different numerologies (e.g. 15 kHz for wide coverage, 30 kHz for high throughput)."}, {"id": "TOP_03", "question": "What is the difference between PDSCH Mapping Type A and Type B?", "specs": "TS 38.214 §5.1.2.1", "detailedAnswer": "PDSCH Mapping Type defines where Demodulation Reference Signals (DM-RS) are placed relative to the slot boundary and how resources are scheduled:\n- **Mapping Type A (Slot-Based Scheduling)**:\n  - DM-RS position: Fixed at symbol 2 or 3 of the slot, regardless of where PDSCH data starts.\n  - Start symbol: S ∈ {2, 3}; Length: L ∈ {3..14}, with S + L ≤ 14.\n  - Typical use: Standard eMBB slot transmissions where the whole slot or large fraction is allocated to one or more UEs.\n- **Mapping Type B (Mini-Slot / Non-Slot Scheduling)**:\n  - DM-RS position: Always at the VERY FIRST symbol of the PDSCH allocation.\n  - Start symbol: S ∈ {0..12}; Length: L ∈ {2, 4, 7}, with S + L ≤ 14.\n  - Typical use: Ultra-Reliable Low-Latency Communication (URLLC) preemption and mid-slot transmissions, allowing immediate channel estimation without waiting for slot boundaries.", "modelAnswer": "PDSCH Mapping Type defines where Demodulation Reference Signals (DM-RS) are placed relative to the slot boundary and how resources are scheduled:\n- **Mapping Type A (Slot-Based Scheduling)**:\n  - DM-RS position: Fixed at symbol 2 or 3 of the slot, regardless of where PDSCH data starts.\n  - Start symbol: S ∈ {2, 3}; Length: L ∈ {3..14}, with S + L ≤ 14.\n  - Typical use: Standard eMBB slot transmissions where the whole slot or large fraction is allocated to one or more UEs.\n- **Mapping Type B (Mini-Slot / Non-Slot Scheduling)**:\n  - DM-RS position: Always at the VERY FIRST symbol of the PDSCH allocation.\n  - Start symbol: S ∈ {0..12}; Length: L ∈ {2, 4, 7}, with S + L ≤ 14.\n  - Typical use: Ultra-Reliable Low-Latency Communication (URLLC) preemption and mid-slot transmissions, allowing immediate channel estimation without waiting for slot boundaries."}, {"id": "TOP_04", "question": "How does 5G NR handle Cross-Carrier Scheduling with mixed numerologies?", "specs": "TS 38.214 §5.1.2.1, TS 38.213 §10.1", "detailedAnswer": "Cross-carrier scheduling allows PDCCH on a scheduling cell (e.g. PCell at lower frequency) to allocate PDSCH/PUSCH on a scheduled cell (e.g. SCell at higher frequency).\nWhen carriers operate at different subcarrier spacings (μ_PDCCH ≠ μ_PDSCH):\n1. **Slot Ratio Alignment**: Slot durations scale by 2^(μ_PDSCH - μ_PDCCH).\n   - If μ_PDCCH < μ_PDSCH (e.g. 15 kHz scheduling 30 kHz): 1 PDCCH slot covers 2 PDSCH slots. DCI indicates which sub-slot (2n or 2n+1) is scheduled.\n   - If μ_PDCCH > μ_PDSCH (e.g. 30 kHz scheduling 15 kHz): Multiple PDCCH slots fall inside one PDSCH slot duration; scheduler enforces non-overlapping grants.\n2. **Timing Offset (K0)**: In DCI, K0 defines the slot offset between PDCCH and PDSCH, computed relative to the scheduled cell's numerology.\n3. **Processing Delay (Np,1)**: The scheduler must ensure the time between PDCCH end and PDSCH start satisfies UE preparation time Np,1 based on min(μ_PDCCH, μ_PDSCH).", "modelAnswer": "Cross-carrier scheduling allows PDCCH on a scheduling cell (e.g. PCell at lower frequency) to allocate PDSCH/PUSCH on a scheduled cell (e.g. SCell at higher frequency).\nWhen carriers operate at different subcarrier spacings (μ_PDCCH ≠ μ_PDSCH):\n1. **Slot Ratio Alignment**: Slot durations scale by 2^(μ_PDSCH - μ_PDCCH).\n   - If μ_PDCCH < μ_PDSCH (e.g. 15 kHz scheduling 30 kHz): 1 PDCCH slot covers 2 PDSCH slots. DCI indicates which sub-slot (2n or 2n+1) is scheduled.\n   - If μ_PDCCH > μ_PDSCH (e.g. 30 kHz scheduling 15 kHz): Multiple PDCCH slots fall inside one PDSCH slot duration; scheduler enforces non-overlapping grants.\n2. **Timing Offset (K0)**: In DCI, K0 defines the slot offset between PDCCH and PDSCH, computed relative to the scheduled cell's numerology.\n3. **Processing Delay (Np,1)**: The scheduler must ensure the time between PDCCH end and PDSCH start satisfies UE preparation time Np,1 based on min(μ_PDCCH, μ_PDSCH)."}, {"id": "TOP_05", "question": "What is the difference between Type-1 (Semi-Static) and Type-2 (Dynamic) HARQ-ACK codebooks?", "specs": "TS 38.213 §9.2.3", "detailedAnswer": "HARQ-ACK codebooks determine how the UE bundles and serializes ACK/NACK bits for multiple PDSCH receptions onto a single PUCCH/PUSCH uplink transmission:\n- **Type-1 (Semi-Static) Codebook**:\n  - Size is determined by RRC configuration: evaluates all possible candidate PDSCH occasions based on the set of K1 values and TDRA SLIV table across all configured component carriers.\n  - Generates a fixed-size bitmap. If an occasion had no grant, UE inserts NACK.\n  - Pro: Robust against missed DCIs (gNodeB and UE always agree on bitmap length and indexing).\n  - Con: Heavy uplink payload overhead when data traffic is sporadic.\n- **Type-2 (Dynamic) Codebook**:\n  - Size adapts dynamically to the actual number of scheduled grants.\n  - Uses Counter DAI (cDAI) and Total DAI (tDAI) in DCI to trace scheduled assignments using modulo-4 counting.\n  - Pro: Highly compact uplink payload.\n  - Con: If trailing DCIs are lost across all carriers, UE cannot detect omission without tDAI coordination.", "modelAnswer": "HARQ-ACK codebooks determine how the UE bundles and serializes ACK/NACK bits for multiple PDSCH receptions onto a single PUCCH/PUSCH uplink transmission:\n- **Type-1 (Semi-Static) Codebook**:\n  - Size is determined by RRC configuration: evaluates all possible candidate PDSCH occasions based on the set of K1 values and TDRA SLIV table across all configured component carriers.\n  - Generates a fixed-size bitmap. If an occasion had no grant, UE inserts NACK.\n  - Pro: Robust against missed DCIs (gNodeB and UE always agree on bitmap length and indexing).\n  - Con: Heavy uplink payload overhead when data traffic is sporadic.\n- **Type-2 (Dynamic) Codebook**:\n  - Size adapts dynamically to the actual number of scheduled grants.\n  - Uses Counter DAI (cDAI) and Total DAI (tDAI) in DCI to trace scheduled assignments using modulo-4 counting.\n  - Pro: Highly compact uplink payload.\n  - Con: If trailing DCIs are lost across all carriers, UE cannot detect omission without tDAI coordination."}, {"id": "TOP_06", "question": "Walk through the exact algorithm for SLIV calculation in PDSCH TDRA.", "specs": "TS 38.214 §5.1.2.1", "detailedAnswer": "The Start and Length Indicator Value (SLIV) compresses the start symbol S and allocated length L into a single integer (0 to 127, 7 bits):\nConstraint: 0 < L ≤ 14 - S.\n\n**Encoding Algorithm**:\n1. Check condition: if (L - 1) ≤ 7:\n   SLIV = 14 * (L - 1) + S\n2. Else:\n   SLIV = 14 * (14 - L + 1) + (14 - 1 - S)\n\n**Decoding Algorithm**:\n1. Compute quotient q = floor(SLIV / 14) and remainder r = SLIV % 14.\n2. If (q + r) ≤ 13:\n   L = q + 1\n   S = r\n3. Else:\n   L = 14 - q + 1\n   S = 14 - 1 - r\n\nExample: S=2, L=12. (L - 1) = 11 > 7.\nSLIV = 14 * (14 - 12 + 1) + (14 - 1 - 2) = 14 * 3 + 11 = 42 + 11 = 53.", "modelAnswer": "The Start and Length Indicator Value (SLIV) compresses the start symbol S and allocated length L into a single integer (0 to 127, 7 bits):\nConstraint: 0 < L ≤ 14 - S.\n\n**Encoding Algorithm**:\n1. Check condition: if (L - 1) ≤ 7:\n   SLIV = 14 * (L - 1) + S\n2. Else:\n   SLIV = 14 * (14 - L + 1) + (14 - 1 - S)\n\n**Decoding Algorithm**:\n1. Compute quotient q = floor(SLIV / 14) and remainder r = SLIV % 14.\n2. If (q + r) ≤ 13:\n   L = q + 1\n   S = r\n3. Else:\n   L = 14 - q + 1\n   S = 14 - 1 - r\n\nExample: S=2, L=12. (L - 1) = 11 > 7.\nSLIV = 14 * (14 - 12 + 1) + (14 - 1 - 2) = 14 * 3 + 11 = 42 + 11 = 53."}, {"id": "TOP_07", "question": "What are TCI States and QCL Types in 5G NR?", "specs": "TS 38.214 §5.1.5", "detailedAnswer": "Quasi-Co-Location (QCL) defines mathematical assumptions that channel properties of one reference signal can be applied to demodulate another:\n- **QCL Types**:\n  - QCL-TypeA: Doppler shift, Doppler spread, Average delay, Delay spread (full multipath profile).\n  - QCL-TypeB: Doppler shift, Doppler spread.\n  - QCL-TypeC: Doppler shift, Average delay.\n  - QCL-TypeD: Spatial Rx parameter (Angle of Arrival / same Rx beamforming weights for mmWave!).\n- **TCI (Transmission Configuration Indicator) States**:\n  - Connects PDSCH DM-RS to source reference signals (SSB or CSI-RS).\n  - RRC configures up to 128 TCI states.\n  - MAC CE activates a subset of up to 8 active TCI states.\n  - DCI 1_1 uses a 3-bit field to dynamically switch between the 8 active beams slot-by-slot.", "modelAnswer": "Quasi-Co-Location (QCL) defines mathematical assumptions that channel properties of one reference signal can be applied to demodulate another:\n- **QCL Types**:\n  - QCL-TypeA: Doppler shift, Doppler spread, Average delay, Delay spread (full multipath profile).\n  - QCL-TypeB: Doppler shift, Doppler spread.\n  - QCL-TypeC: Doppler shift, Average delay.\n  - QCL-TypeD: Spatial Rx parameter (Angle of Arrival / same Rx beamforming weights for mmWave!).\n- **TCI (Transmission Configuration Indicator) States**:\n  - Connects PDSCH DM-RS to source reference signals (SSB or CSI-RS).\n  - RRC configures up to 128 TCI states.\n  - MAC CE activates a subset of up to 8 active TCI states.\n  - DCI 1_1 uses a 3-bit field to dynamically switch between the 8 active beams slot-by-slot."}, {"id": "TOP_08", "question": "How does Rel-16 Dormant BWP improve Carrier Aggregation power efficiency?", "specs": "TS 38.321 §5.13, §5.15", "detailedAnswer": "In Rel-15 CA, when an SCell is deactivated to save battery, all channel measurements stop. Re-activating it requires 24-34 ms for RRC reconfiguration, beam alignment, and channel feedback restart.\n- **Rel-16 Dormant BWP Solution**:\n  - The SCell enters a 'Dormant BWP' where the UE stops PDCCH blind decoding (saving 90% of RF and baseband processing power).\n  - Crucially, the UE **continues** periodic CSI measurements, CQI/RI reporting, and beam tracking towards the PCell.\n  - Switching from Dormant BWP back to Active BWP is executed in **2 to 4 ms** via a dedicated DCI 1_1 or DCI 2_6 bitmap.\n  - Achieves near-instantaneous multi-gigabit CA aggregation bursts with minimal baseline battery drain.", "modelAnswer": "In Rel-15 CA, when an SCell is deactivated to save battery, all channel measurements stop. Re-activating it requires 24-34 ms for RRC reconfiguration, beam alignment, and channel feedback restart.\n- **Rel-16 Dormant BWP Solution**:\n  - The SCell enters a 'Dormant BWP' where the UE stops PDCCH blind decoding (saving 90% of RF and baseband processing power).\n  - Crucially, the UE **continues** periodic CSI measurements, CQI/RI reporting, and beam tracking towards the PCell.\n  - Switching from Dormant BWP back to Active BWP is executed in **2 to 4 ms** via a dedicated DCI 1_1 or DCI 2_6 bitmap.\n  - Achieves near-instantaneous multi-gigabit CA aggregation bursts with minimal baseline battery drain."}, {"id": "TOP_09", "question": "Describe the EN-DC Option 3x architecture and split bearer data flow.", "specs": "3GPP TS 37.340, TS 38.300 §6.5", "detailedAnswer": "E-UTRA-NR Dual Connectivity (EN-DC Option 3x) is the dominant Non-Standalone (NSA) 5G deployment model:\n1. **Control Plane**: Master eNodeB (MeNB, 4G LTE) anchors S1-MME to the EPC. SgNB (5G NR) coordinates with MeNB over X2-C.\n2. **User Plane**: S1-U user plane traffic from the Serving Gateway terminates directly at the 5G SgNB.\n3. **SCG Split Bearer Flow**:\n   - Packets enter NR PDCP at the SgNB, where PDCP Sequence Numbers are assigned.\n   - SgNB splits traffic: sending high-throughput chunks directly over 5G RLC/MAC/PHY, and routing secondary chunks over the X2-U interface to the MeNB for 4G LTE transmission.\n   - Flow control is regulated by TS 38.425 Downlink Data Delivery Status (DDDS) frames sent from MeNB back to SgNB to prevent X2 buffer overflow.", "modelAnswer": "E-UTRA-NR Dual Connectivity (EN-DC Option 3x) is the dominant Non-Standalone (NSA) 5G deployment model:\n1. **Control Plane**: Master eNodeB (MeNB, 4G LTE) anchors S1-MME to the EPC. SgNB (5G NR) coordinates with MeNB over X2-C.\n2. **User Plane**: S1-U user plane traffic from the Serving Gateway terminates directly at the 5G SgNB.\n3. **SCG Split Bearer Flow**:\n   - Packets enter NR PDCP at the SgNB, where PDCP Sequence Numbers are assigned.\n   - SgNB splits traffic: sending high-throughput chunks directly over 5G RLC/MAC/PHY, and routing secondary chunks over the X2-U interface to the MeNB for 4G LTE transmission.\n   - Flow control is regulated by TS 38.425 Downlink Data Delivery Status (DDDS) frames sent from MeNB back to SgNB to prevent X2 buffer overflow."}, {"id": "TOP_10", "question": "How does the MAC PDU structure in 5G NR differ from 4G LTE, and why does it matter for high-speed software architectures?", "specs": "TS 38.321 §6.1.2, TS 36.321 §6.1.2", "detailedAnswer": "In 4G LTE, all MAC subheaders were grouped at the very front of the MAC PDU. This meant the MAC layer had to know the exact sizes of all RLC SDUs in advance before serializing the packet.\n\nIn 5G NR, each MAC subheader immediately precedes its corresponding MAC SDU or MAC CE:\n- **Subheader Layout**: [R/F/LCID/L] placed right before the payload data.\n- **Why it Matters for High-Speed Software**:\n  1. **Just-in-Time Assembly**: RLC can stream SDUs into DMA buffers asynchronously without waiting for the MAC scheduler to compute all multiplexing headers upfront.\n  2. **Hardware DMA Acceleration**: Network interface accelerators can pipeline subheader insertion directly into packet headroom without multi-pass buffer copies (zero-copy processing).\n  3. **Low Latency**: Directly addresses the sub-millisecond slot deadlines (e.g. 125 µs) of 5G NR.", "modelAnswer": "In 4G LTE, all MAC subheaders were grouped at the very front of the MAC PDU. This meant the MAC layer had to know the exact sizes of all RLC SDUs in advance before serializing the packet.\n\nIn 5G NR, each MAC subheader immediately precedes its corresponding MAC SDU or MAC CE:\n- **Subheader Layout**: [R/F/LCID/L] placed right before the payload data.\n- **Why it Matters for High-Speed Software**:\n  1. **Just-in-Time Assembly**: RLC can stream SDUs into DMA buffers asynchronously without waiting for the MAC scheduler to compute all multiplexing headers upfront.\n  2. **Hardware DMA Acceleration**: Network interface accelerators can pipeline subheader insertion directly into packet headroom without multi-pass buffer copies (zero-copy processing).\n  3. **Low Latency**: Directly addresses the sub-millisecond slot deadlines (e.g. 125 µs) of 5G NR."}, {"id": "TOP_11", "question": "What are the latency requirements for a 5G NR MAC scheduler running at 30 kHz SCS?", "specs": "TS 38.211 §4.3, O-RAN Architecture", "detailedAnswer": "At 30 kHz SCS (μ = 1, standard FR1 C-band deployment):\n- **Slot Duration**: Exactly 0.5 ms (500 µs).\n- **Execution Budget Breakdown**:\n  - L1 UCI Ingestion & OLLA Updates: 50 µs\n  - Time-Domain Candidate Filtering & Metric Sorting (PF / QoS): 80 µs\n  - Frequency-Domain RB Allocation & TBS Table Lookup: 150 µs\n  - MAC PDU Subheader Generation & FAPI Dispatch: 100 µs\n  - DMA Baseband Acceleration Transfer & Margin: 120 µs\n  - **Total Real-Time Budget**: Must complete within ~380 µs to guarantee L1 transmission at the slot boundary.\n- **OS Constraints**: Requires PREEMPT_RT Linux kernel, CPU core pinning (`pthread_setaffinity_np`), `mlockall` to eliminate page faults, and lock-free SPSC queues.", "modelAnswer": "At 30 kHz SCS (μ = 1, standard FR1 C-band deployment):\n- **Slot Duration**: Exactly 0.5 ms (500 µs).\n- **Execution Budget Breakdown**:\n  - L1 UCI Ingestion & OLLA Updates: 50 µs\n  - Time-Domain Candidate Filtering & Metric Sorting (PF / QoS): 80 µs\n  - Frequency-Domain RB Allocation & TBS Table Lookup: 150 µs\n  - MAC PDU Subheader Generation & FAPI Dispatch: 100 µs\n  - DMA Baseband Acceleration Transfer & Margin: 120 µs\n  - **Total Real-Time Budget**: Must complete within ~380 µs to guarantee L1 transmission at the slot boundary.\n- **OS Constraints**: Requires PREEMPT_RT Linux kernel, CPU core pinning (`pthread_setaffinity_np`), `mlockall` to eliminate page faults, and lock-free SPSC queues."}, {"id": "TOP_12", "question": "How does Outer Loop Link Adaptation (OLLA) interact with CQI feedback in 5G MAC DL?", "specs": "TS 38.214 §5.2.2 & Scheduler Algorithms", "detailedAnswer": "Inner loop link adaptation converts periodic UE CQI reports (1 to 15) into an initial Signal-to-Interference-plus-Noise Ratio (SINR) using static AWGN simulation lookup tables.\nHowever, real radio environments suffer from multi-path fading, interference fluctuations, and CQI reporting delays.\n\nOLLA maintains an adaptive offset Δ_OLLA to adjust effective SINR:\nSINR_eff = SINR_CQI - Δ_OLLA\n\n**Closed-Loop Step Dynamics**:\n- On HARQ ACK: Δ_OLLA = Δ_OLLA - Δ_down\n- On HARQ NACK: Δ_OLLA = Δ_OLLA + Δ_up\nTo converge to a target 10% BLER:\nΔ_down = Δ_up * (BLER_target / (1 - BLER_target)) = Δ_up * (0.10 / 0.90) = Δ_up / 9.\nExample: Δ_up = 1.0 dB implies Δ_down = 0.111 dB.", "modelAnswer": "Inner loop link adaptation converts periodic UE CQI reports (1 to 15) into an initial Signal-to-Interference-plus-Noise Ratio (SINR) using static AWGN simulation lookup tables.\nHowever, real radio environments suffer from multi-path fading, interference fluctuations, and CQI reporting delays.\n\nOLLA maintains an adaptive offset Δ_OLLA to adjust effective SINR:\nSINR_eff = SINR_CQI - Δ_OLLA\n\n**Closed-Loop Step Dynamics**:\n- On HARQ ACK: Δ_OLLA = Δ_OLLA - Δ_down\n- On HARQ NACK: Δ_OLLA = Δ_OLLA + Δ_up\nTo converge to a target 10% BLER:\nΔ_down = Δ_up * (BLER_target / (1 - BLER_target)) = Δ_up * (0.10 / 0.90) = Δ_up / 9.\nExample: Δ_up = 1.0 dB implies Δ_down = 0.111 dB."}, {"id": "TOP_13", "question": "What is the FAPI interface, and what are the key messages exchanged between MAC and PHY?", "specs": "Small Cell Forum SCF 222 v10.02", "detailedAnswer": "The Small Cell Forum Functional Application Platform Interface (FAPI) standardizes the software messaging contract between Layer 2 (MAC) and Layer 1 (PHY) baseband firmware.\n\nKey Message Flows:\n1. **DL_TTI.request (MAC -> PHY)**: Sent every slot. Contains resource assignments for PDCCH, PDSCH, and CSI-RS (SLIV, PRBs, MCS, precoding matrices).\n2. **TX_Data.request (MAC -> PHY)**: Carries the actual transport block payload bytes generated by MAC multiplexing.\n3. **UL_TTI.request (MAC -> PHY)**: Directs PHY which PUSCH, PUCCH, and PRACH resources to listen for in the upcoming uplink slot.\n4. **UCI.indication (PHY -> MAC)**: Uplink Control Information delivered from PHY to MAC containing decoded HARQ ACK/NACKs, CQI, PMI, and RI.", "modelAnswer": "The Small Cell Forum Functional Application Platform Interface (FAPI) standardizes the software messaging contract between Layer 2 (MAC) and Layer 1 (PHY) baseband firmware.\n\nKey Message Flows:\n1. **DL_TTI.request (MAC -> PHY)**: Sent every slot. Contains resource assignments for PDCCH, PDSCH, and CSI-RS (SLIV, PRBs, MCS, precoding matrices).\n2. **TX_Data.request (MAC -> PHY)**: Carries the actual transport block payload bytes generated by MAC multiplexing.\n3. **UL_TTI.request (MAC -> PHY)**: Directs PHY which PUSCH, PUCCH, and PRACH resources to listen for in the upcoming uplink slot.\n4. **UCI.indication (PHY -> MAC)**: Uplink Control Information delivered from PHY to MAC containing decoded HARQ ACK/NACKs, CQI, PMI, and RI."}, {"id": "TOP_14", "question": "Explain the difference between Type 0 and Type 1 Frequency Domain Resource Allocation (FDRA).", "specs": "TS 38.214 §5.1.2.2", "detailedAnswer": "In 5G NR PDSCH, the gNodeB signals allocated Physical Resource Blocks (PRBs) using one of two methods:\n- **Type 0 (Resource Block Group Bitmap)**:\n  - PRBs are grouped into RBGs of size P ∈ {2, 4, 8, 16} based on BWP bandwidth and RRC table config.\n  - A bitmap in DCI assigns individual RBGs (1 bit per RBG).\n  - Pro: Allows non-contiguous, frequency-selective scheduling to exploit channel peaks.\n  - Con: Larger DCI bit overhead.\n- **Type 1 (Contiguous Resource Indication Value - RIV)**:\n  - Allocates a contiguous block of PRBs defined by starting PRB RB_start and length L_RBs.\n  - Encoded into a compact RIV integer using the triangle number formula.\n  - Pro: Highly compact DCI field (log2(N*(N+1)/2) bits).\n  - Con: Cannot allocate non-contiguous frequency chunks.", "modelAnswer": "In 5G NR PDSCH, the gNodeB signals allocated Physical Resource Blocks (PRBs) using one of two methods:\n- **Type 0 (Resource Block Group Bitmap)**:\n  - PRBs are grouped into RBGs of size P ∈ {2, 4, 8, 16} based on BWP bandwidth and RRC table config.\n  - A bitmap in DCI assigns individual RBGs (1 bit per RBG).\n  - Pro: Allows non-contiguous, frequency-selective scheduling to exploit channel peaks.\n  - Con: Larger DCI bit overhead.\n- **Type 1 (Contiguous Resource Indication Value - RIV)**:\n  - Allocates a contiguous block of PRBs defined by starting PRB RB_start and length L_RBs.\n  - Encoded into a compact RIV integer using the triangle number formula.\n  - Pro: Highly compact DCI field (log2(N*(N+1)/2) bits).\n  - Con: Cannot allocate non-contiguous frequency chunks."}, {"id": "TOP_15", "question": "How can Machine Learning be applied to 5G MAC Downlink Scheduling (connecting to your IISc thesis)?", "specs": "3GPP Rel-17/18 AI/ML in RAN & Candidate Thesis", "detailedAnswer": "In telecom L2 schedulers, classical heuristic algorithms (Proportional Fair, round-robin) struggle under non-ideal backhaul latency and rapid traffic fluctuations.\n\n**IISc Thesis Application (Akhzar's Research)**:\n1. **Problem**: In Inter-Site Carrier Aggregation over 10-20 ms non-ideal backhaul, the PCell must allocate PUCCH resources for SCell HARQ feedback without real-time knowledge of SCell subframe allocations.\n2. **ML Solution**: A Gradient-Boosted Decision Tree model trained on flow-control metrics, buffer occupancy, and arrival variance predicts SCell scheduling probability.\n3. **Real-Time Deployment**: Compiled via **Treelite** into an ultra-compact ~280 KB native C binary running directly inside the real-time MAC scheduling thread.\n4. **Outcome**: Inference executes in **0.42 ms** (fitting the 1 ms TTI deadline), boosting scheduling hit rate from 42% to 91% and slashing uplink PUCCH resource waste from 58% to 6.4%.", "modelAnswer": "In telecom L2 schedulers, classical heuristic algorithms (Proportional Fair, round-robin) struggle under non-ideal backhaul latency and rapid traffic fluctuations.\n\n**IISc Thesis Application (Akhzar's Research)**:\n1. **Problem**: In Inter-Site Carrier Aggregation over 10-20 ms non-ideal backhaul, the PCell must allocate PUCCH resources for SCell HARQ feedback without real-time knowledge of SCell subframe allocations.\n2. **ML Solution**: A Gradient-Boosted Decision Tree model trained on flow-control metrics, buffer occupancy, and arrival variance predicts SCell scheduling probability.\n3. **Real-Time Deployment**: Compiled via **Treelite** into an ultra-compact ~280 KB native C binary running directly inside the real-time MAC scheduling thread.\n4. **Outcome**: Inference executes in **0.42 ms** (fitting the 1 ms TTI deadline), boosting scheduling hit rate from 42% to 91% and slashing uplink PUCCH resource waste from 58% to 6.4%."}], "calculationDrills": [{"id": "DRILL_01", "title": "SLIV Encoding & Decoding Drill", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.1.2.1", "scenario": "PDSCH Time Domain Resource Allocation in FR1 C-band slot.", "problemStatement": "Solve the following 3 SLIV encoding/decoding problems:\n- Problem A (Encoding): PDSCH Mapping Type A, Start Symbol S = 2, Length L = 12 symbols. Compute the exact SLIV value.\n- Problem B (Decoding): Base station sends SLIV = 57. Decode start symbol S and length L.\n- Problem C (Decoding): Base station sends SLIV = 53. Decode start symbol S and length L, and verify boundary validity.", "stepByStepSolution": "**Problem A (Encoding: S = 2, L = 12)**:\n1. Check condition: (L - 1) = 11 > 7.\n2. Apply second formula:\n   SLIV = 14 · (14 - L + 1) + (14 - 1 - S)\n   SLIV = 14 · (14 - 12 + 1) + (14 - 1 - 2) = 14 · 3 + 11 = 42 + 11 = 53.\n\n**Problem B (Decoding: SLIV = 57)**:\n1. Quotient q = ⌊57 / 14⌋ = 4, Remainder r = 57 mod 14 = 1.\n2. Check boundary condition: q + r = 4 + 1 = 5 ≤ 13 (Condition 1 satisfied).\n3. L = q + 1 = 5, S = r = 1. Valid: S + L = 6 ≤ 14.\n\n**Problem C (Decoding: SLIV = 53)**:\n1. Quotient q = ⌊53 / 14⌋ = 3, Remainder r = 53 mod 14 = 11.\n2. Check boundary condition: q + r = 3 + 11 = 14 > 13 (Condition 2 satisfied).\n3. L = 14 - q + 1 = 14 - 3 + 1 = 12.\n4. S = 14 - 1 - r = 14 - 1 - 11 = 2.\n5. Verify validity: S + L = 2 + 12 = 14 ≤ 14. Perfectly valid!", "verifiedAnswer": "Problem A: SLIV = 53 | Problem B: S = 1, L = 5 | Problem C: S = 2, L = 12 (Valid)"}, {"id": "DRILL_02", "title": "Type-1 vs Type-2 HARQ Codebook Determination & Fault Injection", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.213 §9.2.3", "scenario": "3 Component Carriers (CC0, CC1, CC2). DL scheduled on CC0 and CC2; CC1 dropped.", "problemStatement": "A UE is configured with 3 Component Carriers (CC0, CC1, CC2). In monitoring occasion 1, gNodeB schedules CC0 and CC2, while CC1 has no data.\n1. Describe how Type-1 (Semi-Static) codebook sizes the uplink payload.\n2. Trace the Counter DAI (cDAI) and Total DAI (tDAI) assignments for Type-2 Dynamic codebook.\n3. Fault Injection: What happens if the UE misses the DCI for CC0? How does the UE detect the loss?", "stepByStepSolution": "**1. Type-1 Semi-Static Codebook**:\n- The UE evaluates all candidate occasions across all 3 carriers based on RRC K1 set.\n- Generates a fixed 12-bit payload covering CC0, CC1, and CC2.\n- Since CC1 has no grant, UE inserts NACK at CC1's positions.\n- Pro: 100% immune to lost DCIs. Con: High uplink overhead.\n\n**2. Type-2 Dynamic Codebook Tracing**:\n- CC0: cDAI = 1, tDAI = 2 (in binary: cDAI=00, tDAI=01).\n- CC2: cDAI = 2, tDAI = 2 (in binary: cDAI=01, tDAI=01).\n- Total uplink payload: exactly 2 bits [ACK_CC0, ACK_CC2]. Highly compact!\n\n**3. Fault Injection (DCI for CC0 is Lost)**:\n- UE receives only the DCI for CC2.\n- UE inspects DCI on CC2: cDAI = 2, tDAI = 2.\n- Detection: UE sees cDAI = 2 as its first received grant. Since it never saw cDAI = 1, it computes: (cDAI_curr - 1) = 1 missed grant.\n- UE inserts NACK at position 1 and places CC2's feedback at position 2: [NACK, ACK_CC2].\n- Alignment is fully preserved! gNodeB detects that CC0 failed and retransmits CC0 immediately.", "verifiedAnswer": "Type-1 sends fixed 12-bit grid | Type-2 sends 2 bits [cDAI=1, cDAI=2] | Lost CC0 caught via cDAI gap → UE sends [NACK, ACK]"}, {"id": "DRILL_03", "title": "Full Transport Block Size (TBS) Determination", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.1.3.2", "scenario": "100 MHz FR1 C-band channel at 30 kHz SCS (273 PRBs), 64-QAM MCS 20, 1 layer.", "problemStatement": "Calculate the exact Transport Block Size (TBS) in bits and bytes for:\n- Bandwidth: 100 MHz FR1, 30 kHz SCS (n_PRB = 273).\n- Duration: Normal slot (14 OFDM symbols), 1 DM-RS symbol (12 REs per PRB overhead).\n- MCS: MCS 20 from Table 5.1.3.1-1: 64-QAM (Q_m = 6), Target Code Rate R = 772 / 1024, Layers ν = 1.", "stepByStepSolution": "**Step 1: Calculate Resource Elements (N_RE)**:\n- N'_RE = 14 · 12 - 12 (DMRS) = 168 - 12 = 156 REs/PRB.\n- N_RE = min(156, N'_RE) · n_PRB = 156 · 273 = 42,588 REs.\n\n**Step 2: Calculate Unquantized Information Bits (N_info)**:\n- N_info = N_RE · R · Q_m · ν\n- N_info = 42,588 · (772 / 1024) · 6 · 1 = 42,588 · 0.75390625 · 6 = 192,577.8 bits.\n\n**Step 3 & 4: Quantization (N_info > 3824)**:\n- Compute exponent: n = ⌊log2(192577.8 - 24)⌋ - 5 = ⌊log2(192553.8)⌋ - 5 = 17 - 5 = 12.\n- Quantization step = 2^12 = 4096.\n- N'_info = 4096 · round((192577.8 - 24) / 4096) = 4096 · round(47.01) = 4096 · 47 = 192,512 bits.\n\n**Step 5: LDPC Segmentation & Final TBS**:\n- Since R = 0.75 > 1/4 and N'_info > 8424 ⇒ LDPC Base Graph 1 (K_cb = 8448).\n- Code blocks C = ⌈(192512 + 24) / (8448 - 24)⌉ = ⌈192536 / 8424⌉ = ⌈22.855⌉ = 23 code blocks.\n- TBS = 8 · C · ⌈(N'_info + 24) / (8 · C)⌉ - 24\n- TBS = 8 · 23 · ⌈192536 / 184⌉ - 24 = 184 · 1047 - 24 = 192,648 - 24 = 192,624 bits.\n- TBS in bytes: 192,624 / 8 = 24,078 bytes.", "verifiedAnswer": "Exact TBS = 192,624 bits (24,078 bytes) with C = 23 LDPC code blocks on Base Graph 1"}, {"id": "DRILL_04", "title": "Cross-Carrier Scheduling with Mixed Numerologies", "specClause": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.1.2.1, TS 38.213 §10.1", "scenario": "PCell at μ = 0 (15 kHz SCS, 1 ms slot) cross-carrier schedules SCell at μ = 1 (30 kHz SCS, 0.5 ms slot).", "problemStatement": "A base station uses cross-carrier scheduling with PCell (μ_1 = 0, 15 kHz, 1 ms slot) scheduling SCell (μ_2 = 1, 30 kHz, 0.5 ms slot).\n1. How does 1 PDCCH slot on PCell map to SCell slots?\n2. How does DCI differentiate between the sub-slots?\n3. What is the minimum processing delay constraint N_p,1 that the scheduler must obey?", "stepByStepSolution": "**1. Slot Mapping**:\n- Ratio = 2^(μ_2 - μ_1) = 2^(1 - 0) = 2.\n- 1 PDCCH slot on PCell (slot n, duration 1.0 ms) spans exactly 2 slots on SCell (slots 2n and 2n+1, each duration 0.5 ms).\n\n**2. Sub-Slot Indication**:\n- The DCI format 1_1 on PCell contains a 3-bit Carrier Indicator Field (CIF) identifying the SCell.\n- The Time Domain Resource Assignment field and slot offset K0 dynamically address either slot 2n or slot 2n+1 on the SCell.\n\n**3. Processing Delay Constraint (N_p,1)**:\n- TS 38.214 Table 5.3-1 requires the UE to complete PDCCH reception and decoding before PDSCH symbols arrive.\n- The minimum timing offset must satisfy the slower numerology: T_prep ≥ N_p,1 · T_slot^(min(μ_1, μ_2)) = N_p,1 · 1.0 ms.\n- If this constraint is violated, the UE cannot decode PDCCH in time, causing PDSCH reception to abort.", "verifiedAnswer": "1 PCell slot spans 2 SCell sub-slots (2n & 2n+1) | Targeted via K0 offset | Timing must obey Np,1 on slower numerology"}], "diagnosticPackets": [{"logCodeHex": "0xB887", "name": "NR5G MAC PDSCH Status", "layer": "5G NR MAC / PHY", "description": "Real-time transport block decoding report. Shows TB size, number of MIMO layers, modulation order (QPSK/16QAM/64QAM/256QAM), and HARQ outcome.", "keyFieldsToInspect": "TB Size, Modulation Order, MIMO Layers, HARQ Process ID, CRC Result (Pass/Fail/DTX)"}, {"logCodeHex": "0xB883", "name": "NR5G MAC UL Physical Channel Schedule Report", "layer": "5G NR MAC", "description": "Real-time uplink scheduling decisions. Tracks UL grants, PUSCH TBS, and Buffer Status Report (BSR) transmissions.", "keyFieldsToInspect": "Grant Size, PUSCH PRB allocation, BSR Buffer Size, Power Headroom (PHR)"}, {"logCodeHex": "0xB821", "name": "NR5G RRC OTA Packet", "layer": "5G NR RRC", "description": "Complete decoded ASN.1 over-the-air signaling messages. Used to audit cell group and bearer configurations.", "keyFieldsToInspect": "RRCReconfiguration, ServingCellConfig, BWP-Downlink, CrossCarrierSchedulingConfig, pdsch-TimeDomainAllocationList"}, {"logCodeHex": "0xB825", "name": "NR5G RRC Configuration Info", "layer": "5G NR RRC", "description": "Parsed active radio configuration database. Shows MCG and SCG cell IDs, bearer mappings, and security keys.", "keyFieldsToInspect": "MCG/SCG Bearer IDs, Active Cell Group, Security Algorithms, SCell State"}, {"logCodeHex": "0xB97F", "name": "NR5G ML1 Searcher Measurement Database Update Ext", "layer": "5G NR PHY / Beam", "description": "L1 beam management tracking database. Contains real-time L1-RSRP for all SSB and CSI-RS beams, and active TCI states.", "keyFieldsToInspect": "SSB Index, L1-RSRP (dBm), Active TCI State, Beam Failure Instance Counter"}, {"logCodeHex": "0xB173", "name": "LTE MAC DL Transport Block", "layer": "4G LTE MAC", "description": "LTE counterpart for DL TB decoding. Essential during EN-DC Option 3x cross-RAT debugging to compare LTE vs NR BLER.", "keyFieldsToInspect": "TB Size, MCS, HARQ Process ID, CRC Result"}, {"logCodeHex": "0xB14D", "name": "LTE ML1 PDCCH DCI Decode", "layer": "4G LTE PHY", "description": "LTE DCI grant decode report. If grants are received in 0xB14D but no TB in 0xB173, PDSCH demodulation is failing.", "keyFieldsToInspect": "DCI Format, RNTI, PRB Assignment, MCS"}, {"logCodeHex": "0xB139", "name": "LTE ML1 PDSCH Demodulation Status", "layer": "4G LTE PHY", "description": "LTE per-layer SNR, CQI, rank estimation (RI), and modulation order.", "keyFieldsToInspect": "SNR per Rx antenna, CQI reported, Rank Indicator"}], "systemDesigns": [{"id": "DESIGN_01", "title": "Scalable 5G NR MAC Downlink Scheduler (125 µs Deadline)", "latencyConstraint": "125 µs Hard Real-Time Deadline (120 kHz SCS)", "summary": "Enterprise multi-threaded baseband scheduler designed for 1,000 active UEs, executing on isolated CPU cores under PREEMPT_RT Linux without dynamic memory allocation.", "pipelineStages": ["Stage 1: Feedback Ingestion (15 µs) - Process L1 UCI Indications (HARQ ACK/NACK, CQI/PMI), update OLLA offsets per UE, re-queue NACKed processes.", "Stage 2: Time-Domain Candidate Filtering (20 µs) - Scan connected UEs, compute Proportional Fair metrics W_i = (R_i / Avg_R_i) · QoS_i, AVX-512 partial sort top 20 candidates.", "Stage 3: Frequency-Domain Resource Allocation (45 µs) - Retransmissions prioritized, frequency-selective RBG allocation, TS 38.214 TBS lookup.", "Stage 4: PDU Multiplexing & FAPI Dispatch (30 µs) - Assemble MAC subheaders + CEs, pull RLC SDUs via zero-copy descriptors, emit FAPI DL_TTI.request & TX_Data.request.", "Stage 5: DMA Dispatch to L1 Accelerator (15 µs safety margin)."], "memoryAndConcurrency": "CPU Cores 2-7 isolated (isolcpus). Thread affinity pinned. 1 GB hugepages for packet pools. Lock-free SPSC queues with memory_order_acquire/release between stages. Zero mallocs."}, {"id": "DESIGN_02", "title": "Asynchronous Multi-Process DL HARQ Manager", "latencyConstraint": "Sub-millisecond dynamic K1 dispatch across 128 processes", "summary": "Manages 16 parallel HARQ processes per carrier across up to 8 component carriers (128 processes per UE) with Code Block Group (CBG) selective retransmission.", "pipelineStages": ["Process State Machine: IDLE → SCHEDULED → AWAITING_FEEDBACK → (ACK → IDLE) or (NACK → RETRANSMIT_PENDING).", "Single-Cycle Bitwise Allocation: 16-bit bitmask free_process_mask. int pid = __builtin_ctz(free_process_mask); free_process_mask &= ~(1u << pid);", "CBG Selective Tracking: Tracks up to 8 CBGs per TB. On partial NACK, retransmits only corrupted CBGs via CBGTI, saving 75-80% radio resources."], "memoryAndConcurrency": "Bitmask manipulation in L1 cache. Lock-free atomic state updates. Fixed circular buffer descriptors."}, {"id": "DESIGN_03", "title": "High-Throughput Inter-Thread L2 IPC Architecture", "latencyConstraint": "Zero mutex contention, < 1 µs queue handoff", "summary": "Real-time communication pipeline connecting L1 Driver, MAC Scheduler, and RLC AM Entity over shared memory without kernel system calls.", "pipelineStages": ["Lock-free SPSC ring buffers placed in shared memory (shm_open) between producer and consumer threads.", "Acquire/Release memory order prevents CPU bus locking while ensuring cache coherency.", "Credit-based backpressure: MAC grants buffer credits to RLC based on air-interface throughput; RLC throttles PDCP pulls when credits drop to zero."], "memoryAndConcurrency": "alignas(64) on queue heads and tails eliminates false sharing. EventFD for low-jitter wakeups."}, {"id": "DESIGN_04", "title": "Inter-Site CA Coordination over Non-Ideal Backhaul (ML Optimization)", "latencyConstraint": "0.42 ms ML inference budget (within 1 ms TTI)", "summary": "Predicts secondary cell (SCell) scheduling events over 10-20 ms non-ideal backhaul to optimize primary cell (PCell) PUCCH Format 3 allocations, developed by Akhzar Farhan at IISc Bangalore.", "pipelineStages": ["Bottleneck: PCell must allocate PUCCH resources for SCell HARQ without knowing SCell subframe decisions in advance.", "ML Architecture: Gradient-Boosted Decision Tree trained on buffer occupancy, flow variance, and arrival rate.", "Embedded Deployment: Compiled into an ultra-compact ~280 KB native C binary via Treelite.", "Performance: Inference runs in 0.42 ms. Raised scheduling hit rate from 42% to 91% and cut PUCCH waste from 58% to 6.4%."], "memoryAndConcurrency": "Native C decision tree with branch prediction optimization. Zero heap allocations during inference."}]}, "nrQuestionBank": [{"id": "Q001", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "HARQ & Retransmission", "question": "Explain how Downlink HARQ processes are managed in LTE FDD versus TDD. Why is DL HARQ asynchronous while UL HARQ was originally synchronous?", "detailedAnswer": "In LTE FDD, there are 8 parallel DL HARQ processes per serving cell (extended to up to 16 in later releases for TTI bundling/latency reduction). In TDD, the number of DL HARQ processes depends on the UL/DL subframe configuration (e.g. up to 15 processes for Config 5). DL HARQ is asynchronous and adaptive: the eNodeB scheduler can transmit a retransmission at any valid DL subframe after minimum RTT (n+4 in FDD) and can change the PRB allocation, MCS, and Redundancy Version (RV). To achieve this, the PDCCH DCI (e.g. Format 1A, 2A/2B/2C) explicitly conveys the 3-bit (FDD) or 4-bit (TDD) HARQ Process ID, New Data Indicator (NDI), and RV. In contrast, LTE UL HARQ was initially synchronous to save PDCCH overhead: the UE retransmits on the exact same HARQ process and PRBs at n+4 (in FDD) triggered simply by PHICH NACK without needing a new DCI grant unless adaptive retransmission was required.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.321 §5.3.1, TS 36.213 §7.1 & §8.0", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 RAN, Nokia AirScale, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q002", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "HARQ & Soft Combining", "question": "Compare Chase Combining (CC) and Incremental Redundancy (IR) in LTE DL HARQ. How does the circular buffer rate matcher generate the redundancy versions (RV0, RV2, RV3, RV1)?", "detailedAnswer": "Chase Combining retransmits identical parity and systematic coded bits (RV0 repeatedly), allowing the receiver to perform simple maximum-ratio combining (MRC) of LLRs across transmissions, yielding SNR gain (diversity gain). Incremental Redundancy transmits different puncturing patterns of parity bits (RV0, RV2, RV3, RV1), lowering the effective code rate with each retransmission and delivering both coding gain and SNR gain. LTE turbo coder outputs 3 streams: systematic $d_k^{(0)}$ and parity streams $d_k^{(1)}, d_k^{(2)}$. These are sub-block interleaved and placed into a circular buffer where systematic bits are stored first, followed by interleaved parity bits. RV0 starts at index 0 (maximising systematic bit inclusion). RV2 starts at $k_0 \\approx \\frac{2}{3} N_{cb}$, RV3 at $\\frac{1}{3} N_{cb}$, and RV1 at $\\frac{1}{6} N_{cb}$. The sequence RV0 -> RV2 -> RV3 -> RV1 ensures that if RV0 fails, RV2 transmits complementary parity bits, and subsequent transmissions quickly complete the code word.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.212 §5.1.4.1.2 (Circular Buffer Rate Matching)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Baseband, Apple Munich Modem Firmware, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q003", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "HARQ Feedback & Timing", "question": "In LTE TDD, how does the UE transmit HARQ ACK/NACK on PUCCH when there are more DL subframes than UL subframes? Explain HARQ Bundling vs HARQ Multiplexing.", "detailedAnswer": "In LTE TDD asymmetric configurations (e.g. Config 1, 2, 5), multiple DL subframes associate with a single UL subframe $n$ according to the Downlink Association Set $K = \\{k_0, k_1, \\dots, k_{M-1}\\}$. Under HARQ Bundling: The UE performs a logical AND operation across all received DL transport blocks in the association window. A single ACK is transmitted on PUCCH Format 1a/1b only if ALL subframes were decoded successfully; if any single TB was in error or missed, NACK is sent. To detect missed PDCCH grants (lost subframes), the eNB includes a 2-bit Downlink Assignment Index (DAI) in DCI. If the received DAI counter has gaps, the UE forces NACK. Under HARQ Multiplexing: The UE reports individual ACK/NACK/DTX states for each subframe using PUCCH Format 1b with channel selection (conveying up to 4 bits using 4 PUCCH resources and QPSK constellations) or PUCCH Format 3 (which can carry up to 20 ACK/NACK bits via DFT-S-OFDM).", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.213 §10.1.3 (TDD HARQ-ACK reporting procedures)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Bangalore, Samsung SRI-B, Nokia Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q004", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "Multiplexing & PDU Assembly", "question": "Describe the structure of an LTE MAC PDU in Downlink. How do subheaders indicate MAC SDUs, MAC Control Elements, and Padding?", "detailedAnswer": "An LTE DL MAC PDU consists of a MAC header, zero or more MAC Service Data Units (MAC SDUs), zero or more MAC Control Elements (MAC CEs), and optional padding. The MAC header contains one or more MAC subheaders. Each subheader corresponds to either a MAC SDU, a MAC CE, or padding. Subheader formats: 1) Standard subheader with 7-bit/15-bit length: [R (1b) | R (1b) | E (1b) | LCID (5b)] followed by [F (1b) | L (7b or 15b)]. The 'E' (Extension) bit indicates if another subheader follows (E=1) or if payload begins (E=0). 'F' flag defines whether the L field is 7 bits (F=0, size < 128 bytes) or 15 bits (F=1, size >= 128 bytes). 2) Fixed-size subheader: [R | R | E | LCID] without an L field, used for fixed-size MAC CEs (e.g. Timing Advance Command, DRX Command, Long DRX Command) and padding. In LTE, all subheaders are concatenated together at the beginning of the MAC PDU before any MAC CE or SDU payloads.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.321 §6.1.2 (MAC PDU Downlink)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 DU, Apple Munich Modem Firmware, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q005", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "Logical Channel Prioritization", "question": "What is the Logical Channel Prioritization (LCP) algorithm in 3GPP MAC? Explain Prioritized Bit Rate (PBR), Bucket Size Duration (BSD), and the token bucket Bj updating formula.", "detailedAnswer": "LCP is the MAC layer scheduling algorithm used to multiplex data from multiple logical channels (RLC entities) into a single MAC PDU based on QoS priorities. Each logical channel $j$ is configured by RRC with a priority (lower value = higher priority), a Prioritized Bit Rate ($PBR$), and a Bucket Size Duration ($BSD$). The token bucket $B_j$ accumulates tokens: $B_j = B_j + (PBR \\times \\Delta t)$, capped at $BucketSize = PBR \\times BSD$. $B_j$ can become negative if serviced. The LCP execution operates in two distinct phases: Phase 1: Allocate resources to logical channels in decreasing priority order, allocating up to the positive value of $B_j$ for each channel. This guarantees that lower-priority channels with non-zero PBR are not completely starved by high-priority background traffic. Phase 2: If grant space remains after Phase 1, allocate the residual capacity strictly in decreasing priority order until the grant or data buffers are exhausted, regardless of $B_j$.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.321 §5.4.3.1 (Logical Channel Prioritization)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Scheduler, Nokia AirScale, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q006", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "Carrier Aggregation & SCell", "question": "What is the role of PCell vs SCell in LTE Downlink Carrier Aggregation? Why can PUCCH only be transmitted on PCell (unless PUCCH-on-SCell is configured)?", "detailedAnswer": "In LTE Carrier Aggregation (CA), the Primary Cell (PCell) handles RRC connection establishment, security inputs, non-access stratum (NAS) mobility, system information acquisition (BCCH/MIB/SIB1), and Radio Link Monitoring (RLM). Secondary Cells (SCells) are dynamically added/removed via RRC and activated/deactivated via MAC CEs solely to provide additional radio resource bandwidth. PUCCH was originally restricted strictly to the PCell because: 1) Maintaining uplink timing advance and continuous transmit power loops on multiple independent carriers simultaneously increases UE transmitter complexity and battery drain. 2) SCells can be rapidly deactivated or suffer sudden channel fading; hosting critical L1/L2 control signaling (HARQ-ACK, SR, CSI) on an unstable SCell would drop the entire link. In Rel-12 Dual Connectivity and Rel-13 CA enhancement, PUCCH-on-SCell was introduced to support scenarios with large numbers of aggregated CCs (up to 32 CCs) to avoid overloading the PCell PUCCH capacity.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.300 §5.5, TS 36.321 §5.13", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Bangalore, Samsung SRI-B (Candidate Core Strength)", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q007", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "Carrier Aggregation & SCell", "question": "Detail the SCell Activation/Deactivation MAC Control Element format and explain the sCellDeactivationTimer state machine.", "detailedAnswer": "The SCell Activation/Deactivation MAC CE consists of a single octet (or 4 octets in Rel-13 for up to 32 CCs) with LCID 11011 (27 in decimal). The 1-octet format contains 7 bitmap flags $C_7, C_6, C_5, C_4, C_3, C_2, C_1$ and one reserved bit R. $C_i$ corresponds to the SCellIndex $i$ configured by RRC. A bit set to '1' indicates SCell activation, while '0' indicates deactivation. Each active SCell maintains an independent `sCellDeactivationTimer` (configured per SCell by RRC). State machine rules: 1) When MAC CE arrives with $C_i = 1$: SCell is activated, and `sCellDeactivationTimer` is started/restarted. 2) Whenever PDCCH schedules a DL assignment or UL grant on SCell $i$ (or cross-carrier scheduled for SCell $i$), the timer is restarted. 3) When the timer expires, or MAC CE arrives with $C_i = 0$: SCell transitions to Deactivated state immediately. In Deactivated state, the UE MUST NOT transmit SRS, MUST NOT report CSI, MUST NOT monitor PDCCH for the SCell, and flushes all DL HARQ buffers associated with that SCell.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.321 §5.13 & §6.1.3.8", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Samsung SRI-B, Ericsson L2 RAN, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q008", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "Carrier Aggregation & SCell", "question": "What are the 3GPP latency requirements and RF warm-up behaviors when transitioning an SCell from Deactivated to Activated state?", "detailedAnswer": "Per 3GPP TS 36.133 §7.7, SCell activation latency depends on whether the SCell is known or unknown: 1) Known SCell (UE has sent valid CSI measurement within the last 5 seconds and cell remains detectable): Activation delay is at most $24$ ms to $34$ ms ($n + 24$ subframes to $n + 34$ subframes after receiving the MAC CE in subframe $n$). 2) Unknown SCell: Delay can be up to $44$ ms to $54$ ms due to initial AGC settling, frequency/timing synchronization, and initial filtering. Upon receiving activation MAC CE in subframe $n$, the UE starts actions no earlier than $n+8$ subframes (to allow RF synthesizer lock and AGC adaptation). Valid CSI reporting starts at subframe $n+24$ or $n+34$. The eNodeB MAC scheduler must not schedule PDSCH on the SCell until the UE has achieved valid CSI and active PDCCH tracking.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.133 §7.7, TS 36.321 §5.13", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Samsung SRI-B, Rohde & Schwarz Munich, Apple Modem", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q009", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "Inter-Site Carrier Aggregation", "question": "How is Inter-Site Carrier Aggregation implemented between geographically separated eNodeBs over a non-ideal backhaul? How is GTP-U used and how do you handle 10-20 ms backhaul jitter?", "detailedAnswer": "In Inter-Site CA over non-ideal backhaul, the Master eNodeB (MeNB hosting PCell) and Secondary eNodeB (SeNB hosting SCell) are connected via an X2 interface with 5 ms to 20 ms one-way propagation delay and packet jitter. Architecture: Split bearer or SCG bearer architecture is used where user plane data arrives at MeNB PDCP, which forwards PDCP PDUs to SeNB over X2-U using GTP-U encapsulation (GPRS Tunnelling Protocol User Plane). Challenges & Solutions: 1) Flow Control (3GPP TS 36.425 / X2 UP protocol): SeNB periodically sends 'DL Data Delivery Status' frames indicating the highest successfully transmitted PDCP sequence number and desired buffer size. This prevents buffer bloat at SeNB. 2) Uplink PUCCH Coordination: The candidate's M.Tech thesis directly addressed this: PCell must allocate PUCCH Format 3/4 resources for SCell HARQ-ACK without real-time subframe-by-subframe visibility into SeNB DL scheduling. Blind over-allocation wastes 58% UL resources; predictive ML models (like XGBoost deployed via Treelite) predict SCell grant probability to allocate PUCCH dynamically within 1 ms TTI budgets.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.425, TS 36.300 §4.9.2 (Dual Connectivity User Plane)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN, Samsung SRI-B (Candidate Key Project)", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q010", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "RACH Procedure", "question": "Walk through the Contention-Based Random Access (CBRA) procedure in LTE. Explain the exact structure of MAC RAR (Random Access Response) PDU.", "detailedAnswer": "CBRA consists of 4 steps: Msg1 (PRACH Preamble): UE selects preamble from group A or B and transmits in configured PRACH resource. Msg2 (MAC RAR): eNB detects preamble and transmits RAR PDU on PDSCH addressed to RA-RNTI. MAC RAR PDU structure: Contains one or more subheaders followed by RAR payloads. Subheaders include: E (extension), T (Type: 0 for Backoff Indicator, 1 for RAPID), and RAPID (Random Access Preamble ID) or BI (Backoff Indicator). Each matching RAPID payload contains 6 octets (48 bits): - Reserved bit R (1b) - Timing Advance Command (11b) - Uplink Grant (20b, contains hopping flag, resource block assignment, MCS, TPC command, and UL delay) - Temporary C-RNTI (16b). Msg3 (RRC Connection Request): UE transmits scheduled transmission on UL grant using Temporary C-RNTI. Msg4 (Contention Resolution): eNB responds with UE Contention Resolution Identity MAC CE (echoing Msg3 payload). If matched, Temp C-RNTI is promoted to permanent C-RNTI.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.321 §5.1 & §6.1.5", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Protocol, Nokia Munich, Apple Modem", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q011", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "RACH Procedure", "question": "How does the MAC layer handle the Contention Resolution Timer (mac-ContentionResolutionTimer)? When is it started, stopped, and what occurs on expiry?", "detailedAnswer": "The `mac-ContentionResolutionTimer` is configured by RRC (typical values: 16 to 64 subframes). Execution rules: 1) Started: Started in the subframe containing the transmission of Msg3 (plus 4 subframes for HARQ transmission to complete). 2) Restarted: If Msg3 undergoes HARQ retransmission, the timer is restarted after the retransmission. 3) Stopped: Stopped when contention resolution is successful. Success occurs if: (a) UE already has C-RNTI and receives PDCCH grant addressed to its C-RNTI; or (b) UE included its 48-bit identity (e.g. S-TMSI) in Msg3 and receives Msg4 containing matching UE Contention Resolution Identity MAC CE. 4) Expiry: If the timer expires before contention resolution succeeds, the UE discards the Temporary C-RNTI, considers contention resolution failed, increments `PREAMBLE_TRANSMISSION_COUNTER`, and if counter < `preambleTransMax`, applies backoff delay (if BI was received in RAR) and restarts PRACH from Msg1.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.321 §5.1.5 (Contention Resolution)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Baseband, Samsung SRI-B, Nokia AirScale", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q012", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "Timing Advance", "question": "Differentiate between the initial Timing Advance (TA) in RAR and the continuous TA update in MAC CE. What is the mathematical formula for TA adjustment?", "detailedAnswer": "The initial TA in RAR provides an absolute 11-bit index $T_A \\in [0, 1282]$ with step size $16 T_s$ ($T_s = 1 / (15000 \\times 2048) \\approx 32.55$ ns). The initial transmission timing is set as $N_{TA} = T_A \\times 16$, covering a round-trip propagation delay up to $\\approx 667$ µs (cell radius ~100 km). Continuous TA updates are sent via a 6-bit Timing Advance Command MAC CE ($T_A \\in [0, 63]$), carried in DL-SCH with LCID 11101 (29). The formula for updating time alignment is: $N_{TA, new} = N_{TA, old} + (T_A - 31) \\times 16$. Here, index 31 represents no change ($31 - 31 = 0$). Values $T_A > 31$ advance the transmission (moving it earlier), while $T_A < 31$ delay the transmission. Each received TA MAC CE restarts the `timeAlignmentTimer` (TAT). If TAT expires, the UE flushes all HARQ buffers, releases PUCCH/SRS, and ceases all UL transmissions to avoid interfering with other UEs.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.213 §4.2.3, TS 36.321 §5.2", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Baseband, Apple Munich Modem, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q013", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "DRX & Power Saving", "question": "Explain the interaction between onDurationTimer, drx-InactivityTimer, and drx-RetransmissionTimerDL in Connected-mode DRX (cDRX).", "detailedAnswer": "Connected DRX allows the UE to turn off its RF receiver when no data is being scheduled, saving significant battery. Key timers: 1) `onDurationTimer`: Specifies the number of consecutive subframes at the beginning of each DRX Cycle where the UE must monitor PDCCH. 2) `drx-InactivityTimer`: Started/restarted whenever PDCCH indicates a new UL or DL transmission for the UE. The UE remains in Active Time continuously as long as this timer is running. 3) `drx-RetransmissionTimerDL`: Started when a DL HARQ process receives a NACK. It runs after a minimum HARQ RTT timer (`drx-RetransmissionTimerDL` starts when `drx-HARQ-RTT-Timer-DL` expires) to allow the UE to sleep during the eNB retransmission processing delay and wake up only when the retransmission is expected. Active Time includes any subframe where `onDurationTimer`, `drx-InactivityTimer`, `drx-RetransmissionTimerDL`, or `mac-ContentionResolutionTimer` is running, or when an SR is pending.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.321 §5.7 (Discontinuous Reception)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Apple Munich Cellular Modem, Ericsson L2 DU, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q014", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "DRX & Power Saving", "question": "What is the difference between a DRX Command MAC CE and a Long DRX Command MAC CE? How does the UE transition between Short and Long DRX Cycles?", "detailedAnswer": "Both MAC CEs are zero-payload (fixed subheader only) commands used by the eNB to force the UE out of Active Time into DRX sleep: 1) DRX Command MAC CE (LCID 11110 / 30): Stops `onDurationTimer` and `drx-InactivityTimer`. If Short DRX Cycle is configured, the UE enters Short DRX and starts `drx-ShortCycleTimer`. If Short DRX is not configured, it transitions directly to Long DRX. 2) Long DRX Command MAC CE (LCID 11100 / 28, Rel-12): Forces the UE directly into the Long DRX Cycle, stopping `drx-ShortCycleTimer` if running. Autonomous Transition: When `drx-InactivityTimer` expires, if Short DRX is configured, the UE runs in Short DRX until `drx-ShortCycleTimer` expires (e.g. 1-16 short cycles), after which it automatically transitions into Long DRX Cycle.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.321 §5.7 & §6.1.3.3", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 RAN, Apple Munich, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q015", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "Link Adaptation & AMC", "question": "How does Adaptive Modulation and Coding (AMC) work in LTE DL MAC? Explain the closed-loop interaction between UE CQI reports and eNodeB Outer Loop Link Adaptation (OLLA).", "detailedAnswer": "AMC optimizes spectral efficiency by dynamically choosing modulation scheme (QPSK, 16QAM, 64QAM, 256QAM) and code rate based on instantaneous channel conditions. 1) Inner Loop: UE estimates SINR from Cell-Specific Reference Signals (CRS) or CSI-RS and maps it to a 4-bit CQI index (0 to 15) ensuring $\\le 10\\%$ Block Error Rate (BLER). UE reports this periodically on PUCCH or aperiodically on PUSCH. 2) Outer Loop Link Adaptation (OLLA): The eNodeB cannot blindly trust the reported CQI due to CQI feedback delay, UE measurement bias, and interference variation. The scheduler maintains an offset $\\Delta_{OLLA}$: If HARQ transmission results in ACK: $\\Delta_{OLLA} = \\Delta_{OLLA} + \\Delta_{up}$ (e.g. $+0.1$ dB). If HARQ transmission results in NACK: $\\Delta_{OLLA} = \\Delta_{OLLA} - \\Delta_{down}$ (e.g. $-0.9$ dB). The ratio $\\frac{\\Delta_{up}}{\\Delta_{down}} = \\frac{BLER_{target}}{1 - BLER_{target}} = \\frac{0.1}{0.9} = \\frac{1}{9}$. Effective SINR = $SINR(CQI) + \\Delta_{OLLA}$, from which the final MCS is selected from TS 36.213 tables.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.213 §7.2.3, TS 36.321 §5.3", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Scheduler, Nokia Munich, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q016", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "Link Adaptation & AMC", "question": "What is the difference between Periodic CQI (PUCCH) and Aperiodic CQI (PUSCH)? When does an eNodeB trigger Aperiodic CSI reporting?", "detailedAnswer": "Periodic CQI is configured semi-statically by RRC on PUCCH (Format 2/2a/2b) with fixed periodicity (e.g. 2ms to 160ms). It has limited payload capacity (carrying 4-bit wideband CQI or subband CQI for one carrier at a time) and cannot deliver high-resolution frequency-selective channel feedback for heavy carrier aggregation. Aperiodic CQI is dynamically requested on-demand by the eNodeB using the 1-bit or 2/3-bit CSI Request field in UL DCI (Format 0 / DCI 0A/0B). It is transmitted on PUSCH piggybacked with user data or as CSI-only PUSCH. The eNodeB triggers aperiodic CSI: 1) When heavy DL data arrives for a dormant UE to obtain instant channel state. 2) In Carrier Aggregation, where a 2-bit or 3-bit CSI field requests simultaneous feedback for a set of configured serving cells. 3) For CoMP (Coordinated Multi-Point) or high-rank multi-antenna beamforming where detailed PMI/RI matrices are required.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.213 §7.2.1 & §7.2.2", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Scheduler, Samsung SRI-B, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q017", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "DCI & PDCCH", "question": "Explain the difference between DCI Format 1A and DCI Format 2/2A/2B/2C/2D in LTE DL scheduling. What are the blind decoding search spaces?", "detailedAnswer": "DCI Format 1A is a compact grant format used for power-efficient fallback scheduling, Random Access (Msg2/Msg4), paging, and system information (SIB). It supports only single codeword transmission with localized/distributed Resource Allocation Type 2 and contiguous virtual resource blocks. DCI Format 2 series supports spatial multiplexing (MIMO) with up to 2 Transport Blocks (codewords) per TTI: Format 2 (open/closed loop MIMO TM3/TM4), Format 2B (dual-layer beamforming TM8), Format 2C (up to 8-layer MU-MIMO TM9), Format 2D (CoMP TM10). It contains independent MCS, NDI, and RV fields for both CW0 and CW1. Blind Decoding: The UE searches PDCCH candidates across two search spaces: 1) Common Search Space (CSS): Aggregation levels 4 (4 candidates) and 8 (2 candidates), monitoring SI-RNTI, P-RNTI, RA-RNTI, and C-RNTI fallback. 2) UE-Specific Search Space (USS): Aggregation levels 1 (6 candidates), 2 (6), 4 (2), 8 (2), total 16 candidates for C-RNTI and SPS C-RNTI. Total blind decodes = 44 per subframe.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.212 §5.3.3.1, TS 36.213 §9.1.1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Baseband, Nokia AirScale, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q018", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "Resource Allocation", "question": "Explain Resource Allocation Type 0, Type 1, and Type 2 in LTE Downlink. Why does Type 0 use Resource Block Groups (RBGs)?", "detailedAnswer": "In LTE DL, PRBs can be assigned using 3 methods: Type 0 (RBG Bitmap): PRBs are grouped into Resource Block Groups (RBG size $P \\in \\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$ depending on channel bandwidth, e.g. $P=4$ for 20 MHz / 100 RBs). The DCI contains a bitmap of $\\lceil N_{RB}^{DL} / P \\rceil$ bits (25 bits for 20 MHz), where each bit assigns one entire RBG. This dramatically cuts DCI payload size from 100 bits down to 25 bits. Type 1 (RBG Subsets): Divides the carrier into $P$ subsets of RBGs, with a shift parameter and bitmap addressing individual PRBs within a selected subset. Type 2 (Resource Indication Value - RIV): Allocates a set of contiguous localized or distributed PRBs using a single combinatorial RIV number: If $(L_{CRBs} - 1) \\le \\lfloor N_{RB}^{DL} / 2 \\rfloor$: $RIV = N_{RB}^{DL}(L_{CRBs} - 1) + RB_{start}$. Otherwise: $RIV = N_{RB}^{DL}(N_{RB}^{DL} - L_{CRBs} + 1) + (N_{RB}^{DL} - 1 - RB_{start})$. RIV requires only $\\lceil \\log_2(N_{RB}^{DL}(N_{RB}^{DL}+1)/2) \\rceil$ bits (13 bits for 20 MHz).", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.213 §7.1.6 (Resource allocation types)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Scheduler, Apple Munich Modem, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q019", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "Semi-Persistent Scheduling", "question": "How does Semi-Persistent Scheduling (SPS) operate in LTE DL? How is it activated, retransmitted, and released by the MAC layer?", "detailedAnswer": "SPS reduces PDCCH control overhead for periodic, deterministic traffic such as VoIP or periodic industrial IoT telemetry: 1) Configuration: RRC configures SPS C-RNTI and periodicity (e.g. 20 ms). 2) Activation: eNB transmits a DL DCI (Format 1/1A/2) scrambled with SPS C-RNTI where NDI = 0 and validation special fields match (e.g. TPC command set to '00', Cyclic Shift DM-RS set to '000'). Once activated, the UE automatically expects PDSCH on the assigned PRBs every 20 ms without receiving PDCCH. 3) Retransmissions: Retransmissions for SPS are NOT semi-persistent; they are dynamically scheduled by PDCCH scrambled with SPS C-RNTI with NDI = 1. 4) Deactivation / Release: eNB transmits DCI scrambled with SPS C-RNTI where NDI = 0, MCS = 11111, and Resource Allocation bits are all 1s (TS 36.213 Table 9.2-1).", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.321 §5.10, TS 36.213 §9.2", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 RAN, Nokia AirScale, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q020", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "Buffer Status Reporting", "question": "Explain the Buffer Status Report (BSR) MAC Control Elements in LTE: Regular BSR, Periodic BSR, and Padding BSR. How does the MAC determine when to trigger each?", "detailedAnswer": "BSR informs the eNB how much data is buffered across 4 Logical Channel Groups (LCG 0 to 3) in the UE's RLC/PDCP buffers. 1) Regular BSR: Triggered when: (a) data arrives for an LCG with higher priority than existing buffered data; or (b) `retxBSR-Timer` expires while data exists in buffers. A Regular BSR triggers a Scheduling Request (SR) on PUCCH if no UL-SCH grant is available. 2) Periodic BSR: Triggered periodically when `periodicBSR-Timer` expires. 3) Padding BSR: Triggered if UL grant capacity exceeds MAC PDU payload and remaining padding bits can accommodate a BSR subheader + BSR payload (Short BSR = 1 LCG + 6-bit buffer index; Long BSR = 4 LCG buffer sizes). Format: Short BSR (1 octet: 2-bit LCG ID + 6-bit Buffer Size index). Long BSR (3 octets: 6-bit buffer indices for LCG 0, 1, 2, 3).", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.321 §5.4.5 & §6.1.3.1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 MAC, Samsung SRI-B, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q021", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "Power Headroom Reporting", "question": "What is the Power Headroom Report (PHR) MAC CE? Differentiate between Type 1 and Type 2 PHR in LTE Carrier Aggregation.", "detailedAnswer": "PHR provides the eNB scheduler with the difference between UE maximum transmit power $P_{CMAX,c}$ and estimated PUSCH transmit power on serving cell $c$: $PH = P_{CMAX,c} - P_{PUSCH,c}$ (in dB, range -23 dB to +40 dB). 1) Type 1 PHR: Measures headroom considering PUSCH transmission only: $PH_{type1} = P_{CMAX,c} - 10\\log_{10}(M_{PUSCH}) - P_{O\\_PUSCH} - \\alpha \\cdot PL - \\Delta_{TF} - f(i)$. 2) Type 2 PHR: Transmitted specifically for the PCell to convey headroom considering simultaneous PUSCH and PUCCH transmission: $PH_{type2} = P_{CMAX,c} - 10\\log_{10}(10^{P_{PUSCH}/10} + 10^{P_{PUCCH}/10})$. Extended PHR (Rel-10) and Dual Connectivity PHR (Rel-12) include a bitmap indicating active serving cells, reporting individual Type 1 PH and $P_{CMAX,c}$ for each activated SCell.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.321 §5.4.6 & §6.1.3.6, TS 36.213 §5.1.1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Scheduler, Apple Munich Modem, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q022", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "HARQ Entity Architecture", "question": "In an eNodeB MAC DL scheduler software architecture, how does the HARQ entity communicate with the Resource Allocator and the Multiplexing/PDU assembly module?", "detailedAnswer": "The eNodeB MAC DL architecture operates on a strict pipelined subframe timeline: 1) Subframe $n-4$ Feedback Ingestion: The HARQ entity receives ACK/NACK/DTX indications from L1 PHY (from PUCCH/PUSCH decode). 2) HARQ State Update: For ACK, the HARQ process state is transitioned to IDLE, and the associated buffer descriptors are freed back to the zero-copy buffer pool. For NACK or DTX, the retransmission counter is incremented; if counter < `maxHARQ-Tx`, the process is queued in the High-Priority Retransmission List. If max reached, process is released and RLC layer handles recovery. 3) Scheduling Step: At subframe $n$, Resource Allocator scans the High-Priority Retransmission List first. If retransmissions are scheduled, it requests the HARQ entity to re-fetch the existing MAC PDU descriptor and sets NDI unchanged, selecting RV (e.g. RV2). Only residual PRBs are granted to New Data (NDI toggled, RV0) via the Multiplexing module.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.321 §5.3.1, Ericsson/Nokia Baseband Architecture", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Scheduler, Nokia Munich AirScale, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q023", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "Multiplexing & Assembly", "question": "How does the MAC layer calculate Transport Block Size (TBS) in LTE DL given the allocated number of PRBs and MCS index?", "detailedAnswer": "TBS calculation in LTE follows a deterministic table lookup defined in 3GPP TS 36.213: 1) MCS index $I_{MCS} \\in [0, 28]$ is mapped to Modulation Order ($Q_m \\in \\{2, 4, 6, 8\\}$) and TBS Index $I_{TBS} \\in [0, 26]$ using TS 36.213 Table 7.1.7.1-1. 2) Given $I_{TBS}$ and the allocated number of PRBs $N_{PRB} \\in [1, 110]$, the exact TBS in bits is read directly from Table 7.1.7.2.1-1. 3) For spatial multiplexing with 2 layers (Rank 2), if mapping a single transport block to 2 layers, a scaling rule or 2-layer TBS table is applied. 4) Once TBS is determined, if $TBS > 6144$ bits, the transport block is segmented into multiple Code Blocks (CBs) at PHY layer with a 24-bit CRC attached to each CB.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.213 §7.1.7 (Transport Block Size determination)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Baseband, Samsung SRI-B, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q024", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "Carrier Aggregation & SCell", "question": "What happens to the UE MAC HARQ buffers and state machines when an SCell is deactivated while a DL HARQ process is awaiting retransmission?", "detailedAnswer": "Per 3GPP TS 36.321 §5.13: When an SCell is deactivated (either via SCell Deactivation MAC CE or `sCellDeactivationTimer` expiration): 1) The MAC entity MUST flush all DL HARQ buffers associated with that SCell immediately. All pending retransmissions are aborted at MAC layer. 2) Any ongoing HARQ process contexts are reset to IDLE state. 3) If RLC was operating in Acknowledged Mode (AM), the missing data will eventually trigger an RLC Status Report (NACK) from the peer RLC entity once transmission resumes on PCell or another active SCell, initiating an RLC-level retransmission. 4) UE stops transmitting SRS, stops reporting CSI for that SCell, and stops monitoring PDCCH for cross-carrier or direct scheduling on that SCell.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.321 §5.13 (Handling of SCell Deactivation)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Samsung SRI-B, Ericsson L2 RAN, Apple Modem", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q025", "category": "3GPP LTE MAC DL", "subcategory": "PDCCH Order RACH", "question": "Explain the PDCCH Order procedure for initiating non-contention Random Access from Downlink MAC.", "detailedAnswer": "When Downlink data arrives for an RRC_CONNECTED UE whose Uplink is out-of-sync (`timeAlignmentTimer` expired), the eNB cannot schedule DL data because the UE cannot send PUCCH HARQ-ACK. The eNB initiates a PDCCH Order: 1) Transmits DCI Format 1A with CRC scrambled by UE's C-RNTI where localized VRB assignment flag is set to 0 and all PRB bits are set to 1. 2) The DCI contains a 6-bit Preamble Index and a 4-bit PRACH Mask Index. 3) If Preamble Index $\\ne 000000$, it assigns a dedicated contention-free preamble (CFRA). 4) Upon reception, UE MAC triggers CFRA, transmitting the assigned preamble on the PRACH resource dictated by PRACH Mask Index. When RAR is received matching the preamble, UL synchronization is re-established without contention resolution.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.212 §5.3.3.1.3, TS 36.321 §5.1.1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Protocol, Nokia Munich, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q026", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "Numerology & Slot Formats", "question": "Detail 5G NR numerologies (mu = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4). How does Subcarrier Spacing (SCS) alter slot duration, and what are the direct consequences on the MAC scheduler loop deadline?", "detailedAnswer": "5G NR defines scalable OFDM numerology: $\\Delta f = 15 \\times 2^\\mu$ kHz. - $\\mu=0$: 15 kHz SCS, 1 ms slot (14 symbols), 1 slot/subframe (LTE legacy baseline). - $\\mu=1$: 30 kHz SCS, 0.5 ms slot (500 µs), 2 slots/subframe (standard sub-6 GHz / FR1). - $\\mu=2$: 60 kHz SCS, 0.25 ms slot (250 µs), 4 slots/subframe (FR1 & FR2). - $\\mu=3$: 120 kHz SCS, 0.125 ms slot (125 µs), 8 slots/subframe (mmWave / FR2). - $\\mu=4$: 240 kHz SCS, 62.5 µs slot, 16 slots/subframe (SSB beam sweeping in FR2). Impact on MAC Scheduler: In LTE, the scheduler had a generous 1 ms TTI budget to run UE selection, PRB allocation, AMC, and PDU multiplexing. In 5G NR FR1 (30 kHz), the scheduling loop must complete in $< 500$ µs, and in FR2 mmWave (120 kHz), the entire scheduling pipeline must execute deterministically in $< 125$ µs (typically $< 40$ µs compute budget excluding hardware/Fronthaul transport). This mandates lock-free architecture, zero-copy buffers, and SIMD/hardware-accelerated scheduling.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.211 §4.2 & §4.3.2, TS 38.300 §5.1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN, Nokia AirScale, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q027", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "Flexible Slot Formats", "question": "Explain Dynamic Slot Format Indication (SFI) via DCI 2_0 in 5G NR compared to semi-static TDD-UL-DL-ConfigCommon.", "detailedAnswer": "5G NR supports highly flexible TDD symbol allocation where each of the 14 symbols in a slot can be Downlink (D), Uplink (U), or Flexible (F). 1) Semi-Static Configuration: `TDD-UL-DL-ConfigCommon` (and dedicated) broadcast via SIB1/RRC sets a periodic pattern (e.g. DDDFU at 2.5 ms). Symbols set to D or U are fixed and cannot be overridden. 2) Dynamic SFI (DCI 2_0): Monitored on Common Search Space scrambled by SFI-RNTI. It carries slot format combination indices (from 3GPP TS 38.213 Table 11.1.1-1) for one or multiple slots. Dynamic SFI can convert 'Flexible' symbols into explicit Downlink or Uplink symbols on-the-fly to adapt to bursty asymmetric traffic, but CANNOT override semi-statically configured D or U symbols.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.213 §11.1.1, TS 38.331", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 RAN, Nokia AirScale, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q028", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "Mini-Slot Scheduling", "question": "What is Mini-Slot (Type B) scheduling in 5G NR MAC? How does it differ from Slot-based (Type A) scheduling, and how is it used for URLLC?", "detailedAnswer": "Slot-based (Mapping Type A) scheduling starts at symbol 0 (or 1) of the slot and spans 4 to 14 symbols, with DMRS typically located at symbol 2 or 3. Mini-slot (Mapping Type B) scheduling allows PDSCH/PUSCH transmissions to start at ANY arbitrary symbol within the slot (symbols 0 to 12) with a flexible duration of 2, 4, or 7 OFDM symbols. DMRS is located at the first symbol of the mini-slot transmission. Role in URLLC: An urgent URLLC packet arriving mid-slot does not need to wait for the next slot boundary (which would introduce 250-500 µs latency). The MAC scheduler immediately issues a Type B grant that punctures/preempts ongoing eMBB transmissions in the current slot, achieving sub-millisecond radio interface latency.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.1.2.1, TS 38.300 §6.3.2", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN, Nokia Munich, Apple Modem", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q029", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "Bandwidth Part (BWP)", "question": "Explain Bandwidth Part (BWP) framework in 5G NR MAC. How does BWP switching occur via DCI, timers, and RRC?", "detailedAnswer": "A BWP is a contiguous subset of Common Resource Blocks (CRBs) configured with a specific numerology (SCS and cyclic prefix) on a given carrier. A UE can be configured with up to 4 DL BWPs and 4 UL BWPs per serving cell, but only ONE DL BWP and ONE UL BWP can be active at any given time (for single-carrier operation). Types: Initial BWP (used for initial access / SIB1), Active BWP (currently used for scheduling), Default BWP (fallback power-saving BWP). BWP Switching mechanisms: 1) DCI-based: Bandwidth Part Indicator field in DCI 0_1 / 1_1 dynamically switches the active BWP. 2) Timer-based: UE runs `bwp-InactivityTimer`. If no DCI is received on the active BWP before the timer expires, UE automatically switches back to the Default BWP (saving power on a narrow bandwidth). 3) RRC-based: Dedicated RRC reconfiguration. Upon BWP switch: All ongoing HARQ processes on the old BWP are retained, but PRB allocations are mapped to the new BWP's grid.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.15 (Bandwidth Part Operation)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Scheduler, Apple Munich Modem, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q030", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "Beam Management & TCI", "question": "What are Transmission Configuration Indicator (TCI) states and Quasi-Co-Location (QCL) types in 5G NR? How does the MAC layer activate and switch beams?", "detailedAnswer": "In 5G NR mmWave and massive MIMO, high-gain narrow beams are required. TCI states inform the UE which reference signal (SSB or CSI-RS) is quasi-co-located with the PDSCH/PDCCH DMRS, allowing the UE to apply the correct spatial Rx filter (analog beam). QCL Types: - QCL-TypeA: Doppler shift, Doppler spread, average delay, delay spread. - QCL-TypeB: Doppler shift, Doppler spread. - QCL-TypeC: Doppler shift, average delay. - QCL-TypeD: Spatial Rx parameter (critical for beamforming reception). MAC Layer Beam Activation: RRC configures up to 64 or 128 TCI states. MAC CE ('Enhanced TCI States Activation/Deactivation for UE-specific PDSCH MAC CE', LCID 101110) activates a subset of up to 8 TCI states. The 3-bit TCI field in DCI 1_1 then dynamically selects among these 8 active TCI states on a slot-by-slot basis.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.321 §6.1.3.14, TS 38.214 §5.1.5", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Antenna Systems / L2, Nokia Munich, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q031", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "MAC PDU Structure Redesign", "question": "How did 3GPP redesign the MAC PDU structure in 5G NR compared to LTE? Why was this change made for high-speed hardware processing?", "detailedAnswer": "In LTE, all MAC subheaders are chained together at the start of the MAC PDU, followed by all MAC CEs and SDUs at the end. Problem in LTE: The MAC layer could not generate subheaders until all SDU lengths were known, preventing parallel assembly and streaming to hardware PHY. 5G NR Redesign: In TS 38.321 §6.1.2, each MAC subheader is placed immediately preceding its corresponding MAC SDU or MAC CE. Formats: 1) Fixed-length MAC subheader: [R (1b) | F (1b) | LCID (6b)] without L field. 2) Variable-length MAC subheader: [R | F | LCID] followed by 8-bit or 16-bit L field (F=0 for 8-bit, F=1 for 16-bit). Benefit: MAC subheaders and RLC SDUs can be assembled and DMA-transferred to L1 PHY in real-time streaming chunks (pipelined) without waiting for the entire PDU to finish building. Pre-padding is also eliminated; padding subheaders can be placed anywhere.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.321 §6.1.2 (MAC PDU formats)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN Baseband, Apple Munich Modem, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q032", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "HARQ & Dynamic Timing", "question": "Explain dynamic HARQ feedback timing (K1) in 5G NR DL. How does it eliminate the rigid n+4 timing of LTE?", "detailedAnswer": "In LTE FDD, HARQ ACK/NACK feedback had a rigid fixed timing: PDSCH in subframe $n$ was always acknowledged on PUCCH in subframe $n+4$. In 5G NR, feedback timing is fully dynamic: DCI Format 1_0 / 1_1 includes a 3-bit 'PDSCH-to-HARQ_feedback timing indicator' ($K_1$). If PDSCH is scheduled in slot $n$, HARQ feedback is transmitted on PUCCH in slot $n + K_1$, where $K_1 \\in \\{0, 1, 2, \\dots, 15\\}$ configured via RRC parameter `dl-DataToUL-ACK`. Benefits: 1) Accommodates diverse UE processing capabilities: High-end UEs with fast decoders can use $K_1 = 1$ or $2$ (ultra-low latency), while low-cost RedCap/IoT UEs can use $K_1 = 4$ or $6$. 2) Supports flexible TDD: Avoids waiting for fixed subframe patterns.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.213 §9.2.3, TS 38.321 §5.4.2", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Scheduler, Apple Munich Modem, Nokia AirScale", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q033", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "CBG-Based HARQ", "question": "What is Code Block Group (CBG) based HARQ retransmission in 5G NR? How do CBGFI and CBGTI bitmaps reduce retransmission overhead?", "detailedAnswer": "When a large transport block (e.g. 50,000 bits) is transmitted in eMBB, it is segmented into dozens of Code Blocks (CBs), each with its own CRC. In LTE, if even one single CB fails, the entire Transport Block must be retransmitted, wasting radio resources. 5G NR introduces CBG-based HARQ (TS 38.214 §5.1.7.2): 1) RRC groups CBs into up to $N = 2, 4, 6,$ or $8$ Code Block Groups (CBGs). 2) The UE reports a multi-bit HARQ-ACK bitmap where each bit represents the ACK/NACK status of an individual CBG. 3) In retransmission DCI 1_1, the gNodeB includes: - CBGTI (CBG Transmission Information) bitmap indicating exactly which CBGs are being retransmitted. - CBGFI (CBG Flush Indicator) indicating whether the receiver buffer for that CBG should be flushed or soft-combined. Result: Retransmission overhead is reduced by up to 75-85% in high-data-rate scenarios.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.1.7.2, TS 38.321 §5.4.2.1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Baseband, Nokia AirScale, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q034", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "Configured Grants", "question": "Compare Configured Grant Type 1 and Configured Grant Type 2 in 5G NR Uplink MAC. How do they eliminate the SR-BSR-Grant handshake latency?", "detailedAnswer": "Standard dynamic UL grant requires: Data arrival -> Scheduling Request (SR) on PUCCH -> UL Grant on PDCCH -> Buffer Status Report (BSR) -> Final Grant -> PUSCH transmission (latency 8-20 ms). Configured Grants allow autonomous PUSCH transmission without prior dynamic grants: 1) Type 1: The entire grant configuration (PRBs, MCS, periodicity, HARQ process allocation, time-domain offset) is provided purely via RRC signaling (`ConfiguredGrantConfig`). It is active immediately upon RRC configuration without needing any L1 PDCCH activation. 2) Type 2: Periodicity and high-level parameters are configured via RRC, but actual activation and deactivation are triggered dynamically via PDCCH scrambled with CS-RNTI. Crucial for URLLC: UE transmits packets immediately upon arrival at MAC layer, achieving $< 1$ ms uplink latency.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.8.2 (Configured Grant)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN, Nokia Munich, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q035", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "URLLC & Preemption", "question": "How does Downlink Preemption work in 5G NR? Explain DCI 2_1 and how the gNodeB informs eMBB UEs of punctured resources.", "detailedAnswer": "In 5G NR co-existence of eMBB and URLLC, when an urgent URLLC packet arrives, the scheduler cannot wait for an eMBB slot to complete. It immediately punctures (overwrites) the PRBs and OFDM symbols of ongoing eMBB PDSCH transmissions. Because punctured eMBB code blocks will suffer corrupted LLRs in the soft buffer, 3GPP TS 38.213 §11.2 defines Downlink Preemption Indication via DCI 2_1: 1) DCI 2_1 is addressed to INT-RNTI and transmitted in common search space. 2) It contains a 14-bit preemption bitmap per serving cell, mapping to a time-frequency grid (e.g. 14 symbols grouped into intervals and PRBs divided into 2 frequency sets). 3) eMBB UEs decode DCI 2_1 after the fact, zero out the corrupted LLRs in the soft buffer for those punctured REs, preventing soft-combining corruption during HARQ retransmissions.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.213 §11.2, TS 38.300 §6.5", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Scheduler, Nokia Munich AirScale, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q036", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "2-Step RACH", "question": "Explain the 2-Step RACH procedure introduced in 3GPP Rel-16 (MsgA and MsgB). What are Success RAR and Fallback RAR?", "detailedAnswer": "Legacy 4-step RACH requires 4 round-trips: Msg1 (Preamble) -> Msg2 (RAR) -> Msg3 (Scheduled PUSCH) -> Msg4 (Contention Resolution). Rel-16 2-Step RACH merges these into two steps to cut connection setup and small-data latency: 1) MsgA: Transmitted by UE, combining PRACH preamble (in PRACH occasion) and a PUSCH payload (in associated PUSCH occasion / PO) containing UE ID and RRC message. 2) MsgB: Transmitted by gNodeB in response: - Success RAR: Sent if gNodeB successfully decoded both preamble and MsgA PUSCH. Contains Contention Resolution ID, Timing Advance, and assigned C-RNTI. Contention is resolved immediately in Step 2! - Fallback RAR: Sent if gNodeB detected the preamble but failed to decode MsgA PUSCH. Contains RAPID, Timing Advance, and an explicit UL Grant for Msg3, falling back gracefully to 4-step RACH.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.1.1a & §6.1.5a (2-step RACH)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Baseband, Apple Munich Modem, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q037", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "LCP Restrictions in NR", "question": "What are the new Logical Channel Prioritization (LCP) restrictions introduced in 5G NR TS 38.321? How do they ensure URLLC traffic is mapped to suitable radio resources?", "detailedAnswer": "In LTE, LCP only mapped logical channels to grants based on priority and PBR. In 5G NR, different services (URLLC vs eMBB) require radically different physical layer configurations (e.g. 60 kHz SCS vs 15 kHz SCS). TS 38.321 §5.4.3.1.2 introduced 4 explicit LCP restrictions per logical channel: 1) `allowedSCS-List`: Specifies which Subcarrier Spacings the logical channel can be transmitted on (e.g. URLLC channel restricted to $\\ge 60$ kHz). 2) `maxPUSCH-Duration`: Restricts the logical channel to grants with duration $\\le$ configured value (e.g. $\\le 4$ symbols, forcing it onto mini-slots). 3) `configuredGrantType1Allowed`: Controls whether the logical channel can use autonomous Configured Grant Type 1. 4) `allowedServingCells`: Restricts logical channel to specific CCs (e.g. licensed spectrum only). During LCP, the MAC MUST filter out any UL grant that does not satisfy all configured restrictions for that logical channel.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.4.3.1.2 (LCP Restrictions)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Protocol, Nokia Munich, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q038", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "Dual Connectivity (EN-DC)", "question": "In EN-DC (Option 3x), explain the division of responsibilities between LTE MeNB and NR en-gNB MAC entities for split bearers.", "detailedAnswer": "In EN-DC Option 3x (3GPP TS 37.340), the EPC connects to the 5G en-gNB for user plane (S1-U terminates at en-gNB), while MeNB handles S1-MME control plane: 1) Bearer Split: The PDCP layer at en-gNB splits user plane downlink traffic between: - Local transmission path: Routed to en-gNB RLC -> en-gNB MAC -> 5G NR air interface. - Remote transmission path: Forwarded over X2-U to MeNB RLC -> MeNB MAC -> LTE air interface. 2) MAC Autonomy: The LTE MAC and NR MAC entities operate completely independently with their own HARQ entities, schedulers, and numerologies. Neither MAC scheduler knows the instantaneous subframe state of the other. 3) Flow Control: NR PDCP uses X2-U User Plane protocol (TS 38.425) to track delivery status from LTE MeNB and dynamically adjust the splitting ratio based on buffer delays and radio link quality.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 37.340 §4.2.2 & §7.0, TS 38.425", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN, Samsung SRI-B, Nokia Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q039", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "Secondary Cell Group (SCG) Failure", "question": "What triggers an SCG Failure in 5G NR Dual Connectivity at the MAC layer, and what is the reporting procedure?", "detailedAnswer": "In Dual Connectivity, SCG Failure occurs when the connection between UE and Secondary Node (SN / en-gNB) breaks down. MAC-level triggers per TS 38.331: 1) Random Access Problem on PSCell: Contention-based or contention-free RACH on PSCell fails `preambleTransMax` times without success. 2) Radio Link Failure on PSCell: `T310` expires following $N310$ consecutive out-of-sync indications from PHY without recovery. 3) SCG RLC Retransmission Limit: RLC reaches `maxRetxThreshold`. Procedure: The UE suspends all SCG transmissions, stops SCG timers, but maintains the Master Cell Group (MCG) link. It sends an `SCGFailureInformation` RRC message over the LTE PCell (via SRB1 or split SRB) containing failure cause (`synchReconfigFailureSCG`, `scg-l2-failure`, `randomAccessProblem`), allowing the Master Node to trigger SN release or SN change.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.331 §5.7.3 (SCG failure information)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2/L3, Apple Munich Modem, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q040", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "DCI & CORESET", "question": "What is a CORESET (Control Resource Set) in 5G NR? How does it differ from the legacy LTE PDCCH region?", "detailedAnswer": "In LTE, PDCCH spans the entire carrier bandwidth (1.4 MHz to 20 MHz) over the first 1, 2, 3, or 4 OFDM symbols of every subframe indicated by PCFICH. In 5G NR, wide carriers (up to 100-400 MHz) make full-band control monitoring inefficient and power-prohibitive. CORESET (TS 38.211 §7.3.2.2): A localized time-frequency resource allocated for PDCCH: - Frequency: Multiples of 6 PRBs (Resource Block Groups) configured via bitmap. - Time: 1, 2, or 3 consecutive OFDM symbols. Each CORESET is associated with Search Space Sets defining monitoring periodicities and slot offsets. CORESET 0 is configured via MIB/PBCH for initial access (SIB1 decoding). Up to 3 CORESETs can be configured per BWP. Interleaved or non-interleaved CCE-to-REG mapping can be chosen per CORESET to achieve frequency diversity or localized beamforming.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.211 §7.3.2.2, TS 38.213 §10.1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Baseband, Apple Munich Modem, Nokia AirScale", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q041", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "Dynamic Spectrum Sharing (DSS)", "question": "How does Dynamic Spectrum Sharing (DSS) between LTE and 5G NR work at the MAC layer? How are LTE Cell-Specific Reference Signals (CRS) protected?", "detailedAnswer": "DSS enables simultaneous deployment of LTE and 5G NR on the same FDD carrier frequency by dynamically sharing PRBs on a 1 ms / 0.5 ms subframe basis. MAC & PHY Coordination: 1) LTE CRS Protection: LTE requires continuous CRS transmission across 1, 2, or 4 antenna ports in every non-MBSFN subframe. When 5G NR schedules PDSCH in an LTE subframe, NR MAC/PHY must rate-match around the LTE CRS Resource Elements (REs). The gNodeB configures `rateMatchPatternLTE-CRS` in NR RRC, defining LTE carrier center frequency, bandwidth, and antenna ports. 2) SSB Transmission: NR Synchronization Signal Blocks (SSB) span 4 OFDM symbols and cannot rate-match around CRS; DSS deploys LTE MBSFN subframes (which have no CRS in the data region) to transmit NR SSBs. 3) Dynamic MAC Scheduling: Fast inter-stack scheduler communication or joint scheduler dynamically shares PRBs based on real-time buffer loads.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.1.4.3 (Rate matching for LTE-CRS)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Scheduler (Pioneer in DSS), Deutsche Telekom, Nokia", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q042", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "Beam Failure Recovery", "question": "Explain the Beam Failure Recovery (BFR) MAC procedure in 5G NR. What is the BFR MAC Control Element?", "detailedAnswer": "When the active serving beam degrades (e.g. blocked by hand or obstacle) in mmWave: 1) Beam Failure Detection (BFD): PHY assesses radio quality on configured periodic BFD-RS. If hypothetical PDCCH BLER drops below threshold $Q_{out\\_LR}$, PHY sends a 'beam failure instance' (BFI) indication to MAC. 2) MAC BFI Filtering: MAC increments `BFI_COUNTER`. If counter reaches `beamFailureInstanceMaxCount` within `beamFailureDetectionTimer`, Beam Failure is declared. 3) Candidate Beam Identification: PHY searches for a new beam on candidate beam RS with quality above $Q_{in\\_LR}$. 4) BFR Request: MAC initiates BFR via contention-free PRACH on dedicated BFR occasion, or if SCell BFR (Rel-16), sends a BFR MAC CE (LCID 110101) containing serving cell ID and the new candidate beam index. 5) Response: UE monitors dedicated CORESET for response from gNodeB to complete recovery.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.17 (Beam Failure Recovery procedure)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2/L1, Apple Munich Modem, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q043", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "Cross-Carrier Scheduling in NR", "question": "How does Cross-Carrier Scheduling operate in 5G NR when the scheduling cell and scheduled cell have different Subcarrier Spacings (SCS)?", "detailedAnswer": "In 5G NR Carrier Aggregation, Cross-Carrier Scheduling allows PDCCH on a scheduling cell (e.g. PCell at 15 kHz SCS) to schedule PDSCH/PUSCH on an SCell (e.g. at 30 kHz or 120 kHz SCS) using a 3-bit Carrier Indicator Field (CIF). When SCS differs between scheduling and scheduled cells: 1) If Scheduling Cell SCS < Scheduled Cell SCS (e.g. 15 kHz scheduling 30 kHz): One slot of the scheduling cell corresponds to multiple slots of the scheduled cell (2 slots). DCI must include explicit time-domain resource assignment (TDRA) indicating which specific sub-slot in the scheduled cell is being granted. 2) If Scheduling Cell SCS > Scheduled Cell SCS: Multiple PDCCH occasions exist per scheduled cell slot; 3GPP rules restrict minimum scheduling delay ($K_0, K_2$) to account for cross-carrier decoding.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.1.2.3, TS 38.213 §10.1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Scheduler, Apple Munich Modem, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q044", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "RedCap (NR-Light)", "question": "What are the MAC layer implications of 3GPP Rel-17 Reduced Capability (RedCap) UEs?", "detailedAnswer": "Rel-17 RedCap bridges the gap between high-speed eMBB and low-power LPWAN (NB-IoT/Cat-M) for wearables and industrial sensors: Hardware Constraints: Max bandwidth reduced to 20 MHz (FR1) / 100 MHz (FR2), single receiver antenna (1 Rx) mandatory (2 Rx optional), half-duplex FDD (HD-FDD). MAC Layer Adaptations: 1) Relaxed Processing Times: Processing times $N_1$ (PDSCH processing to HARQ feedback) and $N_2$ (PDCCH to PUSCH transmission) are extended, requiring MAC scheduler to use higher $K_1$ and $K_2$ values. 2) RedCap Early Identification: UE indicates RedCap capability during RACH (via separate PRACH occasions or MsgA/Msg3 payload) so MAC scheduler applies RedCap-specific scheduling policies immediately. 3) BWP Restrictions: RedCap operates within a maximum 20 MHz separate initial BWP to coexist with 100 MHz eMBB UEs.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.306 §4.2.21, TS 38.321 §5.1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson IoT / Baseband, Rohde & Schwarz, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q045", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "Power Saving & DCP", "question": "Explain DCI 2_6 (Dormancy and Wake-Up Indication) and its role in 5G NR Connected-mode DRX power saving.", "detailedAnswer": "In legacy cDRX, a UE must wake up its RF circuitry at the start of every DRX cycle to monitor PDCCH during `onDurationTimer`, even when no data is buffered (which happens $>90\\%$ of the time in bursty traffic). Rel-16 introduced DCI 2_6 (DCP - DCI with CRC scrambled by PS-RNTI): 1) Wake-Up Indication: DCI 2_6 is transmitted a configured time ($gap$) before the `onDurationTimer` starts. It contains a 1-bit wake-up indication per UE. If bit = 1: UE wakes up and monitors PDCCH during the upcoming `onDurationTimer`. If bit = 0: UE skips the entire `onDurationTimer`, remaining asleep for that DRX cycle (saving up to 30-50% battery). 2) SCell Dormancy Indication: DCI 2_6 also carries a bitmap instructing UEs to transition specific SCells into a dormant BWP (where CSI reporting continues but PDCCH monitoring stops).", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.213 §10.3, TS 38.321 §5.7", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Apple Munich Cellular Modem, Ericsson L2 DU, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q046", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "Multiple TA Groups", "question": "Why are Multiple Timing Advance Groups (pTAG and sTAG) needed in 5G NR Carrier Aggregation?", "detailedAnswer": "When carrier aggregation involves component carriers from different frequency bands (inter-band CA) or RRHs (Remote Radio Heads) deployed at different geographical sites: Propagation delays from the UE to the different reception points differ significantly. A single Timing Advance value cannot time-align uplinks arriving at different antennas. 3GPP TS 38.321 groups serving cells into Timing Advance Groups (TAGs): - Primary TAG (pTAG): Contains the PCell/PSCell. Uses RAR or MAC CE for TA updates. - Secondary TAG (sTAG): Contains only SCells. Uses dedicated Timing Advance Command MAC CEs. Each TAG maintains its own independent `timeAlignmentTimer`. If an sTAG's timer expires, only SCells belonging to that sTAG are considered out-of-sync and stop transmitting UL, while the pTAG continues operating normally.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.2 (Maintenance of Uplink Time Alignment)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Baseband, Samsung SRI-B, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q047", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "HARQ Entity Architecture", "question": "Compare 5G NR DL HARQ process allocation to LTE: Why does 5G NR support up to 16/32 HARQ processes?", "detailedAnswer": "LTE supported 8 HARQ processes in FDD. In 5G NR: 1) Higher Numerologies and High Latency: At 60 kHz / 120 kHz SCS, slot durations shrink to 250 µs / 125 µs. If the round-trip time (RTT = processing delay + scheduling pipeline) is 2 ms, that spans 16 slots at 120 kHz! Having only 8 processes would cause HARQ stall (no available process to transmit new data while waiting for feedback). 2) 3GPP Rel-15 supported 16 DL HARQ processes per serving cell. 3) Rel-17 expanded this to up to 32 HARQ processes for NTN (Non-Terrestrial Networks / Satellite 5G) where round-trip propagation delays reach 20 ms to 500 ms (GEO/LEO satellites), requiring massive process pools to saturate the link.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.4.2, TS 38.306", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN, Apple Munich Modem, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q048", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "O-RAN 7-2x Functional Split", "question": "In an Open-RAN (O-RAN) architecture, what MAC functions reside in the O-DU versus O-CU, and what is the Fronthaul eCPRI interface split?", "detailedAnswer": "O-RAN adopts the 3GPP 7-2x split between O-RU (Radio Unit) and O-DU (Distributed Unit), and the Higher-Layer Split (Split 2) between O-DU and O-CU (Central Unit): 1) O-RU: Contains RF, digital beamforming, FFT/IFFT, CP insertion, and PRACH filtering. 2) O-DU: Contains High-PHY (channel coding, rate matching, modulation, layer mapping), MAC (Real-Time Scheduler, HARQ, Multiplexing, RACH handling), and RLC. 3) O-CU: Split into O-CU-CP (RRC) and O-CU-UP (PDCP, SDAP). 4) Fronthaul Split 7-2x carries IQ data, beamforming weights, and real-time control (C/U/S/M plane) over eCPRI (10G/25G Ethernet). All hard real-time scheduling decisions must run in the O-DU.", "codeOrSpecRef": "O-RAN Working Group 4 (Fronthaul Spec), 3GPP TS 38.401", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud-RAN, Nokia AirScale, Mavenir Germany", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q049", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "Prioritized Scheduling & Preemption", "question": "How does 3GPP Rel-16 Uplink Cancellation Indication (DCI 2_4) work at the MAC layer to protect URLLC PUSCH from eMBB PUSCH collisions?", "detailedAnswer": "While DCI 2_1 handles DL preemption, Rel-16 DCI 2_4 handles Uplink Cancellation (CI): 1) Scenario: An eMBB UE has been granted a regular slot-based PUSCH. Suddenly, another UE (or the same UE's URLLC logical channel) needs to transmit a high-priority latency-critical URLLC packet on overlapping REs. 2) DCI 2_4: Addressed to CI-RNTI, broadcast to UEs. It contains a bitmap indicating specific time-frequency regions in upcoming symbols where UEs must cancel scheduled PUSCH/PUCCH. 3) MAC Action: The eMBB UE's MAC cancels the transmission of the indicated symbols on-the-fly, preventing inter-UE interference on the URLLC transmission, and reschedules or drops the cancelled MAC PDU.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.213 §11.2A, TS 38.321 §5.4.3", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Scheduler, Nokia Munich AirScale, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q050", "category": "5G NR MAC Evolution", "subcategory": "Carrier Aggregation in NR", "question": "What is PUCCH Carrier Switching in 5G NR Carrier Aggregation, and why was it introduced in Rel-16?", "detailedAnswer": "In 5G NR TDD Carrier Aggregation, different component carriers may use different TDD patterns or may face asymmetric channel conditions: In Rel-15 CA, PUCCH was restricted to the PCell. In TDD, if PCell has a long DL subframe sequence with no UL slot, the UE cannot transmit HARQ-ACK even if an SCell has an available UL slot, stalling DL scheduling! Rel-16 PUCCH Carrier Switching enables dynamic or semi-static switching of PUCCH transmission between PCell and SCell (or between two SCells) on a slot-by-slot basis, selecting whichever CC has an available UL slot to send immediate HARQ-ACK.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.213 §9.0, TS 38.321 §5.1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Baseband, Apple Munich Modem, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q051", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Cache & Memory Alignment", "question": "Explain false sharing in multi-core telecom L2 stacks. How do you prevent it using C++ alignas(64) and hardware cache line padding?", "detailedAnswer": "Modern x86 and ARM server processors load memory into L1/L2 caches in blocks called cache lines (typically 64 bytes). False sharing occurs when two independent threads executing on separate CPU cores modify distinct variables that reside within the same 64-byte cache line. Even though the variables are logically independent, the cache coherency protocol (MESI/MOESI) invalidates the entire cache line whenever either core writes, forcing the other core to flush and reload from L3 cache or RAM (cache-line bouncing), causing a massive 10x-50x latency penalty! Prevention in C++: struct alignas(64) PerCoreQueueHead { std::atomic<uint32_t> head; }; Or insert explicit padding: struct SchedulerStats { uint64_t tx_pkts; uint8_t pad[56]; uint64_t rx_pkts; }; This ensures variables modified by different threads reside on strictly separate cache lines.", "codeOrSpecRef": "C++11/C++17 alignas / alignof, Intel/ARM Architecture Manuals", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN L2, Nokia AirScale, Apple Munich Modem", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q052", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Lock-Free Programming", "question": "Write or explain a Single-Producer Single-Consumer (SPSC) lock-free ring buffer in Modern C++. Why are memory_order_acquire and memory_order_release sufficient instead of seq_cst?", "detailedAnswer": "An SPSC queue operates with two pointers: `head` (written only by Producer, read by Consumer) and `tail` (written only by Consumer, read by Producer). Producer Step: 1) Reads `tail` with `memory_order_relaxed`. If buffer full, return false. 2) Writes payload data to `buffer[head]`. 3) Increments and publishes `head` using `head.store(next_head, std::memory_order_release)`. Consumer Step: 1) Reads `head` using `head.load(std::memory_order_acquire)`. If empty, return false. 2) Reads payload data from `buffer[tail]`. 3) Increments and publishes `tail` using `tail.store(next_tail, std::memory_order_release)`. Why acquire/release is sufficient: `memory_order_release` guarantees that all prior stores (writing packet data) become visible to any thread that executes `memory_order_acquire` on the same atomic variable. We do not need a total global sequential order across all threads (`seq_cst`), avoiding expensive CPU bus-lock cycles and memory barriers on x86/ARM.", "codeOrSpecRef": "C++11 <atomic>, std::memory_order", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Baseband, Apple Munich Modem, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q053", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Memory Models & Hardware Barriers", "question": "What is the difference between compiler reordering and CPU hardware out-of-order execution? How do DMB, DSB, and ISB instructions in ARM relate to C++ memory orders?", "detailedAnswer": "Compiler Reordering: During optimization (-O2, -O3), the compiler reorders non-dependent instructions to maximize register utilization and pipelining. Controlled via compiler barriers: `asm volatile(\"\" ::: \"memory\")`. CPU Hardware Reordering: Out-of-order execution engines (especially weak-ordered architectures like ARM and POWER) reorder loads and stores at runtime. x86 is strongly ordered (TSO - Total Store Order) where stores are never reordered with older stores. ARM Memory Barriers: - DMB (Data Memory Barrier): Ensures that all prior memory accesses appear before subsequent memory accesses to other threads (implements `memory_order_acquire` / `release`). - DSB (Data Synchronization Barrier): Blocks execution until all memory transactions complete. - ISB (Instruction Synchronization Barrier): Flushes CPU pipeline and re-fetches instructions.", "codeOrSpecRef": "ARM Architecture Reference Manual, C++ Memory Model ISO/IEC 14882", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Apple Munich Cellular Firmware, Ericsson Baseband, Intel", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q054", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Zero-Copy Architecture", "question": "How do you design a zero-copy packet buffer descriptor pool (similar to DPDK rte_mbuf or Linux sk_buff) for high-speed L1-L2-L3 communication?", "detailedAnswer": "In telecom stacks handling Gbps throughput, copying packet payloads (`memcpy`) between PHY, MAC, and RLC would consume 100% CPU and saturate memory buses. Zero-Copy Architecture: 1) Pre-allocate a contiguous memory pool (hugepages, 1GB/2MB) during startup. 2) Each packet buffer consists of a Header Descriptor (`mbuf`) and a Data Buffer pointer. Descriptor fields: `data_ptr`, `data_len`, `ref_count` (atomic), `headroom` (reserved space for MAC/RLC headers), and `tailroom` (for padding/CRC). 3) Pipelining: L1 DMA deposits raw payload into Data Buffer. MAC layer simply prepends MAC subheader by adjusting `data_ptr -= header_size` and updates `data_len += header_size`. No payload copying occurs. 4) Retransmission: For HARQ, MAC increments `ref_count`. When ACK is received, `ref_count` is decremented; when it hits 0, buffer is returned to the lock-free pool.", "codeOrSpecRef": "DPDK rte_mbuf design, Linux sk_buff kernel architecture", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud-RAN, Nokia AirScale, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q055", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Real-Time Determinism", "question": "Why is dynamic memory allocation (malloc/free, new/delete) strictly prohibited inside the 1ms / 125µs MAC scheduling thread? How do fixed-size block allocators solve this?", "detailedAnswer": "Reasons dynamic allocation is prohibited in real-time loops: 1) Non-deterministic execution time: Standard heap allocators traverse free-lists or manage buddy-allocator trees ($O(N)$ or $O(\\log N)$ in worst-case fragmentation), with latency variance from 20 ns to $>100$ µs. 2) Mutex Contention: Global heap pools lock internal mutexes; if another thread is allocating, the real-time scheduling thread will be blocked, blowing past its 125 µs TTI deadline. 3) Memory Fragmentation: Continuous allocation/deallocation in 24/7 telecom operations eventually fragments virtual memory, causing catastrophic allocation failures or page faults. Solution: Fixed-size Object Memory Pools (Slab / Arena Allocators). Memory for all UE contexts, HARQ descriptors, and MAC PDUs is pre-allocated at startup. Allocation and deallocation are $O(1)$ operations manipulating a lock-free singly linked list of free indices.", "codeOrSpecRef": "POSIX real-time guidelines, MISRA C++ Rule 18-4-1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Baseband, Apple Munich Modem, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q056", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "RTOS Scheduling", "question": "Compare Rate Monotonic Scheduling (RMS) and Earliest Deadline First (EDF). How does Linux SCHED_FIFO differ from SCHED_DEADLINE?", "detailedAnswer": "1) Rate Monotonic Scheduling (RMS): Static-priority algorithm where tasks with shorter periods are assigned higher priorities (e.g. 125 µs FR2 task has higher priority than 1 ms FR1 task). Utilisation bound for $n$ tasks is $U = n(2^{1/n} - 1) \\approx 69.3\\%$. 2) Earliest Deadline First (EDF): Dynamic-priority algorithm where task whose absolute deadline is nearest gets highest priority. Optimal with theoretical 100% CPU utilisation bound. Linux Kernel Implementations: - `SCHED_FIFO`: POSIX real-time static priority policy (priorities 1 to 99). A `SCHED_FIFO` thread runs until it yields, blocks on I/O, or is preempted by a higher-priority FIFO thread. No time-slicing. - `SCHED_DEADLINE`: Uses Constant Bandwidth Server (CBS) and EDF. Each task specifies runtime, deadline, and period (e.g. 50 µs runtime every 125 µs). The kernel enforces bandwidth reservation; if task overruns, it is throttled.", "codeOrSpecRef": "Linux sched(7), POSIX.1b IEEE Std 1003.1b", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud-RAN, Nokia AirScale, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q057", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Real-Time Linux OS", "question": "What Linux kernel configurations and boot parameters are required to achieve sub-20 microsecond latency jitter for Cloud RAN MAC processing?", "detailedAnswer": "Standard enterprise Linux is unsuitable for L2 scheduling due to scheduling jitter and interrupts. Tuning required: 1) PREEMPT_RT Patch: Converts in-kernel spinlocks into preemptible sleeping mutexes and forces hard-IRQs into threaded handlers. 2) CPU Core Isolation: Boot parameters `isolcpus=2-7` and `nohz_full=2-7` remove isolated cores from OS task scheduling and disable the periodic 1000 Hz timer tick when running a single thread. 3) RCU Offloading: `rcu_nocbs=2-7` offloads Read-Copy-Update callbacks to housekeeping cores (cores 0-1). 4) Interrupt Shielding: Set `/proc/irq/default_smp_affinity` to cores 0-1, preventing device IRQs (NIC, NVMe) from interrupting L2 cores. 5) Memory Locking: Call `mlockall(MCL_CURRENT | MCL_FUTURE)` to lock all virtual memory into physical RAM, eliminating page faults. 6) Thread Configuration: Set scheduling policy to `SCHED_FIFO` priority 98 and pin thread via `pthread_setaffinity_np`.", "codeOrSpecRef": "Linux PREEMPT_RT documentation, Cloud RAN Deployment Best Practices", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN, Nokia AirScale, Intel Telecom", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q058", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Concurrency & Deadlock", "question": "Explain the Priority Inversion problem in RTOS. How do Priority Inheritance Protocol (PIP) and Priority Ceiling Protocol (PCP) eliminate unbounded priority inversion?", "detailedAnswer": "Scenario: Low-priority task $L$ acquires mutex $M$. High-priority task $H$ arrives and blocks waiting for $M$. Before $L$ can finish and release $M$, Medium-priority task $M_{ed}$ (which does not need $M$) preempts $L$. Task $H$ is now blocked indefinitely by $M_{ed}$! This is unbounded priority inversion (caused the famous 1997 Mars Pathfinder reset bug). Solutions: 1) Priority Inheritance Protocol (PIP): When task $H$ blocks on mutex $M$ held by $L$, task $L$'s priority is temporarily boosted to match $H$'s priority until $L$ releases $M$. $M_{ed}$ cannot preempt $L$, bounding inversion to the critical section length. 2) Priority Ceiling Protocol (PCP): Each mutex is assigned a static ceiling priority equal to the highest priority task that can ever lock it. A task can only lock a mutex if its priority is strictly higher than the ceilings of all currently locked mutexes. PCP completely eliminates both unbounded priority inversion and mutual-exclusion deadlocks.", "codeOrSpecRef": "POSIX pthread_mutexattr_setprotocol (PTHREAD_PRIO_INHERIT)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Baseband, Apple Munich Modem, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q059", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Cache-Friendly Data Structures", "question": "Why is Structure-of-Arrays (SoA) often preferred over Array-of-Structures (AoS) when the MAC scheduler iterates over 500 active UE contexts?", "detailedAnswer": "Consider a UE context structure with 256 bytes (HARQ state, RRC configs, timers, CQI, buffer status). In Array-of-Structures (`UEContext ues[500]`): When the scheduler scans all UEs just to check which ones have pending DL data (`buffer_occupancy > 0`), it reads only 4 bytes out of every 256-byte struct. Because CPU fetches 64 bytes per cache line, $75\\%$ to $90\\%$ of cache data loaded into L1/L2 is completely wasted (cache pollution and memory bus bandwidth waste). In Structure-of-Arrays (SoA): struct ActiveUEList { uint32_t buffer_occupancy[500]; uint8_t cqi[500]; uint8_t harq_free_mask[500]; }; In SoA, `buffer_occupancy` values are stored contiguously in memory. A single 64-byte cache line loads the buffer status for 16 consecutive UEs! SIMD vector instructions (AVX-512 / ARM Neon) can evaluate 16 UEs in a single clock cycle, dramatically speeding up candidate selection.", "codeOrSpecRef": "Modern CPU Microarchitecture / Data-Oriented Design (DOD)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN L2 Scheduler, Apple Munich Modem", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q060", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Bitwise Manipulation", "question": "Demonstrate how to pack and unpack 3GPP bitfields (e.g. DCI fields or MAC subheaders) without endianness bugs and without undefined behavior from unaligned pointer casting.", "detailedAnswer": "Common Bug: Casting `uint8_t*` buffer to a C `struct` with bitfields (`struct { uint8_t r:1, f:1, lcid:6; }`) is non-portable because the C/C++ standards leave bitfield packing order (LSB vs MSB) implementation-defined. Moreover, casting unaligned addresses causes SIGBUS on ARM. Robust Approach: Use explicit bitwise shift and mask operations: // Packing NR MAC subheader (1-byte header: R(1b), F(1b), LCID(6b)) inline uint8_t pack_nr_mac_subhdr(uint8_t lcid, bool f) {     return ((f ? 1u : 0u) << 6) | (lcid & 0x3Fu); } // Unpacking RIV (Resource Indication Value): inline void decode_riv(uint32_t riv, uint32_t N_RB, uint32_t &rb_start, uint32_t &L_crbs) {     uint32_t L = (riv / N_RB) + 1;     uint32_t S = riv % N_RB;     if (L <= N_RB / 2) { L_crbs = L; rb_start = S; }     else { L_crbs = N_RB - L + 2; rb_start = N_RB - 1 - S; } } This approach is 100% standard-compliant, portable across endiannesses, and easily autovectorized.", "codeOrSpecRef": "ISO/IEC 9899 (C11) §6.7.2.1, MISRA C:2012 Rule 6.1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Apple Munich Cellular Firmware, Ericsson Baseband, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q061", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "GDB & Post-Mortem Debugging", "question": "What is your systematic process for diagnosing a SIGSEGV / SIGBUS core dump in a real-time multi-threaded MAC baseband engine using GDB?", "detailedAnswer": "Step-by-step triage: 1) Load dump: `gdb <executable> <core.dump>` 2) Immediate crash site: Run `bt` (backtrace) and `info threads` to identify which thread crashed and what all other threads were doing. 3) Register inspection: Run `info registers`. Check `$pc` (program counter) and fault address in `$_siginfo._sifields._sigfault.si_addr`. If address is close to 0 (e.g. 0x28), it is a NULL pointer dereference with struct offset. If address is 0xdeadbeef or non-canonical (0x7fff...), suspect wild pointer or stack overflow. 4) Frame analysis: Switch to crashing frame: `frame <N>`. Inspect local variables: `info locals`, `info args`. 5) Disassembly: If stack is corrupted (trampled return address): `disassemble /m $pc-32, $pc+32` and check CPU registers against assembly instructions to reconstruct function parameters.", "codeOrSpecRef": "GDB User Manual, Linux Core Dump Handling", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Samsung SRI-B, Ericsson L2 Baseband, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q062", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Memory Corruption & Sanitizers", "question": "How do AddressSanitizer (ASan) and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer (UBSan) work under the hood? What are shadow memory and redzones?", "detailedAnswer": "ASan detects out-of-bounds accesses, use-after-free, and stack use-after-scope with ~2x CPU overhead: 1) Shadow Memory: Maps 1/8th of virtual address space directly to 'shadow bytes'. 1 shadow byte tracks the validity of 8 bytes of application memory (e.g. 0 = all 8 bytes addressable; negative/poison = unaddressable). 2) Redzones: ASan surrounds every heap allocation, global variable, and stack variable with inaccessible 'redzones' (poisoned shadow memory). 3) Instrumentation: Compiler inserts checks before every memory load/store: byte* shadow = (addr >> 3) + 0x7fff8000; if (*shadow) __asan_report_error(addr); If code attempts to read or write into a redzone or freed block, ASan triggers an instant crash with a complete allocation/deallocation stack trace.", "codeOrSpecRef": "LLVM Clang AddressSanitizer Documentation (-fsanitize=address)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Software, Apple Munich Modem, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q063", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Race Condition Diagnosis", "question": "How do you detect and fix data races in multi-threaded MAC stacks using ThreadSanitizer (TSan)? What constitutes a data race under the C++ memory model?", "detailedAnswer": "Definition: A data race occurs when two or more concurrent threads access the exact same memory location without synchronization, where at least one of the accesses is a write. In C++, data races result in Undefined Behavior (UB), allowing the compiler to generate corrupted code or stale register caching. TSan Mechanism: TSan instruments memory accesses and synchronisation primitives (mutexes, atomics). It builds a happens-before state machine using vector clocks. If two non-atomic accesses to the same address are not ordered by a happens-before relationship, TSan prints an immediate error with stack traces of both conflicting threads. Fixes: 1) Use `std::atomic<T>` for shared flags/counters. 2) Protect multi-variable invariants with mutexes or read-write locks (`std::shared_mutex`). 3) Redesign into single-writer architectures (e.g. actor model, SPSC queues).", "codeOrSpecRef": "ISO/IEC 14882:2020 §6.9.2, LLVM ThreadSanitizer (-fsanitize=thread)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN, Apple Munich Modem, Nokia AirScale", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q064", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Modern C++20 in Telecom", "question": "How can Modern C++ features like std::span, constexpr lookup tables, and [[likely]] / [[unlikely]] attributes optimize telecom L2 performance?", "detailedAnswer": "1) `std::span` (C++20): Represents a contiguous non-owning view of a memory buffer `(pointer, length)`. Replaces unsafe `(uint8_t* ptr, int len)` pairs and eliminates buffer copying. Enables sub-slicing of MAC SDUs without overhead: `auto sdu = pdu_span.subspan(offset, sdu_len)`. 2) `constexpr` Compile-Time Tables: Heavy 3GPP tables (e.g. CQI-to-SINR, Transport Block Size tables in TS 38.214, RIV formulas) can be evaluated at compile time into static ROM tables: `constexpr auto tbs_table = generate_tbs_table();`, saving runtime CPU cycles. 3) Branch Prediction Hints: `[[likely]]` and `[[unlikely]]` guide compiler branch layout: if (harq_ack == ACK) [[likely]] { free_harq_process(); } else [[unlikely]] { queue_retransmission(); } Ensures the dominant execution path is placed contiguous in instruction memory, maximising CPU instruction pre-fetcher hit rates.", "codeOrSpecRef": "C++20 Standard (ISO/IEC 14882:2020)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Baseband, Apple Munich Modem, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q065", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Lock-Free Ring Buffer Math", "question": "Why must lock-free ring buffer capacities be powers of two (2^N)? Show the branchless index wrapping optimization.", "detailedAnswer": "Standard circular buffer index advancement uses modulo arithmetic: `index = (index + 1) % CAPACITY;`. Modulo (`%`) compiles to integer division instructions (`idiv` on x86, `sdiv` on ARM), which take 10 to 40 CPU clock cycles and stall the arithmetic logic unit (ALU). If `CAPACITY` is constrained to a power of two ($2^N$, e.g. 1024): `(index + 1) % CAPACITY` is mathematically identical to `(index + 1) & (CAPACITY - 1)`. The bitwise AND (`&`) instruction takes exactly 1 clock cycle and is branchless. Furthermore, by using monotonically increasing 64-bit integer counters (`head`, `tail`), the buffer occupancy is calculated simply as `head - tail` without needing explicit wrap-around conditional checks until the index is masked against `CAPACITY - 1`.", "codeOrSpecRef": "Computer Architecture / Bitwise Optimization Algorithms", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Baseband, Apple Munich Modem, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q066", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Volatile vs Atomic", "question": "Why is declaring a variable volatile insufficient for multi-threaded synchronization in C/C++? What does volatile actually do?", "detailedAnswer": "Massive Misconception: Many developers believe `volatile bool flag` is sufficient for inter-thread communication. What `volatile` DOES: It tells the compiler that the memory location can be modified outside the knowledge of the program (e.g. memory-mapped hardware I/O register, interrupt service routine). The compiler is prevented from caching the variable in a CPU register or optimizing away reads/writes. What `volatile` DOES NOT DO: 1) It provides ZERO memory barriers: Hardware CPU out-of-order execution will freely reorder reads/writes across the volatile access! 2) It does NOT provide atomicity: Increments (`val++`) are not atomic and suffer race conditions. `std::atomic<T>` provides BOTH compiler optimization barriers AND hardware CPU memory fences (acquire/release/seq_cst) alongside atomic read-modify-write instructions (LOCK CMPXCHG).", "codeOrSpecRef": "ISO/IEC 14882 §10.1.7.1, Linux Kernel Documentation: volatile-considered-harmful", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Apple Munich Cellular Firmware, Ericsson Baseband, Intel", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q067", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Circular Buffers & Memory Overwriting", "question": "How do you implement an overwrite-on-full circular buffer for real-time modem trace logging without acquiring locks?", "detailedAnswer": "In high-frequency modem trace logging (e.g. QXDM/Shannon logging), log generation must NEVER block or drop the real-time scheduler thread even if the logging daemon is lagging. Implementation: 1) Use a large circular buffer with atomic sequence numbers. 2) Producer (L2 MAC thread): uint64_t seq = write_seq.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed); uint32_t slot = seq & (BUFFER_SIZE - 1); buffer[slot].seq = seq; // write sequence tag memcpy(buffer[slot].data, log_msg, msg_len); 3) Consumer (Background disk/USB writer thread): Tracks `read_seq`. If `read_seq < write_seq - BUFFER_SIZE`, the producer has overwritten data. The consumer simply detects the sequence jump, records a 'DROPPED_LOGS_COUNT', advances `read_seq = write_seq - BUFFER_SIZE`, and resumes reading. The real-time producer runs 100% wait-free without locking or stalling.", "codeOrSpecRef": "Linux trace ring-buffer design, Samsung Shannon Logging Engine", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Samsung SRI-B, Apple Munich Modem, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q068", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Real-Time Machine Learning Inference", "question": "In your M.Tech thesis, how did you deploy an ML model via Treelite into a 280 KB native C binary capable of sub-millisecond inference within a 1 ms TTI budget?", "detailedAnswer": "The candidate's M.Tech thesis solved blind PUCCH Format 3 allocation in LTE-A CA: Challenge: Python ML frameworks (Scikit-Learn, PyTorch, ONNX Runtime) introduce runtime interpreter overhead, heavy shared library loading, and dynamic memory allocations incompatible with a 1 ms hard real-time TTI budget. Treelite Compilation: 1) Trained gradient-boosted decision tree models (XGBoost/LightGBM) were ingested into Treelite. 2) Treelite compiles the entire ensemble of trees directly into native C code as nested `if-else` branches and static lookup tables. 3) Compiled with `-O3 -fPIC` into a tiny ~280 KB native binary with ZERO dynamic memory allocation during inference. 4) Achieved 0.42 ms single-sample inference (0.58 ms batch of 12), fitting easily inside the 1 ms TTI budget, slashing uplink PUCCH wastage from 58% down to 6.4% while raising scheduling hit rate from 42% to 91%.", "codeOrSpecRef": "Treelite Model Compiler, Candidate M.Tech Thesis (IISc)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud-RAN (AI in L2), Samsung SRI-B (Key Strength)", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q069", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Inter-Process Communication (IPC)", "question": "Compare POSIX Shared Memory (shm_open + mmap) with Linux UNIX Domain Sockets for high-throughput L1-to-L2 baseband message passing.", "detailedAnswer": "1) UNIX Domain Sockets (`AF_UNIX`): Involves standard socket API (`sendmsg`/`recvmsg`). Packets pass through the Linux kernel network/VFS stack, requiring memory allocation of `sk_buff` and copying data twice (user-to-kernel, kernel-to-user), with kernel context-switch latency ~5-15 µs. Throughput is limited to ~2-4 GB/s. 2) POSIX Shared Memory (`shm_open` + `mmap`): Both L1 PHY and L2 MAC processes map the exact same physical RAM pages into their respective virtual address spaces. Once mapped, communication occurs at native memory bus speed (>50 GB/s) with ZERO syscalls and ZERO copies! Synchronization is achieved using lock-free atomic ring buffers placed directly inside the shared memory segment. This is the industry-standard architecture for O-RAN O-DU FAPI interfaces.", "codeOrSpecRef": "POSIX.1-2001 shm_open(3), O-RAN FAPI Interface Specification", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN, Nokia AirScale, Mavenir", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q070", "category": "Embedded C/C++ & RTOS", "subcategory": "Memory Leaks in Long-Run Soak Tests", "question": "How do you detect and isolate slow memory leaks in a telecom L2 baseband software system that only crashes after 72 hours of continuous traffic load?", "detailedAnswer": "Systematic isolation strategy: 1) AddressSanitizer/Valgrind are often too slow for 72-hour full-rate soak tests (causing real-time deadline misses). 2) Heap Profiling: Use `gperftools` (TCMalloc) or `jemalloc` with profiling enabled (`MALLOC_CONF=\"prof:true,prof_prefix:jeprof.out\"`). These sample 1 out of every $N$ allocations with minimal (<2%) CPU overhead. 3) Differential Analysis: Dump heap profile at hour 24 and hour 48. Use `jeprof --base=jeprof.24h jeprof.48h --text` to generate a diff showing which specific call site's memory footprint is monotonically growing. 4) Common Culprits in Telecom L2: - RRC connection release failure where UE context or HARQ descriptor is not returned to the free pool. - Timer queue cancellations where callback payload wrapper is leaked. - Fragmented SDU buffers in RLC reassembly queues.", "codeOrSpecRef": "Jemalloc / TCMalloc Profiling, Linux Memory Management", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Samsung SRI-B, Ericsson L2 Software, Apple Munich Modem", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q071", "category": "Telecom Stack Debugging & RCA", "subcategory": "Field Log Debugging (QXDM/QCAT)", "question": "In Qualcomm QXDM log analysis, which log packets do you inspect to diagnose sudden Downlink throughput collapse? What do 0xB173, 0xB14D, and 0xB139 reveal?", "detailedAnswer": "In Qualcomm diagnostic logs: - `0xB173` (LTE MAC DL Transport Block): Displays TB size, MCS, CRC status (Pass/Fail), and HARQ process ID for every scheduled subframe. A throughput collapse accompanied by high CRC failures indicates physical channel fading or interference. - `0xB14D` (LTE ML1 PDCCH DCI Decode): Shows decoded DCI grants. If 0xB14D shows grants are being received but 0xB173 shows no TBs, L1 PDSCH decoding was not triggered. If 0xB14D shows NO grants while UE has active traffic, eNB scheduler stopped scheduling the UE. - `0xB139` (LTE ML1 PDSCH Status / Demodulation): Reveals SNR, channel estimate, Rank, and modulation order per layer. Triage Flow: (1) Check 0xB14D for DCI grants -> (2) Check 0xB173 for CRC and BLER -> (3) Check 0xB139 for SNR/CQI correlation -> (4) Check 0xB171 (MAC DL TB Information) for SDU multiplexing.", "codeOrSpecRef": "Qualcomm QXDM / QCAT Diagnostic Packet Reference Manual", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Samsung SRI-B, Apple Munich Modem, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q072", "category": "Telecom Stack Debugging & RCA", "subcategory": "Viavi TM500 UE Emulation", "question": "How do you use Viavi TM500 test equipment to reproduce and debug a multi-UE Downlink HARQ BLER degradation under high cell load?", "detailedAnswer": "Viavi TM500 is the industry-standard multi-UE network emulator used by Ericsson, Samsung, and Nokia: 1) Configuration: Configure TM500 with a traffic profile of 50 to 100 emulated UEs across various fading channel models (e.g. EPA 5Hz, EVA 70Hz, ETU 300Hz). 2) Real-Time Monitoring: Monitor TM500 KPI dashboard: Cell Aggregate Throughput, Residual BLER, PDCCH DTX rate, and HARQ retransmission distribution (Retx 1, 2, 3, 4). 3) Debugging Degradation: - If 1st transmission BLER is 10% but Residual BLER (after max retx) is high: Check if eNB scheduler is starving HARQ retransmissions to prioritize new data. - If DTX rate is high: UE is failing to decode PDCCH DCI, pointing to PDCCH aggregation level being too low or CCE blocking on eNB. - Export TM500 PHY/MAC trace logs and correlate with eNB L1/L2 internal trace logs using GPS/SPS frame timestamps.", "codeOrSpecRef": "Viavi TM500 Operations Manual, 3GPP TS 36.141 / TS 38.141", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Samsung SRI-B, Ericsson L2 Test, Rohde & Schwarz Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q073", "category": "Telecom Stack Debugging & RCA", "subcategory": "HARQ NACK Storm RCA", "question": "Root Cause Analysis: Field logs show an eNB experiencing a massive Downlink HARQ NACK storm, causing complete throughput stall. Differentiate between PDCCH DTX, PDSCH CRC failure, and PUCCH decoding failure.", "detailedAnswer": "Systematic Triangulation: 1) PDCCH DTX (Discontinuous Transmission): UE never decoded the DCI grant. UE transmits NOTHING on PUCCH in subframe $n+4$. If eNB L1 interprets silence (DTX) as NACK, it needlessly triggers retransmissions. Check eNB L1 DTX detection threshold. 2) PDSCH CRC Failure: UE received DCI, attempted to decode PDSCH, but CRC failed. UE transmits explicit NACK on PUCCH. If CQI reported by UE was high, this indicates sudden interference, radar bursts, or an overly aggressive eNB OLLA algorithm driving MCS too high. 3) PUCCH Decoding Failure at eNodeB: UE successfully decoded PDSCH and transmitted ACK on PUCCH, but eNB failed to decode PUCCH (due to uplink interference or incorrect Uplink Timing Advance) and defaulted to NACK (ACK-to-NACK error). In QXDM: Look at 0xB173 on UE side. If UE logs show CRC PASS, but eNB logs show NACK and retransmits, the root cause is 100% in the Uplink PUCCH decoding path at eNodeB!", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.213 §7.0, Samsung/Ericsson Field Debugging Guide", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Baseband, Samsung SRI-B, Apple Munich Modem", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q074", "category": "Telecom Stack Debugging & RCA", "subcategory": "SCell Activation Failure RCA", "question": "RCA Scenario: eNodeB transmits an SCell Activation MAC CE, but the UE never starts reporting CSI and never monitors PDCCH for the SCell. Walk through your debugging checklist.", "detailedAnswer": "Checklist based on 3GPP TS 36.321 §5.13 and candidate's core expertise: 1) Verify MAC CE Transmission: Check MAC DL logs to ensure the SCell Activation MAC CE was actually transmitted and received ACK on PCell HARQ. Check if the bitmap bit corresponding to the SCellIndex was set to 1. 2) Check RF Warm-Up Timing: Verify if eNB waited at least $n+24$ or $n+34$ subframes before expecting CSI. If eNB scheduled too early, it misinterprets silence as failure. 3) Check `sCellDeactivationTimer`: Check if `sCellDeactivationTimer` was configured with an ultra-short value (e.g. 20 ms) and expired before the first data scheduling arrived. 4) RRC Configuration Mismatch: Inspect UE RRC configuration in logs. Did RRC reconfigure or release the SCell concurrently with the MAC CE? 5) UE RF/Baseband Failure: In QXDM logs, check if UE RF transceiver reported PLL lock failure or AGC saturation on the SCell frequency band.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.321 §5.13, Candidate SCell State Machine Expertise", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Samsung SRI-B, Ericsson L2 RAN, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q075", "category": "Telecom Stack Debugging & RCA", "subcategory": "PHY-MAC Timing Jitter", "question": "What happens when software execution jitter causes the MAC layer to deliver a DL Transport Block to L1 PHY 50 microseconds past the subframe boundary deadline?", "detailedAnswer": "L1 PHY operates on hard hardware DMA deadlines tied to the radio front-end framing. Consequences: 1) PHY Underrun: The digital baseband modulator does not have coded bits to fill the I/Q symbol buffers for the upcoming slot. 2) Transmitter Output: PHY must either transmit zeros (zero-padding) or null symbols, causing carrier leakage and RF spectral emission spikes. 3) UE Impact: The UE decodes garbage on PDSCH, resulting in CRC failure and NACK. 4) DCI Discrepancy: If PDCCH was already transmitted in symbols 0-1 announcing a PDSCH grant that PHY could not transmit, the UE demodulator attempts to decode empty REs, causing severe LLR corruption in soft buffer. Prevention: Cloud-RAN MAC engines enforce hard deadlines; if a task misses deadline $T_{slot} - T_{guard}$, the grant is cancelled and discarded before PHY submission.", "codeOrSpecRef": "O-RAN Fronthaul C-Plane Timing Constraints, 3GPP TS 38.104", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud-RAN, Nokia AirScale, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q076", "category": "Telecom Stack Debugging & RCA", "subcategory": "Radio Link Failure (RLF) Triage", "question": "How do you distinguish between an RF-layer coverage hole and an L2 MAC/RLC scheduling deadlock during Radio Link Failure (RLF) triage?", "detailedAnswer": "RLF Triage: 1) RF Coverage Hole: - In UE logs (QXDM), RSRP and RSRQ degrade below threshold (e.g. RSRP < -120 dBm, SNR < -6 dB). - PHY issues $N310$ consecutive out-of-sync ($Q_{out}$) indications. Timer `T310` starts and expires because $N311$ in-sync ($Q_{in}$) indications are never received. 2) L2 MAC/RLC Deadlock (RF is fine, RSRP = -80 dBm): - RLF cause in log is `maxRetxThreshold` reached in RLC AM entity! - The UE has good radio signal, but RLC data transmitted multiple times never gets acknowledged. - Root cause investigation points to MAC: (a) MAC `timeAlignmentTimer` expired, preventing PUCCH transmission; (b) SR failure (`sr-ProhibitTimer` misconfigured or max SR reached); or (c) eNodeB scheduler lock-inversion preventing grant allocation.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.331 §5.3.11 (Radio link failure)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2/L3, Apple Munich Modem, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q077", "category": "Telecom Stack Debugging & RCA", "subcategory": "SR & BSR Stalls", "question": "A UE has 500 KB of data buffered in RLC, but never transmits a Buffer Status Report (BSR) to the eNodeB. What are the potential failure points in the MAC layer?", "detailedAnswer": "Step-by-step diagnostic chain: 1) Check LCP Configuration: Is the logical channel allowed to transmit on the available carrier/numerology? (Check 5G NR LCP restrictions: `allowedServingCells`, `allowedSCS-List`). 2) Check Scheduling Request (SR) Trigger: Regular BSR was triggered, but did it trigger an SR? If no PUCCH resource is configured for SR, the MAC must initiate Contention-Based RACH. Did RACH trigger? 3) Check `sr-ProhibitTimer`: If `sr-ProhibitTimer` is running, the MAC cannot transmit SR. 4) Check `dsr-TransMax`: Did SR reach maximum transmissions (`dsr-TransMax`)? If so, the MAC releases PUCCH/SRS, initiates RACH, and notifies RRC of connection issues. 5) Check Timing Advance: If `timeAlignmentTimer` expired, the UE cannot use PUCCH for SR and must fall back to PRACH.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.4.4 & §5.4.5", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Samsung SRI-B, Ericsson L2 MAC, Apple Munich Modem", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q078", "category": "Telecom Stack Debugging & RCA", "subcategory": "Real-Time ML Latency Spikes", "question": "In an ML-driven predictive MAC scheduler (like your Treelite PUCCH allocator), what factors can cause inference latency spikes exceeding the 0.5 ms real-time budget, and how do you fix them?", "detailedAnswer": "Based on candidate's M.Tech thesis experience: Root Causes: 1) Feature Extraction Overhead: While the decision tree inference is fast (0.42 ms), extracting and computing 20 features (e.g. rolling average BLER, queue growth rate, channel variance) in C++ can take 0.8 ms if not optimized. Fix: Use ring-buffer running accumulators ($O(1)$ updates) instead of iterating over historical arrays. 2) Cache Eviction: If the 280 KB native model binary gets evicted from L2 cache by heavy MAC PDU processing, loading model weights incurs cold-cache penalties. Fix: Lock model text and data segments in memory using `madvise(MADV_WILLNEED)` or dedicated cache ways via Intel RDT (Resource Director Technology) / CAT (Cache Allocation Technology). 3) Branch Misprediction in Deep Trees: High tree depth causes CPU pipeline stalls. Fix: Prune tree depth using Hyperopt to max-depth 6 and compile with profile-guided optimization (`-fprofile-generate` / `-fprofile-use`).", "codeOrSpecRef": "Candidate M.Tech Thesis, Intel RDT / CAT Architecture", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud-RAN AI, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q079", "category": "Telecom Stack Debugging & RCA", "subcategory": "RLC vs MAC Packet Drops", "question": "How do you determine whether packet drops are occurring at the MAC layer or the RLC layer during UDP iperf testing?", "detailedAnswer": "Packet Drop Analysis: 1) MAC Layer Drops: MAC layer NEVER drops individual SDUs silently during normal operation; it only drops an entire MAC PDU when a DL HARQ process reaches `maxHARQ-Tx` retransmissions without receiving ACK. Check MAC KPI: `HARQ_FAIL_COUNT` or `MAC_DISCARD_PKTS`. 2) RLC Layer Drops: - In RLC UM (Unacknowledged Mode): Drops occur when `t-Reordering` timer expires and missing PDUs are skipped, or when RLC transmission buffer overflows. - In RLC AM (Acknowledged Mode): Drops occur when an AMD PDU exceeds `maxRetxThreshold` (which triggers RLF), or when the PDCP discard timer (`discardTimer`, e.g. 100 ms) expires while data is queued in RLC, causing PDCP to instruct RLC to discard the SDU. Check PDCP Discard KPI to confirm if latency-induced discards are occurring before transmission.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.322 §5.2, TS 38.323 §5.4.3", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 RAN, Nokia AirScale, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q080", "category": "Telecom Stack Debugging & RCA", "subcategory": "Inter-Site CA Backhaul RCA", "question": "During Inter-Site Carrier Aggregation over non-ideal backhaul, the SCell experiences severe scheduling starvation. What metrics on the GTP-U / X2-U interface do you inspect?", "detailedAnswer": "In Inter-Site CA (candidate's specialty): 1) GTP-U Round-Trip Latency: Check ping/echo latency between MeNB and SeNB. If latency spikes above 30 ms, flow control updates arrive stale. 2) DL Data Delivery Status (3GPP TS 36.425 / TS 38.425): Inspect the feedback frames sent by SeNB to MeNB. Check: - `Desired Buffer Size`: If SeNB reports zero or near-zero desired buffer size, MeNB stops sending PDCP PDUs, starving the SCell! - Cause of zero buffer: SeNB internal memory allocation stall or backpressure from L1. 3) Packet Jitter & Reordering: Check sequence numbers in GTP-U packets. If packets arrive out-of-order, SeNB spends CPU reordering instead of scheduling. 4) Uplink PUCCH format: Verify if PCell has granted sufficient PUCCH Format 3 capacity for SCell feedback. If PUCCH is stalled, SeNB halts scheduling to prevent HARQ overflow.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.425, Candidate Inter-Site CA Expertise", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Samsung SRI-B, Ericsson Cloud RAN, Nokia Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q081", "category": "Telecom Stack Debugging & RCA", "subcategory": "Samsung Shannon Modem Trace", "question": "Explain how modem CP crash dumps are captured and decoded in Samsung Shannon / LSI baseband architectures.", "detailedAnswer": "In Samsung Exynos / Shannon modem platforms: 1) CP Crash Trigger: When an assertion fails (`CP_ASSERT`) in L2 MAC or a hardware watchdog expires, the Cellular Processor (CP) halts execution. 2) IPC Dump: The CP writes a crash header containing exception vector, register dump ($r0-$r15, $cpsr, $pc), and stack trace into shared memory (SHMEM). 3) AP Dump Collection: The Application Processor (AP) kernel driver detects the CP crash via PCIe/mailbox interrupt, captures the entire CP DDR memory region into a binary crash dump (`modem_crash.bin`), and resets the modem subsystem. 4) Offline Decoding: Engineers load `modem_crash.bin` into Samsung internal crash analysis tools (Shannon Crash Analyzer) alongside the exact build ELF/SYM symbols to map the crash PC to source code lines and inspect active queue descriptors.", "codeOrSpecRef": "Samsung Shannon Modem Baseband Architecture", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Samsung SRI-B, Apple Munich Modem, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q082", "category": "Telecom Stack Debugging & RCA", "subcategory": "DRX Desynchronization Bug", "question": "How does DRX desynchronization happen between eNodeB and UE, and what are the catastrophic symptoms in commercial networks?", "detailedAnswer": "DRX desynchronization occurs when the eNB and UE have conflicting views of whether the UE is in Active Time (awake) or DRX sleep: Root Causes: 1) Lost DRX Command MAC CE: eNB sends DRX Command MAC CE on PDSCH. eNB decodes an ACK (or misinterprets DTX as ACK), so eNB assumes UE went to sleep. But UE received NACK or missed the grant entirely, remaining in Active Time. 2) Clock Drift / SFN Mismatch: In Long DRX, long sleep intervals (e.g. 2.56 seconds) can cause UE internal low-power RTC (32 kHz) to drift relative to eNB SFN frame timing. Symptoms: - eNB schedules DL data or sends Paging during what it thinks is On-Duration, but UE is asleep (100% DL packet drop). - UE transmits SR on PUCCH, but eNB RF receiver is not listening to the UE's PUCCH resource, causing call drop or high latency.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.321 §5.7, TS 38.321 §5.7", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Apple Munich Cellular Modem, Ericsson L2 RAN, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q083", "category": "Telecom Stack Debugging & RCA", "subcategory": "MCS Downgrade Loop Triage", "question": "What is an MCS Downgrade Loop in Outer Loop Link Adaptation (OLLA), and how do you prevent the scheduler from collapsing to MCS 0 under noisy PUCCH conditions?", "detailedAnswer": "Mechanism of MCS Collapse: 1) An eNB scheduler adjusts MCS based on OLLA offset $\\Delta_{OLLA}$. Each received NACK decrements $\\Delta_{OLLA}$ by $\\Delta_{down}$ (e.g. -0.9 dB). 2) If the Uplink channel suffers sudden interference, the eNB fails to decode valid PUCCH ACKs transmitted by the UE, falsely classifying them as DTX/NACK. 3) The OLLA algorithm incorrectly concludes that the Downlink channel is degraded and aggressively penalizes $\\Delta_{OLLA}$, forcing MCS down to MCS 0 (QPSK lowest code rate). 4) Even at MCS 0, if the UL interference persists, PUCCH decodes still fail, locking the scheduler in an endless loop at minimum throughput. Prevention: - Decouple DTX from NACK: Do not apply full $\\Delta_{down}$ penalty to DTX unless confirmed by consecutive losses. - Impose a hard minimum clamp on $\\Delta_{OLLA}$ (e.g. max -6 dB from reported CQI). - Cross-reference with PUSCH UL SINR to detect Uplink-specific interference.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.213 §7.2, Ericsson L2 Scheduler Algorithms", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Scheduler, Nokia AirScale, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q084", "category": "Telecom Stack Debugging & RCA", "subcategory": "Cross-Layer Handover Deadlock", "question": "Describe a cross-layer race condition between the RRC thread executing an X2 Handover Command and the MAC subframe thread processing DL data.", "detailedAnswer": "Scenario: 1) Thread A (RRC Thread): Receives `RRCConnectionReconfiguration` with `mobilityControlInfo` (Handover Command). It acquires `Mutex_RRC` and attempts to acquire `Mutex_MAC` to pause MAC scheduling and flush HARQ buffers. 2) Thread B (MAC Subframe Thread): Currently executing the 1 ms scheduling loop. It acquires `Mutex_MAC` and detects that a RRC status message must be transmitted in Msg4, attempting to acquire `Mutex_RRC` to query RRC buffer status. Result: Classic AB-BA Deadlock! Neither thread can proceed. The 1 ms scheduling deadline expires, the real-time watchdog fires, and the baseband crashes. Solution: - Establish a strict lock hierarchy (e.g. `Mutex_RRC` MUST always be locked before `Mutex_MAC`). - Better: Replace cross-layer locking with lock-free asynchronous message queues (actors) where RRC posts an event to MAC's queue instead of directly acquiring its mutex.", "codeOrSpecRef": "Modern Multi-Threaded Real-Time Architecture Principles", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Samsung SRI-B, Ericsson L2 Baseband, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q085", "category": "Telecom Stack Debugging & RCA", "subcategory": "GDB Disassembly Corrupted Stack", "question": "When a stack smash overwrites the function return address with garbage, backtrace (bt) in GDB fails. How do you recover the call stack manually using assembly inspection?", "detailedAnswer": "Manual Call Stack Recovery on ARM64 / x86_64: 1) On ARM64: - The Frame Pointer is in `$x29` (FP) and Link Register (return address of current function) is in `$x30` (LR). - Run `info registers x29 x30 sp`. - Inspect memory at stack pointer: `x/32gx $sp`. - Walk the stack frame chain manually: Each standard stack frame pushes the previous FP and LR pair: `[x29] = old_FP`, `[x29 + 8] = old_LR`. - Run `x/2gx $x29`. The second value is the caller's return address! Use `info line *$x30` to resolve it to a source line. 2) On x86_64: - Frame pointer is in `$rbp`. Each frame has `[rbp] = old_rbp`, `[rbp + 8] = old_rip`. - Run `x/2gx $rbp`. The value at `[rbp + 8]` is the caller's instruction pointer (`$rip`). Repeat iteratively to unwind the entire corrupted call stack.", "codeOrSpecRef": "ARM64 AAPCS Calling Convention, System V AMD64 ABI", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Apple Munich Cellular Firmware, Ericsson Baseband, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q086", "category": "Telecom System Design", "subcategory": "Scheduler System Design", "question": "Design a 5G NR Downlink MAC Scheduler capable of scheduling 200 active UEs across a 100 MHz carrier within a hard 125 microsecond execution deadline.", "detailedAnswer": "System Architecture Breakdown: 1) Pipeline Stages: Divide execution into 4 pipelined stages executed across isolated CPU cores: - Stage A (HARQ Ingestion & Metric Calculation): Ingests L1 ACK/NACK, updates OLLA, computes PF weight $W_i = R_i(t) / \\bar{R}_i(t) \\times QoS\\_Weight_i$. - Stage B (Candidate Pruning & Sorting): Filter top 20 candidates out of 200 UEs using SIMD-accelerated partial sort (`std::nth_element`). - Stage C (PRB & MCS Allocation): Assign Resource Block Groups (RBGs) using frequency-selective water-filling algorithm and compute TBS. - Stage D (PDU Assembly & FAPI Dispatch): Build MAC subheaders, construct FAPI `DL_TTI.request` and `TX_Data.request`, and dispatch via shared-memory DMA to L1. 2) Zero-Allocation / Lock-Free: Pre-allocate all memory pools at boot. Use SPSC ring buffers for inter-thread communication. 3) Fallback Mechanism: If Stage C runtime exceeds 80 µs, trigger greedy allocation fallback to guarantee FAPI delivery within 125 µs deadline.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.321, 3GPP TS 38.214, O-RAN FAPI Specification", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN L2, Nokia AirScale, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q087", "category": "Telecom System Design", "subcategory": "Proportional Fair Algorithm", "question": "Explain the mathematics, weightings, and implementation details of the Proportional Fair (PF) scheduling algorithm in a QoS-aware 4G/5G MAC scheduler.", "detailedAnswer": "Proportional Fair strikes an optimal balance between maximizing cell aggregate throughput (Max CQI) and maintaining user fairness (Round Robin). Mathematical Formulation: In each TTI, allocate resource block $k$ to user $i^*$ that maximizes the metric: $i^* = \\arg\\max_{i} \\frac{R_i(k, t)}{[\\bar{R}_i(t)]^\\alpha} \\times \\omega_{QoS, i}$ Where: - $R_i(k, t)$: Instantaneous achievable data rate on PRB $k$ estimated from CQI/SINR. - $\\bar{R}_i(t)$: Exponentially smoothed average throughput of user $i$: $\\bar{R}_i(t+1) = (1 - \\frac{1}{T}) \\bar{R}_i(t) + \\frac{1}{T} r_i(t)$, with filter time constant $T$ (e.g. 500-1000 TTIs). - $\\alpha$: Fairness parameter ($\\alpha = 0$ is Max CQI; $\\alpha = 1$ is standard Proportional Fair; $\\alpha \\to \\infty$ is Round Robin). - $\\omega_{QoS, i}$: Dynamic QoS multiplier derived from 5QI / QCI: For GBR (Guaranteed Bit Rate): $\\omega_{QoS} = \\exp(\\frac{GBR - \\bar{R}_i}{GBR})$. If throughput drops below GBR, weight explodes exponentially, guaranteeing priority.", "codeOrSpecRef": "Telecom Scheduling Theory, 3GPP TS 23.501 (QoS Architecture)", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Scheduler, Nokia Munich AirScale, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q088", "category": "Telecom System Design", "subcategory": "Asynchronous HARQ Manager", "question": "Design a high-performance Asynchronous DL HARQ Manager in C++ capable of managing 32 parallel HARQ processes per UE across 8 aggregated carriers.", "detailedAnswer": "Data Structure & Architecture Design: Total processes = $32 \\text{ processes} \\times 8 \\text{ CCs} = 256$ HARQ processes per UE. 1) Memory Layout: Use a flat contiguous array allocated per UE: `struct alignas(64) HarqProcess { uint64_t tb_size; uint8_t rv; uint8_t ndi; uint8_t state; uint16_t cbg_mask; PacketDescriptor* pdu_desc; };` 2) Fast Allocation ($O(1)$): Maintain a 32-bit bitmap `free_process_mask` per CC. Allocating an idle process is a single hardware intrinsic: `int pid = __builtin_ctz(free_process_mask);` followed by `free_process_mask &= ~(1u << pid);`. 3) Retransmission Queues: Maintain a lock-free priority queue for processes requiring retransmission (ordered by expiration timer and QoS). 4) Soft Buffer Accounting: Track total soft channel bits per UE (3GPP TS 38.306 limited buffer rate matching). 5) CBG Management: When CBG feedback arrives, update `cbg_mask` to re-transmit only corrupted groups.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.4.2, C++ Bitwise Manipulation Intrinsics", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Baseband, Apple Munich Modem, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q089", "category": "Telecom System Design", "subcategory": "L2 Inter-Thread IPC Pipeline", "question": "Design the inter-thread IPC pipeline between L1 PHY Driver, L2 MAC Scheduler, and L2 RLC Layer. How do you prevent buffer bloat and queue buildup under heavy traffic?", "detailedAnswer": "Architectural Blueprint: 1) Thread Partitioning: - Thread 1 (L1-RX Receiver): Ingests FAPI `RX_Data.indication` and `UCI.indication` (HARQ/CQI) via shared memory ring buffer. - Thread 2 (MAC Scheduler & Assembler): Runs real-time TTI loop, executes PF scheduling, and builds MAC PDUs. - Thread 3 (RLC Processing): Segments/concatenates RLC SDUs and manages AM retransmission buffers. 2) Queue Architecture: Connect threads using Single-Producer Single-Consumer (SPSC) lock-free ring buffers with cache-line-aligned heads and tails. 3) Buffer Bloat Mitigation: - Implement Credit-Based Backpressure: RLC maintains an atomic credit counter representing available MAC buffer space. When credit reaches zero, RLC stops fetching packets from PDCP. - Discard Timers: Each packet header carries an absolute timestamp. If a packet dwells in the RLC-MAC queue longer than its 3GPP PDB (Packet Delay Budget, e.g. 50 ms for voice), it is dropped immediately before scheduling.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 23.501 Table 5.7.4-1 (QoS Delay Budgets), DPDK Design", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN, Nokia AirScale, Mavenir Germany", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q090", "category": "Telecom System Design", "subcategory": "Inter-Site CA Architecture", "question": "Draw and explain the end-to-end architecture of Inter-Site Carrier Aggregation between MeNB and SeNB over non-ideal backhaul, incorporating your M.Tech ML thesis for predictive PUCCH allocation.", "detailedAnswer": "End-to-End System Design: 1) Control Plane: MeNB handles RRC. SCell addition/activation is decided by MeNB based on measurement reports (RSRP/RSRQ). MeNB sends `X2-AP SCell Addition Request` to SeNB. 2) User Plane Flow: - S1-U traffic terminates at MeNB PDCP. - PDCP splits packets: local packets go to MeNB RLC/MAC (PCell); remote packets are encapsulated into GTP-U and sent over 10-20 ms backhaul to SeNB RLC/MAC (SCell). 3) The Uplink PUCCH Bottleneck: In LTE-A CA, PUCCH HARQ-ACK for both PCell and SCell must be transmitted on the PCell. Because PCell does not know in advance whether SeNB scheduled DL data in subframe $n$, traditional systems blindly allocate large PUCCH Format 3 resources every subframe, wasting 58% of UL bandwidth. 4) ML Thesis Integration: Candidate deployed an XGBoost classifier compiled via Treelite into a 280 KB native C binary on the PCell MAC. The model takes PCell buffer trends, historical SeNB flow control credits, and traffic patterns to predict whether SCell will transmit DL data. Inference executes in 0.42 ms (< 1 ms TTI), dynamically provisioning PUCCH Format 3 only when needed, cutting uplink wastage from 58% to 6.4%!", "codeOrSpecRef": "Candidate M.Tech Thesis (IISc), 3GPP TS 36.300 §5.5, TS 36.425", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Samsung SRI-B, Ericsson Cloud-RAN, Nokia Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q091", "category": "Telecom System Design", "subcategory": "Dynamic BWP Switching Engine", "question": "Design a dynamic Bandwidth Part (BWP) manager that automatically balances UE power consumption and high-throughput bursts.", "detailedAnswer": "Architecture of BWP Management Engine: 1) Configuration: Configure 2 DL BWPs per UE: - BWP 1 (Power-Saving / Default BWP): 20 MHz bandwidth, 15 kHz SCS (narrowband, low RF power). - BWP 2 (Burst eMBB BWP): 100 MHz bandwidth, 30 kHz SCS (wideband, high throughput). 2) Traffic State Machine: - Low/Idle State: UE operates on BWP 1. MAC monitors incoming RLC buffer occupancy. - Burst Detection: When RLC buffer exceeds threshold $B_{high}$ (e.g. 100 KB) or high-priority 5QI bearer arrives: Scheduler issues DCI 1_1 on BWP 1 with 'Bandwidth Part Indicator' set to BWP 2. - Active Burst State: UE switches to BWP 2 within BWP switching latency ($T_{BWP\\_switch} \\approx 1-3$ slots), receives high-speed PDSCH data. - Inactivity Fallback: `bwp-InactivityTimer` is restarted with every DL grant on BWP 2. When buffer empties and timer expires (e.g. 10 ms), UE autonomously switches back to BWP 1.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.15, TS 38.213 §12.0", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Apple Munich Cellular Modem, Ericsson L2 Scheduler, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q092", "category": "Telecom System Design", "subcategory": "Beam-Aware Scheduler Architecture", "question": "Design a Beam-Aware MAC Downlink Scheduler for massive MIMO (32T32R / 64T64R). How do you group UEs into spatial beams and prevent inter-beam interference?", "detailedAnswer": "System Design for Spatial MU-MIMO Scheduling: 1) Channel State Ingestion: UE reports periodic/aperiodic CSI: CRI (CSI-RS Resource Indicator), Rank Indicator (RI), and Precoding Matrix Indicator (PMI). 2) Spatial Compatibility Matrix: Compute cross-correlation between spatial channel covariance matrices of active UEs: $\\rho_{ij} = \\frac{|h_i^H h_j|}{\\|h_i\\| \\|h_j\\|}$. If $\\rho_{ij} < \\text{threshold}$ (e.g. 0.2), UEs $i$ and $j$ are spatially orthogonal (beams point in different directions). If $\\rho_{ij}$ is high, scheduling them on the same PRB causes severe inter-user interference. 3) Beam Grouping: Use a graph clique or greedy clustering algorithm to form spatial co-scheduling groups. 4) Joint Resource Allocation: Each orthogonal spatial group is assigned common PRBs. Zero-Forcing (ZF) or Minimum Mean Square Error (MMSE) precoding weights are calculated at PHY to null residual leakage.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.2, Ericsson Massive MIMO Technical Papers", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Antenna Systems / L2, Nokia Munich, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q093", "category": "Telecom System Design", "subcategory": "URLLC Preemption Engine", "question": "Design an ultra-low latency slot insertion and puncturing engine that interrupts an ongoing eMBB transmission to insert a 2-symbol URLLC mini-slot within 50 microseconds.", "detailedAnswer": "Architectural Flow: 1) Priority Ingress: URLLC packets arrive on a dedicated high-priority lock-free ring buffer directly from the network NIC, triggering a hardware interrupt on the URLLC scheduler core. 2) Preemption Decision ($< 10$ µs): - Check current slot timeline. If next slot boundary is $> 50$ µs away, trigger Mini-Slot Type B scheduling in the CURRENT slot starting at symbol $S = \\text{current\\_symbol} + 2$. - Identify victim eMBB PRBs overlapping the required URLLC bandwidth. 3) Fast FAPI Dispatch ($< 20$ µs): - Send high-priority FAPI `DL_TTI.request` to L1 PHY specifying PDSCH Mapping Type B (duration 2 symbols). - Mark the victim eMBB PDU as punctured in the MAC HARQ state machine. 4) Post-Facto Preemption Indication: In the subsequent monitoring occasion, construct and transmit DCI 2_1 addressed to INT-RNTI, notifying eMBB UEs of the punctured REs so they can zero out soft buffer LLRs.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.213 §11.2, TS 38.214 §5.1.2.1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN, Nokia AirScale, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q094", "category": "Telecom System Design", "subcategory": "Dual Connectivity Flow Control", "question": "Design a dynamic Flow Control algorithm for a 5G Dual Connectivity (EN-DC / NR-DC) PDCP Split Bearer to prevent packet reordering delays at the UE.", "detailedAnswer": "Problem: When PDCP splits traffic across LTE (slow, 30 ms delay) and NR (fast, 5 ms delay), packets sent over LTE arrive much later at the UE, causing massive PDCP reordering window stalls and stalling TCP! Flow Control Algorithm: 1) SeNB / en-gNB sends periodic `DL DATA DELIVERY STATUS` frames over X2-U/Xn-U (3GPP TS 38.425). 2) Feedback Metrics: Highest Delivered PDCP Sequence Number, Desired Buffer Size ($B_{des}$), and Minimum/Average Packet Delivery Delay ($D_{LTE}, D_{NR}$). 3) Dynamic Water-Filling Split: Calculate transmission ratio: $\\text{Ratio}_{NR} = \\frac{Throughput_{NR} \\times D_{LTE}}{Throughput_{NR} \\times D_{LTE} + Throughput_{LTE} \\times D_{NR}}$. 4) Out-of-Order Avoidance: Assign consecutive blocks of PDCP sequence numbers proportional to delivery rates so that packet $N$ over LTE and packet $N + \\Delta$ over NR arrive at the UE receiver at the exact same physical time.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 38.425 (User Plane Protocol), TS 37.340", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN, Nokia Munich, Samsung SRI-B", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q095", "category": "Telecom System Design", "subcategory": "O-RAN Near-RT RIC & E2 Interface", "question": "How does an O-RAN Near-Real-Time RAN Intelligent Controller (Near-RT RIC) interface with the O-DU MAC layer over the E2 interface? How can your M.Tech ML model be deployed as an xApp?", "detailedAnswer": "O-RAN Architecture Integration: 1) E2 Interface & E2 Nodes: The Near-RT RIC connects to E2 Nodes (O-CU-CP, O-CU-UP, O-DU) via SCTP. The E2 interface uses E2AP (E2 Application Protocol) with Service Models (E2SM): - E2SM-KPM (Key Performance Measurement): O-DU MAC reports real-time telemetry (PRB utilisation, buffer occupancy, CQI distributions) every 10 ms - 100 ms. - E2SM-RC (RAN Control): Allows xApps to send control actions, policy updates, or scheduling parameters. 2) Deploying the M.Tech ML Model as an xApp: - The candidate's predictive resource allocation model operates at two tiers: - Tier 1 (xApp in Near-RT RIC, 10-100 ms loop): Runs high-level policy inference, predicting macroscopic traffic trends and updating PUCCH resource reservation budgets. - Tier 2 (Embedded Treelite Binary in O-DU, sub-1 ms loop): Runs the ultra-fast 0.42 ms real-time inference on the MAC core, directly driving per-slot PUCCH allocation decisions.", "codeOrSpecRef": "O-RAN.WG3.E2SM-KPM-v02.00, O-RAN.WG3.E2SM-RC-v01.00", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud-RAN / O-RAN, Deutsche Telekom, Nokia", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q096", "category": "Telecom System Design", "subcategory": "Multi-Carrier 16-CC Scheduler", "question": "Design a scalable Multi-Carrier Downlink Scheduler supporting up to 16 Component Carriers in 5G NR. How do you distribute computing across NUMA nodes?", "detailedAnswer": "Multi-Carrier Architecture Design: 1) NUMA (Non-Uniform Memory Access) Topology: In modern multi-socket servers (e.g. dual AMD EPYC or Intel Xeon), cross-socket memory access incurs severe latency penalties (~100 ns). Rule: Bind each carrier's scheduling pipeline to the exact NUMA node where its Fronthaul NIC (PCIe lane) and local DDR memory reside. 2) Carrier Partitioning: - Assign 8 CCs to NUMA Node 0 (Cores 2-15) and 8 CCs to NUMA Node 1 (Cores 18-31). - Run completely independent MAC scheduler instances per carrier. Zero cross-carrier mutex locks! 3) Cross-Carrier Coordination: When cross-carrier scheduling or joint power allocation is enabled, exchange control states via cache-aligned lock-free ring buffers between NUMA nodes only during the inter-slot guard period.", "codeOrSpecRef": "NUMA Architecture Guidelines, 3GPP TS 38.300", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN, Intel Telecom Platforms, Nokia AirScale", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q097", "category": "Telecom System Design", "subcategory": "High-Capacity UE Context Manager", "question": "Design a high-capacity UE Context Lookup Manager in C++ capable of storing 10,000 active UEs with O(1) deterministic retrieval time by C-RNTI and UE Index.", "detailedAnswer": "Design Analysis: Standard `std::unordered_map<uint16_t, UEContext*>` is unacceptable for real-time telecom: dynamic node allocation on insertion, cache-unfriendly bucket chaining, and non-deterministic hash collision resolution ($O(N)$ worst-case). Deterministic High-Performance Design: 1) Dual-Index Flat Array: - C-RNTI range in 3GPP is 16-bit (`0x0001` to `0xFFF3`, 65,536 values). - Pre-allocate a flat lookup table of 65,536 uint16_t indices: `uint16_t crnti_to_ue_idx[65536];` (consumes only 128 KB, fits completely in L2 cache!). - Pre-allocate a contiguous array of UE context structures: `UEContext ue_pool[MAX_UES];` (e.g. 10,000 slots). 2) Retrieval: inline UEContext* get_ue_by_crnti(uint16_t crnti) {     uint16_t idx = crnti_to_ue_idx[crnti];     if (__builtin_expect(idx == INVALID_INDEX, 0)) return nullptr;     return &ue_pool[idx]; } Retrieval time is guaranteed $O(1)$, deterministic, and completes in ~2 clock cycles!", "codeOrSpecRef": "C++ High-Performance Low-Latency System Design", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson L2 Architecture, Apple Munich Modem, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q098", "category": "Telecom System Design", "subcategory": "Graceful Degradation Architecture", "question": "Design a fail-safe graceful degradation architecture for a Cloud-RAN MAC scheduler when CPU core temperatures throttle clock speeds or L1 processing overruns occur.", "detailedAnswer": "Graceful Degradation Blueprint: 1) Execution Budget Watchdog: An independent high-resolution hardware timer tracks elapsed execution in the scheduling loop. 2) Multi-Tiered Load Shedding: - Tier 1 (Nominal, budget $< 80$ µs): Full frequency-selective Proportional Fair scheduling with MU-MIMO pairing and aperiodic CSI processing. - Tier 2 (Mild Overrun, 80-100 µs): Disable MU-MIMO spatial search; revert to SU-MIMO. Skip aperiodic CSI computation. - Tier 3 (Severe Overrun, $> 100$ µs): Switch from Proportional Fair to Greedy Round-Robin. Schedule only active high-priority GBR/URLLC bearers. - Tier 4 (Critical Deadline Exceeded): Drop remaining new data grants and transmit only pre-built HARQ retransmissions to protect link stability. 3) Telemetry: Raise OAM alarm (`SCHEDULER_OVERLOAD_TIER_X`) to trigger Kubernetes horizontal pod autoscaling (HPA) or O-DU cell migration.", "codeOrSpecRef": "Cloud RAN High Availability & Resiliency Architectures", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Cloud RAN, Nokia AirScale, Mavenir Germany", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q099", "category": "Telecom System Design", "subcategory": "Test Automation & Emulation Architecture", "question": "Design a Continuous Integration / Hardware-in-the-Loop (HIL) automated test framework for validating 3GPP MAC DL software releases using Viavi TM500.", "detailedAnswer": "HIL Automated Test System: 1) Physical Setup: DUT (Device Under Test - eNB/gNodeB baseband server) connected via RF coaxial cables and attenuators to Viavi TM500 multi-UE emulator. 2) Control Layer: Automated Python framework running on test controller server: - Communicates with Viavi TM500 via TM500 REST API / SCPI commands. - Communicates with baseband DUT via SSH and proprietary diagnostic interfaces. 3) Automated Test Sequences: - Test Suite A: Functional Spec Conformance (3GPP TS 36.521 / 38.521 - RACH, HARQ, SCell activation/deactivation, DRX). - Test Suite B: Peak Throughput & Stress (Full-buffer UDP/TCP traffic across 100 UEs for 12 hours). - Test Suite C: Impairment Testing (Simulating Doppler shift, AWGN noise, fast fading EVA 70Hz, packet drop injection on backhaul). 4) Automated Analysis: Post-test scripts automatically parse QXDM and TM500 logs, verifying KPI thresholds (BLER $< 1\\%$, zero core dumps, zero memory leaks).", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 36.521-1, TS 38.521-1, Viavi TM500 Test Automation", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Samsung SRI-B, Ericsson Test Automation, Rohde & Schwarz", "difficultyLevel": "Medium"}, {"id": "Q100", "category": "Telecom System Design", "subcategory": "Evolution to 6G & AI-Native MAC", "question": "How is the telecom industry evolving from 5G-Advanced (Rel-18/19) towards 6G AI-native MAC layers? What role will your ML thesis background play?", "detailedAnswer": "Evolution towards 6G AI-Native MAC: 1) 3GPP Rel-18/19 (5G-Advanced): Standardizing AI/ML for the Air Interface (TS 38.843 study item), targeting: - AI-driven Channel State Information (CSI) feedback compression and prediction. - AI-driven Beam Management and spatial trajectory tracking. - Intelligent positioning and energy-saving sleep modes. 2) 6G Vision: Deep integration of AI into L1/L2 MAC: - Replacing hand-crafted heuristic schedulers (Proportional Fair) and link adaptation loops (OLLA) with reinforcement learning (RL) and neural surrogate models capable of joint multi-cell optimization. - Neural Receiver and joint source-channel coding. 3) Candidate Advantage: Akhzar Farhan's exact background (5+ years in 3GPP MAC DL at Samsung SRI-B + M.Tech in Data Science from IISc + native real-time ML deployment via Treelite under 1 ms budgets) positions him uniquely at the absolute cutting edge for lead architecture roles in Ericsson Cloud RAN and German telecom R&D teams pioneering AI-native RAN.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TR 38.843 (Study on AI/ML for NR Air Interface), ITU-R 6G Framework", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson Research Aachen, Nokia Bell Labs Munich, Apple Munich", "difficultyLevel": "Expert / Lead Level"}, {"id": "Q_101", "category": "Top 15 High-Yield NR", "subcategory": "Core Spec Mastery", "question": "How does Downlink HARQ differ between 4G LTE and 5G NR?", "detailedAnswer": "In 4G LTE FDD, Downlink HARQ follows a rigid, synchronous timeline: feedback arrives exactly 4 ms after transmission, and retransmissions occur at fixed 8 ms intervals with 8 stop-and-wait processes per carrier.\n\nIn 5G NR, HARQ is completely redesigned for extreme flexibility across varied numerologies (15 kHz to 120 kHz):\n1. **Fully Asynchronous & Dynamic Timing (K1)**: The gNodeB explicitly dictates feedback timing via the 'PDSCH-to-HARQ_feedback timing indicator' field in DCI format 1_0/1_1. The slot offset K1 dynamically instructs the UE when to transmit PUCCH HARQ-ACK.\n2. **Up to 16 Parallel HARQ Processes**: Accommodates high-throughput mmWave and carrier aggregation pipelines, preventing scheduler stall.\n3. **Code Block Group (CBG) Retransmissions (TS 38.321 §5.3.2.2)**: Transport blocks segmented into multiple code blocks can be grouped into up to 8 CBGs. Retransmissions target only failed CBGs via CBGTI bitmasks, cutting radio retransmission overhead by 75-80%.\n4. **Dynamic HARQ-ACK Codebooks (TS 38.213 §9.2.3)**: Uses Counter DAI (cDAI) and Total DAI (tDAI) to size the feedback payload dynamically and catch missed DCIs, replacing LTE's rigid subframe bitmaps.", "codeOrSpecRef": "TS 38.321 §5.3, TS 38.214 §5.1, TS 38.213 §9.2.3", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson, Nokia, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q_102", "category": "Top 15 High-Yield NR", "subcategory": "Core Spec Mastery", "question": "Explain the Bandwidth Part (BWP) framework and its significance in MAC Downlink.", "detailedAnswer": "A Bandwidth Part (BWP) is a contiguous subset of physical resource blocks (PRBs) on a carrier with a specific numerology (subcarrier spacing and cyclic prefix).\n- **Core Architecture**: A UE can be configured with up to 4 DL BWPs and 4 UL BWPs per serving cell, but only ONE DL and ONE UL BWP is active at any given instant (in Rel-15).\n- **Types**: Initial BWP (for initial access/RACH), Active BWP (for current data transfer), and Default BWP (for battery saving when inactivity timer expires).\n- **Significance in MAC DL**:\n  1. **Energy Efficiency**: Eliminates the requirement for UEs to monitor wideband 100 MHz channels when idle, shrinking RF reception bandwidth to 20 MHz or less.\n  2. **Dynamic Adaptation**: MAC switches BWPs dynamically via 1-2 bits in DCI 0_1/1_1 or via `bwp-InactivityTimer`.\n  3. **Multi-Numerology Operation**: Different BWPs on the same carrier can use different numerologies (e.g. 15 kHz for wide coverage, 30 kHz for high throughput).", "codeOrSpecRef": "TS 38.321 §5.15, TS 38.213 §12", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson, Nokia, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q_103", "category": "Top 15 High-Yield NR", "subcategory": "Core Spec Mastery", "question": "What is the difference between PDSCH Mapping Type A and Type B?", "detailedAnswer": "PDSCH Mapping Type defines where Demodulation Reference Signals (DM-RS) are placed relative to the slot boundary and how resources are scheduled:\n- **Mapping Type A (Slot-Based Scheduling)**:\n  - DM-RS position: Fixed at symbol 2 or 3 of the slot, regardless of where PDSCH data starts.\n  - Start symbol: S ∈ {2, 3}; Length: L ∈ {3..14}, with S + L ≤ 14.\n  - Typical use: Standard eMBB slot transmissions where the whole slot or large fraction is allocated to one or more UEs.\n- **Mapping Type B (Mini-Slot / Non-Slot Scheduling)**:\n  - DM-RS position: Always at the VERY FIRST symbol of the PDSCH allocation.\n  - Start symbol: S ∈ {0..12}; Length: L ∈ {2, 4, 7}, with S + L ≤ 14.\n  - Typical use: Ultra-Reliable Low-Latency Communication (URLLC) preemption and mid-slot transmissions, allowing immediate channel estimation without waiting for slot boundaries.", "codeOrSpecRef": "TS 38.214 §5.1.2.1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson, Nokia, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q_104", "category": "Top 15 High-Yield NR", "subcategory": "Core Spec Mastery", "question": "How does 5G NR handle Cross-Carrier Scheduling with mixed numerologies?", "detailedAnswer": "Cross-carrier scheduling allows PDCCH on a scheduling cell (e.g. PCell at lower frequency) to allocate PDSCH/PUSCH on a scheduled cell (e.g. SCell at higher frequency).\nWhen carriers operate at different subcarrier spacings (μ_PDCCH ≠ μ_PDSCH):\n1. **Slot Ratio Alignment**: Slot durations scale by 2^(μ_PDSCH - μ_PDCCH).\n   - If μ_PDCCH < μ_PDSCH (e.g. 15 kHz scheduling 30 kHz): 1 PDCCH slot covers 2 PDSCH slots. DCI indicates which sub-slot (2n or 2n+1) is scheduled.\n   - If μ_PDCCH > μ_PDSCH (e.g. 30 kHz scheduling 15 kHz): Multiple PDCCH slots fall inside one PDSCH slot duration; scheduler enforces non-overlapping grants.\n2. **Timing Offset (K0)**: In DCI, K0 defines the slot offset between PDCCH and PDSCH, computed relative to the scheduled cell's numerology.\n3. **Processing Delay (Np,1)**: The scheduler must ensure the time between PDCCH end and PDSCH start satisfies UE preparation time Np,1 based on min(μ_PDCCH, μ_PDSCH).", "codeOrSpecRef": "TS 38.214 §5.1.2.1, TS 38.213 §10.1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson, Nokia, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q_105", "category": "Top 15 High-Yield NR", "subcategory": "Core Spec Mastery", "question": "What is the difference between Type-1 (Semi-Static) and Type-2 (Dynamic) HARQ-ACK codebooks?", "detailedAnswer": "HARQ-ACK codebooks determine how the UE bundles and serializes ACK/NACK bits for multiple PDSCH receptions onto a single PUCCH/PUSCH uplink transmission:\n- **Type-1 (Semi-Static) Codebook**:\n  - Size is determined by RRC configuration: evaluates all possible candidate PDSCH occasions based on the set of K1 values and TDRA SLIV table across all configured component carriers.\n  - Generates a fixed-size bitmap. If an occasion had no grant, UE inserts NACK.\n  - Pro: Robust against missed DCIs (gNodeB and UE always agree on bitmap length and indexing).\n  - Con: Heavy uplink payload overhead when data traffic is sporadic.\n- **Type-2 (Dynamic) Codebook**:\n  - Size adapts dynamically to the actual number of scheduled grants.\n  - Uses Counter DAI (cDAI) and Total DAI (tDAI) in DCI to trace scheduled assignments using modulo-4 counting.\n  - Pro: Highly compact uplink payload.\n  - Con: If trailing DCIs are lost across all carriers, UE cannot detect omission without tDAI coordination.", "codeOrSpecRef": "TS 38.213 §9.2.3", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson, Nokia, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q_106", "category": "Top 15 High-Yield NR", "subcategory": "Core Spec Mastery", "question": "Walk through the exact algorithm for SLIV calculation in PDSCH TDRA.", "detailedAnswer": "The Start and Length Indicator Value (SLIV) compresses the start symbol S and allocated length L into a single integer (0 to 127, 7 bits):\nConstraint: 0 < L ≤ 14 - S.\n\n**Encoding Algorithm**:\n1. Check condition: if (L - 1) ≤ 7:\n   SLIV = 14 * (L - 1) + S\n2. Else:\n   SLIV = 14 * (14 - L + 1) + (14 - 1 - S)\n\n**Decoding Algorithm**:\n1. Compute quotient q = floor(SLIV / 14) and remainder r = SLIV % 14.\n2. If (q + r) ≤ 13:\n   L = q + 1\n   S = r\n3. Else:\n   L = 14 - q + 1\n   S = 14 - 1 - r\n\nExample: S=2, L=12. (L - 1) = 11 > 7.\nSLIV = 14 * (14 - 12 + 1) + (14 - 1 - 2) = 14 * 3 + 11 = 42 + 11 = 53.", "codeOrSpecRef": "TS 38.214 §5.1.2.1", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson, Nokia, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q_107", "category": "Top 15 High-Yield NR", "subcategory": "Core Spec Mastery", "question": "What are TCI States and QCL Types in 5G NR?", "detailedAnswer": "Quasi-Co-Location (QCL) defines mathematical assumptions that channel properties of one reference signal can be applied to demodulate another:\n- **QCL Types**:\n  - QCL-TypeA: Doppler shift, Doppler spread, Average delay, Delay spread (full multipath profile).\n  - QCL-TypeB: Doppler shift, Doppler spread.\n  - QCL-TypeC: Doppler shift, Average delay.\n  - QCL-TypeD: Spatial Rx parameter (Angle of Arrival / same Rx beamforming weights for mmWave!).\n- **TCI (Transmission Configuration Indicator) States**:\n  - Connects PDSCH DM-RS to source reference signals (SSB or CSI-RS).\n  - RRC configures up to 128 TCI states.\n  - MAC CE activates a subset of up to 8 active TCI states.\n  - DCI 1_1 uses a 3-bit field to dynamically switch between the 8 active beams slot-by-slot.", "codeOrSpecRef": "TS 38.214 §5.1.5", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson, Nokia, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q_108", "category": "Top 15 High-Yield NR", "subcategory": "Core Spec Mastery", "question": "How does Rel-16 Dormant BWP improve Carrier Aggregation power efficiency?", "detailedAnswer": "In Rel-15 CA, when an SCell is deactivated to save battery, all channel measurements stop. Re-activating it requires 24-34 ms for RRC reconfiguration, beam alignment, and channel feedback restart.\n- **Rel-16 Dormant BWP Solution**:\n  - The SCell enters a 'Dormant BWP' where the UE stops PDCCH blind decoding (saving 90% of RF and baseband processing power).\n  - Crucially, the UE **continues** periodic CSI measurements, CQI/RI reporting, and beam tracking towards the PCell.\n  - Switching from Dormant BWP back to Active BWP is executed in **2 to 4 ms** via a dedicated DCI 1_1 or DCI 2_6 bitmap.\n  - Achieves near-instantaneous multi-gigabit CA aggregation bursts with minimal baseline battery drain.", "codeOrSpecRef": "TS 38.321 §5.13, §5.15", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson, Nokia, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q_109", "category": "Top 15 High-Yield NR", "subcategory": "Core Spec Mastery", "question": "Describe the EN-DC Option 3x architecture and split bearer data flow.", "detailedAnswer": "E-UTRA-NR Dual Connectivity (EN-DC Option 3x) is the dominant Non-Standalone (NSA) 5G deployment model:\n1. **Control Plane**: Master eNodeB (MeNB, 4G LTE) anchors S1-MME to the EPC. SgNB (5G NR) coordinates with MeNB over X2-C.\n2. **User Plane**: S1-U user plane traffic from the Serving Gateway terminates directly at the 5G SgNB.\n3. **SCG Split Bearer Flow**:\n   - Packets enter NR PDCP at the SgNB, where PDCP Sequence Numbers are assigned.\n   - SgNB splits traffic: sending high-throughput chunks directly over 5G RLC/MAC/PHY, and routing secondary chunks over the X2-U interface to the MeNB for 4G LTE transmission.\n   - Flow control is regulated by TS 38.425 Downlink Data Delivery Status (DDDS) frames sent from MeNB back to SgNB to prevent X2 buffer overflow.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP TS 37.340, TS 38.300 §6.5", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson, Nokia, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q_110", "category": "Top 15 High-Yield NR", "subcategory": "Core Spec Mastery", "question": "How does the MAC PDU structure in 5G NR differ from 4G LTE, and why does it matter for high-speed software architectures?", "detailedAnswer": "In 4G LTE, all MAC subheaders were grouped at the very front of the MAC PDU. This meant the MAC layer had to know the exact sizes of all RLC SDUs in advance before serializing the packet.\n\nIn 5G NR, each MAC subheader immediately precedes its corresponding MAC SDU or MAC CE:\n- **Subheader Layout**: [R/F/LCID/L] placed right before the payload data.\n- **Why it Matters for High-Speed Software**:\n  1. **Just-in-Time Assembly**: RLC can stream SDUs into DMA buffers asynchronously without waiting for the MAC scheduler to compute all multiplexing headers upfront.\n  2. **Hardware DMA Acceleration**: Network interface accelerators can pipeline subheader insertion directly into packet headroom without multi-pass buffer copies (zero-copy processing).\n  3. **Low Latency**: Directly addresses the sub-millisecond slot deadlines (e.g. 125 µs) of 5G NR.", "codeOrSpecRef": "TS 38.321 §6.1.2, TS 36.321 §6.1.2", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson, Nokia, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q_111", "category": "Top 15 High-Yield NR", "subcategory": "Core Spec Mastery", "question": "What are the latency requirements for a 5G NR MAC scheduler running at 30 kHz SCS?", "detailedAnswer": "At 30 kHz SCS (μ = 1, standard FR1 C-band deployment):\n- **Slot Duration**: Exactly 0.5 ms (500 µs).\n- **Execution Budget Breakdown**:\n  - L1 UCI Ingestion & OLLA Updates: 50 µs\n  - Time-Domain Candidate Filtering & Metric Sorting (PF / QoS): 80 µs\n  - Frequency-Domain RB Allocation & TBS Table Lookup: 150 µs\n  - MAC PDU Subheader Generation & FAPI Dispatch: 100 µs\n  - DMA Baseband Acceleration Transfer & Margin: 120 µs\n  - **Total Real-Time Budget**: Must complete within ~380 µs to guarantee L1 transmission at the slot boundary.\n- **OS Constraints**: Requires PREEMPT_RT Linux kernel, CPU core pinning (`pthread_setaffinity_np`), `mlockall` to eliminate page faults, and lock-free SPSC queues.", "codeOrSpecRef": "TS 38.211 §4.3, O-RAN Architecture", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson, Nokia, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q_112", "category": "Top 15 High-Yield NR", "subcategory": "Core Spec Mastery", "question": "How does Outer Loop Link Adaptation (OLLA) interact with CQI feedback in 5G MAC DL?", "detailedAnswer": "Inner loop link adaptation converts periodic UE CQI reports (1 to 15) into an initial Signal-to-Interference-plus-Noise Ratio (SINR) using static AWGN simulation lookup tables.\nHowever, real radio environments suffer from multi-path fading, interference fluctuations, and CQI reporting delays.\n\nOLLA maintains an adaptive offset Δ_OLLA to adjust effective SINR:\nSINR_eff = SINR_CQI - Δ_OLLA\n\n**Closed-Loop Step Dynamics**:\n- On HARQ ACK: Δ_OLLA = Δ_OLLA - Δ_down\n- On HARQ NACK: Δ_OLLA = Δ_OLLA + Δ_up\nTo converge to a target 10% BLER:\nΔ_down = Δ_up * (BLER_target / (1 - BLER_target)) = Δ_up * (0.10 / 0.90) = Δ_up / 9.\nExample: Δ_up = 1.0 dB implies Δ_down = 0.111 dB.", "codeOrSpecRef": "TS 38.214 §5.2.2 & Scheduler Algorithms", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson, Nokia, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q_113", "category": "Top 15 High-Yield NR", "subcategory": "Core Spec Mastery", "question": "What is the FAPI interface, and what are the key messages exchanged between MAC and PHY?", "detailedAnswer": "The Small Cell Forum Functional Application Platform Interface (FAPI) standardizes the software messaging contract between Layer 2 (MAC) and Layer 1 (PHY) baseband firmware.\n\nKey Message Flows:\n1. **DL_TTI.request (MAC -> PHY)**: Sent every slot. Contains resource assignments for PDCCH, PDSCH, and CSI-RS (SLIV, PRBs, MCS, precoding matrices).\n2. **TX_Data.request (MAC -> PHY)**: Carries the actual transport block payload bytes generated by MAC multiplexing.\n3. **UL_TTI.request (MAC -> PHY)**: Directs PHY which PUSCH, PUCCH, and PRACH resources to listen for in the upcoming uplink slot.\n4. **UCI.indication (PHY -> MAC)**: Uplink Control Information delivered from PHY to MAC containing decoded HARQ ACK/NACKs, CQI, PMI, and RI.", "codeOrSpecRef": "Small Cell Forum SCF 222 v10.02", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson, Nokia, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q_114", "category": "Top 15 High-Yield NR", "subcategory": "Core Spec Mastery", "question": "Explain the difference between Type 0 and Type 1 Frequency Domain Resource Allocation (FDRA).", "detailedAnswer": "In 5G NR PDSCH, the gNodeB signals allocated Physical Resource Blocks (PRBs) using one of two methods:\n- **Type 0 (Resource Block Group Bitmap)**:\n  - PRBs are grouped into RBGs of size P ∈ {2, 4, 8, 16} based on BWP bandwidth and RRC table config.\n  - A bitmap in DCI assigns individual RBGs (1 bit per RBG).\n  - Pro: Allows non-contiguous, frequency-selective scheduling to exploit channel peaks.\n  - Con: Larger DCI bit overhead.\n- **Type 1 (Contiguous Resource Indication Value - RIV)**:\n  - Allocates a contiguous block of PRBs defined by starting PRB RB_start and length L_RBs.\n  - Encoded into a compact RIV integer using the triangle number formula.\n  - Pro: Highly compact DCI field (log2(N*(N+1)/2) bits).\n  - Con: Cannot allocate non-contiguous frequency chunks.", "codeOrSpecRef": "TS 38.214 §5.1.2.2", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson, Nokia, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}, {"id": "Q_115", "category": "Top 15 High-Yield NR", "subcategory": "Core Spec Mastery", "question": "How can Machine Learning be applied to 5G MAC Downlink Scheduling (connecting to your IISc thesis)?", "detailedAnswer": "In telecom L2 schedulers, classical heuristic algorithms (Proportional Fair, round-robin) struggle under non-ideal backhaul latency and rapid traffic fluctuations.\n\n**IISc Thesis Application (Akhzar's Research)**:\n1. **Problem**: In Inter-Site Carrier Aggregation over 10-20 ms non-ideal backhaul, the PCell must allocate PUCCH resources for SCell HARQ feedback without real-time knowledge of SCell subframe allocations.\n2. **ML Solution**: A Gradient-Boosted Decision Tree model trained on flow-control metrics, buffer occupancy, and arrival variance predicts SCell scheduling probability.\n3. **Real-Time Deployment**: Compiled via **Treelite** into an ultra-compact ~280 KB native C binary running directly inside the real-time MAC scheduling thread.\n4. **Outcome**: Inference executes in **0.42 ms** (fitting the 1 ms TTI deadline), boosting scheduling hit rate from 42% to 91% and slashing uplink PUCCH resource waste from 58% to 6.4%.", "codeOrSpecRef": "3GPP Rel-17/18 AI/ML in RAN & Candidate Thesis", "targetCompanyRelevance": "Ericsson, Nokia, Apple Munich, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm", "difficultyLevel": "Hard"}], "nrExtras": {"formulasCheatSheet": [{"name": "Scalable Subcarrier Spacing (SCS)", "spec": "3GPP TS 38.211 §4.2", "formula": "Δf = 15 × 2^μ kHz   (μ ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3, 4} → 15, 30, 60, 120, 240 kHz)"}, {"name": "Slot Duration & Subframe Scaling", "spec": "3GPP TS 38.211 §4.3.2", "formula": "T_slot = 1 / 2^μ ms   (μ=0: 1.0 ms | μ=1: 0.5 ms | μ=2: 0.25 ms | μ=3: 0.125 ms);  N_slot^subframe,μ = 2^μ"}, {"name": "PDSCH Start & Length Indicator Value (SLIV)", "spec": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.1.2.1", "formula": "If (L - 1) ≤ 7:  SLIV = 14 × (L - 1) + S\nElse:            SLIV = 14 × (14 - L + 1) + (14 - 1 - S)   [0 < L ≤ 14 - S]"}, {"name": "Outer Loop Link Adaptation (OLLA) Steady-State Step", "spec": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.2.2 / AMC", "formula": "SINR_eff = SINR_CQI - Δ_OLLA;   Δ_down = Δ_up × (BLER_target / (1 - BLER_target))\nFor 10% BLER_target: Δ_down = Δ_up / 9  (e.g., Δ_up = 1.0 dB ⇒ Δ_down = 0.111 dB)"}, {"name": "3GPP 5G NR Approximate Peak Data Rate", "spec": "3GPP TS 38.306 §4.1.2", "formula": "R_max (Mbps) = 10^-6 × Σ_j [ v_Layers^(j) × Q_m^(j) × f^(j) × R_max × (N_PRB^(BW,μ) × 12 / T_s^μ) × (1 - OH^(j)) ]"}, {"name": "Logical Channel Prioritization (LCP) Token Bucket", "spec": "3GPP TS 38.321 §5.4.3.1", "formula": "B_j = min(B_j + PBR_j × T_elapsed,  PBR_j × BSD_j)   [B_j can go negative after grant allocation]"}, {"name": "Asynchronous DL HARQ Round-Trip Time (RTT)", "spec": "3GPP TS 38.213 §9.2.3", "formula": "T_RTT = K_0 (PDCCH→PDSCH) + K_1 (PDSCH→PUCCH HARQ-ACK) + T_proc,gNB + K_re-tx"}, {"name": "Cross-Carrier Scheduling Slot Ratio & Prep Time", "spec": "3GPP TS 38.214 §5.1.2.1", "formula": "N_ratio = 2^(μ_PDSCH - μ_PDCCH);   T_prep ≥ N_{p,1} × T_slot^{min(μ_PDCCH, μ_PDSCH)};   CIF = 3 bits (up to 8 CCs)"}], "nackStormDecisionTree": [{"step": "Step 1: Inspect PDSCH CRC in 0xB887 (NR5G MAC PDSCH Status)", "branchA": "CRC PASS at UE, yet gNodeB keeps retransmitting the same HARQ PID (NDI not toggled)? ⇒ Uplink Feedback Failure! The gNodeB is failing to decode PUCCH/PUSCH HARQ-ACK (interpreting DTX as NACK). Root cause: Uplink interference, PUCCH power control saturation, or Timing Advance drift.", "branchB": "CRC FAIL at UE on initial transmission and retransmissions? ⇒ Proceed to Step 2."}, {"step": "Step 2: Correlate L1 SNR & Beam RSRP in 0xB97F (NR5G ML1 Searcher Measurement DB)", "branchA": "High SNR (> 20 dB) with 100% PDSCH BLER? ⇒ Link Adaptation / Precoding Mismatch! OLLA offset drifted or CSI-RS report is stale, forcing MCS 27 (256-QAM) on a channel supporting only MCS 16, or DM-RS port / TCI QCL-TypeD beam mismatch.", "branchB": "Sharp SNR / L1-RSRP drop (< 0 dB)? ⇒ Physical RF blockage, external interference, or Beam Failure Recovery (BFR) not triggered in time."}, {"step": "Step 3: Audit RRC OTA Configuration in 0xB821 (NR5G RRC OTA Packet)", "branchA": "Check ServingCellConfig, pdsch-TimeDomainAllocationList (SLIV validity), and BWP-Downlink parameters.", "branchB": "In Carrier Aggregation, verify CrossCarrierSchedulingConfig CIF bit alignment and Type-1 vs Type-2 HARQ-ACK codebook cDAI/tDAI synchronization."}, {"step": "Step 4: L1/L2 Real-Time Baseband Execution Telemetry (FAPI / O-DU)", "branchA": "Inspect baseband thread scheduling jitter and FAPI DL_TTI.request / TX_Data.request arrival timestamps.", "branchB": "If the L2 MAC thread missed the 380 µs slot deadline (at 30 kHz SCS), the L1 PHY transmits an empty/zeroed DMA buffer while sending a valid PDCCH DCI — causing 100% CRC failure at the UE despite perfect radio conditions!"}], "candidateNarrative": {"title": "Executive Candidate Narrative, Thesis Defense & Positioning", "subtitle": "Tailored for Senior / Lead L2 MAC & Baseband Roles (Ericsson, Apple Munich, Nokia AirScale, Rohde & Schwarz, Qualcomm)", "triadPillars": [{"title": "Pillar 1: Production 3GPP L2 MAC Domain Mastery", "details": "5+ years at Samsung R&D Institute Bangalore (SRI-B) engineering commercial 4G LTE & 5G NR MAC Downlink schedulers, Inter-Site Carrier Aggregation over non-ideal backhaul, SCell activation/deactivation state machines, and HARQ/AMC loops deployed across Tier-1 global operators."}, {"title": "Pillar 2: Hard Real-Time C/C++ & RTOS Systems Architecture", "details": "Deterministic sub-millisecond execution (< 1 ms TTI / 125–500 µs slot budgets), lock-free SPSC ring buffers with acquire/release atomics, 64-byte cache-line alignment (alignas(64)) eliminating false sharing, zero-copy DMA packet pools, and Linux PREEMPT_RT core pinning."}, {"title": "Pillar 3: Real-Time ML in Baseband L2 (IISc M.Tech Unfair Advantage)", "details": "Designed and compiled a Gradient-Boosted Decision Tree (XGBoost) into a ~280 KB zero-allocation native C binary via Treelite executing in 0.42 ms inside the 1 ms MAC TTI loop — boosting Inter-Site CA PUCCH scheduling hit rate from 42% to 91% and cutting PUCCH waste by 9× (58% → 6.4%)."}], "metricsReconciliation": ["Live Commercial Testbed (Resume Metric): 42% static baseline → 91% hit rate with deployed Treelite native C binary (9× PUCCH wastage reduction from 58% to 6.4%, 0.42 ms worst-case single-sample latency under bursty SCell traffic).", "Standardized 60-UE E500 Simulation (IISc Thesis Report Metric): 60% static baseline → 85% tuned XGBoost across 5 Resource Indices (0.25 ms inference latency under balanced traffic distributions)."], "verbatimPitch": "\"In our initial commercial testbed experiments with severe bursty SCell traffic, the naive static baseline hit rate dropped to 42% due to high traffic variance, which our deployed Treelite native C binary raised to 91% with a 9× reduction in PUCCH wastage. In our standardized 60-UE E500 simulation documented in my IISc thesis report, under balanced traffic conditions, the static algorithm achieved a 60% baseline, which our tuned XGBoost model improved to 85% with an inference latency of just 0.25 ms across 5 Resource Indices—comfortably within the 1 ms TTI budget.\""}, "actionableChecklist": ["Phase 1 (Daily 06:45 AM Spec Habit): Read 3GPP TS 38.211 §4.2 (Numerologies), TS 38.213 §9.2/§11 (HARQ Codebooks & Slot Formats), and TS 38.321 §5.3/§5.4/§6.1 (HARQ, LCP, MAC PDU).", "Phase 2 (Open-Source Baseband Code Trace): Trace openair2/LAYER2/NR_MAC_gNB/gNB_scheduler_dlsch.c (OAI) and lib/mac/mac_dl_processor.cc (srsRAN Project) to map spec clauses to C++ structs.", "Phase 3 (C++20/23 & Lock-Free Systems Sprint): Practice writing lock-free SPSC ring buffers, arena/pool allocators, custom unique_ptr/shared_ptr, and TS 38.321 MAC subheader encoders from memory.", "Phase 4 (Protocol Math & Calculation Drills): Work through SLIV encoding/decoding, Type-1 vs Type-2 cDAI/tDAI fault injection, full 5-step TBS calculation, and OLLA step-size convergence on paper.", "Phase 5 (Verbal Mock Defense): Rehearse Top 15 5G NR questions, Top C++ concurrency/memory questions, and the IISc Thesis 42%→91% vs 60%→85% pitch aloud until delivery is crisp and effortless."], "targetEmployers": [{"company": "Ericsson (Bangalore/Noida India & Aachen/Frankfurt Germany)", "focus": "Cloud-RAN DU/CU in Linux C++, 5G Advanced MAC DL/UL Schedulers, Carrier Aggregation (up to 16 CCs), EN-DC / NR-DC, O-RAN 7-2x Split, DPDK & PREEMPT_RT.", "pitchAngle": "Emphasize Inter-Site Downlink CA over non-ideal backhaul, GTP-U flow control, multi-core deterministic C++ scheduling within sub-millisecond slot budgets, and 3GPP Rel-16/17 MAC feature ownership."}, {"company": "Apple – Cellular Modem Firmware (Munich Silicon Design Center)", "focus": "In-house 5G/6G cellular baseband modem firmware (C/C++), UE-side L2 MAC/RLC/PDCP protocols, PHY-MAC interface, cDRX power saving, dynamic BWP switching.", "pitchAngle": "Emphasize UE/baseband MAC state machines, bitwise PDU serialization, zero-allocation memory pools, C++17/20 determinism, and deep Qualcomm QXDM / Viavi TM500 trace debugging."}, {"company": "Nokia AirScale & Bell Labs (Munich & Ulm, Germany / Bangalore)", "focus": "AirScale Baseband L2 MAC/Scheduler software, SoC firmware, Open RAN O-DU, Massive MIMO beamforming schedulers (TCI states, CSI-RS).", "pitchAngle": "Emphasize gNodeB Proportional Fair / QoS scheduler pipelines, OLLA link adaptation, multi-carrier HARQ management, and ML-driven PUCCH resource optimization."}, {"company": "Rohde & Schwarz (Munich Global HQ)", "focus": "CMX500 / CMW500 5G/6G protocol stack emulation engines, 3GPP TS 38.521 conformance testing, L1/L2 protocol dissection.", "pitchAngle": "Emphasize authoritative 3GPP specification compliance, Viavi TM500 multi-UE emulation, GDB core dump analysis, and cross-layer PHY-MAC timing verification."}]}};
